    "md5": "c8c33462173b3ffd678dfc40ec7daf48",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1780,
    "chunk_text": "Proposed Organised Crimes Bill faces mounting challenges before Supreme Court\n\nColombo, Nov. 2 (Daily Mirror) - Special Determination petitions are mounting before the Supreme Court challenging the constitutionality of the proposed Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill, with petitioners raising concerns that several provisions could undermine fundamental rights, judicial oversight and media freedom.\n\nAmong these petitions, two journalists, Tharindu Uduwaragedara and Mohamed Fazeer, have filed another Special Determination petition seeking a declaration that several clauses of the proposed Bill are inconsistent with the provisions of the Constitution.\n\nFiling this petition through Gowry Shankari Law Associates, the petitioners contend that Clause 56 of the proposed Bill, which criminalises the disclosure of information likely to prejudice an investigation conducted or proposed to be conducted under the Act, could have serious implications for journalists reporting on matters of public interest.\n\nThey argue that although the provision contains certain protections for legal communications and a defence based on the absence of knowledge or reasonable grounds for suspicion, it does not expressly provide protection for public-interest reporting.\n\nAccordingly, they contend that the broad scope of the provision could create uncertainty for journalists, family members and others seeking to disclose information concerning an investigation, and that it is inconsistent with Articles 12(1) and 14(1)(a) of the Constitution.\n\nThe petitioners have also challenged several other provisions of the Bill, arguing that they confer excessive powers on law enforcement authorities and executive officials while restricting judicial supervision over investigations and detention."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-8d6b556f",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-8d6b556f",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Proposed Organised Crimes Bill faces mounting challenges before Supreme Court",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Proposed-Organised-Crimes-Bill-faces-mounting-challenges-before-Supreme-Court/108-353930",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793597055.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-8d6b556f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b9a3b22ae1881100bd8738b680f41309",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "The petitioners have also challenged several other provisions of the Bill, arguing that they confer excessive powers on law enforcement authorities and executive officials while restricting judicial supervision over investigations and detention.\n\nBill allows organised crime links to be inferred from dress, tattoos and associations\n\nThey further challenge Clause 4(2), which permits membership of an organised criminal group to be inferred from factors such as association with known members, dress, hand signs, language, tattoos, visits to certain premises and repeated arrests while in the company of such members.\n\nThe petitioners argue that these factors do not, by themselves, establish membership of an organised criminal group and could lead to arbitrary treatment.\n\nThey have also questioned Clause 17, which empowers police officers, on reasonable suspicion of a serious crime, to stop and search, question and arrest individuals, search premises without a warrant and seize relevant items.\n\nWith regard to detention, the petitioners challenge Clause 20, which permits a suspect to be held for up to 72 hours before being produced before a Magistrate, excluding journey time, with a further 24 hours of police custody if authorised.\n\nThey argue that the provision unduly restricts the Magistrate's judicial discretion by limiting the available options and preventing an independent determination of whether a suspect should instead be released on bail or discharged.\n\nThe petitioners have also challenged Clause 21, which empowers the Secretary to the Ministry responsible for Police to order the detention of a suspect for periods of up to two months at a time, for a maximum period of one year, to facilitate investigations, obtain evidence or question the suspect.\n\nThey contend that the provision allows an executive official, rather than a court, to determine whether a person should remain in detention for investigative purposes."
  },
