    "chunk_size_bytes": 1098,
    "chunk_text": "In contrast, the services account surplus fell 34.3 percent to US$ 119 million, as outflows rose 36.7 percent to US$ 441 million against an 11.2 percent increase in inflows to US$ 559 million. Overseas travel expenditure jumped 64.9 percent to US$ 122 million, equivalent to about two-thirds of what the country earned from tourism during the month. The cumulative services surplus declined 22.3 percent to US$ 2.21 billion.\n\nForeign investment in government securities recorded a net outflow of US$ 39.9 million in September, compared with a net inflow of US$ 37.8 million a year earlier, while the Colombo Stock Exchange saw a net foreign outflow of US$ 4.7 million.\n\nGross official reserves, including the swap facility with the People\u2019s Bank of China, remained at US$ 6.9 billion at end-September, equivalent to 3.3 months of imports. Meanwhile, the rupee had depreciated 6.8 percent against the US dollar on a year-to-date basis by end-October, with the dollar trading at Rs.332.61 compared to Rs.309.99 at end-2025, deepening from 5.5 percent at end-August and 6.3 percent at end-September."
