    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "96a4435516fa91e8b6a44b96da5f5c92",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1774,
    "chunk_text": "The State must keep time, too\n\nThe scaling back of Port City tax concessions after investors had committed did more damage than any single failed project, because it showed that even our flagship stability zone could alter its own terms\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s stability will not become growth, nor its recovery investment, until the clock that governs citizens also governs the State\n\nEvery Sri Lankan knows the feeling. A tax return is due by a fixed date, and a day\u2019s delay brings a penalty. An appeal must be lodged within a set number of days, or the right to it is lost. A licence lapses at midnight; a renewal fee is surcharged the morning after. The citizen and the business live by the calendar, and the law enforces it without sentiment.\n\nNow consider the other side of the counter. An application for an approval is submitted, and then nothing. There is no date by which the State must answer, no consequence if it does not, and frequently no one who can say where the file is. The citizen is on the clock; the State is not. That asymmetry is not a minor administrative irritation. It is, I would argue, one of the principal reasons Sri Lanka\u2019s hard-won economic recovery has not yet turned into the investment the country needs.\n\nLet us give credit where it is due. Foreign direct investment (FDI) reached approximately $1.06 billion in 2025, the first time inflows have crossed the billion-dollar mark, and a rise of roughly 72% on 2024. Revenue collection has been restored. The Public Debt Management Act, No. 33 of 2024, has placed debt reporting on a transparent statutory footing. Our position on the corruption perceptions ranking improved from 121st to 107th in a single year. These are real achievements, and they belong to the country, not to any one party."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-5e489cf3",
    "num": "dailyftlk-5e489cf3",
    "date_str": "2026-11-04",
    "description": "The State must keep time, too",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-State-must-keep-time-too/4-799606",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793750642.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-04-dailyftlk-5e489cf3-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ea605d32a0506cf812ef893fe23ac7db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1591,
    "chunk_text": "Let us give credit where it is due. Foreign direct investment (FDI) reached approximately $1.06 billion in 2025, the first time inflows have crossed the billion-dollar mark, and a rise of roughly 72% on 2024. Revenue collection has been restored. The Public Debt Management Act, No. 33 of 2024, has placed debt reporting on a transparent statutory footing. Our position on the corruption perceptions ranking improved from 121st to 107th in a single year. These are real achievements, and they belong to the country, not to any one party.\n\nThe clock runs against the recipient, never against the State\n\nConsider what the mid-sized investor, the person who actually makes up most of our transactions, encounters on arrival. To open an Inward Investment Account (IIA), through which capital must legally enter the country, original physical documents must be couriered or hand-delivered from abroad. There is no uniform procedure across licensed banks, with each institution applying its own internal processes. More importantly, the Central Bank has not published prescribed guidelines setting out the mandatory disclosures required from investors or specifying maximum processing timelines. Consequently, while the investor is bound by strict compliance requirements, neither the banks nor the regulator is subject to a corresponding standard of procedural certainty or timeliness. Unless the Central Bank addresses these gaps by introducing uniform procedures, clear disclosure requirements and prescribed processing timelines, the framework remains deficient in both prudence and substance."
  },
