    "chunk_size_bytes": 1396,
    "chunk_text": "Consider what the mid-sized investor, the person who actually makes up most of our transactions, encounters on arrival. To open an Inward Investment Account (IIA), through which capital must legally enter the country, original physical documents must be couriered or hand-delivered from abroad. There is no uniform procedure across licensed banks, with each institution applying its own internal processes. More importantly, the Central Bank has not published prescribed guidelines setting out the mandatory disclosures required from investors or specifying maximum processing timelines. Consequently, while the investor is bound by strict compliance requirements, neither the banks nor the regulator is subject to a corresponding standard of procedural certainty or timeliness. Unless the Central Bank addresses these gaps by introducing uniform procedures, clear disclosure requirements and prescribed processing timelines, the framework remains deficient in both prudence and substance.\n\nThe same pattern runs through the courts. A litigant who misses a prescribed period may lose a case before it is heard. Meanwhile 1.13 million cases are pending across 293 courts. The rules of procedure are strict with the party and silent on the delay. Contract enforcement is now slow enough that every investor prices it into every deal, and we pay that premium, whether or not we ever see the dispute."
  },
  {
