    "md5": "faffc711101e97e64c4aa5ed243aef41",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Beyond promises to real progress\n\nWhat Sri Lanka must do to turn economic recovery into better living standards and lasting prosperity\n\nWhy better incomes, productive investment, effective public administration and responsible spending must become the measures of economic progress\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget Speech is scheduled for 12 November 2026. After years of economic hardship, public expectations are understandably high. Families want relief from living costs, better employment, reliable public services and greater confidence in the future.\n\nDiscussion has focused particularly on public sector salary increases. Fair remuneration matters, but a more fundamental question must guide the Budget debate: Will the government\u2019s decisions produce lasting improvements in people\u2019s lives?\n\nSince the 2022 crisis, Sri Lanka has restored a measure of stability. Growth has resumed, foreign exchange reserves have improved and public financial management has strengthened. According to the World Bank, the economy grew by 4.7% during the first half of 2026. Yet recovery in national statistics does not automatically translate into recovery for households.\n\nThe 2027 Budget should therefore be judged less by the size of its promises than by its capacity to increase real incomes, expand productive employment, attract investment and turn public resources into measurable results.\n\nPublic servants have faced considerable financial pressure, and higher salaries can provide welcome relief. But the increase expected in 2027 largely completes the three-stage salary revision announced in the 2025 Budget, rather than constituting an entirely new initiative.\n\nIt is also important to distinguish between nominal wages\u2014the money an employee receives\u2014and real wages\u2014what that money can purchase. If a worker earning Rs. 60,000 receives an additional Rs. 5,000, the nominal increase is about 8.3%. But if prices have risen by roughly 8% over the same period, the gain in purchasing power is small."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-10-dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "date_str": "2026-11-10",
    "description": "Budget 2027: Beyond promises to real progress",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Budget-2027-Beyond-promises-to-real-progress/4-799867",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794251542.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-10-dailyftlk-feaf92d5-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "76b3463d6628890d3b4620f95ffc222c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "It is also important to distinguish between nominal wages\u2014the money an employee receives\u2014and real wages\u2014what that money can purchase. If a worker earning Rs. 60,000 receives an additional Rs. 5,000, the nominal increase is about 8.3%. But if prices have risen by roughly 8% over the same period, the gain in purchasing power is small.\n\nRegular salary reviews are justified, but they must be financially sustainable and accompanied by efforts to improve productivity, contain living costs and raise earnings across the economy. The objective should be better living standards, not merely higher figures on payrolls.\n\nDespite the improvement in headline indicators, many Sri Lankans remain under financial strain. The Colombo Consumer Price Index recorded annual inflation of 8.3% in October 2026. The World Bank estimated poverty at 16.9% in 2025. Although overall unemployment stood at 4% in the second quarter of 2026, unemployment among people aged 15\u201324 was 17.8%, while labour force participation was only 50%.\n\nThe Health Ministry\u2019s 2025 Nutrition Month data also reported that 16.1% of children under five were underweight. These indicators remind us that economic hardship cannot be measured by GDP growth alone. Poverty, weak labour market participation and poor nutrition are not isolated social concerns: they reduce human potential and ultimately weaken the economy\u2019s productive capacity. Policies should therefore be evaluated for their effects on employment, opportunity and the distribution of gains, not growth alone.\n\nAnother distinction is often overlooked: lower inflation does not necessarily mean lower prices. If a food item rises from Rs. 100 to Rs. 200, a subsequent slowdown in inflation may simply mean that it becomes more expensive at a slower rate. Families still have to pay the higher price."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-10-dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-feaf92d5",
    "date_str": "2026-11-10",
    "description": "Budget 2027: Beyond promises to real progress",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Budget-2027-Beyond-promises-to-real-progress/4-799867",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794251542.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-10-dailyftlk-feaf92d5-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0be5ba85146b542f1d03575197d1848f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "Another distinction is often overlooked: lower inflation does not necessarily mean lower prices. If a food item rises from Rs. 100 to Rs. 200, a subsequent slowdown in inflation may simply mean that it becomes more expensive at a slower rate. Families still have to pay the higher price.\n\nSri Lanka cannot sustainably improve living standards through salary increases, subsidies and tax concessions alone. Those measures depend ultimately on the country\u2019s capacity to produce more goods and services, generate income and collect revenue without creating renewed fiscal instability.\n\nAgriculture, manufacturing, tourism, technology and export-oriented services all offer potential. Small and medium-sized enterprises are especially important because they create jobs and economic activity throughout the country. Yet, many struggle with expensive finance, outdated equipment, limited market access and cumbersome administrative procedures.\n\nFarmers need reliable irrigation, storage, transport and opportunities to add value to what they grow. Entrepreneurs need accessible finance, skilled workers and predictable rules. Exporters need productive infrastructure and efficient trade procedures.\n\nA stronger productive economy also depends on improving the quality of employment. Young Sri Lankans need pathways from education and training into rewarding work. This requires practical cooperation among universities, technical institutes, employers and regional businesses. Supporting enterprise outside Colombo would help distribute opportunities more fairly and reduce the pressure on young people to seek their future abroad.\n\nForeign exchange earnings deserve particular emphasis. Borrowing dollars is not the same as earning dollars. Loans can temporarily meet financing needs, but they bring repayment obligations. Sustainable foreign exchange comes from competitive exports, tourism, services and other productive activities, supported by formal workers\u2019 remittances."
  },
