    "md5": "ddcc83df9c9c1424099b84e58a2953cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1924,
    "chunk_text": "Al-Thani, who is both prime minister and \u200bforeign minister of Qatar, met Trump after visiting Tehran last week.\n\nThe prime minister called his talks with both Trump and Vice President JD Vance positive.\n\n\"Grateful for the positive discussions on reaching a deal and bringing peace to the region,\" he said on X.\n\nIran's proposal to end the war involves \u200ban end to the US blockade of its ports, the lifting of some sanctions and a halt to bombing in return for Tehran stopping its own blockade of the Strait of Hormuz, with \u200btalks on its nuclear programme and other sanctions to follow.\n\nThe US has repeatedly rejected that plan, with Secretary of State Marco Rubio describing Tehran's conditions on Sunday as \"outrageous and ridiculous and really not realistic.\"\n\nIran's Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmaeil \u200cBaghaei defended \u2060the plan on Monday as the most reasonable offer on the table.\n\nSenior US Republicans including Vice President JD Vance have blamed their party's losses in last week's elections on the Iran war.Despite the growing domestic political pressure to end the conflict, Rubio said he did not believe a diplomatic breakthrough was imminent.\n\nA senior United Arab Emirates official, Anwar Gargash, echoed that view on Monday, saying a comprehensive political settlement between the US and Iran appeared difficult.\n\n\"We should... prepare for a long period of uncertainty in which neither a decisive resolution nor stable regional order can be taken for granted,\" he said.\n\nVance, who led the negotiations for a \u200bshort-lived truce in June, will also meet Al-Thani, \u200bwhose country has been a prime mediator since \u2060early in the war, conveying messages between the sides and proposing ways to bridge their differences.\n\nAnxiety over the war has pervaded the region for months, and Turkish President Tayyip Erdogan said on Monday he had told Trump that Ankara did not want to see a new escalation."
