    "chunk_text": "Saudi Arabia's foreign minister and his Omani counterpart stressed in a phone call on Monday the need to press ahead with diplomatic efforts to contain the regional crisis and safeguard \u200bmaritime security and sea lanes, Saudi state media reported.\n\nOn Monday, Trump will meet the prime minister of Qatar, which has been a mediator, though senior US and Gulf officials cast doubt on the chances of an imminent deal.\n\n\"Crude futures are under selling pressure \u200bonce again as oil volumes continue strong through the Strait of Hormuz,\" said Dennis Kissler, senior vice president of trading at BOK Financial.\n\nIn the Medina region, a fire was extinguished after Saudi \u200bArabia's East-West pipeline was struck, an energy ministry source said, according to state media. There were no casualties and the pipeline's operations were not affected.\n\nThe Saudi-led coalition fighting Yemen's Iran-aligned Houthis said on Monday it destroyed \u200ca factory \u2060used by the group to make ballistic missile components and a chemical plant used for producing ballistic missile fuel, without specifying the locations of the targets.\n\nThe Houthis claimed responsibility for an attack on Sunday at Saudi oil giant Aramco's corporate office in the heart of the financial district of the capital Riyadh. Debris from the attempted attack blew out windows across several floors, the Saudi energy ministry said.\n\n\"Fresh tanker incidents and an attack on Saudi Aramco\u2019s offices show that shipping and regional security risks remain unresolved as traders price in a less disruptive outcome,\" said Gelber & Associates.\n\nElsewhere, Russia \u200band Ukraine are coming to US-brokered talks to end \u200bMoscow's war on Ukraine with good intentions, \u2060a US official said on Monday.\n\nIn a briefing ahead of Tuesday's talks in Abu Dhabi, a US official told reporters that both sides have shown a little bit of flexibility privately on territorial issues."
