    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "The Met has said Brand was under \u200binvestigation for two other alleged rapes, one involving a then-16-year-old girl, and the force's chief Mark Rowley said on Friday that he \"wouldn't be surprised if many more came \u200bforward\".\n\nHe acknowledged in evidence he had dedicated a lot of his life to what he described as \"reckless philandering and idiocy\", though he said sexual consent was fundamental.\n\nBut, after a four-week trial, Brand was found guilty of one count of rape and three sexual assaults. He was acquitted of two other rape charges and an attempted indecent assault.\n\nOne of Brand's victims, who accused Brand of sexually assaulting her in 2004 and then orally raping her, said the attack had left her \u200btraumatised. Brand was convicted of sexually assaulting \u200bher but acquitted of rape.\n\n\"That undeserved \u2060feeling of shame and 'what if I had done this or done that' has been carried round inside me for over 20 years,\" she said from behind a screen.\n\nAnother of Brand's victims, whom Brand anally raped in 2009, said in a statement read \u200bby prosecutors that the trauma \"often feels never-ending\" and that she did not report the attack at the time because of \u200bBrand's status.\n\n\"I felt that \u2060if I reported him, I would not be believed,\" she said. \"His fame, wealth and influence created a power imbalance which made me feel powerless.\"\n\nProsecutor Riel Karmy-Jones told the court that \"Brand abused his influence and the power that came with his fame\", and his trial has renewed scrutiny of how Britain's entertainment industry treats stars accused of abusing their position.\n\nBritain's \u2060culture minister \u200bLisa Nandy said on Sunday that there was still a problem with sexual abuse in the \u200bmedia industry.\n\nPolice and prosecutors, however, insisted that fame or the passage of time would not prevent criminal charges."
