    "chunk_text": "\u2019Cruise for life\u2019 ship seized in Singapore\n\nBBC - A round-the-world cruise that left passengers stranded in Cambodia after its US-based operator ran out of money has been seized in Singapore.\n\nThe Odyssey, which set off from Belfast in October 2024 and was meant to take guests on a years-long voyage around the world, is now anchored in Singapore port waters, local authorities say.\n\nAround 200 guests were forced to leave the ship last month in the Cambodian port city of Sihanoukville, after its owner Villa Vie Residences abruptly suspended operations citing \"extraordinary increases in fuel costs\".\n\nThe crew onboard are reported to be well, Singapore's port authority said.\n\n\"Arrangements for their welfare and wellbeing are being managed by the vessel's creditors,\" the authority said, adding that the vessel had been \"placed under arrest pursuant to a court order\".\n\nSingapore law firm Helmsman LLC has been named the \"arresting solicitor\", but it is not clear which party it represents.\n\nThe Odyssey was meant to go on a three-and-a-half-years voyage covering 150 countries.\n\nPassengers were given the options of buying their cabin outright, which allows them to stay onboard beyond its initial voyage, paying for a five-year residency, or renting a cabin for days-long tours.\n\nPermanent residencies in its cabins ranged from $120,000 (\u00a389,272) to $440,000. Those cabins were also available for rent from $3,500 a month, according to its website.\n\n\"For roughly the price of a car, you can own your home on a ship that travels the world,\" its founder Mikael Petterson had earlier said.\n\nHowever the cruise was beset with problems from the start, with its departure delayed for four months because of repairs and paperwork.\n\nWhile in Vietnam, crew members reportedly bought food from local grocery stores because the company owed money to a vendor who refused to deliver food, according to CBS News, the BBC's partner in the US."
