    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. Backtracks on Several Provisions of Organised Crimes Bill\n\nColombo, Nov. 10 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Government is planning to introduce several amendments to the controversial Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill at the parliamentary committee stage, including the removal of a provision allowing organised crime links to be inferred from tattoos, dress and personal associations, and the removal of restrictions on media reporting of serious crimes, the Attorney General informed the Supreme Court yesterday.\n\nSolicitor General Viraj Dayaratne, appearing for the Attorney General, made this submission when the Supreme Court commenced hearing Special Determination petitions challenging the constitutionality of the proposed legislation. The Solicitor General said the amendments would be incorporated into the Bill at the parliamentary committee stage.\n\nDaily Mirror in three separate publications highlighted, drawing attention to provisions of the Bill that could potentially undermine individual liberty and fundamental freedoms.\n\nThe matter was taken up before a three-judge bench comprising Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena and Justices Achala Wengappuli and Sampath Abayakoon.\n\nAmong the proposed changes is the removal of the controversial provision that permitted organised crime connections to be inferred from a person's tattoos, manner of dress and associations.\n\nThe Government also intends to remove the restriction on media reporting relating to serious crimes (Clause 14(3)), which had raised concerns over its potential impact on press freedom and the public's right to information. Under this provision, publishing such material without prior permission was categorised as an offence punishable by up to two years\u2019 imprisonment."
  },
  {
