    "language": "en",
    "md5": "552dab7a37aed90d4756e5519e471b9f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1700,
    "chunk_text": "The Government also intends to remove the restriction on media reporting relating to serious crimes (Clause 14(3)), which had raised concerns over its potential impact on press freedom and the public's right to information. Under this provision, publishing such material without prior permission was categorised as an offence punishable by up to two years\u2019 imprisonment.\n\nThe Solicitor General further informed Court that the definition of a 'serious crime' would be narrowed to cover offences listed in the First Schedule, punishable by more than four years' imprisonment, when committed by a member of an organised criminal group.\n\nHe emphasised that the provisions of the proposed legislation would apply only to structured groups, rather than individuals acting independently.\n\nThe Government also proposed amendments to Clause 20(1) of the Bill, which empowers police to detain a suspect for 72 hours, reducing the period to 48 hours.\n\nThe Government has also decided to remove excise offences from the Schedule to the proposed Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Sanjeeva Jayawardena, appearing for five entities representing the excise industry, drew the Court\u2019s attention to the constitutional concerns arising from the inclusion of excise offences in the proposed legislation.\n\nThe Government is also intending to remove copyright offences from the Schedule to the bill which are available in the Intellectual Property Act. President\u2019s Counsel Romesh De Silva, appearing for a petitioner, stressed the importance of removing offences under the Intellectual Property Act, including trademark offences, arguing that such offences do not constitute serious crimes."
