    "chunk_size_bytes": 1446,
    "chunk_text": "The Government is also intending to remove copyright offences from the Schedule to the bill which are available in the Intellectual Property Act. President\u2019s Counsel Romesh De Silva, appearing for a petitioner, stressed the importance of removing offences under the Intellectual Property Act, including trademark offences, arguing that such offences do not constitute serious crimes.\n\nHe said the need for the legislation had been identified in view of difficulties faced by law enforcement agencies and the necessity of establishing a mechanism to bring offenders back to Sri Lanka in keeping with modern requirements. He added that new laws incorporating novel approaches were necessary to deal with increasingly sophisticated criminal activities.\n\nMeanwhile, The Supreme Court yesterday concluded the arguments put forward in support and against the proposed bill. The parties were directed to file their written submissions. Later on, the Supreme Court\u2019s determination over the proposed Bill is to be communicated to the Speaker.\n\nRomesh De Silva PC, Sanjeeva Jayawardena PC, M.A. Sumanthiran PC, Saliya Pieris PC, Ali Sabry PC, Eraj De Silva PC, Navin Marapana PC, Senior Counsel Suren Fernado, Hejaaz Hizbullah, Pulasthi Hewamanne, Lakshan Dias and several others appeared for the petitioners. Solicitor General Viraj Dayaratne with Sudarshana de Silva ASG, Suharshi Herath SDSG and Awanthi Perera DSG appeared for the Attorney General."
