    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "HIPG and Al-Futtaim Logistics explore closer cooperation to support Gulf vehicle supply chains\n\nCEO of HIPG Wilson Qu presenting Vice Chairman of Al Fuftaim Group\u00a0 Omar Abdulla Hamad Al Fuitaim with memento to mark the visit\n\nHambantota International Port Group (HIPG) and Al-Futtaim Logistics have discussed opportunities to expand their existing business relationship, against a backdrop of Middle East shipping disruption that has highlighted Hambantota\u2019s role as an alternative transhipment gateway for vehicles destined for Gulf markets.\n\nThe discussions took place during a courtesy visit to Hambantota International Port (HIP) by a delegation led by the Vice Chairman of Al-Futtaim Group.\n\nTalks focused on expanding business ties, including the role of the CASCADIA RoRo Feeder Service in strengthening Hambantota\u2019s links with Gulf markets. Discussions extended beyond the scheduled agenda when the delegation expressed interest in investment opportunities at HIP. Port officials welcomed the interest and outlined opportunities across the port\u2019s operations and industrial park. The visit comes in the wake of Al-Futtaim\u2019s recently published white paper, \u201cResilience in Motion\u201d, which identifies Hambantota as one of three alternative gateways used to sustain regional automotive supply chains following disruption around the Strait of Hormuz in March 2026. The other gateways were Port Sultan Qaboos in Muscat and Port Khor Fakkan in the Gulf of Oman.\n\nAccording to Al-Futtaim, the three routes collectively moved more than 25,000 finished vehicles into the UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait and Saudi Arabia, helping maintain dealer supplies and supporting automotive manufacturers in returning to full production capacity. The figure represents the combined contribution of the three routes, rather than volumes handled through Hambantota alone."
