    "chunk_size_bytes": 1723,
    "chunk_text": "According to Al-Futtaim, the three routes collectively moved more than 25,000 finished vehicles into the UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait and Saudi Arabia, helping maintain dealer supplies and supporting automotive manufacturers in returning to full production capacity. The figure represents the combined contribution of the three routes, rather than volumes handled through Hambantota alone.\n\nThe CASCADIA RoRo Feeder Service, launched on 9 June 2026, forms part of the connectivity discussed during the visit, linking Hambantota with onward vehicle supply chains serving the region.\n\nThe service is operated by CASCADIA, a Canada-headquartered maritime logistics specialist with regional operations in key trade hubs, including Dubai. Its operations support the movement of vehicles across international markets.\n\nThe wider importance of such arrangements is examined in Al-Futtaim\u2019s white paper, which emphasises advance contingency planning, shipment visibility and coordination across different customs and regulatory systems. It describes how alternative corridors were brought into operation within weeks and how local capability helped sustain the response.\n\nThese lessons give the discussions between HIPG and Al-Futtaim Logistics a broader commercial context, as logistics providers assess how to maintain reliable supply chains through periods of disruption and develop networks capable of responding to future challenges.\n\nAl-Futtaim Logistics, part of the Dubai-headquartered Al-Futtaim Group, provides integrated logistics solutions across automotive finished vehicles, aerospace, retail, and food and beverage sectors. Its global reach extends to more than 150 countries through its strategic network partners."
