    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b5b4a95a8decf84b9797c9efcb3a1df5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s poorest fifth lose 16% of real household income\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s poorest fifth of households lost 16.4 percent of their average monthly income in real terms between 2016 and early 2025, while the richest fifth gained 16.5 percent, widening the income gap, as poverty returned to its level of nine years earlier.\n\nThe richest fifth earned nearly 15 times as much as the poorest fifth in early 2025, up from about 11 times in 2016, according to an analysis by Institute of Policy Studies of Sri Lanka Research Fellow Dr. Pulasthi Amarasinghe.\n\nDr. Amarasinghe drew on the Census and Statistics Department\u2019s final household survey results for 2016 and 2019 and the preliminary findings for 2025.\n\nAt the 2016 prices, the average monthly income for the poorest fifth fell to Rs.12,403, from Rs.14,843, while that of the richest fifth rose to Rs.184,129, from Rs.158,072.\n\nThe divide widened most sharply after 2019, a period that included the COVID-19 pandemic and Sri Lanka\u2019s economic crisis. The real income fell 19.6 percent for the poorest fifth over that period and rose 6.8 percent for the richest.\n\nThe same survey showed that an estimated 16 percent of Sri Lankans lived below the national poverty line in early 2025, up from 14.3 percent in 2019 and back at the 2016 level.\n\nDr. Amarasinghe said the gap between the household surveys likely concealed a higher poverty rate during the crisis. He cited the revised World Bank estimates, based on a separate poverty line of US $ 4.20 a day, that suggest poverty may have peaked at around 20 percent between the two surveys.\n\nThe national income figures also showed how differently the typical household fared. The average monthly household income rose 93 percent in nominal terms between 2016 and early 2025, from Rs.62,237 to Rs.120,266. Once inflation was taken into account, the increase was just 6 percent. Contd on page 11"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-10-dailymirrorlk-039354a4",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-039354a4",
    "date_str": "2026-11-10",
    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s poorest fifth lose 16% of real household income",
