    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6d4016cf162c0531d73aed47a13a12cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 966,
    "chunk_text": "The national income figures also showed how differently the typical household fared. The average monthly household income rose 93 percent in nominal terms between 2016 and early 2025, from Rs.62,237 to Rs.120,266. Once inflation was taken into account, the increase was just 6 percent. Contd on page 11\n\nThe average household income was nearly 59 percent higher than the median in early 2025, compared with 43 percent in 2016.\n\nDr. Amarasinghe said the widening gap and the growing distance between the richest and poorest fifths pointed to a more uneven distribution of income.\n\nThe full 2025 survey results will help establish whether the Gini coefficient, a broader measure of inequality, also rose.\n\nDr. Amarasinghe argued that Sri Lanka\u2019s return to economic growth should be judged by whether it raises the households\u2019 purchasing power, creates more productive jobs and leaves the economically vulnerable families better able to withstand the future shocks."
