    "chunk_text": "Shangri-La\u2019s Sri Lankan hotels more than triple profit as occupancy defies arrival dip\n\nColombo and Hambantota hotels\u2019 1H profit rises to US $ 6.8mn, from US $ 1.9mn\n\nShangri-La\u2019s two Sri Lankan hotels more than tripled their after-tax profit to US $ 6.8 million in the first half of 2026, as the occupancy at Shangri-La Colombo and Shangri-La Hambantota climbed sharply, even while the island\u2019s tourist arrivals dipped, the interim results filed by Hong Kong-listed parent Shangri-La Asia Limited showed.\n\nThe profit, which reflects the parent\u2019s 90 percent stake in both properties, compares with US $ 1.9 million a year earlier, while the hotel revenue rose 7.7 percent to US $ 21.0 million, from US $ 19.5 million.\n\nThe after-tax margin widened to 32.4 percent, from 9.7 percent and the half-year profit already equals 85 percent of US $ 8.0 million the two hotels earned across 2025.\n\nThe weighted average occupancy rose to 52 percent, from 44 percent, an eight-percentage-point gain that was the largest across the group\u2019s destinations, although the average room rate eased 4.8 percent to US $ 140, from US $ 147.\n\nThe revenue per available room (RevPAR) rose 12.3 percent to US $ 73, from US $ 65, edging past mainland China\u2019s US $ 72 and lifting Sri Lanka off the bottom of the group\u2019s RevPAR rankings.\n\nThe improvement contrasts with the national arrivals, which slipped 1.8 percent to 1,146,573, from 1,168,044 when compared to the same period last year, as per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) data.\n\nThe growth of 9.7 percent in January and 16.2 percent in February gave way to declines of 19.8 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April, a slump the SLTDA linked to the conflict in the Middle East."
  },
