    "chunk_size_bytes": 1217,
    "chunk_text": "The growth of 9.7 percent in January and 16.2 percent in February gave way to declines of 19.8 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April, a slump the SLTDA linked to the conflict in the Middle East.\n\nThe SLTDA figures show the Indian visitors stayed around five nights on average, roughly half the stay of most European markets.\n\nAlthough Sri Lanka generated just 2.1 percent of the group\u2019s US $ 993.3 million in hotel property revenue, it contributed 14.8 percent of the segment\u2019s US $ 45.8 million profit. Its US $ 4.9 million improvement was second only to Hong Kong\u2019s US $ 5.7 million. Meanwhile, the revenue from the group\u2019s Sri Lankan investment properties at One Galle Face grew 10.1 percent to US $ 15.2 million, from US $ 13.8 million on higher rents and occupancy, though profit edged up only 2.8 percent to US $ 7.3 million, from US $ 7.1 million.\n\nThe residential sales slowed, with the group pinning a drop in property sales revenue to US $ 3.1 million, from US $ 5.6 million, mainly on fewer unit sales in Sri Lanka.\n\nLooking ahead, the group described itself as cautiously optimistic for the second half, while flagging the continuing Middle East conflicts as a source of near-term volatility."
