    "md5": "3643d2743683734d1060ec2659cad47f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "Formation of Israeli government held up by thorny political issues\n\nSami Abu Shehadeh, leader of Balad Party of the Arab List, said that Palestinian citizens of Israel are being treated as illegitimate partners despite their turnout\n\nLast Saturday,\u00a0 Israeli President Isaac Herzog said that he would ask the Yashar party chairman, Gadi Eisenkot, to explore the possibility of forming the post-election\u00a0 government. Sixty seven of the 120 Members of the Knesset (MKs) had recommended Eisenkot for the position of Prime Minister, and only 46 backed Benjamin Netanyahu\u2019s coalition. Seven had abstained.\n\nBut all is not well with the two coalitions. Parties are divided on many issues, but the ones which are difficult to solve are two: One, exempting the orthodox Jews (called Haredi or Haredim) from compulsory military service and secondly, striking an alliance with the Arab parties. Traditionally, alliance with any Arab party is considered politically suicidal by the Jewish parties.\n\nThe Haredim and their parties are adamantly demanding exemption from compulsory military service while all other Jewish parties consider it necessary, as Israel is constantly fighting with its Arab neighbours and now Iran also.\n\nThe Arabs, a minority in Israel, have become an important political factor now because the Arab parties have won 19 of the 120 seats in the Knesset. But Jewish parties do not want to be politically tarnished by accepting an alliance with the Arabs, their traditional enemies and rivals.\n\nOn October 20, a week before Israel\u2019s elections, dozens of Orthodox Jews were detained on suspicion of evading compulsory military service or the draft. Those young men were students of Jewish religious schools long exempted from mandatory military service."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-10-dailymirrorlk-006ec26b",
