    "md5": "5173a95880d187ef8c49446f37d4d406",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1792,
    "chunk_text": "On October 20, a week before Israel\u2019s elections, dozens of Orthodox Jews were detained on suspicion of evading compulsory military service or the draft. Those young men were students of Jewish religious schools long exempted from mandatory military service.\n\nWith Israel fighting on several conflicts, those exemptions have become increasingly unpopular. The majority of Jewish voters want Haredi communities to share the burden of fighting for the country. But Haredi political parties are key potential coalition members and never fail to have their way.\n\nHaredi communities tend to prioritise religious Jewishness over responsibilities to the State. Interestingly, most Haredim do not consider themselves Zionist, even though Zionism is the foundational philosophy of the State of Israel. They argue that it was sacrilegious for Jews to artificially create a State of Israel instead of waiting for God to return Jews to the land of Israel. Where nationalist politicians emphasise the secular State and military service, the Haredim see their religious study itself as a service to the Jewish community at large.\n\nHaredi Jews strictly observe Jewish religious law and disapprove of liberal interpretations. They typically live in Haredi-majority neighbourhoods, where large families are the norm \u2013 six children, on average. About half of the men devote themselves to full-time religious study. The poverty rate among them is 33% (more than double the percentage for non-Haredi Jews) according to a piece in the journal \u201cThe Conversation.\u201d\n\nHaredis are about 14%\u00a0 of the Israeli population. But the community is growing quickly because of its high birth rate. Projections by the Israeli Central Bureau of Statistics estimate that almost a quarter of Israelis will be Haredi by 2050."
  },
  {
