    "language": "en",
    "md5": "97b083e35d42230f0278844cb491687a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1756,
    "chunk_text": "Haredis are about 14%\u00a0 of the Israeli population. But the community is growing quickly because of its high birth rate. Projections by the Israeli Central Bureau of Statistics estimate that almost a quarter of Israelis will be Haredi by 2050.\n\nLikud, led by Benjamin Netanyahu, one of the major conservative Jewish parties, is seen by the Haredi parties as a natural ally. They have been coalition partners under Likud administrations. The fact that Likud has needed Haredi parties to form coalitions has given the latter a lot of negotiating power.\n\nHowever, most Jewish Israelis see Haredi exemption from conscription as unfair and costly.\u00a0 Governments have been subsidising the Haredis in many ways. Religious schools for teenage boys and even married Many Haredi families rely on government support.\n\nIn 2024, Israel\u2019s Supreme Court ruled that a blanket exemption from military service for the Haredis was not legal. Netanyahu\u2019s government struggled to enact a compromise, with the result Shas and UTJ left the Likud-led coalition in 2025.\n\nBut this did not lead to mass conscription. In 2026, fewer than 500 draft evaders have been arrested. In July 2026, at the urging of Haredi parties, the government passed a law to temporarily prevent the arrest of draft evaders.\n\nFormer Prime Minister Naftali Bennett \u2013 leader of the Together alliance (seen as a key contender to oust Netanyahu) has threatened to cut subsidies to the Haredi community if they continue to defy the draft. Gadi Eisenkot, leader of the centrist Yashar party (another top contender to replace Netanyahu) has proposed allowing only 3% of Haredi men exemption from the draft, and that too, only to enable them to study. Seven in 10 Jewish Israelis favour drafting the Haredim."
  },
