    "md5": "90c14b9ab21b2f3ec391c975cfb45dc0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1798,
    "chunk_text": "Let\u2019s learn from past disasters\n\nAs in all other issues, the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government is attempting to put the blame for\u00a0 last Friday\u2019s floods in Colombo city on the previous governments, especially certain mayors and ministers of\u00a0 former governments.\n\nConversely, all Opposition parties and their supporters seem to be overjoyed by the floods, and are ridiculing the current government as if this was the first major flashflood in the city.\n\nFrom our point of view, both parties are somewhat justifiable in their stance, while both share the blame. Despite Colombo having gone under water several times and several projects were announced to address the issue for the past two decades, all previous governments failed to find a lasting solution to the problem. They have to take the responsibility for it.\n\nFor instance, the Director General of Meteorology Ajith Wijemanna has pointed out that Colombo had experienced significantly higher rainfall during previous extreme weather events, including the years 1992, 2005, and 2017. He also stated that Colombo has experienced much heavier rainfall in the past, including 493mm in 1992 and 440mm in 2010.\n\nDuring the heaviest rains in 18 years in Colombo, the Parliament complex and its access roads were severely inundated in November 2010. The lower floor of the building was submerged under more than a metre of water. The army deployed amphibious armored vehicles\u2014commonly referred to locally as Unicorn(or Buffel)\u2014to ferry MPs to parliament while Speaker Chamal Rajapaksa had to use a boat to get there.\n\nThe Opposition seems to believe they should not forewarn the government\u2014even when they have prior information or scientific knowledge of disasters \u2014 and would rather let them happen to gain political mileage."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
