    "md5": "1a0a6fa067984ef6a15dde112571a2c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1533,
    "chunk_text": "The Opposition seems to believe they should not forewarn the government\u2014even when they have prior information or scientific knowledge of disasters \u2014 and would rather let them happen to gain political mileage.\n\nCyclone Ditwah that played havoc exactly a year ago should have been an eye opener for the government and\u00a0 officialdom. In fact, the government opened the eyes at least in Colombo District during the District Coordinating Committee (DCC) meeting held a month ago, on October 13, but\u00a0 officials closed their eyes tightly thereafter. The follow-up supervision on the part of the political authority resulted in the disaster. These factors deprived the government as well as the Opposition of any moral right to blame each other and gain political mileage from the difficulties faced by the people.\n\nDuring the DCC meeting, Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya sternly advised the bureaucrats to clean the city\u2019s drainage system, citing the approaching El Ni\u00f1o effects, while the latter attempted to make excuses, claiming there was a dearth of technical officers. However, after\u00a0 Friday\u2019s flash floods in the city, officials were seen busy mobilising employees to clean\u00a0 obstructed canals and waterways.\n\nWithin a day after\u00a0 Friday\u2019s flashfloods, they announced that they had identified 60 locations across five electoral divisions in Colombo where drains and waterways are obstructing the flow of floodwater and steps were taken to clean them. Nobody was heard grumbling over shortage of technical officers."
  },
