    "md5": "36bfeec07ce12ebe551ac817f781b738",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "Ravi K urges HSBC to reconsider closure of Sri Lanka operations\n\nColombo, Nov. 10 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Former Finance Minister and MP Ravi Karunanayake has appealed to HSBC\u2019s global leadership to reconsider its decision to close its Global Service Centre in Sri Lanka warning that the move could affect thousands of livelihoods and limit employment opportunities for young people.\n\nIn a letter addressed to the Group Chairman and Group Chief Executive of HSBC Holdings plc, Karunanayake called for an urgent dialogue to explore ways of retaining the bank\u2019s operations in Sri Lanka before the planned relocation becomes irreversible.\n\nKarunanayake recalled that he had initiated discussions to bring the investment to Sri Lanka while serving as Minister of Trade and Commerce between 2001 and 2004.\n\nHe said HSBC\u2019s decision to establish its operations in Colombo had created significant opportunities for Sri Lankan employees, helping thousands develop internationally recognised skills and build professional careers over the past two decades.\n\nThe operations initially began at the World Trade Centre in Colombo in 2004 before expanding to facilities at Union Place and Mireka Tower.\n\nAccording to Karunanayake, the centre had employed around 4,500 Sri Lankans at its peak. However, HSBC\u2019s notification dated October 7, 2026, identified a workforce of 1,659 employees as of September 30, 2026.\n\nHe noted that HSBC had stated that the proposed relocation followed a global operating-model review and was not a reflection of the performance of its Sri Lankan company.\n\nKarunanayake said this provided a reason to explore alternatives rather than proceed with the closure.\n\nHe also highlighted the potential impact on employees and their families, pointing out that the loss of employment could affect their children\u2019s education, housing commitments and responsibilities towards elderly parents."
  },
