    "md5": "17447fd4ccd504e2eac8f50e2c6f6367",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "ADB approves $300 million for Sri Lanka\u2019s water and sanitation sector\n\nColombo, Nov. 10 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Asian Development Bank (ADB) has approved two projects worth a total of US$300 million to improve water supply and sanitation services across Sri Lanka, with more than 1.3 million people expected to benefit from improved access to safe and reliable drinking water.\n\nThe funding includes a US$200 million Climate-Resilient Sustainable Water Supply and Sanitation Project and a US$100 million second subprogram of the Water Supply and Sanitation Reform Program.\n\nThe US$200 million project will improve access to safe water in the Western and Southern provinces while upgrading sewerage and sanitation services for nearly 250,000 people in Jaffna in the Northern Province.\n\nUnder the project, new climate-resilient water treatment plants, transmission and distribution networks, and storage reservoirs will be constructed in Colombo and Lunugamwehera.\n\nIt will also fund wastewater treatment facilities, sewerage networks and pumping stations in Jaffna.\n\nThe initiative will further focus on reducing water losses, increasing the use of renewable energy and strengthening the institutional capacity of the National Water Supply and Drainage Board (NWSDB), while encouraging greater private sector participation in the water sector.\n\nMeanwhile, the US$100 million second subprogram will build on reforms introduced under the first phase of the Water Supply and Sanitation Reform Program, strengthening governance, improving service delivery and attracting private sector financing for future investments.\n\nThe reforms will focus on improving water resource management, water safety, sanitation management and preparedness for natural hazards, while enhancing the operational and financial performance of institutions in the sector."
  },
