    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Up to 1.49 million Sri Lankans may need food assistance until May 2027\n\nColombo, Nov. 10 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Between 1 million and 1.49 million people in Sri Lanka may require humanitarian food assistance until May 2027, as rising food prices, increasing living costs and weather-related disruptions continue to put pressure on vulnerable households, according to the latest outlook by the Famine Early Warning Systems Network (FEWS NET).\n\nThe report projects widespread Stressed (IPC Phase 2) food security conditions between October 2026 and January 2027, as household food stocks from the 2026 Yala harvest gradually decline and families become increasingly dependent on market purchases.\n\nThe greatest pressure is expected between October and January, particularly among low-income plantation workers, poor coastal fishing communities and the urban poor. Food assistance needs are forecast to peak between October and December. However, no large-scale humanitarian food assistance is anticipated during the October 2026 to May 2027 projection period.\n\nSeasonal rainfall associated with the second inter-monsoon and northeast monsoon could bring floods, strong winds, landslides and rough seas, disrupting agricultural and fishing activities. Combined with high production, fuel and transportation costs, these conditions are expected to affect household incomes and reduce purchasing power.\n\nHowever, food security conditions are expected to improve from February 2027, particularly in the Dry Zone, as the 2026/27 Maha harvest replenishes household food stocks, increases market supplies and creates additional agricultural employment opportunities.\n\nThe Maha harvest, expected between January and March 2027, is forecast to be broadly favourable and comparable to the recent five-year average. Nevertheless, rice prices are expected to remain above their five-year averages despite improved market supplies."
  },
