    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "The Maha harvest, expected between January and March 2027, is forecast to be broadly favourable and comparable to the recent five-year average. Nevertheless, rice prices are expected to remain above their five-year averages despite improved market supplies.\n\nLow-income plantation workers, particularly those dependent on irregular agricultural wages, are among the groups expected to face continued difficulties in meeting both food and essential non-food expenses.\n\nPoor coastal fishing households are also likely to experience difficulties as rough seas and seasonal weather conditions reduce fishing days and catches, while high fuel and operating costs further affect their earnings.\n\nMeanwhile, despite continued economic recovery, renewed inflationary pressure and rising essential costs are affecting household purchasing power.\n\nHeadline inflation increased from 2.3% in January to 8.3% in October 2026, while the rupee depreciated by 6.2% against the US dollar between January and September, according to the report.\n\nFuel costs have also increased, with diesel and gas prices rising by approximately 41% between January and October 2026, partly due to higher global energy and shipping costs. These increases have added to the costs of food transportation, agricultural production, fishing and other essential goods and services.\n\nThe report also warns that below-average rainfall expected during the March-April 2027 first inter-monsoon could restrict water availability and affect agricultural and livestock activities. This could delay land preparation and planting for the next Yala season, particularly in rainfed areas and locations with limited irrigation.\n\nAlthough staple food availability is expected to remain broadly adequate until May 2027, continued pressure from food prices, fuel costs, agricultural inputs and other essential expenses is likely to limit vulnerable households' ability to meet their basic needs."
