    "md5": "8346d582ce712accf04f288742601ff1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1724,
    "chunk_text": "CEJ demands AG implement Supreme Court directives on X-Press Pearl disaster\n\nColombo, Nov. 10 (Daily Mirror) - The Centre for Environmental Justice (CEJ) has formally demanded that the Attorney General take immediate steps to implement the directions issued by the Supreme Court in its landmark judgment on the MV X-Press Pearl disaster, warning that it may seek further legal remedies over alleged delays in giving effect to the ruling.\n\nIn a letter of demand dated November 2026, addressed to Attorney General Parinda Ranasinghe (Jnr.), the CEJ has called for a written explanation of the measures taken to implement the Supreme Court's directives, including investigations into offences arising from the disaster and the consideration of criminal proceedings against those responsible.\n\nThe demand follows the Supreme Court judgment delivered on July 24, 2025, in four fundamental rights applications, including SC/FR/168/2021 filed by the CEJ and its Executive Director, Hemantha Ranjith Sisira Kumara.\n\nIn paragraphs 895 and 896 of the judgment, the Supreme Court held that the failure of the Attorney General to perform his statutory duty of indicting the owners and operators of the MV X-Press Pearl under Section 26(a) of the Marine Pollution Prevention Act, and the decision to institute civil proceedings against the ship-owning group in Singapore rather than before the Sri Lankan High Court exercising Admiralty jurisdiction, infringed the fundamental rights guaranteed under Article 12(1) of the Constitution.\n\nThe Court also issued a series of directions in paragraph 897, requiring further investigations into offences arising from the disaster and consideration of criminal proceedings against all offenders."
  },
