    "chunk_text": "Where did your 100rs go? Budget 2026 explained\n\nWhere did Sri Lanka\u2019s Budget 2026 money go? The biggest share went on interest. According to the Ministry of Finance\u2019s Citizens Budget 2026, Rs. 37 of every Rs. 100 the government set aside for spending went on interest on past loans.\n\nThat is Rs. 2,617 billion, or about Rs. 120,100 for every person in Sri Lanka. Measured against what the government expected to collect, interest took Rs. 49 of every Rs. 100.\n\nUse the interactive dashboard below to see where each rupee of Budget 2026 went: salaries, pensions, Aswesuma welfare, running costs, and capital projects.\n\nBudget 2026, presented in November 2025 and passed in December, set aside Rs. 7.10 trillion for government spending, not counting loan repayments. That number is too big to picture, so we shrank it to Rs. 100. We show it two ways: out of every Rs. 100 the government spent, and out of every Rs. 100 it collected. Each square is one rupee.\n\nData: Ministry of Finance, Citizens Budget 2026 \u00b7 Updated 10 November 2026\n\nHere, Rs. 100 stands for all the money Budget 2026 set aside for spending: Rs. 7.10 trillion, not counting loan repayments.\n\nThe government planned to spend more than it expected to collect. So \"Rs. 100 spent\" and \"Rs. 100 collected\" are different amounts of money, and the same bill takes a bigger bite out of the smaller one.\n\nHere, Rs. 100 stands for the taxes and other income Budget 2026 expected to collect: Rs. 5.37 trillion. Because spending was larger, the grid runs past 100 squares.\n\nInterest cost more than health, education and defence put together\n\nOut of every Rs. 100 Budget 2026 set aside, here is how interest compared with three services people tend to think of first.\n\nThese are the Ministry of Finance's sector totals for health, education and national security. They include salaries and new buildings in each sector, so they overlap with the categories in the grids above: they count spending by purpose, while the grids count it by type."
