    "md5": "9cc17d4c35aaa04a24a9be04affa1b2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "Budgeting for a better Sri Lanka\n\nThe Government serves as the primary governing institution responsible for providing public goods, enforcing law and order, ensuring economic stability, and advancing national development goals. To carry out these wide-ranging economic and social functions, the sovereign state must systematically raise revenue through taxation, duties, and non-tax channels, and strategically deploy these financial resources across public institutions. The execution of these fiscal responsibilities is governed through public finance frameworks, which dictate how public money is collected, prioritised, managed, and audited.\n\nAt the center of public finance lies the legislative instrument known as the Appropriation Bill. Under constitutional standards, such as Article 148 of Sri Lanka\u2019s Constitution, Parliament retains ultimate authority over all public funds, stipulating that no tax may be levied and no revenue withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund without explicit legislative approval.\n\nThe Appropriation Bill represents the formal legislative proposal that authorises the Government to withdraw funds from the Consolidated Fund to meet service expenditures, administrative overheads, and development projects for a specific financial year.\n\nThe national Budget is the central policy document through which a Government translates its economic strategy, policy promises, and development objectives into binding financial commitments. Beyond being a mere accounting ledger of projected receipts and outlays, the Budget functions as an instrument for macroeconomic stabilisation, resource reallocation, and wealth redistribution. It directly impacts inflation targets, employment figures, national debt levels, private sector investment incentives, and overall economic growth trajectories."
  },
