    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d6366610d1d63ebc9d161a3773911b9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1763,
    "chunk_text": "The national Budget is the central policy document through which a Government translates its economic strategy, policy promises, and development objectives into binding financial commitments. Beyond being a mere accounting ledger of projected receipts and outlays, the Budget functions as an instrument for macroeconomic stabilisation, resource reallocation, and wealth redistribution. It directly impacts inflation targets, employment figures, national debt levels, private sector investment incentives, and overall economic growth trajectories.\n\nA deficit Government Budget occurs when total state expenditure exceeds total Government revenues during a fiscal period, requiring the state to bridge the gap through domestic or foreign borrowing. Most modern developing nations, including Sri Lanka, operate under persistent Budget deficits to finance national development obligations and bridge infrastructure shortfalls.\n\nBudget deficits offer clear advantages when managed strategically. In accordance with Keynesian economic policy, targeted deficit spending stimulates aggregate demand by increasing national employment, boosting consumer purchasing power, and encouraging overall economic activity during downturns. Deficits are particularly beneficial for funding productive capital expenditure, allowing Governments to execute large-scale, high-multiplier capital projects\u2014such as ports, energy grids, and digital networks\u2014that generate long-term economic returns far exceeding their initial borrowing cost. Moreover, deficit financing supports crisis response and social protection, enabling states to maintain social safety nets, healthcare systems, and economic relief programs during crises without making sudden, damaging cuts to public services."
  },
