    "chunk_size_bytes": 1635,
    "chunk_text": "Budget deficits offer clear advantages when managed strategically. In accordance with Keynesian economic policy, targeted deficit spending stimulates aggregate demand by increasing national employment, boosting consumer purchasing power, and encouraging overall economic activity during downturns. Deficits are particularly beneficial for funding productive capital expenditure, allowing Governments to execute large-scale, high-multiplier capital projects\u2014such as ports, energy grids, and digital networks\u2014that generate long-term economic returns far exceeding their initial borrowing cost. Moreover, deficit financing supports crisis response and social protection, enabling states to maintain social safety nets, healthcare systems, and economic relief programs during crises without making sudden, damaging cuts to public services.\n\nAs Sri Lanka navigates its post-recovery macroeconomic stabilisation trajectory, fiscal policy remains the ultimate litmus test of state priorities. The presentation of the 2027 Appropriation Bill\u2014outlining an estimated state expenditure framework of Rs. 4,992.8 billion\u2014offers a critical empirical window into how the nation balances short-term institutional stabilisation with medium-term growth objectives. While the headline figure of nearly Rs. 5 trillion underscores an expanding state financial footprint, a granular analysis of spending allocations reveals a classic structural duality. On one side stands the ongoing effort to maintain core administrative and social obligations, while on the other lie strategic capital investments aimed at expanding national productive capacity."
