    "chunk_text": "The allocation of state funds across functional line ministries exhibits extreme concentration, following an aggressive Pareto distribution pattern. The top five portfolios absorb 65% of the entire Budget envelope, while the top nine portfolios control 90%, accounting for Rs. 4,492.0 billion of total expenditure. Consequently, the remaining 18 ministries and agencies are left to share the residual 10%, totaling Rs. 500.8 billion. This heavy fiscal concentration means that critical future-facing sectors\u2014such as the Digital Economy, Energy, Science and Technology, and Trade\u2014operate within a tight nominal fiscal envelope, necessitating exceptionally high capital efficiency and aggressive private-sector synergies to achieve meaningful economic impact.\n\nAt the same time, the state\u2019s capital expenditure strategy focuses sharply on high-multiplier productive assets to catalyse economic growth. The Ministry of Housing, Construction and Water Supply leads this shift, with 97% of its total allocation directed toward capital projects, representing Rs. 80 billion out of an Rs. 82 billion Budget. The Ministry of Energy follows closely with 96% allocated to capital investments, totaling Rs. 29 billion out of Rs. 30 billion. Transport, Highways and Urban Development accounts for the single largest physical development commitment at Rs. 432 billion, representing 86% of its Budget, complemented by Rs. 20 billion for the Digital Economy, which constitutes 77% of its overall allocation. By prioritising physical connectivity, energy grid reliability, and modern digital infrastructure over administrative consumption, the state aims to lay the operational foundation necessary for long-term macroeconomic expansion, with the top six ministries capturing approximately 74% of the total national Budget."
