    "chunk_text": "At the same time, the state\u2019s capital expenditure strategy focuses sharply on high-multiplier productive assets to catalyse economic growth. The Ministry of Housing, Construction and Water Supply leads this shift, with 97% of its total allocation directed toward capital projects, representing Rs. 80 billion out of an Rs. 82 billion Budget. The Ministry of Energy follows closely with 96% allocated to capital investments, totaling Rs. 29 billion out of Rs. 30 billion. Transport, Highways and Urban Development accounts for the single largest physical development commitment at Rs. 432 billion, representing 86% of its Budget, complemented by Rs. 20 billion for the Digital Economy, which constitutes 77% of its overall allocation. By prioritising physical connectivity, energy grid reliability, and modern digital infrastructure over administrative consumption, the state aims to lay the operational foundation necessary for long-term macroeconomic expansion, with the top six ministries capturing approximately 74% of the total national Budget.\n\nHuman capital development also faces structural headwinds within the current Budgetary architecture. While Education, Higher Education, and Vocational Education combined account for 7% of total spending, the capital component within this envelope remains constrained at just 24% capital versus 76% recurrent expenditure. Underinvesting in modernising primary and secondary classrooms, expanding advanced digital research laboratories, and upgrading vocational training infrastructure threatens to stunt innovation-led growth over the coming decade. Furthermore, the combined operational weight of Public Administration and Provincial Councils consumes over 26% of the entire national Budget\u2014swallowed almost entirely by recurrent operational costs\u2014reflecting an oversized public administration burden relative to Sri Lanka\u2019s current wealth generation capacity."
