    "chunk_text": "A similar divergence appears in public sector scale and operational efficiency. Developed economies place heavy emphasis on digital governance frameworks, automated e-citizen service portals, and lean bureaucratic structures to keep administrative recurrent spending low, thereby freeing up substantial fiscal space for targeted welfare programs and high-multiplier public investments. Sri Lanka continues to face high structural wage bills within its central public administration and provincial setups, directly reducing the net fiscal dividend available for broader economic transformation.\n\nTo navigate these structural challenges, decisive policy adjustments must be implemented across three key pillars. First, the state must systematically rationalise public administration by implementing deep structural reforms to curb ballooning recurrent wage and operational bills, explicitly redirecting generated savings toward high-multiplier capital projects. Second, policymakers must enhance capital efficiency by ensuring that major investments in transport, energy, and urban development undergo rigorous cost-benefit analyses, prioritising export-enabling infrastructure that yields measurable macroeconomic returns. Finally, the Government must scale up fiscal allocations for the digital economy, science, and technology to transition Sri Lanka from a factor-driven economy to a resilient, knowledge-driven economy that aligns more closely with developed nation paradigms."
