    "chunk_size_bytes": 1543,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo under water: Is drainage system ready for challenges of a changing climate?\n\nThe floods that inundated several parts of Colombo on November 06, 2026 have once again exposed the vulnerability of Sri Lanka\u2019s commercial capital to heavy rainfall. This incident highlights the urgent need for a comprehensive assessment of the capacity and adequacy of the existing urban drainage system, particularly its ability to withstand increasingly intense rainfall events under changing climatic conditions. The flooding of several key areas brought economic and social activities to a standstill, underscoring the need to strengthen Colombo\u2019s urban flood resilience through improved drainage infrastructure and effective stormwater management.\n\nAccording to reports, approximately 129 mm of rainfall were recorded within a few hours. Officials revealed that the existing drainage system had originally been designed to accommodate approximately 30 mm of rainfall per hour. Meanwhile, some argue that Colombo\u2019s drainage capacity has been significantly enhanced through several infrastructure development projects implemented in recent years and attribute the recent flooding primarily to blockages in the drainage network.\n\nHowever, attributing urban flooding entirely to heavy rainfall would be an oversimplification. While extreme weather can trigger flooding, the severity and duration of its impacts are often determined by inadequate infrastructure, poor maintenance, unplanned urban development and weaknesses in urban water management."
  },
