    "language": "en",
    "md5": "85ddd041b2b117473b8b18075d2c1113",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "However, attributing urban flooding entirely to heavy rainfall would be an oversimplification. While extreme weather can trigger flooding, the severity and duration of its impacts are often determined by inadequate infrastructure, poor maintenance, unplanned urban development and weaknesses in urban water management.\n\nThe second major concern is insufficient drainage capacity. Urban drainage systems must be designed according to rainfall intensity, catchment characteristics, land-use patterns and expected future development. When rainfall exceeds the system\u2019s design capacity, water cannot be discharged quickly enough, resulting in surface flooding. Hence, it is important to reassess the historical design criteria in light of climate-induced changes in rainfall patterns and changes in land use.\n\nRapid and poorly planned urbanisation is another significant contributor to urban flooding. Colombo city and its surrounding areas have undergone considerable transformation through residential development, commercial buildings, road construction and infrastructure expansion. Unfortunately, this development has often come at the expense of natural drainage systems, wetlands, marshlands and open spaces.\n\nNatural landscapes absorb and temporarily store rainwater, allowing it to infiltrate into the ground or gradually flow into waterways. However, when these areas are replaced by concrete buildings, asphalt roads and paved surfaces, the proportion of rainwater entering drainage channels increases considerably. This increased surface runoff places additional pressure on existing drainage infrastructure.\n\nStudies indicate that the total wetland extent in the Colombo Region declined by approximately 40% between 1992 and 2021, primarily due to urbanisation and land-use changes. The reduction of these green spaces and natural water-retention areas is an important factor contributing to increased flood vulnerability."
  },
