    "md5": "0451119ea0f1566ff24c3aec26f44536",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "Studies indicate that the total wetland extent in the Colombo Region declined by approximately 40% between 1992 and 2021, primarily due to urbanisation and land-use changes. The reduction of these green spaces and natural water-retention areas is an important factor contributing to increased flood vulnerability.\n\nIn Colombo, the drainage network involves multiple institutions, including the Colombo Municipal Council, the Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation, the Urban Development Authority and other relevant agencies. Their responsibilities extend across different components of the urban water-management system. Hence, the drainage system should be maintained in a well-coordinated manner to ensure that it functions efficiently. Establishing a single authority to handle the entire drainage system is another option. Integrated drainage planning must therefore consider the entire catchment, from the point where rainwater falls to the location where it is safely discharged.\n\nBuilding a climate-resilient city requires a shift from reactive disaster management to proactive risk reduction. Clearing blocked drains after a flood is necessary, but preventing future floods requires an integrated approach including planning and building climate-resilient infrastructure and the protection of natural ecosystems.\n\nIndividuals and business entities can also contribute to urban flood mitigation by adopting measures such as rooftop rainwater harvesting, green roofs and rain gardens to temporarily retain stormwater. These interventions reduce surface runoff, ease pressure on urban drainage networks and help minimise peak flows during intense rainfall events.\n\nA modern city should not be measured solely by its skyscrapers, highways and commercial developments, but also by its ability to withstand natural hazards while protecting the commercial activities, lives and livelihoods of its people."
