    "md5": "ea6b23045d07ccafb8e007f72410c28b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1512,
    "chunk_text": "Taxed at the till: why the Government must keep its word\n\nTomorrow, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who is also the Minister of Finance, will present the 2027 Budget to Parliament. He will have impressive revenue figures to report. Tax revenue rose from Rs. 1.75 trillion in 2022 to Rs. 5.05 trillion in 2025, and collections grew by a further 25.9% in the first half of this year.\n\nStability deserves recognition, and no responsible person wants to return to the queues of 2022. Yet the Budget must answer a harder question, which is, who has paid for this recovery? In 2025, 77.4% of tax revenue came from indirect taxes and only 22.6% from income tax. For every rupee collected directly, about Rs. 3.40 was collected at the till, at the pump and at the port. In the first half of this year, the balance tilted further, to 79% indirect and 21% direct.\n\nAn indirect tax takes no account of means. A teacher, a three-wheeler driver and a company chairman pay the same VAT on the same item, but it takes a far larger share of the teacher\u2019s income and the driver\u2019s. Dr W. A. Wijewardena, a former Deputy Governor of the Central Bank, recently described the country\u2019s tax burden as an \u201coverdose\u201d. That is the right word for what many households are now experiencing.\n\nThe Department of Census and Statistics has put the official poverty line at Rs. 17,592 per person per month in June 2026. That is about Rs. 586 a day. For a family of four, it comes to Rs. 70,368 a month, simply to meet basic needs."
  },
