    "md5": "9b78d5d3d29e503c7177914e131b3a31",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "The Department of Census and Statistics has put the official poverty line at Rs. 17,592 per person per month in June 2026. That is about Rs. 586 a day. For a family of four, it comes to Rs. 70,368 a month, simply to meet basic needs.\n\nWhat households actually spend tells the same story. According to preliminary findings of the Department of Census and Statistics\u2019 Household Income and Expenditure Survey (HIES) 2025, average monthly household expenditure reached Rs. 116,191 in early 2025, an increase of 84.1% on the Rs. 63,130 recorded in 2019. Median spending nearly doubled, from Rs. 47,544 to Rs. 94,057.\n\nYet, measured at 2016 prices, the picture is very different. Real average monthly spending rose only from Rs. 55,430 to Rs. 63,734, an increase of about 15%, and real median spending from Rs. 41,745 to Rs. 51,593. In plain terms, households are paying far more but consuming only modestly more. Most of the increase has gone on higher prices, not better living standards, and the taxes built into those prices are part of the reason.\n\nThe burden is not evenly spread. Urban households spend an average of Rs. 158,023 a month, about 36% above the national average; rural households spend Rs. 108,918, and estate households only Rs. 88,691. A tax levied on consumption takes no account of these differences. The same survey shows how thin the margins have become. Average household income was Rs. 120,266 a month, leaving the typical household only about Rs. 4,000 after its spending. Estate households, with an average income of Rs. 72,558, spent more than they earned. For these families, every rupee of tax on necessities bites hardest.\n\nThe HIES also gives the Government the evidence it needs to act. The survey divides household spending into 35 categories, 19 for food and 16 for other goods and services. It shows exactly where ordinary families spend their money, and therefore where relief from VAT and other indirect taxes would do the most good."
  },
