    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d62199e68723d546bb9a4a55f103fbcc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1706,
    "chunk_text": "The HIES also gives the Government the evidence it needs to act. The survey divides household spending into 35 categories, 19 for food and 16 for other goods and services. It shows exactly where ordinary families spend their money, and therefore where relief from VAT and other indirect taxes would do the most good.\n\nTwo budgets later, parents are still waiting on school books. In September, the President told a rally at Nochchiyagama that plans were being prepared to remove the tax. Last month, he spoke of reducing VAT rates over the next two to three years. These are welcome words, but a child cannot read a statement of intent, and a parent cannot pay for books with one. The 2027 Budget should remove this tax outright and from a fixed date.\n\nHospital equipment and IT services belong on the same list. Medical machinery and equipment lost their VAT exemption when the rate rose to 18% in January 2024, and the cost of taxing the tools of treatment falls, sooner or later, on the patient. Local IT services have also carried 18% VAT since that date, when an exemption introduced to support the sector was withdrawn. This was the previous administration\u2019s decision, but it is now this Government\u2019s to correct. A country that wants its schools, small firms and public services to go digital should not make the means of doing so more expensive.\n\nDuring the election campaign, people were encouraged to believe that a motorbike, and then a small car, would come within reach of ordinary families. For a young worker, a small trader or a parent on the school run, this was never a matter of status. Where public transport remains inadequate, a vehicle is how people get to work and earn a living."
