    "md5": "88a779d588302a5db85d4feba5888637",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1475,
    "chunk_text": "During the election campaign, people were encouraged to believe that a motorbike, and then a small car, would come within reach of ordinary families. For a young worker, a small trader or a parent on the school run, this was never a matter of status. Where public transport remains inadequate, a vehicle is how people get to work and earn a living.\n\nGood tax policy looks beyond the next collection target. Sri Lanka needs foreign exchange, investment and enterprise, and those who can provide them have choices. The professional earning abroad, the exporter and the investor each decide where their money will sit. If rates climb and rules keep shifting, the rational decision is to keep it outside.\n\nConsider the recent signals. Income from the export of services now bears a 15% income tax. Withholding tax on interest has doubled to 10%. VAT on financial services was raised to 20.5% in July, absorbing the separate 2.5% levy. Each measure can be defended on its own, but together they tell those with money to bring home that the next increase may be only a gazette away.\n\nThe Government should send the opposite message that moderate rates, a tax schedule held stable for several years, simple rules and a clear reward for foreign earnings brought into Sri Lanka through our banks. Every dollar that comes home and is invested here supports the rupee without a surcharge, creates work and, in time, widens the tax base far more durably than another levy on consumption."
  },
