    "md5": "87297e3a48a2dbe8971a43b81acc9af8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "The Government should send the opposite message that moderate rates, a tax schedule held stable for several years, simple rules and a clear reward for foreign earnings brought into Sri Lanka through our banks. Every dollar that comes home and is invested here supports the rupee without a surcharge, creates work and, in time, widens the tax base far more durably than another levy on consumption.\n\nOur nearest neighbour shows that it can be done. For eight years, India has levied its Goods and Services Tax (GST) at four main rates of 5, 12, 18 and 28%, with a compensation cess on top of the highest band. On 22 September 2025, following a unanimous decision of the GST Council, it collapsed these into two principal rates - 5% for essentials and 18% as the standard rate. A separate 40% rate is reserved for tobacco, aerated drinks, large cars and other sin and luxury goods. Fewer rates mean fewer arguments over which rate applies, and less room for disputes and evasion.\n\nSimplification went hand in hand with targeting. It is instructive to see what India did with the very items our own Government promised to relieve.\n\nIn a single reform, India took the tax off the school bag and the medicine cabinet, brought the family motorbike closer within reach, and asked those buying luxury vehicles to pay more. That is very close to what Sri Lankan voters were promised at Tangalle. Nor did India\u2019s revenue collapse, although there was a cost. Gross GST collections grew by only 0.7% in November 2025, the first month to reflect a full month of the lower rates, and analysts have noted that higher spending had not, at first, made up for the cuts. Collections had then recovered, rising by 6.1% year on year in December, and reached a record \u20b92.43 lakh crore in April 2026, although much of that growth came from tax on imports. Revenue held up even as rates came down."
  },
