    "chunk_text": "In a single reform, India took the tax off the school bag and the medicine cabinet, brought the family motorbike closer within reach, and asked those buying luxury vehicles to pay more. That is very close to what Sri Lankan voters were promised at Tangalle. Nor did India\u2019s revenue collapse, although there was a cost. Gross GST collections grew by only 0.7% in November 2025, the first month to reflect a full month of the lower rates, and analysts have noted that higher spending had not, at first, made up for the cuts. Collections had then recovered, rising by 6.1% year on year in December, and reached a record \u20b92.43 lakh crore in April 2026, although much of that growth came from tax on imports. Revenue held up even as rates came down.\n\nOthers in the region have chosen targeting over blanket increases. When Indonesia\u2019s VAT was due to rise from 11 to 12% on 1 January 2025, President Prabowo Subianto confined the increase to luxury goods and services already subject to the luxury sales tax, such as private jets, yachts and luxury homes, and left ordinary goods at the old rate. Vietnam cut its standard VAT rate from 10 to 8% in 2022 and has extended the cut several times, currently to 31 December 2026, while excluding sectors such as banking, finance, telecommunications and real estate. Neither country abandoned fiscal discipline. Each simply decided, with care, who should carry the load."
