    "chunk_size_bytes": 1952,
    "chunk_text": "Others in the region have chosen targeting over blanket increases. When Indonesia\u2019s VAT was due to rise from 11 to 12% on 1 January 2025, President Prabowo Subianto confined the increase to luxury goods and services already subject to the luxury sales tax, such as private jets, yachts and luxury homes, and left ordinary goods at the old rate. Vietnam cut its standard VAT rate from 10 to 8% in 2022 and has extended the cut several times, currently to 31 December 2026, while excluding sectors such as banking, finance, telecommunications and real estate. Neither country abandoned fiscal discipline. Each simply decided, with care, who should carry the load.\n\nIt will be said that there is no fiscal space and that our commitments to the International Monetary Fund (IMF) must be respected. They must. But as Dr Wijewardena has pointed out, revenue has already exceeded the program\u2019s target of 15% of GDP. The IMF\u2019s own figures show that last year\u2019s primary surplus reached 5.4% of GDP, more than double the program\u2019s target of 2.3%. Dr Wijewardena has also noted that only 28% of this year\u2019s capital budget had been spent by the end of September. A Government collecting more than its program demands, while failing to spend what Parliament has already approved, can find room for relief on books and hospital equipment. Better compliance and a wider direct tax base can make up the difference over time.\n\nThe President has the mandate and the opportunity. On 12 November, he should remove VAT from school books, hospital equipment, medical services and IT services; commit to fewer, simpler tax rates, aimed at those most able to pay; set out a dated path to lower VAT and to a fair vehicle tax; and give those holding money abroad a stable reason to bring it home. Recovery will be real when a family of four can feel it, and not only when the Treasury can count it.\n\n(The author is a President\u2019s Counsel and a Member of Parliament)"
