    "chunk_text": "Dr. Ratnayaka said frequent changes in spectacle prescriptions could be an early warning sign, particularly among children. Parents should be alert if their children repeatedly need new spectacles or continue to experience vision problems despite wearing them.\n\nOther symptoms include blurred vision, difficulty seeing at night, halos around lights and differences in vision between the two eyes.\n\nDoctors have also stressed the importance of school medical and vision-screening programmes, particularly examinations conducted among children in Grades 1, 4 and 7, to identify the condition at an early stage.\n\nEarly detection is important as keratoconus can progressively damage vision if left untreated. Spectacles may help correct vision during the early stages, while specialised contact lenses may be required as the condition advances.\n\nCorneal collagen cross-linking is another treatment that strengthens the cornea and helps stop or slow the progression of the condition. Patients with advanced keratoconus may require corneal transplantation.\n\nSpecialists have urged parents to pay attention to frequent eye rubbing, repeated changes in spectacle prescriptions and difficulties in seeing clearly, and to arrange an eye examination if such symptoms are noticed."
