    "md5": "af802a394c794f8409b07e835917976a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1768,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s lost decade: A decade of diverging household incomes\n\nSources: Department of Census and Statistics, HIES 2016 and 2019 Final Results, and HIES 2025 Preliminary Results. All real income figures are expressed in 2016 prices.\n\nFigure: Sri Lanka\u2019s Poverty Trajectory: Crisis and Partial Recovery, 2019\u20132025\n\nReal median household income fell from Rs. 43,511 in 2016 to Rs. 41,623 in 2025, showing that the typical household is worse off despite nominal gains\n\nThe richest 20% of households now earn nearly 15 times more than the poorest 20%, up from 11 times in 2016\n\nPoverty has returned to 16% in 2025, erasing earlier progress and reversing the decline seen in 2019\n\nNearly a decade after 2016, are Sri Lankan households better off? The newly released preliminary results of the Household Income and Expenditure Survey (HIES) 2025 provide an important insight into this question. Despite substantial increases in nominal incomes, the typical Sri Lankan household had less purchasing power in early 2025 than it did nine years earlier, as reported in the 2016 HIES.\n\nMore strikingly, the decade has not affected households equally. While real incomes among the poorest households have fallen substantially, those at the upper end of the income distribution have recorded gains that hide the extent of the loss. The widening gap between average and median incomes further suggests that national averages increasingly conceal the experience of the typical household.\n\nAccording to the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS), average monthly household income increased from Rs. 62,237 in 2016 to Rs. 120,266 in early 2025, an increase of approximately 93%. However, much of this increase reflects inflation rather than improvements in purchasing power."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-11-dailyftlk-9ebaef7f",
