    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0223200d2026c1ec1d690dd43342c76f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "According to the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS), average monthly household income increased from Rs. 62,237 in 2016 to Rs. 120,266 in early 2025, an increase of approximately 93%. However, much of this increase reflects inflation rather than improvements in purchasing power.\n\nThe national figures conceal an even more important development: household incomes have followed sharply different trajectories across the income distribution.\n\nAfter adjusting for inflation, average monthly income among the poorest 20% of households declined from approximately Rs. 14,843 in 2016 to Rs. 12,403 in early 2025, a reduction of 16.4%. In contrast, average real income among the richest 20% increased from approximately Rs. 158,072 to Rs. 184,129, a gain of 16.5%. The divergence is pronounced after 2019, as the COVID-19 pandemic, the economic crisis, and other external shocks produced a decline in real income among the poorest quintile by approximately 19.6%, while income among the richest quintile increased by approximately 6.8% between 2019 and 2025.\n\nThe average income ratio between the richest and poorest quintiles rose from 10.6 in 2016 to 11.2 in 2019 and 14.8 in early 2025. In other words, the households in the richest quintile earned, on average, nearly 15 times as much as those in the poorest quintile in 2025, compared with approximately 11 times in 2016. These suggest the limited improvement in national average income has concentrated at the top end of the distribution while accompanied by a much weaker experience among households at the lower end of the distribution."
  },
