    "language": "en",
    "md5": "810a99a4f4f848229fb5240ec76513b4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "The average income ratio between the richest and poorest quintiles rose from 10.6 in 2016 to 11.2 in 2019 and 14.8 in early 2025. In other words, the households in the richest quintile earned, on average, nearly 15 times as much as those in the poorest quintile in 2025, compared with approximately 11 times in 2016. These suggest the limited improvement in national average income has concentrated at the top end of the distribution while accompanied by a much weaker experience among households at the lower end of the distribution.\n\nThis suggests the mean is influenced by high incomes, while the median represents the household at the midpoint of the income distribution. As a result, improvements among higher-income households can sustain the national average even when the typical household experiences declining purchasing power. Taken together, the widening mean\u2013median gap and growing richest-to-poorest quintile income ratio point towards an increasingly uneven income distribution. However, these indicators are not substitutes for a comprehensive measure of inequality. The 2019 HIES reported an income Gini coefficient of 0.46, compared with 0.45 in 2016. The forthcoming full HIES 2025 results will be important for establishing whether this broader measure has also changed.\n\nThe latest poverty estimates reinforce concerns about the reversal of earlier progress. Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty headcount declined from 16% in 2016 to 14.3% in 2019. However, the preliminary HIES 2025 findings estimate that poverty returned to 16%. The lack of household data between 2019 and 2025 likely hides a peak in poverty that occurred during that period, as the country experienced a plethora of shocks. The World Bank\u2019s revised estimates suggest poverty may have peaked at around 20% during this period."
  },
