    "md5": "29f5ebc79695d754746f6bd534279fd0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1052,
    "chunk_text": "The latest poverty estimates reinforce concerns about the reversal of earlier progress. Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty headcount declined from 16% in 2016 to 14.3% in 2019. However, the preliminary HIES 2025 findings estimate that poverty returned to 16%. The lack of household data between 2019 and 2025 likely hides a peak in poverty that occurred during that period, as the country experienced a plethora of shocks. The World Bank\u2019s revised estimates suggest poverty may have peaked at around 20% during this period.\n\nThe key question is no longer simply whether Sri Lanka\u2019s economy is recovering, but whether that recovery is reaching households across the income distribution. A sustainable recovery therefore requires more than restoring economic output to transform it into something durable. It must translate into sustained growth in real household earnings, more productive employment opportunities, and greater resilience among economically vulnerable households.\n\n(The author is a research fellow at the Institute of Policy Studies of Sri Lanka)"
