    "md5": "d34f9504e47995d441eefb6cbc59e811",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "The money goes around: Does the Budget bring it back to the people?\n\nEvery rupee collected from the people carries an expectation. The real success of a Budget lies not in how much money is collected or spent, but in how much it improves people\u2019s lives\n\nImagine a small shopkeeper closing his shop after a busy day. A salaried employee notices deductions from a monthly salary. A mother pays taxes when she buys food, clothes and other household items. A business owner contributes while trying to keep a business alive and provide jobs. They may never read a national Budget or understand its complicated economic terms. But they share one simple expectation: the money they contribute should return to society in ways that make life better. They do not expect the Government to return the same money to their own pockets. They expect better schools for their children, reliable hospitals, safer roads, efficient public transport and more opportunities for the future.\n\nA national Budget is a journey of public money from people to the Government, and back to society through services and development. The important question is not simply how much the Government collects or spends. It is whether people experience meaningful benefits in return.\n\nThe Government collects revenue from many sources, including income taxes, value added taxes, customs duties, fees and other levies. These resources are pooled to finance public services and national priorities. They support schools and hospitals, maintain roads, assist people in need and contribute to national development.\n\nPublic money is not an ordinary personal payment. The benefits are not expected to match each person\u2019s contribution. A child from a low-income family may receive an education without paying school fees. An elderly person may depend on a public hospital. A village road may benefit hundreds of families who contribute different amounts to government revenue."
  },
  {
