    "language": "en",
    "md5": "68a43007f4a1d1d15ea61e76c163d873",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1640,
    "chunk_text": "Public money is not an ordinary personal payment. The benefits are not expected to match each person\u2019s contribution. A child from a low-income family may receive an education without paying school fees. An elderly person may depend on a public hospital. A village road may benefit hundreds of families who contribute different amounts to government revenue.\n\nConsider a family that pays taxes but still struggles to obtain proper medical treatment. Parents may spend additional money on tuition because their children do not receive sufficient support at school. A small business owner may lose hours visiting several government offices to complete a simple task.\n\nThe Budget may have allocated money for hospitals, schools, roads and business development. But are people experiencing the improvements they expected?\n\nA road may receive funding but remain unfinished. A hospital may acquire modern equipment without enough trained staff to operate it. A school may have a new building but lack teachers. An online service may be introduced, while people still need to visit several offices to complete an application.\n\nIn these situations, expenditure may be recorded, but the intended benefit remains out of reach. A school building matters when children learn better. Hospital equipment matters when patients receive timely treatment. A digital service matters when it saves people time and effort. The value of public spending is measured not only in money spent, but in lives changed.\n\nThis is why a Budget should be assessed beyond allocations and expenditure. It should be examined through the results people can see and experience."
  },
