    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "In these situations, expenditure may be recorded, but the intended benefit remains out of reach. A school building matters when children learn better. Hospital equipment matters when patients receive timely treatment. A digital service matters when it saves people time and effort. The value of public spending is measured not only in money spent, but in lives changed.\n\nThis is why a Budget should be assessed beyond allocations and expenditure. It should be examined through the results people can see and experience.\n\nThis connection is particularly important in taxation. Tax collection is not only about laws, audits and penalties. People are more willing to meet their tax responsibilities when they believe the system is fair and public money is used for the country\u2019s benefit.\n\nThe responsibility is shared, though not identical. Taxpayers are expected to comply with the law; the Government is responsible for managing public revenue wisely and delivering dependable services. A tax receipt records a contribution. The quality of public services helps people understand its wider value.\n\nSome countries have explored ways to bring public spending closer to people\u2019s needs.\n\nNew Zealand has applied a wellbeing approach to its Budget process since 2019. Its Treasury considers broader measures of wellbeing, including health, education, living conditions and quality of life. The message is clear: economic growth matters, but progress should also be reflected in how people live.\n\nIn Porto Alegre, Brazil, participatory budgeting gave residents opportunities to discuss community needs and influence local spending priorities. This experience showed how people could have a voice in deciding how public resources were used. It also demonstrated the importance of sustained institutional support for public participation."
