    "language": "en",
    "md5": "15cc0dbc0cd35d4072abe49877c49685",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "In Porto Alegre, Brazil, participatory budgeting gave residents opportunities to discuss community needs and influence local spending priorities. This experience showed how people could have a voice in deciding how public resources were used. It also demonstrated the importance of sustained institutional support for public participation.\n\nThese experiences differ, but they share a common principle: public spending should respond to people\u2019s needs, and its results should be examined. Sri Lanka does not need to copy another country\u2019s model. It can learn from these experiences and develop approaches suited to its own circumstances.\n\nA Budget promise should not disappear into a document, a meeting or a set of figures. It should lead to something people can see, use and feel in their daily lives.\n\nImagine a village waiting for a road, a mother hoping for better hospital care, or a child who needs a well-equipped classroom. They deserve to know what was promised, where the money went, what was delivered and whether life has improved.\n\nAccountability begins when promises are clear, spending can be followed, results are honestly measured and people have a voice when something goes wrong. It is not about pointing fingers. It is about making sure public money does the job it was meant to do.\n\nA promise made with public money must come with a result people can recognise.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the challenge is not simply to allocate money, but to ensure that public spending leads to visible and meaningful improvements. This calls for clear priorities, responsible implementation, transparency and a willingness to examine whether programmes achieve their intended results. People should be able to understand what the Budget promises, how public money is used and what has been achieved. Communities should have opportunities to express their needs, while public institutions should explain their decisions and outcomes in language people can understand."
  },
