    "description": "The money goes around: Does the Budget bring it back to the people?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-money-goes-around-Does-the-Budget-bring-it-back-to-the-people/4-799902",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794358224.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailyftlk-abc0edc1-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "479daf5ce51ff0ea0e8c1cb24f653db3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 829,
    "chunk_text": "For Sri Lanka, the challenge is not simply to allocate money, but to ensure that public spending leads to visible and meaningful improvements. This calls for clear priorities, responsible implementation, transparency and a willingness to examine whether programmes achieve their intended results. People should be able to understand what the Budget promises, how public money is used and what has been achieved. Communities should have opportunities to express their needs, while public institutions should explain their decisions and outcomes in language people can understand.\n\nPerhaps the most meaningful question we can ask about a national Budget is this: After all the money has been collected and spent, are people\u2019s lives better?\n\nThat is the measure that matters. That is when the money truly comes back to the people."
  },
