    "md5": "b0582273d5865499bcb9f361d6506e06",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1647,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 must turn fiscal discipline into long term growth path\n\nOn 12 November the President, Finance Minister, will present the 2027 Budget to a Parliament. The public that have grown used to fiscal good news have plethora of expectations. The numbers justify some of that optimism. In the first eight months of 2026, revenue and grants came in at Rs. 4,002 billion, an increase of 21% year-on-year and 75.5% of the annual estimate, when a pro-rata basis it would have been 66.7%. The Treasury ran an overall surplus of Rs. 35 billion for the period against a budgeted full-year deficit of Rs. 1,757 billion, and the primary surplus of Rs. 1,645 billion was almost double the Rs. 860 billion targeted for the whole year.\n\nThis remarkably different figures are not by accident. Under the IMF\u2019s Extended Fund Facility, the fiscal target has been a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP; the revised estimates for 2025 show 3.8%. Fiscal revenue, which collapsed to around 8% of GDP in 2021 after the 2019 tax cuts, is now close to 16%. Central government debt fell to 91.6% of GDP at end-2025 from 95.5% a year earlier. Few countries emerging from a sovereign default have consolidated this quickly.\n\nBut a fiscal outturn has two sides, and the expenditure side deserves a closer look. Total spending to August was only 56.2% of the annual budget. Capital expenditure and net lending, at Rs. 436 billion, were just 31.8% of an allocation of roughly Rs. 1,369 billion. Execution of public investment in 2025 also fell short, at about 77% of the estimate. This is not part of fiscal discipline, in fact, under-execution of budgeted capital expenditure."
  },
