    "chunk_size_bytes": 1657,
    "chunk_text": "But a fiscal outturn has two sides, and the expenditure side deserves a closer look. Total spending to August was only 56.2% of the annual budget. Capital expenditure and net lending, at Rs. 436 billion, were just 31.8% of an allocation of roughly Rs. 1,369 billion. Execution of public investment in 2025 also fell short, at about 77% of the estimate. This is not part of fiscal discipline, in fact, under-execution of budgeted capital expenditure.\n\nThe debt ratio has turned, helped by primary surpluses, a recovery in nominal GDP and the restructuring of external debt. Yet it remains well above the 68\u201376% band held during 2006\u20132017, and interest still absorbs close to half of government revenue. The fiscal anchor cannot be loosened yet.\n\nEvery year the run-up to the Budget Speech produces a long list of demands. Public servants want a salary revision, pensioners an allowance, professionals tax relief, importers lower duties, farmers fertiliser subsidies, and every electorate a new road or school building. There are very reasonable demands on its own, unfortunately, added together, they exceed any feasible fiscal envelope.\n\nA national budget is not a magic wand. It is the principal instrument of fiscal policy, through which the government decides how much of national income it will absorb through taxation, how it will allocate that spending, and how it will finance the gap. Through these choices the government steers the economy in the direction it wishes to take. The question to ask on 12 November is not what the budget gives me or my group, but where it is taking the economy and whether the instruments chosen will get us there."
  },
