    "chunk_size_bytes": 1531,
    "chunk_text": "A national budget is not a magic wand. It is the principal instrument of fiscal policy, through which the government decides how much of national income it will absorb through taxation, how it will allocate that spending, and how it will finance the gap. Through these choices the government steers the economy in the direction it wishes to take. The question to ask on 12 November is not what the budget gives me or my group, but where it is taking the economy and whether the instruments chosen will get us there.\n\nPrior to the budget, indication of certain proposals of reasonable expectations\u00a0 were in the public media.\n\nThe first is personal income tax relief. The personal relief, which is the tax-free threshold under the Advance Personal Income Tax (APIT), was raised from Rs. 1.2 million to Rs. 1.8 million a year, or Rs. 150,000 a month, from April 2025, while the 6% band was extended to Rs. 1 million. In September the Deputy Minister of Economic Development told Parliament that the government remains committed to raising the monthly threshold to Rs. 200,000, and the President has publicly promised tax relief in this budget. For the salaried middle class, it would be a welcome adjustment.\n\nThe trade-off should be stated plainly. When the threshold was last raised, the Finance Ministry estimated that around 200,000 of some 800,000 individual taxpayers would drop out of the net, at a cost of about Rs. 60 billion a year. A narrow direct tax base is precisely why we lean so heavily on regressive indirect taxes."
