    "chunk_text": "The trade-off should be stated plainly. When the threshold was last raised, the Finance Ministry estimated that around 200,000 of some 800,000 individual taxpayers would drop out of the net, at a cost of about Rs. 60 billion a year. A narrow direct tax base is precisely why we lean so heavily on regressive indirect taxes.\n\nThe third is policy consistency. Investors can price a high tax rate. What they cannot price is a tax code that changes with every budget. A medium-term revenue strategy, announced once and adhered to, would lower the risk premium on private investment more effectively than any single concession.\n\nThe fourth is a clear and well-communicated direction. Expectations drive investment decisions. The budget should set out where the government wants the economy to be in five years, explain the trade-offs involved, and then hold to that course.\n\nBehind the annual wish lists lies the structural problem that has taken Sri Lanka to the IMF 17 times: the twin deficits. The government has persistently spent more than it collects as tax revenue, and the country has persistently spent more abroad than it earns. In national accounting terms, the current account deficit is simply the gap between domestic investment and domestic savings, and a large, persistent fiscal deficit is public dissaving. The two deficits therefore feed each other. Fiscal expansion raises domestic absorption, much of which leaks into imports, and the resulting external gap has been financed with foreign borrowing, a large share of it by the state itself."
