    "chunk_size_bytes": 1670,
    "chunk_text": "Behind the annual wish lists lies the structural problem that has taken Sri Lanka to the IMF 17 times: the twin deficits. The government has persistently spent more than it collects as tax revenue, and the country has persistently spent more abroad than it earns. In national accounting terms, the current account deficit is simply the gap between domestic investment and domestic savings, and a large, persistent fiscal deficit is public dissaving. The two deficits therefore feed each other. Fiscal expansion raises domestic absorption, much of which leaks into imports, and the resulting external gap has been financed with foreign borrowing, a large share of it by the state itself.\n\nThe second feature is a growth model biased toward non-tradables. Non-tradable goods and services are those whose prices are determined in the domestic market because they cannot easily be traded across borders: construction, real estate, wholesale and retail trade, domestic transport, personal services and public administration. Tradables, by contrast, compete in world markets: manufactured exports, plantation crops, tourism, and IT and logistics services.\n\nGrowth driven by non-tradables looks impressive in the national accounts. But it generates income without generating foreign exchange, while the consumption it finances lifts import demand. Over time it also bids up domestic wages and prices relative to the rest of the world, appreciating the real exchange rate and squeezing the profitability of the export sector. External debt then rises faster than the economy\u2019s capacity to service it. That, in essence, is the story of the decade that ended in default in 2022."
  },
