    "chunk_size_bytes": 1543,
    "chunk_text": "Growth driven by non-tradables looks impressive in the national accounts. But it generates income without generating foreign exchange, while the consumption it finances lifts import demand. Over time it also bids up domestic wages and prices relative to the rest of the world, appreciating the real exchange rate and squeezing the profitability of the export sector. External debt then rises faster than the economy\u2019s capacity to service it. That, in essence, is the story of the decade that ended in default in 2022.\n\nSource\u2019s Annual Reports 2000 and 2003; Sri Lanka Tea Board; EDB (2025); US$ billion\n\nOur two traditional exports illustrate the problem. Apparel earnings rose from about $ 3 billion in 2000 to $ 5 billion in 2025, a compound growth rate of roughly 2% a year. In real terms, the industry earns no more today than it did a quarter of a century ago. Tea has done a little better in nominal terms, rising from $ 0.7 billion to $ 1.5 billion, but it has hovered around the same level for more than a decade as production volumes have stagnated. Together these two products still account for close to half of merchandise exports. An economy that has to service a debt stock of this size cannot depend on two mature industries growing at the pace of world prices. The need of export diversification has become yesterday\u2019s problem rather than a future.\n\nNo single budget can unwind structural weaknesses built up over decades. What it can do is signal, clearly and credibly, a change of direction. Five areas deserve priority."
