    "chunk_size_bytes": 1491,
    "chunk_text": "Our two traditional exports illustrate the problem. Apparel earnings rose from about $ 3 billion in 2000 to $ 5 billion in 2025, a compound growth rate of roughly 2% a year. In real terms, the industry earns no more today than it did a quarter of a century ago. Tea has done a little better in nominal terms, rising from $ 0.7 billion to $ 1.5 billion, but it has hovered around the same level for more than a decade as production volumes have stagnated. Together these two products still account for close to half of merchandise exports. An economy that has to service a debt stock of this size cannot depend on two mature industries growing at the pace of world prices. The need of export diversification has become yesterday\u2019s problem rather than a future.\n\nNo single budget can unwind structural weaknesses built up over decades. What it can do is signal, clearly and credibly, a change of direction. Five areas deserve priority.\n\nRebalance growth toward tradables. Incentives, tariff policy and public investment should be tilted toward activities that earn or save foreign exchange. That means a simpler, lower and predictable tariff schedule that does not tax imported inputs, faster VAT refunds for exporters, trade agreements that widen market access, and an end to the anti-export bias that makes selling at home more profitable than selling abroad. A competitive real exchange rate, sustained by fiscal discipline rather than administrative controls, is part of the same agenda."
