    "language": "en",
    "md5": "45cce55eed77128bb80244691d7648bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Rebalance growth toward tradables. Incentives, tariff policy and public investment should be tilted toward activities that earn or save foreign exchange. That means a simpler, lower and predictable tariff schedule that does not tax imported inputs, faster VAT refunds for exporters, trade agreements that widen market access, and an end to the anti-export bias that makes selling at home more profitable than selling abroad. A competitive real exchange rate, sustained by fiscal discipline rather than administrative controls, is part of the same agenda.\n\nAllocate scarce resources more efficiently. For a small economy, Sri Lanka leaves a surprising share of its factor endowment idle:\n\nnLabour: an ageing working-age population, half of it outside the labour force, while skilled workers continue to migrate.\n\nnLand: large extents of state-owned land remain unused or underused. Long-term, bankable leases for agriculture, plantations, industry and energy would put it to productive use.\n\nnRenewable energy: some of the best wind and solar resources in the region, particularly in the north and north-west. Harnessing them requires investment in the transmission grid and storage, and competitive, transparent procurement, so that lower-cost power reduces production costs across the export sector.\n\nnStrategic location: Colombo lies beside the main east-west shipping lane, next to an Indian subcontinent whose cargo volumes continue to grow rapidly. Port capacity, transhipment, logistics and bunkering services built around that demand are tradables in the purest sense.\n\nProtect capital spending in execution, not only in allocation. A larger capital vote that is only one-third spent by August achieves very little. Better project appraisal, multi-year expenditure commitments and quarterly public reporting on capital budget execution would do more for medium-term growth than a higher headline allocation."
