    "md5": "89d9a42290c082371151ce8f611afa49",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "New railway bridge across Mahaweli river with Indian loan grant scheme\n\nCabinet spokesman says bridge forms part of a planned suburban rail network for Greater Kandy; feasibility study underway, double-tracking proposed for key stretches\n\nAffirms restoration of historic 1865 Black Bridge continues in parallel, led by CECB with technical support from the University of Peradeniya\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved constructing a new railway bridge across the Mahaweli River between Sarasavi Uyana and Peradeniya, with financial assistance from India.\n\nThe bridge will be constructed on the Matale railway line as part of efforts to improve railway connectivity and develop an extensive suburban railway network covering the Greater Kandy area.\n\nAnnouncing the decision at the weekly post-Caninet meeting media briefing yesterday, Cabinet Spokesman and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa said funding for the project will be obtained through an Indian loan grant scheme intended for the renovation and development of railway infrastructure facilities damaged by Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nHe said the proposed bridge is expected to improve the integration of the Matale railway line with the national railway network.\n\n\u201cThe Government is also planning to develop an extensive suburban railway network in the Greater Kandy area, with a feasibility study currently underway. The proposed development includes, converting the main railway line from Kadugannawa to Gampola via Peradeniya into a double-track railway line. Plans also include converting the Matale railway line between Peradeniya and Katugastota into a double-track railway line,\u201d he added.\n\nDr. Jayatissa said the Government has also identified the need to address capacity constraints caused by the existing single-track railway infrastructure, while improving the reliability and connectivity of train services."
  },
