    "md5": "62a36dcb0efa5340d968332592882a9a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Ravi K appeals to HSBC to consider winding down HDPL in Sri Lanka\n\nGlobal banking giant cited a global operating-model review, not local performance, in its 7 Oct. notice\n\nHeadcount was 1,659 on 30 Sept. against a peak of about 4,500\n\nMP who lobbied for the 2004 entry seeks urgent, time-bound talks; offers to marshal Govt. support while sitting in Opposition\n\nSays cost, recruitment, training, infrastructure and policy can be negotiable\n\nOpposition MP Ravi Karunanayake has written personally to HSBC Holdings Group Chairman and Group Chief Executive appealing for the closure of HSBC Electronic Data Processing Lanka to be reversed, and offering to help assemble Government support for keeping the operation in the country.\n\nThe letter, addressed to both executives and titled a personal and national appeal, runs through the centre\u2019s 22-year history, the bank\u2019s stated reasons for the migration, and the livelihoods attached to 1,659 jobs. Karunanayake asks group leadership to reopen the decision and to talk before the move becomes irreversible.\n\nHe rests much of the appeal on the wording of the bank\u2019s own notice. \u201cYour notification of 7 October 2026 expressly states that the proposed migration follows a global operating-model review and does not reflect the performance of the Sri Lankan company. That acknowledgement provides a compelling reason to explore an alternative.\u201d\n\nHe puts the workforce at 1,659 as at 30 September, 2026, and says the operation at its best employed about 4,500 young Sri Lankans. \u201cBehind each position is a family, a set of responsibilities and a future built through years of dedication,\u201d he wrote. Closure, he says, means \u201cuncertainty and abandonment of their aspirations for generations children\u2019s education, housing commitments, care for parents and the prospect of continuing a fulfilling professional life in their own country.\u201d"
  },
