    "chunk_size_bytes": 1607,
    "chunk_text": "He puts the workforce at 1,659 as at 30 September, 2026, and says the operation at its best employed about 4,500 young Sri Lankans. \u201cBehind each position is a family, a set of responsibilities and a future built through years of dedication,\u201d he wrote. Closure, he says, means \u201cuncertainty and abandonment of their aspirations for generations children\u2019s education, housing commitments, care for parents and the prospect of continuing a fulfilling professional life in their own country.\u201d\n\nThe centre opened at the World Trade Centre in Colombo 1 in 2004. It later moved into a built-to-suit facility at 439 Sri Jayewardenepura Mawatha, Kotte, in his constituency, followed by Union Place, Colombo 2 in 2022 and Mireka Tower, Colombo 5. He says each was built to the bank\u2019s Base Build Engineering Guidelines and Standards, and that the sites lifted both footprint and headcount.\n\n\u201cWhat began as an investment became a source of pride and possibility to \u2018both\u2019 our small nation and the HSBC brand a forerunner,\u201d he wrote. \u201cIt demonstrated that our young people could meet global standards and contribute meaningfully.\u201d\n\nThe letter concedes the commercial case rather than dismissing it. Karunanayake notes his later service as Finance and Foreign Affairs Minister and says he appreciates the bank\u2019s responsibilities to shareholders, customers and employees. \u201cConcerns about operating costs, workforce availability, scale and competitiveness merit a serious response. I believe we owe it to this longstanding partnership to examine together whether those concerns can be resolved.\u201d"
  },
