    "chunk_text": "The letter concedes the commercial case rather than dismissing it. Karunanayake notes his later service as Finance and Foreign Affairs Minister and says he appreciates the bank\u2019s responsibilities to shareholders, customers and employees. \u201cConcerns about operating costs, workforce availability, scale and competitiveness merit a serious response. I believe we owe it to this longstanding partnership to examine together whether those concerns can be resolved.\u201d\n\nHe frames that offer as bipartisan. \u201cPreserving opportunity for our people is a national responsibility that transcends political differences.\u201d\n\nThe appeal is made principally on behalf of younger workers, who he says used the centre to acquire knowledge, work across cultures and build careers connected to the wider world. \u201cThe decision to exit from this operation would close an important avenue through which young Sri Lankans can realise their ambitions while remaining in their native country. Their aspirations deserve a central place in our deliberations.\u201d\n\nKarunanayake asks for specific negotiations. \u201cI therefore respectfully request that HSBC\u2019s Group leadership reopen this decision and allow an urgent, time-bound dialogue before the planned migration becomes irreversible. Let us identify the specific changes needed in costs, recruitment, training, infrastructure and policy, and work towards a credible proposition for retaining and reinvesting in the Sri Lankan operation.\u201d\n\nHe sets the decision against the country\u2019s recovery from its 2022 debt default. \u201cSri Lanka is striving to overcome profound difficulties, correct longstanding weaknesses and rebuild confidence in its future. This is precisely the moment when the support of established partners as HSBC is most needed.\u201d A reversal, he argues, \u201cwould protect livelihoods and send a powerful message that recovery can be achieved through partnership, perseverance and shared endeavour.\u201d"
