    "md5": "0515b380d34af276fb9b3ea806fc8e60",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1828,
    "chunk_text": "Lanka Ashok Leyland reports strong 6M performance\n\nGrowth driven by private sector demand and premium bus segment\n\nLanka Ashok Leyland PLC (LAL) yesterday said it has recorded revenue growth of 78% to Rs. 13.8 billion in the six months ended 30 September 2026, up from compared to Rs. 7.75 billion during the corresponding period last year.\n\nThe company\u2019s Profit Before Tax increased by 66% to Rs. 2.8 billion, while Profit After Tax grew by 67% to Rs. 1.95 billion, compared to Rs. 1.17 billion in the previous year. Revenue from new vehicle sales increased by 86% to Rs. 12.61 billion, reflecting strong demand for Lanka Ashok Leyland vehicles.\n\nCommenting on the company\u2019s performance, CEO Umesh Gautam said that one of the most significant achievements during the period was the Company\u2019s ability to generate its entire bus and truck sales from the private sector, without any Government vehicle purchases. This clearly demonstrates our ability to sustain growth independently of Government procurement.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s passenger transport sector is gradually moving towards modernisation, with increasing demand for luxury, comfortable and air-conditioned buses. Private passenger transport operators are increasingly investing in higher-value buses to meet changing passenger expectations.\n\nRecognising this market transformation, Lanka Ashok Leyland has aggressively marketed its Luxura AC luxury bus range, which has contributed positively to the Company\u2019s revenue growth and profitability.\n\nGautam added: \u201cThe passenger transport industry is undergoing a significant transformation, with operators increasingly recognising the commercial potential of modern luxury buses. Our strategic focus on promoting Luxura AC buses has enabled us to capitalise on this growing demand while improving our profitability.\u201d"
  },
