    "md5": "2d4f62f83c765049d2a11a2f4d838aca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "US House blocks bid to impeach Defense Secretary Hegseth\n\n(Reuters) - Republicans in the US House of Representatives blocked an effort on Tuesday to impeach Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, winning a reprieve for the polarizing Pentagon chief \u200bbefore Democrats take control of Congress next year and possibly try again.\n\nThe vote on a procedural motion came just hours after Hegseth blasted efforts to impeach him as \u200ca farce and delivered a defiant defense of his record at the helm of the US military, winning praise from President Donald Trump.\n\nThe chamber voted 211 to 204 in favor of a motion to refer eight articles of impeachment, opens new tab to the Republican-led House Judiciary Committee, a move that the sponsor of the impeachment push, US Representative Thomas Massie, warned would kill it.\n\nMassie, an outgoing Kentucky Republican, had hoped to draw votes from Republicans upset with Hegseth's policies and wary of the unpopularity \u200bof the eight-month-old Iran war but no other Republicans joined him. The US-Israeli led conflict has never been approved by Congress, which is the only branch of government constitutionally permitted to \u200bdeclare war.\n\nDemocrats had voiced strong support for the impeachment articles, which accuse Hegseth of the \"high crimes and misdemeanors\" of illegally ordering the military to wage war \u2060in Iran and Yemen, attack boats in the Caribbean Sea and capture Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro in January. But 11 Democrats, including former House Speaker Nancy Pelosi of California, did not vote.\n\nIf eight of \u200bthem had, the procedural measure would have been defeated, paving the way for a full House impeachment vote.\n\nIan Krager, a spokesperson for Pelosi, said she had missed the vote because she had returned to San Francisco \u200bfor an annual event to honor veterans, but would have voted against the procedural measure."
