    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "Ian Krager, a spokesperson for Pelosi, said she had missed the vote because she had returned to San Francisco \u200bfor an annual event to honor veterans, but would have voted against the procedural measure.\n\nHegseth would have been the first US defense secretary to be impeached. The last time any civilian leader of the US military was impeached was in 1876, when William Belknap held the \u200btitle \"secretary of war.\"\n\nHegseth calls himself secretary of war, part of his effort to rebrand a military he said was too weak and \"woke\" when he took office last year. Trump cannot change the name of the \u200bDefense Department without approval from Congress.\n\nVoter anger over the Iran war, which has settled into an effective stalemate focused on securing the Strait of Hormuz, helped Democrats win control of Congress in last week's midterm elections.\n\nA Reuters/Ipsos \u200cpoll published \u2060on Tuesday found a majority of Americans want the newly elected Democratic-majority US Congress, which takes office in January, to act to end the war with Iran once and for all.\n\nAlthough Vice President JD Vance has publicly questioned the war and blamed it for the Republicans' midterm rout, he voiced support for Hegseth on Tuesday.\n\n\"This isn't about Iran, this is about the fact that Pete took on the bureaucracy at the Pentagon,\" Vance wrote on social media.\n\nBefore the vote, House Democratic leader Hakeem Jeffries said on Monday: \"Pete Hegseth has abused his power, undermined America's national security, and he's the architect of the reckless and costly failed war \u200bof choice in Iran.\"\n\nHegseth has repeatedly defended the \u200bconflict, calling Trump courageous and saying Iran's military \u2060has been left in ruins. He also said his orders to US forces were lawful, despite Massie's accusations of \"high crimes and misdemeanors\".\n\n\"History will judge the Iran war quite differently than the conventional wisdom right now,\" Hegseth said on Tuesday at his first Pentagon briefing in six months."
