    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3a36052c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-11",
    "description": "High-Value Tourism - High Value for Whom?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/features/High-Value-Tourism-High-Value-for-Whom/185-354633",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794372925.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "be4e25b37cb578a66280c0d838d53681",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "High-Value Tourism - High Value for Whom?\n\nRecently at the World Travel & Tourism Council Global Summit, aptly known as \u201cthe Davos of travel and tourism,\u201d 1,000 tourism leaders from around the world came together and one thing that kept resonating over these days in Valletta Malta was the idea of \u201cvalue over volume\u201d when it comes to tourism. This cry is also the latest concern in Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism industry, that earnings are failing to keep pace with visitor numbers. \u201cVolume up, value down\u201d is the lament, followed by calls to move \u201cbeyond arrivals\u201d and attract high-spending tourists. This deserves attention, although more visitors do not necessarily produce a stronger tourism economy, particularly when businesses are discounting heavily and destinations are struggling with congestion.\n\nHaving spent some of my working life managing hotels, I understand the importance of obtaining a decent rate. Without adequate returns, a hotel cannot maintain its property, retain good staff or improve the experience it offers. My concern is with the rather easy assumption that attracting a higher-spending visitor will automatically produce a better outcome for Sri Lanka. A better room rate is welcome, but how much of that additional income reaches the people and businesses around the hotel, and how much remains in the country?\n\nUnless we understand this distinction, we may simply replace the arrival target with an earnings target, and continue celebrating the headline without examining what lies behind it.\n\nWe tend to use \u201chigh spending\u201d, \u201chigh end\u201d and \u201chigh value\u201d as though they mean the same thing. An expensive hotel may offer excellent accommodation and service, and its guests may spend generously. But from the country\u2019s point of view, the question extends beyond the amount on the bill. It includes how much remains in the economy, who benefits from it and what demands the visit places on the destination."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3a36052c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-11",
    "description": "High-Value Tourism - High Value for Whom?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/features/High-Value-Tourism-High-Value-for-Whom/185-354633",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794372925.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "77bb7f2f6a2160d1d8ee5348a1a0e31c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1480,
    "chunk_text": "We tend to use \u201chigh spending\u201d, \u201chigh end\u201d and \u201chigh value\u201d as though they mean the same thing. An expensive hotel may offer excellent accommodation and service, and its guests may spend generously. But from the country\u2019s point of view, the question extends beyond the amount on the bill. It includes how much remains in the economy, who benefits from it and what demands the visit places on the destination.\n\nThis is not an argument for one category of tourism over another. A large, professionally managed hotel can support substantial employment and a dependable network of domestic suppliers. A small establishment can offer poor working conditions, evade regulation and contribute little beyond the owner\u2019s household. Size and price alone tell us too little about the wider benefit.\n\nThe established sustainable tourism literature recognises this distinction. The UNEP\u2013UNWTO framework includes commercial viability, local prosperity, employment quality and social equity among its objectives. Business success matters, but it cannot stand as evidence that the other objectives have also been met. ( https://www.un.org/sustainabledevelopment/)\n\nBefore concluding that Sri Lanka is attracting the wrong type of tourist, it would be good to first be sure that \u00a0we are comparing the figures on the same basis. How much of the reported decline comes from changes in spending and length of stay, and how much comes from changes in the way earnings are estimated?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3a36052c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-11",
    "description": "High-Value Tourism - High Value for Whom?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/features/High-Value-Tourism-High-Value-for-Whom/185-354633",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794372925.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "af3d08ed75673ec43d9390c673b78007",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Before concluding that Sri Lanka is attracting the wrong type of tourist, it would be good to first be sure that \u00a0we are comparing the figures on the same basis. How much of the reported decline comes from changes in spending and length of stay, and how much comes from changes in the way earnings are estimated?\n\nReliance on sample-based surveys therefore raises questions about how fully different spending patterns are captured. These surveys need to be strengthened with systematically tracked tourist spending through credit-card or formal banking data. Mobile network big data (MNBD) has been introduced in some countries to help understand visitor movements. It can show where tourists go, but it cannot, on its own, tell us how much they spend.\n\nActual spending, length of stay, discounting and market composition need to be examined on a consistent basis. If visitors are staying fewer nights, the response may differ from that required when hotels are cutting rates. If tourists have money to spend, but find few worthwhile experiences to purchase, the problem lies partly in the product. Attributing every weakness to the wrong type of tourist allows the industry to avoid examining its own performance.\n\nThe concern that higher earnings may accrue mainly to established hotel groups and DMCs is a reality that needs careful attention. But it should not become an assumption that these businesses contribute little to the wider economy.\n\nA hotel\u2019s contribution extends well beyond the guests staying in it. A regular order for vegetables, fish or laundry services can provide a dependable income for a local business. A DMC can help a small experience provider reach overseas customers, manage bookings and deal with commercial risks that would otherwise be difficult to handle. These connections matter, but they have to be built and maintained. A higher room rate, by itself, tells us little about whether they are becoming stronger."
  },
