    "chunk_text": "A hotel\u2019s contribution extends well beyond the guests staying in it. A regular order for vegetables, fish or laundry services can provide a dependable income for a local business. A DMC can help a small experience provider reach overseas customers, manage bookings and deal with commercial risks that would otherwise be difficult to handle. These connections matter, but they have to be built and maintained. A higher room rate, by itself, tells us little about whether they are becoming stronger.\n\nTwo other issues need to be distinguished. The first is money leaving Sri Lanka through imports and overseas payments, which is international leakage. Offshore bookings and informal payment arrangements raise further questions about how much expenditure enters the domestic economy and how accurately it is recorded. But money outside the formal banking system is not necessarily money lost to Sri Lanka. These flows need to be examined before conclusions are drawn about their scale or economic effect.\n\nThe second issue is money remaining here but accruing disproportionately to a few owners, which is domestic concentration. A business can be entirely Sri Lankan-owned and still distribute its gains narrowly. There is nothing inherently wrong with profitable tourism enterprises. The policy question is whether publicly supported tourism growth creates opportunities beyond the main investors, and whether those opportunities offer worthwhile livelihoods.\n\nI sometimes wonder whether we confuse a more expensive tourism product with a more elaborate building. Sri Lanka does not need to compete everywhere through larger rooms, more marble and increasingly elaborate fittings. Comfort and good service matter, but so does a property\u2019s character, its setting and the experience offered around it. A well-designed place that feels Sri Lankan can have considerable appeal without becoming ostentatious."
  },
