    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794372925.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-3a36052c-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a470f362651929c26307466ecdd4f2fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1920,
    "chunk_text": "I sometimes wonder whether we confuse a more expensive tourism product with a more elaborate building. Sri Lanka does not need to compete everywhere through larger rooms, more marble and increasingly elaborate fittings. Comfort and good service matter, but so does a property\u2019s character, its setting and the experience offered around it. A well-designed place that feels Sri Lankan can have considerable appeal without becoming ostentatious.\n\nThe same discipline should apply to claims of low-impact luxury. A wealthy visitor is not necessarily a responsible visitor. Willingness to pay does not guarantee respect for wildlife, restrained resource use or consideration for residents. Visitor behaviour and destination management must be addressed directly, whatever the price category.\n\nPremium markets require confidence in the product, reliable service and effective distribution. They cannot be attracted simply by changing the language of a campaign.\n\nBefore looking for an entirely different visitor, there is work to be done with the tourism economy already here. Worthwhile experiences must be easy to find and book, guides need the training and remuneration to deliver them properly, and small suppliers must be able to meet the standards hotels require. Encouraging a villager to supply a hotel achieves little if the business cannot finance production or wait for its invoices to be settled.\n\nImproving these conditions may generate benefits sooner than waiting for an entirely different visitor mix, which is a slow process that has to be done by careful re-positioning and marketing. \u00a0Local purchasing requires attention to quality, volume and delivery. It may involve helping producers combine their supplies, improve food safety and gain access to working capital. Hotels cannot be expected to compromise on reliability or standards, but there is scope to help local businesses meet those requirements."
  },
