    "md5": "94ed546756252e3ec853d7ac2090dbcf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1712,
    "chunk_text": "Improving these conditions may generate benefits sooner than waiting for an entirely different visitor mix, which is a slow process that has to be done by careful re-positioning and marketing. \u00a0Local purchasing requires attention to quality, volume and delivery. It may involve helping producers combine their supplies, improve food safety and gain access to working capital. Hotels cannot be expected to compromise on reliability or standards, but there is scope to help local businesses meet those requirements.\n\nThis is also a question I am trying to address in the Managed Yield, Controlled Volume, Diversified Tourism (MYCVD) model approach I have been developing for Sri Lanka Tourism. The intention is to improve earnings through a better balance of visitors and experiences, while keeping volumes within manageable limits. (https://lnkd.in/p/g8ZgWrVe) \u00a0But increasing expenditure per visitor will not be enough if the additional income benefits only a narrow section of the industry. Managed yield must also consider what remains in Sri Lanka and how it reaches employees, suppliers and communities. Otherwise the model would leave unanswered the very question raised in this article.\n\nThe commercial viability of a tourism enterprise cannot be taken lightly. Equally, a national tourism policy must look beyond the hotel\u2019s own returns. The villager supplying its kitchen, the guide interpreting a site and the employee supporting a family, are all part of the same economy. Their gains need to be examined alongside the costs borne by the destination.\n\nUntil that becomes part of how we assess tourism, I would be cautious about calling a visitor \u201chigh value\u201d simply because the invoice is high."
