    "md5": "d003392e6e64da435dd4d98eea2d3e9a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1799,
    "chunk_text": "Lisan ud-Dawat:  The Language of the  Bohras  and how they  keep it alive\n\nTo any untrained ear, the language does sound like an Indian language. However, their conversation does not have the same musical melody of Hindi or Urdu, nor does it have the rhythm of Sri Lankan Muslim Tamil, and it does not have the pauses of Arabic either. Instead, Lisan ud-Dawat possesses a very distinctive acoustic vitality\n\nEveryone in Colombo recognizes the Dawoodi Bohras. Standing out in their distinctive attire, they are a familiar sight whether in the bustling bazaar of Pettah or drinking coffee in a hotel lobby. And if you happen to catch a fragment of their conversation, your ears will encounter a fascinating linguistic phenomenon: they speak a unique language - \u201cLisan ud-Dawat\u201d (literally meaning \u201clanguage of the community\u201d).\n\nTo any untrained ear, it does sound like an Indian language. However, their conversation does not have the same musical melody of Hindi or Urdu, nor does it have the rhythm of Sri Lankan Muslim Tamil, and it does not have the pauses of Arabic either. Instead, Lisan ud-Dawat possesses a very distinctive acoustic vitality.\n\nRashida Tajbhoy, a Mumbai-native member of the community now living in Sri Lanka, notes that while most linguistic sources debate whether it is an entirely different language or a dialect of Gujarati from northern India, it remains mutually intelligible. She explains that most standard Gujarati speakers can easily understand their dialogue, and vice versa.\n\nHowever, Lisan ud-Dawat possesses distinct elements influenced by Persian (Farsi), Urdu, and Arabic.\n\nDespite these influences, Lisan ud-Dawat has retained its distinct identity, largely because it continues to be spoken within Bohra households and passed down through generations."
  },
