    "md5": "09d8087ebc24596d466aefef6ad10892",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "However, Lisan ud-Dawat possesses distinct elements influenced by Persian (Farsi), Urdu, and Arabic.\n\nDespite these influences, Lisan ud-Dawat has retained its distinct identity, largely because it continues to be spoken within Bohra households and passed down through generations.\n\nThe language holds several \u201clinguistic fossils\u201d\u2014ancient features that faded from modern languages but remain used by Bohras. This includes the Persian ezafe used to link words (such as \u201cTarz-e-Jadeed\u201d, meaning novel approach) or suffixing Gujarati plural markers directly onto classical Arabic roots (such as adding the plural suffix \u201c-o\u201d to form \u201ckitabo\u201d for books). Another word that Bohras frequently use is \u201cohbat\u201d meaning preparation, which is not used in Gujarati.\n\nA passerby in the marketplace might be familiar hearing Bohras greeting each other with \u201cAap kem cho? Khuda nu shukr che.\u201d (How are you? Doing well by the grace of God), showcasing loanwords like \u201cKhuda\u201d or God (Persian) and \u201cshukr\u201d or thankful (Arabic).\n\nWords of Arabic origin, such as \u201cdaw\u0101\u201d (medicine), have become so embedded in Gujarati speech that they are used naturally in expressions such as \u201cdaw\u0101 ni duk\u0101n\u201d (medicine shop). These words reflect the different cultures and languages that have shaped Lisan ud-Dawat over centuries.\n\nThe community\u2019s deep historical roots link back to Arabic spoken in Egypt and Yemen, while Lisan ud-Dawat developed when Fatimid emissaries from Cairo first went to Gujarat, India around a thousand years ago.\n\nToday, this global community spans nearly one million people across the world, most being in India with over 2,000 members residing in Sri Lanka.\n\nWhen the global leader of the community, Syedna Mufaddal Saifuddin, visits Colombo, he is often seen on the local news. While onlookers frequently assume he is speaking a larger Middle Eastern or Indian language, he is actually speaking formal Lisan ud-Dawat."
  },
