    "chunk_size_bytes": 1685,
    "chunk_text": "When the global leader of the community, Syedna Mufaddal Saifuddin, visits Colombo, he is often seen on the local news. While onlookers frequently assume he is speaking a larger Middle Eastern or Indian language, he is actually speaking formal Lisan ud-Dawat.\n\nA family gathering might feature everyday terms unique to them, like \u201cthaal\u201d, the large, circular metal platter around which a group of eight sit to share a meal. Another word is \u201cwaaz\u201d \u2013 the religious discourses delivered by clergy.\n\nWhile religious sermons are delivered using formal terms, everyday speech incorporates the local vernacular. Tajbhoy notes that the local Bohras use Lankan words extensively, \u201cI\u2019ve heard all the specific Sri Lankan colloquial terms within the Colombo community when they chit-chat, like naturally dropping an \u201caiyo\u201d or an \u201caney\u201d when they are surprised or expressing sympathy\u201d. Naturally, modern vocabulary like words for \u201ccomputer\u201d or \u201cinternet\u201d are borrowed directly from English. \u201cWe are surrounded by so many other communities, and this naturally affects how we speak,\u201d she adds.\n\nSwitching languages seems to be second nature for most Bohras. Many attend English-medium international schools and are heavily engaged in business or other professional sectors, so most in the island speak fluent English and Sinhala and some are familiar with Tamil as well. So, whether a Bohra is studying engineering in English at Oxford, striking a business deal in Nairobi using Swahili, or trading in Pettah using Sinhala, switching to their native language is second nature. Tajbhoy attributes this linguistic resilience to a \u201cdeep sense of cultural pride.\u201d"
