    "md5": "064ed3a3c86ab72d3e412868eb5425b4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1798,
    "chunk_text": "Five-day business registration pilot launched\n\nColombo, Nov 11 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has launched a pilot project to simplify its 108-year-old business name registration process, enabling sole proprietorships and partnerships to complete registration within five working days.\n\nThe Simplified and Unified Business Name Registration (BNR) pilot project was launched in Anuradhapura yesterday (10) under the patronage of Deputy Minister of Industries and Entrepreneurship Development Chathuranga Abeysinghe.\n\nThe initiative is being implemented jointly by the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development, non-profit organisation Chrysalis and Provincial Councils.\n\nThe reform aims to replace the lengthy registration procedure, which has been in place since 1918, with a simplified and standardised system requiring only basic identity and residency information.\n\nUnder the previous system, applicants had to submit documents including proof of land ownership and obtain approvals from multiple government agencies, making the process time-consuming and complicated.\n\nThe new system separates business name registration from other regulatory licensing requirements, allowing applicants to complete the process within five working days.\n\nThe initial three-month pilot will cover 20 selected Divisional Secretariat divisions across the Central, Northern, North Central and North Western provinces.\n\nFollowing an evaluation of the pilot project, the Government plans to expand the system islandwide, with an online platform also expected to be introduced to facilitate digital registration.\n\nThe initiative is aimed at reducing administrative barriers for aspiring entrepreneurs and small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), while encouraging more businesses to enter the formal economy."
