    "md5": "4ec13c63eaaa1b8260c26d1f71282b2c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1778,
    "chunk_text": "Child dies after kidney surgery; both kidneys reportedly missing, Ministry faces questions\n\nColombo, November 11 (Daily Mirror) - Serious questions have been raised over the Ministry of Health\u2019s handling of the death of a child at the Lady Ridgeway Hospital for Children in Borella, after post-mortem findings reportedly revealed that both kidneys had been removed during surgery, with hospital authorities yet to produce the organs.\n\nDr. Chamal Sanjeewa, President of the Medical Professionals' Alliance for Medical and Civil Rights, has accused the Ministry of failing to take adequate action over the incident, which occurred nearly three years ago.\n\nThe child died following kidney surgery at the hospital. Subsequent post-mortem examinations reportedly revealed that both kidneys were missing, an issue which had also been raised by the child's parents before court.\n\nAccording to Dr. Sanjeewa, the child's kidneys were fused together and had been removed during the surgical procedure.\n\nDr. G. Wijesuriya, who was the hospital director at the time, had initially explained that the second kidney had also been removed because it was attached to the kidney that surgeons intended to remove.\n\nHowever, the parents had disputed this explanation, claiming that medical examinations conducted before the child was admitted to the hospital had confirmed that the two kidneys were located separately within the abdominal cavity.\n\nThe child's death and the subsequent post-mortem findings led to serious concerns, particularly over the failure of hospital authorities to produce the removed kidneys.\n\nThe matter was also raised before court, while steps were reportedly taken at the time to suspend the Judicial Medical Officer who conducted the initial post-mortem examination."
