    "md5": "28b74b4f5798cf55c3c19e714c12a70b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "Good data required  when modelling  forecasts into the future - Dr. Thomas Adams -\n\nDr. Adams at the workshop titled \u2018Flood forecasting on a shoestring budget\u2019\n\nPeople will pay attention to things that they feel are important to them. People are used to seeing the world in a certain way. When you provide them with more information,\u00a0 it just opens up a world of possibilities to them\n\nThere\u2019s one professor at a university in Thailand who has taken charge of this system from one river base,\u00a0 and it\u2019s very important in the region. He has developed the system and has it fully operational right now\n\nTimely and accurate flood forecasting minimises economic and humanitarian costs. Recently, American flood forecasting expert Dr. Thomas Adams visited Sri Lanka to share U.S. expertise and introduce innovative forecasting technology.\n\nDr. Adams holds a Ph.D. in Civil Engineering from Virginia Tech, specialising in water resources and hydrologic prediction. His extensive career includes previous positions with National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\u2019s (NOAA) National Weather Service, including its Hydrologic Research Laboratory and Ohio River Forecast Centre, as well as a previous appointment as a visiting scientist at NOAA\u2019s National Water Centre. He has also worked internationally on flood forecasting and water resources projects in China, Pakistan, Vietnam, Panama, and Romania, including consulting assignments with the World Bank.\n\nDuring his visit, he conducted a country specific training on \u2018Flood Forecasting on a Shoestring Budget\u2019 for officials at the Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock, Land and Irrigation. In addition, engineers, directors, senior lecturers, hydrologists, geologists and scientists from Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation (SLLDC), National Building Research Organisation (NBRO), Regional Integrated Multi-Hazard Early Warning System (RIMES), Open University of Sri Lanka (OUSL) and Mahaweli Authority participated at the technical workshop."
  },
  {
