    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-7faa6b46-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "268857a44f35ff602dcb944674b2fb60",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "During his visit, he conducted a country specific training on \u2018Flood Forecasting on a Shoestring Budget\u2019 for officials at the Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock, Land and Irrigation. In addition, engineers, directors, senior lecturers, hydrologists, geologists and scientists from Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation (SLLDC), National Building Research Organisation (NBRO), Regional Integrated Multi-Hazard Early Warning System (RIMES), Open University of Sri Lanka (OUSL) and Mahaweli Authority participated at the technical workshop.\n\nQ Could you tell us about the training you conducted here in Sri Lanka?\n\nI\u2019m working on a project called \u2018Flood forecasting on a shoestring budget.\u2019 Most countries in the world do not have any kind of flood forecasting capability whatsoever. It\u2019s especially bad for instance in Africa and there just isn\u2019t the data infrastructure to do flood forecasting. Even in some modern countries the process is fairly rudimentary.\n\nIn the past,\u00a0 a lot of countries that had been in that kind of position or had some measure of flood forecasting capability wanted to modernise what they had. But they are relatively poor and entities like the World Bank or Asian Development Bank would offer loans for developing and installing a very modernised system. That would cost tens of millions of dollars that would have to be paid in time. A couple of years ago,\u00a0 we came up with the idea of using freely available data, computer models and other resources to bring flood forecasting capabilities to low and middle income countries at no cost to them. The catch is that the way we had structured it now is to help the countries use these existing models and data to develop the system, but they have to do the work."
  },
