    "md5": "204bade606263a8c89add1e4a690131c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1793,
    "chunk_text": "In the past,\u00a0 a lot of countries that had been in that kind of position or had some measure of flood forecasting capability wanted to modernise what they had. But they are relatively poor and entities like the World Bank or Asian Development Bank would offer loans for developing and installing a very modernised system. That would cost tens of millions of dollars that would have to be paid in time. A couple of years ago,\u00a0 we came up with the idea of using freely available data, computer models and other resources to bring flood forecasting capabilities to low and middle income countries at no cost to them. The catch is that the way we had structured it now is to help the countries use these existing models and data to develop the system, but they have to do the work.\n\nI have a similar project going on in Pakistan and I have a colleague in Romania. We had a training workshop in Bangkok in June this year which kick-started this initiative. That\u2019s what got Sri Lanka involved because there were Sri Lanka representatives who attended this workshop. So it\u2019s in a number of places. There\u2019s one professor at a university in Thailand who has taken charge of this system from one river base,\u00a0 and\u00a0 it\u2019s very important in the region.\n\nHe has developed the system and has it fully operational right now. A major advantage of this system is that right now in countries like Sri Lanka and many others, they basically can only provide a one-day lead time forecast in a qualitative sense about the possibility of flooding. What this system does is that it gives you at least 10 days lead time. All of this is done through the irrigation department which is responsible for flood forecasting here in Sri Lanka.\n\nQ\u00a0\u00a0 Was it the first time that this kind of\u00a0 training was done here?"
