    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "80e533afdeb57640318823cce8b6556b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "He has developed the system and has it fully operational right now. A major advantage of this system is that right now in countries like Sri Lanka and many others, they basically can only provide a one-day lead time forecast in a qualitative sense about the possibility of flooding. What this system does is that it gives you at least 10 days lead time. All of this is done through the irrigation department which is responsible for flood forecasting here in Sri Lanka.\n\nQ\u00a0\u00a0 Was it the first time that this kind of\u00a0 training was done here?\n\nBut they have a footprint in South Asia and into Africa. They have been an integral part of this initiative. In fact, RIMES hosted the workshop that took place in Bangkok back in June. Hats off to the irrigation department for their involvement in doing this. Not all countries have been willing to entertain the possibility of taking this kind of work on. It\u2019s a big step.\n\nThe training is to help them understand this concept of flood forecasting on a shoestring budget system. A lot of documentation goes into this,\u00a0 and it\u2019s to help them understand all the various components. How they work together and what\u2019s needed to be done to implement it. There were people outside of the irrigation department who attended the training as well. One person for instance was interested in applying the system to forecast and identify potential landslide areas and the effects. That\u2019s outside the original intent but the system is fully capable to aid him in that capacity.\n\nQ\u00a0 How important is flood forecasting for an island nation like Sri Lanka?"
  },
