    "md5": "f8cb0363176d11e8f1dd98ad1003874c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1748,
    "chunk_text": "The training is to help them understand this concept of flood forecasting on a shoestring budget system. A lot of documentation goes into this,\u00a0 and it\u2019s to help them understand all the various components. How they work together and what\u2019s needed to be done to implement it. There were people outside of the irrigation department who attended the training as well. One person for instance was interested in applying the system to forecast and identify potential landslide areas and the effects. That\u2019s outside the original intent but the system is fully capable to aid him in that capacity.\n\nQ\u00a0 How important is flood forecasting for an island nation like Sri Lanka?\n\nIt\u2019s very accurate but the issue is when you\u2019re forecasting today out into the future there\u2019s a lot of uncertainty. When you say accuracy,\u00a0 it kind of takes on a different meaning. You have to look at it probabilistically. For instance, if I told you that your house was going to flood tomorrow and water would get one meter deep, I would be lying. Because I don\u2019t have that level of information. But what I can do is say that there\u2019s a 30% chance that water could get to one meter deep in your house. There\u2019s a big difference and that would be a true statement.\n\nQ\u00a0\u00a0 How important is the communication aspect when it comes to natural disasters?\n\nIt\u2019s very important. I could have a perfect system giving a perfect forecast. But if you don\u2019t understand it, you don\u2019t know what\u2019s going to happen. You also need to get it in a timely way. It\u2019s not only about the quality of the forecast, but there are other components that are very critical.\n\nQ\u00a0\u00a0 Sri Lankans mainly speak Sinhala and Tamil. Could this software be used in other languages as well?"
  },
