    "chunk_id": "2026-11-11-dailymirrorlk-9e2c86c9-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d9dd8fe93c318a4025c00d4ae537aad7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "EFC launches book on \u2018Collective Bargaining and Agreements in Sri Lanka\u2019\n\nThe latest publication by the Employers\u2019 Federation of Ceylon (EFC) \u2018Collective Bargaining and Agreements in Sri Lanka\u2019 (Volume 1) aims to provide a detailed overview of the collective agreements signed from 2022 to 2024 between EFC member companies and trade unions. The publication is the fruit of the research efforts by the EFC\u2019s Research and Publications Unit.\n\nThe EFC which is the national employers\u2019 organization and the constituent of the International Labour Organisation (ILO)in Sri Lanka, has consistently pursued its vision to promote industrial harmony through productive employment.\u00a0 Through its proactive engagement with trade unions and government bodies, the EFC has fostered a collaborative approach to industrial relations, promoting collective bargaining as means of conflict resolution which has ensured long-term industrial harmony. Now into its 97th year and representing a diverse set of industries with over 800,000 employees in Sri Lanka, the EFC has established itself as a significant pillar in industrial relations platforms in Sri Lanka.\n\nCollective bargaining process is a tool that can be used to create and enhance industrial relations between the employer and the employees. The foundation of the collective bargaining in Sri Lanka is a combination of statutory provisions, established practices/customs and mutual agreements. The mechanism provides a formal platform for discussion/ negotiations between employer and employee.\n\nThrough such discussions and negotiations, the employees collectively or through trade unions will have the opportunity to table their concerns while the employers will have the opportunity to put forward their expectations. This will create a relationship that is more transparent and it will further reduce the communication gap between the employer and the employee."
  },
