    "md5": "d8518648641bbb25da457b1638a256b0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1898,
    "chunk_text": "Approves US$ 98mn loan to advance digital transformation\n\nThe Asian Development Bank (ADB) has approved a US$98 million loan to support Sri Lanka\u2019s efforts to build an inclusive, secure, and resilient digital economy, enhance public service delivery, and strengthen the foundations for private sector-led growth.\n\nThe Advancing Digital Transformation Project will help expand high-quality broadband connectivity and improve government data infrastructure, while reinforcing institutional capacity for cybersecurity and data protection and enhancing digital skills across the public sector.\n\nThe project aims to boost digital inclusion and equity by expanding access to digital government services for citizens and businesses, particularly women and underserved communities. ADB is providing an additional $1 million technical assistance grant from its Technical Assistance Special Fund to support program preparation, design, and capacity building for the executing and implementing agencies.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka has made strong progress in stabilizing its economy and is now prioritizing digital transformation as a key driver of inclusive growth and productivity,\u201d said ADB Country Director for Sri Lanka Shannon Cowlin. \u201cThis project will help bridge connectivity gaps, improve the reliability and security of digital systems, and strengthen institutions to deliver better services to citizens and businesses.\u201d\n\nDespite high mobile coverage, access to reliable, high-speed broadband remains uneven, particularly in rural and underserved areas. Gaps in digital and data infrastructure, cybersecurity, data protection, and skills continue to constrain the delivery of efficient public services and limit private sector development. The project will address these challenges through a comprehensive, whole-of-government approach aligned with the government\u2019s National Digital Economy Blueprint."
  },
