    "md5": "64ea280ad305a7ad1f6d1ae528303cc4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1665,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP growth can mask loss of natural wealth: Statistics Chief\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economy can grow even as the country depletes the forests, water resources and fertile soils that support key industries, the head of its statistics department said, outlining an initiative to track those losses alongside gross domestic product.\n\nUpper watersheds and coastal and marine ecosystems will be the initial focus of the initiative, Department of Census and Statistics Director General U.V. Jayakody said at a workshop organised by the Centre for Poverty Analysis with support from the Asian Development Bank.\n\nJayakody cited timber harvesting as an example of what GDP can miss. The value of harvested timber counts towards economic output, while the depletion of the forest is only partly reflected in conventional statistics.\n\n\u201cConsequently, the country may experience economic growth while its natural wealth is gradually declining,\u201d he said.\n\nThe proposed natural capital accounts would track changes in the availability and condition of resources and measure the services they provide. Watershed accounts could help assess the contribution of those ecosystems to water supplies, agriculture and hydropower generation. Coastal and marine accounts could do the same for fisheries, tourism and coastal protection.\n\nThe accounts would complement existing national accounts under a United Nations framework that combines environmental and economic data. Jayakody said physical measures of the condition of natural assets would be as important as monetary estimates. The data could inform development planning, investment decisions and environmental protection."
  },
