    "md5": "72317063f0b33d04b2a7749f3ef31960",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "ADB urges Sri Lanka to put natural assets on the investment map\n\nCountry Director Shannon Cowlin says ecosystem data can help Treasury weigh competing demands on public funds\n\nADB studies how upper watersheds support water security, hydropower and farm output\n\nValuing the benefits of restoration could help draw climate finance and private capital\n\nSri Lanka should factor the economic contribution of its forests, watersheds and coastal ecosystems into decisions on where to spend scarce public funds, Asian Development Bank (ADB) Country Director Shannon Cowlin said.\n\nShe noted the services these assets provide to agriculture, fisheries, hydropower and tourism were only partly reflected in the information used to guide economic planning and investment.\n\n\u201cFor policymakers, natural capital accounting has value in its practical application. It helps strengthen evidence-based budget allocation, improve public investment planning, assess trade-offs among competing development priorities, and support the identification and design of investments that generate lasting economic, social, and environmental benefits,\u201d she said while speaking at the ADB\u2013CEPA Natural Capital Accounting Workshop.\n\nBetter data could also help ADB and the Government, she said, identify which natural assets are most important to the economy, where degradation poses the greatest risk and which investments would deliver the highest returns.\n\nADB is supporting analytical work under the proposed Sri Lanka Upper Watershed Ecological Restoration Program. The work examines how watershed ecosystems contribute to water security, hydropower generation and farm productivity, as well as their role in conserving biodiversity, strengthening climate resilience and reducing disaster risk. The aim is to inform future policy and investment decisions.\n\n\u201cOne of the challenges in mobilizing investment for ecosystem restoration and nature-positive development is demonstrating economic value,\u201d Cowlin said."
  },
