    "chunk_size_bytes": 1130,
    "chunk_text": "ADB is supporting analytical work under the proposed Sri Lanka Upper Watershed Ecological Restoration Program. The work examines how watershed ecosystems contribute to water security, hydropower generation and farm productivity, as well as their role in conserving biodiversity, strengthening climate resilience and reducing disaster risk. The aim is to inform future policy and investment decisions.\n\n\u201cOne of the challenges in mobilizing investment for ecosystem restoration and nature-positive development is demonstrating economic value,\u201d Cowlin said.\n\nNatural capital accounting brings environmental and economic data together under a United Nations framework. It tracks the condition of natural assets and the services they provide, giving policymakers a basis for assessing how changes in those assets could affect the economy and livelihoods.\n\nCowlin linked the work to Sri Lanka\u2019s efforts to build sources of growth beyond the recovery. She said putting the framework into practice would require institutions to work together, build technical capacity and commit to using the data in budget and investment decisions."
