    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "COPF questions banks\u2019 lending priorities\n\nColombo, Nov. 11 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Credit extended to Sri Lanka's private sector reached Rs. 2.1 trillion during the first nine months of 2026, up from Rs. 1.9 trillion during the corresponding period last year, Central Bank officials told the Committee on Public Finance (COPF).\n\nThe increase in private sector lending, inflation targeting and the stability of the banking system were among the key issues discussed at a recent COPF meeting held in Parliament under the chairmanship of MP Dr. Harsha de Silva.\n\nCentral Bank Governor Dr. P. Nandalal Weerasinghe and other senior Central Bank officials attended the meeting, along with Deputy Minister Chathuranga Abeysinghe and several MPs.\n\nDuring the meeting, officials explained the Central Bank's decision to maintain the headline inflation target, based on the Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI), at 5% over the next three years.\n\nThey also revealed that seven special reports had been submitted to Parliament on occasions when inflation remained below the lower limit of the target range of 3%.\n\nOf the Rs. 2.1 trillion in private sector credit extended during the first nine months of this year, approximately 74% had been provided by licensed commercial banks.\n\nHowever, the Committee raised concerns over changes in the composition of lending, noting that the share of short-term loans had increased from 42% to 47%, while the share of long-term loans had declined from 35% to 29%.\n\nThe Committee also questioned whether bank lending was being sufficiently directed towards capital formation, new investments and the expansion of production capacity. The growth of different loan categories, including mortgage and vehicle loans, was also discussed.\n\nThe country's long-term economic growth potential was another key area of discussion, with emphasis placed on the need for reforms in the trade, labour and land sectors alongside appropriate monetary policies."
