    "chunk_size_bytes": 1331,
    "chunk_text": "The country's long-term economic growth potential was another key area of discussion, with emphasis placed on the need for reforms in the trade, labour and land sectors alongside appropriate monetary policies.\n\nProviding an update on financial system stability, Central Bank officials said the banking sector's Stage 3 non-performing loan ratio had declined from 9.7% to 8.8%, while capital and liquidity levels remained above regulatory requirements.\n\nRegarding NDB Bank, officials informed the Committee that the forensic audit conducted in relation to the bank, together with the report of the Sri Lanka Accounting and Auditing Standards Monitoring Board, had been referred to the Attorney General on October 15, 2026.\n\nThey said regulatory measures had also been imposed on the bank, including restrictions on dividend distributions, branch expansion and directors' remuneration.\n\nThe Committee also discussed institutions illegally accepting public deposits and financial scams involving the use of artificial intelligence to create fake images and voices of the Central Bank Governor, the Prime Minister and MPs.\n\nCentral Bank officials said measures were being taken in coordination with the Telecommunications Regulatory Commission, Sri Lanka Computer Emergency Readiness Team (CERT), Police and Meta to prevent such scams."
