    "chunk_text": "WindForce posts strong 1H growth, accelerates renewable energy expansion\n\nWindForce PLC recorded solid financial growth during the first half of FY2026/27, while advancing major solar, wind and battery energy storage investments that will significantly expand its renewable energy portfolio.\n\nConsolidated revenue for the six months ended 30 September 2026 increased to Rs. 5.61 billion from Rs. 4.68 billion a year earlier. Gross profit rose to Rs. 3.21 billion, while profit after tax reached Rs. 1.86 billion, compared with Rs. 1.77 billion in the corresponding period. The receipt of the first $ 5 million tranche of the company\u2019s $ 18 million facility with the International Finance Corporation (IFC), a member of the World Bank Group, further strengthened access to long-term international capital.\n\nThese results were delivered despite renewable energy curtailments that caused an estimated Rs. 193.57 million in foregone revenue during the period. Although the Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka has approved a compensation methodology and directed the National System Operator to compensate generators based on applicable energy purchase tariffs, discussions remain ongoing and the timing and recovery of compensation are uncertain.\n\nIn September 2026, the 100 MW Rividhanavi Solar Park at Siyambalanduwa achieved financial closure, securing Rs. 22.736 billion and the rupee equivalent of $ 33 million in syndicated term loan and bank guarantee facilities. The consortium was led by Sampath Bank PLC, with Commercial Bank of Ceylon PLC, National Development Bank PLC and Nations Trust Bank PLC, enabling the project to progress into construction.\n\nThe Company also commissioned the 5 MW Diya Janani floating solar project at Dambulu Oya Reservoir in September. As one of Sri Lanka\u2019s pioneering private-sector floating solar developments, the project demonstrates WindForce\u2019s commitment to innovative, land-efficient renewable energy solutions."
  },
