    "language": "en",
    "md5": "caa623b0a55f571e5faf691c82704203",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1739,
    "chunk_text": "These capitals are not unrelated or independent resources; they reinforce one another and collectively shape a nation\u2019s ability to produce goods and services, build resilience, and support well-being. The IECF can be understood as the seeds of economic growth and sustainable development. Just as seeds require suitable conditions to grow, Government and the private sector should invest continuously in the formation of these capitals. Effective institutions, scientific knowledge, and community participation are needed to deliver lasting economic and social benefits. In fact, institutional capital can serve as a catalyst for capital.\n\nFrom this perspective, the forthcoming Budget should not be regarded simply as an annual statement of Government expenditure and revenue. It should also be viewed as a strategic instrument for developing the country\u2019s eight capitals. This framework does not essentially require higher public expenditure. Instead, it demands more effective coordination, allocation, and evaluation of available resources. Public investment by the Government should be assessed by its contribution to capital formation, productivity, social inclusion, environmental sustainability, and long-term development outcomes.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s five national initiatives present an opportunity to adopt an integrated approach. By aligning their shared objectives with the Eight-Capital Framework, the Government could establish a common basis for planning, Budgeting, implementation, and monitoring. The fundamental question for the national Budget should therefore be: How effectively will public resources be allocated to strengthen the eight capitals underpinning Sri Lanka\u2019s sustainable economic growth and development?"
  },
  {
