    "chunk_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-01e9e5a4-0008",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "880856ec52810e4dc1427d6894a707ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s five national initiatives present an opportunity to adopt an integrated approach. By aligning their shared objectives with the Eight-Capital Framework, the Government could establish a common basis for planning, Budgeting, implementation, and monitoring. The fundamental question for the national Budget should therefore be: How effectively will public resources be allocated to strengthen the eight capitals underpinning Sri Lanka\u2019s sustainable economic growth and development?\n\nNormally, a Budget has two expenditure components: current expenditure and capital expenditure. If the Government allocates funds for investment that strengthens capital, one can claim the Budget is capital-based or development-oriented. A Budget proposal should also include measurable returns or quantifiable indicators for performance appraisal.\n\nIn this context, the writer would introduce Integrated Eight-Capital Budgeting (IECB) as an analytical framework. Under this framework, the Government could classify its development expenditure by the primary capital it strengthens, identify secondary capital benefits, and set measurable indicators for expected outcomes.\n\nFor example, investment in irrigation infrastructure would primarily strengthen physical capital, but could also improve natural capital through better water management, economic capital through increased agricultural productivity, and social capital through collective irrigation governance. This approach would help policymakers understand the broader developmental returns on public investment. The writer does not suggest that IECF replace prevailing systems of Government accounting, Budgeting, or fiscal reporting. Instead, it should supplement them by introducing a development-oriented perspective into Government resource allocation, monitoring, and evaluation."
  },
