    "md5": "43c6471379ed0017ecc02700d362064c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1250,
    "chunk_text": "For example, investment in irrigation infrastructure would primarily strengthen physical capital, but could also improve natural capital through better water management, economic capital through increased agricultural productivity, and social capital through collective irrigation governance. This approach would help policymakers understand the broader developmental returns on public investment. The writer does not suggest that IECF replace prevailing systems of Government accounting, Budgeting, or fiscal reporting. Instead, it should supplement them by introducing a development-oriented perspective into Government resource allocation, monitoring, and evaluation.\n\nFrom an economic perspective, our argument is particularly interesting because conventional growth models implemented in Sri Lanka so far emphasise labour, human capital, physical capital, natural capital, and technological progress. Our proposed IECF extends the analysis by explicitly incorporating economic, cultural, social, and institutional capitals. The IECF can be used as the seeds for growth and sustainable development at community, divisional, district, provincial and national levels to integrate the activities and funding of all five-level initiatives and beyond."
