    "chunk_text": "We opened the door to trade first. Then we stood still\n\nIn 1977, Sri Lanka was among the developing world\u2019s pioneers in open trade and outward orientation. Viet Nam and Bangladesh followed much later, yet both have since moved far ahead. Understanding why matters if Sri Lanka is to recover the promise of those early reforms\n\nIn 1977, Sri Lanka took a bold step. At a time when most developing Asia remained suspicious of international markets, Sri Lanka dismantled import controls, unified the exchange rate, welcomed foreign investors and established the Greater Colombo Economic Commission, including the country\u2019s first export processing zone at Katunayake.\n\nThe response was immediate. Exports, which had contracted during the closed-economy years of the early 1970s, rose to around 30% of GDP by the early 1980s. Sri Lanka briefly became an example to the developing world: a small island prepared to earn its place in the global economy.\n\nAlmost half a century later, an uncomfortable question remains. If Sri Lanka opened its economy first, how did it fall so far behind?\n\nThe contrast is shocking. In 1990, Sri Lanka, Viet Nam and Bangladesh each exported roughly $2 billion in goods annually. Viet Nam was a war-scarred, centrally planned economy only four years into its Doi Moi reforms. Bangladesh was among the poorest countries in the world. Sri Lanka, by comparison, had a thirteen-year head start, a highly literate workforce and a position beside one of the world\u2019s busiest shipping lanes."
