    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "The contrast is shocking. In 1990, Sri Lanka, Viet Nam and Bangladesh each exported roughly $2 billion in goods annually. Viet Nam was a war-scarred, centrally planned economy only four years into its Doi Moi reforms. Bangladesh was among the poorest countries in the world. Sri Lanka, by comparison, had a thirteen-year head start, a highly literate workforce and a position beside one of the world\u2019s busiest shipping lanes.\n\nThe more troubling comparison is not with Viet Nam or Bangladesh, but with Sri Lanka\u2019s own past. In 2000, exports amounted to one-third of GDP. By 2021, goods exports had fallen below 15%, approaching the levels seen during the inward-looking early 1970s that the 1977 reforms were intended to leave behind.\n\nThe economy continued to expand, but growth increasingly relied on borrowing, construction, and domestic consumption rather than income earned abroad. The consequences were painfully clear in 2022, when Sri Lanka ran out of foreign currency to pay for fuel, medicine, and food.\n\nThe problem was not openness or outward-orientation itself. Every economy that has moved from poverty to prosperity without relying on oil or mineral wealth, including Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Malaysia and, more recently, Viet Nam, has done so through exports. A country of 22 million people cannot become prosperous by selling mainly to its own consumers.\n\nThe new policy was good. What failed was Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to sustain it.\n\nSri Lanka stopped reforming. Vietnam and Bangladesh began later, but they continued. Sri Lanka\u2019s liberalisation lost momentum and eventually reversed. Successive Governments introduced para-tariffs such as CESS and the Ports and Airports Development Levy. These measures raised revenue and protected favored industries while creating an anti-export bias."
