    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka stopped reforming. Vietnam and Bangladesh began later, but they continued. Sri Lanka\u2019s liberalisation lost momentum and eventually reversed. Successive Governments introduced para-tariffs such as CESS and the Ports and Airports Development Levy. These measures raised revenue and protected favored industries while creating an anti-export bias.\n\nSri Lanka also remained largely outside global value chains. Modern trade rarely involves producing an entire product within one country. It is built around specialised tasks performed across networks of factories, suppliers and markets in several countries.\n\nViet Nam understood this shift. It actively attracted multinational manufacturers, entered trade agreements with Europe, its Pacific partners and neighboring Asian economies, and made itself one of the region\u2019s most attractive locations for electronics assembly. Foreign-invested companies now account for about 77% of Viet Nam\u2019s exports.\n\nSri Lanka attracted too little foreign investment directed towards exports. It entered relatively few trade agreements outside South Asia and continued to depend heavily on garments, tea and rubber, much as it had a generation earlier.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s export base remained narrow. For decades after independence, tea, rubber and coconut supplied much of the country\u2019s export income. Their prices were volatile and tended to decline relative to the cost of the goods Sri Lanka needed to import.\n\nGarments eventually widened the export base, but much of the subsequent growth came from selling larger quantities of familiar products rather than developing successful new ones. Exporters experimented with hundreds of products, but few achieved lasting commercial success. At the same time, factories imported much of the fabric, machinery, and other components they used. A significant portion of each export dollar therefore flowed back out of the country, limiting the value retained at home."
