    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "Garments eventually widened the export base, but much of the subsequent growth came from selling larger quantities of familiar products rather than developing successful new ones. Exporters experimented with hundreds of products, but few achieved lasting commercial success. At the same time, factories imported much of the fabric, machinery, and other components they used. A significant portion of each export dollar therefore flowed back out of the country, limiting the value retained at home.\n\nTrade relationships were also limited. Competitors entered deep, reciprocal agreements across Asia and with Europe. Sri Lanka concluded only one agreement outside South Asia (Thailand). Most of them, if not all, were ineffective. Even the free trade agreement with India remained underused and unbalanced. Most Governments feared opening to such relationships. Even if Governments were in favor of free trade agreements, such initiatives were strongly opposed by self-interested political parties. Instead, exporters relied heavily on one-way preferences such as the European Union\u2019s GSP+. These arrangements are useful, but they are conditional and can be withdrawn, as Sri Lanka discovered between 2010 and 2017.\n\nMost importantly, Sri Lanka never developed a durable national trade agenda. In Viet Nam and Bangladesh, export growth became a national undertaking that survived changes in political leadership. In Sri Lanka, trade was often portrayed as a threat to employment, farmers, and national sovereignty, rather than as a means of creating more productive and better-paid jobs. Without broad public support, reform remained politically vulnerable. For Governments, introducing another levy was easier than making the case for competition and an open market."
