    "chunk_text": "Most importantly, Sri Lanka never developed a durable national trade agenda. In Viet Nam and Bangladesh, export growth became a national undertaking that survived changes in political leadership. In Sri Lanka, trade was often portrayed as a threat to employment, farmers, and national sovereignty, rather than as a means of creating more productive and better-paid jobs. Without broad public support, reform remained politically vulnerable. For Governments, introducing another levy was easier than making the case for competition and an open market.\n\nBehind all these failures lies a deeper reason. Sri Lanka has never had a national trade policy that was actually in force: a single framework, backed by all parties, that sets the direction of trade and holds every other instrument to it. Without such a policy, tariffs followed the Treasury\u2019s revenue needs, trade agreements were pursued or shelved by whichever ministry held the file, and export and investment promotion each ran on separate plans."
