    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "Behind all these failures lies a deeper reason. Sri Lanka has never had a national trade policy that was actually in force: a single framework, backed by all parties, that sets the direction of trade and holds every other instrument to it. Without such a policy, tariffs followed the Treasury\u2019s revenue needs, trade agreements were pursued or shelved by whichever ministry held the file, and export and investment promotion each ran on separate plans.\n\nThe gap matters even more today. The National Tariff Policy is a welcome step, but a tariff policy is an instrument, not a strategy. It cannot decide which trade agreements Sri Lanka should pursue, how to secure membership of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP), how to respond when the United States changes its tariffs on our goods, or how exporters will meet Europe\u2019s new carbon and deforestation rules. After all, trade policy goes beyond tariffs and encompasses a spectrum of instruments: WTO agreements, regional and bilateral agreements, trade facilitation, GVCs, the trade-FDI nexus, trade-related legal and regulatory measures, and coherence with macroeconomic policies, which are interconnected. Unfortunately, many, including those who advise Governments, think trade policy is just revisions to the import tariff structure.\n\nThe National Tariff Policy proposes a simpler four-band structure of 0, 10, 20 and 30%. The Government has committed to eliminating CESS and the Ports and Airports Development Levy, with the phased removal of CESS due to begin in 2028. The World Bank estimates that removing para-tariffs could increase household consumption by an average of 3.1%, with the poorest households receiving the greatest gains. A National Export Development Plan for 2026 to 2030 has also been prepared, and exporters\u2019 associations have supported the reforms. But without a national trade policy above them, these remain separate instruments, vulnerable to the next budget or the next change of Government."
