    "md5": "20d5ba5e303c7f92811b23e702c47df5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1963,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: Need to revisit APIT\n\nThere is a fundamental principle that deserves greater consideration when determining the taxation of employment income.\n\nA salary is the remuneration an individual receives in return for services rendered. It represents the agreed compensation for the employee\u2019s time, knowledge, skills, experience, responsibilities and contribution to the organisation. An employee earns what he or she receives through the service provided. The amount of remuneration may differ according to professional qualifications, experience, the nature of responsibilities and the value attached to the service. However, the fundamental character of that income remains the same. It is remuneration earned through employment.\n\nWhy should the nature and tax treatment of such earnings be progressively altered merely because the amount of remuneration is higher?\n\nRecognising differences in salaries is one thing; using those differences as the sole basis for progressively changing the tax treatment of employment remuneration is another. The argument is not that all employees should receive equal salaries or that higher earnings should be exempt from taxation. It is that remuneration earned through employment should be recognised as a distinct category of income and subjected to a consistent and predictable tax principle. The fact that an individual earns a higher salary does not change the fundamental nature of that income as remuneration for services rendered. This distinction deserves recognition in the design of a fair taxation system.\n\nIn the light of these considerations, I propose that employment income be treated as a distinct category for personal income taxation, with a simpler and more predictable method of assessment. The proposed arrangement would rest on three basic principles:\n\nA common tax-free threshold: Every employee should enjoy the common personal tax-free threshold applicable under the general tax framework."
