    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Prayer for better life from nation that has endured much\n\nBeyond economic statistics and promises, the people of Sri Lanka are looking for a realistic Budget that restores hope, creates opportunities and makes everyday life less difficult.\n\nToday, 12 November, 2026, Parliament will witness the presentation of the National Budget for 2027 by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake in his capacity as Minister of Finance. It will be the Government\u2019s third Budget, presented at a time when Sri Lanka is still struggling to translate macroeconomic stabilisation into tangible improvements in the lives of its people.\n\nFor millions of Sri Lankans, this is more than another annual exercise in presenting revenue estimates, expenditure allocations and development proposals. It is an occasion that carries a simple but deeply felt expectation: that the coming year will be less difficult than the years they have endured.\n\nPerhaps this is best described as a prayer from the poverty-stricken majority of our population \u2014 a prayer for affordable food, decent employment, reasonable household incomes, accessible healthcare and education, and the opportunity to live with dignity rather than merely survive.\n\nSri Lanka has undoubtedly made progress since the unprecedented economic crisis of 2022. Economic growth has returned, inflation has been brought under control compared with the extraordinary levels experienced during the crisis, debt restructuring has been completed, and the IMF-supported program has helped restore a degree of international confidence.\n\nThese achievements should not be underestimated. However, stabilising the economy and restoring the quality of life of its citizens are two different challenges."
  },
