    "md5": "e0e03cc5a9e6101075f7d9a5ea3f088b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has undoubtedly made progress since the unprecedented economic crisis of 2022. Economic growth has returned, inflation has been brought under control compared with the extraordinary levels experienced during the crisis, debt restructuring has been completed, and the IMF-supported program has helped restore a degree of international confidence.\n\nThese achievements should not be underestimated. However, stabilising the economy and restoring the quality of life of its citizens are two different challenges.\n\nEconomic growth figures, however encouraging, mean little to a family that cannot comfortably afford three meals a day, a parent unable to meet a child\u2019s educational expenses, or a young graduate unable to secure a meaningful job.\n\nThe poverty headcount index, which rose to 16% in the first quarter of 2025 from 14.3% in 2019, illustrates the human cost of the economic crisis. Furthermore, the substantial increase in the official poverty line demonstrates why poverty cannot be measured without taking account of the rising cost of maintaining a basic standard of living.\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s projection of economic growth within the 4%-5% range for 2026 offers some encouragement. Yet the important question is whether this growth is sustainable and capable of generating productive employment, higher real incomes and improved living standards.\n\nA recovery driven largely by the statistical effects of an earlier contraction cannot, by itself, guarantee long-term prosperity. Sri Lanka needs a different quality of growth \u2014 growth supported by private investment, exports, agricultural productivity, technology, innovation and competitive industries.\n\nThis brings us to one of the country\u2019s most pressing concerns: investor confidence."
  },
