    "md5": "51b9f1037e95fa21a051e107e2c13c2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "A recovery driven largely by the statistical effects of an earlier contraction cannot, by itself, guarantee long-term prosperity. Sri Lanka needs a different quality of growth \u2014 growth supported by private investment, exports, agricultural productivity, technology, innovation and competitive industries.\n\nThis brings us to one of the country\u2019s most pressing concerns: investor confidence.\n\nAn investor must have confidence that agreements reached with the Government will be honoured, policies will not change unpredictably and administrative procedures will not become unnecessary obstacles.\n\nInvestment is not simply about bringing foreign currency into the country. It creates employment, transfers technology, develops skills, expands exports and generates tax revenue. A country that wishes to provide better opportunities for its younger generation must become a country in which investors are confident about committing their capital.\n\nAnother important concern is the effectiveness of public expenditure. Allocating billions of rupees for development and welfare is not sufficient if the money remains unutilised or fails to reach its intended beneficiaries.\n\nThe reported slow utilisation of budgetary allocations, including funds intended for people affected by the Ditwah disaster, raises legitimate questions about administrative efficiency and accountability.\n\nThe public does not expect merely impressive budgetary announcements. It expects roads to be repaired, schools to be improved, hospitals to receive essential facilities, disaster-affected families to be assisted and development projects to be completed on time.\n\nThe Government must therefore recognise that public administration is as important as public finance. Greater coordination between ministries, transparent procurement, digitalisation, clear institutional responsibilities and measurable performance targets are essential."
