    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "eef3c29173197b08def6e8f99a7e5a9c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "The Government must therefore recognise that public administration is as important as public finance. Greater coordination between ministries, transparent procurement, digitalisation, clear institutional responsibilities and measurable performance targets are essential.\n\nThe people are not asking for unrealistic subsidies or benefits that the national economy cannot afford. They understand that Sri Lanka has limited fiscal space, substantial debt obligations and continuing external vulnerabilities.\n\nWhat they reasonably expect is a Budget that demonstrates fairness, discipline and compassion.\n\nIt should prioritise targeted assistance for genuinely vulnerable households, rather than politically attractive but economically unsustainable concessions. It should create an environment for private investment and employment, reduce unnecessary barriers to business, strengthen vocational and technical education, improve agricultural productivity and expand opportunities for small and medium-sized enterprises.\n\nIt should also address the heavy burden placed on the formal working population and ensure that taxation is fair, predictable and supportive of productive economic activity.\n\nAbove all, the Government should establish a credible framework for translating budgetary promises into measurable results. Citizens deserve to know not only how much money has been allocated, but also what has actually been achieved with it.\n\nIt would be unrealistic to expect a single Budget to resolve all the structural problems that have accumulated over decades. Nor should anyone imagine that the Budget speech delivered today will immediately change the circumstances of millions of struggling families.\n\nIndeed, the Budget has already been prepared. Nevertheless, the first reading of the 2027 Budget provides an appropriate occasion to remind the country\u2019s leadership of the expectations, anxieties and aspirations of the people on whose behalf public finances are managed."
  },
