    "md5": "7fb6d88586629af677f1fc5d904cdb6d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1943,
    "chunk_text": "Twice promised, never delivered: Tax Ombudsman Sri Lanka still needs\n\nBudget day is when the nation listens for numbers, revenue targets, new rates and new reliefs. Behind those numbers lies a quieter but equally important question. How fairly will the State collect the taxes that fill its coffers?\n\nOver the past few years, Sri Lanka has tightened tax administration considerably, and rightly so. Revenue mobilisation has been central to our recovery. The Inland Revenue Department has expanded its use of data, third-party information, digital filing, risk-based audits and stronger enforcement.\n\nBut enforcement is only one side of the social contract. When the State\u2019s powers to assess, audit, recover and penalise grow, the taxpayer\u2019s ability to seek fair redress must grow with them. As the net tightens, the scales must stay balanced. That is the case for an independent Tax Ombudsman.\n\nTax laws give administrators wide discretion. They can issue assessments based on estimates, call for information, conduct audits, impose penalties and interest, and recover tax through coercive measures. Most officers use these powers professionally and in good faith. Still, every tax system, however well run, will sometimes see actions that fall short of what the law intends.\n\nAround the world, these issues tend to follow familiar patterns:\n\nthe same provision interpreted differently from one taxpayer to another\n\nNone of these is necessarily a dispute about the law itself. They are about how the law is applied in practice, and that is where a dedicated avenue for redress adds the most value.\n\nSri Lanka has formal dispute resolution mechanisms: administrative review within the Department, the Tax Appeals Commission and ultimately the courts. These deal with the amount of tax. They are legalistic, time-consuming and often expensive, and for a small business owner, a salaried employee or a pensioner they are usually out of reach."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-c06441ef",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c06441ef",
    "date_str": "2026-11-12",
    "description": "Twice promised, never delivered: Tax Ombudsman Sri Lanka still needs",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Twice-promised-never-delivered-Tax-Ombudsman-Sri-Lanka-still-needs/4-799996",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
