    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8b7c04287919f66de52a27319ccce809",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1998,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has formal dispute resolution mechanisms: administrative review within the Department, the Tax Appeals Commission and ultimately the courts. These deal with the amount of tax. They are legalistic, time-consuming and often expensive, and for a small business owner, a salaried employee or a pensioner they are usually out of reach.\n\nThe idea is not new to Sri Lanka. A Tax Ombudsman was appointed in 2005, but the office was abolished in 2008. Establishing the office was again proposed in Budget 2017 and in Budget 2023, but neither proposal has been implemented. The lesson is clear, an office without a statute behind it does not last.\n\nSome may see a Tax Ombudsman as a luxury Sri Lanka cannot afford. I would argue the opposite.\n\nTax morale, a citizen\u2019s intrinsic willingness to pay, is one of the most powerful and least costly drivers of revenue. The OECD\u2019s work on tax morale highlights the role of trust between taxpayers and tax administrations in encouraging compliance. When taxpayers feel powerless against the administration, compliance becomes grudging and avoidance becomes rationalised.\n\nA Tax Ombudsman also helps the administration itself. Recurring complaints expose weaknesses in systems and procedures. Seen this way, an Ombudsman is a quality-assurance mechanism, not an adversary of the Inland Revenue Department.\n\nMany countries have recognised that strong tax administrations need equally strong safeguards. Pakistan, South Africa, the United States, the United Kingdom and Australia have all set up independent offices where taxpayers can raise complaints about how they are treated by the tax authorities. The models differ, but the principle is the same, an efficient administration and a protected taxpayer are not in conflict. They reinforce each other.\n\n1. Independence. A statutory office should be established by way of an Act of Parliamemt, independent of the Inland Revenue Department, with secure tenure, its own budget and reporting to Parliament."
  },
