    "md5": "91889bee8605296a5c9746c95aeb0a4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1733,
    "chunk_text": "1. Independence. A statutory office should be established by way of an Act of Parliamemt, independent of the Inland Revenue Department, with secure tenure, its own budget and reporting to Parliament.\n\n3. Accessibility. A simple, digital and free complaint process. A taxpayer should not need a lawyer or consultant to be heard.\n\n4. Teeth. Power to call for records, recommend remedial action and require a written response within set timelines.\n\n5. Transparency. An annual report to Parliament on complaint trends and systemic issues, so individual grievances lead to institutional reform.\n\nTax administration is becoming increasingly data-driven. Third-party data, information exchange and integration with banking and other systems give the State unprecedented visibility into citizens\u2019 financial lives. This is necessary, but it raises legitimate concerns about data privacy, accuracy and use.\n\nWhen a taxpayer believes their data has been mishandled, or that an automated risk flag has unfairly targeted them, where do they go? An Ombudsman provides that outlet and helps ensure that digitalisation builds trust rather than erodes it.\n\nBudgets are statements of intent as much as statements of numbers. Announcing a Tax Ombudsman alongside continued revenue reforms would send a powerful message: the State expects every citizen to pay their fair share, and every citizen can expect to be treated fairly in return.\n\nSri Lanka has asked much of its taxpayers, and many have responded. The next step is to build a system that taxpayers trust, not merely fear. A strong tax administration collects revenue. A fair one sustains it.\n\nThe views and opinions expressed in this article are those of the author in her personal capacity."
  },
