    "md5": "dda296260cbc3b51001288435e148cc1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "The unethical Customs rewards system\n\nWho guards the guardians when they become beneficiaries of penalties they impose?\n\nA power intended to uphold the rule of law should not be exercised to confer undue benefits on the very officers entrusted with enforcing it. Such a practice creates an inherent conflict of interest, undermines institutional integrity, and risks making unethical conduct and corruption widespread in exercising the discretionary powers by such public officers.\n\nThe legislative provision that paved the way for this alarming arrangement was introduced into the Sri Lanka Customs Ordinance through the Customs (Amendment) Act, No. 83 of 1988, under the then UNP Government. This was in the aftermath of the radical economic liberalisation policy introduced by President J. R. Jayewardene in 1977, which opened the country\u2019s economy and significantly relaxed import controls.\n\n(1) of all forfeitures and penalties recovered under this Ordinance or under this Ordinance read with the provisions of any other written law; and\n\n(2) of the proceeds of all such goods as may be disposed of by the Director-General under section 162 of this Ordinance, or under this Ordinance read with the provisions of any other written law,\n\nshall be paid into the hands of such Director-General and shall (after deducting any expenses incurred) be paid and applied as follows : -\n\n(a) one half to the Deputy Secretary to the Treasury, out of which sixty per centum shall be credited to the Consolidated Fund and the balance forty per centum to the Customs Officers Management and Compensation Fund (hereinafter in this Ordinance referred to as \u201c the Fund\u201d) ; and\n\n(b) the other half into a reward fund under the control of the Director-General for distribution, in accordance with a scheme to be approved by the Minister, among customs officers concerned and informers.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-d6222db8",
    "num": "dailyftlk-d6222db8",
    "date_str": "2026-11-12",
    "description": "The unethical Customs rewards system",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-unethical-Customs-rewards-system/4-799997",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794443616.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-d6222db8-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "29c30c418dc9784ebc2c9dc28c87682c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "(b) the other half into a reward fund under the control of the Director-General for distribution, in accordance with a scheme to be approved by the Minister, among customs officers concerned and informers.\u201d\n\nn 30% to the Consolidated Fund: 60% of the first half of the recoveries. (153(2)(a)).\n\nn 20% to the Customs Officers Management and Compensation Fund: 40% of the first half. (153(2)(a))\n\nn 50% to the Reward Fund: the remaining half, to be distributed among the Customs officers concerned and informers under a scheme approved by the Minister. (153(2)(b))\n\nIt should be emphasised that informers, where applicable, receive only a negligible portion of the 50% allocated to the Reward Fund. The overwhelming share of this allocation is therefore intended for the benefit of Customs officers directly.\n\nA legal power intended to punish Customs offences and protect public revenue should never be used to financially benefit the very officers responsible for enforcing the law. When the rewards given to these officers exceed the revenue retained by the State, it is not just a legal anomaly but a serious misuse of public funds and a threat to the integrity of law enforcement.\n\nA request for information was submitted to Sri Lanka Customs on\u00a0 19 October 2026 under the Right to Information Act, No. 12 of 2016, seeking details of the total amounts of penalties and forfeitures collected during the years 2023, 2024 and 2025, together with a breakdown of the amounts distributed among Customs officers and remitted to the Treasury.\n\nThe request also sought clarification as to whether the relevant distribution scheme had received the approval of the Minister in charge, as expressly required under Section 153(2)(b) of the Customs Ordinance. This is particularly important because it has been alleged that the scheme is implemented without obtaining the Minister\u2019s approval, as required by law."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-d6222db8",
    "num": "dailyftlk-d6222db8",
    "date_str": "2026-11-12",
    "description": "The unethical Customs rewards system",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-unethical-Customs-rewards-system/4-799997",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794443616.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-d6222db8-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4b1ebee7dd23cd28c03fb2ce7f0e84d9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "The request also sought clarification as to whether the relevant distribution scheme had received the approval of the Minister in charge, as expressly required under Section 153(2)(b) of the Customs Ordinance. This is particularly important because it has been alleged that the scheme is implemented without obtaining the Minister\u2019s approval, as required by law.\n\nThere is nothing wrong with public officers, particularly those responsible for collecting and protecting Government revenue, receiving reasonable performance-based incentives. However, such incentives should be based on clear annual targets and an objective assessment of each officer\u2019s performance under a transparent scheme applicable equally to all staff. The incentive scheme of the Inland Revenue Department is a good example of this legitimate approach to staff management.\n\nHowever, such a penalty-based reward system can give rise to several serious adverse consequences that deserve urgent attention from both the Government and the public. It must be emphasised that the concern is not directed at the Customs officers as a whole. Many officers perform their duties honestly and diligently. Unfortunately, the questionable conduct of a few can tarnish the reputation of the entire service, undermine public confidence and bring discredit to the many dedicated officers who uphold the integrity of the institution.\n\nA conflict of interest arises when a public officer\u2019s personal or financial interests may influence, or appear to influence, the proper performance of official duties. Public officers are expected to make decisions solely in the public interest and in accordance with the law, without being influenced by the prospect of personal financial benefits."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
