    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4b7bb82e2e5b64a98313084c62fd27be",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "A conflict of interest arises when a public officer\u2019s personal or financial interests may influence, or appear to influence, the proper performance of official duties. Public officers are expected to make decisions solely in the public interest and in accordance with the law, without being influenced by the prospect of personal financial benefits.\n\nThe fundamental problem is that those entrusted with enforcing the law should not have a personal financial interest in the outcome of their own enforcement actions. Law enforcement must be guided by justice, fairness and the protection of public revenue, not by the financial rewards that may arise from punishing those who violate the law.\n\nThe public expects public officials, especially revenue officials such as Customs officers to enforce the law fairly and impartially, without being influenced by personal financial benefits. Importers should not have to worry about whether enforcement decisions are made in the interest of the law or financial gain.\n\nWhen honest importers face unnecessary difficulties, excessive penalties or unofficial demands to clear their goods, they may lose confidence in the system and even resort to unlawful practices. Ultimately, this discourages tax compliance, undermines public confidence and results in losses to State revenue.\n\nNot every Customs violation involves an attempt to evade duties or defraud the State. Some may arise from genuine mistakes, differences in interpretation, documentation errors or technical non-compliance. Such cases should ordinarily be dealt with through additional duties, commonly known as Additional Estimates (AEs), where applicable, rather than punitive penalties and forfeitures that generate financial rewards for enforcement officers."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-12-dailyftlk-d6222db8",
