    "language": "en",
    "md5": "938fe9286cdb9b282cb0a9f1b28f7a79",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Not every Customs violation involves an attempt to evade duties or defraud the State. Some may arise from genuine mistakes, differences in interpretation, documentation errors or technical non-compliance. Such cases should ordinarily be dealt with through additional duties, commonly known as Additional Estimates (AEs), where applicable, rather than punitive penalties and forfeitures that generate financial rewards for enforcement officers.\n\nCustoms officers should measure their performance by the quality of their investigations, accuracy of their decisions, fairness of enforcement and contribution to national revenue.\n\nHowever, when financial rewards are linked to penalties, the focus may gradually shift from professional performance to the amount of money generated through enforcement activities. This can undermine the professional ethics and integrity expected of public officers.\n\nThe rule of law requires that laws be enforced fairly, consistently and without personal interests influencing official decisions.\n\nAn enforcement system should not encourage officers to view every violation as an opportunity to generate financial benefits. Penalties must be imposed according to the law and the seriousness of the offence, not according to the financial rewards they may produce. Otherwise, the distinction between enforcing the law and exploiting the law becomes dangerously dim.\n\nPerhaps the most serious consequence is that such an arrangement can make a conflict of interest part of the institutional structure itself.\n\nNormally, conflicts of interest are matters to be prevented through ethical standards, administrative controls and disciplinary procedures. But when legislation itself creates a financial entitlement for the officers responsible for imposing penalties, the problem becomes institutional rather than merely individual."
  },
