    "md5": "a6f1a91a78aa454b404dad710acaaec2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "Normally, conflicts of interest are matters to be prevented through ethical standards, administrative controls and disciplinary procedures. But when legislation itself creates a financial entitlement for the officers responsible for imposing penalties, the problem becomes institutional rather than merely individual.\n\nAlthough the NPP Government has made considerable progress in combating political corruption, it has been less successful in eliminating bureaucratic corruption within the public service, particularly among revenue agencies that are entrusted with wide discretionary powers in the discharge of their official duties.\n\nRevenue-collecting institutions such as Sri Lanka Customs fall within the direct purview of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, whose long-standing and vocal opposition to corruption has been a defining feature of his political career. Furthermore, the fact that his Secretary previously served as a senior officer of Sri Lanka Customs makes the need for meaningful intervention even more compelling.\n\nIt is therefore the solemn responsibility of this Government to take decisive action to eradicate corruption itself and its potential avenues within revenue-collecting institutions and restore public confidence in their integrity. This responsibility must not be compromised, weakened or diluted by resistance from vested interests, potentially corrupt elements within trade unions, or opportunistic opposition political forces seeking to obstruct genuine institutional reforms. The Government must demonstrate the political courage and determination to pursue these reforms in the public interest, particularly when no other foreseeable political force appears capable of undertaking such bold and necessary measures for the benefit of the nation and its people.\n\nThe fight against corruption cannot end with politicians; it must extend to every public institution and every public official entrusted with State power."
