    "chunk_size_bytes": 1657,
    "chunk_text": "Sir Isaiah Berlin made a breakthrough in political discernment when he said that in politics we often have to make painful choices between desirable values or clusters of values. The three great ideals of the French Revolution\u2014Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\u2014are almost never available together in the real world. It is almost always a lacerating choice. I\u2019d say we are lucky if any two of the three are together, but are luckiest when the three great values appear, however episodically or ephemerally, interlinked.\n\nThe victory of this generation of US Democrats re-bundles the values of Liberty, Equality and Fraternity, even for a moment.\n\nSecondarily, it also reflects the catalytic role of the Democratic Socialist Alliance (DSA) which has seven members in the new Congress making it the largest number of self-declared Socialists in the history of the US Congress.\n\n85-year-old Bernie Sanders inspired a peaceful revolutionary change in public consciousness by combining moral-ethical leadership with sharply analytical socioeconomic critique in his decades-long career and tireless campaigning over the last ten years, all over the United States. The winning Democratic candidates are \u2018Bernie\u2019s Children\u2019, who could \u2018Feel the Bern\u2019 back in 2016. At that election the Democratic party establishment sabotaged Bernie Sanders and threw the nomination to neoliberal Hillary Clinton, though the New York Times had editorialised that the only one who could possibly defeat Trump that year was Bernie because he, unlike Hillary, epitomised change and had fired-up the enthusiasm of youth and blue-collar voters. Trump decisively beat Hillary."
