    "md5": "58cda57c005127057532cf895cb6188d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "In the face of an arrogant President, the dramatic positive changes in the USA became possible only because the main Opposition party transformed. We don\u2019t have mid-term elections in Sri Lanka and the Provincial Council elections which could function as such are nowhere on the horizon. Any positive change becomes possible only if the main Opposition party/parties \u2013 SJB, SLPP\u2014change for the better and campaign differently. The US Democrats have shown how, so there is no excuse not to.\n\nWith unipolarity segueing into the 21st century though, Sri Lankan politics was polarised between economic neoliberals and ultranationalist New Right, both identifying with the US Republicans, UK Conservatives, and even Israel\u2019s Netanyahu, albeit for different reasons.\n\nSJB chief economist Dr Harsha de Silva recently commended the AKD Govt for \u2018economic stabilisation\u2019, adding the caveat \u2018stability is not enough, jobs are also needed\u2019, while by contrast the US Democrats are light-years ahead (as Premadasa was, starting with his 1973 April Rotary Club Colombo West address), critiquing and rejecting economic \u2018stability\u2019 which destabilises the economy of the vast majority of citizens, households, neighbourhoods and public services, as no meaningful, real \u2018economic stability\u2019 at all!\n\nThe SJB criticizes the managerial inexperience and tactics of JVP-NPP economic policy but the US \u2018Blue Tsunami\u2019 criticizes the outcomes and logic of the dominant, billionaire-benefitting, citizen-impoverishing economic strategy and model.\n\n1.That the SJB shift from its neoliberal \u2018Economic Blueprint\u2019 which catastrophically lost it the 2024 election, to the progressive economics of the US Democrats.\n\n2.That the SLPP, SLFP and allies shift from the 2019 deviation of Trump-Netanyahu ultranationalism, back to its progressive center-left roots and draw inspiration from Bernie Sanders, AOC and Mamdani."
  },
