    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "Bikini photoshoots in national parks spark controversy; Environment Ministry launches probe\n\nColombo, Nov. 12 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Questions have been raised over alleged commercial bikini photoshoots inside Sri Lanka\u2019s national parks, with a former wildlife official calling for an investigation while the Environment Ministry says a probe has been launched.\n\nAn accommodation provider in Thanamalwila has also attracted attention after promoting bikini and fashion photoshoots in a wilderness setting near Yala National Park through a social media post.\n\nSeveral photographs featuring foreign women wearing bikinis have since been widely circulated on social media, triggering debate over the commercial use of Sri Lanka's natural environments and the activities taking place in and around protected wildlife areas.\n\nHowever, no evidence has so far established a direct connection between the promotional activities of Anilka Rooms & Eco Estate and the separate allegations of unauthorised commercial photoshoots inside national parks.\n\nDeputy Environment Minister Anton Jayakody told the Daily Mirror that he had not received any information about the alleged activities from the Yala National Park security counter.\n\nHe said there was no legal provision prohibiting visitors from entering a national park wearing bikinis or similar clothing. However, he confirmed that an investigation had been launched into the incident.\n\nMeanwhile, former Director General of the Department of National Zoological Gardens Sharmila Rajapaksa has called for an immediate investigation into alleged commercial bikini photoshoots taking place inside Lunugamvehera, Yala and Kumana National Parks.\n\nIn a Facebook post titled \"Our National Parks Are Sacred Safe Spaces for Wildlife and Visiting Families: Stop This Illegal Bikini Shoot Operation Now!\", Rajapaksa questioned the activities of a private commercial outfit reportedly advertising photography packages featuring foreign models wearing revealing swimwear."
