    "chunk_text": "In a Facebook post titled \"Our National Parks Are Sacred Safe Spaces for Wildlife and Visiting Families: Stop This Illegal Bikini Shoot Operation Now!\", Rajapaksa questioned the activities of a private commercial outfit reportedly advertising photography packages featuring foreign models wearing revealing swimwear.\n\nShe questioned how such activities could take place without the knowledge of wildlife officials and safari trackers assigned to accompany visitors.\n\nRajapaksa has urged the Department of Wildlife Conservation (DWC), Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA), Tourist Police and other relevant authorities to investigate whether the operators had obtained the necessary permits and approvals.\n\nShe also questioned whether the foreign nationals involved were legally permitted to engage in commercial modelling and photography activities while visiting Sri Lanka.\n\nAccording to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, filming within wildlife-protected areas requires prior approval from the Department of Wildlife Conservation. Applications must be submitted at least two weeks before the proposed filming.\n\nThe allegations have raised broader questions about the monitoring of commercial activities inside protected areas, the enforcement of permit requirements and the responsibilities of wildlife officials and safari operators.\n\nHowever, the allegations concerning commercial photoshoots inside the national parks have yet to be independently verified. No official response from the DWC or SLTDA was available at the time of publication.\n\nThe promotional activities of Anilka Rooms & Eco Estate and the separate allegations involving photoshoots inside national parks remain distinct matters, with no confirmed evidence linking the two."
