    "chunk_text": "\u2018Government fails to manage economy\u2019 \u2013 Johnston\n\nColombo, Nov. 12 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) Operations Chief Johnston Fernando has accused the Government of failing to manage the country\u2019s economy, claiming that the cost of living is rising and several State institutions are suffering billions of rupees in losses.\n\n\u201cThis is a boastful Government that does not understand administration,\u201d Fernando said, responding to questions from journalists near the Colombo Fort Court.\n\nHe said that although the President had claimed that taxes had not been increased and some taxes had been reduced, several taxes had in fact been increased, adding to the burden on the public.\n\nFernando said figures released by the Central Bank and other recognised institutions showed the rising cost of living and could not simply be dismissed.\n\nHe also questioned the performance of State institutions, claiming that several were now incurring billions of rupees in losses.\n\nReferring to the container controversy, he questioned the progress of complaints filed in connection with the incident and alleged that law enforcement institutions were being used to suppress political opponents.\n\nHe further accused certain institutions of using court proceedings and remand orders as tools of political repression, while stressing that his party would not be intimidated.\n\nComparing the present situation with the previous government, Fernando claimed that the SLPP administration had transformed loss-making institutions, including Sathosa, into profitable ventures.\n\n\u201cWe did not record a single loss at Sathosa during the entire five years,\u201d he claimed.\n\nHe said the previous administration had used Sathosa to provide essential goods to the public at affordable prices and control market prices, adding that the current situation was different.\n\nFernando said the SLPP would take all possible political measures to challenge what he described as Government repression."
