    "md5": "c833dd574b040f5d833ebb2b6d712db8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "NCCSL urges govt. to overhaul proposed anti-terror, NGO bills\n\nProtest calling upon the government to repeal PSTA. File photo\n\nIssuing a statement on proposed legislation, the state and human rights, the National Christian Council of Sri Lanka (NCCSL) said that it believes that the Rule of Law must remain central to all efforts to maintain public security and respond to corruption. \u201cThe Christian faith affirms the inherent worth and dignity of every human person, calling us to stand for a society where justice is administered fairly, public institutions remain accountable, and no individual is deprived of fundamental rights through arbitrary state action,\u201d the statement read.\n\nThe statement also listed out several positives, dangers and concerns as follows;\n\nRecognizing the State\u2019s Responsibility: The NCCSL recognizes the legitimate duty of the State to combat terrorism, suppress organized crime, and protect citizens, including aligning national frameworks with international instruments such as the UN Convention Against Transnational Organised Crime.\n\nWelcoming the Repeal of the PTA: NCCSL welcomes the long-overdue repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA) No. 48 of 1979, which has for many years been abused by governments to suppress legitimate dissent.\n\nDespite these steps, a rigorous evaluation of the proposed package of Bills- comprising the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill (PSTA), Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCB), and Non-Governmental Organisations (Registration and Supervision) Bill-reveals alarming dangers:\n\nOverbroad Definitions and Protecting State over People: The PSTA shifts focus from protecting civilians to protecting state institutions against dissent. Defining terrorism and serious crime through vague criteria like \u201cunduly compelling a government\u201d or considering dress, tattoos, and associations risks criminalizing identity and peaceful protests."
  },
