    "chunk_size_bytes": 1556,
    "chunk_text": "Lights, Camera, Legal Action: What Every Filmmaker Should Know When Making a Celebrity Biopic\n\nIn Phoolan Devi v. Shekhar Kapoor (1994), the Delhi High Court examined objections to allegedly distorted and intrusive cinematic portrayals, principally concerning privacy rather than a final finding of defamation.\n\nA \u201clife-rights\u201d agreement is a contract, not a universal statutory licence\n\nAn actor\u2019s impression of a pop star is not necessarily unlawful. But a deliberately copied voice used to sell goods can present a very different legal problem\n\nUnited Kingdom and Europe: English law provides no general inheritable celebrity publicity right. Estates may enforce copyrights, trademarks, contracts or passing-off interests\n\nCelebrity biopics \u2014 from Bohemian Rhapsody and Rocketman to films about celebrated actors\u2014invite audiences behind the public persona. Yet the filmmaker who acquires a fascinating story has not necessarily acquired the legal rights needed to tell it.\n\nCan a producer dramatise a singer\u2019s life without consent? Can an actor recreate a distinctive voice? Who controls hit songs, recordings and concert footage? Are the family\u2019s wishes binding after a celebrity dies? The answers depend on the law of the country concerned and, crucially, on the distinction between biographical facts, protected creative works and personal rights. This article addresses musicians, pop stars, actors and other entertainment celebrities\u2014not biographical films about public figures generally.\u00a0 1. Must the celebrity authorise the film?"
