    "chunk_size_bytes": 2015,
    "chunk_text": "Can a producer dramatise a singer\u2019s life without consent? Can an actor recreate a distinctive voice? Who controls hit songs, recordings and concert footage? Are the family\u2019s wishes binding after a celebrity dies? The answers depend on the law of the country concerned and, crucially, on the distinction between biographical facts, protected creative works and personal rights. This article addresses musicians, pop stars, actors and other entertainment celebrities\u2014not biographical films about public figures generally.\u00a0 1. Must the celebrity authorise the film?\n\nThe central legal principle emerging from the decision may be summarised as: The unauthorised portrayal of a real person, including a celebrity, in a biographical film, television drama or other expressive work does not, merely by reason of the absence of consent or the commercial nature of the production, constitute an actionable infringement of the right of publicity.\n\nSuch portrayals enjoy substantial First Amendment protection. This is a very wide interpretation. Nevertheless, its protection is not absolute, particularly where independently actionable defamatory statements or other legally recognised wrongs are established such as the recently enacted two important California laws on protecting performers\u2019 publicity rights, voice, image and likeness against unauthorised digital replication, particularly through artificial intelligence (i.e. the Use of Likeness: Digital Replica Law and Contracts Against Public Policy: Personal or Professional Services: Digital Replicas Law)\n\nThe Indian Supreme Court in R Rajagopal v State of Tamil Nadu (1994) 6 SCC 632 distinguished material drawn from public records from private matters. The judgment establishes that filmmakers may portray events documented in public records without obtaining the individual\u2019s consent. However, portraying intimate aspects of someone\u2019s private life beyond those records may constitute an invasion of privacy, even where the information is truthful."
