    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "The Indian Supreme Court in R Rajagopal v State of Tamil Nadu (1994) 6 SCC 632 distinguished material drawn from public records from private matters. The judgment establishes that filmmakers may portray events documented in public records without obtaining the individual\u2019s consent. However, portraying intimate aspects of someone\u2019s private life beyond those records may constitute an invasion of privacy, even where the information is truthful.\n\nA musician\u2019s biopic may involve at least four interests: copyright in lyrics, copyright in the composition, rights in the sound recording and performers\u2019 rights. The singer may own none of the first three. US Copyright Act, 17 USC \u00a7\u00a7106 and 114, and the UK Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 distinguish musical works from recordings; India\u2019s Copyright Act 1957 does likewise under sections 13\u201314 and 38\u201338B.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Intellectual Property Act protects literary and musical works through sections 6 and 9, author\u2019s moral rights through section 10, performers through section 17 and sound-recording producers through section 18. Therefore, the pop star\u2019s signature on a life-rights agreement cannot automatically authorise songs written, published or recorded under third-party contracts.\u00a0 3. Synchronisation and master-use clearances\n\nTo place a song into a film soundtrack, producers ordinarily negotiate a synchronisation licence for the composition and lyrics; using an existing famous recording ordinarily requires a separate master-use licence. Re-recording the song with an actor can avoid licensing that particular master, but does not ordinarily dispense with synchronisation permission. Examine co-writers, publishers, record-label agreements, neighbouring rights, territories, streaming, trailers, soundtrack albums and duration. US compulsory mechanical licensing for certain audio cover recordings under 17 USC \u00a7115 does not supply a general right to synchronise songs with films."
