    "chunk_text": "To place a song into a film soundtrack, producers ordinarily negotiate a synchronisation licence for the composition and lyrics; using an existing famous recording ordinarily requires a separate master-use licence. Re-recording the song with an actor can avoid licensing that particular master, but does not ordinarily dispense with synchronisation permission. Examine co-writers, publishers, record-label agreements, neighbouring rights, territories, streaming, trailers, soundtrack albums and duration. US compulsory mechanical licensing for certain audio cover recordings under 17 USC \u00a7115 does not supply a general right to synchronise songs with films.\n\nRecreating a singer on stage is different from inserting an existing television broadcast or concert recording. The latter may involve audiovisual copyright, the song, the recording, performers\u2019 rights and a broadcaster\u2019s rights. In Zacchini v Scripps-Howard Broadcasting Co, 433 US 562 (1977), the US Supreme Court recognised protection against appropriation of a performer\u2019s entire commercially valuable act. That was not a ruling banning ordinary dramatic re-enactments. Under Sri Lanka\u2019s Intellectual Property Act, sections 17, 18 and 20 are pertinent to performances, sound recordings and broadcasts. Choreography, stage artwork, photographs and distinctive graphic designs may also involve separate rights. A newly staged sequence should therefore be built from cleared musical and visual elements rather than assumed to be free because the original concert was public.\u00a0 5. Voice imitation, likeness and personality"
