    "md5": "0da5e95885b9f89a482d53d5c1f7bc50",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1817,
    "chunk_text": "Recreating a singer on stage is different from inserting an existing television broadcast or concert recording. The latter may involve audiovisual copyright, the song, the recording, performers\u2019 rights and a broadcaster\u2019s rights. In Zacchini v Scripps-Howard Broadcasting Co, 433 US 562 (1977), the US Supreme Court recognised protection against appropriation of a performer\u2019s entire commercially valuable act. That was not a ruling banning ordinary dramatic re-enactments. Under Sri Lanka\u2019s Intellectual Property Act, sections 17, 18 and 20 are pertinent to performances, sound recordings and broadcasts. Choreography, stage artwork, photographs and distinctive graphic designs may also involve separate rights. A newly staged sequence should therefore be built from cleared musical and visual elements rather than assumed to be free because the original concert was public.\u00a0 5. Voice imitation, likeness and personality\n\nA film may identify the real star without automatically becoming an \u201cofficial\u201d production. Yet posters, product tie-ins, T-shirts and advertising can create a false impression of endorsement. In Fenty v Arcadia Group Brands Ltd [2015] EWCA Civ 3, Rihanna succeeded in passing off against Top shop over a particular T-shirt presentation. The UK court did not recognise an automatic monopoly over every celebrity photograph.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, copyrighted photographs and album artwork engage sections 6 and 9 of the Intellectual Property Act; registered trademarks and section 160\u2019s unfair-competition provisions may become relevant to unauthorised branded merchandise or deceptive association. Producers should separately clear official logos, promotional photography, album sleeves and third-party marks where their intended use requires it.\u00a0 7. Privacy is not extinguished by fame"
