    "chunk_size_bytes": 1542,
    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka, copyrighted photographs and album artwork engage sections 6 and 9 of the Intellectual Property Act; registered trademarks and section 160\u2019s unfair-competition provisions may become relevant to unauthorised branded merchandise or deceptive association. Producers should separately clear official logos, promotional photography, album sleeves and third-party marks where their intended use requires it.\u00a0 7. Privacy is not extinguished by fame\n\nA celebrity biopic may become defamatory when fictionalised scenes, dialogue or allegations falsely portray an identifiable individual as dishonest, immoral or criminal. Even a film described as \u201cbased on true events\u201d may attract liability if viewers reasonably interpret damaging fictional incidents as facts.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, civil defamation remains actionable under Roman-Dutch law. In De Costa v. The Times of Ceylon, the courts affirmed the application of Roman-Dutch principles. In Mendis v. Chulanie [1999] 3 Sri LR 328, the Court of Appeal recognised that defamation may occur through words, pictures or other representations. These principles may extend to biographical films and online publications.\n\nIn the United Kingdom, the Defamation Act 2013, sections 1\u20134, addresses serious reputational harm and defences of truth, honest opinion and public interest. In litigation concerning The Lost King (2022), the English High Court found that the film\u2019s overall portrayal of an identifiable individual carried a defamatory meaning. The case subsequently settled in 2025."
