    "chunk_size_bytes": 1722,
    "chunk_text": "Online defamation: Publication through YouTube, Netflix, social media, trailers and promotional interviews can likewise create liability. Digital republication may amplify reputational harm across jurisdictions.\n\nFilmmakers should verify allegations, distinguish fact from dramatic invention and undertake legal clearance before theatrical or online release.\u00a0 9. Adapting biographies, interviews and archives\n\nUnited States: In Estate of Presley v. Russen, 513 F. Supp. 1339 (D.N.J. 1981), concerning an Elvis Presley impersonation show, the federal court held under its interpretation of New Jersey law that Presley\u2019s commercial right of publicity survived and became part of his estate. The Georgia Supreme Court reached a similar conclusion in Martin Luther King, Jr. Center for Social Change, Inc. v. American Heritage Products, Inc., 250 Ga. 135, 296 S.E.2d 697 (1982): the publicity right was inheritable and devisable. California Civil Code \u00a73344.1(b) expressly makes specified posthumous publicity rights transferable or descendible; and \u00a73344.1(d) allocates rights among qualifying relatives, generally for 70 years. New York Civil Rights Law \u00a750-f similarly provides a transferable posthumous publicity right, generally lasting 40 years, for qualifying New York-domiciled personalities and performers. Expressive-work exemptions mean there is no automatic veto over biopics.\n\nSurvivors may also possess independent privacy interests. In National Archives and Records Administration v. Favish, 541 U.S. 157 (2004), the US Supreme Court recognised surviving relatives\u2019 own privacy interests in death-scene photographs under the federal Freedom of Information Act. That does not confer film-approval rights."
