    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Survivors may also possess independent privacy interests. In National Archives and Records Administration v. Favish, 541 U.S. 157 (2004), the US Supreme Court recognised surviving relatives\u2019 own privacy interests in death-scene photographs under the federal Freedom of Information Act. That does not confer film-approval rights.\n\nGermany offers protection: in the Marlene Dietrich decision, Bundesgerichtshof, 1 December 1999, I ZR 49/97, the court held that economically valuable personality interests pass to heirs. Germany\u2019s Kunsturhebergesetz \u00a722 also generally requires relatives\u2019 consent to publication of portraits for ten years after death, subject to \u00a723 exceptions, including contemporary history and artistic interests.\n\nIndia takes a contrasting approach. In Krishna Kishore Singh v. Sarla A. Saraogi (Delhi High Court, 11 July 2023), concerning the late actor Sushant Singh Rajput, the court held that the asserted privacy, personality and publicity rights were not inheritable by his father.\u00a0 Sri Lanka has no equivalent general posthumous publicity statute as yet although it is time such law sees the light of the day. Copyright can survive under section 13 of the Intellectual Property Act No. 36 of 2003 and devolve according to ownership and succession rules; family privacy, confidentiality and unfair competition require separate analysis. Filmmakers must identify rights holders. However, in an overall analysis, the progressive approach that has been taken both by the legislature and the Supreme Court of United States seems to give justa causa to posthumously protecting the privacy and the publicity rights of celebrities or any other for that matter in a technologically advancing world privacy, and publicity rights of human are eroded in an unprecedented manner.\u00a0 \u00a012. AI replicas and digital resurrection"
