    "chunk_text": "India takes a contrasting approach. In Krishna Kishore Singh v. Sarla A. Saraogi (Delhi High Court, 11 July 2023), concerning the late actor Sushant Singh Rajput, the court held that the asserted privacy, personality and publicity rights were not inheritable by his father.\u00a0 Sri Lanka has no equivalent general posthumous publicity statute as yet although it is time such law sees the light of the day. Copyright can survive under section 13 of the Intellectual Property Act No. 36 of 2003 and devolve according to ownership and succession rules; family privacy, confidentiality and unfair competition require separate analysis. Filmmakers must identify rights holders. However, in an overall analysis, the progressive approach that has been taken both by the legislature and the Supreme Court of United States seems to give justa causa to posthumously protecting the privacy and the publicity rights of celebrities or any other for that matter in a technologically advancing world privacy, and publicity rights of human are eroded in an unprecedented manner.\u00a0 \u00a012. AI replicas and digital resurrection\n\nIn Sri Lanka, even without a dedicated general digital-replica statute, the use of training footage, recordings, images and branded promotion can engage copyright, performers\u2019 rights, contractual duties and unfair competition under the Intellectual Property Act. Obtaining explicit digital-replica provisions in actor, estate and archival agreements, and assess whether audiences could be misled into believing the celebrity genuinely spoke or sang new material is therefore the wise correct path. Contracts must distinguish rights actually granted from artistic approval or mere cooperation. Sri Lankan producers making films for global streaming must also account for laws of major distribution territories; a Sri Lankan clearance alone may not settle US, UK or Indian risks."
