    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "The fall  of a giant:  How the SLFP lost its recognition\n\nFollowing a leadership dispute within the UNP in 1951, S.W.R.D. Bandaranaike broke away and founded the SLFP\n\nThough largely ignored today and unknown to the younger generation, the Sri Lanka Freedom Party is an institution that left a massive imprint on Sri Lankan political history.\n\nDecades earlier, in the early 1980s, the SLFP\u00a0 faced another massive leadership tussle when former Minister Maithripala Senanayake challenged the leadership of Sirimavo Bandaranaike.\n\nThe Elections Commission has informed the factions fighting for the leadership of the Sri Lanka Freedom Party (SLFP) that the SLFP will no longer be considered a recognised political party, due to ongoing leadership disputes.\n\nThe Commission stated last Friday that the core reason for this decision is the failure of the party\u2019s leaders to resolve their internal conflicts for a long time.\n\nOnce a dominant political force that played an important role as both the ruling party and the main opposition since 1951, the SLFP was almost wiped off the political landscape following the 2019 presidential election. Compounding its decline, infighting has escalated dramatically since 2022, leading to 18 lawsuits mutually being filed against various leaders of the party.\n\nFormer Minister Nimal Siripala de Silva is said to be the party leader now. He noted last January that only one out of 18 cases remained unresolved\u2014the one filed against the party leadership by Dayasiri Jayasekara, the current Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) MP who previously served as the SLFP General Secretary. Jayasekara filed the lawsuit claiming his removal from the General Secretary post was illegal."
  },
