    "md5": "1395125b148e5f4171269563f5819ae5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1336,
    "chunk_text": "However, the vast majority of loyalists within the SLFP and the broader UPFA remained steadfast supporters of Rajapaksa. In 2016, when Basil Rajapaksa formed the Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) under Mahinda Rajapaksa\u2019s leadership, those loyalists also change party allegiance. Sirisena was left with only a handful of supporters and the empty nameplate of the \u201cSLFP.\u201d\n\nThe Bandaranaike government\u2014and subsequently the government of his wife, Sirimavo Bandaranaike, who took power after his assassination\u2014enacted several policies considered progressive at the time. A key milestone was S.W.R.D. Bandaranaike removing the British naval and air bases that had remained in Sri Lanka even after independence. Fifteen years later, Mrs. Bandaranaike severed all remaining administrative ties with Britain and declared Sri Lanka a Republic in 1972.\n\nIt was during S.W.R.D. Bandaranaike\u2019s tenure that the Employees\u2019 Provident Fund (EPF) was established for workers. May Day was declared a public holiday, private bus transport services were nationalised, and the Paddy Lands Act was passed to safeguard the welfare of agricultural tenants. Although he took the initial steps toward the devolution of power through the Bandaranaike-Chelvanayakam Pact, he later tore up the agreement due to fierce opposition from Buddhist monks."
