    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5581e77a5a5e1d4c31da7979bd48ea2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Short-term loans take bigger share as private credit hits Rs.2.1tn\n\nPrivate-sector credit rose to Rs.2.1 trillion in the first nine months of 2026, as short-term loans took a larger share, prompting the lawmakers to ask whether enough lending was going towards new investment and expanding production capacity.\n\nThe credit extended during the period increased from Rs.1.9 trillion a year earlier, with the licensed commercial banks accounting for about 74 percent of the total, the Central Bank officials told Parliament\u2019s Committee on Public Finance.\n\nThe share of short-term loans climbed to 47 percent, from 42 percent, while that of long-term loans fell to 29 percent, from 35 percent.\u00a0 The committee, chaired by MP Harsha de Silva, sought the details on growth in mortgage and vehicle loans and discussed whether lending was adequately supporting capital formation.\n\nThat discussion fed into a wider review of Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term growth prospects.\n\nThe briefing, attended by Central Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe, covered trade, labour and land reforms alongside monetary policy.\n\nThe participants also identified skills development, higher productivity and foreign direct investment as priorities for sustaining growth.\n\nOn monetary policy, the Central Bank officials said the headline inflation target, measured by the Colombo Consumer Price Index, would remain at 5 percent over the next three years. They said seven special reports had been submitted to Parliament on occasions when inflation stayed below the 3 percent lower bound of the target range.\n\nThe Central Bank Act requires the bank to report to Parliament when it misses its inflation target for two consecutive quarters.\n\nThe briefing also covered the health of bank balance sheets. The officials said the banking sector\u2019s Stage 3 nonperforming loan ratio had fallen to 8.8 percent, from 9.7 percent, while capital and liquidity remained above the regulatory requirements."
  },
