    "chunk_size_bytes": 1731,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s IMF crossroads puts major-power balancing act in focus\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s decision on whether to continue with the International Monetary Fund (IMF), after its current programme, would test its ability to navigate competition among major powers, former Foreign Secretary Ravinatha Aryasinha said.\n\nDelivering the Gamani Corea Memorial Lecture in Colombo on November 4, Aryasinha said the IMF programme had brought competing powers together during Sri Lanka\u2019s economic crisis, temporarily easing pressure on Colombo to choose sides.\n\nHe said that room for manoeuvre was narrowing as March 2027 approached. Whether Sri Lanka continued with the IMF and on what terms, would have consequences for its foreign relations as well as its economy. Aryasinha said he sensed that jockeying for position had already begun and warned against aligning too closely with any one power.\n\nHe drew on Corea\u2019s work to explain how the smaller countries could widen their options through multilateral institutions.\n\n\u201cDr. Corea\u2019s contribution to international economic diplomacy was rooted in the conviction that developing countries, including smaller states, need not simply accept the terms of an international order shaped by others. Through the strength of his ideas, his diplomatic engagement and his work in institutions such as UNCTAD and the South Centre, he helped give collective expression to the concerns of developing countries and advance the case for a more equitable international economic system.\u201dFor Sri Lanka, Aryasinha said, cultivating ties with a wider range of partners could give Colombo more options. Turning those relationships into trade and investment would also require addressing the weaknesses at home."
  },
