    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cDr. Corea\u2019s contribution to international economic diplomacy was rooted in the conviction that developing countries, including smaller states, need not simply accept the terms of an international order shaped by others. Through the strength of his ideas, his diplomatic engagement and his work in institutions such as UNCTAD and the South Centre, he helped give collective expression to the concerns of developing countries and advance the case for a more equitable international economic system.\u201dFor Sri Lanka, Aryasinha said, cultivating ties with a wider range of partners could give Colombo more options. Turning those relationships into trade and investment would also require addressing the weaknesses at home.\n\nThis calls for a foreign policy that is informed by a broader national consensus, supported by effective institutional safeguards and implemented through a professional diplomatic service.\u201d\n\nAryasinha said an easing of diplomatic tensions had yet to deliver the wider market access or stronger investor interest Sri Lanka had expected. He pointed to the structural barriers that kept the economy uncompetitive and said the country had made limited progress in diversifying what it exports and where it sells.\n\nThe export figures he cited showed the gains in India and China alongside a weaker performance in two established Western markets. In the first nine months of 2026, the shipments to India rose 12 percent and those to China climbed 16 percent. The exports to the United States, Sri Lanka\u2019s largest single market, were flat, while the shipments to the United Kingdom fell more than 9 percent.\n\nAryasinha said Sri Lanka had placed considerable hopes on joining the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership. Its application has been pending since 2023, he said and the region still accounts for a small share of Sri Lanka\u2019s trade in goods and services and its investment flows."
