    "md5": "36a49c7efcfd603191f95e8d9ec9dfd9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "Government plans major overhaul of international airports\n\nColombo, Nov. 12 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0President Anura Kumara Dissanayake today announced a series of development projects for Sri Lanka's international airports, including expanding passenger facilities at the Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA), upgrading Ratmalana Airport and attracting new investors to Mattala Airport.\n\nPresenting the 2027 Budget in Parliament, the President said increasing BIA's passenger handling capacity was a priority as the airport was already handling more passengers than the first terminal's designed capacity of around six million.\n\nWork has already commenced to increase the first terminal's capacity from six million to nine million passengers annually, with an investment of Rs. 15 billion by Airport and Aviation Services (Sri Lanka) Limited (AASL).\n\nConstruction of the long-delayed second terminal is also scheduled to commence shortly with assistance from the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA).\n\nA domestic air terminal will be constructed within the airport premises next year at a cost of Rs. 800 million to facilitate international passengers travelling to domestic destinations.\n\nThe Government will also allocate Rs. 200 million for a feasibility study on widening the road in front of the airport and constructing a flyover to ease traffic congestion. A new car park will be built by AASL at a cost of Rs. 400 million.\n\nThe President further announced plans to establish a re-export hub within BIA on a Build-Operate-Transfer (BOT) basis, with private sector participation, to develop the airport as an air cargo handling hub.\n\nMeanwhile, the Ratmalana International Airport will be developed to facilitate pilot training, domestic flights, business aviation and regional aviation."
