    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794479573.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-12-dailymirrorlk-ceb9bf4e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f193ef21d21042381f4465b54926001e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1764,
    "chunk_text": "Rs. 4 billion allocated for industrial estate development\n\nColombo, Nov. 12 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Government has allocated Rs. 4 billion for the development of industrial estates across the country, including the Valachchenai Industrial Estate, while further development work will be carried out at several other industrial zones under the 2027 Budget.\n\nThe allocation will also cover feasibility studies and preliminary plans for the proposed Puliyankulam Industrial Estate and infrastructure improvements at existing industrial estates.\n\nDevelopment work will continue at industrial estates in Ingiriya, Dambulla, Raigama-Millaniya and Katunayake.\n\nMeanwhile, Rs. 750 million has been allocated to promote exports, improve competitiveness and support high-value exports. The funds will also be used to organise Sri Lanka Expo 2027 in January and implement priorities identified under the National Export Development Plan.\n\nThe Government also plans to conduct a feasibility study to establish a knowledge and experience centre to strengthen links between universities and industries, promote research and development, and support the commercialisation of new products and processes.\n\nIn another initiative, production at the Kurunchantivu Salt Pan, where operations have remained suspended, will be resumed using funds from the National Salt Limited. The move is expected to generate employment opportunities in the Northern Province.\n\nThe General Treasury has allocated Rs. 236 million for 2026 to construct access roads and provide the necessary infrastructure for the project.\n\nThe Government also plans to amend the National Gem and Jewellery Authority Act No. 50 of 1993 to introduce legal provisions required for issuing licences to the jewellery industry."
  },
