    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka Budget 2027 explained: the key numbers at a glance\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Budget 2027 was presented to Parliament on 12 November 2026 by Anura Kumara Dissanayake as Minister of Finance. It is the Government\u2019s third Budget, and its stated aim is to move the economy \u201cfrom economic stability towards transformation\u201d.\n\nThe Budget follows a year shaken by Cyclone Ditwah and fuel price rises linked to the Middle East war. The Government plans to collect Rs. 6,180 billion and spend Rs. 7,673 billion, leaving a deficit of 3.8% of GDP. That rises to 4.5% with the Rs. 260 billion Ditwah reconstruction estimate. Interest alone costs Rs. 2,643 billion.\n\nThe Budget Speech runs to 367 pages. The Daily Mirror used AI to turn it into this interactive dashboard and checked every figure against the official document. Tap, scroll, and switch views to see where the money comes from, where it goes, and how the economy is doing.\n\nWhere the Government's money comes from, where it goes, how big the gap is, and the state of the economy behind the numbers.\n\nAll figures from the official Budget Speech 2027, Ministry of Finance, Planning and Economic Development\n\nThe 2027 Budget comes with a separate Rs. 260 billion Supplementary Estimate to finish Cyclone Ditwah reconstruction. Switch views to include it.\n\nInterest is the single biggest bill. Rs. 2,643 billion goes on interest in 2027. That is about Rs. 43 of every Rs. 100 the Government collects in revenue.\n\nTaxes on goods and services, which include VAT, bring in more than half of all revenue. Interest takes the largest share of spending.\n\nShares are of gross spending items. Annexure III also lists a small net \"Other\" adjustment of Rs. -11 bn.\n\nSpending has grown faster than revenue, so the deficit has widened from Rs. 745 billion in 2025 to Rs. 1,493 billion in the 2027 Budget.\n\nRs. billion unless shown as % of GDP. Brackets show negative values. Rows without a published % of GDP figure are hidden in that view."
