    "chunk_text": "Spending has grown faster than revenue, so the deficit has widened from Rs. 745 billion in 2025 to Rs. 1,493 billion in the 2027 Budget.\n\nRs. billion unless shown as % of GDP. Brackets show negative values. Rows without a published % of GDP figure are hidden in that view.\n\nInflation, Oct 20268.3%watchUp from 2.1% in Dec 2025, driven by fuel prices\n\nCentral govt debt to GDP95%betterEnd-2025, down from 114% in 2022\n\nInterest as share of revenue39.6%betterSept 2026. Was 79.1% in 2022, 45.9% in 2025\n\nGovt revenue to GDP, 202516.6%betterHighest in 23 years; 8.2% in 2022\n\nWorkers' remittancesUSD 8.1 bnbetterRecord in 2025; over USD 9 bn expected in 2026\n\nCurrent account, Jan-Sep 2026-USD 420 mnwatchDeficit, against a USD 1,853 mn surplus a year earlier\n\n52 key State enterprises+Rs. 444.4 bnbetterProfit before tax in 2025, from a Rs. 774.6 bn loss in 2022\n\nTotal foreign debt service per year, as stated in the speech\n\nCyclone Ditwah damage and losses estimated at about USD 3.4 billion (Rs. 1.022 trillion), per the Post-Disaster Needs Assessment.\n\nMore than Rs. 147 billion spent to cushion the impact of the Middle East war and the Strait of Hormuz closure on fuel prices.\n\nEl Ni\u00f1o expected to peak in February 2027, a risk to farming, food prices, water and hydropower. Rs. 5 billion is set aside in advance.\n\nFuel import bill: USD 4,487 million by September 2026, against USD 4,042 million in 2025.\n\n16% \u2192 20%Revenue at no less than 16% of GDP in the medium term, rising to about 20% in the long term\n\n13.6%Primary spending in 2027 (temporarily higher because of Ditwah), back to 13% of GDP from 2028\n\n> 4%Public investment as a share of GDP from 2027. In 2027 it is 5.12% including the Ditwah estimate\n\n87%Public debt to GDP forecast by the IMF 6th Review, against the programme target of 95% by 2032\n\nMar 2027IMF Extended Fund Facility due to end. A committee headed by the Deputy Minister of Finance is studying whether to