    "md5": "8efd43e019a7380e8a516e7f78738380",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1797,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Key proposals on growth, welfare and development\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake presented the 2027 Budget in Parliament yesterday, outlining a wide range of proposals aimed at strengthening the economy, improving public services, creating employment opportunities and raising living standards.\n\nThe Budget places emphasis on increasing local production, attracting domestic and foreign investment, expanding exports, developing infrastructure, improving education and healthcare, and strengthening social protection programmes.\n\nIt also includes major investments in roads, railways, electricity, agriculture, irrigation, housing and digital technology, while providing financial assistance to low-income families and vulnerable groups.\n\nThe Government has allocated Rs. 2 trillion for capital expenditure in 2027, representing public investment of 5.12 per cent of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP), including provisions for reconstruction following Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nThe Budget aims to maintain economic stability while moving towards a more productive and export-oriented economy. The Government has set a medium-term target of achieving sustainable economic growth of 7 per cent or higher.\n\nThe Government expects to maintain public revenue at 15.8 per cent of GDP in 2027, while total expenditure is projected to reach 20.3 per cent of GDP, including the supplementary provisions for disaster reconstruction.\n\nThe budget deficit is projected at 4.5 per cent of GDP, while the primary surplus is expected to remain at 2.3 per cent.\n\nThe Government also expects to maintain public investment above 4 per cent of GDP from 2027 onwards.\n\nThe President said the Government would continue to strengthen public finances, manage debt and improve the efficiency of public institutions."
  },
