    "chunk_text": "Rs. 5,000 million has been allocated to complete houses that were partially constructed under previous housing programmes.\n\nAnother Rs. 4,000 million has been allocated for houses for displaced people in the Northern and Eastern provinces.\n\nThe Budget also includes allocations for water supply projects in Greater Kilinochchi, Kundasale-Haragama, Eheliyagoda and the Jaffna and Vanni districts.\n\nThe Government has allocated Rs. 2,900 million for 13 new disaster management projects.\n\nThese include improving safe centres, purchasing emergency response equipment, establishing an island-wide digital radio communication system and improving early warning facilities.\n\nA modern Doppler Radar System is also expected to be installed in 2027 with Japanese assistance.\n\nAnother Rs. 5,000 million has been allocated as advance preparedness to meet possible El Ni\u00f1o-related risks.\n\nThe Government has also proposed a supplementary allocation of Rs. 260 billion for 2027 to complete reconstruction activities following Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nEnvironmental protection measures include coastal conservation, air quality monitoring and the protection of the Central Highlands.\n\nSeveral tax changes have been proposed to reduce costs for businesses, encourage investment and improve the competitiveness of the economy.\n\nThe Special Commodity Levy on imported sprats will be reduced from Rs. 100 to Rs. 10 per kilogram, while the levy on Maldive fish will be reduced from Rs. 302 to Rs. 10 per kilogram.\n\nThe levy on powdered and unpowdered dried chillies will also be reduced by Rs. 50 per kilogram with effect from November 13, 2026.\n\nThe Government has proposed a 25 per cent reduction in existing CESS rates on 800 selected import categories from April 2027.\n\nThe Ports and Airports Development Levy on imported newsprint under specified categories will be removed from January 1, 2027.\n\nHowever, the Special Commodity Levy on imported sugar will be increased by Rs. 10 per kilogram to protect the local sugar industry."
