    "chunk_text": "Kelani Valley, Main and Coastal lines. Whole programme: over Rs. 1,200 bn\n\nAim: lift plantation exports from USD 3 bn to USD 5 bn by 2030\n\nSeparate from the main Budget and financed from the available cash buffer\n\nMoving the Department of Motor Traffic to WeraheraRs. 600 mn\n\nBetter Quality of LifeRs. 65.42 bn \u00a0 10 proposals \u00b7 18.0% of new money\u25be\n\nA Peaceful & Fair SocietyRs. 46 bn \u00a0 9 proposals \u00b7 12.7% of new money\u25be\n\nHigher allowances for disabled people and kidney patientsRs. 7.1 bn\n\nPublic ServiceRs. 27.6 bn \u00a0 8 proposals \u00b7 7.6% of new money\u25be\n\nHigher allowances for Grama Niladharis and field officersRs. 3 bn\n\nEconomic ServicesRs. 5 bn \u00a0 7 proposals \u00b7 1.4% of new money\u25be\n\nHigher allowances for disabled people and kidney patientsRs. 7.1 bn\n\nParliament approved a Rs. 500 billion Supplementary Estimate for Ditwah reconstruction. Part of it went unused, partly because of the Middle East war, so Rs. 260 billion has to be provided again for 2027. It is tabled separately from the Budget and will be financed from the available cash buffer.\n\nDamage and lossesUSD 3.4 bnAbout Rs. 1.022 trillion (Post-Disaster Needs Assessment)\n\nRe-provided for 2027Rs. 260 bnLifts the 2027 deficit from 3.8% to 4.5% of GDP\n\nRoads, rail and bridgesRs. 90 bnRestoring damage, from the Ditwah estimate\n\nCascade tanks and minor irrigationRs. 21 bnEstimated rehabilitation cost; work began in 2026\n\nEvery figure comes from the Budget Speech 2027 published by the Ministry of Finance, Planning and Economic Development and presented to Parliament on 12 November 2026, including its Annexures and Technical Notes. Figures were checked against the English text and the numbers cross-checked against the Sinhala text.\n\nSector totals and the Rs. 363.1 bn total for new proposals are our own sums of the 73 items in Annexure II, grouped under the headings used there. Proposal names are shortened for readability.\n\nThe Budget is still subject to debate and approval by Parliament."
