    "chunk_text": "\"The risk is not that Nvidia \u200bsuddenly loses its technology roadmap ... that roadmap is largely visible for the next several years. The risk is execution. Jensen has been the chief conductor of an extraordinarily complex system,\" said Michael Feldman, portfolio manager at ClearBridge Investments.\n\n\"In a world with real bottlenecks, that coordination is a competitive advantage in itself.\"\n\nThe departure could also have far-reaching \u200bconsequences for US chip export policy, given Huang's push to preserve access to the Chinese market and his close ties with US President Donald Trump.\n\nTrump said on Sunday that Huang \u200chad \"done an \u2060amazing job for our country\" and that the Nvidia chief had told him he had no idea the Chinese woman he had an affair with was a spy.\n\nFor years, Nvidia was best known to gamers chasing top-tier graphics. But its chips' ability to perform many calculations at once has made them the oil of the AI boom and a strategic prize in the US-China race to dominate the technology. It also made Nvidia the world's most valuable company.\n\nThat turned \u200bHuang into a rock-star CEO, mobbed by \u200bfans in Taiwan and South Korea \u2060who ask him to sign books, posters and sometimes even their bodies. Such is his pull that stocks moved at his mere mention of company names at events.\n\nBut scrutiny of his conduct has intensified in recent days after reports of \u200bhis relationship with Alice Huang, a former executive at Singapore-based cloud company Megaspeed who US authorities have concluded \u200bwas a Chinese intelligence \u2060asset.\n\nThe two, who have been photographed together in Taiwan, Singapore and California, are not related.\n\nWashington has also investigated Megaspeed, a coveted official Nvidia cloud partner with priority access to its chips, over suspicions it helped China obtain advanced AI chips restricted under US export controls, according to media reports. Beijing has denied Alice Huang is a spy."
