    "chunk_size_bytes": 1582,
    "chunk_text": "Pakistan 112/2 in 15.5 overs (Sadaf Shamas 37, Muneeba Ali 31 not out; Sobhana Mostary 1/4, Shorna Akter 1/15)\n\nThailand 136/2 in 20 overs (Natthakan Chantham 55, Nannapat Khoncharoenkai 54 not out; Heather Siegers 2/15)\n\nMeanwhile, Pakistan strengthened their position in the first Test against Sri Lanka, reaching 57 for one in their second innings before rain and bad light brought an early end to the fourth day's play at Rawalpindi Cricket Stadium.\n\nPakistan now hold an overall lead of 45 runs, with Azan Awais unbeaten on 22 and Mohammad Taha not out on 24 at stumps.\n\nSri Lanka had earlier taken a 12-run first-innings lead after being dismissed for 446 in reply to Pakistan's 434 for nine declared.\n\nDhananjaya de Silva and captain Kusal Mendis were the standout performers for Sri Lanka, scoring 167 and 119 respectively while sharing a massive 249-run partnership that put the visitors in a strong position.\n\nHowever, debutant Saqib Khan produced an outstanding bowling performance to bring Pakistan back into the contest, claiming seven wickets for 130 runs as Sri Lanka lost their final six wickets for just 42 runs on the fourth morning.\n\nAsitha Fernando provided Sri Lanka with an early breakthrough in Pakistan's second innings, dismissing opener Tayyab Arif for 11. However, Azan and Taha steadied the innings before the weather intervened.\n\nWith Pakistan holding a 45-run advantage and nine second-innings wickets remaining, the opening Test remains finely poised heading into the final day's play.\n\nSri Lanka reach 404/4 as De Silva and Kusal Mendis hit centuries"
