    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "67af72469c665fb058856d65f283bdff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "Oversight committee presses for EV, battery standards and stronger State research bodies\n\nHighlights no mandatory regulations or standards currently govern imported EV batteries, controllers and charging stations\n\nFlags shortage of human resources in R&D institutions; calls for suitable salary and allowance schemes to attract and retain qualified engineers and researchers\n\nDiscusses proposal to amend Intellectual Property Act to introduce \u2018Petty Patent\u2019 system, to protect inventors\u2019 rights and speed up commercialisation of inventions\n\nParliament\u2019s Sectoral Oversight Committee (SOC) on Science, Technology and Digital Transformation has called for mandatory regulations and standards for electric vehicles (EV) and battery technology, while also reviewing the performance of state research institutions and the commercialisation of innovations.\n\nThe committee met under the Chairmanship of MP Dr. Janaka Senarathna. It considered the 2022 Annual Report of the Sri Lanka National Engineering Research and Development Centre (NERDC), the 2023 Annual Report of the National Science and Technology Commission, the 2024 Annual Report of the Sri Lanka Inventors Commission, and the 2025 Annual Performance Report of the Science and Technology Ministry.\n\nMembers were told that NERDC has the capacity to test battery capacity, durability and lifespan, among other parameters. Officials warned, however, that no mandatory regulations or standards currently govern imported EVs, batteries, controllers and charging stations.\n\nThey urged that relevant standards be set and that testing and certification be made compulsory.\n\nThe Committee also discussed the technology for converting fuel vehicles to electric, the need for training programs, and standards and regulatory mechanisms to ensure the safety of converted vehicles."
  },
