    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "24d8bbbc12b1fda096431f7c84027ed7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1490,
    "chunk_text": "The IMF, World Bank and ADB must reinvent themselves for a new world order\n\nAs highlighted recently at a webinar organised by the International Chamber of Commerce Sri Lanka and Daily FT , the world is changing at a pace that would have been difficult to imagine even a decade ago. By 2030, the global economic landscape could look fundamentally different, shaped by the emergence of new economic superpowers, shifting geopolitical alliances, wars, technological disruption and an unprecedented concentration of private wealth among a handful of entrepreneurs.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, three of the most influential institutions in modern economic history \u2014 the International Monetary Fund (IMF), the World Bank and the Asian Development Bank (ADB) \u2014 must seriously rethink their purpose, priorities and modus operandi.\n\nFor decades, these institutions have played a pivotal role in shaping the economic destinies and livelihoods of billions of people. They have provided financial assistance during crises, supported infrastructure development, strengthened institutions and helped countries navigate economic transitions. Their contribution to global development cannot be understated. However, past achievements cannot be a substitute for future relevance. The fundamental question is whether their traditional operating models are equipped to deal with a world that is rapidly moving beyond the economic and geopolitical assumptions on which these institutions were originally built."
  },
