    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "num": "dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "date_str": "2026-11-13",
    "description": "The IMF, World Bank and ADB must reinvent themselves for a new world order",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-IMF-World-Bank-and-ADB-must-reinvent-themselves-for-a-new-world-order/4-800046",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794525966.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "68485d2926fd54cf66c2ef5eb4b3d1f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1952,
    "chunk_text": "For decades, these institutions have played a pivotal role in shaping the economic destinies and livelihoods of billions of people. They have provided financial assistance during crises, supported infrastructure development, strengthened institutions and helped countries navigate economic transitions. Their contribution to global development cannot be understated. However, past achievements cannot be a substitute for future relevance. The fundamental question is whether their traditional operating models are equipped to deal with a world that is rapidly moving beyond the economic and geopolitical assumptions on which these institutions were originally built.\n\nA new generation of world leaders and a changing geopolitical order\n\nThe geopolitical landscape is also changing, with a new generation of younger political leaders emerging across different regions. Their priorities and expectations are increasingly shaped by a different world order, where economic security, sustainability, climate resilience, poverty reduction, youth unemployment and social mobility are becoming central political concerns.\n\nThe IMF, World Bank and ADB must develop a much deeper understanding of these changing geopolitical realities. Geopolitics can no longer be viewed simply through the traditional lens of major-power rivalries, trade relationships and security alliances. It must also recognise the growing influence of the Global South, demographic change, resource security, technology and the aspirations of younger populations.\n\nThese emerging leaders will increasingly demand practical outcomes rather than policy prescriptions alone. They will want to know how international financing can create jobs, reduce poverty, protect the environment and improve education, support entrepreneurship and make their economies more resilient. Multilateral institutions that fail to understand these changing priorities risk losing their credibility and influence"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
