    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-IMF-World-Bank-and-ADB-must-reinvent-themselves-for-a-new-world-order/4-800046",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794525966.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bf3ea16dfc1d7b4a87b7c50a0f419ac4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "These emerging leaders will increasingly demand practical outcomes rather than policy prescriptions alone. They will want to know how international financing can create jobs, reduce poverty, protect the environment and improve education, support entrepreneurship and make their economies more resilient. Multilateral institutions that fail to understand these changing priorities risk losing their credibility and influence\n\nPerhaps the most compelling argument for reinvention emerged at the recent IMF\u2013World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok. World Bank President Ajay Banga highlighted a staggering demographic challenge: approximately 1.2 billion young people in developing economies will reach working age over the next 10 to 15 years, while current projections suggest that only around 400 million jobs will be created. This leaves a potential gap of approximately 800 million jobs.\n\nThis is more than an economic statistic. It is a potential social and economic crisis. Youth unemployment, underemployment and persistent poverty threaten social stability, economic inclusion and confidence in institutions. A generation without meaningful employment is a generation at risk of losing hope, dignity and the opportunity to participate productively in society. Banga made a powerful observation: a job is not simply a statistic; it represents dignity and the ability to build a future.\n\nIMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva also highlighted the disruptive implications of artificial intelligence, describing it as a slow-moving tsunami for the labour market. The IMF estimates that around 40% of jobs globally could be affected by AI through augmentation, transformation or displacement. Young people entering the workforce, particularly those seeking their first jobs, are especially exposed."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "num": "dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "date_str": "2026-11-13",
    "description": "The IMF, World Bank and ADB must reinvent themselves for a new world order",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-IMF-World-Bank-and-ADB-must-reinvent-themselves-for-a-new-world-order/4-800046",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794525966.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b496868ddba6af91bb06087a1ddc6167",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva also highlighted the disruptive implications of artificial intelligence, describing it as a slow-moving tsunami for the labour market. The IMF estimates that around 40% of jobs globally could be affected by AI through augmentation, transformation or displacement. Young people entering the workforce, particularly those seeking their first jobs, are especially exposed.\n\nThe IMF, World Bank and ADB must therefore place job creation, human capital development, youth employability and poverty reduction much closer to the centre of their development strategies.\n\nPerhaps an even more profound development is the growing influence of technology billionaires and private capital. Individuals controlling global technology platforms, artificial intelligence ecosystems, digital payment networks and enormous pools of private wealth are increasingly capable of influencing global economic discussions.\n\nEntrepreneurs such as Elon Musk and philanthropists such as Bill Gates demonstrate how individual wealth, technology and private initiatives can shape conversations on healthcare, education, energy, climate change and economic opportunity.\n\nIn some cases, these individuals command financial resources and technological capabilities that rival those of entire nations. Their ability to mobilise capital, make decisions and deploy solutions across borders can be considerably faster than the traditional processes of multilateral institutions. Will the future of development finance be determined as much by technology entrepreneurs and private philanthropists as by the IMF, World Bank and ADB?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "num": "dailyftlk-b107bb11",
    "date_str": "2026-11-13",
    "description": "The IMF, World Bank and ADB must reinvent themselves for a new world order",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-IMF-World-Bank-and-ADB-must-reinvent-themselves-for-a-new-world-order/4-800046",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794525966.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-b107bb11-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2a8b5c89083ffe75f6f94ddd69f68410",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1920,
    "chunk_text": "In some cases, these individuals command financial resources and technological capabilities that rival those of entire nations. Their ability to mobilise capital, make decisions and deploy solutions across borders can be considerably faster than the traditional processes of multilateral institutions. Will the future of development finance be determined as much by technology entrepreneurs and private philanthropists as by the IMF, World Bank and ADB?\n\nArtificial intelligence, automation, digital currencies, biotechnology and advances in healthcare will fundamentally change how economies function, how people work and how long they live.\n\nAt the recent Annual Meetings, Banga also emphasised the potential of practical, affordable AI applications to improve productivity in agriculture, healthcare, education and small businesses in developing economies. Georgieva highlighted the need for reliable electricity, digital infrastructure, appropriate skills and safeguards to ensure developing countries can participate in this transformation. However, technology also presents serious risks. Cyberattacks, digital vulnerabilities, data security and the concentration of technological power could expose governments, financial systems and multilateral institutions to new forms of systemic risk.\n\nDigital platforms and financial technology could fundamentally change how development assistance reaches people. Instead of money passing through multiple layers of bureaucracy, technology could enable targeted funding, digital payments, healthcare services and educational opportunities to reach intended beneficiaries more directly. The institutions must help countries prepare for these transitions rather than simply respond after crises emerge. They must also ensure that AI becomes a tool for creating opportunities rather than widening the divide between those who possess technology and those who are left behind."
  },
  {
