    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "40244bd74f1e0de1332354f296e72a62",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1275,
    "chunk_text": "Digital platforms and financial technology could fundamentally change how development assistance reaches people. Instead of money passing through multiple layers of bureaucracy, technology could enable targeted funding, digital payments, healthcare services and educational opportunities to reach intended beneficiaries more directly. The institutions must help countries prepare for these transitions rather than simply respond after crises emerge. They must also ensure that AI becomes a tool for creating opportunities rather than widening the divide between those who possess technology and those who are left behind.\n\nFor countries such as Sri Lanka, this transformation is particularly important. Assistance should go beyond stabilisation programs, debt restructuring and infrastructure financing. Countries need support in building competitive industries, attracting foreign direct investment, developing human capital, retaining talent and creating sustainable employment. Economic reforms must ultimately translate into better livelihoods, reduced poverty and improved living standards. Fiscal discipline and macroeconomic stability are indispensable, but they must create the foundation for productive investment and inclusive growth, not become ends in themselves."
  },
