    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "BIMSTEC offers more trade, investment and regional cooperation opportunities for SL\n\nSri Lanka could gain significant opportunities to expand regional trade, attract investment and strengthen economic and strategic cooperation through the Bay of Bengal Initiative for Multi-Sectoral Technical and Economic Cooperation (BIMSTEC), as the regional organisation seeks to strengthen collaboration among its member States.\n\nThis was highlighted by BIMSTEC Secretary-General Ambassador Indra Mani Pandey, who said that maritime security, energy cooperation, business connectivity and technology transfer were emerging as important areas of regional engagement.\n\nThe seven-member organisation is seeking to strengthen collaboration among its member States while addressing common economic and security challenges.\n\nAddressing the 7th RCSS Strategic Dialogue on \u2018BIMSTEC and the Evolving Regional Order\u2019, organised by the Regional Centre for Strategic Studies (RCSS), Ambassador Pandey highlighted the opportunities available to Sri Lanka through regional cooperation, emphasising that the country could benefit from greater trade, investment, tourism, technology access and people-to-people exchanges.\n\n\u201cThrough regional cooperation under BIMSTEC, Sri Lanka can make substantive gains, whether through more trade, more investment, more travel, more tourism and more cultural exchanges,\u201d he said.\n\nThe seven-member organisation, comprising Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Thailand, provides a regional platform connecting South and Southeast Asia through cooperation in several areas of mutual interest.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, participation is particularly relevant as the country seeks to strengthen its economic relationships, expand investment opportunities and benefit from regional cooperation in maritime affairs, energy, science and technology."
  },
