    "chunk_text": "For Sri Lanka, participation is particularly relevant as the country seeks to strengthen its economic relationships, expand investment opportunities and benefit from regional cooperation in maritime affairs, energy, science and technology.\n\nAryasinha outlined the progress achieved by the RCSS, with the support of The Asia Foundation, on non-traditional security in the Bay of Bengal, the deployment of emerging technologies to address maritime security challenges, and an impending study on building a resilient and inclusive blue economy and critical infrastructure.\n\nHighlighting the importance of stronger business-to-business relationships, Pandey said BIMSTEC was considering several initiatives to improve commercial connections among member States.\n\nA proposal to establish a BIMSTEC Chamber of Commerce and Industry had reached an advanced stage, although he acknowledged that institutional initiatives within the organisation often required considerable time to move from proposals to implementation.\n\nHe said BIMSTEC was also looking at establishing a platform connecting the investment promotion authorities of member countries, including Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and India.\n\nAccording to Pandey, such a platform would enable countries to collectively promote the BIMSTEC region as a global investment destination, creating opportunities to attract investors and strengthen business relationships.\n\n\u201cWe want to create a platform for investment promotion authorities,\u201d Pandey said, explaining that stronger links among these institutions could help promote investment opportunities across the region.\n\nHe identified this initiative, together with the proposed BIMSTEC Chamber of Commerce and Industry, as important steps towards strengthening regional business cooperation."
