    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe want to create a platform for investment promotion authorities,\u201d Pandey said, explaining that stronger links among these institutions could help promote investment opportunities across the region.\n\nHe identified this initiative, together with the proposed BIMSTEC Chamber of Commerce and Industry, as important steps towards strengthening regional business cooperation.\n\nPandey noted that the recognition of certificates was particularly important when businesses attempted to operate across national markets.\n\nBangladesh, which leads the relevant area, had supported a concept aimed at addressing these issues. Pandey expressed hope that member States would respond promptly to enable further progress.\n\nHe also highlighted the importance of the BIMSTEC Business Summit in bringing business communities together and strengthening links among chambers of commerce and other private-sector organisations.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, stronger engagement with regional business networks could provide opportunities to expand trade, attract investment and develop partnerships with companies across member countries.\n\nAddressing the progress of the proposed BIMSTEC Free Trade Agreement, Pandey said the framework agreement had already been concluded, but negotiations on its six constituent agreements were continuing.\n\nThese negotiations cover different aspects of the proposed free trade arrangements, including rules of origin, customs cooperation, trade facilitation, trade in services and dispute settlement, including arbitration.\n\nHe said some negotiating groups had made sufficient progress and could move towards concluding their respective agreements with further engagement.\n\nHowever, other groups had made limited progress because meetings had been held infrequently.\n\n\u201cIn some subgroups, we have made sufficient progress. With a few more pushes, we might reach the stage where we can conclude those agreements,\u201d he said."
