    "chunk_text": "Pandey described the exercise as an important beginning in addressing common maritime security challenges through practical cooperation.\n\nThe exercise was intended to translate the guidelines and mechanisms developed by member States, including information-sharing and consultation, into concrete action.\n\nAccording to Pandey, BIMSTEC had also established mechanisms involving national security advisers, who had met five times during the previous nine years.\n\nIn addition, BIMSTEC had recently initiated two mechanisms to address transnational organised crime affecting member States.\n\nPandey emphasised that economic cooperation and security cooperation were not separate priorities.\n\n\u201cBoth go together. But it\u2019s up to our member states to determine the future cooperation through a collective consensus-building,\u201d he said.\n\nEnergy cooperation is another important component of BIMSTEC\u2019s regional agenda, with particular attention being given to the interconnection of electricity grids among member States.\n\nPandey said the BIMSTEC Energy Centre (BEC) in Bengaluru, India, would play an important role in supporting regional cooperation in the energy sector and would function as the secretariat for initiatives relating to interconnected power grids.\n\nHowever, he pointed out that connecting electricity grids required considerable technical preparation and capacity-building.\n\n\u201cThe primary area of work has been interconnecting the power grids of our member states. Therefore, one of our roles in the energy sector will be to act as the secretariat for the interconnected power grids, but it needs a lot of capacity building,\u201d he said.\n\nWith countries increasingly using diverse sources of electricity, managing interconnected power systems had become a major technical challenge."
