    "chunk_text": "Pandey emphasised that BIMSTEC\u2019s cooperation framework was based on the collective priorities of its seven member States, with each country having an equal role in determining the organisation\u2019s direction.\n\nPandey also highlighted the progress made in developing cooperation on the green economy and climate-related issues.\n\nClimate change-related cooperation, particularly disaster management and the maritime component of humanitarian assistance and disaster relief, was another important area.\n\nHe said the guidelines developed for maritime HADR cooperation would help relevant agencies work together when disasters occurred in maritime areas of the Bay of Bengal.\n\nSuch cooperation would enable member States to improve coordination and respond collectively to emergencies affecting the region.\n\nHe added that member countries believed they could benefit from progress made under BIMSTEC, irrespective of which country led a particular sector.\n\nExplaining BIMSTEC\u2019s collaboration with the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) on the proposed BIMSTEC Convention on Human Trafficking, Pandey said it would provide a legal framework for member States to work together more effectively.\n\n\u201cHuman trafficking and other transnational crimes are important concerns for member states. These issues, including scam centres operating around the Myanmar\u2013Thailand border, have also been discussed at meetings of National Security Advisers and Home Ministers,\u201d he said.\n\nThe seven member States have opportunities to discuss common challenges, share their experiences and identify areas for joint action.\n\n\u201cThrough these discussions, practical areas of cooperation can be incorporated into regional action plans and implemented collectively,\u201d he pointed out.\n\nPandey highlighted both the opportunities available through BIMSTEC and the need for stronger commitment from member States to translate regional initiatives into practical results."
