    "md5": "ee37611337945e16f3864f92a1853813",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1529,
    "chunk_text": "What social media can, and cannot shape or predict around elections\n\nElon Musk\u2019s $ 366 million, and the Twitter/X feed he owns, did not keep the House Republican, and nobody can show that Lula\u2019s volunteer army of millions on WhatsApp won him Brazil\u2019s election. However, both consequential results bear out what I studied for my PhD around Sri Lanka\u2019s socio-political developments, and how platforms reflect a political mood far more often than they make one.\n\nElon Musk spent roughly $ 366 million on this year\u2019s American midterms, $276 million of it since July, and owns the platform whose feed a Harvard study found rewarding divisive posts and which the statistician Nate Silver argues favours right-leaning accounts, yet Democrats have won the House with what NBC News projects as 258 seats and flipped at least seven Senate seats, among them Texas, Ohio, Iowa and Maine, where his political action committee (a vehicle for funding campaigns) had targeted its spending. Nine days earlier in Brazil, Luiz In\u00e1cio Lula da Silva, who had trailed Fl\u00e1vio Bolsonaro in the first round, won the runoff. Lula\u2019s the first candidate since the two-round system began in 1989 to win after finishing second.\n\nThe two results look unrelated, though they answer the same question, which is whether the loudest voices online decide elections, and the answer I reached in my 2021 doctoral thesis, after studying, inter alia, four major crises in Sri Lanka, and 1 in New Zealand, was that they rarely decide anything on their own."
  },
