    "chunk_size_bytes": 1610,
    "chunk_text": "The two results look unrelated, though they answer the same question, which is whether the loudest voices online decide elections, and the answer I reached in my 2021 doctoral thesis, after studying, inter alia, four major crises in Sri Lanka, and 1 in New Zealand, was that they rarely decide anything on their own.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2018 constitutional coup taught the same lesson in miniature, since over 60 accounts of the party backing Mahinda Rajapaksa\u2019s unconstitutional appointment as prime minister tweeted the most, yet the 22 accounts of the party opposing it drew the highest engagement, leading me to conclude in my PhD thesis that \u201ca high production volume is a poor measure of actual engagement\u201d.\n\nThe Brazilian case is more interesting still, because it shows people rather than money learning to work the machine. Alexandre Afonso, a political economist at Leiden University, Netherlands, joined a WhatsApp channel called O Ex\u00e9rcito do Lula (Lula\u2019s Army), launched by the deputy Andr\u00e9 Janones on the night of the first round and, by its own count, 10 million strong within 3 weeks, and went through its 1,151 text messages and 104 voice notes. What he found was a plain playbook in which members were told to act at the same instant \u201cto create that explosion\u201d, since platforms such as Twitter/X reward sudden spikes over steady popularity, to flood hashtags until an opponent\u2019s slogan fell off the trending list, to ignore the other side\u2019s posts because even a rebuttal feeds the algorithm, and to type a neutral-sounding search into Google so that curiosity did the persuading."
