    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "68530b2f03bc260a745b4726c58e8452",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1562,
    "chunk_text": "I recognise the craft, because Sri Lankans stumbled on it during the 2018 coup, when activists behind #riseuplk and Rajapaksa\u2019s supporters behind #letmevote fought an \u201copen, enduring contestation\u201d, each \u201cvying for attention and influence\u201d, while a video of two young drama festival winners refusing to shake hands with 2 senior politicians allied to the President, though poorly recorded and without any post-production flair, drew over 187,000 views on a single Facebook page in under a day, and by far exceeded the combined interactions of nearly 60 official party Twitter accounts.\n\nRajapaksa\u2019s 2019 victory means rage cannot be written off as an electoral force, in Sri Lanka or anywhere else, though what 2026 shows, read through what I would call mood, method and money, is that engagement-driven amplification is neither sufficient nor reliably decisive, and that a few hundred million dollars cannot substitute for a favourable political context. Mood is the offline condition, and in Sri Lanka in 2019 it was fear after the Easter Sunday attacks, sharpened by revelations that the President and Prime Minister had been repeatedly warned and did nothing, which delivered an electorate to a strongman who promised security, whereas in America in 2026 prices and a war delivered the opposite. Method is how cheaply and cleverly people can synchronise, which Brazil and Colombo both show to be within anyone\u2019s reach, the propagandist\u2019s as much as the activist\u2019s. Money, as Musk\u2019s $ 366 million suggests, buys reach without buying belief."
  },
