    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "Rajapaksa\u2019s 2019 victory means rage cannot be written off as an electoral force, in Sri Lanka or anywhere else, though what 2026 shows, read through what I would call mood, method and money, is that engagement-driven amplification is neither sufficient nor reliably decisive, and that a few hundred million dollars cannot substitute for a favourable political context. Mood is the offline condition, and in Sri Lanka in 2019 it was fear after the Easter Sunday attacks, sharpened by revelations that the President and Prime Minister had been repeatedly warned and did nothing, which delivered an electorate to a strongman who promised security, whereas in America in 2026 prices and a war delivered the opposite. Method is how cheaply and cleverly people can synchronise, which Brazil and Colombo both show to be within anyone\u2019s reach, the propagandist\u2019s as much as the activist\u2019s. Money, as Musk\u2019s $ 366 million suggests, buys reach without buying belief.\n\nThe Brazilian strategic playbook also travels in every direction, because hashtags can be used for anything, by anyone. The same synchronisation that lifted #NortheasternersWithLula can amplify hate against minorities in Sinhala across Facebook (and this is in fact happening even today). To wit, whoever reads America\u2019s midterms as proof that algorithms no longer matter, or Brazil\u2019s runoff as proof that catchy, clever hashtags can win elections will legislate, campaign and moderate for the wrong problem. That is a costly mistake for Sri Lanka, whose politics now plays out largely on Facebook, WhatsApp and Twitter/X, platforms whose algorithms and content rules are set by American companies with little to no regard for Sri Lankan voters, and lives."
  },
