    "md5": "be23777f2effa6e13c79b79c2c12aade",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1561,
    "chunk_text": "Solutions to youth problems: The case of IT qualifications\n\nCreating more free or subsidised seats in IT degree or certificate programs appears the obvious solution. But what if those programs are substandard or obsolete?\n\nSerbia and Nepal have governments formed by youth. The governments in power in Sri Lanka and Bangladesh owe their fortunes to youth-led urban uprisings. The blue wave that washed out Republican candidates in the US was powered by youth. Some of Sri Lanka\u2019s most senior politicians have said that no progress can be achieved without greater participation of youth.\n\nI was at a recent Youth Forum in Colombo that highlighted the problems of the youth and presented the solutions they had come up with. I was there on a weekday morning because I agree with the need to give youth a greater voice in governance. I strongly believe we need to bring young people into the political and policy mainstream. That belief has been operationalised in various ways, most recently through a TV program and associated social media stream, where every week I discuss with a young person a policy problem that has been left unresolved by my generation.\n\nBut the young person is not there simply because of his/her age. They have been invited because they are able to make evidence-based, informed contributions or have experience of the ground situation. The discussion is, in part, a pressure-testing of their ideas and the evidence that supports their conclusions. It is in this context that I looked at an example of a solution released at the Forum."
  },
