    "md5": "4dde27fce54fb06a804db21f19049ae6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "But the young person is not there simply because of his/her age. They have been invited because they are able to make evidence-based, informed contributions or have experience of the ground situation. The discussion is, in part, a pressure-testing of their ideas and the evidence that supports their conclusions. It is in this context that I looked at an example of a solution released at the Forum.\n\n\u201cRecent college graduates in India could struggle to land technology jobs as companies automate entry-level coding and software testing work with artificial intelligence, the top executive at the country's largest industry body for technology companies told Nikkei Asia.\n\nRajesh Nambiar, president of the National Association of Software and Services Companies, said the hurdle could last until India's software exporters adjust to the new business reality. That would involve opening up new opportunities in cybersecurity and AI consulting as well as implementation and maintenance, among others, in order to offset reduced workloads in key revenue streams such as technology infrastructure management.\n\nAs revenue models become based on the actual outcome rather than the number of people deployed on a project, the goalposts will shift for fresh graduates, said Nambiar, a former chairman and managing director for India at information technology services company Cognisant. That suggests a need to refresh the computer studies curriculum, otherwise India could find itself with thousands of engineering graduates with mismatched abilities.\u201d\n\nThe \u201csolution\u201d of more IT graduates was somewhat understandable in 2019, when the results of the last National IT BPM Workforce Survey were released by the ICT Agency. Even then, those who could look beyond their noses could see that the BPO/BPM industry which according to the BOI employed the majority of the 175,000 persons in the IT and ITES sector was endangered. Now the conventional software firms are also curtailing hiring."
  },
