    "md5": "3045c824935e070c1c5039702f09f2cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1659,
    "chunk_text": "The \u201csolution\u201d of more IT graduates was somewhat understandable in 2019, when the results of the last National IT BPM Workforce Survey were released by the ICT Agency. Even then, those who could look beyond their noses could see that the BPO/BPM industry which according to the BOI employed the majority of the 175,000 persons in the IT and ITES sector was endangered. Now the conventional software firms are also curtailing hiring.\n\nWhat is needed is not the ramping up the production of IT graduates, but urgent reforms in what today\u2019s students are taught. Industry must work with educationists to execute this pivot.\n\nIt is extraordinarily important to communicate the concerns of the young to decision makers, the media, and through multiple channels to the general public. For this, the organisers of the Forum deserve our thanks.\n\nFor effective communication, systematic qualitative research may be enough. Stories are what people remember and are persuaded by, not numbers. But media attention is more likely for the findings of quantitative research given as percentages. For example, a news story stated: \u201cThe survey found that 80% of respondents identify primarily as Sri Lankan, and 73.7% feel a strong national belonging. However, 49.1% stated they would leave Sri Lanka if given the opportunity.\u201d\n\nThe problem with good survey research is cost. Eliciting responses from representative (randomly drawn) samples across the country is expensive. Multi-method research is good, but properly conducted truly random sample surveys cannot be dispensed with. Findings drawn from convenience samples do little more than pollute the conversation."
  },
