    "md5": "682322dce27b63bc395f14252720b94f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1208,
    "chunk_text": "The problem with good survey research is cost. Eliciting responses from representative (randomly drawn) samples across the country is expensive. Multi-method research is good, but properly conducted truly random sample surveys cannot be dispensed with. Findings drawn from convenience samples do little more than pollute the conversation.\n\nThe enthusiasm and energy of the youth are essential to social change. Youth drive movements. But they are not especially suited to formulate solutions, even to their own problems. For that, the primary qualifications are expertise and experience. Youth should be involved, to the extent that they have engaged in research and/or can bring insights from the field to the policy formulation process.\n\nCreating more free or subsidised seats in IT degree or certificate programs appears the obvious solution. But what if those programs are substandard or obsolete? A proper understanding of current trends and requirements in the IT and ITES sector will point to a different solution. The wrong solution will waste money and years of a young person\u2019s life. The understanding needed to avoid that comes only from those with expertise and experience, irrespective of age."
