    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aadfb52a784d0ec103b982973d0864cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1923,
    "chunk_text": "The Government targets economic growth of at least 7% in the medium term, while the World Bank projects 4.2% for 2027. Its own revenue forecast of 15.8% of GDP for 2027 also sits below its medium-term ambition of at least 16%.\n\nThese ambitions require clearly identified investments, institutional reforms and productivity improvements. Economic stability is an achievement, but it is not the same as economic prosperity.\n\nBut millions of low-income earners do not pay personal income tax. They gain little from a higher PAYE threshold. They struggle with food, transport, electricity, school expenses and insecure earnings.\n\nColombo inflation had risen to 8.3% in October 2026. Lower inflation in a future month would not mean prices had returned to their previous levels.\n\nThe World Bank estimates that poverty was 16.9% in 2025 and notes that food prices remain more than twice their pre-crisis level.\n\nTargeted relief is necessary, but it cannot substitute for sustained improvements in household purchasing power.\n\nThe Budget needs a convincing plan to reduce production and distribution costs, improve competition and raise real wages. Families cannot meet their daily expenses with favourable economic statistics.\n\nYouth unemployment is among the clearest tests of development policy.\n\nOfficial labour statistics put overall unemployment at 4% in the second quarter of 2026, but unemployment among those aged 15\u201324 was 17.8%. Among young women, it was 22.4%.\n\nThe Budget proposes training, digital initiatives and further public-sector recruitment. Filling essential vacancies can improve public services. But the state cannot become the principal employer of an entire generation.\n\nWhere is the measurable plan for private-sector jobs in export industries, technology, modern agriculture, logistics and high-value services?\n\nHow many new jobs will be created, at what wage levels, in which districts, and by when?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-ad276818",
    "num": "dailyftlk-ad276818",
