    "md5": "64dc8f06a813f2101c1599283b5e3657",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "Investors seek predictable taxation, reliable energy, secure land rights, efficient approvals, skilled workers and consistent policy.\n\nThe proposed national single window for investment is still at the consultant-selection stage. This illustrates the distance between announcing investment facilitation and actually making it work.\n\nA competitive future requires export diversification, technology adoption, stronger local supply chains and meaningful access to international markets.\n\nTourism and remittances are valuable, but they cannot alone deliver the productivity and economic resilience the country needs.\n\nOnly 16.3% of the 2026 annual capital-expenditure allocation had been spent by the end of June. Even by September, reported capital spending was only 31.6% of the annual allocation.\n\nSpending need not be distributed evenly throughout the year, but such slow implementation requires explanation.\n\nAgainst that record, announcing Rs. 2 trillion for public investment in 2027 creates an obligation to explain how procurement, land acquisition, project preparation and inter-agency coordination will improve.\n\nParliament needs quarterly reports showing funds spent, projects completed, jobs created and economic returns achieved.\n\nOtherwise, the Budget risks becoming another impressive document whose development promises remain largely on paper.\n\nThe Government\u2019s manifesto, A Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life, promised a fairer and more productive society.\n\nYet the Budget\u2019s immediate political appeal relies heavily on individual allowances, salary adjustments and visible spending commitments.\n\nBeneficiaries may deserve such support, but political popularity is not economic transformation.\n\nThe Rs. 50 billion allocation for district and decentralised development, including Rs. 10 million per Member of Parliament, also requires transparent selection criteria and measurable development returns.\n\nThere is nothing improper about responding to people\u2019s immediate needs."
  },
