    "language": "en",
    "md5": "13f535ad59251193187cb0ee4fed547b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2015,
    "chunk_text": "The Rs. 50 billion allocation for district and decentralised development, including Rs. 10 million per Member of Parliament, also requires transparent selection criteria and measurable development returns.\n\nThere is nothing improper about responding to people\u2019s immediate needs.\n\nThis gap between promises and lived experience could deepen public frustration and social tension, even without another immediate economic crisis.\n\nPerhaps the most revealing passage concerns the International Monetary Fund.\n\nThe President stated that the current IMF program is expected to conclude in March 2027 and that a Cabinet-appointed committee is examining the possibility and form of another program.\n\nTherefore, renewed IMF engagement is not merely an opposition allegation. The Government itself is studying that possibility.\n\nAnother IMF arrangement would not automatically mean economic failure. A precautionary programme could provide protection against external shocks.\n\nBut if Sri Lanka continues to require external financial assistance because exports, private investment and debt-servicing capacity have not improved sufficiently, the promised economic transformation will remain incomplete.\n\nThe Budget itself acknowledges a deterioration in the external current account during the first nine months of 2026.\n\nOur objective should not merely be to complete one IMF program and prepare for another. It should be to build an economy capable of standing increasingly on its own.\n\nThe Government should publish a transparent, time-bound development strategy linking major expenditure proposals to employment, exports, productivity and poverty reduction.\n\nIt must remove investor bottlenecks, strengthen public administration and publish independently verifiable results.\n\nAbove all, it must explain how economic growth will reach ordinary households rather than remain confined to national accounts.\n\nSri Lanka does not need another contest over who announces the largest allocation or distributes the most benefits."
  },
