    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2698e5011bcf2b04917e6a7d8f90c9d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Personal tax relief in Budget 2027\n\nIn Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery, the salaried employee has been the most reliable taxpayer. Tax comes out of their pay every month before they receive it. In the hardest years of the crisis, when prices rose far faster than salaries, they paid their share in full and on time. The personal income tax revision is the centrepiece of this year\u2019s tax proposals. It won\u2019t fix every problem in the system, but it is a meaningful step towards a fairer deal for people who carry the most visible part of the burden\n\nThe Budget Speech, constituting the Second Reading of the Appropriation Bill for 2027, was delivered in Parliament yesterday. This will be followed by seven allotted days for the Second Reading debate, culminating in the Second Reading vote at 6.00 p.m. on 20 November. The Committee Stage debate will then run through to 14 December, when the Third Reading vote is scheduled for 6.00 p.m.\n\nBudget 2027 therefore arrives at an important juncture. This article focuses on the key budget proposal in relation to revision of personal income tax rates and slabs.\n\nIn Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery, the salaried employee has been the most reliable taxpayer. Tax comes out of their pay every month before they receive it. In the hardest years of the crisis, when prices rose far faster than salaries, they paid their share in full and on time. The personal income tax revision is the centre-piece of this year\u2019s tax proposals. It won\u2019t fix every problem in the system, but it is a meaningful step towards a fairer deal for people who carry the most visible part of the burden.\n\nFrom 1 April 2027, the annual tax-free threshold goes up from Rs. 1.8 million to Rs. 1.95 million. An individual earning up to Rs. 162,500 a month will pay no tax."
  },
