    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Personal-tax-relief-in-Budget-2027/4-800051",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794532188.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-60dd2325-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1bad55b4f943877287f0fac9c455f3af",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery, the salaried employee has been the most reliable taxpayer. Tax comes out of their pay every month before they receive it. In the hardest years of the crisis, when prices rose far faster than salaries, they paid their share in full and on time. The personal income tax revision is the centre-piece of this year\u2019s tax proposals. It won\u2019t fix every problem in the system, but it is a meaningful step towards a fairer deal for people who carry the most visible part of the burden.\n\nFrom 1 April 2027, the annual tax-free threshold goes up from Rs. 1.8 million to Rs. 1.95 million. An individual earning up to Rs. 162,500 a month will pay no tax.\n\nAs a result, the 36% top rate now starts at a monthly salary of about Rs. 433,333, compared with about Rs. 358,333 today. The Government estimates the cost of the revision at Rs. 16 billion a year.\n\nThe relief comes at a cost. The Budget estimates that all income tax revisions together will reduce revenue by Rs. 16 billion a year. The personal income tax changes are expected to make up a large part of this, although the Budget does not show them separately.\n\nFor a salaried employee, the table below shows the monthly tax for employees whose only income is salary, before and after the revision.\n\nFor an employee earning Rs. 300,000 a month, tax falls by more than a quarter, from Rs. 18,500 to Rs. 13,250. Their marginal rate drops from 24% to 18%.\n\nThe biggest proportional gain goes to people earning around Rs. 400,000 a month. They save Rs. 13,750 a month, or Rs. 165,000 a year. Their effective tax rate falls from 12.5% to 9.1%, and their marginal rate drops from 36% to 30%. Many of these people are mid-career professionals: engineers, accountants, doctors, IT specialists and managers. They have felt the steep progression of the current slabs most sharply, and many of them are the people Sri Lanka can least afford to lose to migration."
  },
  {
