    "language": "en",
    "md5": "77f22f89063da31bc57566f70628202f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1768,
    "chunk_text": "The biggest proportional gain goes to people earning around Rs. 400,000 a month. They save Rs. 13,750 a month, or Rs. 165,000 a year. Their effective tax rate falls from 12.5% to 9.1%, and their marginal rate drops from 36% to 30%. Many of these people are mid-career professionals: engineers, accountants, doctors, IT specialists and managers. They have felt the steep progression of the current slabs most sharply, and many of them are the people Sri Lanka can least afford to lose to migration.\n\nAt the lower end, the relief is smaller. An employee earning Rs. 200,000 a month saves Rs. 750. That reflects how little tax they were already paying, but it is a reminder that for lower-income households, the cost of living, VAT and indirect taxes matter far more than income tax.\n\nRs. 15,750 a month won\u2019t change anyone\u2019s life. But the revision matters for three reasons.\n\nFirst, it builds on previous relief without putting revenue at risk. Even after the threshold was raised from Rs. 1.2 million to Rs. 1.8 million, PAYE/APIT collections grew by 6.1% to Rs. 152.7 billion in the first eight months of 2026. A fair tax system and an adequate one are not in conflict.\n\nSecond, it improves retention. Skilled professionals compare their take-home pay across countries, not tax rates in isolation. Widening the bands and pushing back the point at which the 36% rate starts improves Sri Lanka\u2019s offer at the margin.\n\nThird, it supports tax morale. Salaried employees can\u2019t avoid tax, so they notice when the burden falls unevenly. Relief for compliant taxpayers, together with the Budget\u2019s move to bring crypto assets, high-value jewellery and other wealth into the Capital Gains Tax net, sends a signal that the system aims to ask a fair share from everyone."
  },
  {
