    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3e05c866f884a9b8ef1a06faeea9d170",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1563,
    "chunk_text": "Third, it supports tax morale. Salaried employees can\u2019t avoid tax, so they notice when the burden falls unevenly. Relief for compliant taxpayers, together with the Budget\u2019s move to bring crypto assets, high-value jewellery and other wealth into the Capital Gains Tax net, sends a signal that the system aims to ask a fair share from everyone.\n\nThe 36% rate still starts early. At about Rs. 433,000 a month, it remains a high marginal rate for senior professionals. As fiscal space improves, the starting point of the top band should be reviewed.\n\nThe base needs to widen. Salaried employees / Individuals shouldn\u2019t keep carrying the burden of people outside the system. The revenue to fund future relief must come from bringing in people who earn taxable income but don\u2019t pay tax. Better data matching, digital tax administration and stronger compliance enforcement are the levers to use here, rather than higher rates on people who already comply.\n\nImplementation must be smooth. Employers and payroll providers will need revised APIT tables and clear guidance well before April 2027, so that the relief shows up in pay from the first month.\n\nBudget 2027 begins to return some of that contribution in a way that is targeted, affordable and fiscally responsible. The next step is to make that fairness permanent, through indexation, a broader tax base and a system in which the people who pay their taxes most reliably are not the ones who pay the most.\n\n(The views and opinions expressed in this article are those of the author in her personal capacity.)"
  },
