    "md5": "7ac9ec191a7f14cba38e54559fd9a2c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1696,
    "chunk_text": "But the outage, which began around noon on the US East Coast, caused serious headaches, especially when it dragged on for more than an hour or two.\n\nMicrosoft 365 services that interact with Exchange Online and email services were all impacted. That includes Outlook, SharePoint, Teams, 365 and Copilot AI.\n\nIn Australia, Friday began with a realization that the Microsoft cloud was unresponsive. The outage took out live feeds from courts, and schools texted parents to phone in any absences until email systems were restored.\n\nAustralia\u2019s national broadcaster, ABC, reported people missing job interviews because they couldn\u2019t connect to virtual meetings, and even some supply issues with distribution centers stocking supermarkets due to the outage.\n\nAt first, Microsoft said the connection issues were happening \u201cin the Americas,\u201d but over time it became clear the troubles were global.\n\nDowndetector showed a peak of 130,000 reports about Outlook outages, but that figure understated the scope of the problem, since most people do not report their experiences.\n\nAccording to Downdetector, the number of reports slowed as the business day ended in the US.\n\nAround that time, Microsoft indicated that some servers were back up and running, but email access was still sporadic for many users.\n\n\u201cOur actions taken thus far have not provided the anticipated relief,\u201d the company said in a post at 10:09 p.m. GMT.\n\nMicrosoft tried to reassure customers that it was taking all possible steps to restore access.\n\nBut a subsequent update said that \u201cthe servers we returned to service can become unresponsive again once they receive traffic\u201d \u2014 in other words, Outlook was down again."
