    "chunk_size_bytes": 2000,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 brings relief closer to home\n\nColombo, Nov. 13 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget offers salaried taxpayers higher take-home pay from April, while lower-income households stand to benefit from continued welfare payments and lower import levies on selected foods. For communities hit by Cyclone Ditwah, the Government is seeking Rs. 260 billion to carry reconstruction into next year.\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake presented the Budget, third under his government, to Parliament yesterday against a backdrop of renewed price pressure. Annual inflation rose to 8.3% in October from 2.1% last December, due to higher fuel prices linked to the Middle East conflict among the factors. The economy grew 5% in 2025, but growth slowed to 4.2% in the second quarter of this year from 5.1% in the first.\n\n\u201cIt is our duty to build economic stability, better jobs, fair incomes, a quality public service, a safe social environment and a sustainable future for future generations,\u201d Dissanayake told Parliament.\n\nThe earliest changes are due at the border on Friday. The import levy on sprats is set to fall to Rs. 10 from Rs. 100 a kilogram, and that on Maldive fish to Rs. 10 from Rs. 302. The levy on dried chillies, including powdered chillies, is due to fall by Rs. 50 a kilogram. How much shoppers save will depend on what importers and retailers pass on. The Budget separately proposes raising the levy on imported sugar by Rs. 10 a kilogram to support local producers.\n\nFrom April, the Government proposes raising the monthly income tax threshold to Rs. 162,500 from Rs. 150,000. A worker earning Rs. 160,000 a month in salary and allowances would fall below the proposed threshold. Wider bands would also reduce tax bills further up the income scale, with the top rate of 36% applying from about Rs. 433,000 a month, up from Rs. 358,000. Dissanayake put the additional monthly relief for taxpayers at between Rs. 750 and Rs. 15,750, depending on income."
