    "chunk_text": "From April, the Government proposes raising the monthly income tax threshold to Rs. 162,500 from Rs. 150,000. A worker earning Rs. 160,000 a month in salary and allowances would fall below the proposed threshold. Wider bands would also reduce tax bills further up the income scale, with the top rate of 36% applying from about Rs. 433,000 a month, up from Rs. 358,000. Dissanayake put the additional monthly relief for taxpayers at between Rs. 750 and Rs. 15,750, depending on income.\n\nPublic servants are due to receive the final phase of the basic salary revision announced in 2025, with Rs. 110 billion allocated for 2027. Across all three phases, the revision raises the minimum monthly basic salary by Rs. 15,750. The Government also proposes higher allowances for selected public employees, including doubling the monthly special duty allowance for nurses and staff in paramedical and supplementary medical services to Rs. 12,000.\n\nFor public servants who retired before 2020, the final phase of an earlier pension adjustment is due next year. Those who retired from 2020 through 2024 would receive an interim allowance of Rs. 2,500 a month from July, pending a phased pension revision planned to begin with the 2028 Budget.\n\nThe Government has allocated Rs. 131 billion in capital spending for public healthcare. The 1990 Suwa Seriya service is due to add 100 ambulances next year with Indian assistance, while a separate Rs. 3 billion is proposed to replace ageing ambulances in the wider health service. The Budget also proposes additional funding for equipment including MRI and CT scanners.\n\nTo bring care closer to communities, the Government has set aside Rs. 3.6 billion for Arogya Wellness Centres in 2027. It plans to build 1,000 centres with provincial councils by 2029, following a pilot involving 39 centres."
  },
