    "md5": "f1f89adbdda26e0db3c3dce483b2a487",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1764,
    "chunk_text": "To bring care closer to communities, the Government has set aside Rs. 3.6 billion for Arogya Wellness Centres in 2027. It plans to build 1,000 centres with provincial councils by 2029, following a pilot involving 39 centres.\n\nA further Rs. 77 billion is allocated for drinking water, sanitation and water supplies for economic activity. The Budget provides Rs. 5.19 billion to start 13 drinking water projects and Rs. 2 billion to improve community water schemes. It also proposes Rs. 1 billion to tackle problems including seawater intrusion and bacterial contamination in Jaffna and the Vanni, and allocates Rs. 1.74 billion for wastewater projects in Ella, Moratuwa and Ekala.\n\nThe proposed Rs. 260 billion supplementary estimate would restore in 2027 the unused portion of a previously approved Rs. 500 billion reconstruction package. Dissanayake cited a post-disaster assessment that put Ditwah\u2019s damage and economic losses at about US$3.4 billion, with housing, industry, railways, farms, schools and health services affected.\n\nIncluding the supplementary estimate, the Treasury projects a 2027 budget deficit of 4.5% of gross domestic product, compared with 3.7% in the revised estimate for this year. The deficit is projected at 3.8% of GDP before the additional reconstruction provision.\n\nThe Government is also looking to small businesses to support jobs and incomes. It has allocated Rs. 67.69 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing, which it expects to support Rs. 174 billion in concessional lending through State and private banks. For larger projects, it expects to seek proposals within two months for a public-private logistics centre at the Port of Colombo and bring public-private partnership legislation to Parliament early next year."
