    "md5": "8bde96a9f4fe5d5744b9c55d0ccbb23d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "What we need is not merely a Government that collects revenue, but a Government that delivers value to the people for every tax rupee it collects. What we need is not a large Government machinery, but an efficient and transparent Government machinery that is accountable to the people.\n\nOur policy is to recognize, at every level of governance, that the true owners of public money are the citizens of this country.\n\nEvery Budget proposal we present is part of a continuous programme moving towards an interconnected common goal. Moreover, this Budget has not been prepared based solely on the views of one individual or one party. It is a programme prepared collectively, incorporating the views, proposals and experiences of various stakeholders. Due attention has been paid to every proposal submitted to us. This Budget has been prepared based on our policy priorities, available resources, fiscal space and implementation capacity.\n\nIt is no exaggeration to say that this economic stability of our country was built during a most difficult period. The war raging in the Middle East is becoming more prolonged than ever before, creating great uncertainty in the world. The closure of the Strait of Hormuz has affected nearly 20 percent of global petroleum shipments. Accordingly, the increase in fuel prices, premiums and shipping and insurance charges had a very adverse impact on our energy market. We have still not been able to free ourselves from the pressure this created on our economy and on people\u2019s lives. As a result, more than Rs. 147 billion was spent to minimize the impact on the people and on economic stability.\n\nFurther, according to the Post-Disaster Needs Assessment (PDNA) report, the damage caused by Cyclone Ditwah to housing, industries, railway systems, agriculture, education and the health system has been estimated at USD 2 billion, which, together with economic losses of USD 1.4 billion, amounts to a total of about USD 3.4 billion (Rs.1.022 trillion)."
