    "md5": "240efada0b9bb10ddaef2236606e3c61",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1755,
    "chunk_text": "Further, according to the Post-Disaster Needs Assessment (PDNA) report, the damage caused by Cyclone Ditwah to housing, industries, railway systems, agriculture, education and the health system has been estimated at USD 2 billion, which, together with economic losses of USD 1.4 billion, amounts to a total of about USD 3.4 billion (Rs.1.022 trillion).\n\nAs forecast by the World Meteorological Organization, it has been confirmed that there is a 100 percent probability that the El Ni\u00f1o weather condition that has now developed will reach its peak in February 2027. This El Ni\u00f1o weather condition poses a risk of adverse impacts on agriculture, food prices, water resources and hydropower. We consider it one of our foremost responsibilities to identify such risks that may arise both globally and domestically in advance, and to further stabilize the economy so that it is capable of facing them.\n\nOur target is not only to manage today\u2019s economic challenges, but also to transform the country into one where future generations will never again have to face an economic crisis such as the one that prevailed before. The essence of our policy statement, \u201cA Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life\u201d, is not merely to improve the performance of economic indicators, but to create a society in which people can live with dignity and pride, and a country that has confidence in its future.\n\nThe main objective of this Budget is to move from economic stability towards transformation. It is to achieve a progressive transformation in every sphere, including investment, exports, productive employment, social welfare and sustainability.\n\nDuring the past two years, our government has succeeded in achieving remarkable macroeconomic stability. Accordingly;"
