    "md5": "1fe39c0448ca186fd17d27044e03a623",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1632,
    "chunk_text": "The main objective of this Budget is to move from economic stability towards transformation. It is to achieve a progressive transformation in every sphere, including investment, exports, productive employment, social welfare and sustainability.\n\nDuring the past two years, our government has succeeded in achieving remarkable macroeconomic stability. Accordingly;\n\nThe negative primary balance, which had recorded over a long period, reported the highest positive balance of 5.4 percent in 2025 in its history. Against the positive primary balance target of Rs. 360 billion for end-2026, a positive primary balance of Rs. 1,844 billion was recorded by end-September.\n\nIn 2025, the budget deficit recorded its lowest value in 68 years at 2.3 percent.\n\nGovernment revenue, which was at a historic low of 8.2 percent of GDP in 2022, reached 16.6 percent in 2025, the highest value recorded in 23 years, amounting to about 110 percent of the expected revenue.\n\nIn 2025, we were able to record the highest-ever current account surplus in history of USD 1,719 million.\n\nIn 2025, workers\u2019 remittances recorded the highest value in history at USD 8.1 billion. Further, by October this year, it amounted to USD 7.6 billion. Accordingly, remittances exceeding USD 9 billion are expected this year.\n\nCentral Government debt, which was 114 percent of GDP at the end of 2022, was reduced to 95 percent by the end of 2025 as per the records of Public Debt Management Office (PDMO).\n\nIn 2022, 79.1 percent of Government revenue was spent on interest payments, which declined further to 45.9 percent by 2025 and to 39.6 percent by September 2026."
