    "chunk_text": "Central Government debt, which was 114 percent of GDP at the end of 2022, was reduced to 95 percent by the end of 2025 as per the records of Public Debt Management Office (PDMO).\n\nIn 2022, 79.1 percent of Government revenue was spent on interest payments, which declined further to 45.9 percent by 2025 and to 39.6 percent by September 2026.\n\nEven though total fuel import expenditure increased to USD 4,487 million by September 2026 compared to USD 4,042 million in 2025 due to the increase in world market fuel prices, and amidst the opening of the vehicle import market, foreign reserves were recorded at over USD 7 billion by October 2026.\n\nNevertheless, annual inflation, which was recorded at 2.1 percent in December 2025, increased to 8.3 percent by October 2026 due to the rise in fuel prices in the face of the Middle East conflict and other related factors.\n\nCredit extended by commercial banks to the private sector increased by 25.2 percent, from Rs. 8,156 billion in 2024 to Rs. 10,212 billion in 2025.\n\nDuring the past two years, Sri Lanka has been able to achieve significant progress in global rankings, including economic and social rankings.\n\nAccording to the 2026 Fitch Ratings, Sri Lanka has been upgraded from \u2018CCC+\u2019 to \u2018B-\u2019.\n\nAccording to the Corruption Perceptions Index (2025/2026) issued by Transparency International, Sri Lanka has advanced 14 places in the global rankings to 107th place.\n\nAccording to the Global Peace Index (2026), Sri Lanka advanced 14 places to be ranked 67th globally.\n\nAccording to GlobeTrotter & Flash Pack, in 2025, Sri Lanka was ranked first in the world index under the solo travellers category.\n\nThe World Economic Forum has named Sri Lanka 10th in the world among the countries that have shown the fastest progress among tourism economies.\n\nThe international magazine Cond\u00e9 Nast has named Sri Lanka the best destination in the world for the whole family to travel together."
