    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bc368f5665a6a900795d3b533424cc66",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "The World Economic Forum has named Sri Lanka 10th in the world among the countries that have shown the fastest progress among tourism economies.\n\nThe international magazine Cond\u00e9 Nast has named Sri Lanka the best destination in the world for the whole family to travel together.\n\nThe World Bank had classified Sri Lanka as a lower middle-income country in 2020, down from the status of an upper middle-income country. Reflecting the recovery of the economy, the classification was upgraded again in 2026 to an upper middle-income country.\n\nThe above facts are not the only criteria that measure the success of our fiscal policy. The Government\u2019s support for and care of distressed children, the elderly, persons with disabilities, those suffering from chronic illnesses, low-income households and families who have lost their livelihoods due to disasters are equally important.\n\nThrough the 2025 Budget, we laid the foundation for economic stability and social justice. Through the 2026 Budget, greater attention was paid to production, investment, digitalization and institutional reforms. What we propose through the 2027 Budget is to implement the reforms and proposals for a new economic transformation required, based on the economic stability we have achieved so far, to increase production, to involve the people as stakeholders engaged in the economy, to ensure that the gains achieved in the economy are distributed fairly among the people, and to achieve the sovereignty of the national economy.\n\nEconomic transformation does not mean presenting yet another list of expenditures. It means that every investment made by the Government, every tax concession granted, every borrowing undertaken and every social protection programme proposed will be assessed on the basis of the economic and social benefits generated through them."
