    "md5": "85b3e39b2ffd30eaf61662817a9c59ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "Economic transformation does not mean presenting yet another list of expenditures. It means that every investment made by the Government, every tax concession granted, every borrowing undertaken and every social protection programme proposed will be assessed on the basis of the economic and social benefits generated through them.\n\nWhen the Budget for 2026 was presented to this House, we emphasized the need to build an economy that can move forward unshaken in the face of internal and external challenges. At the end of 2025, Cyclone Ditwah severely affected the lives of our people, production activities and infrastructure. Due to the Middle East conflict that escalated in the first half of 2026, new pressure exerted on energy prices, import costs and supply chains.\n\nIn facing a disaster such as Cyclone Ditwah, what was important for a Government was that we had built fiscal space in advance. Through this, we were able to strengthen the financial capacity required to provide relief to the people affected by the disaster, to maintain essential services and to deploy resources for reconstruction activities according to priorities.\n\nThe fundamental structural macroeconomic problem that Sri Lanka has faced for a long time has been the deficits in the budget, the balance of payments and the current account. Although we were able to reduce the budget deficit significantly through correct revenue and expenditure policies, the external sector has not yet been strengthened as required. While a current account surplus of USD 1,853 million was recorded in the first nine months of 2025, a deficit of USD 420 million was recorded in the corresponding period of 2026. The progress achieved in the fiscal sector must be made the foundation for a structural transformation that strengthens the external sector."
