    "md5": "98f82e24006fdb65c419aa21d9a1a52f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1713,
    "chunk_text": "The fundamental structural macroeconomic problem that Sri Lanka has faced for a long time has been the deficits in the budget, the balance of payments and the current account. Although we were able to reduce the budget deficit significantly through correct revenue and expenditure policies, the external sector has not yet been strengthened as required. While a current account surplus of USD 1,853 million was recorded in the first nine months of 2025, a deficit of USD 420 million was recorded in the corresponding period of 2026. The progress achieved in the fiscal sector must be made the foundation for a structural transformation that strengthens the external sector.\n\nWe need a long-term plan capable of withstanding these conditions.\n\n2.1 Safeguarding Macroeconomic Stability and Strengthening the Resilience of the Economy\n\nWhile safeguarding macroeconomic and fiscal stability, our main objective is to ensure debt sustainability and to prepare Sri Lanka to face future internal and external shocks. For this, sustainable Government revenue, prudent expenditure management, planned debt repayments, adequate fiscal space and strong foreign reserves are essential. It is also important to maintain adequate coordination between fiscal and monetary policies, while respecting the legal mandate of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka in maintaining price stability.\n\nCentral Government debt stood at 114 percent in 2022, the year in which the economy was plunged into crisis. What is taken into consideration in debt sustainability is the capacity to repay debt relative to GDP and Government revenue, and whether there is adequate liquidity to settle the debt servicing obligations falling due in the near term."
