    "md5": "1c23dce697b16cac8fdd71afcf841af5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1797,
    "chunk_text": "Our objective is to maintain Government revenue at a level not less than 16 percent of GDP in the medium term, and to gradually increase it to about 20 percent in the long term. To achieve these targets, the necessary reforms will be implemented to broaden the tax base, rationalize tax exemptions and incentives, digitalize tax administration and strengthen tax compliance.\n\nWe expect to maintain public investment at a level exceeding 4 percent of GDP from 2027 onwards. In 2027, capital expenditure of Rs. 2,000 billion has been allocated, amounting to total public investment of 5.12 percent of GDP, comprising 4.48 percent through the Budget and 0.64 percent through the Supplementary Estimate for Ditwah.\n\nThe Extended Fund Facility programme entered into with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) is scheduled to conclude in March next year. We expect to successfully complete the 17th IMF programme. While the programme to stabilize the economy domestically is proceeding successfully, it is difficult to forecast the impacts arising from global and natural disasters at a time when we are preparing for an economic transformation for growth. We believe that our reserves must be further strengthened to successfully face such situations.\n\nTherefore, the Cabinet of Ministers has appointed a committee headed by the Deputy Minister of Finance and Planning to examine and submit a report on the suitability of entering into another programme after the successful completion of the current IMF programme, and on what type of programme it should be.\n\nFor social justice and the protection of the people, targeted social protection programmes will be further strengthened to protect low-income and vulnerable households who face the highest risk in the face of economic and natural disasters."
  },
