    "md5": "2b0db425347ee1a31fed20b1bbcb2850",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "For social justice and the protection of the people, targeted social protection programmes will be further strengthened to protect low-income and vulnerable households who face the highest risk in the face of economic and natural disasters.\n\nA new and strong legal framework and institutional structure are required to achieve the economic transformation we envisage and to enhance efficiency. Some of our laws are more than 100 years old. The historical role of some of our institutions has come to an end. Those laws and institutions have become shackles that bind our feet from reaching the economic transformation we envisage. Therefore, in order to achieve the new economic transformation, it is necessary toreformulate old and outdated laws and institutions so that they can face present day challenges, and to build new laws and institutions that correspond to the new circumstances that have arisen. Hence, we cannot move forward without removing these shackles. Accordingly;\n\nThe \u201cRescue, Rehabilitation and Insolvency (Corporate and Personal) Act, No. 12 of 2026\u201d has been passed to provide an opportunity for businesses that collapsed due to various crises such as pandemics, natural disasters and terrorist acts to rebuild, contribute to the economy and repay their creditors.\n\nThe laws required to strengthen the prevention of money laundering have been passed through the \u201cPrevention of Money Laundering (Amendment) Act, No. 16 of 2026\u201d.\n\nThe \u201cFinancial Transactions Reporting (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2026\u201d has made provisions to minimize the risk arising from transactions not being reported to the financial system and to enforce the law against non-compliers.\n\nThrough the \u201cConvention on the Suppression of Terrorist Financing (Amendment) Act, No. 18 of 2026\u201d, the financing of global terrorism using Sri Lanka, which poses a threat to national security, has been prevented by strengthening the financial system."
  },
