    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4c5dadbbfb3c71f6389b12567b8b61dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "We expect to update the existing Free Trade Agreements and enter into new trade agreements with more countries in order to connect Sri Lanka to the global trade network.\n\nAn officials\u2019 committee has already been appointed by the Cabinet of Ministers to expand the existing Free Trade Agreement with India.\n\nAn application has been made to accede to the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP).\n\nIt is expected to enter into Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreements with the United Arab Emirates and Oman.\n\nThe \u201cSri Lanka\u2013European Union Sustainable Trade and Economic Integration Project\u201d will be implemented under a European Union grant of Rs. 3,430 million during the period 2027-2030, to promote export competitiveness, export diversification and sustainable production.\n\nFurther, we will work to enter into new Free Trade Agreements with other countries following a feasibility study.\n\nA transformative change in the energy sector is required for the future economic transformation, national security and the improvement of the living standards of the people. The plans required for a new transformation in the energy sector, aimed at ensuring a stable and uninterrupted energy supply and reducing production costs, have been included in this Budget.\n\nToday, around 62 percent of our installed capacity comes from renewable energy. Plans have been prepared to increase this to 69 percent by 2029.\n\nAs energy sources that vary with weather and seasonal conditions, such as solar and wind power, are increasingly added, we must pay greater attention to the stability control of the system. The national power system failures that occurred in February 2025 and October 2026 were an important lesson for us to reflect on our system protection, frequency control, power flow management and network protection mechanisms. We expect to make an investment of Rs. 26.5 billion to technically strengthen our grid system so that such incidents do not recur."
  },
