    "md5": "b5bc2e3e56ec660976a428ec1637d4f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "As energy sources that vary with weather and seasonal conditions, such as solar and wind power, are increasingly added, we must pay greater attention to the stability control of the system. The national power system failures that occurred in February 2025 and October 2026 were an important lesson for us to reflect on our system protection, frequency control, power flow management and network protection mechanisms. We expect to make an investment of Rs. 26.5 billion to technically strengthen our grid system so that such incidents do not recur.\n\nA Wide Area Monitoring System will be established under the \u201cWorld Bank Technical Assistance Project\u201d to identify unstable conditions in the system in advance and to make prompt and effective decisions in emergencies.\n\nWe have already introduced Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS) to store the surplus electricity we receive during the daytime. In 2026, 160 MW is received by the system through BESS. Plans have been made for this quantity to be 560 MW in 2027 and for the total quantity to be 1,245 MW by 2029.\n\nThe price selected in the first phase (160 MW) of calling bids for the BESS system in 2026 was Rs. 2.033 million per megawatt per month. In the second phase in 2026, when bids were called for 250 megawatts, the bid received had decreased to Rs. 1.808 million per megawatt per month. Accordingly, through competitive bidding, it has been possible to reduce the price by around 11 percent within this year itself.\n\nWith the aim of promoting renewable energy generation, enhancing energy security, and ensuring stability by minimizing imbalances in the national power system, it is proposed to provide a subsidy for the purchase of the batteries required for energy storage to domestic consumers who install rooftop solar power systems equipped with battery storage facilities under the Net Plus scheme."
  },
