    "md5": "328581cb4d3c1c9a37cbb41c7cdd2c97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1675,
    "chunk_text": "A new capacity of 370 MW generated from wind power will be added to the system by 2029. Of this, steps will be taken to add 50 MW in 2027. By the end of 2029, the installed capacity generated from wind power will increase to 674 MW.\n\nFour Distribution Control Centres will be established in Kuliyapitiya, Peradeniya, Battaramulla and Dehiwala to manage power failures, optimize the power grid, manage electricity demand, integrate and manage solar energy resources and other distributed energy resources into the power system, and strengthen data-driven planning of electricity distribution systems.\n\nThe Petroleum Development Authority of Sri Lanka (PDASL) has called for bids for oil and natural gas exploration and production (Sri Lanka Licencing Round 2026) in 4 blocks in the Mannar Basin, located off the western coast. The bids are expected to be opened on February 2, 2027.\n\nTo increase fuel storage capacity, 6 tanks with a capacity of 64,000 m3 are being constructed at the Kolonnawa Petroleum Complex with an investment of Rs. 3,746 million in 2025, 2 tanks with a capacity of 22,000 m3 with an investment of Rs. 1,446 million at the end of this year, and 3 tanks with a capacity of 40,000 m3 at the Muthurajawela Storage Terminal with an investment of Rs. 3,786 million in September 2026. Accordingly, our storage capacity will increase by 126,000 m3, up to the requirement for 45 days.\n\nBids have been called for the construction of a pipeline system from Kolonnawa to the Port with an investment of Rs. 16,332 million, with the aim of reducing the time taken to unload oil from ships from 96 hours to 48 hours. This is expected to be completed within 18 months."
