    "language": "en",
    "md5": "40751c5d8e066328ee1cfb25b9162b87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Bids have been called for the construction of a pipeline system from Kolonnawa to the Port with an investment of Rs. 16,332 million, with the aim of reducing the time taken to unload oil from ships from 96 hours to 48 hours. This is expected to be completed within 18 months.\n\nThe Sapugaskanda Oil Refinery will be upgraded from its current limit of 50,000 barrels per day to a capacity of 100,000 barrels under a public-private partnership. For this, Requests for Proposals (RFP) will be called in 2027 based on the Expressions of Interest (EOI) already called.\n\nDigital transformation is no longer merely a technological initiative, but a fundamental requirement for improving the efficiency of the public sector, strengthening governance, facilitating economic growth, and ensuring that every citizen can access Government services. Digitalisation has been identified as a fundamental tool for modernizing public administration, improving the efficiency and transparency of Government activities, and strengthening institutional capacity.\n\nReaching the level of development this sector has attained in today\u2019s world is a challenging and multifaceted process. At the same time, this sector is constantly evolving. Therefore, in order to reap its true benefits, the necessary legal and institutional structure must first be built.\n\nThe GovTech institution has been established under the 100 percent ownership of the Treasury to guide and implement the digitalization of public services, and has already commenced its operations.\n\nThe Personal Data Protection Act has been amended and the Data Protection Authority has already been established and made operational.\n\nThe Bill for the establishment of the Digital Economy Authority and the National Digital Economy Council (Digital Economy Bill) is expected to be passed in the first quarter of 2027."
