    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dea08beecceeb2aa6b6996ff5ba3b1ad",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "The feasibility study currently being carried out for the development of the Colombo North Port is expected to be completed in the first half of next year. It is expected to spend Rs. 300 million from the funds of the Ports Authority to expeditiously carry out the additional study on the Kelani River estuary, which is a decisive factor in the development of the North Port.\n\nRequests for Proposals (RFP) for the establishment of a Logistics Centre in the Port of Colombo through a public-private partnership will be called within the next 2 months.\n\nWith the number of vessels arriving at Sri Lankan ports increasing day by day, there is great potential to expand and strengthen bunkering capacity and to establish Sri Lanka as a competitive regional bunkering hub. Accordingly, with the support of the Ceylon Petroleum Corporation (CPC), it is expected to increase capacity by 50,000 metric tons within the Port of Colombo under the participation of the private sector on a Build-Operate-Transfer (BOT) basis.\n\nThe Ceylon Petroleum Corporation and the private sector will work jointly to develop the storage and pipeline systems required for bunkering services centred on the Port of Galle.\n\nThere is potential to develop the Port of Trincomalee as a fuel storage hub as well as a strategic hub that can be connected to the energy supply network of the South Asian region. Steps will be taken to develop this jointly through State and international partnerships.\n\nRepair work on 2 of the 5 selected tanks in the Trincomalee Upper Tank Farm has already been completed. The construction of a new pipeline system from the tank farm to the port has been planned. The Ceylon Petroleum Corporation expects to spend Rs. 7,375 million for this purpose."
  },
