    "md5": "b1e4257a2da415924ed20da47f08f8e0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "Repair work on 2 of the 5 selected tanks in the Trincomalee Upper Tank Farm has already been completed. The construction of a new pipeline system from the tank farm to the port has been planned. The Ceylon Petroleum Corporation expects to spend Rs. 7,375 million for this purpose.\n\nWe believe that once these development activities are implemented, Sri Lanka\u2019s maritime competitiveness will be significantly strengthened and the country will be established as a key energy hub in the global shipping and maritime industry, including offshore bunkering.\n\nAt present, although the passenger capacity of the first terminal of the Bandaranaike International Airport is around 6 million, a larger number of passengers is being handled. It has been forecast that a capacity of 16 million will be required by 2032. Accordingly, increasing the passenger capacity of the airport has already been delayed. Therefore, we consider its development a priority.\n\nWork to increase the capacity of the first terminal from 6 million to 9 million has already commenced with an investment of Rs. 15,000 million by Airport and Aviation Services.\n\nConstruction work on the delayed second terminal is scheduled to commence very soon with the assistance of the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA).\n\nConstruction of a Domestic Air Terminal within the airport premises will commence next year by Airport and Aviation Services (Sri Lanka) Limited (AASL) with an investment of Rs. 800 million, to enable international air passengers to travel more conveniently to domestic destinations.\n\nA feasibility study will be carried out for the widening of the road in front of the airport and the construction of a flyover to minimize traffic congestion at and around the airport. It is proposed to allocate Rs. 200 million for this purpose.\n\nA new car park will be constructed by AASL at a cost of Rs. 400 million to minimize vehicle congestion in and around the airport."
