    "chunk_text": "An Integrated Plant Nutrient Management System (IPNM) is required to promote the integrated use of chemical fertilizer and organic fertilizer, increase crop productivity and strengthen soil quality and nutrient status. It is proposed to implement a 4-year project for this purpose, and Rs. 47 million has been allocated for the year 2027 as an initial step.\n\nRs. 400 million has been allocated for the development of the entire value chain, aimed at enhancing the productivity, quality and global market access of the cinnamon industry.\n\nIt is expected to develop and maintain identified farms of the National Livestock Development Board as \u201cBreeding Farms\u201d. Rs. 1,800 million has been allocated for the importation of foundation breeding animal stocks of high genetic quality required to establish specialized Nucleus Herds of \u201cdairy cattle and buffaloes\u201d at the Nikaweratiya Farm, \u201cpigs\u201d at the Horakele Farm, and \u201csheep\u201d at the Siringapatha Farm.\n\nProvisions have been allocated for a feasibility study to establish a special economic zone, centred on the Western and North Western Provinces, for the export of chicken and chicken-related value-added products to foreign markets.\n\nProvisions of Rs. 3,000 million were provided in 2026 to recommence the construction of the new Milco milk processing factory at Badalgama, on which EUR 57 million had been spent but which had been suspended for a long time. Rs. 3,000 million has been allocated for the year 2027 to complete its work and establish the Milco factory there.\n\nThis project, commenced in 2015 and was to be completed in 2019, has given rise to many problems due to its suspension for a long period, such as damage to machinery and construction, additional rehabilitation costs, increases in the prices of machinery parts, and the submission of delay claims by the contractor. Milco has already commenced discussions with the contractor, DESMI of Denmark, to resolve these issues."
