    "chunk_text": "This project, commenced in 2015 and was to be completed in 2019, has given rise to many problems due to its suspension for a long period, such as damage to machinery and construction, additional rehabilitation costs, increases in the prices of machinery parts, and the submission of delay claims by the contractor. Milco has already commenced discussions with the contractor, DESMI of Denmark, to resolve these issues.\n\nDeveloping and maintaining a strong irrigation system is essential to increase agricultural production, ensure food security, strengthen the livelihoods of rural people and efficiently manage water resources in the face of climate change. Through this Budget, Rs. 77,000 million has been allocated, providing adequate and prioritized financial provisions for irrigation development, to accelerate ongoing irrigation development activities and to commence new projects.\n\nRs. 21,000 million has been estimated for the rehabilitation of the cascaded tank systems damaged by \u201cDitwah\u201d and the associated minor irrigation infrastructure and agricultural roads. These programmes commenced in 2026 with the Ditwah provisions, and the remaining provisions required will be provided in 2027.\n\nAlthough a feasibility study on the Jaffna Irrigation Project was carried out in 2018 to improve the water security of the people of the Jaffna Peninsula, it has not been implemented to date. Rs. 75 million has been allocated for further feasibility studies, including on this project.\n\nRs. 7,500 million has been allocated to accelerate the work of the Lower Malwathu Oya Project, construction of which has already commenced, for the agricultural and social development of the Anuradhapura, Vavuniya and Mannar districts.\n\nIt is proposed to allocate Rs. 1,000 million to meet the water requirements of the Batticaloa and Ampara districts, minimize flood risk and commence the Mundeni Aru River Basin Development Project."
