    "chunk_text": "A large number of vacancies existed due to the non-recruitment of health staff over a long period. In order to deliver a quality health service, the required human resources have been recruited, namely 1,797 doctors, 303 indigenous medical practitioners, 64 dental surgeons, 3,441 nurses, 952 public health midwives and 3,415 support staff in various areas.\n\nIn response to the requests of the people of those areas and to provide an optimal service at the level of national hospitals, it is proposed to transfer to the central Government the District General Hospitals of Mannar, Mullaitivu, Vavuniya, Kilinochchi, Gampaha and Avissawella, which are administered by the Provincial Councils, out of the 20 District General Hospitals.\n\nIt is proposed to allocate Rs. 300 million for the construction of an Accident and Emergency Treatment Unit with modern facilities at the Thambuttegama Base Hospital.\n\nProvisions of Rs. 65,587 million are required to fully meet, within 03 years, the requirement for high-end biomedical equipment identified through a formal study, including MRI, CT and Cath Lab, which is necessary to enhance diagnostic and treatment services in all hospitals. For this, it is proposed to allocate Rs. 5,000 million in addition to the provisions of Rs. 15,500 million allocated for the year 2027.\n\nThere are 400 ambulances in the health service that are more than 20 years old, of which around 50 percent are more than 25 years old. In order to ensure a better quality and more efficient service to the people, it is proposed to allocate Rs. 3,000 million in 2027 to add new ambulances to the system in place of the old ambulances. In addition, 25 ambulances through Asian Development Bank loan assistance and 20 new ambulances as a grant from Japan, coordinated by the Asian Development Bank, will be added within the next year."
