    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "In addition to the development projects currently being implemented in hospitals providing secondary and tertiary care services, Rs. 1,245 million has been allocated to commence a number of new projects, including official quarters, at the Badulla and Batticaloa Teaching Hospitals, the Matale and Nawalapitiya District General Hospitals, and the Puttalam Base Hospital.\n\nIt is proposed to establish a centre within the Faculty of Medicine, Ragama to train doctors in Robotic Surgery. It is expected to provide training for the work of this centre in collaboration with foreign universities. It is proposed to allocate Rs. 250 million for this purpose.\n\nThe \u201cRabies-Free Sri Lanka by 2030\u201d program is being implemented with the goal of eradicating canine rabies in the country by 2030, primarily through the administration of rabies vaccines to dogs and the streamlining of canine sterilization programs. It is proposed that this initiative be carried out as a collaborative effort, integrating the canine rabies control program managed under the Ministry of Health and Mass Media with technical support from the Department of Animal Production and Health, while also utilizing the infrastructure available within local government institutions. We intend to ensure that the allocations already provided to the institutions dealing with this matter are properly coordinated and implemented.\n\nThe diversity of Sri Lanka\u2019s tourist attractions is the most important and strongest potential of the country\u2019s tourism industry. Tourism is one of the main sectors that activates the country\u2019s economy, earns foreign exchange and generates employment. The earnings from tourist arrivals by September this year amounted to USD 2,245 million. Therefore, attention has been paid to uplifting the rural economy and generating employment through the tourism industry."
