    "md5": "38a80af2e7312254e48bbd4d9eeecab2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1834,
    "chunk_text": "The diversity of Sri Lanka\u2019s tourist attractions is the most important and strongest potential of the country\u2019s tourism industry. Tourism is one of the main sectors that activates the country\u2019s economy, earns foreign exchange and generates employment. The earnings from tourist arrivals by September this year amounted to USD 2,245 million. Therefore, attention has been paid to uplifting the rural economy and generating employment through the tourism industry.\n\nRs. 35 million has been allocated, in addition to the capital contribution of Rs. 133 million provided by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA), to establish a centralized online e-ticketing system covering the main tourist attractions of Sri Lanka.\n\nWith the financial assistance of the World Bank, at a cost of USD 77 million, the historic and ancient buildings located around Colombo Fort will be restored in a manner that protects their historical value and developed as a tourism zone.\n\nIt is proposed to allocate Rs. 500 million to develop the Indian-assisted trilingual school premises in Polonnaruwa into a hotel school, including trilingual teaching, and to develop the Pinnawala Hotel School.\n\nThe historic Dutch forts located in the areas of Jaffna, Mannar, Kalpitiya and Batticaloa will be promoted as tourism development hubs under public-private partnerships (PPP), while ensuring the antiquarian value and conservation of the historic heritage. It is expected to deploy Rs. 240 million for this purpose from the underutilized funds of the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA).\n\nIt is expected to deploy Rs. 500 million from the funds of the Sri Lanka Tourism Promotion Bureau (SLTPB) for a concessionary loan scheme for tourism service providers to obtain the necessary equipment, including boats, to promote Marine Tourism."
