    "chunk_size_bytes": 1856,
    "chunk_text": "It is expected to deploy Rs. 500 million from the funds of the Sri Lanka Tourism Promotion Bureau (SLTPB) for a concessionary loan scheme for tourism service providers to obtain the necessary equipment, including boats, to promote Marine Tourism.\n\nIt is expected to further strengthen the Tourist Police service to promote Sri Lanka as a safe tourist destination. It is proposed to provide a prompt and efficient security service to tourists by providing specialized training, deploying officers with multilingual capabilities, establishing special patrol units around major tourist sites, and introducing a 24-hour tourist emergency hotline service and a mobile application.\n\nIt is our duty to safeguard environmental balance for sustainable economic development. It is one of our fundamental responsibilities to secure the natural resources of the country for future generations by facing the challenges of climate change, protecting biodiversity and moving towards a green economy approach.\n\nOur Government, which has understood the importance of the wildlife sector, has recruited 313 wildlife guards and 3,451 electric fence maintenance assistants to strengthen the services of the Department of Wildlife Conservation for its protection and management. Provisions have been allocated for the recruitment of 114 wildlife rangers. Vehicles and equipment, including 16 double cabs, 84 single cabs, 181 motorcycles and 09 tractors, have been added in 2026 for field operations to deliver wildlife services efficiently.\n\nWith the aim of minimizing the human-elephant conflict and protecting human lives and wild elephants, the Handapanagala, Koholankala and Wetahira Kanda elephant corridors were released in June 2026, and it is expected to declare the elephant corridor located between the Nachchaduwa Wewa and Maha Kanadarawa Wewa as a wildlife reserve."
