    "chunk_size_bytes": 1805,
    "chunk_text": "With the aim of minimizing the human-elephant conflict and protecting human lives and wild elephants, the Handapanagala, Koholankala and Wetahira Kanda elephant corridors were released in June 2026, and it is expected to declare the elephant corridor located between the Nachchaduwa Wewa and Maha Kanadarawa Wewa as a wildlife reserve.\n\nA pilot project has been launched using Artificial Intelligence (AI) and thermal cameras to minimize accidents caused by wild elephants colliding with trains. Accordingly, it has been possible to reduce the number of such deaths this year. Based on its results, it is expected to implement this project nationally.\n\nA land area of 03 acres has been identified within the Nuwaragam Forest Reserve in the Anuradhapura district to construct a hospital capable of treating wild animals for animal welfare. It is expected to allocate Rs. 700 million from the Wildlife Fund for the establishment of this hospital.\n\nStranded baby elephants from all areas are currently rehabilitated at the Udawalawe Elephant Transit Home, and their lives are at risk due to the need to transport the baby elephants over very long distances. Therefore, it is expected to provide estimated provisions of Rs. 5,000 million in several phasesfrom the Wildlife Fund for the construction of a new elephant transit home on a land area of 15 acres in the Polonnaruwa district.\n\nRs. 50 million has been allocated from the Wildlife Fund for the initial work on constructing a centre for the treatment and rehabilitation of injured marine animals such as turtles and dolphins on a land area of 2 acres in the Dodanduwa area.\n\nWe expect to further strengthen the legal framework to protect the wild elephant resource, which is a living heritage of Sri Lanka, and to prevent the cruelty inflicted on them."
