    "chunk_text": "Rs. 50 million has been allocated from the Wildlife Fund for the initial work on constructing a centre for the treatment and rehabilitation of injured marine animals such as turtles and dolphins on a land area of 2 acres in the Dodanduwa area.\n\nWe expect to further strengthen the legal framework to protect the wild elephant resource, which is a living heritage of Sri Lanka, and to prevent the cruelty inflicted on them.\n\nIt is proposed to amend the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance to increase, in addition to the existing term of imprisonment, the fine for killing, hunting, shooting, wounding or capturing a wild elephant from the current fine of Rs. 150,000 to Rs. 500,000 to Rs. 500,000 to Rs. 1 million. It is proposed to amend the Increase of Fines Act, No. 12 of 2005 to increase the fine for illegally keeping an elephant in possession from the current Rs. 150,000 to Rs. 250,000 to Rs. 500,000 to Rs. 1 million.\n\nAnimals may face a water shortage due to the drying up of tanks within wildlife reserves. To face this situation, funds will be allocated through the Wildlife Conservation Fund for the rehabilitation in 2027 of the tanks currently identified by the Department of Wildlife Conservation as requiring rehabilitation. Further, an additional Rs. 90 million has been allocated for this purpose from the 2027 Budget.\n\nIn addition to the above provisions, it is proposed to allocate Rs. 5,000 million as advance preparedness to meet the requirements that may arise from the El Ni\u00f1o risk expected in the future.\n\nProvisions have already been allocated to obtain vessels and machinery worth Rs. 3,380 million under Japanese cooperation to strengthen the capacity to respond promptly to marine and coastal pollution caused by oil spills, in order to protect the environment of the coastal belt."
