    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Rs. 1,500 million has been allocated to establish a new digital Driver Demerit Points System and a road safety enforcement system, integrating the Department of Motor Traffic and the Sri Lanka Police, to minimize road accidents and for road safety.\n\nRs. 5,000 million has been allocated to purchase 5 new trains for the railway service.\n\nTrain operations between Colombo and Peradeniya have been resumed by deploying the resources of the Department of Railways with maximum efficiency to restore the Up-Country railway line damaged by the Ditwah disaster.\n\nUnder the Government of India loan assistance programme, the work required to rehabilitate the railway section from Rambukkana to Peradeniya, the Up- Country railway line from Peradeniya to Gampola, and the Matale railway line in a safer and more stable manner, in accordance with modern engineering standards, has commenced. It is expected that trains will be able to operate up to Kandy within the first quarter of 2027.\n\nAccordingly, it is expected to fully rehabilitate the Up-Country railway line and restore train operations up to Badulla by the middle of 2027.\n\nThe Government of India provided assistance for the rehabilitation of infrastructure damaged by Cyclone Ditwah, the preparation of housing and water supply projects, the rehabilitation of 13 bridges including the Moragahakanda Bridge, the construction of Bailey bridges, and to sectors including health and education.\n\nUnder the Cyclone Ditwah damage rehabilitation programme of the Government of China, rehabilitation work on 9 bridges located in the Gampaha, Kegalle, Ampara, Kurunegala, Polonnaruwa and Batticaloa districts has been prepared through a grant for the reconstruction of 14 destroyed bridges."
