    "md5": "3b67f1a67d6193b6ce182d39cb1c4e31",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "It is proposed to provide a monthly interim allowance of Rs. 2,500, with effect from July 01, 2027, as a relief to pensioners who retired during the period from 2020 to 2024, until the pension revision scheduled to be carried out under the new 2025 basic salary structure is implemented.\n\nThese provisions will also apply to public sector pensioners whose salaries were determined under Department of Management Services Circulars 02/2025, 03/2025, 04/2025 and 05/2025.\n\nThe overall objective of all these measures is to provide fair relief to public servants who retired at different times by systematically narrowing the pension disparities among them, and to establish a sustainable pension system in the long term.\n\n36.1 Increasing the Personal Income Tax Relief and Revising the Progressive Tax Slabs\n\nOur Government increased the annual personal income tax free threshold from Rs. 1.2 million to Rs. 1.8 million in 2025. Through this measure, individual taxpayers gained a monthly tax relief ranging from a minimum of Rs. 3,500 to a maximum of Rs. 20,500.\n\nTo provide further relief to personal income taxpayers, it is proposed to further increase the annual personal income tax free threshold and to revise the personal income tax structure by expanding the taxable income brackets.\n\nWe were able to increase the tax-free threshold from Rs. 100,000 per month to Rs. 150,000. Through the proposed revisions, it will be further increased to Rs. 162,500. Accordingly, a person receiving a monthly salary and allowances of up to Rs. 162,500 will not be required to pay tax on his or her salary.\n\nThe tax slab to which the 6 percent tax rate applies will be Rs. 70,833 per month, and thereafter the tax slabs will be widened from Rs. 41,667 per month to Rs. 50,000 per month. The tax rates will be set at 6 percent, 12 percent, 18 percent, 24 percent, 30 percent and 36 percent, applying a rate of 12 percent instead of 18 percent after the 6 percent rate."
