    "md5": "ec00f1b9437f1d4e94b7f61d06c60e20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1799,
    "chunk_text": "In order to ensure greater transparency, consistency and fairness in valuing taxable supplies denominated in foreign currencies, it is proposed to use the Indicative Exchange Rate published by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka for the conversion of foreign currencies into Sri Lanka Rupees for VAT purposes. The Commissioner General of Inland Revenue (CGIR) will specify by way of a circular the manner in which such exchange rate is to be applied.\n\nThe supply of bunker fuel to ships engaged in international transport is an important component of Sri Lanka\u2019s maritime services sector. This industry operates in a highly competitive international market.\n\nWith the aim of safeguarding the competitiveness of the industry and strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s position as a regional maritime hub, we propose to exempt the supply of fuel to ships engaged in international transport from the Social Security Contribution Levy.\n\n36.8 Review and Rationalization of the Tax Structure applicable to the Betting and Gaming Industry\n\nAddressing the long-standing need for an effective regulatory framework for the betting and gaming industry, the Government has established the Gambling Regulatory Authority (GRA) and commenced its operations.\n\nGiven that a regulatory framework is now in place, it is proposed to conduct a comprehensive review of the taxes, fees, and charges applicable to this industry with the view to eliminate instances of double taxation and inconsistencies, align the tax structure with international best practices, and ensure a fair and balanced approach that supports the sustainable development of the industry while safeguarding government revenue.\n\nThis review aims to establish a simple, transparent, and integrated tax structure that is easy to administer and facilitates compliance."
