    "chunk_text": "Given that a regulatory framework is now in place, it is proposed to conduct a comprehensive review of the taxes, fees, and charges applicable to this industry with the view to eliminate instances of double taxation and inconsistencies, align the tax structure with international best practices, and ensure a fair and balanced approach that supports the sustainable development of the industry while safeguarding government revenue.\n\nThis review aims to establish a simple, transparent, and integrated tax structure that is easy to administer and facilitates compliance.\n\nAdditionally, we propose to review certain stringent procedural requirements which could lead to taxpayers denied legitimate tax refunds or input tax credits due technical or procedural noncompliance while ensuring that adequate safeguards remain in place to protect government revenue.\n\nThese measures aim to establish a fair, transparent, and efficient tax administration system that encourages voluntary tax compliance, safeguards the taxpayers\u2019 legitimate entitlements, and protects government revenue.\n\n36.10 Revision of the Special Commodity Levy (SCL) on Several Essential Food Items\n\nWith the aim of minimizing the cost-of-living pressure on consumers caused by rising prices of essential food items, it is proposed to reduce the Special Commodity Levy on imported sprats from Rs. 100 to Rs. 10 per kilogram, on Maldive fish from Rs. 302 to Rs. 10 per kilogram, and on powdered and unpowdered dried chillies by Rs. 50 per kilogram.\n\nThese tax revisions are scheduled to be implemented with effect from November 13, 2026.\n\nIt is proposed to reduce by 25 percent, the existing CESS rates applicable to 800 selected Harmonized System (HS) code lines, under the Government\u2019s programme of phased reduction of import tariffs, with the aim of simplifying the import tax structure, reducing trade-related costs and enhancing the competitiveness of the economy."
