    "md5": "0f22e719040f4765fe6aaf5511c39e2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1703,
    "chunk_text": "It is proposed to reduce by 25 percent, the existing CESS rates applicable to 800 selected Harmonized System (HS) code lines, under the Government\u2019s programme of phased reduction of import tariffs, with the aim of simplifying the import tax structure, reducing trade-related costs and enhancing the competitiveness of the economy.\n\n36.12 Supporting the Domestic Sugar Industry to Compete in the Market\n\nTo protect the domestic sugar industry, it is also proposed to increase the Special Commodity Levy imposed on imported sugar by Rs. 10 per kilogram, in order to minimize the tax disparity between domestically produced sugar and imported sugar, create equal competitive opportunities and a fair competitive environment for both parties, and ensure a more neutral and equitable tax treatment.\n\nWith the aim of reducing the tax burden on the tourism industry, enhancing its competitiveness and ensuring sustainable financing for tourism development, it is proposed to abolish the Tourism Development Levy currently charged on the turnover of tourism establishments.\n\nIn order to ensure continued funding for tourism promotion and development, steps will be taken in the future to formulate a suitable financing mechanism. Until then, the required funds will be provided by the Treasury on requests made by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority.\n\nThe 2025 Budget proposed to introduce a new Excise Act in place of the centuryold Excise Ordinance with the aim of improving revenue management.\n\nHowever, until a new Act is introduced, we propose to bring amendments to the Excise Ordinance by further simplifying this complex process and incorporating amendments into the existing Ordinance itself."
