    "md5": "d838f65e8fbb7a85668a37341194f2ef",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1963,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 banks on goods and services taxes to lift revenue to Rs. 6.15 trn\n\nThe government is counting on taxes on goods and services to deliver nearly 70 percent of next year\u2019s increase in government revenue, under a budget that targets Rs. 6.15 trillion in collections while proposing relief for personal income taxpayers and cuts to selected import levies.\n\nThe budget presented to parliament yesterday by President and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake projected revenue to rise by Rs. 199 billion, or 3.3 percent, from the revised 2026 estimate of Rs. 5.95 trillion. Including Rs. 30 billion in grants, total revenue and grants are forecast at Rs. 6.18 trillion.\n\nTaxes on goods and services are expected to bring in Rs. 3.59 trillion, up Rs. 138 billion, and account for nearly six in every 10 rupees of government revenue. Taxes on external trade are projected to rise by Rs. 41 billion to Rs. 754 billion, while income tax receipts are expected to increase by Rs. 30 billion to Rs. 1.34 trillion.\n\nThe three categories are forecast to lift total tax revenue by Rs. 209 billion to Rs. 5.69 trillion. A projected Rs. 10 billion decline in non-tax revenue to Rs. 463 billion will absorb part of that increase.\n\nEven with higher collections in rupee terms, government revenue is projected to fall to 15.8 percent of gross domestic product from 16.5 percent in the revised 2026 estimates. Tax revenue is forecast to ease to 14.6 percent of GDP from 15.2 percent.\n\nThe Treasury estimates that the measures listed in the budget\u2019s revenue-impact table will reduce net collections by Rs. 21.15 billion in 2027. Their annual cost is put at Rs. 28.42 billion, with income tax revisions and the next phase of import CESS cuts accounting for the bulk of the reduction.\n\n\u201cAccordingly, a person receiving a monthly salary and allowances of up to Rs. 162,500 will not be required to pay tax on his or her salary,\u201d Dissanayake said in his budget speech."
  },
