    "md5": "096cac51992c396f1b5c1b951a6c0583",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1826,
    "chunk_text": "The Treasury estimates that the measures listed in the budget\u2019s revenue-impact table will reduce net collections by Rs. 21.15 billion in 2027. Their annual cost is put at Rs. 28.42 billion, with income tax revisions and the next phase of import CESS cuts accounting for the bulk of the reduction.\n\n\u201cAccordingly, a person receiving a monthly salary and allowances of up to Rs. 162,500 will not be required to pay tax on his or her salary,\u201d Dissanayake said in his budget speech.\n\nThe government also proposed cutting existing CESS rates by 25 percent on 800 selected tariff lines, at an estimated annual cost of Rs. 10 billion, and removing the 10 percent Ports and Airports Development Levy on specified newsprint imports from January. The revision of the Special Commodity Levy was the only measure in the table projected to yield additional revenue on an annual basis, at Rs. 950 million.\n\nDissanayake also proposed changes to tax laws to reflect new forms of transactions and a review of procedural requirements that can prevent taxpayers from receiving refunds or input tax credits.\n\n\u201cThese measures aim to establish a fair, transparent, and efficient tax administration system that encourages voluntary tax compliance, safeguards the taxpayers\u2019 legitimate entitlements, and protects government revenue,\u201d he said.\n\nTo encourage investment in industries that earn foreign exchange, the budget proposes a capital allowance equivalent to 200 percent of qualifying expenditure for eligible Board of Investment-approved export manufacturing and tourism projects with a minimum qualifying investment of US$ 3 million. It also proposes allowing newly VAT-registered exporters to qualify earlier for expedited refunds. The speech did not quantify the additional export or tourism earnings expected from those measures."
  },
