    "chunk_size_bytes": 1645,
    "chunk_text": "Higher net interest income lifts BOC\u2019s nine-month profit to Rs. 58.7bn\n\nBank of Ceylon reported a net profit of Rs. 58.7 billion for the nine months ended Sept. 30, 2026, as interest income grew faster than interest expenses and the bank set aside less for loan losses than a year earlier. The state-owned lender posted a pre-tax profit of Rs. 94.1 billion.\n\nNet interest income rose 10 percent year on year to Rs. 169.2 billion, with interest income increasing 6 percent to Rs. 390.8 billion and interest expenses rising 3 percent to Rs. 221.6 billion.\n\nBank of Ceylon Chairman, Kavinda de Zoysa said, \u201cOur performance over the first nine months of 2026 reflects the strength of BOC\u2019s franchise, the confidence of our customers and the disciplined execution of our strategy. Beyond profitability, our priority remains strengthening our capital position, maintaining sound risk management and further advancing financial inclusion.\n\nDuring the first nine months of 2026, BOC continued to deliver resilient financial performance, supported by effective balance sheet management, disciplined funding strategies and sustained growth in its core banking operations.\n\nNet Interest Income (NII) reached Rs. 169.2 billion, reflecting an 10 percent increase compared with the corresponding period of the previous year. Interest income increased by 6 percent to Rs. 390.8 billion, while interest expenses increased by 3 percent to Rs. 221.6 billion. This performance reflects the Bank\u2019s continued focus on optimizing its earning assets, managing funding costs and maintaining an effective asset-liability position amid changing market conditions."
