    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "Nations Trust Bank profit dips 28% in 3Q on higher costs, trading loss\n\nNations Trust Bank saw its net profit fall 28 percent to Rs. 4.42 billion in the September quarter, as higher operating costs, a swing to a trading loss and a larger tax bill outweighed growth in lending and fee income.\n\nProfit attributable to shareholders declined from Rs. 6.12 billion a year earlier, while earnings per share fell to Rs. 13.26 from Rs. 18.35, according to the bank\u2019s interim financial statements. Profit before income tax fell 11 percent to Rs. 7.34 billion.\n\nGroup net interest income rose 20 percent to Rs. 11.78 billion. Interest income increased 37 percent, while interest expense climbed 56 percent. Net fee and commission income rose 41 percent to Rs. 3.29 billion.\n\nThe group recorded a net trading loss of Rs. 2.11 billion, compared with a gain of Rs. 791 million a year earlier. Total operating income still rose 14 percent to Rs. 14.96 billion, helped by higher interest and fee income and a recovery in other operating income.\n\nThe group booked a net impairment charge of Rs. 34 million, compared with a reversal of Rs. 945 million a year earlier. That limited growth in net operating income after impairment to 6 percent. Operating expenses rose 36 percent to Rs. 5.70 billion, led by higher staff and other operating costs, while income tax expense increased 38 percent to Rs. 2.92 billion.\n\nAt bank level, standalone net profit fell 23 percent to Rs. 4.67 billion. Nations Trust said costs associated with its acquisition of HSBC Sri Lanka\u2019s retail banking business and initial provisions on the acquired portfolio weighed on the quarter. The Rs. 21.78 billion cash acquisition was completed on 1 May, and the group was still finalising the purchase price allocation at the end of September."
  },
