    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "What we know about the global Microsoft outage\n\nBBC - A major global Microsoft outage has caused problems, or in some cases disabled entire services, including emails and artificial intelligence products.\n\nReports of problems with the email provider Outlook peaked globally at nearly 140,000 around 18:00 GMT on Thursday, according to monitoring website Downdetector.\n\nMicrosoft has said the outage was not caused by a cyber-security incident, but efforts to fix the issues have so far been largely unsuccessful.\n\nHere's what we know about the services affected and what went wrong.\n\nMicrosoft 365 Services, including Teams, Outlook and Microsoft's artificial intelligence software Copilot, have been affected by \"connectivity problems\".\n\nWhile customers are reporting varying levels of access - many have not been able to send or receive emails, view certain files or details on their calendar apps.\n\nSome users have been able to use Teams as normal and are using messaging and online meeting functions as usual.\n\nThe full scale of the outage has not been confirmed, but monitoring website Downdetector had 140,000 people from 74 countries report issues with Outlook on Thursday evening.\n\nMore than 430 million people use Microsoft 365 apps according to Microsoft's website.\n\nThe software is also used by companies worldwide and is expected to have a knock-on effect to other services.\n\nTech giants OpenAI and Anthropic have both reported problems as a result of the outage.\n\nChatGPT-maker OpenAI said it was investigating an issue affecting its connections to Microsoft's services, which has caused some tools to not work as expected.\n\nGovernment agencies and services, including the Singaporean government and Australia's spy agency, have also said they have been affected.\n\nSeveral GP practices across the UK have warned of potential problems, with some instructing patient to make urgent queries over the phone instead of by email or web form."
