    "md5": "2290e8009f4f78d4330c3fdb38769a35",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1608,
    "chunk_text": "Government agencies and services, including the Singaporean government and Australia's spy agency, have also said they have been affected.\n\nSeveral GP practices across the UK have warned of potential problems, with some instructing patient to make urgent queries over the phone instead of by email or web form.\n\nThe term refers to when servers are handling a much higher volume of stress than they usually do.\n\nIn the case of the Microsoft outage, a large number of servers became \"unexpectedly unavailable\", the company said, which sent added traffic to remaining servers, straining these.\n\nOn Friday morning, Microsoft said it had observed \"marginal success\" in its efforts to treat the incident.\n\nThe company said in its latest update that is it continuing to monitor activity across regions to evaluate server status and determine what actions it can take to improve capacity.\n\nIn an earlier statement, the tech giant had said that its attempted fixes were \"not taking as expected\".\n\nAustralian cyber-security expert Troy Hunt told the BBC the outage could have far-reaching consequences.\n\n\"Microsoft is a massive part of the internet backbone and reminds us how critical email still is.\n\n\"Not only does this outage affect many people's ability to work but also things like the ability to communicate about appointments, purchase things that need verification, create or access accounts and services,\" he said.\n\nPaul Spain from New Zealand-based advisory firm Gorilla Technology said that with e-mails and services offline, companies have practically \"lost a business day\", which is \"frustrating\" users\"."
