    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe main objective of this Budget is to move from economic stability towards transformation. It is to achieve a progressive transformation in every sphere, including investment, exports, productive employment, social welfare and sustainability,\u201d said President Dissanayake.\n\nThe project pipeline is taking shape at different speeds as the Colombo logistics centre has a timetable for seeking proposals. In minerals, a committee has been appointed to select an investor to process sands mined by Lanka Mineral Sands Ltd., while the search for a partner for the Kahatagaha graphite project will follow an assessment of the mine\u2019s reserves. For smaller businesses, the immediate question is how much funding reaches them. The Government expects Rs. 67.69 billion in interest subsidies and refinancing to support Rs. 174 billion in concessional loans through State and private banks. That includes a proposal to support a further Rs. 20 billion in loans at 5 percent under Re-MSME Plus for capital and working capital. A proposed mechanism to help small and medium-sized companies list on the Colombo Stock Exchange would give eligible firms another way to raise capital. The Budget also allocates Rs. 4 billion for work on industrial estates and Rs. 1.99 billion for the National Single Window for Trade. Following a pilot covering selected goods, exporters are expected to be connected in the first half of 2027, with importers and re-exporters to follow in the second half.\n\nThe Government also working to expand the existing free trade agreement with India, has applied to join the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership and is pursuing economic partnership agreements with the United Arab Emirates and Oman."
