    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "Earlier, speaking during the presser at Sydney's Cricket Central, Cummins said, as quoted by Fox Sports, that Khawaja's claims that everyone, including the bowling quartet of Cummins, Mitchell Starc, Josh Hazlewood and Nathan Lyon, were aware of sandpaper being brought to the field were 100 per cent wrong.\n\nWhile he still termed Usman a friend, he also went on to make serious accusations of a casual approach towards telling his story involving his own long-time teammates and friends and called for some accountability from him.\n\n\"Usman is a friend, but friends are not casual with each other's lives and reputations,\" he said. \"In his book, he has been casual with my life and the lives of many others, and I think Australian cricket. I support him to tell his story, 100 per cent. I am glad he feels like he can, but we have to be accountable for the things that we say and do,\" he continued.\n\nThe skipper said that the years-old controversy and the way Khawaja has casually told his story affect Australian cricket and people who are a part of it.\n\n\"It affects real people. It affects people's lives. It is also strange. I love Australian cricket. It has given me so much,\" said Cummins. \"This really affects Australian cricket. And I think the way that he has presented his story, I think he's done that in a very casual manner. And it affects a lot of people. That is what I am grappling with at the moment because I do not think it is okay,\" he continued.\n\nCummins also said that Usman had been \"quite casual and flippant\" with the lives of people and Australian cricket.\n\n\"Uzzy is a friend. But I think the way he is quite casual and flippant with people's lives and Australian cricket, I do not think that is okay,\" Cummins said.\n\n\"I played 15 years, my debut for New South Wales and with Australia, Usman was right there, and he has been awesome to me and he is a friend, but this is people's real lives, and this is serious ... this affects not only people, but people's families.\""
