    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "\u2019\u2019Only an inhuman person could dismiss his mother\u2019s condition as non-exceptional\u2019\u2019 - Defence Counsel\n\nColombo, Nov. 14 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0President\u2019s Counsel Ali Sabry, appearing for Parliamentarian Namal Rajapaksa, told the Colombo High Court that while some people might be able to act like robots without considering their parents\u2019 health, his client could not do so, particularly when his mother was facing a life-threatening medical condition with 95% blockage in her heart. He argued that such circumstances clearly constituted an exceptional situation and maintained that the Bribery Commission\u2019s application seeking to suspend the bail order had no legal basis.\n\nThe Colombo High Court yesterday announced that it would deliver its order on November 18 on whether to issue an interim injunction suspending the bail order issued by the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court releasing Parliamentarian Namal Rajapaksa, who had been remanded in connection with the Krrish transaction.\n\nThe order was announced when the revision application filed by the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption, challenging the bail order granted to Namal Rajapaksa on November 3 on the grounds that it was legally flawed, was taken up before Colombo High Court Judge Lal Bandara.\n\nAssistant Director of Legal Affairs of the Bribery Commission, Thanuja Bandara, appearing for the Commission, submitted that the bail order issued by the Colombo Chief Magistrate was contrary to the law.\n\nShe submitted that under Section 149(1) of the Anti-Corruption Act, a suspect produced before court with a certificate issued by the Director General of the Bribery Commission must be remanded until the conclusion of the trial.\n\nShe further stated that although the law provides an exception allowing bail in exceptional circumstances, the Magistrate had erred in invoking that exception to release the suspect in this case."
  },
