    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailymirrorlk-5d2c7650-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1eac01878381dc36853161f4df0ecf28",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1836,
    "chunk_text": "She submitted that under Section 149(1) of the Anti-Corruption Act, a suspect produced before court with a certificate issued by the Director General of the Bribery Commission must be remanded until the conclusion of the trial.\n\nShe further stated that although the law provides an exception allowing bail in exceptional circumstances, the Magistrate had erred in invoking that exception to release the suspect in this case.\n\nShe submitted that a witness in the case, Nimal Perera, was currently residing in Australia due to alleged threats to his life.\n\nArguing that there was a risk of witnesses being influenced following the suspect\u2019s release on bail, she requested court to issue an interim injunction suspending the bail order issued by the Colombo Chief Magistrate on November 3.\n\nHowever, President\u2019s Counsel Ali Sabry, appearing for Namal Rajapaksa, strongly objected to the application.\n\nHe questioned the urgency of the Bribery Commission\u2019s application, pointing out that his client was still in remand custody.\n\nHe further argued that the interim relief sought by the Commission was identical to the final relief sought in the revision application.\n\nReferring to Supreme Court judgements, he submitted that a final order sought through a petition could not be granted as an interim injunction before the conclusion of the proceedings.\n\nHe maintained that the Chief Magistrate had issued the bail order within the framework of the law and questioned the legal basis for the Commission\u2019s application, arguing that the serious heart condition of the suspect\u2019s mother had been reasonably considered by the Magistrate as an exceptional circumstance.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Ali Sabry stated that Namal Rajapaksa\u2019s mother had 95% blockage in her heart and questioned how such circumstances could be considered ordinary."
  },
