    "md5": "b5f8d9489dcbe99bd9504d3eee4430f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclonic storm threat looms over Sri Lanka as heavy rains forecast\n\nSri Lanka could face severe weather conditions in the coming days, with a developing low-pressure system over the Bay of Bengal threatening to intensify into a cyclonic storm, raising fears of heavy rainfall, flash floods and landslides across several parts of the country, the Asia Pacific Alliance for Disaster Management Sri Lanka (A-PAD SL) has warned.\n\nIn a weather advisory issued on Friday (13), A-PAD SL said a low-pressure area had formed over the southeastern Bay of Bengal and was expected to develop into a depression around November 15.\n\nThe system could further intensify into a cyclonic storm over the central Bay of Bengal by November 16, according to the advisory, which was based on updates issued by the Department of Meteorology.\n\nThe organisation warned that Sri Lanka could experience increased rainfall from November 14 and 15, with heavy showers expected to affect several parts of the country in the following days.\n\nThe Northern, North Central, North Western, Western and Eastern provinces are among the areas likely to experience significant rainfall.\n\nA-PAD SL cautioned that the country could face severe weather impacts if the depression moves northward along Sri Lanka\u2019s eastern coastline. However, it noted that the system\u2019s exact trajectory and intensity remained uncertain.\n\nThe risk of flash floods and landslides is considered particularly high as the ground across much of the country is already saturated following heavy rainfall experienced over the past week.\n\nThe advisory further noted that approximately 17 major reservoirs had reached spill levels last week, while landslide early warnings had been issued for seven districts from November 12."
