    "chunk_text": "To Rousseau, any private faction or partial society that demands a separate exemption from the common law breaks the social pact, for the collective citizenry alone holds the absolute authority to govern the republic.\n\nThe foundational architecture of this self-inflicted crisis was laid during the post-independence era.\n\nThe structural decay began under the 1972 Constitution, introduced by the Sirimavo Bandaranaike-led United Front Government.\n\nIn a bid to appease a majoritarian Buddhist psyche, the 1972 constitution granted Buddhism the \u2018foremost place\u2019 and mandated the State to \u2018protect and foster\u2019 it.\n\nThis piece of constitutional mechanics legally transformed the monastic hierarchy from spiritual guides into constitutional stakeholders holding an implicit veto over State policy.\n\nThis historical trap was aggressively expanded in 1978 by that Latin-epigram-loving political fox, J.R. Jayewardene (JRJ).While fondly quoting \u2018Infelix qui sancta docet si vivat inique\u2019\u2014\u2019Unfortunate is the one who teaches holy things but lives wickedly\u2019\u2014JRJ executed a maneuver of profound neo-liberal mischief.\u201d\n\nJRJ expanded the constitutional mandate to include the preservation of the nebulous \u2018Buddha Sasana\u2019.\u00a0 By institutionalising the \u2018Sasana\u2019 a term elastic enough to encompass not just spiritual sublime doctrine, but vast networks of untaxed monastic wealth, extensive land ownership, and autonomous temporalities such as the Dalada Maligawa, Sri Padasthana, and Atamasthana\u2014the State created a parallel, unregulated, but venerated quasi-State.\n\nThe subsequent creation of a dedicated Buddha Sasana Ministry effectively insulated the clergy from secular auditing and legal oversight, rendering the hierarchy structurally immune to the rule of law.\n\nWe would do well to remember that even the Kandyan King Keerthi Sri Rajasinha, who facilitated the historic 1753 return of the higher ordination from Siam, fiercely retained absolute sovereign authority over the clergy."
