    "chunk_text": "They are aided by private television channels brazenly dedicated to giving them unhindered, unlimited exposure under the convenient cover of freedom of expression.\n\nUnder free-market global commerce, the essence of Prince Siddhartha\u2019s renunciation has been distorted into a corporate shield by an entrepreneurial priestly class.\n\nHas anyone ever bothered to ask some of these saffron-garbed potentates why they sport an additional, glistening saffron shawl thrown so stylishly over their shoulders?\n\nTo some, the saffron robe has ceased to be a symbol of world-renunciation. It has become a fashion statement and a marker of elite status.\n\nThis sorry state completely exposes the modern myth of the Arahat Dhajaya\u2014a faith marketer\u2019s coinage designed to stress the absolute sacred status of the robe-wearer. In the cultural imagination of the island, the saffron robe is revered as the very pennant of Arahat Mahinda, son of Ashoka, who according to legend brought the Dhamma to the rock of Mihintale.\n\nYet, according to eminent historian Romila Thapar, Arahat Mahinda\u2019s visit to Mihintale was not a miraculous, vacuum-born conversion of an ignorant king.\n\nRather, his objective was institutional organisation. Mahinda arrived as an official envoy of a highly sophisticated Mauryan State to formalise religious ties, organise monastic orders, establish a disciplined monastic code (Vinaya), and structurally align the Anuradhapura monarchy with the State-patronised Buddhist models of Emperor Ashoka. The deeper details of that structural discussion could await another day.\n\nThe saffron robe was meant to signify the ultimate conquest of ego, greed, and worldly power.\n\nBut when the robe is used to protect individuals from criminal investigations, evade court orders, and host political strategy sessions with discredited politicians, it ceases to be the pennant of Mahinda.\n\nIt becomes a cynical costume of political convenience, degrading the very heritage it claims to protect."
