    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "But when the robe is used to protect individuals from criminal investigations, evade court orders, and host political strategy sessions with discredited politicians, it ceases to be the pennant of Mahinda.\n\nIt becomes a cynical costume of political convenience, degrading the very heritage it claims to protect.\n\nThe modern Sri Lankan citizenry has learned a brutal economic lesson. They now know that a politician\u2019s duplicitous devotion at a temple does not prevent the bankruptcy of a nation.\n\nThe organic consciousness of the Aragalaya acts as a discerning firewall against these manufactured crises, rendering the old guard\u2019s tools obsolete.\n\nThe entire Buddhist monastic establishment is now forced to make a defining choice between 21st-century modernity or tribal primitivism.\n\nWill the Maha Sangha evolve into an exemplary spiritual body bound by moral accountability, or will it persist as an unregulated parallel Government clinging to feudal land ownership, untaxed wealth, and medieval legal immunities?\n\nIf President AKD and the NPP Government are genuinely committed to a systemic overhaul, they must confront this historical trap head-on. The State must completely repeal the British-inspired Buddhist Temporalities Ordinance, cutting monastic property management off from the State\u2019s benevolent Nelsonian eye.\u201d\n\nTo rescue the spiritual heritage from political manipulation, the State must look back to move forward. It must re-establish the core tenets of Ashokan Buddhism that Arahat Mahinda brought to the island.\n\nAshokan governance was defined not by giving a monastic monopoly unchecked constitutional vetoes, but by active State purification of the Sangha, enforcing strict moral accountability.\n\nThese make-believe edifices built on elite preservation and structural immunity are historically unsustainable."
