    "language": "en",
    "md5": "37ad1339d9961de234aaa93a34b4d773",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part I\n\nThe problem therefore requires a change of philosophy. Sri Lanka must move from flood control to integrated flood-risk management. The distinction is significant. Flood control seeks to keep water out or move it away. Flood-risk management accepts that extreme rainfall will occur and seeks to ensure that the landscape, infrastructure, institutions and population can absorb its consequences without catastrophic damage\n\nColombo\u2019s recent inundation is not adequately explained by exceptionally heavy rainfall alone. It is the consequence of an increasingly dangerous convergence of meteorological extremes, urban development, degraded natural drainage, infrastructure limitations, institutional fragmentation and human behaviour.\n\nThe immediate trigger was an extraordinary burst of rainfall on 6 November. The underlying vulnerability, however, has been accumulating for decades.\n\nOn 7 November, Colombo Municipal Council (CMC) officials reported approximately 129 mm of rain in one hour, against a drainage system design capacity of approximately 30 mm per hour.\n\nThe Meteorological Department separately attributed the event to intense thunderstorms associated with the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ) and second inter-monsoon conditions. It also identified obstructed drains as an aggravating factor.\n\nThis distinction is fundamental. Meteorology supplied the extraordinary volume and intensity of water. Urban development reduced the terrain\u2019s ability to absorb and retain it. Inadequate drainage and maintenance prevented its timely removal. Institutional and social shortcomings increased the consequences.\n\nIt was a perfect storm \u2013 except it was worse than imperfect for hundreds of thousands of inundated householders, stranded motorists, sundry soggy pedestrians et al."
  },
