    "md5": "40900446d717839c5c6facc02d643c22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "This distinction is fundamental. Meteorology supplied the extraordinary volume and intensity of water. Urban development reduced the terrain\u2019s ability to absorb and retain it. Inadequate drainage and maintenance prevented its timely removal. Institutional and social shortcomings increased the consequences.\n\nIt was a perfect storm \u2013 except it was worse than imperfect for hundreds of thousands of inundated householders, stranded motorists, sundry soggy pedestrians et al.\n\nThe recommended approach is a national program of integrated flood-risk management, combining engineering, ecological restoration, land-use reform, institutional accountability, meteorological intelligence and community preparedness.\n\nFirstly, there are the immediate atmospheric conditions. This early November event occurred during Sri Lanka\u2019s second inter-monsoon season, which normally unfurls over the island in October\u2013November.\n\nThe country\u2019s rainfall calendar is not governed by one continuous monsoon. It comprises four principal seasons:\n\nThe first inter-monsoon (March-April) has localised thunderstorms and convection as its principal characters. This is followed by the south-west monsoon, with heavy rainfall in western and southwestern areas.\n\nThe second inter-monsoon is what you and I are wading through at present. That is followed by the north-east monsoon (December-February), with rainfall particularly in the northern and eastern regions. It often affects the central areas too, of late, as does the south-west monsoon before it.\n\nFirstly, the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ), which is a broad belt of low pressure where winds from the Northern and Southern Hemispheres converge. This convergence encourages warm, moist air to rise. As it rises, it cools and condenses, producing clouds and thunderstorms.\n\nDuring the second inter-monsoon, the ITCZ migrates across Sri Lanka. Its presence, combined with favourable atmospheric instability, can produce exceptionally intense rainfall."
  },
