    "md5": "7f9280cbde7d434a4b58a9699516c724",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "Firstly, the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ), which is a broad belt of low pressure where winds from the Northern and Southern Hemispheres converge. This convergence encourages warm, moist air to rise. As it rises, it cools and condenses, producing clouds and thunderstorms.\n\nDuring the second inter-monsoon, the ITCZ migrates across Sri Lanka. Its presence, combined with favourable atmospheric instability, can produce exceptionally intense rainfall.\n\nThis is particularly dangerous in Colombo. A drainage system might cope with 100 mm of rain distributed over 24 hours, but fail catastrophically when a comparable proportion falls in under one hour. The 6 November event demonstrated precisely this catastrophic vulnerability.\n\nThirdly, atmospheric oscillations and oceanic influences, in addition to which two additional phenomena deserve attention. The Madden\u2013Julian Oscillation (MJO), a large-scale eastward-moving atmospheric disturbance with a cycle of approximately 30-60 days, which influences tropical cloud formation and rainfall. And the El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation (ENSO) and the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) that influence rainfall variability over longer periods.\n\nResearch published in the Journal of Climate in 2023 identifies particularly strong MJO-related influences on Sri Lankan rainfall during the second inter-monsoon and southwest monsoon seasons. Research also identifies associations between MJO phases and extreme precipitation events.\n\nThese phenomena help explain why rainfall can become unusually intense during particular periods. However, without a detailed meteorological attribution study, it would be premature to identify a particular MJO, ENSO or IOD configuration as the cause of the 6 November storm."
