    "md5": "68a36c41e9f1b264c754a7dd24b015fb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2019,
    "chunk_text": "These phenomena help explain why rainfall can become unusually intense during particular periods. However, without a detailed meteorological attribution study, it would be premature to identify a particular MJO, ENSO or IOD configuration as the cause of the 6 November storm.\n\nA warmer atmosphere can hold approximately 7% more water vapour for every 1\u00b0C increase in temperature, under the relevant thermodynamic approximation. This can increase the potential intensity of rainfall when atmospheric conditions produce precipitation.\n\nOther relevant factors include: changes in monsoon circulation; variations in the frequency and intensity of thunderstorms; changes in the distribution of rainfall between seasons; rising sea levels, which can impede the discharge of inland stormwater; and greater exposure of urban settlements to extreme weather.\n\nA recent World Bank assessment identified Greater Colombo as particularly vulnerable because of its concentration of people, economic assets and infrastructure. It also documented the expansion of flood-exposed built-up areas.\n\nThe policy conclusion is that Sri Lanka should design infrastructure for future rainfall conditions, rather than relying exclusively on historical rainfall averages.\n\nThere is an interesting cultural and philosophical dimension to Sri Lanka\u2019s relationship with rain.\n\nThe Pali expression \u201cdevo vassatu kalena, sassasampatti hotu\u201d is best translated: \u2018May rain fall in due season; may the harvest be plentiful.\u2019\n\nThis traditional Buddhist blessing expresses a relationship between rainfall, agricultural prosperity, social wellbeing and the natural order.\n\nIt is particularly meaningful in a country whose civilisation developed around agriculture and sophisticated water management.\n\nBut there is an important distinction between rain in due season and destructive rainfall.\n\nThe blessing is not an endorsement of unlimited precipitation. Its underlying aspiration is for rainfall that is timely, sufficient and conducive to life."
