    "md5": "76c2462e8a9bd11e64ad441440be0ba2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "But there is an important distinction between rain in due season and destructive rainfall.\n\nThe blessing is not an endorsement of unlimited precipitation. Its underlying aspiration is for rainfall that is timely, sufficient and conducive to life.\n\nThe tank-and-reservoir system developed over centuries was based on capturing, retaining, regulating and distributing water.\n\nThe cascade systems of the Dry Zone connected small tanks, larger reservoirs, streams and agricultural land into interconnected hydrological networks.\n\nThe principle was not simply to dispose of excess water. It was to retain water where it could be used, while allowing controlled movement through the landscape.\n\nThe ancient principle commonly expressed as \u2018wewai dagabai gamai pansalai\u2019 captures the relationship between the water tank, temple, village and community.\n\nThere is a lesson here for modern urban planning. Colombo\u2019s wetlands, marshes, canals and retention areas are not vacant land awaiting development. They are part of a living water-management system.\n\nThere is a contradiction between Sri Lanka\u2019s historical reverence for water and some contemporary practices such as filling wetlands to create commercial and residential property, building on natural drainage paths, dumping household and commercial waste into canals, treating marshes as wasteland, and regarding flood protection as the responsibility of Government rather than a shared civic obligation.\n\nIt would be simplistic to blame these practices on a decline in cultural values alone. They also reflect economic incentives, land scarcity, planning failures, poverty and inadequate enforcement.\n\nNevertheless, the cultural dimension matters. A sustainable flood policy requires a change in the way society understands wetlands and waterways: from obstacles to development into essential public infrastructure. And a closer inquiry into why Sri Lanka has suffered repeated flooding."
  },
