    "md5": "8d24b4386a6e7f58030fca480584c26f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1723,
    "chunk_text": "Nevertheless, the cultural dimension matters. A sustainable flood policy requires a change in the way society understands wetlands and waterways: from obstacles to development into essential public infrastructure. And a closer inquiry into why Sri Lanka has suffered repeated flooding.\n\nThe important question is why a country with such a long history of water management continues to experience devastating floods.\n\nThe historical record illustrates the persistence and geographical breadth of the problem:\n\nsevere cyclone and subsequent flooding, particularly in the eastern and north-central regions (1978)\n\nsignificant flooding (1989), exceptional rainfall inundated Colombo: approximately 494 mm was recorded within 24 hours (1992)\n\nsevere flooding and landslides, with extensive loss of life (2003)\n\nColombo experienced approximately 440 mm of rainfall in 16 hours, causing major urban inundation (2010)\n\nmajor floods and landslides, with 224 reported deaths (2017), extensive flooding and landslides (2018)\n\nThe record reveals two important things: first, flooding is a national problem, not merely a Colombo problem; second, the nature of the risk varies substantially according to location.\n\nFirstly, riverine flooding occurs when rivers overflow their banks following prolonged or exceptionally heavy rainfall in their catchments. Major vulnerable river systems include Kelani Ganga, Kalu Ganga, Gin Ganga, Nilwala Ganga, Mahaweli Ganga and Attanagalu Oya.\n\nThe Kelani basin is particularly important because of its proximity to Colombo and its exposure to rainfall in the central highlands. Heavy rainfall upstream can produce flooding downstream, even when Colombo itself has experienced relatively little rain."
