    "chunk_text": "The Kelani basin is particularly important because of its proximity to Colombo and its exposure to rainfall in the central highlands. Heavy rainfall upstream can produce flooding downstream, even when Colombo itself has experienced relatively little rain.\n\nThirdly, reservoir-related flooding. Reservoirs are essential to Sri Lanka\u2019s irrigation and electricity systems. However, high inflows, reservoir operating constraints and releases can contribute to downstream flooding. This requires coordinated water management, particularly where several reservoirs and hydropower facilities affect the same river basin.\n\nFourthly, coastal and estuarine flooding. High tides, storm surges, sea-level rise and sediment accumulation near river mouths can impede the discharge of inland water. This is especially relevant to Colombo, where drainage outlets ultimately depend on the hydraulic conditions of the Kelani River and the Indian Ocean.\n\nFifthly, landslides and secondary disasters. Flooding and landslides frequently occur together. Heavy rainfall in the central highlands can cause slope failures, damage roads, interrupt communications and isolate communities. The 2016 and 2017 disasters demonstrated how one meteorological episode can produce multiple interconnected emergencies.\n\nWhy is Colombo so particularly vulnerable? The city occupies a low-lying coastal basin. Its historical landscape included extensive marshes, paddy fields, wetlands, lakes and interconnected waterways.\n\nThese features performed an essential hydrological function. They absorbed rainfall, temporarily stored excess water and allowed its gradual discharge into rivers and the sea. Urbanisation has progressively compromised this system."
