    "chunk_size_bytes": 1776,
    "chunk_text": "These features performed an essential hydrological function. They absorbed rainfall, temporarily stored excess water and allowed its gradual discharge into rivers and the sea. Urbanisation has progressively compromised this system.\n\nIts assessment of the city\u2019s wetland system also demonstrated that drainage tunnels and pumping stations alone cannot adequately protect Colombo if its natural retention areas are lost.\n\nA 2025 University of Moratuwa study examining Colombo\u2019s land-use changes between 1999 and 2022 found that urban expansion and the reduction of green spaces and natural retention areas increased surface runoff and flood vulnerability.\n\nThen there is all of this unplanned urban development whereby buildings, roads, car parks and paved surfaces prevent rainwater from infiltrating the soil. Consequently, rainfall that would previously have been absorbed by vegetation and marshland becomes surface runoff.\n\nThe cumulative consequences are substantial. More water enters drainage channels, water reaches canals more quickly, peak flows become higher, existing drains become overloaded, flooding occurs even in locations that previously experienced little inundation.\n\nThe World Bank\u2019s assessment of Greater Colombo records a significant expansion of flood-exposed built-up areas.\n\nA key factor is inadequate drainage infrastructure. The reported disparity between the recent rainfall intensity and the drainage system\u2019s design capacity is particularly revealing\u2026\n\nReported rainfall intensity versus drainage design capacity is instructive. Reported rainfall on 6 November was 129 mm/hour. Reported drainage design capacity is 30 mm/hour. Therefore the approximate ratio of reported rainfall intensity to reported drainage design capacity was x4.3."
