    "md5": "421de22be7650b93984d781e464a6957",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Reported rainfall intensity versus drainage design capacity is instructive. Reported rainfall on 6 November was 129 mm/hour. Reported drainage design capacity is 30 mm/hour. Therefore the approximate ratio of reported rainfall intensity to reported drainage design capacity was x4.3.\n\nSeveral recurring problems have been identified in technical studies: waste accumulation in canals and drains; unauthorised construction obstructing waterways; commercial development within wetland reservations; inadequate maintenance of drainage channels; insufficient storage capacity; restricted outfalls and backwater effects.\n\nA particularly important technical consideration is that Colombo\u2019s drainage network is connected to the Kelani River and the sea. High river levels or tides can restrict the discharge of stormwater. In certain circumstances, a drainage outlet that normally carries water away may become ineffective.\n\nA study of Colombo's flood-adaptation options identifies inadequate outfall capacity, insufficient storage and reduced canal conveyance as three central problems.\n\nInstitutional fragmentation is perhaps the most important governance issue. For example, the Land Development Corporation is responsible for major drainage channels, while the Municipal Council has responsibility for much of the local drainage network.\n\nNor is Colombo\u2019s drainage system managed by one institution: different responsibilities are distributed among the Colombo Municipal Council, Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation, Urban Development Authority, Road Development Authority, Irrigation Department and other local authorities and public agencies.\n\nThe World Bank has specifically identified the division of responsibilities between these institutions.\u00a0 The danger is that a problem at one point in the system can undermine the performance of the entire network. A municipality may clear its drains, for example, but if a downstream canal is obstructed, the benefit may be limited."
