    "md5": "de3a1ea10f02eb543dab61dac41ab3ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "The World Bank has specifically identified the division of responsibilities between these institutions.\u00a0 The danger is that a problem at one point in the system can undermine the performance of the entire network. A municipality may clear its drains, for example, but if a downstream canal is obstructed, the benefit may be limited.\n\nFirstly, there is unequal enforcement. Unauthorised structures can obstruct canals and waterways. But enforcement must apply consistently to residential encroachments, commercial developments and politically connected property interests. It would be unfair and ineffective to place the entire responsibility on low-income communities living in flood-prone locations.\n\nSecondly, there are development incentives whereby land reclamation and construction can generate immediate private economic returns while transferring the cost of increased flood risk to the wider public. This is a classic case of externalised environmental costs.\n\nA third is political short-termism: drainage maintenance, wetland conservation and flood-resilience investments rarely produce the immediate political visibility of roads, buildings and other construction projects. The result can be an imbalance between investment in development and investment in the systems that make development sustainable.\n\nA fourth is institutional discontinuity: flood mitigation is a long-term undertaking. Changes in political leadership, administrative priorities and public sector budgets can interrupt projects that require continuity over decades.\n\nA fifth is inadequate accountability: after major floods, investigations, emergency meetings and declarations of new programs are common. The more important test is whether the findings lead to measurable improvements in infrastructure, enforcement and maintenance."
