    "md5": "c43993f5a71fc0679bfa20befb9778b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1680,
    "chunk_text": "A fifth is inadequate accountability: after major floods, investigations, emergency meetings and declarations of new programs are common. The more important test is whether the findings lead to measurable improvements in infrastructure, enforcement and maintenance.\n\nSri Lanka has treated flooding as an emergency rather than a permanent development challenge. Our resilient island nation has repeatedly demonstrated that it can mobilise resources after a flood. The harder task is to prevent the underlying conditions from recurring.\n\nThere is already considerable technical knowledge available. The Metro Colombo Urban Development Project, initiated in 2012, included improvements to drainage, canals, retention areas and urban infrastructure. The Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation also undertook substantial hydrological and hydraulic modelling.\n\nThese initiatives established important foundations. But the recurrence of serious flooding suggests that engineering interventions have not been sufficient to eliminate systemic vulnerabilities.\n\nThe problem therefore requires a change of philosophy. Sri Lanka must move from flood control to integrated flood-risk management. The distinction is significant. Flood control seeks to keep water out or move it away.\n\nFlood-risk management accepts that extreme rainfall will occur and seeks to ensure that the landscape, infrastructure, institutions and population can absorb its consequences without catastrophic damage. This is the foundation of the policy framework being proposed in this article.\n\n(The author is the Editor-at-large of LMD and is a senior journalist with a Post-graduate Diploma in Politics and Governance)"
