    "chunk_size_bytes": 1596,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s foreign policy paradoxes: Geo-politics, development and human rights\n\nFormer Foreign Secretary Ambassador Ravinatha Aryasinha has said the 21st Century paradoxes Sri Lanka\u2019s foreign policy faces\u2014regional geo-politics, economic development and human rights accountability\u2014cannot be resolved simply by responding individually to satisfying the competing \u2018zero-sum\u2019 demands made by major powers, but required addressing the underlying weaknesses that have made Sri Lanka vulnerable to such external pressures in the first place.\n\nHe said, \u201cOur strategic location should be an asset, not a source of perpetual uncertainty; our economic relationships should generate opportunities for sustainable growth, rather than deepen existing vulnerabilities; and our engagement with the international organisations should be guided by a consistent commitment to international law, rather than playing along ambiguously. This calls for a foreign policy that is informed by a broader national consensus, supported by effective institutional safeguards, and implemented through a professional diplomatic service\u201d.\n\nAmb. Aryasinha, who is presently the Executive Director of the Regional Centre for Strategic Studies (RCSS), made these observations when he delivered the 2026 \u2018Gamani Corea Memorial Lecture\u2019 on the theme The Paradoxes of Sri Lanka\u2019s Foreign Policy in the 21st Century on 4 November 2026 at the BCIS in Colombo, amidst a representative gathering comprising serving and retired public figures, diplomats, entrepreneurs, academics, civil society, media and students."
