    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "Amb. Aryasinha, who is presently the Executive Director of the Regional Centre for Strategic Studies (RCSS), made these observations when he delivered the 2026 \u2018Gamani Corea Memorial Lecture\u2019 on the theme The Paradoxes of Sri Lanka\u2019s Foreign Policy in the 21st Century on 4 November 2026 at the BCIS in Colombo, amidst a representative gathering comprising serving and retired public figures, diplomats, entrepreneurs, academics, civil society, media and students.\n\nAmb. Aryasinha commencing the lecture, said \u201cDr. Corea\u2019s contribution to international economic diplomacy was rooted in the conviction that developing countries, including smaller states, need not simply accept the terms of an international order shaped by others. Through the strength of his ideas, his diplomatic engagement and his work in institutions such as UNCTAD and the South Centre, he helped give collective expression to the concerns of developing countries and advance the case for a more equitable international economic system.\u201d\n\nIn his address, Amb. Aryasinha observed that Sri Lanka\u2019s foreign policy ever since independence was replete with paradoxes, but they were consciously faced head-on, straddled with, and to a considerable extent overcome. Despite Prime Minister D.S. Senanayaka in 1947 signing Defence and Foreign Affairs agreements with the British, UNP administrations resisted western colonialism, opposed war reparations on Japan, and went on to sign the Sino-Lanka Rubber Rice Pact. Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike a founding leader of the NAM in 1961, also demonstrated scrupulous objectivity between the West and the East. President J.R. Jayewardene though adopting the western neo-liberal economic model in 1977, nevertheless proclaimed that \u201cnon-alignment runs like a golden thread through the fabric of our country\u2019s foreign policy\u201d."
