    "chunk_text": "In his address, Amb. Aryasinha observed that Sri Lanka\u2019s foreign policy ever since independence was replete with paradoxes, but they were consciously faced head-on, straddled with, and to a considerable extent overcome. Despite Prime Minister D.S. Senanayaka in 1947 signing Defence and Foreign Affairs agreements with the British, UNP administrations resisted western colonialism, opposed war reparations on Japan, and went on to sign the Sino-Lanka Rubber Rice Pact. Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike a founding leader of the NAM in 1961, also demonstrated scrupulous objectivity between the West and the East. President J.R. Jayewardene though adopting the western neo-liberal economic model in 1977, nevertheless proclaimed that \u201cnon-alignment runs like a golden thread through the fabric of our country\u2019s foreign policy\u201d.\n\nThe third paradox of concern was human rights accountability, where getting the balance right in protecting human rights, while fighting terrorism/violent extremism has been a challenge in modern times. He noted that while Sri Lanka had adopted postures of compromise, collaboration, and confrontation in the past, currently the paradox lies in the fact that the posture adopted in the HRC by Sri Lanka since 2024 can at best be described as one of ambiguity - opposing the Sri Lanka Accountability Project (SLAP) in its statements in the HRC, but being unwilling to challenge it and permitting it to be passed unopposed. Additionally, in parallel asking the HRC to do away with the \u2018\u00c7ountry Specific Mandate\u2019 targeting Sri Lanka, while the High Commissioner for Human Rights (HCHR) insists that an international mechanism is vital for confidence building of the victims, but unwilling to share the OHCHR findings. Whether the driving factor in this is Norms, Laws, or Power Politics is hotly contested the world over - and clearly evidenced in Sri Lanka, with many - including most victims on all sides, seeing it as the latter."
