    "chunk_text": "The third paradox of concern was human rights accountability, where getting the balance right in protecting human rights, while fighting terrorism/violent extremism has been a challenge in modern times. He noted that while Sri Lanka had adopted postures of compromise, collaboration, and confrontation in the past, currently the paradox lies in the fact that the posture adopted in the HRC by Sri Lanka since 2024 can at best be described as one of ambiguity - opposing the Sri Lanka Accountability Project (SLAP) in its statements in the HRC, but being unwilling to challenge it and permitting it to be passed unopposed. Additionally, in parallel asking the HRC to do away with the \u2018\u00c7ountry Specific Mandate\u2019 targeting Sri Lanka, while the High Commissioner for Human Rights (HCHR) insists that an international mechanism is vital for confidence building of the victims, but unwilling to share the OHCHR findings. Whether the driving factor in this is Norms, Laws, or Power Politics is hotly contested the world over - and clearly evidenced in Sri Lanka, with many - including most victims on all sides, seeing it as the latter.\n\nAmbassador Aryasinha said straddling and overcoming these paradoxes, calls for a foreign policy that is informed by a broader national consensus, supported by effective institutional safeguards, and implemented through a professional diplomatic service capable of anticipating developments rather than merely reacting to them. It also requires us to look beyond the immediate calculations of major-power relations, to cultivate partnerships with middle powers and regional groupings, and to make fuller use of multilateral institutions in advancing issues of common interest. Above all, Sri Lanka must recover the capacity to articulate what it seeks from the world, and to pursue those objectives with consistency, credibility and purpose. It is in this respect that the example of Dr. Gamani Corea remains so relevant."
