    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d1612a615cdae624d9d2fed7d9bc3aab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1956,
    "chunk_text": "Building economy our grandchildren can depend on\n\nOur grandchildren will inherit more than the financial obligations of our generation. They will inherit the opportunities we create, or fail to create, while we have the chance. Those still unborn had no voice in the borrowing decisions of today; surely they deserve our foresight in building tomorrow\u2019s earning capacity. We cannot promise them a future without risk or debt. We can, however, work to leave them an economy less dependent on borrowing to meet its needs, and better able to prosper through what it produces and earns\n\nOur grandparents planted trees without knowing who would gather the fruit. We continue to enjoy their gift. Let us respond with the same quiet sense of duty: open the channels now, enable new activities to take root, and allow time for them to flourish. The harvest may take years, but the planting must be done now\n\nWe plan future debt repayments and strive to grow earnings from established sources. But can these alone secure our future? We must also open entirely new income pathways today, so that future generations can reap the benefits\n\nImagine a little girl preparing to enter school in January 2027, excited about her new uniform, books and the world opening before her. She knows nothing about international borrowing, debt restructuring or the foreign currency obligations Sri Lanka will face as she grows up. She did not choose those obligations. Yet the decisions we make today will influence the opportunities available to her and, one day, to children not yet born.\n\nIn our village gardens stand magnificent old fruit trees planted by grandparents and great-grandparents. Some of those who planted them never lived to enjoy their full harvest. We still gather the fruit and enjoy the shade, often without thinking about the people whose foresight made this possible. They planted for generations they might never meet. What are we planting today for ours?"
  },
