    "md5": "d7d8c555d8db147f7a3ce454352da07d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1642,
    "chunk_text": "In our village gardens stand magnificent old fruit trees planted by grandparents and great-grandparents. Some of those who planted them never lived to enjoy their full harvest. We still gather the fruit and enjoy the shade, often without thinking about the people whose foresight made this possible. They planted for generations they might never meet. What are we planting today for ours?\n\nThe danger is real, even if it is not always visible in daily life. The IMF\u2019s May 2026 assessment projects foreign currency public debt service averaging about 3.3% of GDP annually over 2027 to 2032. That is a substantial call on the country\u2019s future external resources, alongside the foreign exchange needed for imports and other international payments. Borrowing can be productive and necessary, but recurring dependence on new external financing, without expanding our capacity to earn, leaves future generations exposed. The child entering school next year will live with the consequences long after today\u2019s decisions have passed from public memory.\n\nOur Treasury rightly devotes serious attention to fiscal management, debt sustainability and future repayment schedules. Yet an improved Budget balance in rupees does not, by itself, ensure access to the dollars needed for external obligations. That requires a strong and resilient capacity to earn from the world, together with prudent external financing and reserves. The Treasury\u2019s Medium-Term Debt Management Strategy for 2026 to 2030 shows the value of planning ahead. Should we not complement that discipline with a similarly purposeful plan for creating additional earning capacity?"
  },
