    "chunk_id": "2026-11-13-dailyftlk-56998622-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e5ec47a9c759b08b1e5eb9dac3eebb02",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "Our Treasury rightly devotes serious attention to fiscal management, debt sustainability and future repayment schedules. Yet an improved Budget balance in rupees does not, by itself, ensure access to the dollars needed for external obligations. That requires a strong and resilient capacity to earn from the world, together with prudent external financing and reserves. The Treasury\u2019s Medium-Term Debt Management Strategy for 2026 to 2030 shows the value of planning ahead. Should we not complement that discipline with a similarly purposeful plan for creating additional earning capacity?\n\nA fruit tree takes years to mature, but planting it need not. A new industry may likewise take years to develop products, earn the trust of customers and produce substantial recurring income. In many cases, however, the enabling step is much quicker: clarify a regulation, remove an avoidable restriction, introduce a workable procedure or secure a coordinated decision. Some proposals will require legislation or substantial safeguards, and those must be respected. But the natural time required for a business to grow should never be confused with the time needed to make its establishment possible.\n\nAs an industrialist who has spent decades developing products and entering international markets, I have found that entrepreneurs rarely lack the motivation to seek customers or take commercial risks. Often, their most valuable help from Government is not another promotional program, but a predictable, competitive environment in which they are free to act. An entrepreneur may already have a supplier, an overseas customer, financing and a sound business model, yet be unable to proceed because existing arrangements do not permit the transaction. Finding that entrepreneur another customer will not solve the problem. What is needed is an environment in which the business can start and grow."
  },
