    "chunk_size_bytes": 1662,
    "chunk_text": "As an industrialist who has spent decades developing products and entering international markets, I have found that entrepreneurs rarely lack the motivation to seek customers or take commercial risks. Often, their most valuable help from Government is not another promotional program, but a predictable, competitive environment in which they are free to act. An entrepreneur may already have a supplier, an overseas customer, financing and a sound business model, yet be unable to proceed because existing arrangements do not permit the transaction. Finding that entrepreneur another customer will not solve the problem. What is needed is an environment in which the business can start and grow.\n\nThe establishment of ViYASA, the National Business Facilitation Centre (NBFC), under the Presidential Secretariat is therefore a welcome development. Its work in overcoming regulatory and administrative obstacles provides an opportunity to go beyond solving problems faced by existing businesses. With a clear mandate and effective coordination, it could also enable new earning and foreign exchange saving activities that are not yet practical under present arrangements.\n\nThere are surely more opportunities than any one institution can identify on its own. A few proposals for new pathways are understood to have reached ViYASA already, and many more could come from businesses, professional bodies, universities and industry associations. The important step is to give promising, viable ideas a dependable route from submission to a decision and, where justified, to an operational framework. Until that happens, even an excellent proposal remains only an idea."
  },
