    "md5": "7498b3b6f02962202651ec31c5eeda62",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1616,
    "chunk_text": "There are surely more opportunities than any one institution can identify on its own. A few proposals for new pathways are understood to have reached ViYASA already, and many more could come from businesses, professional bodies, universities and industry associations. The important step is to give promising, viable ideas a dependable route from submission to a decision and, where justified, to an operational framework. Until that happens, even an excellent proposal remains only an idea.\n\nThe program need not attempt to forecast the precise earnings of every new venture. Instead, a simple quarterly report could record three things: how many new pathways have become legally and operationally available, how many businesses have begun using them, and what genuinely additional net foreign exchange they have earned or saved. The last measure must account for associated foreign currency costs and distinguish realised results from projections. Such reporting would connect present facilitation with the earnings that may emerge over the following five or ten years.\n\nA brief commitment in the forthcoming Budget would be a valuable beginning, even if the full program is developed during 2027. It would not require Government to choose every business, find its customers or finance its investments. It would establish something more enduring: a responsibility to keep opening productive opportunities and to show, quarter by quarter, what has become possible. The question at each review would be simple: what new earning activity can Sri Lankans undertake today that they could not undertake three months ago?"
  },
