    "chunk_text": "The Deputy Minister also highlighted Sri Lanka\u2019s significant potential for further renewable energy development. Particular attention was drawn to opportunities for floating solar projects in identified reservoirs, as well as solar and wind development in the Northern, Eastern and Southern regions. The ongoing development of wind power projects in Mannar and the assessment of Sri Lanka\u2019s offshore wind potential were also highlighted as areas of future opportunity.\n\nSri Lanka further emphasised the importance of technology, knowledge-sharing, investment and capacity-building in translating its renewable energy ambitions into tangible outcomes.\n\nThe discussions covered practical areas of cooperation including solar integration with the national grid, energy storage, digital grid management, floating solar and solarisation of agriculture, as well as opportunities to translate existing cooperation into concrete projects. Particular attention was given to the operationalisation of the Solar Technology Application Resource Centre (STAR-C) at the University of Moratuwa, which provides a platform for strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s technical capacity and application of solar technologies. The meeting reaffirmed the importance of practical cooperation, technology and knowledge-sharing in supporting Sri Lanka\u2019s renewable energy transition and strengthening energy security.\n\nSri Lanka has also been working closely with the ISA on a number of initiatives, including the establishment of the STAR-C at the University of Moratuwa, the development of a digital system to facilitate the approval of solar energy projects, cooperation on solar applications in agriculture, and support for floating solar development and the preparation of a National Solar Energy Roadmap for Sri Lanka."
