    "chunk_size_bytes": 1908,
    "chunk_text": "Enduring resistance\n\nChina has served as a convenient bipartisan adversary. It has taken the place of the Soviet Union in reflecting both tremendous technological potential and a supposed enemy that must be contained. But military confrontation in the Pacific remains perhaps even less likely. The demonstration effect is powerful in what the US and Russia have encountered while trying to subdue far weaker countries in the age of drone and missile defence\n\nThere is a renewed possibility of a political challenge to finance on a world scale. The interest rate shock initiated by US Federal Reserve chairman Paul Volcker in the late 1970s was the death knell for proposals for a New International Economic Order. It plunged developing countries into severe debt crisis as debt servicing costs skyrocketed in the 1980s. But the new Democratic affordability agenda raises broader questions about what kind of finance is possible under alternative, more progressive circumstances\n\nAlready Trump has crowed that the reversal in stock market gains in the aftermath of Democratic victory is the result of the threatened overturning of his AI agenda. If finance is heavily intertwined with the fortunes of a decent segment of the American electorate, then it is unclear what other kind of settlement would produce an electoral bloc that can resist the tendency to conflate fiduciary responsibility for people\u2019s retirement portfolios with all the aggressive entrapments of what world systems scholar Peter Gowan called the \u2018Dollar-Wall Street Regime\u2019\n\nNew political frameworks are being hammered out in an ongoing battle across an extremely fragmented global landscape, despite tremendous dangers, whether war, fascism, or worse. As the recent elections in the American antipodes show, this struggle has far from been determined. That elusive moment when the tide looks like it may turn offers hope amid chaos"
  },
