    "md5": "e9488d38ea5e9a1adb06abdfc7316e8d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "New political frameworks are being hammered out in an ongoing battle across an extremely fragmented global landscape, despite tremendous dangers, whether war, fascism, or worse. As the recent elections in the American antipodes show, this struggle has far from been determined. That elusive moment when the tide looks like it may turn offers hope amid chaos\n\nThe US midterm elections\u2014right on the heels of Lula\u2019s crushing of the far-right candidate, Flavio Bolsonaro, in Brazil\u2014proved to be a real shock. The US results appear to be as decisive as Trump\u2019s initial victory in 2016, when he won the upper Midwest that has long been associated with post-industrial decay.\n\nIn the case of the most recent electoral results, the inroads of the Democratic Party into the South anticipate a potential transformation. That includes the possibility that the Democratic party line affiliation becomes competitive again in the region, only this time organised along the lines of multiracial democracy.\n\nIt appears to be the end of the generational loss of the South to the Democratic Party, which Lyndon Baines Johnson feared would be the outcome of the Voting Rights Act. That Act was the legislative capstone of the Civil Rights Movement. Its dismantling by the Supreme Court in the Louisiana v. Callais case this year is what shaped the political earthquake of the midterm elections.\n\nWhile keeping this poetic reversal in mind, there is also a brute reality we must face: as much as \u2018vibes\u2019 have arguably taken over elections in many places around the world, in places like the US there is still a material contradiction at the core of the AI boom that has yet to be resolved. The economy continues to diverge between the fortunes of capital and labour. However, there is no guarantee that the diverse constituencies marshalled under opposition to Trump will cohere into a durable bloc."
