    "chunk_text": "While keeping this poetic reversal in mind, there is also a brute reality we must face: as much as \u2018vibes\u2019 have arguably taken over elections in many places around the world, in places like the US there is still a material contradiction at the core of the AI boom that has yet to be resolved. The economy continues to diverge between the fortunes of capital and labour. However, there is no guarantee that the diverse constituencies marshalled under opposition to Trump will cohere into a durable bloc.\n\nMeanwhile, what might the US posture be towards the rest of the world because of the tremendous churn in the country\u2019s politics? It is not obvious if, and even how, it might try to revive its hegemonic position. Deploying the 1960s \u2018Cold Warrior\u2019 as the closest analogy, New York Times columnist Jamelle Bouie argued that in his victory speech, Jon Ossoff, the victorious Democratic senator from Georgia, embraced rhetoric very close to the \u2018evil empire\u2019. Only in this case, Ossoff used it to describe MAGA fascism, not the Soviet Union.\n\nAs a domestic metaphor, it was a deft counterpoint to the right-wing attempt to turn the US midterm elections into another Red Scare, which was laced with noxious Islamophobia for good measure. But in terms of foreign policy, the explanatory power of this analogy is limited by the fact that the US battle against domestic totalitarianism cannot be projected as part of a reassertion of its global hegemony, which continues to unravel."
