    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "As a domestic metaphor, it was a deft counterpoint to the right-wing attempt to turn the US midterm elections into another Red Scare, which was laced with noxious Islamophobia for good measure. But in terms of foreign policy, the explanatory power of this analogy is limited by the fact that the US battle against domestic totalitarianism cannot be projected as part of a reassertion of its global hegemony, which continues to unravel.\n\nOf course, Israel remains the wild card given the paroxysm of violence it has unleashed. In his piece, \u201cThe Collapse of Zionism\u201d, Israeli historian Ilan Pappe observed the parallel with the dying apartheid regime in South Africa.\n\nAt the same time, there is Russia. It remains a gravitational lens for the West\u2019s political anxieties\u2014in the past conservative, today progressive, with the latter\u2019s fears surrounding the global expansion of the far right. Even in the age of Marx, he considered the Tsardom the \u2018gendarme of European reaction\u2019. Strategically though, Russia\u2019s aggressive expansionism is a non-starter as the rationale for renewed US ambition, given that the European Union sees the absolute need, at least rhetorically, for a separate defence umbrella.\n\nThe transatlantic relationship has permanently ruptured under Trump. That does not mean that the US and the EU will no longer collaborate as part of a residual hegemony that they exercise on a contingent basis in other parts of the world. But the relationship is no longer an unquestionable pillar of support for the US\u2019s global ambitions.\n\nMeanwhile, China has served as a convenient bipartisan adversary. It has taken the place of the Soviet Union in reflecting both tremendous technological potential and a supposed enemy that must be contained. But military confrontation in the Pacific remains perhaps even less likely. The demonstration effect is powerful in what the US and Russia have encountered while trying to subdue far weaker countries in the age of drone and missile defence."
