    "chunk_size_bytes": 1496,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, China has served as a convenient bipartisan adversary. It has taken the place of the Soviet Union in reflecting both tremendous technological potential and a supposed enemy that must be contained. But military confrontation in the Pacific remains perhaps even less likely. The demonstration effect is powerful in what the US and Russia have encountered while trying to subdue far weaker countries in the age of drone and missile defence.\n\nThis looming question is where US domestic politics will almost certainly play a role in determining the unravelling of its imperial trajectory. Democrats may discover a purpose not only in regulating frontier AI development because of its impact on local communities, but also the corollary that by confronting tech overlords, they must reckon with a major shift in attitude towards the exercise of military power abroad.\n\nAlready Elon Musk\u2019s gamble to throttle Starlink in Ukraine has produced murmurs about the need for nationalisation. And Musk\u2019s role in the assault of the \u2018Department of Government Efficiency\u2019 on federal bureaucracy is almost certain to come under powerful congressional scrutiny. However, war making is not only the arena of private tech oligarchs. It is also the animating principle of the modern State.\n\nPerhaps even more relevant, it remains extremely unclear whether there is genuine space for anti-imperialism, given that the US\u2019s financial fortunes have been tied to the maintenance of its global hegemony."
