    "chunk_text": "Perhaps even more relevant, it remains extremely unclear whether there is genuine space for anti-imperialism, given that the US\u2019s financial fortunes have been tied to the maintenance of its global hegemony.\n\nThis argument does not mean we must revert to the assumption that the \u2018labour aristocracy\u2019 in the West is constitutively incapable of producing material alliances with working people in the global South. But it is a fact of politics that the organisation and maintenance of class hegemony in the US through finance has inserted a time bomb into the social order.\n\nIt must be defused carefully to avoid provoking the kind of resentment and frustration that could support revanchist moves to try and claim as much plunder abroad to compensate for further downward mobility among the ill-defined \u2018middle class\u2019. Trump\u2019s gambit might have temporarily failed in Iran. But unless it is replaced by a clear alternative, the most extreme elements of aggression abroad could reappear.\n\nNevertheless, with the US midterm results, there is a renewed possibility of a political challenge to finance on a world scale. The interest rate shock initiated by US Federal Reserve chairman Paul Volcker in the late 1970s was the death knell for proposals for a New International Economic Order. It plunged developing countries into severe debt crisis as debt servicing costs skyrocketed in the 1980s. But the new Democratic affordability agenda raises broader questions about what kind of finance is possible under alternative, more progressive circumstances."
