    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "Nevertheless, with the US midterm results, there is a renewed possibility of a political challenge to finance on a world scale. The interest rate shock initiated by US Federal Reserve chairman Paul Volcker in the late 1970s was the death knell for proposals for a New International Economic Order. It plunged developing countries into severe debt crisis as debt servicing costs skyrocketed in the 1980s. But the new Democratic affordability agenda raises broader questions about what kind of finance is possible under alternative, more progressive circumstances.\n\nAt the same time, the potential reversal of the class project of finance capital may not provide immediate relief to peripheral countries that are on the frontlines of climate change and debt crisis, even while facing IMF-mandated austerity. As others have pointed out, the latter has restrained the investments needed to cope with shocks. But growing scrutiny of Big Tech, and, by extension, finance within the metropole could create breathing room for countries in the global South to at least conduct more of their own domestic experiments. In this case, the results of the US midterm elections are yet another data point revealing a possible progressive turn in the global order, even if they are not the ultimate result.\n\nAt the very least though, we should be aware of how our shared global terrain is being reshaped. Latin American historian Greg Grandin has pointed out in his lyrical history \u2018America, Am\u00e9rica\u2019 that North and South are interlinked in their destinies. Much as Franklin Delano Roosevelt\u2019s New Deal helped facilitate Import Substitution Industrialisation in Latin America under figures like Get\u00falio Vargas, and vice versa, we may be at another historical juncture."
