    "chunk_text": "At the very least though, we should be aware of how our shared global terrain is being reshaped. Latin American historian Greg Grandin has pointed out in his lyrical history \u2018America, Am\u00e9rica\u2019 that North and South are interlinked in their destinies. Much as Franklin Delano Roosevelt\u2019s New Deal helped facilitate Import Substitution Industrialisation in Latin America under figures like Get\u00falio Vargas, and vice versa, we may be at another historical juncture.\n\nThere is plenty to fear about the potential tendency towards dispossession. Sri Lanka was threatened with it only several years ago at the height of its economic crisis, including the attempted fire sale of its assets. But the US midterm elections and Lula\u2019s victory in Brazil also show that there are always going to be forces of resistance. More than the national boundaries that supposedly resemble coherent geopolitical blocs, it is the ongoing \u2018civil war\u2019, as Lenin reminds us, that will be central to restraining finance capital and the imperial powers backing it.\n\nIn the context of recent political developments abroad, even a small country like Sri Lanka may find more room for manoeuvre to engage in experiments that were long since banished as anathema to the market. That includes revitalising the public sector without fear of the kind of foreign policy crisis that temporarily resulted in the suspending of US aid in the early 1960s, when Sri Lanka nationalised its oil industry. In this regard, geopolitics is intimately tied to economic relations.\n\nBut equally important are the ideological imaginaries shared across places, which can inspire solidarity. New political frameworks are being hammered out in an ongoing battle across an extremely fragmented global landscape, despite tremendous dangers, whether war, fascism, or worse. As the recent elections in the American antipodes show, this struggle has far from been determined. That elusive moment when