    "md5": "c0cf315d1919c916a1bc19a089e5f67e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1398,
    "chunk_text": "Why we must treat atmosphere as global common\n\nThe same right that allows the mining of the commons of air for private profit must recognise the right to replacement, where privately produced gasses are placed into the commons of air. In this way the Global Commons of Air could act very much as a global bank to hold and account for the critical stocks it accepts into and dispenses out of the global capital\n\nThere is an urgent need to consider our responsibility by the most ubequitious and essential common resource, air! The atmosphere has been identified as a Global Common by every national and internaional statement to date. The Global Commons are the Earth\u2019s shared natural resources and environmental domains that lie outside the political jurisdiction of any single nation-state.\u00a0 The assumption being that, because no individual country owns them, all nations share access to and responsibility for them.\n\nInternational law traditionally recognises four core domains as Global Commons.\n\n1. High Seas: The open ocean areas beyond national exclusive economic zones (more than 200 nautical miles from a coast).\n\n2. Atmosphere: The layer of gases surrounding the planet that regulates climate and air quality.\n\n3. Antarctica: The southernmost continent, governed by the Antarctic Treaty System\n\n4. Outer Space: The void beyond Earth\u2019s atmosphere, including celestial bodies and orbits"
