    "md5": "e7894c3678041251810dabce64f5b591",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1743,
    "chunk_text": "2. Atmosphere: The layer of gases surrounding the planet that regulates climate and air quality.\n\n3. Antarctica: The southernmost continent, governed by the Antarctic Treaty System\n\n4. Outer Space: The void beyond Earth\u2019s atmosphere, including celestial bodies and orbits\n\nCurrently, the free Oxygen in the atmosphere is about 1.2x1015 tonnes (12,000,000,000,000,000 t), but it is unstable in our planet\u2019s atmosphere and must be constantly replenished by photosynthesis of green plants.\u00a0 Recent human activity, mostly the burning of fossil fuels and deforestation have led to an increase in the concentration of Carbon Dioxide and a decrease in Oxygen.\n\nIn addition to gasses, Earth\u2019s atmosphere also contains variable quantities of suspended aerosols and particulates that can effect human health and create weather features such as clouds and hazes. Particulates or fine particulate matter with a diameter of 2.5 micrometers or less, which is about 30 times smaller in diameter than a human hair, is known as PM 2.5. Because of its tiny size, it stays in the air for a long time and can travel deep into the lungs and bloodstream.\n\nAnother significant pollutant of the atmosphere are the Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs). These are carbon-based chemicals that easily turn into gas and evaporate into the air at room temperature. When VOCs react with sunlight and nitrogen oxides in the atmosphere, they form ground-level ozone and are a primary driver of photochemical smog and atmospheric haze. Chronic or high-level exposure can cause permanent damage to the liver, kidneys, and central nervous system. Certain common VOCs, such as benzene, formaldehyde, and methylene chloride, are known or suspected carcinogens linked to cancer."
