    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "Another significant pollutant of the atmosphere are the Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs). These are carbon-based chemicals that easily turn into gas and evaporate into the air at room temperature. When VOCs react with sunlight and nitrogen oxides in the atmosphere, they form ground-level ozone and are a primary driver of photochemical smog and atmospheric haze. Chronic or high-level exposure can cause permanent damage to the liver, kidneys, and central nervous system. Certain common VOCs, such as benzene, formaldehyde, and methylene chloride, are known or suspected carcinogens linked to cancer.\n\nRecent research\u00a0 by scientists at the Stockholm University reveals that rainwater across every region of Earth is now contaminated not only with microplastics but also now contains toxic per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) at levels\u00a0 that exceed safety limits set by major health agencies.\n\nMuch like plastics they are known as \u2018forever chemicals\u2019 due to their extreme resistance to natural degradation, these man made compounds have circulated through the global water cycle, contaminating even the most pristine and isolated regions, including Antarctica and the Tibetan Plateau. This pattern of widespread dispersal means that rainfall everywhere on the planet is no longer safe to consume without specialised purification.\n\nThese chemicals have been manufactured for decades in products like nonstick cookware, waterproof gear, and firefighting foams, leaching into waterways and cycling into the atmosphere via ocean spray. Exposure to such persistent pollutants is linked to severe long-term health risks, including various forms of cancer, compromised immune systems, decreased fertility, and developmental delays in children."
