    "chunk_size_bytes": 1611,
    "chunk_text": "These chemicals have been manufactured for decades in products like nonstick cookware, waterproof gear, and firefighting foams, leaching into waterways and cycling into the atmosphere via ocean spray. Exposure to such persistent pollutants is linked to severe long-term health risks, including various forms of cancer, compromised immune systems, decreased fertility, and developmental delays in children.\n\nThe commons of air have largely become a repository to the \u2018negative externalities\u2019 produced by industrial and transport activities of the modern economic system. These negative externalities are exemplified by the process of obtaining energy for industry or transport. All the combustion gasses of burning fossil carbon compounds, provoke a cost to the environment but that cost is considered a \u2018negative externality\u2019 and the person discharging these toxins has no responsibility nor cost for that action.\n\nThis assumption implies, that if a process produces a benefit to the environment during its activity, it must be considered a \u2018positive extenality\u2019 and reflect a value for that action. If the product is something that is being taken out or mined out of the commons, the replacement of what has been lost must have value. The same right that allows the mining of the commons of air for private profit must recognise the right to replacement, where privately produced gasses are placed into the commons of air. In this way the Global Commons of Air could act very much as a global bank to hold and account for the critical stocks it accepts into and dispenses out of the global capital."
