    "md5": "0baf79611c3b5da6e5b95b7d9d1ac341",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "Assassination of Kavantissa: Coincidence, negligence, or an ideological message?\n\nWas the handling of Kavantissa\u2019s case, culminating in his death and followed almost immediately by an initiative to protect all elephants, intended to send an ideologically charged message rooted in communist thinking that the NPP-JVP Government would grant special treatment to no one, regardless of merit, circumstance, and public sentiment, even when the consequence is the loss of a valuable life? Are you, as a Sri Lankan, prepared to be a victim of such an ideology? Today it may be Kavantissa. Tomorrow, could it be you?\n\nThe title poses a difficult and uncomfortable question. Before dismissing it, however, it is worth examining the remarkable chain of events that followed the alleged shooting of Kavantissa in late September. Failures appear to have occurred, or been permitted to occur, at every stage, from the incident itself through to Kavantissa\u203as burial and its aftermath. Such widespread failures rarely stem from a simple error of judgement by one individual or the shortcomings of a single institution. More often, they point to something deeper and more serious. This article explores those possibilities, but first we must establish the facts.\n\nKavantissa, the legendary tusker from Galgamuwa, believed to be about 45 years old, died on 16 October 2026 after his health deteriorated over several weeks following a reported shooting in September. Many wildlife conservationists, enthusiasts, animal lovers, and others reportedly kept the authorities regularly informed of Kavantissa\u203as condition through various channels. Many accounts point to what appears to have been a striking lack of engagement by the authorities responsible for Kavantissa\u203as welfare. Given the animal\u2019s iconic status, critics, such as Dr Ajantha Perera and Tissa Jananayake, publicly argue that the response of the Department of Wildlife Conservation and other officials fell far short of what the situation warranted."
