    "chunk_text": "Kavantissa, the legendary tusker from Galgamuwa, believed to be about 45 years old, died on 16 October 2026 after his health deteriorated over several weeks following a reported shooting in September. Many wildlife conservationists, enthusiasts, animal lovers, and others reportedly kept the authorities regularly informed of Kavantissa\u203as condition through various channels. Many accounts point to what appears to have been a striking lack of engagement by the authorities responsible for Kavantissa\u203as welfare. Given the animal\u2019s iconic status, critics, such as Dr Ajantha Perera and Tissa Jananayake, publicly argue that the response of the Department of Wildlife Conservation and other officials fell far short of what the situation warranted.\n\nThe controversy did not end with Kavantissa\u203as death. Reports alleged that the animal\u2019s body was severed at the neck into two parts and buried separately within hours, in a manner that many regarded as undignified and deeply disrespectful to one of Sri Lanka\u203as most celebrated tuskers. The handling of the remains sent shockwaves throughout the country and compounded the grief and outrage felt by thousands of people. We saw grown men and women crying in public.\n\nThroughout the entire saga, the apparent indifference displayed by senior Government leaders, including the President, was particularly striking. Despite widespread public concern, there appeared to be little acknowledgement of responsibility, little expression of remorse, and little urgency in addressing the growing criticism surrounding the case. Yet, within days of Kavantissa\u203as death, the Government announced a new initiative aimed at safeguarding all elephants and ensuring their proper treatment across Sri Lanka."
