    "md5": "7d0074d76cc1c56ce1a4c97030da3c2f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1633,
    "chunk_text": "Throughout the entire saga, the apparent indifference displayed by senior Government leaders, including the President, was particularly striking. Despite widespread public concern, there appeared to be little acknowledgement of responsibility, little expression of remorse, and little urgency in addressing the growing criticism surrounding the case. Yet, within days of Kavantissa\u203as death, the Government announced a new initiative aimed at safeguarding all elephants and ensuring their proper treatment across Sri Lanka.\n\nThe alleged shooting was a failure of wildlife protection. The untimely death was a failure of wildlife treatment. The undignified burial was a failure of compassion and remorse. Together, these events have left thousands of grieving Sri Lankans asking one simple question: Why was Kavantissa, an innocent animal with no known history of attacking humans, treated in this manner? Was this merely a failure of an individual or an institution, or does it point to something more sinister that every Sri Lankan should be concerned about?\n\nJanatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), the dominant force within the NPP coalition Government, is rooted in a political tradition heavily influenced by communist ideology. Communist ideology is broad, but one of its central principles is the pursuit of greater social and economic equality, and at its core lies a deep suspicion of privilege, hierarchy, and special treatment. While the pursuit of equality is a noble objective, problems emerge when equality is interpreted so rigidly that excellence, distinction, and achievement are viewed as undesirable or even threatening."
