    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), the dominant force within the NPP coalition Government, is rooted in a political tradition heavily influenced by communist ideology. Communist ideology is broad, but one of its central principles is the pursuit of greater social and economic equality, and at its core lies a deep suspicion of privilege, hierarchy, and special treatment. While the pursuit of equality is a noble objective, problems emerge when equality is interpreted so rigidly that excellence, distinction, and achievement are viewed as undesirable or even threatening.\n\nWhen senior NPP-JVP figures declare, \u201cwe have the Government, but not the State,\u201d they are revealing more than they intend, and far more than many Sri Lankans realise. The significance of that statement deserves a detailed discussion of its own, but we do not have space to discuss the meaning of this statement in detail here. In essence, however, it suggests that while political power has been secured through electoral victory, the broader project of transforming the nation\u203as institutions, values, culture of governance, and ultimately its constitutional order strictly along communist lines remains incomplete. In that context, Kavantissa was more than a tusker, but an ideologically uncomfortable, symbolically profound obstacle.\n\nKavantissa was a distinguished tusker, celebrated for his size, stature, temperament, and grandeur. He was a symbol of distinction, prestige, and public admiration. To an ideology uncomfortable with distinction, prestige, and status such a symbol can become an obstacle, not much as a physical obstacle, but as a powerful psychological one. When an ideology seeks to reshape a society, it does not stop with changing institutions. It seeks to redefine what people admire, respect, and aspire to become."
  },
