    "description": "Apparel exporters press for quick action on Budget 2027 pledges",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Apparel-exporters-press-for-quick-action-on-Budget-2027-pledges/215-354910",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794642155.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-f9e25315-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f052db17a3dfc41360ff01aea87452a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Apparel exporters press for quick action on Budget 2027 pledges\n\nThe apparel industry has urged the government to move quickly on the Budget 2027 measures to cut the trade costs and spur investment, while asking whether the smaller manufacturers can qualify for a proposed capital allowance with investments below US $ 3 million.\n\nThe Joint Apparel Association Forum (JAAF) said the budget adopted several of its proposals, including a 200 percent allowance on qualifying capital expenditure for the export-oriented manufacturers approved by the Board of Investment that invest at least US $ 3 million. The industry body said the measure could encourage the manufacturers to upgrade technology and expand capacity and sought a lower threshold for the small and medium-sized enterprises.\n\n\u201cBudget 2027 gives the exporters a constructive base to build on,\u201d JAAF Chairman Felix Fernando said.\n\n\u201cAnnouncements create confidence but implementation delivers results. We look forward to working with the government to ensure these measures are put into practice quickly, so that the investors can plan with certainty.\u201d\n\nThe JAAF said apparel was Sri Lanka\u2019s largest export earner, with the shipments exceeding US $ 5 billion in 2025.\n\nThe budget allocates Rs.1.99 billion to the National Single Window for Trade, which is scheduled to connect the exporters in the first half of 2027. The JAAF sought the details on the scope and timing of the rollout and backed the plans to overhaul the Customs Ordinance, modernise the Temporary Importation for Export Processing scheme and move towards paperless clearance.\n\nThe association also asked the government to identify the 800 tariff lines covered by a proposed 25 percent reduction in the cess rates and clarify whether they include imported materials used to build new manufacturing facilities."
  },
