    "chunk_text": "The association also asked the government to identify the 800 tariff lines covered by a proposed 25 percent reduction in the cess rates and clarify whether they include imported materials used to build new manufacturing facilities.\n\nThe industry body flagged the energy costs and reliability of supply as factors in manufacturing competitiveness and said it hoped to take part in the labour law review through the National Labour Advisory Council.\n\nThe JAAF reiterated its support for Sri Lanka\u2019s reapplication for the European Union\u2019s (EU) GSP+ tariff preferences, citing the importance of continued access to the market. It also welcomed a Rs.3.43 billion EU-funded project running from 2027 to 2030, to support the export competitiveness and sustainable production."
