    "chunk_size_bytes": 1826,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 could do more to attract foreign capital: CCC\n\nCalls for faster land and labour reforms and changes to public procurement to speed up project delivery\n\nSays more targeted and actionable proposals could have been introduced to unlock potential of sectors with significant growth opportunities, including tourism, agriculture, IT/BPO and logistics\n\nAcknowledges more than 20 of its proposals are reflected in budget, including establishment of a professionally managed investment promotion agency\n\nAsserts priority now must be effective and timely implementation to translate policy commitments into investment\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s largest private sector representative body said Budget 2027 could have done more to attract foreign direct investments, calling for faster land and labour reforms and changes to public procurement to speed up project delivery.\n\nThe Ceylon Chamber of Commerce (CCC) said the budget maintained fiscal discipline and carried forward key reforms from 2026, giving businesses greater policy certainty.\n\nIt said more targeted measures in tourism, agriculture, IT and business process outsourcing and logistics would have helped Sri Lanka make the transition from economic stabilisation to investment-led growth.\n\n\u201cMore targeted and actionable proposals could have been introduced to unlock the potential of sectors with significant growth opportunities, including tourism, agriculture, IT/BPO and logistics,\u201d the CCC said in a statement.\n\nIt said more than 20 of its proposals were reflected in the budget, including the establishment of a professionally managed investment promotion agency.\u00a0 It also welcomed the continued efforts to strengthen the one-stop services for the investors and noted that the government was considering a successor programme with the International Monetary Fund."
  },
