    "md5": "97795f0ae26c51894a0216ea567a23bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1519,
    "chunk_text": "Draconian Bills meet the test of democracy\n\nThe government, despite its fiery and brave political rhetoric, buoyed by its two-thirds majority, introduced two draconian pieces of legislation \u2013 the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill and the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill (PSTB).\n\nThe two proposed laws speak volumes about the government\u2019s intentions \u2013 the suppression of dissent in a bid to consolidate a power base which is on the wane due to its failure to deliver. Alongside this, come what may, lawmakers should bear in mind that basic principles must be adhered to in the legislative process. A law should conform to the norms of the civilised world. Given the original contents of these two Bills, one wonders whether the government had an iota of concern for such principles in drafting them.\n\nIf that had been the case, the government would not have allowed connections to organised crime to be inferred from a person\u2019s tattoos, manner of dress and associations under the Organised Crimes Bill.\n\nOnce the Bills were presented to Parliament and entered the public domain, aggrieved parties began challenging their constitutionality. Civil society activism peaked, with various groups lobbying for the scrapping or dilution of their provisions.\n\nThe Daily Mirror, as a newspaper that stands for the democratic rights of citizens, gave prominence to the issue in its coverage. Court proceedings were widely reported in a bid to mount pressure on the government to budge from its adamant stand."
  },
  {
