    "md5": "6bab22091a9d6f6aa00862cdfc515a40",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "The Daily Mirror, as a newspaper that stands for the democratic rights of citizens, gave prominence to the issue in its coverage. Court proceedings were widely reported in a bid to mount pressure on the government to budge from its adamant stand.\n\nSolicitor General Viraj Dayaratne, appearing for the Attorney General, made this submission when the Supreme Court commenced hearing Special Determination petitions challenging the constitutionality of the proposed legislation. He said the amendments would be incorporated into the Bill at the parliamentary committee stage.\n\nThe matter was taken up before a three-judge bench comprising Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena and Justices Achala Wengappuli and Sampath Abayakoon.\n\nThe government also intends to remove the restriction on media reporting relating to serious crimes under Clause 14(3), which had raised concerns over its potential impact on press freedom and the public\u2019s right to information. Under this provision, publishing such material without prior permission of the court was categorised as an offence punishable by up to two years\u2019 imprisonment.\n\n\u201cAny person who prints or publishes any matter in relation to any proceeding in any court with respect to a serious crime under this Act, without the previous permission of such court, commits an offence and shall on conviction after summary trial before a Magistrate, be liable to a term of imprisonment of either description for a term not exceeding two years or to a fine or to both such imprisonment and fine.\u201d\n\nThe Solicitor General further informed the Court that the definition of a \u2018serious crime\u2019 would be narrowed to cover offences listed in the First Schedule, punishable by more than four years\u2019 imprisonment, when committed by a member of an organised criminal group.\n\nHe emphasised that the provisions of the proposed legislation would apply only to structured groups rather than individuals acting independently."
