    "chunk_text": "It is amidst this suffering and the brutality of winter that Palestinians were reminded of the horror of all horrors on November 2, the day Britain issued a declaration supporting the establishment of a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine. Not all Jews wanted such a national home. It was the project of the European Zionist Jews who collaborated with the European powers.\n\nOn November 2, 1917, Arthur James Balfour, then Britain\u2019s foreign secretary, sent a letter to Lord Walter Rothschild, a British Zionist leader, declaring:\n\nMahatma Gandhi regarded the creation of a Jewish national home in Palestine at the expense of its Arab population as morally unjustifiable. He said: \u201cPalestine belongs to the Arabs in the same sense that England belongs to the English or France to the French.\u201d\n\nIn a famous quote, British author and journalist Arthur Koestler described his country\u2019s move as \u201cone nation solemnly promising to a second the country of a third.\u201d\n\nBut Britain, which then ruled the waves and waived the rules, has shown little remorse. Successive governments have had no qualms about defending the Balfour Declaration.\n\nIt is often claimed that the Balfour Declaration arose out of a noble intention to help European Jews subjected to anti-Semitism. Far from it, the declaration that came after the secret Sykes-Picot Agreement of 1916 smacks of Britain\u2019s intention to further its war efforts and advance its colonial agenda.\n\nWhen the declaration was issued, Hitler was not even on the European political landscape. Zionist Jews were financially influential in the United States, Britain and other parts of Europe. Anti-Semitism was waning, especially after the infamous Dreyfus Affair in France."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-a929f359",
