    "time_ut": 1794643000.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-a929f359-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "31c0e9f86be700499792b8b7eb64ae87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "When the declaration was issued, Hitler was not even on the European political landscape. Zionist Jews were financially influential in the United States, Britain and other parts of Europe. Anti-Semitism was waning, especially after the infamous Dreyfus Affair in France.\n\nOne hundred and nine years ago, before this criminal declaration was made, the Jews living in harmony with their Arab brethren in the Middle East had not even heard of the Zionist movement founded by Austrian activist Theodor Herzl in 1897. Unaware of the Zionists\u2019 ulterior motives, Palestinians welcomed European Jews. Most of them were Ashkenazi Jews whose origins lay in present-day Ukraine and eastern Russia. When, in the 1930s, Arabs realised the danger and revolted, Britain, which was governing Palestine under a League of Nations mandate, crushed the uprising and killed several prominent Palestinian leaders.\n\nAfter 109 years, three major Arab-Israeli wars, two intifadas, scores of peace efforts and a live-streamed genocide, Palestinians have yet to be freed from the yoke of colonialism.\n\nToday, they possess less than 17 percent of the territory that the 1947 UN partition plan had allocated to them. Laws are regularly passed in Israel to annex as much of the remaining Palestinian territory as possible. As Jewish settlements expand in occupied Palestine, Israel has become an apartheid state.\n\nIt was not the Palestinians who persecuted the Jews during World War II. Palestinians are paying for Europe\u2019s ugliest crime. Sadly, the persecuted have become the persecutors. As Palestinians mark the Balfour Declaration with tears, the international community remains powerless to compel Israel to withdraw to the 1967 borders. The promise of a two-state solution is a charade perpetrated by Israel and its key ally, the United States."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "date_str": "2026-11-14",
    "description": "Another Depression, crash, and world war?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Another-Depression-crash-and-world-war/131-354919",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794644209.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0845e4a07d50f20f29ae8f641fa00baa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1629,
    "chunk_text": "Another Depression, crash, and world war?\n\nDoes this mean another US economic crash along the lines of 1929? Prophesy is a dangerous thing. All we can do is to examine why it happened, in peace time, eleven years after World War I ended\n\nWhat is notable is that World War I paved the way to a great expansion of American industry and the US was able to overtake her industrial rivals Germany and Britain\n\nOver the past three years, ever since Russia invaded Ukraine, a third world war has been predicted. Accompanying this is another dire forecast, that a worldwide economic depression along the lines of the Great Depression of 1929 would happen.\n\nThe Russian invasion of Ukraine began four years and nine months ago. World War II lasted six years and a day. It was ended by two nuclear bombs. Historians argue that Japan could have been defeated without the use of nuclear bombs. However, the war may have dragged on for months more if nuclear bombs were not used.\n\nMillions of Americans lost their job in the Great Depression of 1929 (photo credit Delhi Mind Clinic)\n\nFears and predictions that a the Russia-Ukraine conflict would escalate into a world war with the use of nuclear weapons have so far been unfounded, though both the US and key NATO allies such as Germany and Sweden provided arms including tanks, missiles and aircraft to Ukraine and continue their military aid.\n\nThis war is now stale news, though it\u2019s still being waged ferociously. Casualty figures have increased significantly over the past year, and Russian casualties alone since the war began in 2022 now amount to 1.6 million according to estimates."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "date_str": "2026-11-14",
    "description": "Another Depression, crash, and world war?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Another-Depression-crash-and-world-war/131-354919",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794644209.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "947911878c432761544c9c9fe8862f20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "This war is now stale news, though it\u2019s still being waged ferociously. Casualty figures have increased significantly over the past year, and Russian casualties alone since the war began in 2022 now amount to 1.6 million according to estimates.\n\nFor this to happen, the US economy has to crash. This is what happened in 1929. As of October 2026, the direct military cost of the U.S. war with Iran has reached US$47.1 billion according to AP News and Pentagon figures reported via The New York Times.\n\nDirect military expenditures are estimated at approximately $47.1 billion as of mid-to-late October 2026, growing at a rate of roughly $2 billion to $3 billion per month. If that $2-3 billion is added to Sri Lanka\u2019s economy, (which has a nominal GDP of roughly $110.68 billion), that would go a long way to stabilise it. The International Monetary Fund (IMF) Extended Fund Facility (EFF) amounts to just $2.9 billion, and the entire population is paying dearly for this \u2018largesse.\u2019\n\nThe Soviet Union collapsed due to excessive military spending coupled with a consumer economy too small to sustain it. At the time of its collapse in 1991, by various estimates the Soviet economy was roughly the second-largest in the world after the United States, with a GNP that hovered around 50% to 52% of the U.S. level in the 1980s,\u00a0 with per capita GNP at roughly 35% to 38% of the U.S. level.\n\nBut these figures don\u2019t tell the whole story. While American-made consumer goods had large export markets, the Soviet consumer products were largely for the domestic market and Eastern Europe (China, learning from this, did not repeat that mistake when it liberalised its economy).\n\nBetween 1989 and 1992, Soviet GDP per capita fell by approximately 40% as the command system fractured.\u00a0 By 1991, the consolidated budget deficit of the USSR and the Russian Federation soared to about 31% of GDP, and the country dissolved with an external debt of roughly $66 billion alongside minimal foreign exchange and gold reserves."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3093f7df",
    "date_str": "2026-11-14",
    "description": "Another Depression, crash, and world war?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Another-Depression-crash-and-world-war/131-354919",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794644209.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-14-dailymirrorlk-3093f7df-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2e723e9227a110c3bcfe1358619be9f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Between 1989 and 1992, Soviet GDP per capita fell by approximately 40% as the command system fractured.\u00a0 By 1991, the consolidated budget deficit of the USSR and the Russian Federation soared to about 31% of GDP, and the country dissolved with an external debt of roughly $66 billion alongside minimal foreign exchange and gold reserves.\n\nDespite its current, massive military spending, the US economy is in better shape. But it has a huge budget deficit.\u00a0 For fiscal year ending\u00a0 September 30, 2026, the U.S. federal budget deficit totalled $1.99 trillion (widely rounded to $2.0 trillion), representing 6.2% of GDP.\n\nDoes this mean another US economic crash along the lines of 1929? Prophesy is a dangerous thing. All we can do is to examine why it happened, in peace time, eleven years after World War I ended. One doesn\u2019t need to be an economist to figure out that almost all major financial crises of the 20th and 21st centuries took place when no powerful nations were at war with each other.\n\nThroughout the 1920s till the crash, the US economy prospered. Under three Republican presidents \u2013 Harding, Calvin Coolidge, and Herbert C. Hoover, the economy boomed, though with widening inequalities. Then in October 1929 came the Wall Street Crash, resulting in the Great Depression which affected not just the US but the rest of the world, too.\n\nIn November 1932, Democrat Franklin D. Roosevelt was elected president, and he worked energetically to stabilise the situation. While the Republican presidents had allowed private enterprise a free hand during the boom years, curbing government regulation, Roosevelt brought back government supervision.\n\nWhat is notable is that World War I paved the way to a great expansion of American industry and the US was able to overtake her industrial rivals Germany and Britain. US consumer industries grew rapidly. In 1918, there were seven million cars in the US. By 1929, there were 24 million."
  },
  {
