    "language": "en",
    "md5": "082bcedf2579dbef9e52494552bf97df",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 915,
    "chunk_text": "What is notable is that World War I paved the way to a great expansion of American industry and the US was able to overtake her industrial rivals Germany and Britain. US consumer industries grew rapidly. In 1918, there were seven million cars in the US. By 1929, there were 24 million.\n\nNone of this would directly cause an economic crash. High oil prices cause domestic dissatisfaction and hardships in the US, but\u00a0 not even the massive oil price hike of 1973, when oil prices quadrupled,\u00a0 did not lead to a worldwide economic crash. It caused a sharp global recession rather than a permanent worldwide depression, accompanied by a rare economic phenomenon known as stagflation (high inflation combined with high unemployment and sluggish growth).\n\nSo, it may be that we could be spared the worst, from worldwide economic depression to a devastating third world war. But, as noted earlier, prophesy is dangerous."
