    "description": "Rs. 85 borrowed for every Rs. 100 spent on development: Namal",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Rs-85-borrowed-for-every-Rs-100-spent-on-development-Namal/108-354933",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794721098.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-15-dailymirrorlk-3b7153e4-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4c343655861eba3d7b1084abff13f097",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "Rs. 85 borrowed for every Rs. 100 spent on development: Namal\n\nColombo, Nov. 15 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka borrows Rs. 85 for every Rs. 100 spent on development, while the Government has failed to strengthen the economy despite the funds available to it, Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) National Organiser and MP Namal Rajapaksa said.\n\nAddressing Parliament today during the Budget debate, he challenged the Government's claims of financial management, saying that much of the money spent on development projects, including roads, highways, bridges and schools, came from borrowing.\n\n\"For every Rs. 100 you boast about spending on development, you borrow Rs. 85. Another Rs. 3 comes as foreign aid. The balance is met through State revenue,\" he said.\n\nRajapaksa also criticised the Government's economic performance, claiming that economic growth had declined from 5.3% when it assumed office to 4.2% under its third Budget.\n\nHe said inflation, which had been negative when the Government took office, had risen to 8.3%, while the prices of essential commodities had increased by at least 10%. He also pointed out that the US dollar had risen from Rs. 295 to Rs. 332.\n\nWhile acknowledging global challenges, including ongoing wars, he said the Government remained responsible for managing the country's economy.\n\nHe accused the Government of following a tax policy that benefited the wealthy while placing a greater burden on poorer sections of society.\n\nAlthough the Government had removed VAT from school textbooks, he questioned why similar relief had not been extended to essential food items, milk powder and medicines.\n\nHe also questioned the decision to provide free smart meters to consumers using more than 180 electricity units, saying the Government should instead focus on reducing electricity costs for industries, entrepreneurs and low-income households."
  },
