    "language": "en",
    "md5": "26ba26d8894acfa7ced9b139c76509ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1267,
    "chunk_text": "He also questioned the decision to provide free smart meters to consumers using more than 180 electricity units, saying the Government should instead focus on reducing electricity costs for industries, entrepreneurs and low-income households.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka needed to improve its investment climate and strengthen existing institutions instead of establishing new committees.\n\nReferring to foreign investment during the 2005\u20132015 period, he said Sri Lanka had attracted a large number of investors under the existing system.\n\nHowever, he alleged that the current Government treated investors with suspicion and viewed officials who expedited investment approvals as corrupt.\n\n\"Do not look at every investor with suspicion. You consider every investor a thief and make the official who helped that investor a thief. As a result, officials will not be able to work,\" he said.\n\nRajapaksa also referred to President Anura Kumara Dissanayake's earlier criticism of religious observances, saying he was pleased that the President had now referred to Buddhist teachings, including the Kalama Sutta, during the Budget speech.\n\nHe said the President should apply the principle of examining issues objectively when addressing the country's economic and social challenges."
