    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "SpaceX Starship returns to landing pad from orbit, acing test for reusable rockets\n\nReuters\u00a0- SpaceX's (SPCX.O) Starship spacecraft on Saturday returned from a 22-hour voyage in orbit and descended for the first time into the \u200bgrasp of giant mechanical arms at the Texas tower it was launched from a day earlier, a catch that is essential to the company's \u200cdecade-long effort to develop a fully reusable rocket.\n\nThe 171-foot-tall (52-meter) Starship fell out of thick clouds over SpaceX's Starbase launch site, reignited engines to flip itself vertical and slowly descended into the tower's \"Mechazilla\" arms, engines still roaring. Charred from the blazing heat of its return from space, the ship shut down its engines and hung still after the rocket system's longest mission yet.\n\n\"That was the missing link, the holy grail of \u200bspaceflight,\" SpaceX mission commentator Dan Huot said of the ship catch.\n\nThe towering two-stage rocket's Super Heavy booster \u2014 the rocket's bottom half \u2014 carried Starship to space on Friday, \u200blifting off from SpaceX's Starbase, Texas, facilities. That booster had failed to make a similar return to the launch pad some 10 minutes \u2060after liftoff, nixing SpaceX's plan to recover both parts of the rocket for the first time.\n\nBut the Starship upper stage's successful return after orbiting Earth 14 times for 22.5 hours marked \u200ba key engineering milestone in the whirlwind, multibillion-dollar development of Starship, which billionaire Elon Musk sees as the future workhorse for SpaceX's lucrative Starlink satellite business, as well as ambitions to \u200bput data centers in orbit, return humans to the moon and, eventually, settle on Mars.\n\nAfter deploying 26 Starlink satellites in orbit Friday night while cruising some 161 miles (260 km) in altitude, Starship on Saturday fired one of its engines to begin an atmospheric plunge while flying eastward over the Pacific Ocean."
