    "chunk_text": "After deploying 26 Starlink satellites in orbit Friday night while cruising some 161 miles (260 km) in altitude, Starship on Saturday fired one of its engines to begin an atmospheric plunge while flying eastward over the Pacific Ocean.\n\nWith its aerodynamic fins folded back, two small fittings near the top of Starship hooked onto the \u200blaunch tower's mechanical arms that had closed in to grasp it, allowing the vessel to hang a few hundred feet off the ground.\n\nMusk and SpaceX engineers began inspecting the ship within hours \u200cof its \u2060catch.\n\n\"Ship 42 looks great!\" Musk wrote on X, posting photos and videos from the launch tower.\n\nSpaceX's dominance in the global launch industry is centered on its workhorse Falcon 9 rocket, which has launched over 700 times since 2010. That rocket's first stage is reusable, but its second stage - the part that delivers payloads into orbit - is discarded into the ocean after each flight.\n\nWith Starship, Musk wants a rocket system that can fly to space and back in a manner and tempo akin to commercial airplanes, with \u200bminimal refurbishment between flights.\n\nThough Saturday's Starship recovery \u200bis a key milestone in that effort, \u2060SpaceX has yet to recover both Starship stages in a single mission, which would mark the first-ever return of an orbital launch system.\n\nAnd Starship's heat shield, composed of hundreds of black hexagonal tiles that protect it from the blazing heat of atmospheric reentry, is itself not yet fully reusable.\n\nSpaceX \u200bis trying to engineer new tile designs that will require little to no refurbishment after each mission.\n\nNASA's Space Shuttle system that \u200bferried astronauts to the \u2060International Space Station before its 2011 retirement was partially reusable; its external fuel tanks that got it off Earth were discarded after each flight. The reusable spacecraft itself had to go through extensive refurbishment before re-flying."
