    "chunk_size_bytes": 1657,
    "chunk_text": "Top scorer on South Korean tax exam confesses to cheating with AI\n\nReuters\u00a0- The marks attained by the top scorer on South Korea's national exam for aspiring \u200btax accountants this year seemed too good to be \u200ctrue and on Sunday, sceptics were proven right when he confessed to using generative AI to cheat.\n\nThe scandal follows months of speculation after \u200bhe scored near-perfect marks on hundreds of questions over \u200btwo exams taken in April and July. It has \u2060also raised the stakes for organisers of South Korea's high-stakes \u200bannual college entrance exam this Thursday.\n\n\"I cheated using electronic devices \u200band generative AI. My passing the exam with the top score was not the result of fair effort,\" he said, identifying himself by the \u200bsurname Han, as he apologised on the YouTube channel 'Attorney Lee Don-ho'.\n\nHan \u200bsaid he cheated by taking photos of the exam questions with a microcamera, \u200csending \u2060them to an AI tool, and checking the answers on a smartphone.\n\nSpeaking on camera wearing a face mask, Han added that he had submitted a written confession to Seoul police.\n\nA Reuters \u200bcall to the \u200bSeoul Jungbu \u2060police station went unanswered on Sunday.\n\nThis week, Prime Minister Han Seok-sook acknowledged public concerns over AI-assisted \u200bcheating ahead of the national college entrance exam, \u200ba \u2060high-pressure event that many South Korean students have spent much of their lives preparing for.\n\nThe government \"will prepare thoroughly in advance and \u2060closely oversee \u200bthe process until the very end \u200bto ensure the college entrance exam is conducted fairly and stably,\" a statement \u200bfrom her office said."
  },
