    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "UK ethics chief accused of groping former police colleague\u2019s breasts\n\nINDEPENDENT - The chief of the government\u2019s ethics watchdog left a previous role in the police after being investigated over sexual misconduct accusations including allegedly grabbing a colleague\u2019s breasts, according to a report.\n\nThe Sunday Times reported that Stuart Sterling has stepped back from his role as chief executive of the Ethics and Integrity Commission (EIC), a body that \u201cpromotes the highest standards in public life\u201d, and deals with the prime minister and public authorities on matters concerning ethical standards.\n\nThe 48-year-old will now face pending a civil service inquiry. A Cabinet Office spokesman said: \u201cThese are serious allegations. Stuart Sterling has stepped back from his role whilst we swiftly establish the facts.\u201d\n\nMr Sterling was suspended from his detective constable role in Northamptonshire Police in 2003 after the groping incident, the outlet reported. He was reportedly permitted to return to his duties after he admitted wrongdoing and considerations regarding whether to initiate criminal proceedings were dropped.\n\nYears later, he left the force after being investigated over another allegation of sexual misconduct, which he denies. Sterling says he \u201cleft voluntarily\u201d - but did not confirm whether he was under investigation at the time.\n\nThe Independent understands that the investigation into Sterling\u2019s role at the EIC will assess whether the correct and appropriate information was given before his appointment.\n\nIn 2002, sources told the newspaper that a 24-year-old Sterling had walked up to a female colleague in her mid-thirties from behind after she took off her stabproof vest, and placed two hands on her breasts."
  },
