    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "In 2002, sources told the newspaper that a 24-year-old Sterling had walked up to a female colleague in her mid-thirties from behind after she took off her stabproof vest, and placed two hands on her breasts.\n\nAfter his return to work Sterling was moved to a different police stations, going on to be promoted to detective sergeant and receiving two commendations.\n\nIn 2006, another incident allegedly took place in a social setting following a police shift, but Mr Sterling has denied it ever occurred.\n\nHe was investigated again as the Northamptonshire Police\u2019s anti-corruption unit began probing the previous investigation that allowed him to stay in the force half a decade earlier, the outlet reported.\n\nSterling left the police before becoming a civil servant under the Cabinet Office in December 2009.\n\nHe has since served in multiple Cabinet Office, Home Office and Foreign Office roles, was seconded to the National Police Chiefs\u2019 Council, and received an OBE in 2023 for his role in Queen Elizabeth II\u2019s funeral in 2022.\n\nA representative of Sterling told The Times: \u201cStuart states that a full disciplinary process was held regarding the complaint in 2002, he complied with this fully. It resulted in a sanction.\n\n\u201cHe denies that the alleged incident in 2008 ever took place. He was never subject to any criminal investigation, but the allegation was examined by professional standards.\n\n\u201cHe was not required to resign from the force. He left voluntarily. He is not aware of any anti-corruption investigation ever taking place.\u201d\n\nA Northamptonshire police spokesman told The Independent: \u201cWe have a brief digital record confirming a misconduct process in 2002\u201d but that much of the added information would have been \u201cpaper-based\u201d."
