    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c380add6dcf911f089d818e7bfe694fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "Philippines wins first Eurovision Song Contest Asia\n\nBBC - The Philippines have won the very first Eurovision Song Contest Asia, hosted in Bangkok and with 11 countries vying for the title.\n\nNineteen-year-old singer Carmelle was always a front runner with her women's empowerment anthem Sobra-Sobra, complete with its catchy rap section.\n\nShe earned 211 points in total - 93 from the jury and 118 from the public vote - ahead of Thailand's MANG x DAOU in second place with 170 points.\n\nThere was a joyous atmosphere at the One Bangkok Arena as the crowd cheered the contestants and celebrated the popular winner.\n\nCarmelle, the youngest contestant in the competition, was an obvious favourite with the juries and the public despite strong showings from several nations.\n\nOther notable acts included MANG x DAOU, whose emotive duet Aroma elicited huge cheers from the home crowd.\n\nBangladeshi beatboxers Beatmosphere eschewed the traditional backing track, instead using their voices to replicate the sounds of drums and basslines.\n\nAnd Laos's Thinla performed her crowd-pleasing song Bae in a traditional Lao hilltribe outfit.\n\nHowever, the Laotian song had a unintentional second outing after the music video was played over an apparent technical issue during the broadcast - meaning it was the last song the audience heard before the vote.\n\nThe voting system worked much the same as the European Eurovison, with the public vote accounting for 50% of the result and the other 50% coming from the 11 national juries - with additional points awarded from a rest of the world vote.\n\nAlso performing during the broadcast was 2026 Eurovision Song Contest winner Dara from Bulgaria, who performed her winning song Bangaranga and new song Bad Idea.\n\nThai superstar Jeff Satur also made an appearance, hitting the high notes in his performance of Black Tie.\n\nEurovision in Asia was an idea that has been talked about for decades, but one that never seemed to materialise - despite many false starts - until now."
  },
