    "chunk_text": "The newly opened Professorial Ward Complex is equipped with modern healthcare facilities and has a total capacity of 300 beds. These beds are allocated for medical, surgical, psychiatric and specialized gynaecological wards. The complex also features an eight-bed Intensive Care Unit (ICU), three fully equipped operating theatres, eight outpatient clinic rooms and a dedicated clinical examination hall.\n\nIn addition to government funding through annual budget allocations, the project received valuable support from the Ruhuna University Medical Faculty Alumni Association (RUMSAA) and several philanthropists.\n\nWith the sponsorship of the Mahabodhi TempleTemple in Guangzhou, China, and coordination facilitated by the Consular Office, essential hospital beds were donated for the wards, operating theatres and ICU. Meanwhile, groups of medical faculty alumni contributed furniture for the entrance lobby, outpatient clinics, examination hall and medical wards, with the total value of these donations exceeding Rs. 25 million.\n\nFurthermore, an agreement has been reached through Lions Clubs International to provide a donation of USD 150,000 to fully equip one of the operating theatres.\n\nDuring his address, Minister Jayatissa also provided an update on developments in healthcare sector recruitment. He stated that the cadre review of the health sector, which had not been carried out since 2017, has now been completed. Approval is expected to be obtained to recruit approximately 7,000 doctors over the next three years.\n\nThe Minister further explained that appointments had initially been planned for 1,472 doctors who had completed their internship training. However, once the ongoing legal proceedings are concluded, the government intends to expedite appointments for nearly 2,000 individuals, including these doctors.\n\nAdditionally, arrangements have been made to recruit 3,000 nursing officers and 500 graduates as part of efforts to strengthen the country's healthcare workforce."
