    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s energy policy  and the democratic dilemma\n\nBy protecting citizen-led rooftop solar\u00a0 and investing in storage and grid modernisation, not only can the country reduce its dependence on imported fossil fuels and move toward energy security but also drive the much-needed equal wealth distribution that the Government touts is the foundation of prosperity. Not only the elected officials, but we, the public, also have a responsibility to ensure that administrative institutions implement democratic policy for the good of the country rather than obstructing it with ulterior motives\n\nBy Prof. Emeritus I. M. Dharmadasa, Brig. Ranjan de Silva, Eng. Vidhura Ralapanawe,\u00a0and Dr. Geewananda Gunawardane\n\nDemocratic governments depend on a professional civil service to turn laws and policy goals into sustained public action. Because bureaucrats exercise substantial influence without being directly accountable to voters, tension can arise when administrative decisions diverge from elected priorities or the public interest, a problem often described as the \u201cdemocratic dilemma\u201d. Sri Lanka\u2019s electricity sector provides a consequential example, particularly in the treatment of renewable energy and citizen-funded rooftop solar.\n\nDrawing on four decades of experience in renewable-energy research and commercialisation, we offer these observations to the President and the public in the hope that Sri Lanka can resolve the democratic dilemma in favor of economic and social progress. A capable bureaucracy is essential to maintaining beneficial policies through frequent changes in political leadership. Sri Lanka has a clear path to energy independence, the foundation of the prosperity this Government promised, yet bureaucratic obstruction continues to slow progress. This article examines whether that failure arises from gaps in knowledge, institutional capacity, or accountability, and what must change to protect the public interest."
