    "md5": "747a82a05dee360ec14d526ca74084ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Drawing on four decades of experience in renewable-energy research and commercialisation, we offer these observations to the President and the public in the hope that Sri Lanka can resolve the democratic dilemma in favor of economic and social progress. A capable bureaucracy is essential to maintaining beneficial policies through frequent changes in political leadership. Sri Lanka has a clear path to energy independence, the foundation of the prosperity this Government promised, yet bureaucratic obstruction continues to slow progress. This article examines whether that failure arises from gaps in knowledge, institutional capacity, or accountability, and what must change to protect the public interest.\n\nDuring the program\u2019s six years, the British Council, the British High Commission in Colombo, and the Ministry of Science and Technology organised high-profile conferences. IMD also promoted renewable energy directly with nearly every science and technology minister from Bernard Soysa onward, as well as two former presidents. From 2002 to 2004, while Milinda Moragoda served as Minister of Economic Reform, Science and Technology, IMD joined an expatriate scientist exchange program to advance solar-energy research and development. The collaboration involved the ministry, the Ceylon Institute of Scientific and Industrial Research (now ITI), the National Engineering Research and Development (NERD) Centre, and Sri Lankan universities.\n\nInstitutional resistance to solar-energy development emerged during these high-level discussions. At one conference, the consortium invited the Ceylon Electricity Board (CEB) to present its national energy plans. Senior policymaker Shavi Fernando delivered a PowerPoint presentation whose concluding slide, projecting rapidly increasing demand, stated: \u201cThe future of Sri Lanka is coal, coal, and coal.\u201d"
