    "chunk_text": "Institutional resistance to solar-energy development emerged during these high-level discussions. At one conference, the consortium invited the Ceylon Electricity Board (CEB) to present its national energy plans. Senior policymaker Shavi Fernando delivered a PowerPoint presentation whose concluding slide, projecting rapidly increasing demand, stated: \u201cThe future of Sri Lanka is coal, coal, and coal.\u201d\n\nDespite successive governments\u2019 limited promotion of solar energy, the introduction of Soorya Bala Sangramaya in 2016 marked an important advance. Its net-metering, net-accounting, net-plus, and net-plus-plus schemes enabled entrepreneurs and households to expand rooftop generation. According to the Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka\u2019s (PUCSL) December 2025 report, the country had 129,300 to 150,000 rooftop solar connections, representing about 1940 MW to 2,090 MW of installed capacity. Nearly 1,000 solar photovoltaic companies now support about 40,000 skilled jobs. Notably, this expansion was financed by private citizens rather than the Government.\n\nWe welcome the current Government\u2019s support for battery installation, an approach the CEB neglected for three decades. Batteries can store surplus daytime solar generation for nighttime use, reducing reliance on exceptionally costly diesel power. The Cabinet\u2019s approval of a $ 35 million ADB financing package for a nationwide rooftop-solar project is also encouraging. These initiatives should be aligned with policies that preserve, rather than restrict, citizen participation in rooftop generation.\n\nThe 2025 PUCSL report identifies practical measures for integrating more rooftop solar, including formal connection standards, improved monitoring, advanced inverter functions, and battery storage. The following excerpt shows that the regulator treats higher distributed generation as a challenge to be managed through technical rules and grid visibility, rather than as a