    "chunk_text": "The 2025 PUCSL report identifies practical measures for integrating more rooftop solar, including formal connection standards, improved monitoring, advanced inverter functions, and battery storage. The following excerpt shows that the regulator treats higher distributed generation as a challenge to be managed through technical rules and grid visibility, rather than as a reason to halt rooftop-solar expansion:\n\nFurthermore, as distributed generation continues to expand, improving real-time visibility and controllability of rooftop PV systems will be essential for national system stability. The integration of battery storage, advanced inverter functions, and monitoring systems will support this transition by enabling better management of mid-day surplus generation and reducing the operational burden on the national grid.\u201d\n\nMany CEB engineers support renewable energy, yet senior decision makers appear to discount their views while continuing to favor imported, polluting fossil fuels. Without transparent, evidence-based explanations, this pattern suggests that longstanding bureaucratic resistance persists despite restructuring and personnel changes, the \u201cdemocratic dilemma\u201d in practice. Authorities commonly argue that accommodating more rooftop solar would require a $ 4-5 billion grid upgrade taking 7\u201312 years or longer. Because the basis and alternatives behind that estimate have not been publicly explained here, it should be independently examined rather than treated as conclusive."
