    "md5": "9cc78a052011a9db2d4b8aba43f16b36",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "Athukorala\u2019s Asian global production experiences: Lessons for Sri Lanka\n\nDrawing on rich historical evidence, Athukorala shows that the island has missed many opportunities, apart from its resilient garment and apparel industry which Athukorala says has benefitted from the transfer of technology and management skills\n\nProfessor Prema-chandra Athukorala, the Sri Lankan-born economist honoured by the Australian Government as a Member of the Order of Australia (AM) in 2026, has brought together his lifelong research on global production sharing, drawing on the experiences of some of Asia\u2019s leading economies.\n\nIn this new book, he devotes two important chapters to his country of birth, Sri Lanka. He recently retired as Professor of Economics at the Australian National University, which he fondly calls his \"intellectual home\", and the university has named him Professor Emeritus. Yet an academic of his standing rarely leaves intellectual life behind. From our past conversations, I know that he remains actively involved in research and doctoral supervision. When recently asked to write a foreword for a publication, he could not do so immediately because he was busy editing a student\u2019s doctoral thesis.\n\nAthukorala continues to make Sri Lanka proud and stands alongside the island\u2019s internationally renowned economists, such as Dr. Gamani Corea and Dr. Lal Jayawardena. His contribution to the field is clear: Google Scholar records well over 14,000 citations of his work, an achievement matched by very few contemporary economists.\n\nAthukorala\u2019s latest book, Global Production Sharing, Industrialisation and Trade Patterns: Lessons and Insights from Asia, is published by World Scientific Publishing in Singapore. It contains 14 chapters, and the author acknowledges several co-authors who helped prepare the study."
  },
