    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2535af180d12371f989d504f5f231511",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "Athukorala\u2019s latest book, Global Production Sharing, Industrialisation and Trade Patterns: Lessons and Insights from Asia, is published by World Scientific Publishing in Singapore. It contains 14 chapters, and the author acknowledges several co-authors who helped prepare the study.\n\nAthukorala focuses more specifically on the Global Manufacturing Value Chain (GMVC), as the book examines the physical manufacturing and assembly stages within these global networks. This focus makes the subject easier for ordinary readers to understand. It is also important because manufacturing has helped developing nations create industrial jobs, build wealth, and achieve prosperity. However, with the rise of artificial intelligence and advanced technology, a global services chain is also emerging. We may hope that, in his future work, Athukorala broadens his focus to a \"global value chain plus\" framework to cover these new digital developments.\n\nFinding a place in the Global Manufacturing Value Chain (GMVC) is a complex task for any nation. It requires firm commitment from top policymakers, consistent long-term policies, quick action to solve problems, continued public support, and the ability to withstand external shocks caused by unpredictable global leaders. Success ultimately depends on the vision and leadership of the country\u2019s top politicians. To illustrate this challenging journey, Athukorala introduces the \"Smiling Curve\", a concept originally proposed by Stan Shih, the founder of Taiwan\u2019s Acer Corporation.\n\nThe Smiling Curve is a simple visual tool shaped like a smile. Economists use it to show how much value (or economic profit) is added at each stage of making a product. For developing nations, the discouraging feature is that value addition falls to its lowest level in the middle stage before rising again to complete the smile."
  },
