    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d5fec8270a2639e192b2f9a196744496",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1626,
    "chunk_text": "The Smiling Curve is a simple visual tool shaped like a smile. Economists use it to show how much value (or economic profit) is added at each stage of making a product. For developing nations, the discouraging feature is that value addition falls to its lowest level in the middle stage before rising again to complete the smile.\n\nHowever, the curve rises again as a country masters the stages on the right-hand side: distribution, marketing, final sales, and after-sales services. Every country must work its way along the curve to reach these more profitable stages. As Athukorala stresses, there is no shortcut to success. Nations must work hard and remain committed to climbing from the lowest point to the peak. Among the Asian nations reviewed in the book, Singapore, China, Malaysia, and Thailand stand out as clear winners, while newer entrants such as Indonesia, India, the Philippines, and Vietnam are moving rapidly towards success.\n\nBut what about Sri Lanka? Drawing on rich historical evidence, Athukorala shows that the island has missed many opportunities, apart from its resilient garment and apparel industry which Athukorala says has benefitted from the transfer of technology and management skills.\n\nAlthough the overall story is disappointing, it also offers hope to policymakers seeking a fresh start. To use the door metaphor, a newcomer with a fresh approach may find it easier to open a completely closed door than one already jammed halfway. In my view, this is the starting point for Sri Lanka\u2019s current administration which is committed to delivering a rich country and a beautiful life to people."
  },
