    "md5": "795dbc77392947b7b7c77a32bdb07f7a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "Although the overall story is disappointing, it also offers hope to policymakers seeking a fresh start. To use the door metaphor, a newcomer with a fresh approach may find it easier to open a completely closed door than one already jammed halfway. In my view, this is the starting point for Sri Lanka\u2019s current administration which is committed to delivering a rich country and a beautiful life to people.\n\nIn his analysis of China, a clear winner in global production sharing, Athukorala explains the country\u2019s rapid rise as a leader in global production networks and its implications for ongoing US-China trade tensions. China is now a major global exporter whose importance cannot be ignored, even by its critics.\n\nThe punitive tariffs imposed by US administration did not emerge overnight. In the early years, US policy favoured accommodating China as a development partner. As Athukorala notes, both major political parties in Washington agreed that integrating China into the global economy brought net gains to the US through lower consumer prices, more competitive companies, and greater opportunities for multilateral production\u2014another term for global production networking. Although the influx of cheap Chinese exports created costs at home, American leaders believed these were temporary difficulties that would fade once the economy had adjusted.\n\nHowever, this favourable view of bilateral trade broke down during the 2016 US presidential election. Mainstream economists such as Jagdish Bhagwati and Alan Blinder argued that American job losses were mainly caused by domestic automation and rapid productivity growth. Populist politicians, however, labelled the problem the \"China Shock\"."
