    "chunk_size_bytes": 1848,
    "chunk_text": "However, this favourable view of bilateral trade broke down during the 2016 US presidential election. Mainstream economists such as Jagdish Bhagwati and Alan Blinder argued that American job losses were mainly caused by domestic automation and rapid productivity growth. Populist politicians, however, labelled the problem the \"China Shock\".\n\nAthukorala wrote this chapter just before the Trump camp suffered a heavy defeat in the US midterm elections in November 2026. Although the wider world hopes this political shift may help end the rivalry, an early settlement of the tariff war remains unlikely, given America\u2019s longstanding commitment to putting \"America First\". Nevertheless, Athukorala closes the chapter on a reassuring note. He observes that other countries in global production networks specialise in distinct components, making them essential partners and helping to prevent the global trading system from collapsing.\n\nIn his case studies of Asian countries, Athukorala follows a clear structure: reviewing their history, assessing their current position, and offering concluding remarks. For ordinary readers, these conclusions bring together the book\u2019s main lessons. Although the countries have taken different paths, their experiences offer a useful guide to development strategies.\n\nSingapore stands out as a leading success story of the GMVC model. Its national development strategy centres on multinational enterprises (MNEs)\u2014large companies that operate in several countries. Singapore which hosted these companies on its land has benefited greatly as these enterprises expanded their manufacturing operations into newly opened economies in Eastern Europe and Central Asia that had previously relied on central planning. These gains are particularly visible in the information and communications technology (ICT) sector."
