    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Singapore stands out as a leading success story of the GMVC model. Its national development strategy centres on multinational enterprises (MNEs)\u2014large companies that operate in several countries. Singapore which hosted these companies on its land has benefited greatly as these enterprises expanded their manufacturing operations into newly opened economies in Eastern Europe and Central Asia that had previously relied on central planning. These gains are particularly visible in the information and communications technology (ICT) sector.\n\nThe Philippines has made limited progress despite joining global production networks early. Malaysia, by contrast, has been highly successful, with the Penang Export Hub playing a central role in its GMVC strategy. However, Athukorala warns that Malaysia must do more to sustain this success. It needs institutional reforms, targeted investment promotion, quick solutions to investors\u2019 problems, world-class infrastructure, focused vocational training to develop a skilled workforce, and stronger links between multinational enterprises and local firms. Thailand has carved out a niche in the automotive industry, placing it in a strong position to enter the electric vehicle (EV) sector, which offers considerable potential for global growth.\n\nAmong the newer entrants, Vietnam has worked hard to join GMVCs by promoting foreign direct investment (FDI)\u2014including investment by foreign companies in local factories\u2014and strong export growth. These efforts have made Vietnam an important participant in global manufacturing. India, though a late entrant, is also making steady progress. It has developed growing automotive and electronics export sectors while moving away from the import substitution policies it followed for decades\u2014the strategy of producing goods locally while restricting imports."
  },
