    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Among the newer entrants, Vietnam has worked hard to join GMVCs by promoting foreign direct investment (FDI)\u2014including investment by foreign companies in local factories\u2014and strong export growth. These efforts have made Vietnam an important participant in global manufacturing. India, though a late entrant, is also making steady progress. It has developed growing automotive and electronics export sectors while moving away from the import substitution policies it followed for decades\u2014the strategy of producing goods locally while restricting imports.\n\nAthukorala\u2019s two chapters on Sri Lanka are among the book\u2019s most revealing and important sections for local readers. The country sought to industrialise as a way to overcome slow growth and severe unemployment. With the major economic shift in 1977, Sri Lanka began looking outward to global markets rather than inward. Yet inconsistent policies since then have held back a lasting transformation of its industrial structure.\n\nSri Lanka can fairly be described as a country of missed opportunities. It has repeatedly allowed promising global openings to slip away, with little apparent concern among its political leaders. Athukorala presents extensive evidence to support this disappointing account. He also notes that Sri Lanka is not alone: many developing nations have fallen into similar policy traps in the years since World War II.\n\nSumming up his analysis of Sri Lanka, Athukorala offers a clear warning: \"In sum, the analysis of regime shifts and economic performance under State-led import substitution strategy and liberalisation reforms in this paper makes a strong case for averting backsliding in policy, continuing the market-oriented reforms agenda that was left incomplete in the late 1990s, and setting up institutional safeguards to avert further policy backsliding.\""
