    "chunk_text": "Summing up his analysis of Sri Lanka, Athukorala offers a clear warning: \"In sum, the analysis of regime shifts and economic performance under State-led import substitution strategy and liberalisation reforms in this paper makes a strong case for averting backsliding in policy, continuing the market-oriented reforms agenda that was left incomplete in the late 1990s, and setting up institutional safeguards to avert further policy backsliding.\"\n\nRather than producing a whole garment, the country could focus on particular parts of the global value chain. This would require specialising in components and activities in which Sri Lanka has competitive and comparative advantages. One area that comes to mind is garment design using advanced AI and design technologies. In my view, Sri Lankans\u2019 strong learning skills could help them master these technologies.\n\nThis is an important, non-partisan recommendation that Sri Lanka\u2019s current administration should follow in both letter and spirit. To open the door to lasting prosperity, the country\u2019s leaders must avoid short-sighted policy reversals, commit to long-term structural reforms, and establish legal and institutional safeguards to protect the economy from future political shifts.\n\nAthukorala offers a bold and clear analysis of the economic challenges facing these Asian countries. His book not only explains past failures but also points to the steps they need to take next. It is a timely and valuable guide for developing nations, especially Sri Lanka, seeking lasting prosperity for their people. Athukorala has laid out the roadmap; it is now up to Sri Lanka\u2019s policymakers to read it and act on its lessons"
