    "md5": "a4e6c6b9e1c566ae6f0745aba751de9f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: The proof  of the pudding is in the execution\n\nThe intent is progressive. But for three years running, Sri Lanka has budgeted far more for development than it has managed to spend. Is the problem the estimates, or the machinery that is meant to deliver them?\n\nThe 2027 Budget, presented on 12 November, reads well. Its stated aim is to strengthen the production economy, protect small and medium industrialists, make room for investment, connect technology to the economy, protect the environment and ensure social justice. It does this while keeping to the fiscal discipline the country has painfully rebuilt since 2022.\n\nI have no quarrel with the intent. It is progressive, and in several areas it is genuinely bold. But budgets are not judged by their speeches. They are judged by what is built, paid for and delivered by the following December. And on that measure, the record of the last two budgets should give us pause.\n\nPublic investment of Rs. 2,000 billion, or 5.1% of GDP, up from 4.1% in 2026, covering roads and rail, drinking water, irrigation, the power grid and housing.\n\nincome tax relief rises from Rs. 1.8 million to Rs. 1.95 million a year, the tax bands are revised, and Rs. 230 billion goes to social protection, including Aswesuma.\n\nSupport for enterprise: About Rs. 68 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing to support Rs. 174 billion of concessionary lending to micro, small and medium enterprises; a 200% capital allowance for large export-oriented manufacturing and tourism investments; and the abolition of the Tourism Development Levy.\n\nInstitutional reform: A draft Investment Protection Bill, a holding company for 34 State-owned\n\nenterprises, a Public-Private Partnership Act, a Digital Economy Bill and a review of labour laws.\n\nDiscipline: A primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP and an overall deficit held at 4.5% of GDP."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
