    "md5": "ccf0ae9bcc6a2d1673ddacd8663f1149",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1769,
    "chunk_text": "Institutional reform: A draft Investment Protection Bill, a holding company for 34 State-owned\n\nenterprises, a Public-Private Partnership Act, a Digital Economy Bill and a review of labour laws.\n\nDiscipline: A primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP and an overall deficit held at 4.5% of GDP.\n\nHere is the uncomfortable truth. For three consecutive years, the State has allocated far more for capital investment than it has spent.\n\n2024: Capital expenditure was originally estimated at Rs. 1,525 billion. The Treasury's own report shows Rs. 815 billion was actually spent, about half the original estimate.\n\n2025: according to the Asian Development Bank, capital spending reached 3.0% of GDP against a target of 4.0%, roughly $ 3.3 billion against an envelope of $ 4.4 billion. Over 2022 to 2025, capital spending averaged about 3% of GDP, against the 5% to 6% typically allocated.\n\n2026: Of a capital allocation of Rs. 1,380 billion, only 16.7% had been spent by mid-year, according to the World Bank. By the end of September, sources quoted by EconomyNext put the figure at around Rs. 385 billion, still less than 30%. Only 8% of the Rs. 500 billion budgeted for Cyclone Ditwah reconstruction had been disbursed by mid-year.\n\nAgainst this record, the 2027 Budget raises the capital allocation to Rs. 2,000 billion, 45% more than the 2026 allocation, and more than five times what had been spent in the first nine months of this year. The Government says most capital spending traditionally happens in the final quarter. That may be so. But that is precisely the problem: money rushed out of the door in November and December is rarely money well spent.\n\nThe question every reader should ask is why. I see two possible explanations, and the evidence suggests both are at work."
  },
