    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "Against this record, the 2027 Budget raises the capital allocation to Rs. 2,000 billion, 45% more than the 2026 allocation, and more than five times what had been spent in the first nine months of this year. The Government says most capital spending traditionally happens in the final quarter. That may be so. But that is precisely the problem: money rushed out of the door in November and December is rarely money well spent.\n\nThe question every reader should ask is why. I see two possible explanations, and the evidence suggests both are at work.\n\nThe second is that the line ministries lack the capacity to deliver. The World Bank is explicit on this point. It says the under-execution \u201creflects weaknesses in public investment management, including project selection, procurement, and implementation capacity,\u201d and not a shortage of finance. That matters. With revenue running ahead of target, money is no longer the binding constraint. The machinery is.\n\nThe Citizens Budget itself gives a telling picture of that machinery. The public service employs over 225,000 teachers and more than 100,000 development officers. But its cadre table lists just 1,138 officers in the Sri Lanka Engineers\u2019 Service, 802 in the Planning Service, 1,611 Sri Lanka Accountants and 55 architects. Other technical staff sit in agencies and state enterprises, but this core cadre is the one that plans, procures, supervises and pays for the roads, water schemes and canals the Budget promises. Rs. 2,000 billion of capital investment rests on a remarkably thin layer of technical capacity.\n\nAdd to this outdated procurement rules, land acquisition delays, weak coordination between agencies and the long-standing habit of starting more projects than can be finished, and the pattern becomes clear."
  },
  {
