    "md5": "91e9d6c769c04a6b1156c759f6a010f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Add to this outdated procurement rules, land acquisition delays, weak coordination between agencies and the long-standing habit of starting more projects than can be finished, and the pattern becomes clear.\n\nSome will argue that unspent money is money saved. It is not.\n\nIt is hidden austerity. When capital spending falls short, fiscal targets are met by default, not by design. The deficit looks better, but the investment the economy needed never happened.\n\nIt costs growth. Every rupee of public investment that is not spent is a road not built, a canal not repaired, a contractor not paid and a worker not hired.\n\nIt damages credibility. Investors, contractors and the capital market plan around announced projects. Equity analysts have already rated construction and building materials companies positively on the strength of the Rs. 2,000 billion programme. If it is not delivered, that confidence will not return easily.\n\nIt weakens the case for the next Budget. Development partners watch disbursement rates. When projects stall, so does the next tranche of concessional funding.\n\nA Budget has vision and strategy. Execution needs system, structure competency\n\nIn my work with organisations, I often use a simple framework. Vision is where we are going. Strategy is how we will get there. But neither delivers anything without a system, the processes, measures and incentives through which work actually gets done, and a structure that makes clear who owns what.\n\nBudget 2027 has a clear vision and, in most areas, sensible choices. What it does not yet have is a delivery system and a structure of accountability strong enough to carry Rs. 2,000 billion of investment. That is where the Government's attention must now turn."
  },
