    "md5": "894784d1f7472853ca56a5c203558a58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 has a clear vision and, in most areas, sensible choices. What it does not yet have is a delivery system and a structure of accountability strong enough to carry Rs. 2,000 billion of investment. That is where the Government's attention must now turn.\n\nThe usual comparisons between the two budgets mislead more than they inform. Set side by side in dollars, India\u2019s Budget dwarfs ours, and some have even claimed that Sri Lanka spends more per citizen. That ignores the fact that India\u2019s Union Budget covers only its Central Government, while its states run most health, education and policing from their own budgets. The fairer comparison is in ratios, and there the two countries look surprisingly alike. India\u2019s Central Government plans a fiscal deficit of 4.3% of GDP; ours is 4.5%. India spends 26% of its Budget on interest; we spend about a third. Interest absorbs about 40% of India\u2019s central revenue and about 43% of ours. Where the two countries part company is in what happens after the Budget is passed.\n\nIn 2024-25, India\u2019s Central Government spent about 95% of its original capital budget, against about 75% in Sri Lanka in 2025. In 2025-26, it spent about \u20b910.75 trillion, 98% of its revised target, as reported by Construction World citing a senior official. Sri Lanka, by contrast, had spent less than 30% of its 2026 capital allocation by the end of September.\n\nPart of the difference lies in a discipline India has built into its Budget process. Since 2017, every major central scheme has been published with an Output-Outcome Monitoring Framework: about 400 schemes and nearly 4,000 indicators, tracked on a public dashboard and tabled in Parliament with the Budget. This is accountability demonstrated. Under India\u2019s General Financial Rules, a scheme\u2019s performance against those targets helps decide whether it continues and how much it receives the following year. In other words, ministries are funded for outcomes, not just for intentions."
  },
