    "md5": "a2bb18532504ac593b318b485454d985",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Part of the difference lies in a discipline India has built into its Budget process. Since 2017, every major central scheme has been published with an Output-Outcome Monitoring Framework: about 400 schemes and nearly 4,000 indicators, tracked on a public dashboard and tabled in Parliament with the Budget. This is accountability demonstrated. Under India\u2019s General Financial Rules, a scheme\u2019s performance against those targets helps decide whether it continues and how much it receives the following year. In other words, ministries are funded for outcomes, not just for intentions.\n\n1. Fund only projects that are ready. No major project should enter the budget without a completed feasibility study, appraisal, land and approvals in place. Projects that are not ready should be funded for preparation, not construction.\n\n2. Budget for outcomes and publish execution every quarter. As India does with its Output-Outcome Monitoring Framework, each ministry\u2019s allocation should come with measurable outputs and outcomes. Parliament\u2019s Committee on Public Finance and the public should see allocation, spending and project milestones every quarter, and next year\u2019s allocation should depend on this year\u2019s delivery. What gets measured and published gets managed.\n\n3. Create a delivery unit with real authority. A small, highly skilled unit of professionals under the President, of the kind Malaysia used to drive its transformation program, should track the top 50 projects, unblock bottlenecks across ministries and report progress openly.\n\n4. Fix procurement. Expand e-procurement, update the Administrative and Financial Regulations, set fixed timelines for each procurement stage, and delegate authority to the level where decisions can be made quickly.\n\n5. Buy in the capacity we lack. Engage engineers, quantity surveyors and project managers on contract, use the new PPP framework, and draw on private-sector project management where the state lacks skills."
  },
