    "md5": "8e04c16497fafe8d2bfa667e12101277",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1422,
    "chunk_text": "5. Buy in the capacity we lack. Engage engineers, quantity surveyors and project managers on contract, use the new PPP framework, and draw on private-sector project management where the state lacks skills.\n\n7. Budget on a realistic baseline. Start each year's capital allocation from what was actually delivered the year before, plus a credible pipeline of ready projects. Ambition should be shown in delivery, not in the size of the allocation.\n\nThis Government came to office on a promise of a different kind of governance, and its fiscal management has earned genuine credit. Revenue is up, the deficit is under control, and the country is emerging from the IMF program in March 2027 in far better shape than it entered it.\n\nBut the next phase is harder. Stabilisation was largely about saying no: to waste, to new taxes on the wrong people, to unfunded promises. Growth is about saying yes, and then delivering. The President's aspirations can only be realised through ministries, departments and officials who have the competency, the systems and the confidence to act.\n\nThe pudding has been well described. By 31 December 2027, we will know whether it was cooked.\n\n(The author is a former Senior Banker, Regional Director of CIMA for the Middle East, South Asia and North Africa, Head Of PPP (2017) and has served on two national Education Reform Commissions. He is the Founder of Business Athletes and Teen Academy)"
