    "md5": "1751279b502bdf78e26e84cefd0fa2ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "Investing in Sri Lanka\u2019s resilience: The case for patient capital\n\nWhenever economies emerge from turbulent periods, recovery narratives are almost always framed through macroeconomic scorecards such as foreign exchange reserves, currency stabilisations, and sovereign credit trajectories. When Fitch Ratings upgraded Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating to 'B-' with a Stable Outlook was one encouraging sign. Tourist arrivals\u00a0 reaching 1.64 million visitors in late 2026 nearly offered another indication of recovery, although challenges remained.\n\nYet for international investors, recovery presents a deeper question of purpose. Some investors enter emerging markets when valuations are low, seeking quick returns, and preparing to leave at the first hint of volatility.\n\nThat approach fundamentally misunderstands how resilient economies are built. Sustainable recovery requires more than capital seeking a quick return. It requires patient capital, a willingness to invest across economic cycles, develop people\u2019s skills, and stay committed through difficult periods.\n\nEnduring commercial value is created through resilience, not retreat. When a host nation faces external shocks, the conventional corporate playbook advises retrenchment by halting capital expenditure, cutting payrolls, and mothballing operational assets until complete certainty returns.\n\nThe approach must be to continue investing through those difficult periods. For example, our hospitality arm Asia Leisure, chose to maintain and upgrade boutique properties such as Le Grand Galle, The Habitat Kosgoda, and Tamarind Hill through the most challenging seasons of recent years. Rather than scaling back, we focused on continuous property enhancements, service refinement, and operational readiness."
  },
