    "md5": "a1941aae05c17c0173e2a903c7d9bf98",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1527,
    "chunk_text": "The approach must be to continue investing through those difficult periods. For example, our hospitality arm Asia Leisure, chose to maintain and upgrade boutique properties such as Le Grand Galle, The Habitat Kosgoda, and Tamarind Hill through the most challenging seasons of recent years. Rather than scaling back, we focused on continuous property enhancements, service refinement, and operational readiness.\n\nContinuing to invest also demonstrates confidence in Sri Lanka\u2019s future and a commitment to an enduring partnership.\n\nNowhere is the compounding power of patient capital clearer than in hospitality. As Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya recently emphasised, achieving Sri Lanka's 2030 tourism ambitions requires more than physical infrastructure development. It demands a skilled workforce, quality employment generation, and the active empowerment of youth and women.\n\nFar from a purely recreational sector, tourism operates as an essential economic catalyst, directly driving national employment and ensuring that the benefits of growth reach regional communities.\n\nThere is an outdated assumption that commercial returns and community welfare exist in conflict, as if generating shareholder value requires minimising local expenditure. In truth, sustainable commercial success and local economic prosperity reinforce each other. A hotel\u2019s long-term success is closely tied to the wellbeing of the community around it. Its long-term viability depends on the vitality, skill, and goodwill of that community."
  },
