    "language": "en",
    "md5": "486bea6a94cf52238e65f7f90c80ec8b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "There is an outdated assumption that commercial returns and community welfare exist in conflict, as if generating shareholder value requires minimising local expenditure. In truth, sustainable commercial success and local economic prosperity reinforce each other. A hotel\u2019s long-term success is closely tied to the wellbeing of the community around it. Its long-term viability depends on the vitality, skill, and goodwill of that community.\n\nViewing Sri Lanka through the lens of Southeast Asia\u2019s economic journey reveals striking parallels. Over three decades, ASEAN economies navigated repeated financial crises, geopolitical shifts, and external shocks. What propelled the region into one of the world's most vibrant economic blocs was not the absence of shocks, but the resilience of private enterprise and the steady inflow of patient capital that looked past short-term headlines.\n\nSri Lanka possesses important strengths, including an educated and adaptable workforce, an extraordinary cultural and ecological heritage, and a strategic geographic position in the Indian Ocean.\u00a0 For long-term investors, periods of volatility do not erase that potential.\n\nBy committing early and demonstrating staying power, international investors do more than generate financial returns. Their continued investment can give others greater confidence in Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term potential and encourage them to explore opportunities of their own.\n\nNational resilience is ultimately built over decades, not measured in quarterly earnings.\n\nGovernments establish the policy frameworks and monetary conditions for recovery, but private capital must breathe life into the real economy. When businesses reject the easy exit and choose instead to reinvest in people, preserve physical infrastructure, and uplift regional communities, they cease to be foreign entities observing from the margins. They become genuine stakeholders in the country's collective future."
  },
