    "md5": "eff57a5746ab2a0abac071a657bc3982",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "Water must move to the centre of climate action\n\nWater is how most people first experience climate change. Whether through prolonged drought, devastating floods or increasingly unpredictable rainfall, its effects reach directly into people\u2019s lives, threatening food production, livelihoods, public health and economic security\n\nFor three decades, the international response to climate change has focused on reducing greenhouse gas emissions. The energy transition has become central to this effort, shaping national policies, mobilising investment and driving innovation. Now, as the world confronts the consequences of climate change already under way, we must give water the same prominence in our approach to adaptation.\n\nWater is how most people first experience climate change. Whether through prolonged drought, devastating floods or increasingly unpredictable rainfall, its effects reach directly into people\u2019s lives, threatening food production, livelihoods, public health and economic security.\n\nSo, if energy has been the defining concern of climate mitigation, water must become the defining concern of adaptation, and to be effective, recognition must be matched by resources. Water already features in the national climate plans of most countries and is the sector most frequently identified in their adaptation measures. Yet inclusion in a national plan is not the same as meaningful action. Investment remains insufficient, fragmented and poorly coordinated.\n\nThe scale of the shortfall is considerable. The World Bank estimates that closing the annual financing gap for universal access to safe drinking water and sanitation requires between\n\n$ 131 billion and $ 141 billion. Behind this figure are communities without reliable drinking water, children exposed to preventable disease and populations whose livelihoods and security are threatened by drought and flooding."
  },
