    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b84f6d5de3d47cef214f080316dd707e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1844,
    "chunk_text": "$ 131 billion and $ 141 billion. Behind this figure are communities without reliable drinking water, children exposed to preventable disease and populations whose livelihoods and security are threatened by drought and flooding.\n\nThere are encouraging examples of the leadership required. The Madrid Compact, endorsed by 30 governments, offers a framework for stronger political commitment, integrated policies, sustainable financing and accountability. The Heads of State Initiative, supported by Sanitation and Water for All, has demonstrated how national leadership can translate commitments on water and sanitation into action. These approaches deserve wider application.\n\nThe same determination is needed internationally. Water connects the three Rio Conventions, addressing climate change, biodiversity loss and land degradation. Yet their respective work does not always reflect the interconnected nature of these challenges, or the central role water plays across all three.\n\nCOP31 offers an opportunity to change this. I call on world leaders to give water a stronger and more systematic place across the Rio Conventions, ensuring that their policies, programmes and investments are better aligned. This does not require new institutions or additional bureaucracy. It requires greater coordination through the structures we already have, with water recognised as a shared priority rather than an issue confined to individual policy discussions.\n\nLeaders must close the gap between their commitments and the resources allocated to deliver them. Water infrastructure, sanitation and resilient water management cannot remain underfunded priorities in national adaptation strategies. They require sustained investment, clear accountability and political leadership capable of translating plans into tangible improvements in people\u2019s lives"
  },
