    "md5": "b4fa292db1017e5720651cb52ca44759",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1015,
    "chunk_text": "Leaders must close the gap between their commitments and the resources allocated to deliver them. Water infrastructure, sanitation and resilient water management cannot remain underfunded priorities in national adaptation strategies. They require sustained investment, clear accountability and political leadership capable of translating plans into tangible improvements in people\u2019s lives\n\nWe cannot afford to treat adaptation as secondary to mitigation, or water as secondary to adaptation. Leaders must recognise water as fundamental to the security, prosperity and wellbeing of their people, to match their commitments with the necessary investment, and to work together across the Rio Conventions.\n\nThe decisions made at COP31 and carried forward to Abu Dhabi can help establish the political foundations for a more water-secure future. What is needed now is the determination to act.\n\n(The author is former Prime Minister of Bhutan and member of the Global Leadership Council of Sanitation and Water for All)"
