    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3c6cafb742f8ca319d067839ea6670b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "These measures can help attract investment, strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s processing capabilities and create additional employment across the industry.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s seafood industry has demonstrated the considerable potential of value-added processing, particularly through the importation of raw materials for processing and re-export.\n\nThis transformation highlights the importance of maintaining policies that enable Sri Lankan processors to source raw materials internationally, add value locally and supply global markets.\n\nSEASL emphasises that continued access to imported raw materials for value addition is essential to sustaining factory utilisation, expanding employment and increasing foreign exchange earnings.\n\nThe proposed modernisation of the Temporary Importation for Export Processing (TIEP) scheme, customs digitalisation and the National Single Window for Trade should help simplify procedures, reduce costs and improve export competitiveness.\n\nThe Association supports the proposed Rs. 5 billion investment in Northern fisheries infrastructure, including Pesalai Harbour and fishery anchorages, which should improve landing facilities, product handling and livelihoods in coastal communities.\n\nSEASL is particularly encouraged by the opportunities for Sri Lanka\u2019s aquaculture farmers, who have the potential to play an increasingly important role in the development of the seafood export industry.\n\n\u201cWe are excited about the opportunities ahead for our aquaculture farmers, particularly those engaged in shrimp farming. They have an important contribution to make towards expanding production, creating rural employment and strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s seafood exports. As an industry, we are committed to supporting their growth, encouraging investment and helping connect farmers with international markets,\u201d SEASL\u2019s Dilan Fernando said."
  },
  {
