    "language": "en",
    "md5": "737057e2232ed1ec6afdd119cc8a5591",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1178,
    "chunk_text": "While the Budget provides an encouraging policy direction, SEASL stresses that timely implementation and close cooperation between Government and industry will be essential to achieving the country\u2019s export ambitions.\n\n\u201cThe proposed financing, investment incentives and fisheries development programs provide a strong foundation for growth. Our industry has already demonstrated what can be achieved through value addition, with its contribution to seafood exports increasing from 4% in 2022 to 26% in the first nine months of 2026.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe priority now is implementation. We must ensure that exporters can access these incentives, that raw-material imports for value-added processing continue without unnecessary restrictions, and that sustainable fisheries management remains a national priority. With the right policies, Sri Lanka can achieve $ 680 million in seafood export earnings by 2030,\u201d he added.\n\nSEASL reaffirmed its commitment to working with the Government, fishing communities, aquaculture farmers and international partners to expand seafood exports, attract investment, create employment and strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s position in global seafood markets."
