    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Mexican drug lord Miguel Angel Felix Gallardo dies age 80\n\nBBC -\u00a0Notorious Mexican drug lord Miguel \u00c1ngel F\u00e9lix Gallardo has died in hospital aged 80.\n\nF\u00e9lix Gallardo was widely considered one of the biggest drug smugglers in history and is credited with building an alliance between criminal groups in Colombia and his own Mexican cartel.\n\nKnown as The Godfather or the 'Boss of Bosses', F\u00e9lix Gallardo was the head of the all-powerful Guadalajara Cartel, which reigned over Mexican drug trafficking at the height of the cocaine boom of the 1980s.\n\nAt the time, it created a new modus operandi in the illegal drug trade which endures to this day. He had been in state custody since 1989.\n\nThe name Miguel \u00c1ngel F\u00e9lix Gallardo will echo through the annals of drug-trafficking history more than any other except perhaps Pablo Escobar's.\n\nHe led the organisation which revolutionised drug smuggling in the region by entering into partnership with the Colombian cartels, particularly Escobar's Medell\u00edn Cartel, to combine production in the Andes with Mexican transportation across the border into the United States.\n\nAt one point an estimated 80% of all cocaine that entered the US came from the Medell\u00edn Cartel. F\u00e9lix Gallardo and his organisation played a major role in smuggling it in.\n\nAt its height, the Guadalajara Cartel under F\u00e9lix Gallardo influenced Mexican politics and law enforcement, controlling entire police networks and state governorships.\n\nIn 1985, the net started to tighten around the leaders of the cartel following the torture and murder of a DEA agent, Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena.\n\nCamarena had angered them after he had discovered and then destroyed a vast marijuana plantation in Chihuahua, which belonged to the criminal group.\n\nHis death sparked one of the biggest manhunts the US government has ever launched in North America."
