    "chunk_text": "Lakdhanavi secures $ 75 m contract to build 57 MW power plant in India\n\nColombo, Nov 16 (Daily Mirror) - Lakdhanavi Ltd., a member of the LTL Group, has signed a turnkey Engineering, Procurement and Construction (EPC) contract worth approximately Rs. 25 billion ($ 75 million) to build a 57 MW natural gas-based fired power plant on India\u2019s Andaman Island.\n\nLTL Group is the largest power sector company in Sri Lanka with interests in power generation, transmission, and also distribution. Originally set up as a joint venture between the Ceylon Electricity Board (CEB) and a European company, currently National Transmission Network System Provider Ltd., (NTNSP), a Government-owned entity under the Energy Ministry, holds 35% stake in LTL Holdings Ltd.\n\nThe contract was awarded by NTPC Vidyut Vyapar Nigam Ltd., (NVVN), a wholly owned subsidiary of National Thermal Power Corporation (NTPC) Ltd., which is owned by the Government of India, following an international competitive bidding (ICB) process in which Lakdhanavi emerged as the lowest priced bidder (L1).\n\nThe contract was signed in September at the NTPC Office Complex in Noida, New Delhi, in the presence of senior representatives of NVVN, Lakdhanavi, and LTL Holdings. The parties also discussed project implementation and future collaboration between NVVN and Lakdhanavi.\n\nThe project will be located at Hope Town, Port Blair, in Andaman Island and is expected to strengthen the island\u2019s electricity infrastructure, enable long-term energy security through LNG infrastructure, and facilitate future economic development. The Lakdhanavi Ltd., engineering team would be responsible for the execution of the project, with W\u00e4rtsil\u00e4 Corporation of Finland being the supplier of the power-generating equipment to Lakdhanavi Ltd."
