    "chunk_text": "Oil prices fall on expectations of coming supply surplus\n\n(Reuters) - Oil prices fell \u200bon Monday on expectations of a supply surplus as shipments from the Middle East through the Strait of \u200cHormuz have been nearly restored to pre-war levels and Chinese refinery throughput fell in October, indicating lower crude demand.\n\nBrent futures were down 54 cents, or 0.6%, to $95.30 a barrel by 0427 GMT. US West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude fell 22 cents, or 0.3%, to $88.34.\n\nOil closed lower on Friday as the market weighed signs \u200bof lower geopolitical risk from the US-Israeli war against Iran, now in its ninth month. The benchmarks fell 16% and 13%, \u200brespectively, the prior week.\n\nThe world oil market will return to a surplus in the fourth quarter of this \u2060year, the International Energy Agency said on Friday, as Middle East exports recover faster than global oil demand despite continued attacks on \u200bshipping.\n\n\"For the first time since the US-Iran conflict began, traders are actively contemplating the possibility of excess supply rather than persistent tightness. \u200bThat shift in narrative is clearly dampening the tone,\" said Tim Waterer, chief analyst at KCM Trade.\n\nOil supply will average 105 million barrels per day in the fourth quarter, 1.7 million bpd higher than forecast demand, the IEA said. The IEA had previously forecast a fourth-quarter supply deficit.\n\nThe agency also gave more \u200bdetails of remaining volumes to come from a record release of oil from its members' emergency reserves pledged in March, saying 71 \u200bmillion barrels would be made available by the end of Februaryincluding 15 million barrels of diesel to ease market tightness.\n\nSigns of lower demand in China, the world's \u200csecond-largest \u2060oil consumer, also pressured prices. The latest data from the country's National Bureau of Statistics on Monday showed refinery throughput for October fell 2% from a month earlier and was down 8.1% from a year ago."
