    "chunk_size_bytes": 1337,
    "chunk_text": "Signs of lower demand in China, the world's \u200csecond-largest \u2060oil consumer, also pressured prices. The latest data from the country's National Bureau of Statistics on Monday showed refinery throughput for October fell 2% from a month earlier and was down 8.1% from a year ago.\n\nStill, KCM Trade's Waterer noted \u200bthat while supply was loosening, \u2060shipping rates remained high, putting a floor on oil prices.\n\nRates remain high because of the risks the tankers face carrying crude through the Strait of Hormuz and because the number of ships tied \u200bup in shuttling oil through the waterway for transfer to other ships that take shipments to refineries \u200bhas reduced the \u2060vessel supply.\n\nOn Sunday night, Iran's navy said that an oil tanker caught fire after striking a naval mine east of the Strait of Hormuz, state media reported.\n\nHowever, some analysts remain positive on the shipping situation for next year.\n\n\"Our core view sees a \u2060preliminary deal \u200bto reopen the Strait of Hormuz reached in Q1 2027. This will pave \u200bthe way for a sustained improvement in maritime security conditions, allowing freight and insurance markets to normalise and logistical distortions to unwind,\" said BMI analysts in a client \u200bnote.\n\n\"In turn, regional crude exports will stabilise, eclipsing pre-conflict levels as production grows.\""
