    "chunk_size_bytes": 1398,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka will not need an 18th IMF programme - Here is why\n\nSri Lanka's economic policy over the past decade has been dominated by the need to restore public finances viz a viz a higher tax to GDP ratio, the narrative was to manage government debt and rebuild foreign-exchange reserves. This is misplaced as our issue has always been a fragile external sector, and the lack of strengthening and creating long-term stability. The rupee revenues, were necessary priorities, particularly following the economic crisis of 2022, as the narrative spun was that tax cuts were the cause of the crisis. However, as the country moves into the next phase of recovery, an equally important question deserves attention: are we building the productive capacity required to sustain economic stability over the long term?\n\nThe distinction is important. A government can improve its fiscal position through higher taxation, expenditure restraint and local debt restructuring. Whilst it can only rebuild reserves through trade surpluses, remittances, tourism receipts and external financing. Therefore lasting external balance sheet economic strength requires something more: an economy with a competitive business model, expanding productive assets, technological capabilities and the ability to earn foreign exchange through widening its export products and services. This is where Budget 2027 should be assessed."
  },
