    "md5": "0dc52ab210ab5d54914f8b514edd39eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "The distinction is important. A government can improve its fiscal position through higher taxation, expenditure restraint and local debt restructuring. Whilst it can only rebuild reserves through trade surpluses, remittances, tourism receipts and external financing. Therefore lasting external balance sheet economic strength requires something more: an economy with a competitive business model, expanding productive assets, technological capabilities and the ability to earn foreign exchange through widening its export products and services. This is where Budget 2027 should be assessed.\n\nA modern approach to public finance is paramount for the national balance sheet growth journey. In this context we need to view public investment as a catalyst for private-sector competitiveness rather than a drain on the treasury. Every rupee of public capital investment should lower the cost of doing business, raise economy-wide productivity, and create productive assets that power future external revenue growth. Equally, education and workforce training must be directly linked to the needs of emerging industries. By combining growth-supportive taxation, reliable infrastructure, and industry-aligned skills with targeted R & D and global partnerships, the state can strengthen corporate balance sheets and help viable local firms compete internationally. Similarly public finance needs to re-orient towards the SME sector as it accounts for 52% of the real economy, 45% of employment and 20% of exports and should be encouraged to capture regional markets. In doing so it\u2019s imperative for SME to have access to low-cost finance, subsidized inputs and technology. Therefore, a national Industrial policy which includes MSMEs should be carved out encouraging industrial innovation, product development and international competitiveness. Most importantly, these measures should be connected through a coherent national strategy rather than implemented as isolated annual budgetary programs."
  },
