    "chunk_text": "As of now Government rupee revenue has improved to Rs 4.7 trillion (Jan-Sep 2026, Ministry of Finance Fiscal Review Report), debt restructuring has advanced and gross foreign-exchange reserves have reached USD 7.0 billion. The composition of the USD 7.0 billion is important as it comprises of swaps amounting to USD 3.5 billion and borrowings of USD 2.2 billion.\n\nSri Lanka's merchandise trade performance illustrates the challenge. In 2026, exports i.e. trade, amounted to approximately USD10.7 billion, while imports reached USD 18.6 billion. The resulting the cumulative trade deficit widened to USD 7.9 billion during January\u2013September 2026, compared to USD 5.2 billion in the corresponding period of 2025. More revealing is the composition of total consumer goods imports, as it reached approximately USD 4.0 billion during the first eight months of 2026, up from USD 3.3 billion in the same period in 2025. Over the full nine-month period through September, cumulative consumer import expenditure grew 17.5%. Whilst personal vehicle imports alone reached approximately USD 3.0 billion (2025 -2026), compared with just USD 221 million in 2024. These figures need to be interpreted carefully. Consumer imports and intermediary imports are not necessarily economically undesirable, as they are essential inputs for domestic production. Similarly, not every import automatically creates productive capacity. Nevertheless, the composition raises a legitimate policy question: is Sri Lanka allocating sufficient resources towards expanding the productive assets and industrial capabilities that will generate future foreign-exchange earnings?"
