    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "IMF\u2019s March 2026 report also identifies the high proportion of debt instruments in external liabilities as a continuing vulnerability. The lesson is that fiscal adjustment, while necessary, cannot by itself transform the productive structure of an economy. Sri Lanka must ensure that the resources released through fiscal stabilization are now accompanied by policies that encourage industrial capacity, productive investment, and strengthen domestic companies enabling them to expand new consumer high end value products and in turn sufficient export volumes at competitive prices.\n\nWhile Sri Lanka cannot simply copy the large deficit spending of China, Germany, or Japan because those economies borrowed in their own currencies while running massive export surpluses, it can adopt their core strategic discipline and focus on the new balance sheet economic model: shifting public resources from consumption to productive export capacity. Following the World Bank\u2019s 2026 industrial framework, Sri Lanka can apply this model within its fiscal limits by phasing out blanket tax holidays in favor of targeted new industrial investments in energy, port, new production technology, logistics, workforce skills, and high-value agro-industrialization, using catalytic public capital and multilateral partnerships to crowd in private local investment as well as attract Foreign Direct Investment (FDI). Thereby sustaining gross fixed capital formation to approximately 38% to 40% of GDP annually over a 10 year period.\n\nA national budget should ask not only what the Government can afford to spend this year, but also what industries, technologies and capabilities the country needs to develop over the next 10 to 15 years.\n\nStrengthening domestic production/ industrial base, MSMEs must become a central priority"
