    "chunk_text": "A national budget should ask not only what the Government can afford to spend this year, but also what industries, technologies and capabilities the country needs to develop over the next 10 to 15 years.\n\nStrengthening domestic production/ industrial base, MSMEs must become a central priority\n\nThis is why public policy must prioritize strengthening corporate balance sheets, the true foundation for capturing global market share, rather than shielding inefficient firms behind permanent protectionism. The government should empower viable domestic companies/industries to compete internationally. It can do this by extending its own (government) balance sheet by delivering reliable infrastructure, predictable regulation, growth-supportive taxation, and access to low-cost finance, while investing in R&D, skills development, and targeted industrial policy. By helping local businesses identify emerging product markets and forging partnerships across universities, research institutions, and global firms, the state can build internationally competitive enterprises. The Government's role should therefore be to create the necessary investible legislation conditions in which businesses can generate profits, retain earnings and reinvest in productive capacity."
