    "chunk_text": "This is why public policy must prioritize strengthening corporate balance sheets, the true foundation for capturing global market share, rather than shielding inefficient firms behind permanent protectionism. The government should empower viable domestic companies/industries to compete internationally. It can do this by extending its own (government) balance sheet by delivering reliable infrastructure, predictable regulation, growth-supportive taxation, and access to low-cost finance, while investing in R&D, skills development, and targeted industrial policy. By helping local businesses identify emerging product markets and forging partnerships across universities, research institutions, and global firms, the state can build internationally competitive enterprises. The Government's role should therefore be to create the necessary investible legislation conditions in which businesses can generate profits, retain earnings and reinvest in productive capacity.\n\nHigher production and exports will generate foreign exchange earnings, strengthen the national external balance sheet and reduce dependence on external borrowing. This is the connection between the Government's balance sheet, corporate balance sheets and the country's external balance sheet. Budget 2027 should therefore be judged not only by its revenue targets, fiscal deficit or primary surplus, but also by whether it advances this productive transformation. Sri Lanka has made important progress in its economy. The next challenge is to build an economy capable of sustaining that stability through stronger domestic companies, greater productivity and a more competitive export sector. That is how fiscal consolidation can become the foundation for lasting economic development, reducing external balance sheet dependencies reversing the negative net worth of USD 52 billon rather than moving to the 18th IMF program which is not an end in itself."
