    "description": "Anti-Houthi forces retreat in Yemen as Iran strikes Bahrain",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/international/Anti-Houthi-forces-retreat-in-Yemen-as-Iran-strikes-Bahrain/107-354974",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794810840.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-16-dailymirrorlk-84e5b47c-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "291f093c14b02934ad4ecf430f642283",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1377,
    "chunk_text": "\"The Southern Armed Forces announce the implementation of a redeployment operation for its forces from Dhubab Airport \u200band the Bab el-Mandeb area, west of Taiz Governorate,\" the main military force fighting for Yemen's government in the south of the country said in a statement.\n\nThere was no immediate response from the Houthis, or from Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe retreat signals a setback for efforts to reverse rapid gains the Houthis made in September to capture the coast along the Red Sea and the Bab el-Mandeb Strait that controls access to it.\n\nA Saudi-backed government offensive last month retook key positions around the strait but failed to drive the Houthis entirely away from the area or retake the city of al-Mocha further up the coast.\n\nThe \u200bHouthis have responded by intensifying drone and missile attacks on Saudi Arabia, grounding flights with strikes on its main airports as well as setting global oil markets on edge by targeting energy facilities.\n\nOn Sunday, the militant group said it attacked \u200bthe King Khalid International Airport and a financial centre in Riyadh and two pumping stations on the East-West oil pipeline.\n\nWith the conflict in the Middle East disrupting oil flows through the Strait of Hormuz, Riyadh has rerouted \u200cabout 4 million \u2060barrels per day of crude via the East-West Pipeline to Yanbu, equivalent to about 4% of global supply."
  },
