    "chunk_text": "The strain peaks on Sundays and public holidays, when factories and offices close and demand falls while solar output keeps flowing. National System Operator (NSO) Chairman Dr. Pradeep Perera said in August that commercial and industrial buildings with solar installations above 200 kW were being asked to disconnect on Sundays to balance the grid. Rooftop owners were asked to switch off their systems from 8.00 a.m. to 3.00 p.m. over the Vesak long weekend in May. Earlier, an island-wide blackout on a sunny Sunday in February 2025 was attributed to low system stability amid high solar output and weak demand.\n\nFor businesses, storage offers a way to bank Sunday and holiday solar rather than switch it off. Factories and hotels that rely on diesel generators during outages also stand to benefit, as Sungrow\u2019s C&I systems share load with gensets to cut fuel bills. A demonstration at the summit showed storage units lifting their share of a charging station\u2019s load to 70 percent while the diesel generator\u2019s share fell to 30 percent. PowerStack 3.0\u2019s microgrid module also offers a zero-millisecond switchover to off-grid operation to keep production lines running.\n\nNationally, wider storage could ease the country\u2019s reliance on costly imported fuel. Finance Ministry data released last week showed rooftop solar capacity standing at 2,529 MW by August, up 50.2 percent from a year earlier. However, a 42 percent rise in rooftop output in the first eight months was not enough to offset higher demand and a 22.6 percent fall in hydropower, pushing fuel-fired thermal generation up 89.6 percent to 2,543 GWh."
