    "md5": "38ce2253dde6db319152b9547d67a9c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "One-off Rolls-Royce gain cushions SriLankan\u2019s Rs.20bn five-month loss\n\nSriLankan\u2019s revenue climbed 35% to Rs.154.6bn between April and August, but a 78% surge in jet fuel costs tipped the airline into an operating loss of Rs.5.8bn\n\nThe President told parliament that the national carrier\u2019s accumulated losses since 2008 have reached Rs.528bn\n\nSriLankan Airlines would have posted a net loss more than twice as large in the first five months of its financial year but for a Rs.11.4 billion one-off reversal of Rolls-Royce withheld discounts and suspended credits, as a jet fuel shock and currency losses swamped a 35 percent rise in revenue, Finance Ministry data showed.\n\nThe national carrier\u2019s revenue rose to Rs.154.6 billion in the April to August period from Rs.114.6 billion a year earlier. However, expenditure grew faster, climbing 35.2 percent to Rs.160.4 billion as aircraft fuel costs surged 78.2 percent, leaving the airline with an operating loss of Rs.5.8 billion.\n\nBelow the operating line, financing costs of Rs.7.1 billion and foreign exchange losses of Rs.11.9 billion, together more than three times the operating loss, pushed the group\u2019s net loss to Rs.20.3 billion, or about Rs.4 billion a month, before the Rolls-Royce adjustment pared it to Rs.8.9 billion.\n\nThe airline\u2019s loss before tax had already tripled to Rs.23.2 billion in the 2025/26 financial year from Rs.7.6 billion a year earlier, as debt servicing and currency costs wiped out earnings before interest and tax of Rs.26.4 billion. The ministry noted that Middle East tensions had disrupted flight operations, restricted airspace and forced costly route diversions this year, weighing on a carrier heavily exposed to Middle Eastern routes."
