    "chunk_text": "The airline\u2019s loss before tax had already tripled to Rs.23.2 billion in the 2025/26 financial year from Rs.7.6 billion a year earlier, as debt servicing and currency costs wiped out earnings before interest and tax of Rs.26.4 billion. The ministry noted that Middle East tensions had disrupted flight operations, restricted airspace and forced costly route diversions this year, weighing on a carrier heavily exposed to Middle Eastern routes.\n\n\u201cSriLankan Airlines has become a burden on the General Treasury,\u201d he told Parliament.\n\nMeanwhile, the government has extended US$ 30 million, or about Rs.10 billion, in short-term assistance to meet the airline\u2019s immediate operational and liquidity needs. It is also restructuring the airline\u2019s US$ 175 million sovereign-guaranteed bond that matured in 2024 and pursuing a settlement arrangement, initiated in 2025, for its legacy debt. The Treasury also guarantees Rs.3.8 billion of SriLankan\u2019s obligations to the International Air Transport Association (IATA).\n\nIn June, the Cabinet appointed a committee chaired by Presidential Senior Adviser on Digital Economy Dr. Hans Wijayasuriya, with the International Finance Corporation (IFC) as transaction adviser, to conduct a strategic review and draw up a restructuring framework to cut the airline\u2019s reliance on state support. The Finance Ministry warned that sustained restructuring, prudent cash-flow management and greater operational efficiency would be essential to contain fiscal risks to the government. (NF)"
