    "md5": "cf20ec5bb1b405689e4025052eb0eca4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1684,
    "chunk_text": "Hoteliers praise budget\u2019s tourism direction\n\nTHASL expects TDL collections to reach Rs.4.5-5bn this year, while the budget estimates annual cost of abolishing the levy at Rs.40mn\n\nThe association wants the US$ 3mn entry threshold for the 200% capital allowance lowered to US$ 500,000 so that boutique hotels can benefit\n\nSLTDA and SLTPB will fund Rs.1.4bn of Rs.1.9bn in new tourism measures in the budget\n\nThe Hotels Association of Sri Lanka (THASL) praised the 2027 budget\u2019s direction for tourism, while seeking talks with authorities on bridging the funding gap left by the abolition of the Tourism Development Levy (TDL) and on lowering the US$ 3 million threshold for a new capital allowance, THASL President Asoka Hettigoda told Mirror Business.\n\nHettigoda acknowledged that Many of the association\u2019s proposals had been captured in the budget, citing the expansion of Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA) and its planned domestic terminal, a cruise terminal, hotel schools, domestic airports and digital ticketing for attractions, all of which he expected to help draw high-value travellers to the island. \u201cAt least from our side, we feel that it\u2019s a very progressive budget,\u201d he added, noting that tourism had been duly recognised as a key industry.\n\nHe also described the budget as a much bigger investment in tourism than last year\u2019s, pointing to World Bank support to develop heritage sites, including historic forts, as attractions, which he said the sector had not received from donor agencies in the past. Among these is a US$ 77 million World Bank-funded project to restore historic buildings around Colombo Fort and develop the area as a tourism zone."
  },
