    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f4a920bb358a1051770e89fda95c2b2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1828,
    "chunk_text": "He also described the budget as a much bigger investment in tourism than last year\u2019s, pointing to World Bank support to develop heritage sites, including historic forts, as attractions, which he said the sector had not received from donor agencies in the past. Among these is a US$ 77 million World Bank-funded project to restore historic buildings around Colombo Fort and develop the area as a tourism zone.\n\n\u201cWe want to have a dialogue with the authorities to see how the gap is going to be bridged,\u201d Hettigoda stressed, adding that the association was also pressing for a global marketing campaign. He pointed out that small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) had been the main beneficiaries of TDL-funded promotions such as overseas travel fairs and familiarisation tours for foreign journalists, since larger companies maintain their own marketing networks.\n\n\u201cLarge companies don\u2019t need travel shows,\u201d he observed, noting that between 65 and 70 of the 84 or so Sri Lankan companies at the recent World Travel Market (WTM) in London were SMEs, many of which were now enquiring whether such support would continue.\n\nThe budget\u2019s revenue annex costs the abolition, effective April 1, 2027, at only Rs.40 million a year, or less than 1 percent of the industry\u2019s estimate, as the levy is credited to the Tourism Development Fund rather than the Consolidated Fund. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who is also the Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, told Parliament the move was aimed at \u201creducing the tax burden on the tourism industry, enhancing its competitiveness and ensuring sustainable financing for tourism development,\u201d while the Treasury will release funds on requests made by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) until a new financing mechanism is formulated."
