    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "The budget\u2019s revenue annex costs the abolition, effective April 1, 2027, at only Rs.40 million a year, or less than 1 percent of the industry\u2019s estimate, as the levy is credited to the Tourism Development Fund rather than the Consolidated Fund. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who is also the Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, told Parliament the move was aimed at \u201creducing the tax burden on the tourism industry, enhancing its competitiveness and ensuring sustainable financing for tourism development,\u201d while the Treasury will release funds on requests made by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) until a new financing mechanism is formulated.\n\nHettigoda remarked that the existing threshold was \u201crather steep\u201d for SMEs, given that a five- to 10-room boutique hotel requires far less capital, adding that travellers were increasingly favouring unique, smaller and high-value properties.\n\nMeanwhile, budget documents show that the SLTDA and the Sri Lanka Tourism Promotion Bureau (SLTPB), both self-financing bodies funded through the Tourism Development Fund, will finance Rs.1,413 million of the Rs.1,948 million in new measures in the budget\u2019s tourism chapter, leaving the Treasury\u2019s direct share at Rs.535 million. The chapter\u2019s total amounts to about a quarter of the Rs.7.8 billion in destination, promotion and training proposals in the 2026 budget, while the tourism development programme under the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism has been cut by 41 percent to Rs.618 million.\n\nThe SLTPB also remitted Rs.2 billion in levies to the Treasury in the first eight months of 2026, double its payment for the whole of 2025, Finance Ministry data showed. (NF)"
