    "chunk_size_bytes": 1776,
    "chunk_text": "Interpreting Budget 2027: Focus on priorities in an increasingly volatile world\n\nThe Budget does have disparate housing and agricultural development programmes, particularly in fisheries\n\nIn a world prone to various shocks in recent years, a focus on the food system with an emphasis on self-sufficiency remains an urgent priority within or outside the provisions of the Budget\n\nBudgets provide government priorities for the next year, but they also point to the economic trajectory over the next many years. How do we interpret Budget 2027 announced few days ago, and more importantly, how do we engage with it towards social\u00a0and economic change?\n\nIn the concluding section of the Budget Speech, the President has the following to say: \u201cCriticise the Government. Question our policies. Present a better alternative programme than ours. We fully accept your democratic right to do so. But bring alternative policies instead of curse verses, creative solutions instead of sorcery \u2026\u201d\n\nI believe we should take this call for engagement seriously. Because budgets and for that matter economic policies, including even the best of them, will not materialise without people\u2019s engagement, participation and mobilisation. Indeed, unless there is dialogue, debate and struggle both within the state and in the hinterlands, demanding that provisions of the budget are implemented, changed or alternatives created, there will be little progress in our economic situation.\n\nIn other words, budgets are the basis for ideological struggle, for concrete demands and alternatives that should not only be posed to the Government but the public at large. In this column I address what is lacking, the significant provisions and the alternatives to be forged in relation to Budget 2027."
  },
