    "md5": "f73ada0b6629180405d7726889550f69",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1746,
    "chunk_text": "In other words, budgets are the basis for ideological struggle, for concrete demands and alternatives that should not only be posed to the Government but the public at large. In this column I address what is lacking, the significant provisions and the alternatives to be forged in relation to Budget 2027.\n\nThe major gap in the Budget is employment creation. Sri Lanka for decades has not had credible labour and employment policies. Labour market analysis itself is non-existent. We know that the unemployment data is for the most part meaningless given how it is calculated. In the context of the long economic crisis that the country has been mired in, the lack of budgetary provisions to increase employment and revitalise livelihoods is worrying.\n\nThere are many subsidised loan schemes in the budget for MSMEs in the hope of creating employment, but from past experience we know that such subsidies will not create the levels of employment necessary to address the protracted economic crisis. Firms will merely use the subsidies to reduce their losses or increase their profits.\n\nWhat is necessary is to increase effective demand through public investment that clearly seeks to multiply employment and strengthen the food system. The Budget has a static balance sheet approach towards surplus from revenues in relation to expenditure, rather than a dynamic conception of multipliers to increase production, employment and livelihoods. In budget and policy making, it is important to understand that the whole is greater than the sum of the parts, and the state should engage in counter-cyclical policies. In other words, the government\u2019s role including its deficits and debt has very different dynamics from that of\u00a0firms and households."
  },
