    "md5": "ec164ab86ce2321d97f6d8bafa48d19f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1719,
    "chunk_text": "What is necessary is to increase effective demand through public investment that clearly seeks to multiply employment and strengthen the food system. The Budget has a static balance sheet approach towards surplus from revenues in relation to expenditure, rather than a dynamic conception of multipliers to increase production, employment and livelihoods. In budget and policy making, it is important to understand that the whole is greater than the sum of the parts, and the state should engage in counter-cyclical policies. In other words, the government\u2019s role including its deficits and debt has very different dynamics from that of\u00a0firms and households.\n\nThe Budget is perhaps strongest in the much needed approach to energy transformation. Indeed, the large investment in energy infrastructure, particularly renewable energy production, storage and transmission as well as facilities for storing and refining fuel are encouraging measures given the energy shocks we are likely to see. The plans for electric trains and the broader shift away from fossil fuels that will also reduce the large import bill for fuel \u2013 in times when fuel prices are likely to be volatile \u2013 is welcome.\n\nSri Lanka historically has very low investment in research and development, and we have done little to manage our large natural resource base. The Budget seeks to both invest in and create a number of state institutions to address this gap lasting decades. The initiatives to address our mineral resources and exploration of oil and gas depend on how they are taken forward. It is important to have sovereign control over our natural resources, and ensure that multi-national corporations and extractive powers are kept away."
  },
