    "md5": "23c0166b0017db2185f603bb335e7394",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka historically has very low investment in research and development, and we have done little to manage our large natural resource base. The Budget seeks to both invest in and create a number of state institutions to address this gap lasting decades. The initiatives to address our mineral resources and exploration of oil and gas depend on how they are taken forward. It is important to have sovereign control over our natural resources, and ensure that multi-national corporations and extractive powers are kept away.\n\nIt is no secret that Budget 2027 is constrained by the policy decision of the NPP Government when it came to power to remain within the IMF programme. The Public Finance Management Act and the numerous other laws that came as conditionalities of the IMF programme and its austerity push have shackled the Government\u2019s capacity to grow the economy,\u00a0 and for that matter to meet\u00a0the needs of the citizenry.\n\nIt is critical that the Government does not go into another IMF programme after it comes to an end in March 2027. The many laws and constraints that came with the IMF programme should be amended or repealed. Those are the preconditions for setting the economy on the path of growth while serving the needs of the working people.\n\nSri Lanka has adequate resources, but we lack the institutions and the capacity for economic rejuvenation, in many ways due to the approach of past regimes in power and the neoliberal policies spanning close to five decades. Amidst an unravelling global order and years ahead bound to be times of turmoil, Sri Lanka needs to leave behind its history of dependency on external powers and forge a different economic programme. Sri Lanka as a small state \u2013 facing reckless global giants with little concern for consequences and middle powers guarding their own interests \u2013 needs to think of both its domestic policies and external relations in ways to ensure the welfare of its people."
  },
