    "md5": "10dafd328c78032cec8afd3234cd9411",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: What Is Beyond Colombo Port City?\n\nGlobal trade and investment are important drivers of economic growth, with the ultimate aim of improving people\u2019s living standards. Special Economic Zones (SEZs) are one of the instruments used by countries around the world to attract investment. They are designated areas within a country\u2019s sovereign territory where businesses are offered specific incentives and operating conditions to encourage economic activity.\n\nChina\u2019s first Special Economic Zone was established in Shenzhen on August 26, 1980, under the leadership of Deng Xiaoping.\n\nShenzhen has since developed into a major global manufacturing and technology centre, producing a substantial share of the world\u2019s consumer electronics and hosting technology companies such as Tencent, Huawei and DJI.\n\nFrench geographer and economic-zones expert Professor Fran\u00e7ois Bost of the University of Reims has examined the development of SEZs, their contribution to economic growth and their increasing importance in attracting foreign investment.\n\nIn his paper, he argues that SEZs have become important instruments for countries seeking to accelerate industrialisation, diversify their economies, generate employment and integrate into international trade and investment networks.\n\nAccording to UNCTAD statistics cited in the paper, the world had 5,383 active SEZs in 2019, spread across approximately 150 countries. Of these, 4,046, or 75%, were located in Asia, highlighting the region\u2019s dominance in the development of such economic zones.\n\nChina stands out as the leading example, accounting for 2,543 SEZs, or 47.2% of the global total. Since 1978, China has made the development of SEZs a major component of its economic development strategy. These zones have helped attract domestic and foreign investment, facilitate manufacturing,expand exports and introduce new economic activities. Importantly, many Chinese zones also cater to the domestic market rather than being exclusively export-oriented."
  },
