    "md5": "6527f8c5a766ef28ea35fc74ea7f4026",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1695,
    "chunk_text": "The experience of established Asian SEZs, particularly those in China, demonstrates the potential of such developments to support economic diversification and attract international capital. Colombo Port City, however, is still at a nascent stage compared with mature zones in China, Dubai, Malaysia and India. Sri Lanka is not yet widely recognised as an international investment destination and does not have the depth of business ecosystems, financial markets or investor networks available in those centres.\n\nReports of cybercrime networks shifting westwards from established centres in Cambodia, Myanmar and other parts of Southeast Asia raise legitimate concerns for Sri Lanka. The regional scam-compound industry involves online fraud, romance scams, fake cryptocurrency investment schemes and illegal\u00a0 betting operations, with criminal networks moving when enforcement pressure increases in one jurisdiction. The United Nations has estimated that hundreds of thousands of people have been trafficked into such operations across the region.\n\nSri Lanka has also recorded arrests linked to cybercrime operations. Official figures cited in reports indicate that about 430 foreign nationals were arrested over cybercrime-related offences in 2024, while more than 1,000 were reportedly arrested during the first five months of 2026. The arrest of 130 Chinese\u00a0 \u00a0nationals in Kundasale in June 2025, 85 of whom were subsequently deported, was another high-profile incident. These cases point to a problem that warrants sustained attention, but they should not be used to attribute criminal conduct to any particular nationality or to assume that Port City itself is a centre for such activity."
