    "md5": "5d93901e6bc4edc2bf3582606f37283b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has also recorded arrests linked to cybercrime operations. Official figures cited in reports indicate that about 430 foreign nationals were arrested over cybercrime-related offences in 2024, while more than 1,000 were reportedly arrested during the first five months of 2026. The arrest of 130 Chinese\u00a0 \u00a0nationals in Kundasale in June 2025, 85 of whom were subsequently deported, was another high-profile incident. These cases point to a problem that warrants sustained attention, but they should not be used to attribute criminal conduct to any particular nationality or to assume that Port City itself is a centre for such activity.\n\nThe Commission has also said that sponsoring companies must screen foreign recruits and submit recent police-clearance certificates authenticated by a diplomatic mission. Employment contracts, passport details and job designations are checked against approved manpower plans, while new measures include cyber-abuse and cybercrime audits of relevant authorised persons and monthly risk reporting.\n\nCompanies employing foreign nationals are also required to submit monthly staff information and provide the nearest police station with employee lists and residential addresses.\n\nImportantly, the CPCEC does not have the final authority to grant visas or permit entry into Sri Lanka. It makes recommendations, but the Department of Immigration and Emigration retains responsibility for immigration and security checks, including screening against Interpol records, international police notices and information from local and foreign law-enforcement agencies. Even a Commission-recommended endorsement does not guarantee admission; immigration officers may still refuse entry. The Commission has also said it is working towards memoranda of understanding with domestic agencies and regulators to formalise information-sharing and coordination."
