    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fe2e517faf5de66231b9a4e5c6d32663",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1518,
    "chunk_text": "Importantly, the CPCEC does not have the final authority to grant visas or permit entry into Sri Lanka. It makes recommendations, but the Department of Immigration and Emigration retains responsibility for immigration and security checks, including screening against Interpol records, international police notices and information from local and foreign law-enforcement agencies. Even a Commission-recommended endorsement does not guarantee admission; immigration officers may still refuse entry. The Commission has also said it is working towards memoranda of understanding with domestic agencies and regulators to formalise information-sharing and coordination.\n\nSri Lanka cannot expect to compete for international investment on the same terms as mature global financial and commercial centres. Established hubs have stronger business ecosystems, deeper financial markets and well-developed investor networks. As Sri Lanka continues to establish its position as an investment destination, tax incentives are therefore a necessary policy tool to bridge the competitiveness gap, reduce initial barriers and encourage international businesses to consider locating here.\n\nSuch incentives need to be supported by regulatory certainty, efficient infrastructure, reliable public services, skilled human resources and a business-friendly environment. The objective should be to attract reputable companies whose activities generate employment, foreign exchange, technology transfer and opportunities for domestic businesses."
  },
