    "chunk_size_bytes": 1584,
    "chunk_text": "Such incentives need to be supported by regulatory certainty, efficient infrastructure, reliable public services, skilled human resources and a business-friendly environment. The objective should be to attract reputable companies whose activities generate employment, foreign exchange, technology transfer and opportunities for domestic businesses.\n\nWhat is now noteworthy is that the economic logic behind the Colombo Port City has been well established by the government. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, in his November 12 budget speech, laid emphasis on attracting foreign investment into Colombo Port City. That is with the prime intention of placing Sri Lanka as a regional hub for digital services, financial activities and technology-based businesses. This gives credence to the economic relevance of the project. It is not meant to lie idle.\u00a0 A key highlight is the proposed amendment to the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act. The draft Colombo Port City Economic Commission (Amendment) Bill has been referred to the Attorney General, with the stated objective of attracting more foreign investment and providing greater facilities to investors and people employed within the Port City.\u00a0 Besides, the government has proposed establishing a National Single Window for Investment, with an allocation of Rs.100 million, alongside a Treasury-owned investment promotion company. It also plans to introduce an Investment Protection Bill and a Public-Private Partnership Act, while appointing a\u00a0 \u00a0committee to address delays in releasing land for investment projects."
