    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 fails to address people\u2019s nutritional needs: Harsha\n\nColombo, Nov. 16 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) MP Dr. Harsha de Silva told Parliament today that Budget 2027 has failed to address pressing nutritional deficiencies in the country, pointing out that high food costs prevent Sri Lankans from consuming adequate amounts of protein.\n\nReferring to recent remarks by Minister K. D. Lalkantha that Sri Lankans consume excessive amounts of rice, Dr. de Silva agreed with the observation, noting that citizens are unable to afford protein-rich foods.\n\n\"A person requires 60 grams of protein daily, yet the high cost of essential food items prevents people from meeting this requirement. No tax concessions have been granted for essential goods,\" the MP said.\n\nHighlighting the cost barriers to protein sources, he pointed out that chicken is currently subject to an 18 percent Value Added Tax (VAT), while the import duty on corn\u2014a primary input for poultry feed\u2014has surged from Rs. 25 per kilogram a few years ago to Rs. 1,000 today. With Sri Lanka producing only 50 percent of its corn requirement locally, the remaining half must be imported at steep costs.\n\nDr. de Silva also noted that a person would need to consume around 60 sprats a day to meet their daily protein requirement, adding that roughly 3.6 million Sri Lankans currently cannot afford even one proper meal a day. \"The nutritional crisis highlighted by Minister Lalkantha has been completely overlooked in this budget,\" he charged.\n\nTurning to public sector remuneration, the SJB MP criticized the targeted salary hikes proposed in Budget 2027, claiming that only 16 percent of state employees stand to benefit, leaving 84 percent of the public workforce without relief."
