    "time_ut": 1794840541.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-16-dailymirrorlk-324686bb-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3b47299f4b0dd0bb83b431df06c6aff1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Jasim al-Bahadli, an Iraqi security analyst specialising in studying armed Shi'ite groups aligned with Iran, said the government anti-smuggling campaign has become a political confrontation with the militia.\n\n\"More broadly, it appears to be an attempt to contain the influence of Iran-backed \u200barmed groups by targeting economic networks that underpin their power, particularly after Kataib Hezbollah resisted government efforts to bring armed factions more firmly under state control.\"\n\nSo far, nine senior \u200bsecurity and oil officials have been dismissed following a high-level government investigation into oil smuggling, focused on Jurf al-Sakhar in Babil province, a Kataib Hezbollah stronghold about 50 km (30 miles) south of Baghdad.\n\nZaidi has ordered \u200call suspects \u2060to be referred to the courts, and dismissed the Babil police chief, the province's intelligence and security commanders and the director of the state-run Oil Pipelines Company (OPC).\n\nNow he has ordered senior military and security commanders to deploy additional forces around Jurf al-Sakhar and prepare for the entry of government troops into the town, potentially bringing the government into a direct confrontation with fighters.\n\nThe groups have built up arsenals that include assault rifles, machineguns, anti-tank guided missiles, mortars, rockets, armed drones and, according to Iraqi security reports and assessments, some short-range ballistic and \u200bcruise missile capabilities supplied by Iran or \u200bderived from Iranian systems.\n\nTwo security sources who \u2060took part in an inspection mission on Saturday to Jurf al-Sakhar said investigators had found evidence that smugglers had punctured a crude oil pipeline and siphoned crude into makeshift storage pits dug in the ground.\n\nThe 26-inch pipeline carries at least 200,000 barrels per day of crude, according \u200bto an Iraqi Oil Ministry document reviewed by Reuters."
  },
