    "md5": "87fb2119252e371105ad90494f14d60b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2014,
    "chunk_text": "The bodies behind the call are HERCA, the Heads of the European Radiological Protection Competent Authorities; WENRA, the Western European Nuclear Regulators Association; and NORA, the Heads of the Nordic Radiation Safety Authorities.\n\nTogether they represent 38 countries, including \u200ball EU countries, the US, Britain, Canada and Japan, among others.\n\n\"Because of \u2060this new tactic of the Russians, ... clearly the highest risk will be this winter for a nuclear accident,\" he told Reuters.\n\nThe worst-case scenario, he said, would be a nationwide blackout of the Ukrainian power grid that would affect Ukraine's nine producing nuclear reactors simultaneously.\n\n\"Nine reactors at the same time with the potential of (radioactive) releases would be quite a severe situation,\" he said.\n\nHe stressed that the situation could be avoided if action was taken now and Russia \u200bwas pressured to stop its attacks on \u200benergy infrastructure.\n\nIf not, European countries \u2060must work together on their emergency preparedness to tackle the possible consequences of a nuclear accident, he said.\n\nOn Saturday, the International Atomic Energy Agency said military activity had caused instability in Ukraine's electricity in the preceding week, \u200bresulting in power reductions at some operating units in the country's nuclear power plants.\n\nUkraine has four nuclear power \u200bplants, of which \u2060three are in operation, providing some 80% of the power used this winter. Its fourth, Zaporizhzhia, Europe's largest, is under Russian control in an area close to the front line.\n\nIt is not currently producing electricity but relies on external power to keep the nuclear material cool and avoid a meltdown. The plant's equipment is \u2060powered by electricity \u200bsupplied from Ukraine.\n\nSince the start of the war, Zaporizhzhia has lost off-site power 27 \u200btimes, the IAEA said on Saturday, forcing it to switch to emergency diesel generators. Since late March, the plant has relied on a single off-site power line, the IAEA said."
  },
