    "chunk_size_bytes": 1343,
    "chunk_text": "Seven decades of Budgets, one unbroken economic failure: Can Sri Lanka finally escape its past?\n\nFrom Bandaranaike\u2019s State-led economy to Jayewardene\u2019s liberalisation, Rajapaksa\u2019s infrastructure ambitions and Dissanayake\u2019s fiscal discipline, successive Governments have promised prosperity through competing economic models. Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget proposes an ambitious transition from stabilisation to development. But does it address the institutional failures that have repeatedly prevented economic recovery from becoming lasting prosperity?\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget arrives at an extraordinary moment in the country\u2019s economic history. Four years after sovereign default, foreign reserves have recovered, Government revenue has strengthened and economic growth has returned. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake can reasonably point to an economy considerably more stable than the one that collapsed in 2022. Yet stability, however necessary, is not transformation. The consequential question is whether this Budget represents a fundamental restructuring of Sri Lanka\u2019s economy or another restoration of financial order within a system whose underlying weaknesses remain unresolved. For a country that has repeatedly mistaken economic recovery for lasting development, the distinction is neither academic nor insignificant."
