    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget arrives at an extraordinary moment in the country\u2019s economic history. Four years after sovereign default, foreign reserves have recovered, Government revenue has strengthened and economic growth has returned. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake can reasonably point to an economy considerably more stable than the one that collapsed in 2022. Yet stability, however necessary, is not transformation. The consequential question is whether this Budget represents a fundamental restructuring of Sri Lanka\u2019s economy or another restoration of financial order within a system whose underlying weaknesses remain unresolved. For a country that has repeatedly mistaken economic recovery for lasting development, the distinction is neither academic nor insignificant.\n\nThe Bandaranaike era marked an expansion of State intervention, reflecting postcolonial ambitions to strengthen domestic ownership and reduce dependence on foreign commercial interests. Sirimavo Bandaranaike\u2019s administrations subsequently deepened nationalisation, import substitution and State-led industrial development. These policies emerged from legitimate concerns about sovereignty and inequality, while Sri Lanka\u2019s broader welfare commitments contributed to important achievements in education and healthcare. Nevertheless, extensive protection, foreign exchange restrictions and administrative controls constrained investment and competition. The World Bank subsequently cited estimates suggesting that economic distortions during 1971\u201377 reduced annual growth by approximately two percentage points. The lesson was not that public intervention was inherently misguided, but that ownership and protection alone could not guarantee productive efficiency.\n\nSri Lanka has learned how to survive an economic crisis. The greater challenge is proving that it has learned how not to create another"
