    "chunk_text": "The Bandaranaike era marked an expansion of State intervention, reflecting postcolonial ambitions to strengthen domestic ownership and reduce dependence on foreign commercial interests. Sirimavo Bandaranaike\u2019s administrations subsequently deepened nationalisation, import substitution and State-led industrial development. These policies emerged from legitimate concerns about sovereignty and inequality, while Sri Lanka\u2019s broader welfare commitments contributed to important achievements in education and healthcare. Nevertheless, extensive protection, foreign exchange restrictions and administrative controls constrained investment and competition. The World Bank subsequently cited estimates suggesting that economic distortions during 1971\u201377 reduced annual growth by approximately two percentage points. The lesson was not that public intervention was inherently misguided, but that ownership and protection alone could not guarantee productive efficiency.\n\nSri Lanka has learned how to survive an economic crisis. The greater challenge is proving that it has learned how not to create another\n\nFiscal recovery is measurable. But whatW does the 2027 Budget actually change?\n\nAgainst this history, Dissanayake\u2019s fiscal achievements deserve recognition. According to the Budget Speech, Government revenue increased from 8.2% of GDP in 2022 to 16.6% in 2025, while the overall deficit narrowed to 2.3% of GDP in 2025. Economic growth reached 5%. Nevertheless, stabilisation began under the IMF-supported program negotiated during Ranil Wickremesinghe\u2019s presidency. The NPP Government deserves credit for maintaining fiscal continuity despite its earlier opposition to aspects of the prevailing economic framework. Pragmatism need not indicate ideological weakness, but it raises an important question: what distinguishes the administration\u2019s promised transformation from the recovery program it inherited?"
