    "chunk_text": "Against this history, Dissanayake\u2019s fiscal achievements deserve recognition. According to the Budget Speech, Government revenue increased from 8.2% of GDP in 2022 to 16.6% in 2025, while the overall deficit narrowed to 2.3% of GDP in 2025. Economic growth reached 5%. Nevertheless, stabilisation began under the IMF-supported program negotiated during Ranil Wickremesinghe\u2019s presidency. The NPP Government deserves credit for maintaining fiscal continuity despite its earlier opposition to aspects of the prevailing economic framework. Pragmatism need not indicate ideological weakness, but it raises an important question: what distinguishes the administration\u2019s promised transformation from the recovery program it inherited?\n\nThe more revealing concern is implementation. The IMF reported that capital expenditure was under-executed by approximately 1% of GDP annually during 2023\u201325. This suggests that stronger fiscal balances have partly coincided with the State\u2019s inability to execute planned development expenditure. The Government\u2019s new investment commitments therefore require independently evaluated feasibility studies, transparent procurement, published implementation schedules and measurable economic returns. Without these safeguards, Sri Lanka risks repeating a familiar pattern in which announcements acquire greater political importance than completed projects."
