    "chunk_text": "The more revealing concern is implementation. The IMF reported that capital expenditure was under-executed by approximately 1% of GDP annually during 2023\u201325. This suggests that stronger fiscal balances have partly coincided with the State\u2019s inability to execute planned development expenditure. The Government\u2019s new investment commitments therefore require independently evaluated feasibility studies, transparent procurement, published implementation schedules and measurable economic returns. Without these safeguards, Sri Lanka risks repeating a familiar pattern in which announcements acquire greater political importance than completed projects.\n\nThe real achievement of this Budget will not be determined by the size of its allocations, the elegance of its projections or the confidence of its parliamentary presentation. It will be determined by whether the economy emerging from recovery is fundamentally stronger than the one that failed\n\nThe Budget proposes increasing the monthly personal income tax-free threshold from Rs 150,000 to Rs 162,500 and revising progressive tax brackets. These changes provide relief to sections of the formal workforce, while the proposed VAT exemption for printed books and exercise books recognises educational affordability. However, the projected Rs 3.592 trillion in taxes on goods and services, compared with Rs 1.341 trillion from income taxes, demonstrates continued reliance on consumption taxation. This raises substantial distributional concerns because lower-income households spend larger proportions of their incomes on necessities, while personal income tax concessions primarily benefit those above the tax threshold. A credible progressive fiscal framework must assess not merely revenue collection but who ultimately finances recovery."
