    "chunk_text": "The Budget proposes increasing the monthly personal income tax-free threshold from Rs 150,000 to Rs 162,500 and revising progressive tax brackets. These changes provide relief to sections of the formal workforce, while the proposed VAT exemption for printed books and exercise books recognises educational affordability. However, the projected Rs 3.592 trillion in taxes on goods and services, compared with Rs 1.341 trillion from income taxes, demonstrates continued reliance on consumption taxation. This raises substantial distributional concerns because lower-income households spend larger proportions of their incomes on necessities, while personal income tax concessions primarily benefit those above the tax threshold. A credible progressive fiscal framework must assess not merely revenue collection but who ultimately finances recovery.\n\nAgriculture offers a particularly revealing example of the structural challenge. The Budget acknowledges that the sector employs approximately 23.9% of the workforce while contributing only around 8.4% of economic output. Its Rs 44 billion fertiliser subsidy, supporting payments of Rs 30,000 per hectare, may reduce production costs, but subsidies alone cannot resolve low productivity, fragmented cultivation or weaknesses in marketing. The proposed collective Yaya production system, improved agricultural service centres and greater flexibility in cultivating underutilised paddy lands could improve efficiency. Their success, however, depends upon farmer participation, market access, water management and fair pricing. Sri Lanka must move beyond measuring agricultural policy through subsidies distributed and begin evaluating productivity, farmer incomes and value added per hectare."
