    "chunk_text": "Agriculture offers a particularly revealing example of the structural challenge. The Budget acknowledges that the sector employs approximately 23.9% of the workforce while contributing only around 8.4% of economic output. Its Rs 44 billion fertiliser subsidy, supporting payments of Rs 30,000 per hectare, may reduce production costs, but subsidies alone cannot resolve low productivity, fragmented cultivation or weaknesses in marketing. The proposed collective Yaya production system, improved agricultural service centres and greater flexibility in cultivating underutilised paddy lands could improve efficiency. Their success, however, depends upon farmer participation, market access, water management and fair pricing. Sri Lanka must move beyond measuring agricultural policy through subsidies distributed and begin evaluating productivity, farmer incomes and value added per hectare.\n\nFor decades, Sri Lanka\u2019s political establishment has treated economic recovery as evidence of national progress. The events of 2022 demonstrated how misleading that assumption can become\n\nForeign direct investment remains central to the Government\u2019s development ambitions. The proposed 200% enhanced capital allowance for qualifying Board of Investment-approved export manufacturing and tourism investments, subject to a minimum investment of $3 million, could encourage capital formation. However, tax concessions cannot indefinitely compensate for unpredictable regulations, administrative delays, expensive energy or weak contract enforcement. Sri Lanka must examine whether investment incentives generate additional economic activity that would otherwise not occur, rather than simply reducing taxation on investments already commercially attractive."
