    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "Energy policy is equally consequential. The Budget outlines plans to increase battery energy-storage capacity from 160 MW in 2026 to 560 MW in 2027, alongside a proposed subsidy of up to Rs 250,000 for eligible domestic rooftop solar battery systems. These measures could improve renewable-energy integration and reduce vulnerability to imported fuel, although their benefits depend upon grid reliability, competitive procurement and transparent subsidy targeting. Energy security is not simply an environmental objective: reliable and competitively priced electricity is essential for industrial competitiveness, household welfare and investor confidence.\n\nThe Budget also proposes increased allowances for medical officers, nurses, university academics and other public employees, partly phased across 2027 and 2028. Such measures may help retain skilled personnel and recognise rising living costs, particularly in essential services. Nevertheless, recurrent commitments create continuing fiscal obligations. They should therefore be integrated with transparent workforce planning, performance standards and broader institutional improvements rather than treated as substitutes for reform.\n\nThe ultimate test: Has Sri Lanka changed the economy or merely its management?\n\nExternal vulnerabilities remain substantial. The Budget projects foreign debt-service obligations of approximately $2.99 billion in 2027 and $3.57 billion in 2028. With the existing IMF program approaching its scheduled conclusion in March 2027, the Government must determine how stability can be sustained without recurring emergency assistance. International support may remain valuable, particularly amid geopolitical instability and climate shocks, but the ultimate measure of recovery cannot be the successful negotiation of successive rescue arrangements. It must be the development of institutions and industries capable of reducing the likelihood that another rescue becomes necessary."
