    "chunk_text": "External vulnerabilities remain substantial. The Budget projects foreign debt-service obligations of approximately $2.99 billion in 2027 and $3.57 billion in 2028. With the existing IMF program approaching its scheduled conclusion in March 2027, the Government must determine how stability can be sustained without recurring emergency assistance. International support may remain valuable, particularly amid geopolitical instability and climate shocks, but the ultimate measure of recovery cannot be the successful negotiation of successive rescue arrangements. It must be the development of institutions and industries capable of reducing the likelihood that another rescue becomes necessary.\n\nThe 2027 Budget contains credible proposals across infrastructure, agriculture, enterprise development, social protection, energy and human capital. Its ambitions address genuine structural weaknesses, yet the proposals remain promises until their economic effects can be demonstrated. Sri Lanka\u2019s history offers no simple ideological solution: State-led development struggled with efficiency, liberalisation expanded opportunity without eliminating vulnerability, infrastructure investment created assets alongside financing risks, and fiscal stabilisation restored confidence without guaranteeing productive transformation.\n\nDissanayake now confronts the challenge that successive administrations have approached through competing economic philosophies: converting public policy into durable institutional capability and sustained prosperity. Sri Lanka has demonstrated that it can collect taxes, restrain expenditure and rebuild reserves. It has not yet demonstrated that it can consistently attract productive investment, generate sophisticated exports, create high-quality employment and distribute the gains of growth fairly."
