    "md5": "cca093b1c32cf305445d458a6bd4644f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1588,
    "chunk_text": "PSTA: Still in violation of international norms\n\nIt is the job of the Government, not of the Supreme Court, to ensure that Bills are consistent with international norms set out in international conventions that Sri Lanka has ratified, such as the International Convention on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR). The Court\u2019s determination on the Protection of the State against Terrorism Bill (PSTA) only addressed questions of consistency with the Constitution, based on some curious rationalisations. That is within the authority of the Court.\n\nIn its determination, the Court did not mandate any changes to section 27 that empowers the Secretary of the Ministry to issue detention orders nor change the word \u201cshall\u201d to \u201cmay\u201d in section 26(3), preserving the ability of the Executive to deprive a person of liberty without judicial oversight. The review committee established under section 30 was mentioned in positive tone, without proposing any changes to its appointment by the Minister without Constitutional Council concurrence and its powers being only recommendatory (section 30(8)). The broad powers given to the President to indefinitely proscribe organisation were discussed but no material changes were mandated.\n\nThis leaves it up to the Executive, headed by the President, to make the necessary changes. Indeed, the President has stated that the Government will consult with civil society on the PSTA and on the even worse Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCA). The Bills cannot be taken up by Parliament until the budget debate has been concluded in any case."
  },
