    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a0b874160bf67f9c893fc5f53b110161",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "This leaves it up to the Executive, headed by the President, to make the necessary changes. Indeed, the President has stated that the Government will consult with civil society on the PSTA and on the even worse Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCA). The Bills cannot be taken up by Parliament until the budget debate has been concluded in any case.\n\nThe Executive should step back from the insistence that the decision to incarcerate a person suspected of having committed an offence under the PSTA for up to two months is its alone and does not involve the judicial branch. The Government does not appear to trust Magistrates with decisions on granting bail. Perhaps the decisions to detain a person should also be moved up the judicial hierarchy to the High Court and out of the Executive. This action will make the PSTA compliant with ICCPR Article 9(3).\n\nThe requirement of an appeal of a detention order under ICCPR Article 9(4) may be met easily. The review committee proposed in section 30(4) should be made truly independent and its decisions final. Independence can be strengthened by having the Constitutional Council approve the appointments. Amendment of section 30(8) removing the powers of the Minister to disregard the committee\u2019s recommendations will complete the remediation.\n\nThe most puzzling aspect of the Supreme Court\u2019s determination is that relating to the President\u2019s untrammeled powers to proscribe organisations (section 59). Article 155 of the Constitution allows the Executive to take urgent measures under the Public Security Ordinance (No. 25 of 1947) but requires Parliament to concur every month. As the Court notes, it may be necessary to take prompt action on terrorist organisations. That is the purpose of the Public Security Ordinance."
  },
