    "chunk_size_bytes": 1237,
    "chunk_text": "The most puzzling aspect of the Supreme Court\u2019s determination is that relating to the President\u2019s untrammeled powers to proscribe organisations (section 59). Article 155 of the Constitution allows the Executive to take urgent measures under the Public Security Ordinance (No. 25 of 1947) but requires Parliament to concur every month. As the Court notes, it may be necessary to take prompt action on terrorist organisations. That is the purpose of the Public Security Ordinance.\n\nThe Presidency is a political position. From the time of the 17th Amendment, the trend has been to introduce checks and balances on the enormous powers granted the President by the original 1978 Constitution. An organisation that has been unjustly proscribed by the President is asked to appeal to the Supreme Court instead of the Court of Appeals, as though that is a practical solution in that the organisation is unable to use its own resources even to launch the appeal because its bank accounts are frozen by the proscription order. In the same way that the addition of a phrase to section 29(2) does not remedy the core problem of the Executive depriving persons of liberty, this too makes changes at the margin, without addressing the core issue."
