    "md5": "f4cb5a9db0434ae356de9c965f8e1666",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: AI audit that audited itself\n\nSoon after the Budget Speech 2027 was tabled, a social media post landed in my feed claiming the document was full of arithmetic errors and other mistakes. I wanted to test that claim myself. So I uploaded the full English text to an AI tool and asked it a blunt question: find the mistakes.\n\nIt took less than 30 seconds. The list read like the opening of a scandal. A Rs. 1.5 billion arithmetic error in the minor-irrigation allocations. MSME loan schemes worth Rs. 140 billion inflated to Rs. 174 billion. A nine-month primary surplus of Rs. 1,844 billion that the tool called mathematically impossible. A World Bank upgrade to upper-middle-income status that, it claimed, never happened. A primary expenditure projection of 13.6% of GDP that broke the 13% ceiling in the Public Financial Management Act.\n\nI had a social media post drafted within minutes. The angle wrote itself. A government that had just allocated Rs. 28,500 million for the digital economy, and announced a National Artificial Intelligence Centre of Excellence, had senior officials who couldn\u2019t spend 30 seconds running their own document through basic AI.\n\nThen I checked each claim against the speech and public data. The draft went in the bin.\n\nThe Rs. 1.5 billion error came first. The speech lists four minor-irrigation allocations: Rs. 2,000 million to the Department of Agrarian Development, Rs. 7,500 million for Provincial Council tanks, anicuts and canals, Rs. 2,500 million under the \u201cWari Mahimaya - Ape Urumaya\u201d program, and Rs. 1,500 million for schemes damaged by Cyclone Ditwah. They add to Rs. 13,500 million. The next sentence reads: \u201cIncluding these, around Rs. 15,000 million has been allocated directly for the maintenance and development of minor irrigation schemes.\u201d\n\nIncluding these. The total covers the four named items plus other spending. The AI read a subset as a sum and manufactured a Rs. 1.5 billion hole."
  },
