    "chunk_text": "The Rs. 1.5 billion error came first. The speech lists four minor-irrigation allocations: Rs. 2,000 million to the Department of Agrarian Development, Rs. 7,500 million for Provincial Council tanks, anicuts and canals, Rs. 2,500 million under the \u201cWari Mahimaya - Ape Urumaya\u201d program, and Rs. 1,500 million for schemes damaged by Cyclone Ditwah. They add to Rs. 13,500 million. The next sentence reads: \u201cIncluding these, around Rs. 15,000 million has been allocated directly for the maintenance and development of minor irrigation schemes.\u201d\n\nIncluding these. The total covers the four named items plus other spending. The AI read a subset as a sum and manufactured a Rs. 1.5 billion hole.\n\nThe primary surplus claim was weaker still. Central Bank data reported in June showed a primary surplus of Rs. 1.13 trillion in the first five months of 2026. Revenue and grants reached Rs. 5,485.6 billion in 2025. A Rs. 1,844 billion surplus by September is large, and the end-year target of Rs. 360 billion tells you spending is loaded into the final quarter. It is entirely possible.\n\nThe World Bank claim was flatly false. On 1 July 2026 the Bank reclassified Sri Lanka as upper-middle-income, with Atlas GNI per capita of $4,670 against a threshold of $4,636. Dr. W.A. Wijewardena wrote about that narrow $34 margin in July. Every major Sri Lankan outlet carried the news.\n\nThe fiscal law claim read only half the law. The PFM Act caps primary expenditure at 13% of GDP, and it also contains an escape clause for exceptional events. The Government activated that clause after Cyclone Ditwah, when the Rs. 500 billion supplementary estimate pushed 2026 primary expenditure to 13.7% of GDP. The 2027 speech projects 13.6% as a temporary increase and commits to returning to 13% from 2028."
  },
