    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "The fiscal law claim read only half the law. The PFM Act caps primary expenditure at 13% of GDP, and it also contains an escape clause for exceptional events. The Government activated that clause after Cyclone Ditwah, when the Rs. 500 billion supplementary estimate pushed 2026 primary expenditure to 13.7% of GDP. The 2027 speech projects 13.6% as a temporary increase and commits to returning to 13% from 2028.\n\nA legitimate policy debate also sits behind the false \u201cillegal\u201d claim. Using the escape clause in two consecutive years tests the credibility of a ceiling that Verit\u00e9 Research has already described as the most extreme of its kind in the world. That debate deserves serious analysis, built on what the law actually says.\n\nLanguage models predict plausible text. Plausible and correct overlap most of the time, which is exactly why the gaps are dangerous. When the tool scanned the budget speech, it did what a pattern-matcher does: it found numbers near a total and assumed they should add up. It skipped the word \u201cincluding.\u201d It appears to have leaned on outdated information about Sri Lanka\u2019s income status from before July 2026. Then it delivered every guess in the same confident tone as the one real error.\n\nHad I posted the original draft, I would have accused public servants of sloppiness using claims far sloppier than anything in the speech. A share-hungry audience would have carried it. The correction would have arrived days later and reached a fraction of those readers.\n\nThe post that sent me looking may well have started the same way: an AI summary, read once, shared without a second check.\n\nThe budget\u2019s digital agenda now has to manage exactly this risk. The speech commits money to digital government transformation, a Cyber Security Authority and an AI Centre of Excellence at Wijerama. Those investments will put AI tools in the hands of thousands of officials drafting procurement papers, policy notes and, eventually, budget documents."
  },
