    "chunk_text": "He said that he is being held in solitary confinement for up to 22 hours a day, where he is allowed neither visits by his family nor access to books and television, and that this has \"taken a heavy toll\" on his mental health.\n\nHe also claimed he had only seen his lawyers four times over the past year.\n\n\"I will only remain able to prove my innocence if I stay alive,\" he wrote.\n\n\"[He] is currently the most well-accommodated prisoner in Pakistan,\" the minister told reporters.\n\nAuthorities also say Khan was taken to a government-run hospital for an eye injection a week ago, where doctors deemed him \"clinically stable\".\n\nKhan, who previously captained the Pakistan national cricket team, served as Pakistan's prime minister from 2018 to 2022.\n\nHis arrest in May 2023 sparked large-scale, violent protests by supporters, who ransacked the corps commander's residence in Lahore, smashing chandeliers and making away with furniture, paintings, and even peacocks, among other things.\n\nAt least eight people died in the protests and the army were called in to quell the violence.\n\nEven behind bars, Khan remains a force in Pakistani politics.\n\nThousands of his supporters took to the streets in early October in a dayslong march, demanding his release. Authorities later ordered a crackdown on protests and imposed a state of emergency in Khan's party stronghold, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.\n\nFormer international cricket captains have made several appeals in recent months to Pakistan's Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif, insisting that Khan be given proper medical care."
