    "chunk_text": "Death row prisoner saved, police exposed\n\nColombo, Nov. 17 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Court of Appeal has set aside the death sentence imposed on a man convicted of possessing and trafficking 236.3 grammes of heroin, holding that serious lapses in the police investigation had rendered the conviction unsafe and observing that the raid conducted by the officer-in-charge of the Kandy Police Narcotics Division appeared to have been carried out \u201cfor mockery\u201d, ultimately leading to the suspect\u2019s acquittal.\n\nA two-judge Bench comprising Justices Amal Ranaraja and Dr. Sumudu Premachandra, allowing the appeal, held that the prosecution had failed to establish the essential elements of the case beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nThe accused, Wasantha Wijaya Kumara Yatawara, alias Bunga, had been convicted by the Kandy High Court on two counts relating to the possession and trafficking of 236.3 grammes of heroin following a raid conducted near a leading school in Kandy, on May 5, 2021. He was sentenced to death on September 9, 2024.\n\nIn its judgement, the High Court highlighted serious shortcomings in the conduct of the raid, particularly the failure of the principal investigating officer, Sub-Inspector H. H. Madushan, to maintain contemporaneous records of the operation.\n\nThe officer, who had reportedly conducted around 400 drug-related raids, admitted that he had not taken his Police Pocket Notebook (Sakku Satahan Potha) when leaving for the operation and had failed to record several crucial events, including the handling and custody of the seized heroin.\n\nThe Court observed that maintaining contemporaneous notes was a fundamental requirement of police duties and that the failure to record important events raised serious doubts about whether the raid had actually taken place in the manner described by the prosecution."
