    "chunk_text": "Premature babies need lifelong follow-up care, Deputy Health Minister says\n\nColombo, Nov. 17 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The responsibility of healthcare services should not end with saving the life of a premature baby, as continuous medical care and follow-up are essential to ensure the child's healthy growth and development, Deputy Health Minister Dr. Hansaka Wijemuni said.\n\nHe made these remarks at a special awareness programme organised by the Perinatal Society of Sri Lanka to mark World Prematurity Day, which falls on November 15.\n\nThe event was held yesterday at the Courtyard by Marriott hotel at Colombo City Centre, under the patronage of Dr. Wijemuni.\n\nThe Deputy Minister stressed that premature babies require continued medical attention even after being discharged from Neonatal Intensive Care Units (NICUs).\n\nHe warned that the lack of proper follow-up care could lead to complications affecting children's overall development, including their eyesight and respiratory health.\n\nHe said strengthening maternal and neonatal healthcare services remained a key priority of the Ministry of Health and commended healthcare professionals for their contribution to managing high-risk pregnancies and saving the lives of premature babies.\n\nHe also stressed that caring for premature babies should not be considered the sole responsibility of the state healthcare system, adding that professional associations, the private sector and other stakeholders must work together to ensure these children receive the care they need.\n\n\"Every premature infant deserves the care and opportunities required to reach their full potential in life. All stakeholders must act with a sense of collective responsibility,\" he said.\n\nA special expert discussion involving the Deputy Minister and medical specialists was also held during the programme. A set of special guidelines aimed at improving the health and well-being of premature babies and their long-term health outcomes was unveiled."
