    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Oil slips as recovering Mideast exports blunt Gulf tensions\n\nReuters\u00a0- Oil prices fell on Tuesday as expectations of a supply surplus, driven by recovering Middle East exports, overshadowed concerns \u200babout continued attacks on tankers in the region and broader geopolitical tensions.\n\nBrent crude futures \u200cdropped 78 cents, or 0.8%, to $91.66 a barrel by 0430 GMT, while US West Texas Intermediate (WTI) futures fell 73 cents, or 0.9%, to $84.99.\n\n\"Oil is recalibrating lower as the market increasingly accepts that supply has finally caught up with \u200bdemand and may now be set to exceed it. That shift in the supply-demand balance \u200bis the main factor weighing on prices today,\" said Tim Waterer, chief analyst at \u2060KCM Trade.\n\nThe International Energy Agency said on Friday that the world oil market will return to a \u200bsurplus in the fourth quarter of this year, as Middle East exports recover faster than global oil \u200bdemand despite attacks on shipping.\n\nIranian attacks on US bases in Bahrain and disruptions to shipping in the Strait of Hormuz have continued, maintaining uncertainty around one of the world's most important oil-export routes.\n\n\"The geopolitical risk premium from the US-Iran conflict \u200bhas not disappeared, but it is clearly taking a back seat for now,\" KCM Trade's Waterer said.\n\nMarket \u200bconcerns are easing over reports that oil flows continue to pick up, but the oil market's resilience is being \u200cseverely \u2060stressed, while supply, inventories, refining and effective spare capacity are vulnerable, ANZ analysts said in a note.\n\n\"The supply flows are also unsustainable over the longer term. An increasing amount must find its way via alternative routes that are highly exposed to disruptions from attacks from Iran or constraints on logistics,\" they \u200bsaid, adding that throughput \u200bin the Strait of \u2060Hormuz is only 49% of pre-war levels."
