    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "50 deep-sea vessels planned to supply local canned fish industry\n\nColombo, Nov. 17 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Sri Lanka Consumer Cooperative Societies Federation Limited (COOPFED) and the Richwin Group have jointly launched 28 new \u201cCoopFish\u201d canned fish products under a public-private partnership aimed at strengthening the domestic fisheries industry and expanding the cooperative sector\u2019s role in the market.\n\nThe products were officially launched on November 15, at a ceremony held at Hotel Royal Ramesses in Seeduwa, under the patronage of Minister of Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Cooperative Development Wasantha Samarasinghe and Deputy Minister Upali Samarasinghe.\n\nAddressing the event, Minister Wasantha Samarasinghe said the Government was rebuilding the cooperative movement as a key pillar of the national economy. Plans were underway to establish 10,000 cooperative retail outlets and 25 modern \u201cMega Coop City\u201d complexes across the country by 2029, enabling consumers to purchase quality goods at competitive prices, he said.\n\nHe also said import restrictions had been imposed to protect the local canned fish industry, while the Government aimed to save approximately US$20 million in foreign exchange spent annually on importing raw materials for canned fish production.\n\nAs part of efforts to develop the sector, concessionary loans of up to Rs. 500 million per vessel at an interest rate of 5% had been introduced to facilitate the purchase of deep-sea fishing vessels. The Government had also allocated Rs. 15 billion to develop the fisheries industry into an export-oriented sector, he said.\n\nCOOPFED Chairman Dr. Shantha Jayarathne said the CoopFish products would be distributed at fair prices across the country through a network of nearly 4,000 outlets operated by 305 cooperative societies, serving a membership base of around 8.2 million."
  },
