    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-23f33a81-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "174020b2617c359f2786eab58ccc4be2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "Seafood exporters seek easier import rules in push for US $ 680mn by 2030\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s seafood exporters have called for simpler rules on importing raw materials for processing and re-export, saying continued access to overseas supplies will be critical to reach US $ 680 million in annual export earnings by 2030.\n\nThe Seafood Exporters\u2019 Association of Sri Lanka (SEASL) said the value-added products generated US $ 64 million of US $ 246.12 million earned from seafood exports in the first nine months of 2026. That lifted their share of seafood export earnings to 26 percent, from 4 percent in 2022.\n\n\u201cThe priority now is implementation. We must ensure that the exporters can access these incentives, that the raw-material imports for value-added processing continue without unnecessary restrictions and that sustainable fisheries management remains a national priority. With the right policies, Sri Lanka can achieve US $ 680 million in seafood export earnings by 2030,\u201d said Dilan Fernando of SEASL.\n\nThe association said the proposed overhaul of the Temporary Importation for Export Processing scheme, together with the Customs digitalisation and National Single Window for Trade, could simplify the procedures and cut costs.\n\nIt said access to imported raw materials helps the processors keep the factories running and expand production for overseas markets.\n\nBudget 2027 proposes loans of up to Rs.500 million, at 5 percent annual interest for qualifying large-scale fisheries enterprises. The SEASL said the financing could support investment in processing plants, equipment and cold-chain infrastructure.\n\nA proposed 200 percent enhanced capital allowance for qualifying Board of Investment-approved export manufacturing projects worth at least US $ 3 million could also encourage the processors to expand capacity and develop higher-value products, the association said."
  },
