    "md5": "20f49a9780ddee9b8a04e10148b003b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "Rubber traders push for early extension of plantation leases\n\nCRTA says leases due to expire in 2045 are already a consideration for long-term estate investment\n\nAgeing trees, insufficient replanting and rising costs weigh on domestic rubber production\n\nTraders seek help tracing rubber supplies to meet EU deforestation rules\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s rubber traders have called for an early extension of the land leases held by the Regional Plantation Companies, saying the uncertainty over what happens when the agreements expire in 2045 could hold back investment in replanting and estate modernisation.\n\nThe Colombo Rubber Traders\u2019 Association (CRTA) made the call in a statement marking 150 years of natural rubber cultivation in Sri Lanka.\n\nIt said clearer terms on long-term land tenure would encourage the companies to replace the ageing trees, improve yields and upgrade infrastructure.\n\nThe association gave no proposed lease duration or estimate of the investment at stake.\n\n\u201cHowever, this anniversary must be more than a celebration of our past. It must serve as a renewed commitment to revitalising the industry, addressing the challenges confronting our stakeholders and securing a commercially sustainable, competitive and prosperous future for Sri Lankan natural rubber,\u201d CRTA Chairman Harin de Silva said.\n\nThe association said productivity had stagnated or declined in many traditional growing areas, where the producers face the rising costs, labour shortages and inadequate use of rain guards, which allow tapping to continue in wet weather. It called for better access to quality planting material and practical support for the smallholders to keep cultivation commercially viable.\n\nThe supply challenge extends beyond the estates. Natural rubber feeds the domestic manufacturing industries that produce solid tyres, surgical gloves and latex bedding."
