    "chunk_size_bytes": 1474,
    "chunk_text": "The association said productivity had stagnated or declined in many traditional growing areas, where the producers face the rising costs, labour shortages and inadequate use of rain guards, which allow tapping to continue in wet weather. It called for better access to quality planting material and practical support for the smallholders to keep cultivation commercially viable.\n\nThe supply challenge extends beyond the estates. Natural rubber feeds the domestic manufacturing industries that produce solid tyres, surgical gloves and latex bedding.\n\n\u201cA competitive industry will depend not only on producing more rubber but also on producing it efficiently, maintaining consistent quality, meeting international standards and delivering products that create sustainable value for all participants in the supply chain,\u201d de Silva said.\n\nThe CRTA also urged Sri Lanka to assess whether rubber could be grown in suitable drier areas outside the traditional wet-zone growing regions. It said expansion would depend on climate and land-use assessments and the development of planting material and cultivation practices suited to those areas.\n\nFor the exporters, the association flagged a separate pressure on market access. It called for coordinated data collection and technical assistance to help the growers, processors and traders document the origin of rubber supplies and meet the traceability and geolocation requirements of the European Union Deforestation Regulation."
