    "time_ut": 1794896166.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-029207f1-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e11cdf6c11c7a8cabcccc1a5202ffb3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1923,
    "chunk_text": "Indirect taxes back at 78% of tax revenue; consumers see no relief: KPMG\n\nDirect tax share slumps to 22%, from 33% in 2023, far off 40% goal\n\nMiddlemen pocketed 2019 VAT relief worth Rs.540bn; Budget 2027 levy cuts on sprats and Maldive fish face same leak\n\nIMF-backed Tax Policy Analysis Unit at risk of becoming a \u201cwhite elephant\u201d\n\nRs.100,000 earner pays 16.2% of income as VAT, Rs.1mn earner just 7.2%\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tax burden has swung back onto the consumers, with the direct taxes slumping to 22 percent of the tax revenue, from a peak of 33 percent in 2023, yet the indirect tax relief fails to reach them, as the country lacks an anti-profiteering law and its new tax policy unit has gone quiet, a senior tax expert warned.\n\nAddressing the Institute of Certified Management Accountants of Sri Lanka (CMA Sri Lanka) Budget Highlights 2027 forum in Colombo yesterday, KPMG Sri Lanka Tax and Regulatory Principal Suresh Perera noted that the share of direct taxes in total tax revenue has moved like a yo-yo.\n\nHe pointed out that it climbed from 23 percent in 2021 to as much as 33 percent, eased to 27 percent and has now slid to 22 percent, as per the latest numbers published with Budget 2027.\n\nWith the direct share shrinking by a third from its peak, the state now collects roughly Rs.3.50 through indirect taxes for every rupee of direct taxes.\n\nUnder the 60:40 split between indirect and direct taxes that the successive governments have pledged, that figure would be just Rs.1.50. Perera stressed that reaching the targeted ratio requires a far more sophisticated approach than the ad hoc changes of recent years.\n\nHe argued that the heavier reliance on Value Added Tax (VAT) and other consumption taxes makes the system regressive.\n\nTo illustrate, he showed that a person earning Rs.100,000 a month, with Rs.90,000 of taxable spending, pays about Rs.16,200 in VAT at 18 percent or 16.2 percent of income."
  },
