    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5d245469ce15bf2be5d44a7adfbb08ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "He argued that the heavier reliance on Value Added Tax (VAT) and other consumption taxes makes the system regressive.\n\nTo illustrate, he showed that a person earning Rs.100,000 a month, with Rs.90,000 of taxable spending, pays about Rs.16,200 in VAT at 18 percent or 16.2 percent of income.\n\nAccording to Perera, that burden falls hardest on the low and middle-income households and runs against Adam Smith\u2019s principle of taxing according to the ability to pay.\n\nPerera pointed out that even when the indirect taxes are cut, the benefit seldom reaches the consumer. Recalling the sweeping tax changes of late 2019, when VAT was slashed from 15 percent to 8 percent, a reduction of nearly 47 percent, he estimated that the state gave up around Rs.540 billion by changing the VAT rules. Questioning whether that relief ever reached the consumers, he was unequivocal.\n\n\u201cThe answer is no. Why? The vendors profiteered; the middleman pocketed that benefit,\u201d he told the gathering.\n\nAt the time, the Inland Revenue Department put the annual cost of the VAT rate cut and the higher registration threshold alone at Rs.143 billion. Within weeks, its then Commissioner General acknowledged that some bakery manufacturers and hoteliers had not passed the benefit on to the customers.\n\nPerera himself pressed for anti-profiteering clauses in a column published in December 2019, days after the rate cut took effect.\n\nHis warning comes days after Budget 2027 slashed the Special Commodity Levy (SCL) on imported sprats from Rs.100 to Rs.10 per kilogramme, a 90 percent cut and on Maldive fish from Rs.302 to Rs.10 per kilogramme, a reduction of nearly 97 percent.\n\nThe levy on the dried chillies was lowered by Rs.50 per kilogramme, while that on imported sugar was raised by Rs.10 per kilogramme, with the cuts taking effect from November 13.\n\n\u201cWe have a fundamental error in the Sri Lankan VAT Act. We don\u2019t have what is called an anti-profiteering clause,\u201d Perera observed."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-029207f1",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-029207f1",
