    "description": "Indirect taxes back at 78% of tax revenue; consumers see no relief: KPMG",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Indirect-taxes-back-at-78-of-tax-revenue-consumers-see-no-relief-KPMG/215-355072",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794896166.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-029207f1-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c5936bbe1d039b883f7bee494dbf3977",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "The levy on the dried chillies was lowered by Rs.50 per kilogramme, while that on imported sugar was raised by Rs.10 per kilogramme, with the cuts taking effect from November 13.\n\n\u201cWe have a fundamental error in the Sri Lankan VAT Act. We don\u2019t have what is called an anti-profiteering clause,\u201d Perera observed.\n\nHe dismissed the Consumer Affairs Authority Act as ineffective in this role. He recalled that the previous government granted relief in the expectation that the food prices at the canteens and bakeries would come down but nothing happened and the authorities eventually resorted to gazettes to control the prices of hoppers.\n\n\u201cThese things are failures because we don\u2019t have an anti-profiteering mechanism in Sri Lanka. It\u2019s a must,\u201d he stressed.\n\nCiting examples from India, Singapore, Malaysia, Australia and New Zealand, Perera highlighted India\u2019s statutory tax-benefit pass-through under Section 171 of its Central GST Act, Singapore\u2019s Committee Against Profiteering and Malaysia\u2019s Price Control and Anti-Profiteering Act of 2011 and its 2018 regulations.\n\nMeanwhile, Perera questioned the whereabouts of the Tax Policy Analysis Unit, which he believes should be steering any shift in Sri Lanka\u2019s tax mix.\n\nThe Finance Ministry formally established the unit under its Fiscal Policy Department in February this year, as an IMF structural benchmark, after the IMF conducted a capacity-building programme for its staff from January 19 to 30. The unit\u2019s stated functions include revenue analysis, distributional analysis of tax policies and the evaluation of tax expenditures.\n\n\u201cAfter that, have you heard anything about it? Any output coming from that unit?\u201d he questioned, more than nine months after its launch.\n\n\u201cOur concern should be that this unit should not end up becoming a white elephant because we have not seen this unit, who is in this unit, what activity is being done in this unit.\u201d"
  },
  {
