    "language": "en",
    "md5": "04f4d0d97d6e994eece2fde5985f3c24",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cAfter that, have you heard anything about it? Any output coming from that unit?\u201d he questioned, more than nine months after its launch.\n\n\u201cOur concern should be that this unit should not end up becoming a white elephant because we have not seen this unit, who is in this unit, what activity is being done in this unit.\u201d\n\nHe outlined the government\u2019s plan of no new taxes or rate increases, raising additional revenue by broadening the direct and indirect tax base and lowering the VAT rate in a few years. Perera warned that such a transformation could not be achieved without the proper institutions in place.\n\nTo broaden the direct tax base, Perera proposed bringing offshore indirect transfers of shares, crypto assets and online betting into the tax net, along with stronger tax administration.\n\nOn the indirect side, he called for a reverse charge mechanism and platform liability for digital VAT, together with fewer exemptions. He insisted that any reduction of VAT on essential goods must come with an anti-profiteering mechanism attached.\n\nHowever, he cautioned that the indirect taxes are only one driver of the consumer burden, with the interest rates, exchange rates, global prices, production and supply chain costs, financing costs and business margins all playing a part.\n\nHe also warned that the government could not afford to grant relief irresponsibly, as the country still depends heavily on tax collections.\n\n\u201cRight now, Sri Lanka needs taxes to keep it afloat,\u201d he noted.\n\nDescribing the single 18 percent VAT rate as very high compared to the region, Perera advocated a multiple-rate structure with around 5 percent on essentials and 20 to 25 percent on luxury items.\n\n\u201cYou can\u2019t have something like perfume and say milk or some essential item being taxed at the same rate. That to me doesn\u2019t sound correct,\u201d he said."
  },
