    "chunk_text": "Describing the single 18 percent VAT rate as very high compared to the region, Perera advocated a multiple-rate structure with around 5 percent on essentials and 20 to 25 percent on luxury items.\n\n\u201cYou can\u2019t have something like perfume and say milk or some essential item being taxed at the same rate. That to me doesn\u2019t sound correct,\u201d he said.\n\nBudget 2027 has proposed exempting printed books and exercise books from VAT.\n\nHe also called for the number of VAT exemptions to be brought down. He noted that they once covered more than 200 line items and still stand at around 40, whereas New Zealand and Australia operate with fewer than five.\n\nHe further argued that the restrictions on claiming input tax credits under Section 22 of the VAT Act artificially distort the calculation of value addition, meaning that Sri Lanka is not truly collecting a tax on value added.\n\nDescribing the Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL) as a turnover tax under another name, Perera recalled that Sri Lanka abandoned the turnover tax in 1998 because of its cascading effect, only to see it return under different names, including the Nation Building Tax, which was introduced in 2009 and abolished in 2019.\n\n\u201cSocial security levy is nothing but turnover tax,\u201d he declared, while urging a gradual phase-out, given the revenue at stake.\n\nThe government has already folded the 2.5 percent SSCL into VAT for financial services from July 1 this year, creating a consolidated rate of 20.5 percent.\n\nHe also renewed his call to scrap the SCL, which allows the minister to determine the rates without the parliamentary approval. He noted that this mirrors the structure of the Special Goods and Services Tax Bill, several clauses of which the Supreme Court determined in 2022 to be inconsistent with the constitution."
