    "md5": "0909b1e3e64a753f8f1bc3d680d44eb0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027:  Political Promises Vs People\u2019s  Everyday Realities\n\nHowever, beyond these concessions, the fundamental question remains: Has the 2027 Budget adequately addressed the economic difficulties confronting ordinary Sri Lankans?\n\nThe Government deserves recognition for introducing several positive tax concessions in its 2027 Budget. The increase in the monthly personal income tax-free threshold from Rs. 150,000 to Rs. 162,500, reductions in certain income tax rates, and the removal of VAT on printed books and exercise books are welcome measures. The proposed removal of the 10% Ports and Airports Development Levy on imported newsprint from 1 January 2027 is also commendable, particularly for the newspaper and printing industries struggling with rising production costs.\n\nSimilarly, reductions in the Special Commodity Levy on imported sprats, Maldive fish and dried chillies are positive steps towards making essential food items more affordable. These measures indicate some recognition of the financial difficulties faced by ordinary people and businesses.\n\nHowever, beyond these concessions, the fundamental question remains: Has the 2027 Budget adequately addressed the economic difficulties confronting ordinary Sri Lankans? The answer must be examined against people\u2019s actual living conditions rather than the Government\u2019s financial projections, development promises, and announcements.\n\nThe continuing high prices of fuel, LP gas and electricity have created a chain reaction throughout the economy. Increased energy and transport costs inevitably push up the prices of food, consumer goods, construction materials, medicines and almost every other essential commodity. When businesses face higher operating expenses, they have little choice but to increase prices.\n\nConsumers, meanwhile, are left with less disposable income. Families are increasingly compelled to prioritise food, education, healthcare and utility bills while abandoning other spending."
  },
