    "md5": "ed69eed31773fbe64778a83cb49b4d09",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1485,
    "chunk_text": "The continuing high prices of fuel, LP gas and electricity have created a chain reaction throughout the economy. Increased energy and transport costs inevitably push up the prices of food, consumer goods, construction materials, medicines and almost every other essential commodity. When businesses face higher operating expenses, they have little choice but to increase prices.\n\nConsumers, meanwhile, are left with less disposable income. Families are increasingly compelled to prioritise food, education, healthcare and utility bills while abandoning other spending.\n\nA self-employed artist today needs at least Rs. 200,000 per month to support a family, pay utility bills, purchase groceries and consumer goods, and meet children\u2019s educational expenses. This is not necessarily a demand for a luxurious lifestyle. It reflects the actual financial requirements of maintaining a family under present economic conditions.\n\nThe Government should introduce a dedicated low-interest bank loan scheme for self-employed artists and small creative businesses. Such assistance would enable them to purchase equipment, improve production facilities, expand their services and develop sustainable businesses.\n\nAlthough concessionary financing for small and medium enterprises has been included in the Budget, the crucial issue is whether self-employed individuals and micro-enterprises can actually access these facilities without excessive collateral requirements and complicated procedures."
