    "md5": "00b3ad3f0129bc8244e6aa26badf79af",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1793,
    "chunk_text": "Although concessionary financing for small and medium enterprises has been included in the Budget, the crucial issue is whether self-employed individuals and micro-enterprises can actually access these facilities without excessive collateral requirements and complicated procedures.\n\nThe demands raised by the Government Medical Officers\u2019 Association also require serious consideration. These include extra-duty and holiday allowances, increases in disturbance, availability and transport allowances, revisions to Advance Personal Income Tax, and solutions to staffing and other professional benefit issues. Instead of allowing industrial disputes to continue, the Government should engage in meaningful negotiations with healthcare professionals. Equally important is the need to strengthen local pharmaceutical manufacturing. Sri Lanka must reduce its dependence on imported medicines and medical equipment by encouraging domestic production.\n\nAnother concern raised by sections of the public is that the Government appears to devote considerable attention to legislation, regulations and constitutional amendments, while the economic difficulties affecting ordinary people remain unresolved. The public does not expect political leaders to intervene personally in every investigation into corruption or unlawful activities.\n\nFighting corruption is essential, but it must be pursued through independent institutions and transparent legal procedures. It should not become a substitute for addressing the country\u2019s economic and social problems. The Attorney General\u2019s Department, the police, CIABOC and independent legal institutions have the responsibility to investigate wrongdoing and enforce the law.\n\nThere were no allocated provisions for long-delayed Provincial Council elections."
