    "chunk_text": "Fighting corruption is essential, but it must be pursued through independent institutions and transparent legal procedures. It should not become a substitute for addressing the country\u2019s economic and social problems. The Attorney General\u2019s Department, the police, CIABOC and independent legal institutions have the responsibility to investigate wrongdoing and enforce the law.\n\nThere were no allocated provisions for long-delayed Provincial Council elections.\n\nThe Government\u2019s primary responsibility should be to create an environment in which people can afford food, education, healthcare, transport and electricity. The Budget must focus on poverty reduction, affordable essential services and sustainable employment rather than political considerations.\n\nSri Lanka cannot achieve long-term prosperity merely by increasing taxation, borrowing money or depending on external assistance. The country must become a production-oriented economy capable of generating employment, exports and foreign exchange. Agriculture, fisheries, manufacturing, tourism, pharmaceuticals, technology and creative industries should receive greater attention. Local entrepreneurs must be encouraged to manufacture goods that are currently imported. Small businesses should receive affordable credit, technological assistance and access to markets.\n\nThe easing of the West Asian conflict following recent developments may reduce some external economic uncertainty. Nevertheless, Sri Lanka cannot depend on international developments to resolve its domestic economic difficulties. It must strengthen local production and build economic resilience.\n\nThe Government must also demonstrate that the substantial public investment announced in the Budget will produce measurable economic benefits rather than remain a collection of financial allocations and promises."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
