    "date_str": "2026-11-17",
    "description": "Budget 2027:  Political Promises Vs People\u2019s  Everyday Realities",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Budget-2027-Political-Promises-Vs-Peoples-Everyday-Realities/172-355084",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794898168.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-3a4af44e-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a1ff5ff30a243ebe52b1492ffbba6446",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 676,
    "chunk_text": "The Government must also demonstrate that the substantial public investment announced in the Budget will produce measurable economic benefits rather than remain a collection of financial allocations and promises.\n\nA Budget should not merely satisfy financial institutions, external stakeholders or government revenue targets. Its ultimate responsibility is to improve the living standards of the people and create a country in which families can live with dignity and economic security. The real success of the 2027 Budget will not be determined by how impressive it appears in Parliament, but by whether an ordinary Sri Lankan can feel a genuine improvement in everyday life."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-986835dc",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-986835dc",
    "date_str": "2026-11-17",
    "description": "Are we going for the 18th IMF programme?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-we-going-for-the-18th-IMF-programme/172-355086",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794898347.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-986835dc-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6b2cf2250880973214223919f33311b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1657,
    "chunk_text": "Are we going for the 18th IMF programme?\n\nDuring his 2027 budget speech,\u00a0 President Anura Kumara Dissanayake stated that a committee has been appointed to help take a decision on another International Monetary Fund (IMF) assisted programme once the current 17th programme\u00a0 comes to end in March 2027.\n\nThe President also said that the country is now moving from stability to economic transformation. One has to accept that the current Extended Fund Facility (EFF) programme of the IMF not only assisted\u00a0 the country to recover from the bankruptcy it had fallen into some four years ago.\n\nThe choice of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) by the Sri Lankan people at the 2024 Presidential election and the Parliamentary election, in place of the other traditional main political parties, was a dangerous decision, in the light of the NPP\u2019s policy towards the IMF. As a traditional leftist party, the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP)\u2014the core party in the NPP coalition\u2014had completely rejected the IMF and its programmes until just a few weeks before the elections. Yet their stance had shifted when they smelt victory.\n\nWhen they sensed the prospect of the first major electoral victory in their 60-year long history, pragmatism prevailed among the leadership of the JVP and the NPP. Two days prior to the Presidential election, NPP candidate Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced that they would continue with the IMF programme with modifications decided upon with the IMF officials. Imagine what the situation would be like if the NPP had decided otherwise, given the current fuel price hikes driven by external pressures like the West Asian conflict."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-986835dc",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-986835dc",
    "date_str": "2026-11-17",
    "description": "Are we going for the 18th IMF programme?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-we-going-for-the-18th-IMF-programme/172-355086",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794898347.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-17-dailymirrorlk-986835dc-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "17a66863b08c0391f2bd0d7e41f83a57",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "When they sensed the prospect of the first major electoral victory in their 60-year long history, pragmatism prevailed among the leadership of the JVP and the NPP. Two days prior to the Presidential election, NPP candidate Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced that they would continue with the IMF programme with modifications decided upon with the IMF officials. Imagine what the situation would be like if the NPP had decided otherwise, given the current fuel price hikes driven by external pressures like the West Asian conflict.\n\nIt was against this backdrop that President Gotabaya Rajapaksa approached the IMF for assistance in March 2022, following a long period of hesitation driven by the advice of certain Central Bank and Treasury officials. The IMF\u2019s formula was entirely its own, created without any local inputs. Debt restructuring and structural reforms constituted the main pillars of the IMF\u2019s current EFF arrangement, in line with the recommendations already put forward in the 2021 country report or 2021 Article IV Consultation of the IMF.\u00a0 A $2.9 billion financial aid package covering four years was agreed upon later, during staff-level discussions between the IMF and the Sri Lankan government.\n\nIn a press release by the Executive Board of the IMF on February 25, 2022, the urgency of implementing a credible and coherent strategy to restore macroeconomic stability and debt sustainability was stressed. It was also recommended that \u2018reforms should focus on strengthening VAT and income taxes, through rate increases and base broadening measures. Fiscal adjustment should be accompanied by energy pricing reforms to reduce fiscal risks from lossmaking public enterprises.\u2019 These recommendations were later agreed upon at the staff-level agreement and were implemented following the approval of the EFF by the Executive Board on March 20, 2023."
  },
