    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bdaa8544b4bbc9909d8e3a33f32a32ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1519,
    "chunk_text": "In a press release by the Executive Board of the IMF on February 25, 2022, the urgency of implementing a credible and coherent strategy to restore macroeconomic stability and debt sustainability was stressed. It was also recommended that \u2018reforms should focus on strengthening VAT and income taxes, through rate increases and base broadening measures. Fiscal adjustment should be accompanied by energy pricing reforms to reduce fiscal risks from lossmaking public enterprises.\u2019 These recommendations were later agreed upon at the staff-level agreement and were implemented following the approval of the EFF by the Executive Board on March 20, 2023.\n\nHowever,\u00a0 the country\u2019s needs have changed now. The President stated that the economy is now moving from stability to transformation. And in the meantime, Cyclone Ditwah and the West Asian war have underscored the urgency of having a backup strategy in the event of such eventualities, especially external shocks. These challenges would guide the deliberations of the committee that is examining the need for another IMF programme.\n\n\u2018Your Thought\u2019 is a space, a right of the readers to support or contradict and discuss the issues highlighted in the editorial and other articles in the editorial and op-ed pages. Designed as the reader\u2019s editorial; our readers can send in their writings, with a word count not exceeding 200, to \u2018Your Thought\u2019, Daily Mirror Political Features Desk, No 8, Hunupitiya Cross Road, Colombo 2 or email to [email\u00a0protected]"
  },
