    "chunk_text": "Former Deputy Excise Commissioner denied bail over Rs.21.17 Mn bribery allegations linked to W.M. Mendis\n\nColombo, Nov. 17 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Colombo Chief Magistrate Asanga S. Bodaragama, today refused bail to former Deputy Commissioner of Excise Channa Jayanath Alwis Weerakkody, who is being held over allegations of accepting bribes through cheques issued from the accounts of W.M. Mendis & Company.\n\nAppearing for the suspect, Senior Counsel Asoka Weerasooriya submitted that his client had no ability to influence any of the six matters outlined at the end of the first B report.\n\nCounsel further informed court that the suspect\u2019s daughter had been receiving psychiatric treatment for some time and that her condition had worsened following his arrest.\n\nHe also assured court that his client was willing to extend his fullest cooperation to the investigations in any manner required and sought bail on his behalf.\n\nThe investigation conducted by the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption relates to allegations that the suspect received 12 cheques amounting to Rs. 21.17 million from current account No. 805, maintained by W.M. Mendis & Company at the Bank of Ceylon\u2019s Corporate Branch, during 2015.\n\nInvestigations have revealed that the suspect had allegedly accepted cheques issued unlawfully from accounts belonging to W.M. Mendis & Company, a duly licensed entity that operated under his supervision while he was serving as the Excise Superintendent of Kalutara and Acting Excise Superintendent (Distilleries) in Aluthgama.\n\nThe investigation has also examined a series of cheques allegedly issued from the company\u2019s bank account.\n\nInvestigators also informed court that statements had to be recorded from the directors of W.M. Mendis & Company who had signed the relevant cheques.\n\nOfficers also informed court that statements needed to be recorded from the suspect\u2019s family members as part of the ongoing investigations."
  },
