    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "While the long-term impact of the strike on was not immediately clear, the bridge has been used to move troops and supplies into Crimea and towards the front line.\n\nThe railway section of the bridge appears to still be intact but it has been damaged previously.\n\nMonday night's strikes came a day after Ukraine began its most recent attempt to disable the Crimea bridge, in an attack which a Russian official said killed two and reportedly caused less significant structural damage.\n\nCrimea's Moscow-backed governor Sergei Aksyonov confirmed on Telegram that traffic over the bridge had been suspended.\n\nZelensky had warned of major attacks in the days leading up to a meeting between Putin, Donald Trump and Xi Jinping in China's Shenzhen on Thursday.\n\nMeanhile, loud explosions were heard in Kyiv just before midnight on Tuesday, with Klitschko saying shortly after that the city had suffered \"damage to critical infrastructure systems\" and disruption to heating and water.\n\nSome of Kyiv's above-ground subway services were damaged in the overnight attacks, officials said, after civilians were told to go to civil defence shelters.\n\nResidents of Kyiv typically shelter in the city's underground stations during air raids.\n\nEight major bridges span the Dnipro River connecting the western and eastern parts of Kyiv, and an estimated three million residents rely on the bridges to travel across the city.\n\nThese latest Russian strikes will cause major disruption, with traffic already reportedly building in the capital.\n\nElsewhere, three people have been injured in a Russian missile attack in the Cherkast region, according to local officials.\n\nThe latest round of tit-for-tat strikes came after US, Ukrainian and European officials held talks on ways to facilitate \"direct engagement\" between Russia and Europe to end the war."
