    "md5": "d95eeeedd5e3f9c74de5bc863875c544",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Japan sends up jets after alleged Russian incursion of its airspace\n\nBBC - Japan says it launched fighter jets and \"strongly\" protested after two suspected Russian aircraft violated its airspace early on Tuesday.\n\nSouth Korea also says it deployed fighter jets, while Taiwan says it \"responded\" to Russian military aircraft detected in their respective air defence identification zones.\n\nThese are unofficial buffer areas beyond territorial airspace where governments identify approaching aircraft for security purposes. Moscow has yet to comment.\n\nThe alleged incursions are a reminder of Russia's military power ahead of the Apec economic summit in China this week, which Vladimir Putin will attend alongside US President Donald Trump.\n\nJapan's Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara said the flight paths of the Russian aircraft, off the south-western Okinoshima island, were a \"serious infringement of Japan's sovereignty\" and \"completely unacceptable\".\n\nThe last such incursion was in 2024, when a Russian military patrol plane entered Japanese airspace off Rebun island, Hokkaido.\n\nTaiwan says its military \"responded accordingly\" after two Russian military aircraft entered its air defence identification zone, also on Tuesday.\n\nSouth Korea says it sent up jets after an unspecified number of Russian military aircraft entered its air defence identification zone over the Sea of Japan. The aircraft departed without violating South Korean airspace, authorities said.\n\nIt is unclear if the three neighbours were responding to the same or different aircraft.\n\nThe East China Sea contains self-declared, overlapping air defence identification zones between China, Taiwan, Japan and South Korea.\n\nForeign military aircraft are expected, but not legally required, to notify governments before entering such air defence identification zones.\n\nTensions are flaring between Japan and Russia, which are locked in a decades-old territorial dispute over a cluster of islands in the Pacific Ocean."
