    "md5": "f4808b0e93035a09dc8e6c6f2b916819",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "Netanyahu\u2019s and Eisenkot\u2019s parties met Sunday on the possibility of joining a unity government but the meeting appeared to be largely a formality, with both men saying they should be prime minister.\n\nNetanyahu, who has been urging US President Donald Trump to resume war on Iran, has talked up the threat from Tehran as he insists on the need for a unity government.\n\nIf Kahana takes charge, he is expected as soon as Wednesday to start the process of undoing much of the agenda of Netanyahu, Israel\u2019s longest serving prime minister who has been in and out of office for three decades.\n\nThe new parliament is expected first to move to rescind sweeping reforms to reduce the independence of the judiciary that were advanced by Netanyahu, who has been enraged over corruption charges against him that could land him in prison if he loses power.\n\nThe judicial reforms initiated shortly after Netanyahu began his latest term in December 2022 triggered some of Israel\u2019s largest ever protests, bringing tens of thousands to the streets, with liberals fearing an erosion of the country\u2019s democracy.\n\nThe movement was eventually overshadowed on October 7, 2023 when Hamas militants carried out the deadliest attack ever against Israel, which responded with a relentless retaliatory offensive in Gaza.\n\nEisenkot has also vowed to quickly set up a state commission of inquiry to investigate security failures that led to the October 7 attack.\n\nNetanyahu, who has built his brand on saying he will ensure Israel\u2019s security, has refused an independent probe, instead proposing one whose members would be chosen by politicians.\n\nKahana, 54, like Eisenkot is a veteran military leader. He served in a short-lived non-Netanyahu government in 2021 and 2022 as minister for religious services in which he clashed with ultra-Orthodox Jews by seeking to reduce the power of the Chief Rabbinate."
