    "description": "2027 Budget: Can the financial sector turn policy ambitions into economic growth?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/2027-Budget-Can-the-financial-sector-turn-policy-ambitions-into-economic-growth/4-800214",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794964055.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-ae498395-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6f015d3ee6cee7f62e97881bad749402",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: Can the financial sector turn policy ambitions into economic growth?\n\nBanks are expected to mobilise Rs. 174 billion in concessionary lending, but the real test will be whether that money generates productive investment, exports and sustainable employment without compromising financial stability\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget places considerable emphasis on investment, enterprise development, exports and the revival of the rural economy. Yet, behind many of these ambitions lies an institution whose contribution could determine whether the proposals translate into meaningful economic outcomes by the financial sector.\n\nThe banking and financial system is expected to play a much wider role than simply providing loans. It must mobilise domestic savings, direct capital towards productive activities, support businesses, facilitate international trade, manage financial risks and help channel private investment into the economy.\n\nOne of the most significant financial-sector initiatives in the Budget is the allocation of Rs. 67.687 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing, with the expectation of generating approximately Rs. 174 billion in concessionary lending through State and private banks.\n\nThis represents a substantial intervention in the credit market. The Government reported that the Rs. 54.136 billion allocated for similar purposes in 2026 supported Rs. 97.1 billion in lending. The higher target for 2027 therefore signals an intention to use the banking system more aggressively as an instrument of economic recovery and transformation.\n\nHowever, the central question is not simply how much money banks will lend. It is whether that financing will create additional productive capacity, strengthen businesses, generate employment and improve the country\u2019s foreign-exchange earnings."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-ae498395",
    "num": "dailyftlk-ae498395",
    "date_str": "2026-11-18",
    "description": "2027 Budget: Can the financial sector turn policy ambitions into economic growth?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/2027-Budget-Can-the-financial-sector-turn-policy-ambitions-into-economic-growth/4-800214",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794964055.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-ae498395-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "01676b440296d67dcf357d6530b8a4c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1778,
    "chunk_text": "However, the central question is not simply how much money banks will lend. It is whether that financing will create additional productive capacity, strengthen businesses, generate employment and improve the country\u2019s foreign-exchange earnings.\n\nThe interest rates proposed for selected programs are significantly below prevailing market lending rates. The average weighted rate on new SME lending was 13.17% in September 2026, according to the Budget Economic and Fiscal Position Report. Against this background, concessionary rates of 3, 4 or 5% could provide meaningful relief to businesses facing high financing costs.\n\nFor many small enterprises, access to finance remains a fundamental constraint. Businesses may have viable products, established markets and opportunities for expansion but lack sufficient collateral, financial records or the credit history required by conventional lending models.\n\nBanks therefore need to move beyond an approach that relies excessively on fixed-asset collateral. Cash-flow-based lending, sector-specific credit assessment and the use of reliable digital transaction records could help identify viable businesses that remain outside the formal credit system.\n\nThe effectiveness of these programs will also depend on the quality of the lending process. Subsidised interest rates should not become a reason to relax credit assessment. Banks must continue to evaluate repayment capacity, business viability and the intended use of funds.\n\nA successful program should ultimately produce stronger enterprises and healthier loan portfolios, rather than merely higher disbursement figures.\n\nThe role of banks becomes even more important when the Budget\u2019s financing initiatives are considered against Sri Lanka\u2019s external-sector position."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-ae498395",
    "num": "dailyftlk-ae498395",
    "date_str": "2026-11-18",
    "description": "2027 Budget: Can the financial sector turn policy ambitions into economic growth?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/2027-Budget-Can-the-financial-sector-turn-policy-ambitions-into-economic-growth/4-800214",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794964055.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-ae498395-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c2e78ce27091419f6155561798d52e7e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "A successful program should ultimately produce stronger enterprises and healthier loan portfolios, rather than merely higher disbursement figures.\n\nThe role of banks becomes even more important when the Budget\u2019s financing initiatives are considered against Sri Lanka\u2019s external-sector position.\n\nThese figures underline the importance of directing credit towards activities that strengthen the country\u2019s capacity to earn foreign exchange.\n\nBanks can support this objective through pre-shipment and post-shipment finance, export receivables financing, documentary trade instruments, supply-chain finance and foreign-exchange risk management.\n\nThe fisheries sector, agro-processing, food manufacturing, high-value manufacturing, logistics and tourism-related businesses offer opportunities for financing that can contribute to export earnings or reduce dependence on imported products.\n\nNevertheless, import financing should not be treated as inherently unproductive. Machinery, technology and intermediate goods can increase domestic production and future export capacity. The critical distinction is whether borrowed funds contribute to sustainable economic value.\n\nThe banking sector should therefore assess the economic purpose of financing rather than simply categorising transactions as import-related or export-related.\n\nAlthough commercial banks are assigned a prominent role in the concessionary lending programs, Sri Lanka\u2019s financial-sector contribution should not be confined to traditional bank lending.\n\nLicensed finance companies and leasing institutions can provide financing for machinery, commercial vehicles, equipment, renewable-energy systems and other productive assets. These institutions can serve smaller businesses that may not always fit conventional banking products."
  },
