    "md5": "e760f2e607f2d7d4b618097859e569f4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "However, encouraging SMEs to list requires more than financial incentives. Businesses need support in accounting, corporate governance, auditing, valuation, legal compliance and investor relations. Market liquidity and investor confidence are equally important.\n\nThe Budget\u2019s ambition to expand affordable credit must be considered alongside the prevailing macroeconomic environment.\n\nThe Fiscal Position Report records inflation of 8.3% in October 2026, while the Central Bank\u2019s Overnight Policy Rate was increased by 100 basis points to 8.75% in May and maintained at that level in September. The average weighted new lending rate stood at 12.65% in September.\n\nAt the same time, private-sector credit expanded by 23.6% year-on-year by the end of September 2026, with outstanding credit reaching approximately Rs. 11.77 trillion.\n\nThis indicates that credit demand is already growing substantially. While that is encouraging for economic recovery, rapid credit expansion must be accompanied by strong underwriting, appropriate provisioning and effective portfolio monitoring.\n\nThere is also a potential tension between subsidised lending and the cost of funds. Banks must be confident that interest subsidies and refinancing arrangements will be funded and reimbursed on time. Otherwise, delayed payments could affect lending margins, liquidity planning and the willingness of institutions to participate.\n\nImportantly, an interest subsidy should not automatically be interpreted as a Government guarantee against borrower default. The respective responsibilities of the Treasury and lending institutions must be clearly defined.\n\nAnother issue is the relationship between Government borrowing and private-sector financing. The Fiscal Position Report places gross public debt at approximately Rs. 32,977 billion at the end of June 2026. Treasury Bonds represented 83.8% of central government domestic debt at the end of August."
  },
