    "md5": "96d37b09b9ddcae9f70b652e26e6adea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Another issue is the relationship between Government borrowing and private-sector financing. The Fiscal Position Report places gross public debt at approximately Rs. 32,977 billion at the end of June 2026. Treasury Bonds represented 83.8% of central government domestic debt at the end of August.\n\nDespite the breadth of the proposed concessionary lending initiatives, several aspects of the program architecture require greater clarity.\n\nThe Budget identifies an overall expectation of Rs. 174 billion in concessionary lending. However, the individual program amounts described in the Speech add up to approximately Rs. 140 billion based on the stated figures. A program-by-program reconciliation would help explain the difference, including any existing allocations, overlaps or additional financing components.\n\nMore importantly, the published proposals would benefit from a consolidated framework setting out borrower eligibility, participating institutions, lending conditions, subsidy reimbursement arrangements, credit-risk allocation and performance-reporting requirements.\n\nThe distinction between additional lending and the relabelling of ordinary bank lending is also important. Public funds should generate financing that would not otherwise have been available on comparable terms.\n\n(The author has an academic background in finance, economics and applied statistics, holding a degree in Finance and Investments from the University of South Wales, an MA in Economics and a Postgraduate Diploma in Applied Statistics from the University of Colombo, and a BA in Economics from the University of Peradeniya. The author is also associated with the Association of Chartered Certified Accountants (ACCA), UK)\n\nPerformance should therefore be assessed through indicators such as new businesses financed, additional investment generated, jobs created and sustained, export earnings, productivity improvements, repayment performance and the fiscal cost of each program."
