    "md5": "9b71686411816eae7611c0d216bce6da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1960,
    "chunk_text": "Performance should therefore be assessed through indicators such as new businesses financed, additional investment generated, jobs created and sustained, export earnings, productivity improvements, repayment performance and the fiscal cost of each program.\n\nThe need for financial resilience is also highlighted by the estimated Rs. 1,022.5 billion required for Cyclone Ditwah recovery and reconstruction over three years, including an additional expenditure requirement of approximately Rs. 260 billion in 2027. Such events demonstrate why climate-risk assessment, insurance coverage, business continuity and disaster-resilient lending must become integral parts of financial-sector planning.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s financial sector now faces an opportunity to become a more active partner in national economic transformation. But this requires a shift in emphasis from the volume of credit disbursed to the economic value created through that credit.\n\nThe Government should establish a consolidated concessionary-credit framework, publish clear subsidy and risk-sharing arrangements, and introduce a common reporting mechanism covering participating financial institutions. Independent evaluations after 12 and 24 months would help establish whether the programs are delivering genuine additionality.\n\nBanks, meanwhile, should strengthen sector-specific lending strategies, expand cash-flow-based financing, support export-oriented enterprises and maintain rigorous risk management. Finance companies, leasing institutions, insurers and capital-market participants should also be recognised as complementary sources of finance and risk protection.\n\nUltimately, the 2027 Budget will not succeed merely because banks achieve a Rs. 174 billion lending target. Its success will depend on whether that financing creates productive businesses, improves competitiveness, generates sustainable employment, strengthens foreign-exchange earnings and remains financially sound."
