    "md5": "55e36ea32ca2bf4a53f61f39b06ddc3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1779,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 gives Sri Lanka\u2019s exporters room to grow: An exporters perspective\n\nFor a Sri Lankan manufacturer, winning a first overseas order can take months. Missing the vessel by a day can undo much of that work. Having spent much of my career in export manufacturing, I read the 2027 Budget, presented by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake on 12 November, with that reality in mind. Can it help more Sri Lankan businesses win orders, produce at a competitive cost and deliver on time?\n\nThe President and the Government deserve credit for addressing several of the practical constraints that determine whether an exporter can do all three. The proposals on trade facilitation, VAT eligibility and investment in manufacturing are especially welcome. Their value will be measured in time saved, cash released for production and new orders won.\n\nThe National Single Window for Trade is one of the Budget\u2019s most practical reforms. A pilot for selected exports began on 5 November. The Government plans to bring exporters onto the system in the first half of 2027, followed by imports and re-exports in the second half, with nearly Rs. 2 billion allocated for the rollout. A single point of submission can reduce the time businesses spend sending the same information to several agencies. For a small exporter with a shipment deadline, being able to see where an application stands is valuable in itself.\n\nThe proposal to modernise the Temporary Importation for Export Processing scheme deserves attention for similar reasons. Many local manufacturers need specialised inputs from abroad to produce finished goods here. Simpler access to those inputs under the scheme\u2019s tax concessions can help smaller firms take on export orders that would otherwise stretch their resources."
  },
