    "md5": "c215e003af43f3acbeff1ce20dd7ba70",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1167,
    "chunk_text": "Can new investment promotion agency boost FDI beyond $ 2 b?\n\nThe President's Budget 2027 announced a proposal to set up a new company dedicated to promoting investment is a welcome step. It is to be wholly-owned by the Treasury, staffed by professionals with private sector and international experience, and offer market-competitive salaries. A bit like \u2018GovTech\u2019 today, and similar to \u2018InvestSL\u2019 envisaged in the Economic Transformation Act (2024).\n\nThe announcement is a timely recognition that the current arrangement has not served Sri Lanka well. Annual average FDI inflows have languished around $ 1.2 billion over the past decade, and in the last five years it barely even reached $ 1 billion a year (CBSL Macroeconomic Chart Pack, 14 January 2026). The Board of Investment has long been asked to be promoter of investment, regulator and facilitator of entry, approver of incentives, and landlord of zones all at once. An agency that must court investors while also policing them, and bound by public sector pay scales and procedures, inevitably struggles to compete with many of the more agile, specialised promotion agencies seen in the rest of Asia."
  },
