    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2335b914b738ee9adf06244b7236b606",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "The announcement is a timely recognition that the current arrangement has not served Sri Lanka well. Annual average FDI inflows have languished around $ 1.2 billion over the past decade, and in the last five years it barely even reached $ 1 billion a year (CBSL Macroeconomic Chart Pack, 14 January 2026). The Board of Investment has long been asked to be promoter of investment, regulator and facilitator of entry, approver of incentives, and landlord of zones all at once. An agency that must court investors while also policing them, and bound by public sector pay scales and procedures, inevitably struggles to compete with many of the more agile, specialised promotion agencies seen in the rest of Asia.\n\nBeyond the announcement, what will matter next is how the new agency is designed. Two questions deserve careful thought.\n\nFirst, how will the agency attract the right people? Competitive pay is necessary but not sufficient. The roles must offer a good amount of autonomy and a chance to work on deals that matter, or the best candidates won\u2019t be interested to come. Nor should it be assumed that \u201ca private sector background\u201d alone makes someone the right fit. Selling a country to investors is a distinct skill, needing people who understand both how investors decide and how Government works. The agency should also be open to hiring foreign nationals, including market specialists from the countries Sri Lanka most wants to attract investment from. They can bring networks, language and an insider's understanding of how firms there make decisions. I recall how our approach and messaging to a Japanese investor audience during an investment promotion mission to Tokyo was greatly enriched and refined because the BOI had an in-house Japanese national advisor stationed from JETRO at that time."
  },
