    "md5": "e62350491b7c6f6e4da960531a7e8514",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1503,
    "chunk_text": "First, how will the agency attract the right people? Competitive pay is necessary but not sufficient. The roles must offer a good amount of autonomy and a chance to work on deals that matter, or the best candidates won\u2019t be interested to come. Nor should it be assumed that \u201ca private sector background\u201d alone makes someone the right fit. Selling a country to investors is a distinct skill, needing people who understand both how investors decide and how Government works. The agency should also be open to hiring foreign nationals, including market specialists from the countries Sri Lanka most wants to attract investment from. They can bring networks, language and an insider's understanding of how firms there make decisions. I recall how our approach and messaging to a Japanese investor audience during an investment promotion mission to Tokyo was greatly enriched and refined because the BOI had an in-house Japanese national advisor stationed from JETRO at that time.\n\nA new, dedicated investment promotion agency is a necessary adjustment to the institutional architecture to drive FDI, but not a sufficient one on its own. An investor's interest converts to actual investment in the country only after land has been allocated, incentives are clarified, approvals have been granted, and operations begin. That is where Sri Lanka has too often lost investors. A recent Auditor General's report was quite revealing, in that regard (as reported in The Morning newspaper of 15 November 2026)."
