    "md5": "b34e4b33ef00c62ab490845f7462c643",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "A new, dedicated investment promotion agency is a necessary adjustment to the institutional architecture to drive FDI, but not a sufficient one on its own. An investor's interest converts to actual investment in the country only after land has been allocated, incentives are clarified, approvals have been granted, and operations begin. That is where Sri Lanka has too often lost investors. A recent Auditor General's report was quite revealing, in that regard (as reported in The Morning newspaper of 15 November 2026).\n\nThis is why the Economic Transformation Act did not stop at a promotions agency. It envisaged Invest SL as one part of a wider institutional overhaul, working alongside a high-powered Economic Commission that would replace the Board of Investment and take charge of facilitating investment, from approvals and incentives to coordinating across Government. Such a holistic reform is needed to avoid this new promotional agency becoming a well-paid front office for a back office that cannot deliver results.\n\nEqually, investment promotion cannot sit apart from strategic thinking on which sectors will transform the economy. The ETA of 2024 recognised this. It tasked the Economic Commission with formulating national investment and trade policy together (section 32), determining investments that are of strategic importance (section 12), and ensuring that trade negotiations are aligned with the country's strategic export and investment objectives (section 14).\n\nSri Lanka is on a stronger macroeconomic footing today, and averages of FDI inflows in the recent past need not be an indication of future prospects. A new agency can help lift FDI well beyond the current $ 1.2 billion average, to $ 2 billion and beyond, but only if promotion and facilitation are fixed together, and promotion is linked to larger strategic economic goals."
