    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: Navigating Sri Lanka\u2019s pivot from stabilisation to transformation\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake presents 2027 Budget in\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 National Budget, presented on 12 November 2026, by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake in his capacity as of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, represents a decisive inflection point in the nation\u2019s post-crisis economic trajectory. Framed explicitly as an intentional pivot \u201cfrom economic stability towards transformation,\u201d the Budget seeks to transition the economy from four years of painful austerity and macroeconomic stabilisation into a phase of high-velocity, investment-led expansion. Crucially, this shift occurs at a moment of profound institutional transition: the scheduled March 2027 conclusion of Sri Lanka\u2019s four-year International Monetary Fund (IMF) Extended Fund Facility (EFF) program. Crafting a transformative Budget amidst lingering structural fragilities requires walking a macroeconomic tightrope. The\n\nRs. 2 trillion public capital expenditure drive while simultaneously absorbing severe exogenous shocks\u2014most notably the economic devastation of Cyclone Ditwah, which inflicted $ 3.4 billion in estimated damages, elevated global crude oil prices driven by Middle Eastern geopolitical conflict, and the looming threat of an El Ni\u00f1o peak in early 2027. For policymakers, international investors, and the broader public, the 2027 Budget is far more than an annual accounting exercise; it is a stress test of whether Sri Lanka has genuinely broken free from its historical cycle of twin deficits, debt distress, and policy slippage, or whether the post-IMF era risks reintroducing the very vulnerabilities that triggered the 2022 sovereign default."
