    "chunk_text": "Rs. 2 trillion public capital expenditure drive while simultaneously absorbing severe exogenous shocks\u2014most notably the economic devastation of Cyclone Ditwah, which inflicted $ 3.4 billion in estimated damages, elevated global crude oil prices driven by Middle Eastern geopolitical conflict, and the looming threat of an El Ni\u00f1o peak in early 2027. For policymakers, international investors, and the broader public, the 2027 Budget is far more than an annual accounting exercise; it is a stress test of whether Sri Lanka has genuinely broken free from its historical cycle of twin deficits, debt distress, and policy slippage, or whether the post-IMF era risks reintroducing the very vulnerabilities that triggered the 2022 sovereign default.\n\nFiscal sustainability in Sri Lanka has historically been undermined by quasi-fiscal losses generated by State-Owned Enterprises (SOEs). The 2027 Budget builds upon a remarkable turnaround across 52 key SOEs, which shifted from a catastrophic aggregate loss of Rs. 774.6 billion in 2022 to a pre-tax profit of Rs. 444.4 billion in 2025 through cost-reflective pricing and balance sheet restructuring. To prevent remaining loss-makers from eroding fiscal space, the Government has appointed an expert committee to restructure Sri Lankan Airlines\u2014burdened by Rs. 528 billion in accumulated losses, while opening Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport to international investor proposals. Equally critical is the strict institutional firewall separating fiscal policy from monetary operations. Following the October 2026 inflation surge to 8.3%, the Budget reaffirms absolute adherence to the Central Bank of Sri Lanka\u2019s (CBSL) statutory price-stability mandate under the Central Bank Act, explicitly ruling out monetary financing of the fiscal deficit. Maintaining cost-recovery energy pricing and central bank independence will be the ultimate litmus test of institutional maturity once IMF quarterly reviews conclude in March 2027."
