    "chunk_text": "Fiscal sustainability in Sri Lanka has historically been undermined by quasi-fiscal losses generated by State-Owned Enterprises (SOEs). The 2027 Budget builds upon a remarkable turnaround across 52 key SOEs, which shifted from a catastrophic aggregate loss of Rs. 774.6 billion in 2022 to a pre-tax profit of Rs. 444.4 billion in 2025 through cost-reflective pricing and balance sheet restructuring. To prevent remaining loss-makers from eroding fiscal space, the Government has appointed an expert committee to restructure Sri Lankan Airlines\u2014burdened by Rs. 528 billion in accumulated losses, while opening Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport to international investor proposals. Equally critical is the strict institutional firewall separating fiscal policy from monetary operations. Following the October 2026 inflation surge to 8.3%, the Budget reaffirms absolute adherence to the Central Bank of Sri Lanka\u2019s (CBSL) statutory price-stability mandate under the Central Bank Act, explicitly ruling out monetary financing of the fiscal deficit. Maintaining cost-recovery energy pricing and central bank independence will be the ultimate litmus test of institutional maturity once IMF quarterly reviews conclude in March 2027.\n\nExogenous shocks: Navigating oil volatility and the 2027 El Ni\u00f1o\n\n(The author served as the Special Adviser to the Office of the President of Namibia from 2006 to 2012 and was a Senior Consultant with the UNDP for 20 years. He was a Senior Economist with the Central Bank of Sri Lanka [1972-1993]. He can be reached via [email\u00a0protected])"
