    "md5": "41f4df4c0eacbe3f4cd7494b565bd390",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Policy sets direction. Who takes responsibility for implementing?\n\nMost who know me will know that much of my working life has been spent in senior corporate management, particularly in the tourism industry, setting business strategy, developing action plans and budgets, and leading operational teams responsible for carrying them through. We were responsible for delivering results, and explaining why something had not happened, and what course corrections we made to rectify the situation.\n\nAfter retirement, for close upon seven years now, I have been working as a consultant to a leading development bank in Sri Lanka. This has required me to learn a different set of processes and appreciate the complexities of working with public institutions. In all honesty, it has not been easy, and some aspects still frustrate me. I do realise that the Government has responsibilities and constraints that a business does not, and it would be unreasonable to judge every public-sector decision by corporate standards. Even so, I find myself repeatedly asking what happens between agreeing on a desirable outcome and actually delivering it.\n\nOver these years, my colleague and teammate, who is a trained professional in development, and I have had many discussions about public policy, its purpose and whether it ultimately achieves what it sets out to do, particularly in the Sri Lankan context. Those exchanges have made me question some of my own assumptions, but they have also sharpened my concern about the importance attached to formulating policies, compared with the effort devoted to implementing them.\n\nSo I am going to venture into unfamiliar waters and approach this subject from a management perspective. What follows is a reflection from someone whose working instincts were formed in corporate management and who is still trying to understand why an approved policy so often appears to receive more recognition than a result delivered."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "date_str": "2026-11-18",
    "description": "Policy sets direction. Who takes responsibility for implementing?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Policy-sets-direction-Who-takes-responsibility-for-implementing/4-800219",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794967535.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1aa7fcb59dce48d96d12594e47b64bc1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "So I am going to venture into unfamiliar waters and approach this subject from a management perspective. What follows is a reflection from someone whose working instincts were formed in corporate management and who is still trying to understand why an approved policy so often appears to receive more recognition than a result delivered.\n\nGovernment needs an agreed direction that can outlast an individual minister. China\u2019s successive five-year plans, for example, have kept industrial upgrading as a long-term priority while allowing the measures used to pursue it to change. In the United States, on the other hand, the federal clean vehicle tax credits expanded under Biden were brought to an early end under legislation signed by Trump in 2025.\n\nThe lesson for Sri Lanka, I think, is to seek broad agreement on a few long-term priorities and give them enough time to work, while remaining willing to change the approach when the evidence calls for it. My concern is where policy approval sits in the journey to achieving results. I have tried to make a comparison to illustrate the stages that I think must be followed in both the corporate and public sectors.\n\nIn business, a strategic plan normally leads to an action plan, tested against available resources and translated into a budget. Responsibilities are assigned and performance reviewed.\n\nGovernment has a more complicated task. It must reconcile competing interests (such as immigration/security giving opposing visa free options), respect legal rights and operate through multiple institutions with separate mandates. Cabinet approval does not automatically provide funding, amend legislation or give one ministry authority over another. These complications make a clear implementation plan more necessary. The sequence remains recognisable: policy direction, a delivery strategy, a budget action plan, funding and authorisations, assigned responsibility, execution and review."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "date_str": "2026-11-18",
    "description": "Policy sets direction. Who takes responsibility for implementing?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Policy-sets-direction-Who-takes-responsibility-for-implementing/4-800219",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794967535.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bff26124112a0a8088cb81d9a6971972",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Government has a more complicated task. It must reconcile competing interests (such as immigration/security giving opposing visa free options), respect legal rights and operate through multiple institutions with separate mandates. Cabinet approval does not automatically provide funding, amend legislation or give one ministry authority over another. These complications make a clear implementation plan more necessary. The sequence remains recognisable: policy direction, a delivery strategy, a budget action plan, funding and authorisations, assigned responsibility, execution and review.\n\nAnnouncing the destination without organising the means of getting there is precisely the weakness that concerns me. If we were going to encourage solar generation on this scale, we should have planned, managed the roll out, and funded the work needed to accommodate it on the grid at the same time.\n\nRegulations are one means of giving effect to policy. They can establish legally enforceable requirements, but they are not substitutes for operating capacity. A rule requiring proper waste disposal will achieve little where collection is unreliable, inspections are absent or enforcement is selective. A policy, a regulation and a functioning public service each perform a different task.\n\nThere is substantial research behind this concern. Matt Andrews, Lant Pritchett and Michael Woolcock, in their 2012 paper Escaping Capability Traps and their subsequent book Building State Capability, describe Governments adopting the outward appearance of effective institutions without acquiring their ability to perform.\n\nThey call this \u201cisomorphic mimicry\u201d. New structures, procedures and policy frameworks can demonstrate that reform is taking place, even when the service experienced by the public scarcely changes. (Andrews, Pritchett and Woolcock, 2012; 2017)"
  },
