    "md5": "199f4956b0a364a052b1fa9358a7dd7e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1682,
    "chunk_text": "They call this \u201cisomorphic mimicry\u201d. New structures, procedures and policy frameworks can demonstrate that reform is taking place, even when the service experienced by the public scarcely changes. (Andrews, Pritchett and Woolcock, 2012; 2017)\n\nThe National Export Strategy received Cabinet approval in 2018 and was launched that July. It aimed to diversify exports and increase export revenue to US$28 billion by 2022. (Export Development Board, 2018a)\n\nAccording to the Export Development Board\u2019s own account, it identified actions, lead and supporting implementation partners, starting and finishing dates, tentative costs, checkpoints and milestones. It was explicitly designed to maintain continuity through political changes.\n\nOn 20 May 2024, after its 2018\u20132022 implementation period had ended, the Daily Mirror published \u201cNES stuck in a NEST\u201d. The commentary argued that ministries and institutional heads remained unaware of the documents and questioned who was responsible for advancing its logistics component. These are the writer of the article\u2019s assessments, rather than findings of an independent implementation audit. (Daily Mirror, 2024)\n\nSome implementation did occur: the Ministry\u2019s 2024 progress report records EDB initiatives under NES priority sectors. The criticism therefore concerns incomplete and poorly coordinated delivery, rather than total inactivity. (Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development, 2024)\n\nTake for example the recent budget proposals. It is reported that out of Rs. 1,380 billion allocated for capital projects in 2026, only about Rs. 385 billion (roughly 28%) was actually spent through September (EconomyNext)"
  },
