    "md5": "4cb3b9269b85852625e5a5006602204c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "Take for example the recent budget proposals. It is reported that out of Rs. 1,380 billion allocated for capital projects in 2026, only about Rs. 385 billion (roughly 28%) was actually spent through September (EconomyNext)\n\nCorruption affects which projects are chosen, who receives contracts and whose breaches are overlooked. Extending his principle to performance reporting, I would argue that institutions also need the freedom to acknowledge failure. A review cannot correct asprogram if everyone involved feels obliged to describe it as progressing satisfactorily, painting a rosy picture.\n\nAt the same time, we should resist turning Singapore into a simple formula for Sri Lanka. Its experience deserves study, but meritocracy, pragmatism and honesty do not automatically settle questions about public accountability, unequal opportunity or the rights of affected communities. Citizens must be able to question a decision even when its authors consider it efficient.\n\nIt is tempting to attribute the whole difficulty to bureaucracy. Excessive procedure, repeated referrals and reluctance to decide can certainly obstruct delivery. An official may be asked to carry out a commitment without sufficient funds or legal authority. A minister may support reform until it affects an influential constituency, while a transparent approval system may threaten those who benefit from discretion. In such circumstances, delay can reflect political resistance rather than administrative confusion.\n\nThe World Bank\u2019s World Development Report 2017 makes this point. Policy effectiveness depends on credible commitments, coordination and cooperation, while unequal power and vested interests can undermine them. (World Bank, 2017)\n\nApplied to our circumstances, it raises a question that receives too little attention at the launch of a policy: who is capable of carrying this through, and will that person have the authority to do so?"
  },
  {
