    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "29e57a5fc85f57b1033d2cd114d5b105",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1792,
    "chunk_text": "Applied to our circumstances, it raises a question that receives too little attention at the launch of a policy: who is capable of carrying this through, and will that person have the authority to do so?\n\nA management team would be questioned about its inability to execute a business strategy and meet its agreed performance targets. The public sector needs equally serious attention to delivery capability, alongside the safeguards appropriate to public office. Its bureaucracy must be able to turn political intent into reliable administration.\n\nEven competent public servants can spend much of their time securing routine clearances or sending the same file from one office to another. Yet a decision that has been held up for months can sometimes move in days when someone with political influence intervenes. There are safeguards that public administration must retain, but there is a problem when getting ordinary work done depends on who can pick up the phone. The task is to simplify procedures that serve little purpose and give officials the authority to make routine decisions, with clear accountability for how they use it.\n\nSri Lanka does not lack institutions intended to monitor Government work. The Treasury\u2019s Department of Project Management and Monitoring has responsibilities for reviewing major development initiatives, reporting performance and undertaking evaluations. Cabinet also approved arrangements in December 2023 for implementing the National Evaluation Policy from 2024, including a National Evaluation Steering Committee. (Department of Project Management and Monitoring, n.d.; Cabinet of Ministers, 2023)\n\nThe Auditor General and parliamentary committees provide further scrutiny. The relevant question is what happens after a weakness has been identified."
  },
