    "md5": "651263e3f7d4833119e73088307b8c70",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1547,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka does not lack institutions intended to monitor Government work. The Treasury\u2019s Department of Project Management and Monitoring has responsibilities for reviewing major development initiatives, reporting performance and undertaking evaluations. Cabinet also approved arrangements in December 2023 for implementing the National Evaluation Policy from 2024, including a National Evaluation Steering Committee. (Department of Project Management and Monitoring, n.d.; Cabinet of Ministers, 2023)\n\nThe Auditor General and parliamentary committees provide further scrutiny. The relevant question is what happens after a weakness has been identified.\n\nLast year\u2019s island-wide power-outage prompted inquiries, committees and reports. Recently there was another outage and investigations are again under way. The cause appears to be the same- unreliable sources of generation, an unstable grid with inadequate controls for safety. But the public is entitled to know what corrective measures were recommended after the earlier incident, which were implemented, and whether they worked as intended. Commissioning another inquiry cannot substitute for accounting for the findings of the previous one. It illustrates the distinction between reporting a problem and resolving it. A meeting can receive an explanation for a delay, record concern and request another report without changing the conditions causing the delay. Follow-up has value only when it produces a decision, assigns responsibility and establishes whether the correction was made."
  },
