    "md5": "7da7021e4bac86a3c70c1186629061fb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "Last year\u2019s island-wide power-outage prompted inquiries, committees and reports. Recently there was another outage and investigations are again under way. The cause appears to be the same- unreliable sources of generation, an unstable grid with inadequate controls for safety. But the public is entitled to know what corrective measures were recommended after the earlier incident, which were implemented, and whether they worked as intended. Commissioning another inquiry cannot substitute for accounting for the findings of the previous one. It illustrates the distinction between reporting a problem and resolving it. A meeting can receive an explanation for a delay, record concern and request another report without changing the conditions causing the delay. Follow-up has value only when it produces a decision, assigns responsibility and establishes whether the correction was made.\n\nPublishing a policy, conducting workshops or launching a promotional campaign may contribute to that effort, but none, by itself, demonstrates that we have achieved higher yield. Someone must remain answerable for whether the intended result actually follows.\n\nAn implementation plan should therefore settle responsibility and resources early. It needs realistic costs, clarity about what depends on whom, and a way to resolve obstacles beyond the lead agency\u2019s authority. This means involving the agencies, businesses and communities whose cooperation is needed while the plan is still being drawn up. They can identify practical difficulties and help establish what each can realistically contribute. That engagement should continue during implementation, with reviews examining whether the intended service or benefit is reaching people, alongside expenditure and schedules."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98",
