    "md5": "5ea07c5b6f50d6ae4b9131607bbc385c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1720,
    "chunk_text": "An implementation plan should therefore settle responsibility and resources early. It needs realistic costs, clarity about what depends on whom, and a way to resolve obstacles beyond the lead agency\u2019s authority. This means involving the agencies, businesses and communities whose cooperation is needed while the plan is still being drawn up. They can identify practical difficulties and help establish what each can realistically contribute. That engagement should continue during implementation, with reviews examining whether the intended service or benefit is reaching people, alongside expenditure and schedules.\n\nOne practical response would be to require a clear delivery arrangement for every major policy. Where implementation crosses several ministries, a named programs director should lead a small dedicated team, with staff seconded for a defined period and their normal duties reassigned.\u00a0 This is important for continuity, without having ad-hoc ministry officials participating at different meetings.\n\nThe team should have a clear action plan, with budgets and deliverables, delegated authority, deadlines and measurable outcomes. Funding must cover implementation and continuing operations. Holding someone responsible without giving them the authority and resources to act is hardly a credible management arrangement.\n\nA small inter-ministerial steering committee, backed by an accountable political sponsor, should resolve obstacles beyond the team\u2019s authority. Scheduled reviews must produce decisions, with corrective actions assigned to named individuals and completion dates. Progress should be measured against benefits delivered to the public, with independent verification where appropriate."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2add0d98",
    "date_str": "2026-11-18",
    "description": "Policy sets direction. Who takes responsibility for implementing?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Policy-sets-direction-Who-takes-responsibility-for-implementing/4-800219",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1794967535.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailyftlk-2add0d98-0009",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4958811a221d5d8e1d50f20c2417f4ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "A small inter-ministerial steering committee, backed by an accountable political sponsor, should resolve obstacles beyond the team\u2019s authority. Scheduled reviews must produce decisions, with corrective actions assigned to named individuals and completion dates. Progress should be measured against benefits delivered to the public, with independent verification where appropriate.\n\nThere is also the question of whether the policy itself is sound.\n\nEvidence-informed policymaking means examining the problem, comparing possible responses and using research, practical experience and evaluation to guide decisions (OECD, 2020). In tourism, the evidence available to do this varies considerably.\n\nWe may know how many visitors arrive in the country, yet not know\u00a0 about what they spend in a particular destination, what are their primary needs or why they stay for shorter periods. Without that information, it is easy to prescribe more promotion when the real problem may be poor access or too little of the correct type of visitors. Sometimes the first step in making policy is to have good data and finding out what it really tells us.\n\nComing from corporate life, I would regard much of this as ordinary management discipline: establish why an intervention should work, test the assumptions and check the results. A capable team can execute a poorly conceived policy and still waste public money. Equally, excellent research cannot compensate for the absence of authority, funding or responsibility for delivery. We need both, with the willingness to change course when the results fail to support the original assumptions.\n\nMy reservation is about how enthusiastic we are to give credit for a policy. Formulating it is an achievement in its own right, but its value becomes clearer when we see what it changes for the people it is meant to serve."
  },
