    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dca34284a29e41ebf2b3d122cd4dbf22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1788,
    "chunk_text": "A profitable order can still leave a business short of cash when wages, suppliers and instalments fall due before customers pay. Positive operating cash flow gives SMEs and entrepreneurs room to reinvest and hire. Firms should test collection periods and working-capital needs before committing to new orders.\n\nFCCISL appreciates the steps the Government has taken since assuming office to improve public systems and advance policy, regulatory and administrative reforms. The Federation welcomes Budget 2027 funding for digitisation and the institutions, policies and regulations that support it, as well as efforts to broaden the tax base and strengthen revenue administration. Legislative changes include the Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act, No. 2 of 2025, which revised personal income tax provisions, and the Sri Lanka Electricity (Amendment) Act, No. 14 of 2025, which amended the legal framework for electricity sector policy.\n\nThe reform agenda offers scope to lower energy and utility costs, review duties and levies, and streamline regulatory, procurement, approval and payment procedures. Redesigning and digitising those processes can cut duplicate submissions and speed up approvals and payments. Automation and appropriate use of AI can handle routine tasks, with officials accountable for decisions. Shorter approval times, reliable utilities and timely payments can free working capital, reduce idle time and lower unit costs. That gives firms more room to invest in skills and technology, improve productivity and compete for domestic and export orders.\n\nFCCISL and its chamber and industry association networks stand ready to support implementation by identifying the procedures that add the most time and cost and sharing practical feedback with the responsible agencies."
  },
  {
