    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b704972cec6a5e6c62eb0550cc1fb2f6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1881,
    "chunk_text": "Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Deputy Minister Chathuranga Abeysinghe stressed the need for stronger policy support to nurture entrepreneurship and innovation. \u201cEntrepreneurship is a key requirement. We must recognise it, categorise it, and develop the policy framework necessary to build an ecosystem that enables it to move forward,\u201d he said.\n\nAddressing the challenge of commercialising innovation, Weeraratne said many promising ideas struggle to progress beyond the research stage because founders often focus on technology rather than market demand. He stressed the importance of shifting from an engineering mindset towards building products that solve clearly defined problems and create value for customers.\n\nAccording to Weeraratne, entrepreneurs must understand the markets they are targeting, develop multidisciplinary teams and think beyond local opportunities if they hope to attract investment and compete internationally. He warned that competing solely on price is rarely enough for long-term success and encouraged founders to identify gaps in existing industries where they can deliver something distinctive. \u201cFirst, identify the problem you are going to solve. Then identify the resources you need to solve it.\u201d\n\nHe added that innovators must also consider how they will access customers, investors and strategic partners as they move from concept to commercialisation.\n\nDiscussing branding in an era shaped by digital platforms and artificial intelligence, Jayasundara said the channels used to communicate with customers may have changed dramatically, but the foundations of strong brands remain the same.\n\nShe argued that credibility, trust and consistency continue to be the most valuable assets a business can build. While technologies evolve, successful brands are those that remain clear about who they are and what purpose they serve."
  },
