    "md5": "d2a35c52b757d39218aa3e8f038ac7bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "She argued that credibility, trust and consistency continue to be the most valuable assets a business can build. While technologies evolve, successful brands are those that remain clear about who they are and what purpose they serve.\n\n\u201cEmbrace the changes around your brand, but remain clear about its identity, its personality, and the need it fulfils.\u201d\n\nShe said this clarity enables entrepreneurs to respond to new opportunities while maintaining the trust they have built with customers.\n\nReflecting on his own entrepreneurial journey, de Mel spoke about the importance of understanding both products and markets before pursuing growth opportunities.\n\nHe explained how entering the fishing-flies business required learning about customers, supply chains, international markets and industry trends. That process, combined with active engagement in business organisations and industry networks, provided valuable knowledge and confidence as the company expanded.\n\nDe Mel said one of the most important decisions made during the company's growth journey was moving away from competing primarily on price and instead focusing on specialised, higher-value products capable of serving niche markets.\n\n\u201cNever give up, and build relationships with the partners in your ecosystem. Networking was key for me.\u201d\n\nHe encouraged entrepreneurs to seek guidance, build relationships and learn from others rather than trying to navigate business challenges alone.\n\nLooking at the impact of artificial intelligence, Herath said today's entrepreneurs have advantages that previous generations did not enjoy.\n\nHe argued that AI-powered tools have lowered barriers to entry, reduced costs and made it easier for entrepreneurs to test ideas, launch products and learn from failures more quickly than before. \u201cThe cost of failure is much lower now. You can afford to experiment, fail, and try again.\u201d"
