    "chunk_text": "He argued that AI-powered tools have lowered barriers to entry, reduced costs and made it easier for entrepreneurs to test ideas, launch products and learn from failures more quickly than before. \u201cThe cost of failure is much lower now. You can afford to experiment, fail, and try again.\u201d\n\nTurning to the broader startup ecosystem, Weeraratne said Sri Lanka needs policies and institutional support mechanisms that make it easier for startups to scale and attract investment.\n\nHe highlighted the importance of improving access to investors, strengthening innovation frameworks and creating pathways for startups to secure their first major customers.\n\n\u201cFunding is important, but so is the value investors can bring through market access and connections.\u201d\n\nWeeraratne also pointed to ongoing discussions around procurement reforms, innovation-focused investment mechanisms and greater access to data, which could help entrepreneurs develop and demonstrate new products and services.\n\nJayasundara said entrepreneurs often make the mistake of focusing on their product before fully understanding the problem they are trying to solve.\n\nShe argued that effective communication begins with a clear understanding of customer needs and pain points. Rather than simply promoting a product, businesses should explain why it exists and how it addresses a specific customer challenge. \u201cHere is the pain point, here is the problem, and here is my solution.\u201d\n\nAccording to Jayasundara, these considerations should form part of product development from the beginning rather than becoming an afterthought once a marketing campaign begins.\n\nDiscussing international expansion, de Mel stressed the importance of market research, preparation and adaptability.\n\nHe noted that different markets often require different pricing strategies, product variations and approaches to customer engagement. Entrepreneurs must therefore invest time in understanding customer expectations and market conditions before pursuing growth overseas."
