    "chunk_text": "Instead, entrepreneurs should identify underserved customer needs and market gaps where they can create meaningful differentiation and deliver greater value. \u201cLearn from the leading brands, but find your own niche.\u201d\n\nReturning to the subject of international growth, de Mel encouraged entrepreneurs to adopt a global mindset and make better use of available networks.\n\nHe said entrepreneurs should actively seek support from mentors, investors and ecosystem partners capable of helping them navigate unfamiliar markets and access new opportunities.\n\n\u201cFind your niche, then identify the stakeholders who can help you develop it.\u201d\n\nThe ability to build relationships and remain connected to global entrepreneurial communities, he added, can significantly improve the prospects of long-term growth.\n\nWeeraratne also outlined efforts to modernise public services and improve digital interactions between citizens, businesses and government institutions.\n\nHe said the objective is to reduce friction by enabling more services to be accessed online while creating opportunities for private-sector innovation through digital platforms and safe testing environments.\n\n\u201cThe direction is towards connected platforms that allow institutions to make services available digitally.\u201d\n\nHe added that more efficient digital services and payment systems could support broader economic participation and entrepreneurship.\n\nShe said entrepreneurs themselves often become the face of their businesses, making reputation and customer relationships particularly important during the early stages of growth.\n\n\u201cBuilding trust and credibility is one of the most important things you can do.\u201d\n\nAs customer feedback becomes increasingly visible through social media and digital platforms, he said businesses must be willing to listen, engage and respond constructively."
