    "md5": "32bc799b043a58aa466a0173278dbd8f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "BBC and Ikea among nearly 200 websites hit by hackers\n\nNearly 200 prominent websites worldwide, including those of the BBC, Ikea and Air France, were hit by malicious cyberattacks on the same day, as hackers sought to steal users\u2019 financial information and compromise Apple devices. Separately, Apple suffered widespread system outages that same day following a faulty internal update, but clarified that the disruption was unrelated to a cyberattack.\n\nAFP, citing a report by specialist technology news outlet Cyber News (\u300a\u7f51\u7edc\u65b0\u95fb\u300b) on Tuesday (17 November), said the attacks stemmed from hackers injecting compromised JavaScript code into tools hosted by digital experience platform Optimizely. Many major companies use the platform to customise users\u2019 online experiences.\n\nThe attack targeted about 178 websites, aiming to steal users\u2019 credit card details and cryptocurrency, and to plant code on Apple devices in an attempt to compromise them further.\n\nInformation security researcher Scott Helme, who first discovered the incident, wrote in a blog post on Monday (16) that the victims included some of the world\u2019s most visited websites. Among them were video communications platform Zoom, the BBC, CNN, software company Salesforce, code-hosting platform GitLab, Ikea, HSBC in the UAE, credit reporting giant Equifax, Air France-KLM, cryptocurrency wallet maker Ledger and streaming platform Peacock.\n\nHelme noted that the attackers\u2019 cryptocurrency wallet received about US$7,040 (S$9,070) in just nine hours on 16 November. However, he said most of the malicious code on the affected websites had been removed on Monday.\n\nOptimizely subsequently said in a statement that it had \u201cidentified and contained a security incident affecting some Optimizely Web Experimentation customers\u201d.\n\nThe company added on social media that the attack vector had been blocked, the injected code removed and affected customers contacted directly."
  },
