    "chunk_size_bytes": 1685,
    "chunk_text": "Optimizely subsequently said in a statement that it had \u201cidentified and contained a security incident affecting some Optimizely Web Experimentation customers\u201d.\n\nThe company added on social media that the attack vector had been blocked, the injected code removed and affected customers contacted directly.\n\nSeparately, Bloomberg reported that a faulty internal update at Apple on Tuesday caused widespread system outages, disrupting employee tools, retail operations and consumer services. The company was working to restore services.\n\nApple made clear that the outage was not caused by a cyberattack.\n\nCiting people familiar with the matter, the report said the outage brought down several internal services, including employee portal AppleWeb, internal login system AppleConnect and the virtual private network (VPN) used by remote workers. Employees also had trouble downloading internal software builds, checking company calendars and booking meeting rooms. Even the internal website used to track outages was unavailable.\n\nMany employees were unable to log in to systems requiring Apple credentials for hours, bringing their work to a complete halt. The incident also affected Apple stores worldwide, disrupting sales, repairs and customer service.\n\nAccording to Apple\u2019s online status page, the problems also extended to consumer, school and business services. Users were unable to access iCloud services or sign up for AppleCare coverage directly on their devices.\n\nApple said in a statement: \u201cMany of our systems are back up and running after an internal update caused some outages this morning, we\u2019re working to get the remaining systems online as soon as possible.\u201d"
  },
