    "md5": "3f1ebd13c98f844306d8e132c6642631",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "Rubio says US not \u2019under systemic attack\u2019 after series of tech outages\n\nBBC - US Secretary of State Marco Rubio has dismissed widespread concerns online that the series of recent tech outages in the past week are connected, telling reporters there was \"zero evidence\" of any link.\n\nRubio acknowledged that \"people are fearful\" the spate of outages, which included all flights being grounded in the US and widespread disruptions to banking, Apple and Microsoft services, could be linked or part of a plot.\n\n\"We are not under systemic attack,\" he said. \"These events, one has nothing to do with the other, they are not inter-related.\"\n\nCyber-security experts have said there is no evidence connecting the outages, and many of the issues appear due to updates to old infrastructure.\n\nIn the past week, there has been a massive Microsoft outage, an apparent tech glitch which grounded all US flights for hours, and issues for the National Security Agency, several US banks and Apple.\n\nEvery affected company or agency has said this was not a cyber-related incident.\n\nRubio's comments came hours after US Director of National Intelligence Jay Clayton also addressed the issue, telling Fox News the outages were not the product of a cyber-attack. He explained \"lots of institutions\" are patching or upgrading systems, which has caused some disruption.\n\nRubio was asked whether the Trump administration was concerned public fears could lead to stockpiling or a run on the banks.\n\nAcknowledging those concerns, Rubio said the best way to deal with that is to share with the American people what the US government knows.\n\n\"Every indicator we have is that every single one of these events has an explanation or a cause that has nothing to do with a cyber-criminal, has nothing to do with a hostile actor or another country,\" he said.\n\nDaily technology-related disruptions have happened since Thursday, when a Microsoft outage disrupted many of its services, including email."
