    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "\"Every indicator we have is that every single one of these events has an explanation or a cause that has nothing to do with a cyber-criminal, has nothing to do with a hostile actor or another country,\" he said.\n\nDaily technology-related disruptions have happened since Thursday, when a Microsoft outage disrupted many of its services, including email.\n\nOn Monday a United Airlines technology failure delayed about 150 flights and a JPMorgan Chase software problem disrupted some banking services.\n\nApple users were impacted by outages affecting iCloud and Apple Pay on Tuesday. The company said the cause was not a cyber-attack but the outage was triggered by an an internal update.\n\nA highly classified network used by the US National Security Agency (NSA) for internal communications also went offline for a period of time on Tuesday.\n\nCybersecurity experts say there is no evidence connecting the Microsoft, airline, and bank disruptions from the last five days.\n\nKris Beevers, CEO and co-founder of the network and infrastructure management company NetBox Labs, acknowledged the circulating theories on social media linking the barrage of outages.\n\n\"There is something more ordinary, and more important to understand: the infrastructure that runs the world is changing faster than ever, and change is risk. Much of it is 30 to 40 years old, with decades of technical debt, and it's now being asked to change very quickly.\"\n\n\"It's putting more strain on these legacy networks, and it's also creating real urgency around security,\" he said.\n\n\"Our customers are the amazing teams behind these networks at airlines, banking, energy, and retail who are essentially doing heart surgery on a global scale to get these networks back up and running quickly.\""
