    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "Pakistan, Sri Lanka handed big WTC27 points deduction\n\nICC - Sri Lanka have suffered a massive setback in World Test Championship standings after being docked 15 points for maintaining a slow over rate in the first Test against Pakistan.\n\nPakistan have also been penalised, losing five points for falling five overs short of the required rate.\n\nAs a result, Sri Lanka have slipped from 7th to 8th, with their point percentage taking a massive hit from 33.33% to 15.47%. Pakistan are at the bottom, in 9th with 3.33%.\n\nAs per the ICC World Test Championship Playing Conditions, teams are deducted one point for every over they fall short of the required rate. Sri Lanka were found to be 15 overs behind the target, while Pakistan fell five overs short.\n\nBoth teams have also been fined in accordance with Article 2.22 of the ICC Code of Conduct for Players and Player Support Personnel, as per which players are fined five per cent of their match fee for every over their side fails to bowl in the allotted time, subject to a maximum cap of 50%. Pakistan have been fined 25%, while Sri Lanka were fined 50% of their match fees.\n\nDean Cosker of the Emirates ICC International Panel of Match Referees imposed the sanctions after charges were levelled by on-field umpires Adrian Holdstock and Rod Tucker, third umpire Allahuddien Paleker and fourth umpire Faisal Afridi.\n\nCaptains Babar Azam and Kusal Mendis pleaded guilty to the offences and accepted the proposed sanctions, so no formal hearing was needed.\n\nThe opening Test ended in a hard-fought draw following five days of gripping cricket in Rawalpindi.\n\nPakistan declared their first innings after posting a strong total of 434/9, with five batters reaching half-centuries. In reply, Sri Lanka edged ahead with a slender 12-run lead, thanks to centuries from Dhananjaya de Silva and captain Kusal Mendis. The visitors were on course for a massive lead before debutant Saqib Khan\u2019s seven-for derailed their innings."
