    "chunk_text": "The proposal to modernise the Temporary Importation for Export Processing scheme deserves attention for similar reasons. Many local manufacturers need specialised inputs from abroad to produce finished goods here. Simpler access to those inputs under the scheme\u2019s tax concessions can help smaller firms take on export orders that would otherwise stretch their resources.\n\nFor manufacturers considering a larger investment, the Budget proposes a capital allowance equal to 200 per cent of qualifying expenditure for eligible projects approved by the Board of Investment. Export manufacturers would need to invest at least US$3 million within three years. Alongside the Rs. 4 billion allocated for work on new and existing industrial estates, this can support decisions to install new machinery, expand production and bring more local suppliers into export supply chains.\n\nThe Budget\u2019s Rs. 750 million allocation covers Sri Lanka Expo 2027, planned for January, and priorities under the National Export Development Plan. A well-organised exhibition can give a small firm access to buyers it might spend months trying to reach. The commercial value comes afterwards, when a buyer requests samples, agrees on specifications and places an order. The EU-supported sustainable trade project can complement that work by helping businesses meet buyer requirements on testing, traceability and certification.\n\nThe Government\u2019s efforts to widen market access are also welcome. The Budget outlines work to expand the existing trade agreement with India, Sri Lanka\u2019s application to join the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership, and prospective agreements with the United Arab Emirates and Oman. To use any new trading terms, an exporter needs clear answers about tariffs, product standards and the documents required to qualify."
  },
