    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d9208d43e1b4b4e9bd37358b492a3002",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1361,
    "chunk_text": "The Government\u2019s efforts to widen market access are also welcome. The Budget outlines work to expand the existing trade agreement with India, Sri Lanka\u2019s application to join the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership, and prospective agreements with the United Arab Emirates and Oman. To use any new trading terms, an exporter needs clear answers about tariffs, product standards and the documents required to qualify.\n\nI also welcome the Government\u2019s commitment to fiscal discipline. An exporter quoting today for delivery several months from now has to estimate the cost of materials, wages, freight and finance well before the buyer pays. Greater economic predictability gives businesses more confidence to make those commitments.\n\nThe Government has chosen several reforms that can make a difference on the factory floor and at the port. A year from now, I would judge progress by three practical questions. Are shipments clearing faster? Are eligible firms receiving their VAT refunds sooner? And are more small manufacturers turning a first overseas order into repeat business? That second order is one of the clearest signs that a buyer trusts what Sri Lanka can deliver.\n\nDhammika Fernando is Sri Lanka\u2019s High Commissioner to Kenya and a former Chairman of the Free Trade Zone Manufacturers\u2019 Association. He writes in a personal capacity."
  },
