    "chunk_text": "Each machine costs more than Rs. 600 million, while the total investment in a complete radiation therapy unit at each hospital exceeds Rs. 1 billion.\n\nOf the five machines, three have so far been delivered to Badulla, Ratnapura and Hambantota.\n\nMeanwhile, the failure to commence services in Badulla and Hambantota has forced patients to seek treatment at already burdened facilities, including Apeksha Hospital in Maharagama and the National Hospital in Kandy.\n\nDr. Sanjeewa warned that this was adding further pressure to existing waiting lists, which already stretch for several months.\n\nWith delays in the public health system, some patients are being forced to seek radiation treatment from private hospitals, where the cost could range from Rs. 500,000 to Rs. 2 million.\n\nThe Association of Radiation Technologists has also raised concerns over the failure to utilise the newly installed machines, pointing out that the required dosimetry equipment could be temporarily obtained from Apeksha Hospital.\n\nIt has cited previous instances in which equipment from Apeksha Hospital was used to facilitate the commencement of Linear Accelerator treatment services at other hospitals.\n\nAt present, Linear Accelerator radiation therapy is available at five public institutions: Apeksha Hospital, the National Hospital in Kandy, the National Hospital in Galle, the Teaching Hospital in Jaffna and the Teaching Hospital in Batticaloa.\n\nDr. Sanjeewa has urged President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to intervene urgently and ensure that the newly installed machines are put into operation without further delay.\n\nHe said patients should not be made to suffer because of administrative delays when millions of rupees had already been spent on facilities intended to provide them with life-saving treatment."
