    "md5": "478ab2ab7a39fb8d966a243eb6438e87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1410,
    "chunk_text": "WHEN THE CEO BECOMES THE CRISIS\n\nJensen Huang has spent more than three decades building Nvidia into one of the world's most valuable companies. Now the technology executive, whose chips are central to the artificial intelligence boom, is facing a crisis that reaches into his personal life and raises questions about national security. Huang has publicly apologised for being unfaithful to his wife, while Nvidia's board has launched an investigation following reports that he may have been targeted by a foreign intelligence operation. This week on our BBC show, When It Hits the Fan, my co-host David Yelland and I discussed Huang's response, the difficulties of managing a departing chief executive's legacy, and why the search for the next James Bond continues to generate so much publicity.\n\nThere are two statements worth examining in Nvidia's response. The first came from Huang himself, in a personal statement to employees on 8 November, acknowledging his infidelity and apologising to his family and colleagues. He also addressed the national security concerns, promising to cooperate with the company's investigation. I thought it was a well-judged statement. Huang accepted responsibility for his personal conduct without offering excuses or lengthy explanations. Most of the statement concerned his family, with shorter passages addressing his professional responsibilities and the investigation."
