    "chunk_size_bytes": 1089,
    "chunk_text": "There are two statements worth examining in Nvidia's response. The first came from Huang himself, in a personal statement to employees on 8 November, acknowledging his infidelity and apologising to his family and colleagues. He also addressed the national security concerns, promising to cooperate with the company's investigation. I thought it was a well-judged statement. Huang accepted responsibility for his personal conduct without offering excuses or lengthy explanations. Most of the statement concerned his family, with shorter passages addressing his professional responsibilities and the investigation.\n\nThere is a delicate balance here. A board must reassure the market without appearing to prejudge the outcome of its own investigation. The language of confidence may be intended to provide stability, but it also creates an expectation that the board will have to manage as the inquiry progresses. I've advised chief executives facing the prospect of their private lives becoming public, and some of the most difficult conversations happen long before a statement is released."
