    "chunk_text": "There is a delicate balance here. A board must reassure the market without appearing to prejudge the outcome of its own investigation. The language of confidence may be intended to provide stability, but it also creates an expectation that the board will have to manage as the inquiry progresses. I've advised chief executives facing the prospect of their private lives becoming public, and some of the most difficult conversations happen long before a statement is released.\n\nJensen Huang's personal apology to his family, colleagues and shareholders, published on the 8th of November 2026.\n\nNVIDIA's board announces an independent investigation while reaffirming its confidence in Jensen Huang. Date: 8th of November 2026.\n\nAfter nearly four decades at Macquarie, Shemara Wikramanayake has stepped down as chief executive of the Australian financial group. Her departure has brought renewed attention to the bank's reputation in Britain, particularly its former ownership of Thames Water. In a rare interview with The Sunday Times (UK), Wikramanayake discussed her career, her relatively unconventional lifestyle and Macquarie's extensive investments in the UK. She also challenged the negative perceptions surrounding the bank's involvement with Thames Water. Macquarie has invested in businesses ranging from transport and energy infrastructure to housing. Yet its association with Thames Water, whose financial difficulties and environmental record have attracted sustained criticism, remains particularly prominent in Britain."
