    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "After nearly four decades at Macquarie, Shemara Wikramanayake has stepped down as chief executive of the Australian financial group. Her departure has brought renewed attention to the bank's reputation in Britain, particularly its former ownership of Thames Water. In a rare interview with The Sunday Times (UK), Wikramanayake discussed her career, her relatively unconventional lifestyle and Macquarie's extensive investments in the UK. She also challenged the negative perceptions surrounding the bank's involvement with Thames Water. Macquarie has invested in businesses ranging from transport and energy infrastructure to housing. Yet its association with Thames Water, whose financial difficulties and environmental record have attracted sustained criticism, remains particularly prominent in Britain.\n\nThere was a revealing moment on LinkedIn after Macquarie published a warm farewell to its former chief executive. The post attracted the congratulations and praise familiar to anyone who spends time on the platform. Then PR executive Matthew Freud entered the discussion with a sharply critical assessment of the bank's legacy. The contrast was striking. Macquarie was celebrating a long-serving leader, while a prominent critic was reminding readers of the controversy surrounding one of its best-known investments. Wikramanayake is entitled to explain her record and challenge what she considers unfair criticism. Her achievements at Macquarie are substantial. But the public may judge her tenure through a different set of experiences, particularly where an essential service such as water is concerned. For departing executives, that can be difficult to accept. Their preferred account of their career is only one part of how it will be remembered."
