    "md5": "e10e20e049326051a9bef59de9f5c0e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "The biggest lacuna in the Budget 2027\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s education policymakers, perhaps owing to the nature of bureaucratic culture, have failed to grasp or are wilfully blind to the long-term degenerative impact of the country\u2019s low tertiary education\n\nSome time back, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake told the party faithful and curious onlookers at his political rallies that the government would build 50 new technical and vocational colleges. That was a solemn and well-meaning undertaking, though I would say given Sri Lanka\u2019s painfully low Gross Enrolment ratio in tertiary education ( 20.7%), the country needs at least 200 new institutions of tertiary education to generate vocational and technical education opportunities for an additional 200,000 intake of youth.\n\nStill, the president\u2019s promise was a good first step, which if implemented correctly add additional 50,000 annual intake into tertiary and vocational education. Alas, the budget 2027 he delivered last week makes no mention of any of them. Instead, all you have is Rs 11 billion in capital investment earmarked to \u2018upgrade facilities, update curricula, integrate new technologies, and expand public-private partnerships across the vocational sector\u2019.\n\nEven for those who enter local universities, the stubborn truth is that none of them is within the top 1000 global universities\n\nOf the said capital investment, Rs. 1.5 billion has been allocated for 2027 into a Rs. 6 billion project to modernise 25 selected Technical Colleges across 25 districts.\n\nIn comparison, the government has allocated Rs 451 billion for two expressways: Kurunegala\u2014Galewela and Kahathuduwa\u2014Ingiriya. Bimal Ratnayaka, Transport and Highways and Urban Development Minister, touts it as being funded 100% by public funds sans foreign loans. This is where misplaced priorities in limited resource allocation come into play. While Highways matter, very much so, stunted human capital already has a pernicious impact on the country and the overall economy."
  },
