    "md5": "8eef8d7503293834a7f192c70ba2db42",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "In comparison, the government has allocated Rs 451 billion for two expressways: Kurunegala\u2014Galewela and Kahathuduwa\u2014Ingiriya. Bimal Ratnayaka, Transport and Highways and Urban Development Minister, touts it as being funded 100% by public funds sans foreign loans. This is where misplaced priorities in limited resource allocation come into play. While Highways matter, very much so, stunted human capital already has a pernicious impact on the country and the overall economy.\n\nThese numbers reveal the quality of human capital, upward mobility and long-term economic modernisation. More than any other social indicator, GER in tertiary education has the most significant positive correlation with the long-term economic position of a country. While in rich countries,\u00a0 more people stay in higher education, it is also true that aspiring nations tend to offer more higher education opportunities for their youth. Sri Lanka sadly trails behind even its South Asian peers. India\u2019s Tertiary GER is 34%\n\nI would say this is a sad but predictable outcome of Sri Lanka\u2019s misguided quasi-state monopoly in higher education. Take, for instance, a kid born in 2006-2007. The total number of births in that year was 386,573. ( Department of Census). These kids would come of age well after the end of the war and therefore should have a better shot at success than most of us who preceded their generation.\u00a0 However, the reality is damning.\n\nEighteen (18) per cent of children of that birth cohort dropped out of school before GCE O/levels. Another 21 per cent failed O/Levels and dropped out.\u00a0 Meaning that by the time of Advanced Levels, nearly 40 per cent ( four in every ten children) born in 2006-7 had already dropped out of formal education. And another 4 per cent, who passed O/L, opted out of A/level. That means by the time A/levels, 43 per cent of children of their birth cohort are out of the school system.\u00a0 What happened to them is anybody\u2019s guess."
  },
