    "chunk_size_bytes": 1602,
    "chunk_text": "Eighteen (18) per cent of children of that birth cohort dropped out of school before GCE O/levels. Another 21 per cent failed O/Levels and dropped out.\u00a0 Meaning that by the time of Advanced Levels, nearly 40 per cent ( four in every ten children) born in 2006-7 had already dropped out of formal education. And another 4 per cent, who passed O/L, opted out of A/level. That means by the time A/levels, 43 per cent of children of their birth cohort are out of the school system.\u00a0 What happened to them is anybody\u2019s guess.\n\nThe starting point of the fix would be to ensure that every child receives a mandatory 13-year education and, where necessary, hold parents accountable by law. The government says that is its intention too, but there is little noticeable progress. Still, even then, the generations before them, who missed out, should be offered the same opportunity to catch up. For that, the government should not only invest, but recognise upgrading and enhancing tertiary education as a major national policy initiative, in the same way some African countries immediately after their independence embarked on a national drive for literacy, before their misplaced statism cannibalised them.\n\nAnd back to the kids of the 2006-7 birth cohort.\u00a0 Fifty-seven per cent of them sat for the Advanced Levels, and 37.9 per cent ( 146,405) qualified to go to university, of which a mere 42,000 or around 11 per cent of the total birth cohort, got admitted to a public university. While a roughly similar number enter MOHE-approved non-state degree institutes and foreign-affiliated colleges annually."
