    "chunk_text": "And back to the kids of the 2006-7 birth cohort.\u00a0 Fifty-seven per cent of them sat for the Advanced Levels, and 37.9 per cent ( 146,405) qualified to go to university, of which a mere 42,000 or around 11 per cent of the total birth cohort, got admitted to a public university. While a roughly similar number enter MOHE-approved non-state degree institutes and foreign-affiliated colleges annually.\n\nA proactive government, one would expect the NPP to be one such, should reorganise the existing Technical and Vocational centres into a coherent and uniform mechanism and upgrade the facilities and staff to induct 200,000 annually, including those who drop out by A/L and past generations who missed out on the opportunity.\n\nWith a US$ 1 billion investment, the president could establish a well-established network of technical and Vocational education, with the participation of well-meaning foreign partners and local business bodies. A two-year associate degree can drastically increase productivity and upward mobility of this cohort and Sri Lanka in general.\n\nThe second group, those who qualify for universities, deserve a different treatment. Though around 50 per cent of this group has some form of Higher education, another half fail to secure higher education opportunities matching their potential. Even for those who enter local universities, the stubborn truth is that none of them is within the top 1000 global universities."
  },
