    "md5": "134d29cadeb16d4f2b9756145893144b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1456,
    "chunk_text": "The second group, those who qualify for universities, deserve a different treatment. Though around 50 per cent of this group has some form of Higher education, another half fail to secure higher education opportunities matching their potential. Even for those who enter local universities, the stubborn truth is that none of them is within the top 1000 global universities.\n\nTo address the dearth of quality academics in higher education, perhaps the government could allocate $ 50 million annually and send the\u00a0 500 top- performing students for PhDs in the top 10 global universities in their discipline. On return, they would become better academics than their teachers. That is how countries like Singapore cultivated their talent.\n\nThe government can also set up a second tier of technological universities similar to India\u2019s National Institutes of Technology (NIT) and also invite the top 300 global universities to open campuses in Sri Lanka and finance education through a tuition fee loan system.\n\nSri Lanka is today free of two major crippling economic and social handicaps: the war and the high population growth both cannibalised the living. With the country is now a Upper Middle income group, and the government touting of its savings, some of the government\u2019s savings should be invested where it matter.\u00a0 Without fixing education at all levels, Sri Lanka\u2019s other potentials would be of little help in its long-term economic prospects."
