    "md5": "3788a6b10e7e47708a1ed02db2ae1f70",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "Gold slides to over one-year low on firm dollar, US rate outlook\n\nReuters\u00a0- Gold prices fell more than 1% on Wednesday to their lowest level \u200cin over a year, as a firmer dollar and expectations of higher US interest rates weighed, while investors awaited minutes from the Federal Reserve's meeting for clues on the policy outlook.\n\nSpot \u200bgold was down 1.6% at $3,787.65 per ounce, as of 0428 GMT, after hitting \u200bits lowest level since September 2025 earlier in the session.\n\nUS gold \u2060futures for December delivery dropped 1.9% to $3,789.40.\n\n\"The bearish movement in gold prices have \u200bbeen primarily driven by sentiment and technicality, with hawkish reaffirmation from Fed officials and \u200ba push up in the US Treasury yields in turn leading to a firmer US dollar,\" said Kelvin Wong, senior market analyst at OANDA.\n\nThe dollar held steady near a 19-month high, making greenback-priced \u200bbullion more expensive for buyers overseas.\n\nFed Bank of New York President John Williams said \u200bon Tuesday the US central bank will likely need to raise rates again to lean against \u200cinflationary \u2060pressures, but he pushed back against the idea the Fed needs to shift to a more aggressive path of action.\n\nMarkets see a 95.7% chance of a 25-basis-point US rate hike in December, while the probability of a larger 50-basis-point move is priced \u200bat 4.3%, according to \u200bCME's FedWatch tool.\n\nHigher \u2060rates increase the opportunity cost of holding non-yielding bullion, reducing its appeal to investors.\n\n\"Gold is again being treated as a liquidity \u200basset, with positions cut to cover volatility and free up \u200bcapital elsewhere \u2060in the market,\" Tim Waterer, chief market analyst at KCM Trade, said in a note.\n\nInvestors now await the minutes of the Fed's October 27-28 Federal Open Market Committee meeting, \u2060due \u200bat 1900 GMT.\n\nAmong other metals, spot silver slipped \u200b3.1% to $45.94 per ounce, platinum dropped 1.5% to $1,408.30 and palladium slid 2% to $951.42."
  },
