    "chunk_text": "\"The harder question is whether Nvidia can preserve the strategic advantage Huang helped build,\" Hathorn added. \"His exit is unlikely to change orders already committed, but could matter more when Nvidia makes its next round of decisions on products, partnerships and investment.\"\n\n\"It is business as usual,\" she said. \"Our partners, our suppliers, all of them are 100% behind us.\"\n\nShe added that Nvidia's leadership team has \"stood up very strongly over the last week... working seamlessly in all of their departments and projects\".\n\n\"Our momentum is real. Our demand is accelerating. Our supply is growing. Our customers and our partners are with us, and employees are committed.\" Kress said before leaving the analyst call. \"We've got this.\"\n\nThe interim CEO also said Nvidia is no longer shipping Hopper data centre semiconductors to China.\n\nThe Hopper technology is in Nvidia's advanced H200 processors, which the firm won approval to sell in China after Huang's repeated lobbying efforts in Washington.\n\nThey had previously been restricted over concerns that selling them to China would give its industries and military an edge over the US.\n\nKress did not elaborate on why the shipments had been stopped.\n\nDespite board chairman Jones saying earlier that the company would not answer analysts' questions about Huang or Nvidia's investigation into his conduct, he did address the search for a permanent replacement.\n\nHe said Nvidia is working with an executive search firm and is considering internal and external candidates, but that the company would not rush its selection.\n\n\"With the advanced technology and the national security dynamics in the marketplace, our new CEO will have to navigate those waters carefully,\" Jones said. \"The search will be thorough and we want to do it as quickly as possible, but we won't do it before we find the exact right person.\"\n\nMichael Monaghan from investment firm Founder ETFs is among those who want Huang back in the top job at Nvidia."
