    "md5": "2fdc8c382f00cf315a0d85f2412f5c05",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "South Asian students studying abroad  face tough challenges\n\nOver 1.8 million students from India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Nepal, and Sri Lanka have been pursuing education abroad since 2022\n\nIt is also important for Western educational institutions to first understand the cultural context of South Asian students, instead of rushing to punish them if they did not meet local standards\n\nInternational Students\u2019 Day falls on November 17. Though it marks Czechoslovakian students\u2019 resistance to Nazi brutality in November 1939, the occasion is used to take stock of the contemporary condition of students in various parts of the world. Here is a profile of South Asian students studying abroad and the challenges they face.\n\nIn the last few years, South Asia has emerged as a powerhouse of international student mobility. Over 1.8 million students from India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Nepal, and Sri Lanka have been pursuing education abroad since 2022. In 2025, South Asian students constituted 28 % of the university student population in Canada, 22% in the UK, 15% in Australia, 7% in Germany.\n\nIn overseas student enrolment, India continues to lead. But Bangladesh, Nepal, Pakistan and Sri Lanka have also shown accelerated outbound growth. Among South Asian students, there is a rising interest in Science, Technology, Engineering and Maths (STEM), healthcare, business analytics and public policy programmes.\n\nPost-degree work rights and migration pathways remain key motivations to go abroad. However, post-degree employment has been coming under scrutiny and restrictions, especially in the US.\n\nAccording to www.pienews.com, over 750,000 Indian students were in universities abroad in 2022, rising to 930,000 in 2023 and peaking at 1.33 million in 2024. Despite Ottawa\u2019s clampdown on international students with 74% of Indian study permit applications rejected in August 2025, Canada still hosts the largest number of Indian students, with 427,085 on the rolls."
  },
