    "md5": "90ce179c41a9590c793774c77c42197b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "According to www.pienews.com, over 750,000 Indian students were in universities abroad in 2022, rising to 930,000 in 2023 and peaking at 1.33 million in 2024. Despite Ottawa\u2019s clampdown on international students with 74% of Indian study permit applications rejected in August 2025, Canada still hosts the largest number of Indian students, with 427,085 on the rolls.\n\nThe Indian Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) showed that in 2025, the UK had 173,190 Indian students; Australia 138,579; Germany 59,000;\u00a0 Russia 27,000; Kyrgyzstan 16,500; and Georgia 16,000.\n\nAccording to another study, females accounted for 40% of Indian students abroad. Interestingly, these females came from smaller cities like Coimbatore, Indore and Bhubaneswar.\n\nPolicy changes in the host countries are impacting Indian students\u2019 decisions.\u00a0 Canada has cut new international study permit allocations by 49 to 55% as part of a broader crackdown on temporary resident numbers. Nine out of 10 students now fear for their visas.\n\nPostgraduate enrolments are falling across UK universities, even though institutions there are facing a potential loss of \u00a3925-per-international student denied admission.\n\nMeanwhile, the annual cost of studying in the US has risen over the five years, to British pounds 8,200 to 8,300. With the result, there has been a rise of new destinations such as Germany, Russia, Kyrgyzstan and Georgia which are lower-cost destinations, especially for STEM and medical education.\n\nFrom Bangladesh, more than 120,000 students went abroad between 2022 and 2025. Although, the US, the UK, Australia, Canada, Germany, Japan and some other European countries are the usual destinations for Bangladeshi students, in recent years many have opted for other countries."
  },
