    "md5": "4f57aa44910f7aa77d668446852b38e8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "From Bangladesh, more than 120,000 students went abroad between 2022 and 2025. Although, the US, the UK, Australia, Canada, Germany, Japan and some other European countries are the usual destinations for Bangladeshi students, in recent years many have opted for other countries.\n\nThere is a strong demand for foreign education in Pakistan though\u00a0 Pakistanis also face visa challenges. But undeterred by this, 150,000 Pakistani students were abroad in 2022-25. Typically, they headed to UK, USA, Australia and Germany. Rejections from UK and US in 2024\u201325 triggered increased interest in Turkey and Malaysia.\n\nPakistani applicants face strict vetting on financial proof, authentic student intent, and soaring rejection rates in key markets. For instance, recent reports highlight that UK student visa rejection rates for Pakistan climbed sharply (reaching over 40% in recent tracking), prompting students to look towards more accessible study hubs.\n\nMore than 95,000 Nepalese were abroad for education in the 2022-25 period, mainly in Canada, but also in Australia, UK and Japan. But Australia\u2019s 2025 rules for Genuine Temporary Entrants (GTE) led to decreased interest, pushing students toward Canada and Japan.\n\nSubjects of interest to Nepalese were Nursing, Hospitality, Environmental Science, and IT. Nepalese students were heavily reliant on Nepalese education consultants and were therefore vulnerable to misinformation.\n\nBetween 2022 and 2025, 45,000 Sri Lankan students went abroad for education, mostly to UK, Australia, India, Malaysia, and Germany. There has been a growing interest in STEM and postgraduate coursework programmes in Medicine, Finance, Education and Psychology. There is also a growing interest in getting scholarships through Commonwealth programmes, particularly in the UK and Australia."
