    "md5": "a7835fae08e13d39eab403282c79de83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1940,
    "chunk_text": "Between 2022 and 2025, 45,000 Sri Lankan students went abroad for education, mostly to UK, Australia, India, Malaysia, and Germany. There has been a growing interest in STEM and postgraduate coursework programmes in Medicine, Finance, Education and Psychology. There is also a growing interest in getting scholarships through Commonwealth programmes, particularly in the UK and Australia.\n\nStudents preferred studying abroad also due to dissatisfaction with the local higher education system. University graduates tend to migrate for education and not return home. This can cause both short-term and long-term brain drain and outflow of foreign exchange.\n\nAmong the challenges faced by South Asian students are visa rejections (mainly due to weak reporting, financial gaps and issues in the interview); rising tuition and housing costs; language barriers in both English- and non-English-speaking destinations and dependence on unregulated agents.\n\nThere has been a 36% year-over-year decline in new US F-1 visa (student Visa) issuances as of March 2026. India, the largest source of foreign students, recorded a 60% drop in the 2025 summer issuances. On January 12, 2026, the State Department said that it had revoked more than 100,000 visas, including 8,000 student visas \u201cto keep America safe.\u201d President Trump had also ordered to stop vias to citizens of 39 countries.\n\nFor decades, the US allowed foreign students to remain in the United States as long as they maintained their studies. But under the new system, those who need more time must file for an extension through the US Citizenship and Immigration Service (USCIS), pay a $420 fee (as of May 2026), and complete biometrics.\n\nHowever, on Sept 14, 2026, a federal judge in Massachusetts issued a nationwide preliminary injunction temporarily blocking that order.\n\nBy going ahead with these restrictions, the US is said to be foregoing a lot of income - $43 billion annually."
