    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794990262.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailymirrorlk-b5dbc15e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fb71fb3b4e9fdd50e46fde26264b5f27",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1700,
    "chunk_text": "Tax credit lifts DFCC\u2019s third-quarter shareholder profit by 23%\n\nDFCC Bank PLC\u2019s third-quarter profit attributable to shareholders rose 23 percent to Rs. 3.38 billion from a year earlier, as an income tax credit offset a drop in pre-tax earnings, its unaudited interim financial statements showed.\n\nGroup profit before income tax fell 23 percent to Rs. 3.33 billion in the three months ended September 30, 2026. The group booked a Rs. 98.7 million income tax credit, against a Rs. 1.52 billion charge a year earlier. Group profit after tax rose 23 percent to Rs. 3.43 billion, while earnings per share increased to Rs. 7.55 from Rs. 6.28.\n\nNet interest income rose 11 percent to Rs. 8.66 billion and net fee and commission income climbed 14 percent to Rs. 2.32 billion. The group recorded a trading loss of Rs. 591 million, against a gain of Rs. 1.17 billion a year earlier, pulling total operating income down 8 percent to Rs. 10.72 billion.\n\nImpairment charges fell 80 percent to Rs. 177 million during the quarter, cushioning the decline in operating income. Operating expenses rose 18 percent to Rs. 6.02 billion, led by a 23 percent increase in personnel costs. Operating profit before taxes on financial services fell 19 percent to Rs. 4.52 billion.\n\nDFCC completed its acquisition of Standard Chartered\u2019s Sri Lankan wealth and retail banking business on August 1 for Rs. 4.42 billion in cash, inclusive of taxes. The transaction brought about 50,000 customer accounts and 260 employees into DFCC, Chief Executive Thimal Perera said. The bank cited staff joining through the acquisition and higher card-related costs among the factors behind its increase in expenses over the nine months."
  },
