    "description": "Above-band inflation prompts call for Treasury-CBSL talks",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Above-band-inflation-prompts-call-for-Treasury-CBSL-talks/215-355201",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1794990591.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-18-dailymirrorlk-9b9d215f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8f299b8ef1846c2b0f803cd9f2436fa0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe have provided independence to the Central Bank, which is the correct thing, but of course we must now set up a mechanism where the Treasury officials and the Central Bank officials will meet at least once in two weeks and discuss how they should jointly provide relief to the people when inflation is above 7 percent,\u201d Dr. Wijewardena said.\n\nHowever, the former central banker questioned the CBSL\u2019s tendency to attribute inflation to external shocks whenever it strays outside the band in either direction.\n\n\u201cWhen the rate is below 3 percent, that is due to external shocks. When the rate is above 7 percent, it\u2019s also due to external shocks. So it means the Central Bank had been following the policy of inactivity,\u201d he observed.\n\nAt the same time, he conceded that the CBSL had little room to act alone. He pointed out that dragging inflation down from around 8 percent to 3 percent by curbing aggregate demand would trigger a massive contraction in the economy, leading to unemployment and recession. Such a downturn, he warned, would itself become \u201cpublic enemy number one\u201d.\n\nMeanwhile, Dr. Wijewardena acknowledged that he had since rethought the monetarist doctrine he upheld during his years at the CBSL. That doctrine held that inflation was \u201calways and everywhere a monetary phenomenon\u201d and the economy\u2019s foremost threat.\n\n\u201cAs an outsider today when I look at it, I might think differently. Inflation had been public enemy number one, but today there are other things which are public enemy number one, and inflation might be reduced to, say, public enemy number two,\u201d he noted.\n\nDrawing on \u2018Inflation: A Guide for Users and Losers\u2019 by political economists Mark Blyth and Nicol\u00f2 Fraccaroli, he contended that once inflation breaches the upper limit and the central bank is unable to rein it in, the responsibility falls on the fiscal authorities. In his view, budgetary policy then has to be geared towards providing relief to the people."
  },
