    "chunk_text": "Trump said on Tuesday the US was taking seriously the possibility that Iran could \u200bbe planning an attack on Washington, when asked about a CNN report that cited US intelligence assessments on a potential Iranian plot involving a so-called dirty \u200bbomb.\n\nIran's Foreign Ministry had denied the allegations on Tuesday, \u200ccalling them \"baseless.\"\n\nA \u2060dirty bomb uses conventional explosives to disperse radioactive material. Unlike a nuclear weapon, it does not produce a nuclear explosion, but can spread radioactive contamination and cause significant disruption and public alarm.\n\nThere are diplomatic efforts to resolve the conflict, which started when the US and Israel struck Iran on February 28 and has since widened to include Iranian strikes on Israel and US assets in the Middle East.\n\nQatari and Iranian officials met on Tuesday for talks aimed at \"bridging perspectives and narrowing \u200bthe gap between proposals presented on the \u200bground,\" Qatari spokesperson Majed Al \u2060Ansari said.\n\nThat followed a visit to Oman by Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araqchi, where he held talks on safeguarding maritime corridors and ensuring the smooth flow of global shipping and trade, Oman's Foreign Ministry said.\n\nOman and Iran lie on \u200bopposite sides of the Strait of Hormuz, a vital waterway that carried about one-fifth of global energy supplies before \u200bthe war but which \u2060is now partly blocked by Iran.\n\nAraqchi said in a post on Telegram that he met Oman's Sultan Haitham bin Tariq and informed him about Iran's diplomatic initiative to end the war.\n\nOman has previously held talks with Tehran about controlling traffic in the strait, which is a sticking point in negotiations.\n\nA surge in tanker attacks since October \u2060threatens to \u200bfurther strain global oil supplies. On Tuesday, a crew abandoned a liquefied petroleum gas tanker off \u200bOman's Khasab."
