    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "Bancroft sandpaper claims heap pressure on Cummins\n\nBBC - In a detailed testimony, published by the West Australian,, external batter Bancroft - the player caught with the sandpaper during the Test match against South Africa - has given arguably the most complete account of the events in Cape Town eight years ago.\n\n\"In 2018 not the entire truth was told,\" said Bancroft. \"I have never been completely honest about what happened.\"\n\nFor the past two weeks, Australian cricket has been embroiled in a debate over the ball-tampering saga following the publication of Usman Khawaja's autobiography.\n\nKhawaja wrote that the whole Australia team knew of the plot, claims supported by David Warner and Chadd Sayers. Cummins described Khawaja's account as \"wrong\" and \"nonsense\".\n\nOn the day Cricket Australia announced a forum into the sandpaper episode, Bancroft published his account, explaining he was motivated by the news conference given by Cummins.\n\n\"The final straw for me to write this account was Australian cricket captain Pat Cummins' press conference,\" said Bancroft.\n\n\"What I saw, experienced and lived with - not only him, but everyone else on that tour in South Africa - is not the public account he gave in his press conference and in the years previously when he has spoken on this time.\"\n\nOpener Bancroft was given a nine-month ban for his part in the plot.\n\nSteve Smith was stripped of the Australia captaincy and given a year-long ban, the same suspension given to vice-captain Warner. No other players or coaches were punished.\n\nKhawaja's autobiography and the subsequent claims made by Warner and seamer Sayers have reopened old wounds related to the sandpaper saga.\n\nStill, those connected have remained true to the accounts they have presented during the intervening eight years.\n\nThe most significant denial came from Cummins, who rebuffed Khawaja's account in an extraordinary 68-minute address to the media in Sydney. Lehmann and Starc have also reiterated their denials."
