    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "Still, those connected have remained true to the accounts they have presented during the intervening eight years.\n\nThe most significant denial came from Cummins, who rebuffed Khawaja's account in an extraordinary 68-minute address to the media in Sydney. Lehmann and Starc have also reiterated their denials.\n\nCummins is due to lead Australia in the first Test against New Zealand on 9 December.\n\nA statement released on behalf of Cummins said: \"Whilst we feel for Cam and everything he's been through there's nothing in the statement that hasn't been answered by Pat in his press conference.\"\n\nA Cricket Australia statement said: \"We respect that Cameron Bancroft has told his story, and we will take time to consider his comments.\n\n\"We expect that there will be many dimensions to the events of 2018, which is why we have established the forum for individuals to give their version of events.\n\n\"We are finalising the terms of reference and will commence the process as soon as possible.\"\n\nIn his account, Bancroft said: \"I have spent eight years speaking about accountability, honesty and the lessons I learned from one of Australian cricket's darkest moments.\n\n\"However, in 2018 not the entire truth was told. I have never been completely honest about what happened.\n\n\"It was not just the three of us \u2014 Steve Smith, David Warner and I \u2014 who knew about me using sandpaper on the ball in South Africa. It was the whole team.\n\n\"When I was questioned during Cricket Australia's investigation, I did not give a complete and truthful account.\n\n\"I withheld important details, including when the use of sandpaper began, how long it went on for, and the involvement and awareness of others.\n\n\"I did this to protect Cricket Australia's investigation and not implicate others around me.\""
