    "chunk_text": "\"I withheld important details, including when the use of sandpaper began, how long it went on for, and the involvement and awareness of others.\n\n\"I did this to protect Cricket Australia's investigation and not implicate others around me.\"\n\nThe only person within the camp to express unease at the ball-tampering, according to Bancroft, was media manager Kate Hutchison.\n\n\"I received wraps and encouragement from members of the playing and coaching staff, especially David Saker,\" said Bancroft.\n\n\"Our media manager, Kate Hutchison, expressed her objection to what was happening.\n\n\"I recall her making it clear that she believed it was wrong, that she wanted nothing to do with it, and walking away from the discussion.\n\n\"Somebody had demonstrated that being part of the team did not mean having to accept everything the team was doing. And I ignored it.\"\n\nBancroft identifies Saker as the coach Khawaja claimed encouraged him to work harder on the ball during the third Test in Cape Town. The 33-year-old said he took \"feedback\" from all the bowlers and wicketkeeper Tim Paine, who took over the captaincy when Smith was sacked.\n\nIn Cape Town, Bancroft was confronted by the umpires when TV cameras showed him concealing the sandpaper in his trousers. He claimed Lehmann sent a message to him via Handscomb.\n\n\"Peter Handscomb ran out at the drinks break after I'd been caught and shoved the sandpaper down my pants,\" wrote Bancroft.\n\n\"He said to me: 'I think it's time I take that off you now.' And Pete was doing his job after Boof [Lehmanm] shouted to him on the walkie talkie \"get the sandpaper off Bangers [Bancroft] now\".\n\n\"If he didn't know he wouldn't have said that. And if it wasn't clear enough before, then it was obvious now.\"\n\nIn 2021, Bancroft was playing county cricket with Durham and gave an interview to The Guardian in which he said the bowlers' involvement in the plot was \"self-explantory\"."
