    "md5": "9a791031c7a7c88ccf41a99acec59b95",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "eBird exhibit to introduce public to global citizen science\n\nColombo, Nov. 18 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Visitors to the P. B. Karunaratne Memorial Exhibition at the University of Colombo will have an opportunity to learn how they can contribute to global bird research through eBird, an online platform that allows people to record bird sightings and share observations with scientists.\n\nThe exhibit is part of the 50th anniversary celebrations of the Field Ornithology Group of Sri Lanka (FOGSL), which is based in the Department of Zoology and Environment Sciences at the University of Colombo.\n\nThe exhibition aims to introduce schoolchildren and the general public to ornithology and scientific research while encouraging them to become citizen scientists and actively participate in nature conservation.\n\nThrough eBird, people can record birds they observe in their surroundings and contribute valuable information to a global database used by researchers to study bird distribution, population changes and migration patterns.\n\nThe exhibition will explain how ordinary citizens, including birdwatchers, students and nature enthusiasts, can support scientific research by submitting their observations.\n\nSuch public contributions help researchers collect information from different locations and monitor changes in bird populations and their habitats.\n\nFOGSL has been involved in scientific research on Sri Lanka's birdlife, operating bird-banding stations, studying migratory patterns and training generations of citizen scientists over the past five decades.\n\nThe eBird display will be among several interactive exhibits at the P. B. Karunaratne Memorial Exhibition, which will also feature a simulated rainforest, wetland biodiversity displays, bird songs and a live bird-banding station.\n\nVisitors will also be able to learn how researchers use modern technologies, including satellite tagging, to track bird movements across continents."
