    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "Asked about the impact of those purchases on its balance sheet, Seker said the amount was \"statistically ... not a large amount,\" adding that Binance would not need to \u200braise fresh funds to absorb the costs.\n\nSeker said the company publishes its \"proof-of-reserves,\" which show what crypto tokens it holds for customers, though it does not share its financial statements - including profit and loss statements, balance sheets and cash flow figures - with investors, including Abu Dhabi-backed investment group MGX.\n\n\"We focus our public information on the verifiability of actual customer assets,\" he said. \"But P&L, we do not publish ... we don't publish that to anyone, across the board. And that's entirely consistent with the private company \u200bsetup.\"\n\nThe Binance heist, which was the largest \u200bknown crypto theft, has renewed scrutiny of \u2060cyber risks in the crypto industry.\n\nSeker said Binance contacted a number of regulators, including those in Japan, South Korea, Australia, Thailand and New Zealand, within the first 36 to 48 hours of the incident, to address questions about the scale of its \u200bimpact and how the attack unfolded.\n\n\"Think of all the financial services regulators and markets where we are present,\" he said, \u200badding that there was \u2060no one country that led the charge in the investigations.\n\nBinance said the Abu Dhabi Global Market's Financial Services Regulatory Authority is its primary regulator and it pays taxes in jurisdictions where it has a taxable presence, as required by local laws. The firm does not have a formally established headquarters.\n\nSeker said that while discussions with regulators were ongoing, the conversation \u2060had moved \u200bbeyond the heist to broader concerns about security threats facing the crypto industry.\n\n\"The conversations now are 'well, this \u200bis an omnipresent and spectrum-wide threat, and how does an entire ecosystem prepare for this?' It's moved away from the original incident to a much broader conversation,\" he said."
  },
