    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan budget preserves debt reduction path - Fitch Ratings\n\nFitch Ratings - Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 budget signals the government\u2019s ongoing commitment to fiscal discipline and debt reduction while accommodating higher spending on investment and cyclone reconstruction, Fitch Ratings says. Fiscal discipline, demonstrated again by the outperformance of fiscal targets in 2026, and a focus on debt reduction were key factors behind our upgrade of the sovereign rating to \u2018B-\u200c\u2019/Stable from \u2018CCC+\u2019 in September.\n\nThe budget targets a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP, down from an estimated 3.6% in 2026. The 2026 surplus is well above the 2.5% target in last year\u2019s budget and our earlier 2.6% forecast, reflecting stronger revenue. The decline in the surplus in 2027 reflects lower revenue and higher capital spending but remains consistent with a debt reduction path. This implies a 2027 deficit of 4.5% of GDP, wider than the 3.7% in 2026.\n\nBudget assumptions appear credible and broadly align with our forecasts from our September sovereign review. The 2027 primary-surplus target is slightly below our 2.8% forecast because planned spending is higher than we assumed. However, we think there is potential for further under-execution on capex again this year, which could drive a slightly higher surplus.\n\nPrimary expenditure is budgeted at 13.6% of GDP in 2027, above the 13% statutory ceiling under the 2024 Public Finance Management Act (PFMA). The temporary deviation from the PFMA mainly reflects capital spending for reconstruction following Cyclone Ditwah, as well as higher public capex plans. The plans include LKR260 billion carried forward from a supplementary budget for cyclone reconstruction passed in December 2025 but not spent in 2026 as a result of under-execution. This is the second year the government has sought a deviation from the PFMA, although budget under-execution meant the ceiling was not actually breached in 2026."
