    "chunk_size_bytes": 1611,
    "chunk_text": "Primary expenditure is budgeted at 13.6% of GDP in 2027, above the 13% statutory ceiling under the 2024 Public Finance Management Act (PFMA). The temporary deviation from the PFMA mainly reflects capital spending for reconstruction following Cyclone Ditwah, as well as higher public capex plans. The plans include LKR260 billion carried forward from a supplementary budget for cyclone reconstruction passed in December 2025 but not spent in 2026 as a result of under-execution. This is the second year the government has sought a deviation from the PFMA, although budget under-execution meant the ceiling was not actually breached in 2026.\n\nThe budget is broadly aligned with International Monetary Fund programme parameters. This should support the seventh review of Sri Lanka\u2019s Extended Fund Facility. The current programme ends in March 2027, and the government announced the establishment of a committee to assess a potential follow-on arrangement, which could provide a further policy anchor.\n\nSustained efforts to cement revenue gains and expenditure restraint will remain central to achieving ongoing debt reduction and for the future path of the sovereign rating. Over the medium term, the government aims to maintain a primary surplus of 3.0% of GDP, requiring politically difficult trade-offs to comply with the 13% expenditure ceiling under the PFMA. Efforts to boost investment spending could help lift potential GDP growth, which we forecast at 4.2%, and support steady fiscal outcomes. However, this will depend on capex execution, effective targeting and progress on broader structural reforms."
