    "chunk_text": "Despite these impressive early figures, a deeper analytical look reveals a more nuanced commercial picture. When evaluated strictly against global Hollywood behemoths such as Christopher Nolan\u2019s Oppenheimer or other recent tentpole releases, the film\u2019s initial five-day international revenue remains noticeably lower. Declaring the project a complete global crossover success requires qualification.\n\nConsequently, while the film represents a pioneering foray into foreign markets, its ultimate profitability remains anchored to domestic box office performance in India. Nevertheless, this ambitious release model provides vital data and strategic lessons for future Indian productions aiming for international crossover appeal.\n\nBeyond its commercial trajectory, the film has sparked intense debate over its narrative choices and characterisation\u2014most prominently in its portrayal of Ravana, the primary antagonist of the epic.\n\nTraditionally depicted as a ten-headed demon king symbolising moral failing and hubris, Ravana is presented in this adaptation as a deeply nuanced figure: a scholar of extraordinary intellect, a formidable warrior, a master of arts and sciences, and a ruler of vast capacity. This complex portrayal gives the character dramatic weight that rivaled\u2014and in the eyes of many viewers, overshadowed\u2014the heroic arc of Ram.\n\nThe strength of this performance sparked widespread discourse across social media, with one prominent digital influencer cheekily dubbing the release \u201cRavana Leela\u201d to highlight how thoroughly the antagonist commands the screen. By reframing Ravana through a lens of tragic grandeur and intellectual mastery, the film has reopened long-standing discussions around villainy, nuance, and character perspective within ancient Indian literature.\n\nThe film\u2019s release has also reignited the debate surrounding the historical validity of the epic itself, dividing audiences into distinct interpretive camps:"
