    "md5": "38714b6b03c6d4adb9d94feb249b1864",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "Debate behind Budget debate\n\nResting on the legacy of stellar economic achievement of paternal Presidents, Sajith and Namal are scornful of the Government\u2019s mediocre performance and exude confidence that each could revive rapid development. Sajith\u2019s discourse is more social-democratic and pluralist, parsing Buddhist scripture to present its rational core; Namal\u2019s is more hard-charging, bearing a nationalist religio-cultural sub-text. Voters will have two credible options for change\n\nThis year\u2019s Budget debate is different. Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya spotted the change in the Opposition\u2019s \u2018batting order\u2019.\n\n\u2018\u2026\u201cUsually, Opposition MP Harsha de Silva opens the Budget debate on behalf of the Opposition. This has been the practice since I entered Parliament in 2020. We generally prepare ourselves to respond to his remarks. Whether we are in the Government or the Opposition, we have always been ready to listen to his opening statement following a Budget presentation. However, we see a change in that practice today,\u201d she said\u2026\u2019\n\n\u2018\u2026The Prime Minister said the Government\u2026remained keen to hear MP Harsha de Silva\u2019s views on the 2027 Budget.\n\nShe said his remarks would help determine whether there had been a shift in the SJB\u2019s economic policies or whether the party continued to maintain its previous position.\n\nReferring to the remarks made by Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa at the beginning of the debate, the Prime Minister accused him of presenting misleading narratives concerning the economy, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank.\n\nShe said the Government was now awaiting MP Harsha de Silva\u2019s contribution to establish whether other SJB parliamentarians shared the Opposition Leader\u2019s position on these economic matters.\u2019 (Ibid)"
  },
