    "chunk_size_bytes": 1718,
    "chunk_text": "Referring to the remarks made by Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa at the beginning of the debate, the Prime Minister accused him of presenting misleading narratives concerning the economy, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank.\n\nShe said the Government was now awaiting MP Harsha de Silva\u2019s contribution to establish whether other SJB parliamentarians shared the Opposition Leader\u2019s position on these economic matters.\u2019 (Ibid)\n\nMost revealing is the PM\u2019s question as to \u201ca shift in the SJB\u2019s economic policies\u201d (from Harsha\u2019s?) and whether the SJB parliamentarians \u201cshared the Opposition Leader\u2019s position on these economic matters\u201d.\n\nWhat would the NPP Government prefer\u2014that the SJB does or doesn\u2019t share its leader\u2019s position on economic matters?\n\nSajith\u2019s speech showed consistency in his long-standing rejection of \u2018trickle-down\u2019 in favour of balancing \u2018growth with equity\u2019 articulated during Budget debates going back 15 years. Furthermore, he has been advocating \u2018social democracy\u2019 in Parliament since November 2022. Sajith\u2019s speech this time was a more full-blooded elaboration of his basic economic doctrine and critique of Government policy. By opening for his side, the SJB leader signalled that his progressive-centrist economics would be the banner under which he would campaign\u2014and run\u2014against the Government.\n\nThe surreal irony of Harini\u2019s question regarding SJB economic ideology was that all the speeches on the Government side including her own, reflect the degeneration of JVP-NPP economic ideology to a monolithic, dogmatic defence of liberal economic orthodoxy under the rubric of \u2018stabilisation\u2019 and \u2018transformation\u2019."
  },
