    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "The surreal irony of Harini\u2019s question regarding SJB economic ideology was that all the speeches on the Government side including her own, reflect the degeneration of JVP-NPP economic ideology to a monolithic, dogmatic defence of liberal economic orthodoxy under the rubric of \u2018stabilisation\u2019 and \u2018transformation\u2019.\n\nClearly, the ruling JVP-NPP rather than Sajith\u2019s SJB or Namal\u2019s SLPP, has become the main agency of neoliberal economic doctrine, strategy and policy in Sri Lanka.\n\nDr Harsha de Silva\u2019s argument is that economic stabilisation has been positive and successful, the NPP Government\u2019s \u2018reform agenda\u2019 and \u2018direction\u2019 are laudable, but insufficient because growth is now needed. The NPP comfortably concurs, calling it \u2018transformation\u2019. His critique of the Government\u2019s economics is tactical and managerial: ignorance and incompetence in implementation.\n\nHarsha adds an absurdity: the lingering Marxism-Leninism of the JVP foredooms it to failure at generating rapid, sustainable growth in a market economy. He should identify and prove the existence of the \u2018Marxist-Leninist\u2019 component instead of taking Handunetti\u2019s word for it. (Sunil should know it isn\u2019t meant to be \u2018lodged in the heart\u2019, but the head.) Harsha clearly doesn\u2019t agree with Sajith that the JVP has betrayed Marxism/socialism and converted to neoliberalism.\n\nHarsha\u2019s contention on Marxism-Leninism and economics is vaporised by the achievement of the Communist Parties of China and Vietnam. His position is different from Sajith\u2019s contestable yet understandable declaration that China and Vietnam\u2019s economies are capitalist. (I\u2019d say they are better understood as \u2018hybrid systems\u2019 or a \u2018systemic fusion\u2019.) Sajith wisely never contradicts the fact that the foundations and core convictions of the Chinese and Vietnamese CPs are Marxist-Leninist."
