    "chunk_size_bytes": 1884,
    "chunk_text": "Claims only $ 1 b FDI came in 2025 as several projects exited\n\n\u2018\u2026Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa yesterday\u2026charged that the 2027 Budget widens inequality, fails to draw foreign investment and offers no relief to farmers, small businesses or public servants.\n\n\u201cThe poor were made poorer, and the rich were made richer,\u201d he said.\n\n\u2026 The Government had failed to change the debt sustainability analysis (DSA) agreed with the International Monetary Fund (IMF), as promised in its manifesto \u201cA Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life\u201d. He said Lazard, the firm that brokered the deal with international sovereign bondholders, had also handled restructurings for Ghana and Senegal, which secured better terms.\n\nHe took issue with the macro-linked Bonds in the deal, under which debt relief shrinks as economic growth rises.\n\n\u201cThen what incentive or motivation do we have as a country to increase the economic growth rate? As the growth rate rises, the amount of foreign debt we have to repay also increases. Lazard defrauded Sri Lanka,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said there had been no debt restructuring for the 263,000 micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) in distress in 2022, a number he said had since grown. The promised development bank for them had not been set up. The Government had suspended the parate law, which lets banks auction pledged property without going to court, but had not restructured MSME debt, he said.\n\n\u2026He said farmers were promised Rs. 150 per kg of paddy, but prices had fallen to a maximum of Rs. 95. He said the promised interest-free loans and price support through small and medium millers had not materialised, and that cultivation of big onions, potatoes, chillies, upland crops, fruit, export crops and upcountry vegetables had collapsed.\n\n\u201cYou have not provided even an atom of support for agriculture through this Budget,\u201d he said."
