    "chunk_text": "Greater tax powers need greater taxpayer safeguards\n\nBudget 2027 has made a welcome start in fixing the fine print of tax administration. But as the Commissioner General is equipped with stronger enforcement tools, taxpayers need an equally strong safeguard when the system gets it wrong. An independent Tax Ombudsman should be the next step, not an afterthought. Fair taxes require fair process. And fair process requires somewhere for the taxpayer to turn\n\nBudget 2027 makes welcome changes to the fine print of tax administration. But as enforcement powers expand, the absence of an independent Tax Ombudsman remains a significant gap\n\nWhen a Budget is read, the headlines naturally go to the big numbers: a higher tax-free threshold, wider tax slabs, a levy abolished or a tariff cut. These measures grab attention because people feel them directly in their pay packets and shopping baskets.\n\nYet some of the more consequential proposals in Budget 2027 are tucked away in the annexures, expressed in the technical language of section numbers and procedure. They may not trend on social media, but they shape the day-to-day relationship between the taxpayer and the tax administration.\n\nOne passage in the Budget Speech is particularly important. Under \u201cStrengthening Tax Administration and Facilitating Taxpayer Compliance\u201d, the Government acknowledges that stringent procedural requirements can result in taxpayers being denied legitimate refunds or input tax credits because of technical or procedural non-compliance. It proposes to remove ambiguities, accommodate evolving business practices and create what it describes as a \u201cfair, transparent, and efficient tax administration system\u201d.\n\nThat is an important acknowledgement. Tax administration should protect Government revenue, but it should also protect taxpayers\u2019 legitimate entitlements."
