    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "82a3f799e2002da13a056da8b3623e86",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 958,
    "chunk_text": "If enforcement powers are strengthened on one side, the institutional mechanisms protecting taxpayers must evolve on the other. This is why the absence of a Tax Ombudsman is clearly missed opportunity in Budget 2027.\n\nEvery legitimate credit denied, every refund unnecessarily delayed and every uncertainty left unresolved weakens that confidence. Every ambiguity removed, every procedure made more workable and every genuine entitlement protected strengthens it.\n\nBudget 2027 has made a welcome start in fixing the fine print of tax administration. But as the Commissioner General is equipped with stronger enforcement tools, taxpayers need an equally strong safeguard when the system gets it wrong. An independent Tax Ombudsman should be the next step, not an afterthought. Fair taxes require fair process. And fair process requires somewhere for the taxpayer to turn.\n\n(The views expressed in this article are those of the author in her personal capacity)"
  },
