    "md5": "ea19e86c5244e90e5c9ea64dd5e63848",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1519,
    "chunk_text": "2027 Budget: Red herring to neutralise public and relink Sri Lanka with new financial colonialism\n\nTrade Unions and People\u2019s Power, a coalition of 38 trade unions and civil society organisations, in a statement on Tuesday raised concerns that the 2027 Budget shifts the burden onto the public, with indirect taxes set to rise by Rs. 170 billion against just Rs. 30 billion in direct taxes, while subsidies and transfers are cut by Rs. 100 billion and corporate tax holidays continue.\n\nThe group said 85% of the expected increase in tax revenue would fall on the general public. It also accused the Government of deliberately underspending Budget allocations to create surpluses that would help Sri Lanka return to International Sovereign Bond (ISB) markets under the International Monetary Fund (IMF) program.\n\nThe statement is as follows: \u201cPro-Government entities and the neoliberal apologists broadly hail the 2027 Budget as a step in the right direction, claiming that it balances the cost-of-living concerns of the public with business investment aspirations. While Sri Lanka\u2019s corporate sector benefits by maintaining enormous tax breaks, this mainstream view exaggerates the crumbs thrown to the general public through indirect tax relief on a few essentials. Reducing taxes on sprats, dry fish and exercise books\u2014alongside nominal salary increases for Government servants\u2014will do nothing to halt the worsening living costs or reverse the steep cuts in essential spending mandated by the 2027 proposals."
  },
