    "md5": "1709d18e8e37076a315d53fa81a215f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1707,
    "chunk_text": "The statement is as follows: \u201cPro-Government entities and the neoliberal apologists broadly hail the 2027 Budget as a step in the right direction, claiming that it balances the cost-of-living concerns of the public with business investment aspirations. While Sri Lanka\u2019s corporate sector benefits by maintaining enormous tax breaks, this mainstream view exaggerates the crumbs thrown to the general public through indirect tax relief on a few essentials. Reducing taxes on sprats, dry fish and exercise books\u2014alongside nominal salary increases for Government servants\u2014will do nothing to halt the worsening living costs or reverse the steep cuts in essential spending mandated by the 2027 proposals.\n\nThe burden of higher indirect taxes and soaring living costs also restricts productive investments. Higher living costs inevitably fuel wage demands, inflating production costs without raising real wages. This erodes the competitive power of domestic producers in both domestic and global markets, accelerating the flight of manufacturing investments, while driving speculative and foreign-exchange-draining capital into property and real estate and illicit capital flows to hedge against inflation.\n\nConversely, shifting towards a higher direct-to-indirect tax ratio by revoking corporate income tax breaks and enforcing strict tax compliance would ease the cost of living, curb inflation, and lift families out of poverty and child malnutrition, while improving fiscal stability (a recent IMF study shows government revenue can rise by as much as 1.9% of Gross Domestic Product merely through enforcing tax compliance and upgrading the technological infrastructure of tax collecting institutions)."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
