    "md5": "3f135223d5c744693c848c4f1256d7b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1559,
    "chunk_text": "Conversely, shifting towards a higher direct-to-indirect tax ratio by revoking corporate income tax breaks and enforcing strict tax compliance would ease the cost of living, curb inflation, and lift families out of poverty and child malnutrition, while improving fiscal stability (a recent IMF study shows government revenue can rise by as much as 1.9% of Gross Domestic Product merely through enforcing tax compliance and upgrading the technological infrastructure of tax collecting institutions).\n\nExamining the striking gap between the 2026 Budget\u2019s expenditure proposals and actual Government spending so far this year reveals a clear playbook: essential public investments (excluding highways and expressways) are choked through relentless and deliberate underspending. This has become the trademark strategy of the current administration: neutralise public outrage through exaggerated spending figures during Budget speeches, and fulfil the targets of global speculative capital\u2014represented by the International Monetary Fund (IMF)\u2014by brutally underspending budgetary allocations to manufacture artificial budget surpluses.\n\nThis disingenuous strategy is laid bare by the current Government utilising only 8% of the funds allocated for Cyclone Ditwah victims by mid-2026, according to the World Bank. By overachieving revenue targets and underspending 2026 budgetary allocations by roughly Rs. 1 trillion compared to the proposed Rs. 7 trillion, the Government has manufactured a surplus of Rs. 22 billion on the back of neglected public services."
  },
