    "md5": "25e34d676812f1098402e15e4da36125",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "This disingenuous strategy is laid bare by the current Government utilising only 8% of the funds allocated for Cyclone Ditwah victims by mid-2026, according to the World Bank. By overachieving revenue targets and underspending 2026 budgetary allocations by roughly Rs. 1 trillion compared to the proposed Rs. 7 trillion, the Government has manufactured a surplus of Rs. 22 billion on the back of neglected public services.\n\nThe primary goal of the current regime is to manufacture budget surpluses to appease international speculative finance and regain the capacity to issue new International Sovereign Bonds (ISBs) under IMF mandates. The fifth and sixth IMF reviews under the External Fund Facility (EFF) for Sri Lanka clearly project $ 3 billion in new ISB borrowings for 2027 and 2028. Slashing Government spending and raising regressive taxes are manoeuvres designed solely to lower the risk premiums demanded by international finance capital.\n\nEver since the 2008/09 Western financial collapse, global speculative finance has aggressively targeted the Global South as a high-interest dumping ground for surplus capital, disregarding and violating prudent lending practices, and relying on the IMF to extract repayments through restructuring deals favourable to itself\u2014such as the one signed onto by Sri Lanka in 2024\u2014when third world debt bubbles ultimately burst. This marks the new wave of financial colonisation of the Global South.\n\nSri Lanka has remained decoupled from this cycle for the past five years due to the 2022 economic collapse, but the IMF\u2019s primary mission today is to forcefully reintegrate Sri Lanka into global financial markets as a vehicle for surplus extraction. This is precisely why the IMF opposes exchange controls in the third world. These measures will enable the region to function independently of global speculative capital, delivering a lethal blow to this new form of dependency and surplus extraction towards the core from the periphery."
  },
