    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Recovery for whom?\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa responding to the 2027 Budget speech in Parliament\n\nThe current Government deserves credit for sustaining the nominal stability that the previous Government achieved. However, having exceeded the IMF fiscal targets, it should now slow the pace of consolidation and move decisively to a credible growth plan, at least 8% growth sustained for a generation, driven by private investment, exports and productivity. With that in mind, the poorest fifth has lost almost a fifth of its real income and support for them is being cut by Rs. 100 billion, and this asks us to rethink, and the valid question raised in Parliament by the Opposition Leader, recovery for whom?\n\nReading the economics behind the Opposition Leader\u2019s reply to Budget\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake presented Budget 2027 to Parliament on 12 November, with a message of recovery, since the Budget promised growth of 7% or more. The next day, Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa made a response. This article tests this speech against official figures and my own comments.\n\nThe speech\u2019s main argument concerns the distribution of income. \u201cBetween 2019 and 2025, income has been redistributed upwards again from the poor to the rich,\u201d the Opposition Leader said. According to the Household Income and Expenditure Surveys, the real household income of the poorest 20% fell by 19.6% over this period, while that of the richest 20% rose by 6.8%. The richest fifth now earns 14.8 times as much as the poorest fifth, up from 11.2 times in 2019. \u201cThe poor have been made poorer and the rich richer,\u201d he said."
