    "chunk_text": "The speech\u2019s main argument concerns the distribution of income. \u201cBetween 2019 and 2025, income has been redistributed upwards again from the poor to the rich,\u201d the Opposition Leader said. According to the Household Income and Expenditure Surveys, the real household income of the poorest 20% fell by 19.6% over this period, while that of the richest 20% rose by 6.8%. The richest fifth now earns 14.8 times as much as the poorest fifth, up from 11.2 times in 2019. \u201cThe poor have been made poorer and the rich richer,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cThe World Bank report of October 2026 says that 60% of our people are poor,\u201d the Opposition Leader cites. The World Bank estimates that 61.1% of Sri Lankans lived below the upper-middle-income poverty line of $ 8.30 a day in 2025. He further questions the official poverty line of Rs. 17,742 a month: \u201cCan one person live for 30 days on Rs. 17,000 or 18,000?\u201d Yet subsidies and transfers fall from Rs. 1,572 billion in 2026 to Rs. 1,472 billion in 2027, a cut of Rs. 100 billion.\n\nIn 2025, the Government ran a primary surplus of 5.4% of GDP, more than twice the IMF target of 2.3%, partly by leaving capital budgets unspent. Taxes on goods, services and trade will bring in about 76.4% of tax revenue in 2027, and they weigh most on the poor. Over-collecting from consumers while cutting support for the poorest is questionable.\n\n\u201cNothing is incoming; everything is outgoing,\u201d the Opposition Leader further states, listing projects that have left, including the China Merchants logistics hub at the Port of Colombo. On garment firms expanding abroad, he asked: \u201cThey close one factory here and open 10, 15 or 20 there. How can we create jobs?\u201d"
