    "chunk_size_bytes": 1739,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cNothing is incoming; everything is outgoing,\u201d the Opposition Leader further states, listing projects that have left, including the China Merchants logistics hub at the Port of Colombo. On garment firms expanding abroad, he asked: \u201cThey close one factory here and open 10, 15 or 20 there. How can we create jobs?\u201d\n\nThe NPP manifesto promised to renegotiate the debt sustainability analysis (DSA) with the IMF. \u201cWhere is the promise to change the DSA? This was the greatest deception,\u201d the Opposition Leader asks. On the macro-linked bonds, under which debt relief shrinks if growth exceeds expectations, he asked: \u201cThen what incentive, what motivation, do we have as a country to raise our growth rate?\u201d\n\nThe idea that part of the gain from faster growth flows automatically to foreign creditors while reducing gain for the country is correct. However, the pressing issue is financing. The deficit rises to Rs. 1,753 billion, or 4.5% of GDP, and 94% of it is to be borrowed at home. The gross borrowing requirement is Rs. 3,800 billion, almost 10% of GDP. The 91-day Treasury Bill yield has already risen from 8.2% in May to 9.35% in November, crowding out private investment. And the IMF program ends in March 2027; with a Fitch's sovereign rating B\u2212, borrowing abroad would cost around 11%. Where is the plan for life after the IMF?\n\n\u201cThe paddy price that was promised at Rs. 150 a kilo is today 98 or 95,\u201d the Opposition Leader reminded the Parliament, adding that the Budget had given agriculture \u201cnot even the weight of a mustard seed\u201d. He noted that about 263,000 small and medium enterprises fell into difficulty in 2022, yet there is still no national development bank and no restructuring of their loans."
