    "chunk_size_bytes": 1610,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe paddy price that was promised at Rs. 150 a kilo is today 98 or 95,\u201d the Opposition Leader reminded the Parliament, adding that the Budget had given agriculture \u201cnot even the weight of a mustard seed\u201d. He noted that about 263,000 small and medium enterprises fell into difficulty in 2022, yet there is still no national development bank and no restructuring of their loans.\n\nThe Opposition Leader listed unfulfilled promises, from a 33% cut in electricity bills to relief for pensioners, and proposed giving vehicle permits to professionals instead of politicians, \u201cwe do not want them. Give them to those professionals.\u201d\n\nThe NPP promised to raise the PAYE tax-free threshold to Rs. 200,000 a month, however, the Budget raises it only to Rs. 162,500. For a doctor, engineer or senior academic, relief of at most Rs. 15,750 a month will not change a decision to emigrate. As a head of department, I see brain drain in our universities every year. The Budget should have set up a high-level task force to retain skilled people and bring back those abroad, looking into due recognitions and rewards.\n\nThe Opposition Leader summed up his case by saying the Government has \u201csignalled left and turned right\u201d and playing on the Sinhala word for Budget: \u201cThis is not an ayawaya [Budget]; it is a pachawaya [a Budget of tall tales].\u201d As an alternative, he called for \u201cthe middle path, social democracy and a social market economy\u201d. A social market economy would let markets drive growth and investment while the State protects the vulnerable, which is exactly the balance Budget 2027 lacks."
