    "chunk_text": "Consistency and reform focus good; people, institutions and action need fixing\n\nTreasury Secretary, Dr. Harshana Suriyapperuma said Budget 2027 has delivered continuity and consistency, with no major deviations from Government discussions with the business community and citizens. Addressing the gathering at the post-Budget forum hosted by KPMG, Dr. Suriyapperuma stated the Government is ready to listen and work with the private sector, adding Free Trade Agreements will play a significant role in the journey ahead as our market is too small.\n\nOpening the forum, KPMG Country Manager Suren Rajakarier noted the IMF program offers Sri Lanka a good policy buffer amidst the global exigencies. In a recorded statement, Deputy Minister of Finance Dr. Anil Jayantha Fernando, stressed the need to create a conducive environment for investment and safeguard macro stability. He said the Government is striving to achieve a sustainable 7% annual growth, and enable rural communities to be better engaged in larger economic activity. The Deputy Minister pointed to the need for a central platform to attract investment to the country, adding the Government hopes to put in place the legal framework to achieve that end through parliament early next year. This will include structural changes to the public service.\n\nHis comments were echoed by the Treasury Secretary, who said the Government is very serious on refining the legal system to ensure all four corners of the nation are tapped into development and economic expansion.\n\n\u201cEnergy is a big-ticket item, and there is a lot of focus on Mannar basin. We have negotiated prices down with some projects and the benefits will be felt by all businesses and citizens of the country. Those best suited for the job will get the job. Digitisation"
  },
