    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cEnergy is a big-ticket item, and there is a lot of focus on Mannar basin. We have negotiated prices down with some projects and the benefits will be felt by all businesses and citizens of the country. Those best suited for the job will get the job. Digitisation\n\nDuring the panel stage, MillenniumIT ESP CEO Harendra Samarasinghe noted that despite ambitions to reach $ 5 billion in ICT exports by 2030, Sri Lanka doesn\u2019t have enough graduates coming out of the system to meet that target. Samarasinghe said Sri Lanka must attract more Global Capability Centres, but the process of attracting and nurturing talent to suit that end needs a serious rethink. On the other hand, he saw the current global challenges as a \u201cperfect storm\u201d, where everything is in shock and almost every sector and company must reimagine themselves to stay current.\n\nICT Agency Chairman and Presidential Adviser Dr. Hans Wijayasuriya, enthused the budget has set out a multipronged approach to the digital economy, including the provision of high-speed broadband to all parts of the country as a priority. This will be completed by 2029. Dr. Wijayasuriya, too, pointed to the potential of data centres, but said Sri Lanka is energy constrained, and expressed hope this sphere be pursued more aggressively come 2028 following planned enhancements to power generation.\n\n\u201cThe Digital Economy Authority is a principal tool that will drive, regulate and manage the digital transformation. The confluence of several regulatory bodies including the TRCSL and other agencies will further strengthen the digital economy, where the council will be the apex body. It will include the President, so that we can provide quick responses to any query. We must establish the necessary regulations and set in place the cybersecurity so we stay ahead of the curve,\u201d Dr. Wijayasuriya said."
  },
