    "md5": "81afac55f6173c7f299de5a5883db98d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1618,
    "chunk_text": "Responding to his concerns during the panel discussion, Treasury Secretary Dr. Suriyapperuma noted the amendments to the Customs Ordinance are the final stage and it could come into effect early next year, and said attracting talent is a key factor for Government, and a renewed KPI-driven approach is being mooted in this regard.\n\nAddressing the second half of the forum, Industry and Enterprenuership Development Deputy Minister Chathuranga Abeysinghe, averred the Public-Private Partnership bill will be a game change for the country, and called on the private sector for active participation once enacted. He identified logistics, tourism, energy and IT as the primary sectors that will attract investment into Sri Lanka at this juncture, and said in terms of fiscal orientation the Government is very private sector focused. At the same time, fiscal discipline is critical for central and local Government, and there will be no leakage and digitisation will help that. Abeysinghe said, the Government has made no new appointments to the public service that are unnecessary.\n\n\u201cThere are many changes in the regulatory aspect, and more are coming. There is a pilot underway where you could register a business in just two days. Access to market is a barrier we are struggling with, especially for SMEs, but we are looking to support them by going digital. Our programs are aligned to help them scale all the way to even reach global markets. But we should get rid of the word protection. Our consumers must have the best. We need to have a set of standards for what is coming into the country,\u201d Abeysinghe said."
