    "md5": "0ef3734f34bbe70cc2d45af082d84fbc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2008,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Can Sri Lanka turn ambition into growth?\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget, presented by the President on 12 November 2026, is an important test of the country\u2019s economic recovery. It comes at a critical juncture: the current IMF program is due to conclude in March 2027, debt remains a major constraint, and the economy must move from stabilisation towards sustainable, investment-led growth.\n\nThe Budget has several positive features. It recognises the need to increase capital investment, strengthen revenue administration, support SMEs, improve infrastructure and accelerate digital transformation. The proposed public investment of approximately Rs. 2 trillion, equivalent to 5.12% of GDP, including the supplementary allocation for Cyclone Ditwah recovery, is significant. However, the real test will not be the size of the allocation, but how much is translated into productive investment.\n\nSri Lanka has repeatedly demonstrated an ability to announce ambitious budgets. Its more persistent weakness has been implementation.\n\nThe Government\u2019s gross borrowing requirement of Rs. 3.8 trillion raises important questions about financing sustainability. With no International Sovereign Bond issuance explicitly indicated in the 2027 borrowing plan, the extent to which domestic financial institutions will be required to finance Government borrowing deserves scrutiny. Excessive reliance on domestic borrowing could crowd out private-sector credit and increase the banking system\u2019s exposure to sovereign debt.\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake making his way along the Parliament corridors on 12 November on his way to deliver the 2027 Budget Speech\n\nApproximately Rs. 4,850 billion is required for debt servicing, equivalent to nearly 79% of projected Government revenue of approximately Rs. 6.15 trillion. With foreign debt-service obligations expected to rise further in 2028, the Government must demonstrate how it intends to build adequate foreign-exchange buffers."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-19-dailyftlk-94076ac8",
    "num": "dailyftlk-94076ac8",
    "date_str": "2026-11-19",
    "description": "Budget 2027: Can Sri Lanka turn ambition into growth?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Budget-2027-Can-Sri-Lanka-turn-ambition-into-growth/4-800274",
    "lang": "en",
