    "time_ut": 1795050417.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailyftlk-94076ac8-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7be9257a5ee2973c023f6ec64e48ff11",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Approximately Rs. 4,850 billion is required for debt servicing, equivalent to nearly 79% of projected Government revenue of approximately Rs. 6.15 trillion. With foreign debt-service obligations expected to rise further in 2028, the Government must demonstrate how it intends to build adequate foreign-exchange buffers.\n\nThe Budget\u2019s tax proposals provide some relief. Increasing the personal income tax-free threshold to Rs. 162,500 per month will benefit salaried employees, while reducing CESS rates on 800 selected Harmonised System (HS) code lines by 25% could lower trade costs and improve competitiveness.\n\nHowever, these tax concessions are estimated to result in a net annual revenue loss of approximately Rs. 28.4 billion. The Government must balance relief measures with revenue mobilisation and ensure that these concessions do not compromise its revenue targets.\n\nThe most important question is whether the Government can execute its ambitious capital program. Historical performance shows that only 64% of the capital budget was utilised in 2023, 54% in 2024 and 76% in 2025. Increasing allocations without addressing procurement delays, fragmented approvals, weak project management and shortages of technical expertise will not deliver the desired economic returns.\n\nThe Government must move towards a project-based accountability system. Every major investment should have a clearly identified project owner, measurable milestones, a realistic cash-flow plan and quarterly progress reporting. Projects should be assessed for economic viability and implementation readiness before funds are allocated.\n\nEqually important is the capacity of the public service to deliver. The Government must attract and retain the engineers, procurement specialists and project managers required to implement an investment program of this scale. Budget allocations must translate into completed infrastructure rather than remain unspent provisions."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
