    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailyftlk-94076ac8-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bfb40c2606a182ac4281001d028f9948",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Equally important is the capacity of the public service to deliver. The Government must attract and retain the engineers, procurement specialists and project managers required to implement an investment program of this scale. Budget allocations must translate into completed infrastructure rather than remain unspent provisions.\n\nThe Government\u2019s medium-term ambition of achieving sustainable economic growth of 7% or higher will require a more competitive investment environment, predictable policies, faster approvals and stronger investor protection. The challenge is how quickly the Government can translate its ambitions into an investable project pipeline capable of attracting domestic and foreign investors.\n\nThe proposed SME financing programs are welcome, but credit schemes must be supported by appropriate risk-sharing arrangements, sound lending criteria and transparent reporting of repayment performance. Ultimately, sustainable SME growth must come from improving productivity, market access and competitiveness rather than relying solely on subsidised credit.\n\nDigital transformation offers an opportunity to improve tax collection, public procurement and service delivery. Investment in revenue administration systems is particularly important if Sri Lanka is to broaden its tax base and improve compliance. Digital project tracking, e-procurement and AI-based analytics could also help identify implementation delays, reduce inefficiencies and strengthen public accountability.\n\nHowever, technology investment must be accompanied by investment in people. Government officers need the digital, analytical and AI competencies to use these systems effectively. Cybersecurity and the protection of citizens\u2019 personal data are equally important.\n\nParliament, business chambers and citizens should also have access to regular progress reports showing how Budget proposals are being implemented, how much has been spent and what outcomes have been achieved."
  },
