    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5fd3fe6d5e0b5349fee5eca4c5348e78",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 994,
    "chunk_text": "Parliament, business chambers and citizens should also have access to regular progress reports showing how Budget proposals are being implemented, how much has been spent and what outcomes have been achieved.\n\nBudget 2027 has the potential to become a turning point. But potential is not performance. The real measure of success will be whether the Government can convert its plans into working projects, its allocations into productive assets and its reforms into sustainable economic opportunities.\n\nUltimately, the Government\u2019s challenge is not only to grow revenue through taxes and fees and strengthen public finances, but also to grow the economy, create productive employment and improve the livelihoods of ordinary people, thereby reducing poverty. Fiscal discipline must be a means to an end, not an end in itself.\n\nThe true measure of Budget 2027 will be whether economic growth translates into better incomes, greater opportunities and a higher quality of life for all Sri Lankans."
  },
