    "md5": "8d30cd6a52db5b9584dd2a8d74d72ef2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "Prime Lands Residencies delivers higher 1H revenue growth and profitability\n\nPrime Lands Residencies PLC said it reported revenue of Rs. 7.6 billion in the six months to end-September 2026, up 48% from a year ago.\n\nProfit for the period increased 10% to Rs. 1.03 billion, compared to Rs. 940.7 million a year earlier, reflecting continued revenue growth and an expansion in gross and operating profits. Gross profit improved 31% to Rs. 2.11 billion, while operating profit rose 33% to Rs. 1.47 billion, demonstrating the company\u2019s ability to sustain growth at the operating level. Profit before taxation increased by 9% to Rs. 1.41 billion, while earnings per share improved to Rs. 1.10 from Rs. 1.00.\n\nExecutive Chairperson Sandamini Perera added: \u201cThe notable percent growth in revenue demonstrates the strength of our business performance during the period under review. Improvements in gross and operating profits provide a solid foundation for the remainder of the financial year. Our focus remains on translating revenue growth into stronger earnings and maintaining the financial capacity to support the company\u2019s continuing business objectives.\u201d\n\nThe robust 1H results for FY2026/27 position Prime Lands Residencies on a positive earnings trajectory for the 2026/27 financial year. With revenue, gross profit and operating profit all recording year-on-year growth, the company plans to sustain and improve on its operational performance, cost management and financial discipline as it progresses through the second half of the year.\n\nThe company\u2019s financial position also remains strong. As at 30 September 2026, total equity stood at Rs. 11.72 billion, up from Rs. 11.44 billion at 31st March 2026, even after dividends of Rs. 750 million were paid to shareholders during the half-year. Net cash generated from operating activities was Rs. 4.15 billion for the six months, and bank overdrafts were reduced to Rs. 179 million from Rs. 2.15 billion at the start of the period."
