    "md5": "fca294c94b38e3fc2bc5acc8990577e1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "Economists to focus on knowledge economy as Sri Lanka grapples with eroding return on capital\n\nSLEA President Prof. Sirimevan Colombage (second from left) addresses the media briefing of the SLEA Annual International Conference 2026 in Colombo, accompanied by (from left) Vice President Dr. B. L. J. S. Balasooriya, Immediate Past President Prof. Ven. W. Wimalaratana Thero and General Secretary Nihal Rodrigo - Pic by Ruwan Walpola\n\nSLEA conference on Saturday\u00a0 to chart path to knowledge economy\n\nSLEA President says investment needed to generate Rs. 1 of GDP has doubled since 1960\n\nExports down to 14% of GDP from 26% in 1960; high-tech only 1.7% of manufactured exports\n\nAs Sri Lanka grapples with eroding returns on capital, with the investment needed to generate Rs. 1 of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) doubling since 1960, the Sri Lanka Economic Association (SLEA) will hold its Annual International Conference on 21 November under the theme \u201cPaving the Way for Sri Lanka\u2019s Knowledge Economy\u201d, SLEA President Prof. Sirimevan Colombage said yesterday.\n\n\u201cIn 1960, if we invested Rs. 3, we could increase our GDP by Rs. 1. In 2025, we have to invest Rs. 6. That means we have to invest more and more to increase income,\u201d Prof. Colombage said, citing the incremental capital output ratio (ICOR), which measures how much investment is needed to generate an additional unit of output. Declining returns on capital were the reason the country must shift to a knowledge economy, he said.\n\nThe conference, organised by the SLEA in collaboration with the Gamani Corea Foundation, will be held at the Bandaranaike Memorial International Conference Hall (BMICH) in Colombo. It will bring together local and international economists, policymakers and practitioners to discuss knowledge-driven growth strategies as the country prepares to resume external debt repayments in 2028, following economic stabilisation under the International Monetary Fund (IMF) framework in the wake of the 2022 sovereign debt default."
