    "md5": "d923124a9f2ea5969b3f074e5c46e245",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "The conference, organised by the SLEA in collaboration with the Gamani Corea Foundation, will be held at the Bandaranaike Memorial International Conference Hall (BMICH) in Colombo. It will bring together local and international economists, policymakers and practitioners to discuss knowledge-driven growth strategies as the country prepares to resume external debt repayments in 2028, following economic stabilisation under the International Monetary Fund (IMF) framework in the wake of the 2022 sovereign debt default.\n\n\u201cOther countries have rapidly developed and achieved upper middle-income and high-income levels. But Sri Lanka is trapped in the lower middle-income range,\u201d he said.\n\nAverage growth since 1978 has been around 3.5% to 4%, against more than 8% in fast-growing economies, he said. Sri Lanka achieved 8% growth on only two occasions: after economic liberalisation in 1978 and after the end of the war in 2009. Growth is expected at around 3% to 4% this year, and Central Bank and IMF projections put it at only about 3% over the next three years. Around 22% of the population lives below the poverty line, while the migration of young people is causing a loss of human capital.\n\nExport-led growth, the objective of liberalisation, has not materialised, Prof. Colombage said. Exports as a share of GDP fell from around 26% in 1960 to 14% in 2025. In 1960, exports were sufficient to finance 90% of imports, but by 2025 they covered only 68%.\n\n\u201cWe have become more and more inward-oriented rather than outward-oriented,\u201d he said.\n\nGarments still account for nearly 50% of manufactured exports, even as countries such as Vietnam and Bangladesh have opened up their economies. High-tech exports such as microchips and electronics make up only about 1.7% of Sri Lanka\u2019s manufactured exports, against about 30% in South Korea.\n\n\u201cOur export industry is very much basic, I am rather disappointed to say,\u201d Prof. Colombage said."
  },
