    "chunk_text": "Garments still account for nearly 50% of manufactured exports, even as countries such as Vietnam and Bangladesh have opened up their economies. High-tech exports such as microchips and electronics make up only about 1.7% of Sri Lanka\u2019s manufactured exports, against about 30% in South Korea.\n\n\u201cOur export industry is very much basic, I am rather disappointed to say,\u201d Prof. Colombage said.\n\n\u201cWe are rather allergic to foreign direct investment,\u201d he said.\n\nThe innovation phase requires heavy investment in research and development and education, but Sri Lanka spends less than 2% of GDP on education, and expectations of raising it to 6% have not materialised, he said.\n\nProf. Colombage acknowledged that Sri Lanka has fairly strong science and technology institutions, including the National Science Foundation and the National Science and Technology Commission. He pointed to a recent Budget proposal to amalgamate science and technology institutions under one umbrella, as well as digitalisation initiatives.\n\n\u201cSuccessive governments have not completely neglected this. But we need an aggressive policy framework aimed at promoting science and technology. So far, no government has produced that kind of framework,\u201d he said.\n\nThe SLEA, established in 1985 and incorporated by an Act of Parliament in 2011, has more than 900 active members. Since February, it has held two public economic forums and six stakeholder seminars with the Gamani Corea Foundation.\n\nFor The international conference, Prof. Colombage said the SLEA chose knowledge economy as a cross-cutting theme spanning agriculture, industry and services, after recent annual sessions focused on each sector."
