    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s young people most optimistic in world: Oxford Scholastica Academy study",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Sri-Lanka-s-young-people-most-optimistic-in-world-Oxford-Scholastica-Academy-study/34-800291",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795052613.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailyftlk-bc011c0f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6a59e282aaa7f8aef70c373dd5cde5eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s young people most optimistic in world: Oxford Scholastica Academy study\n\nA new global survey of young people has ranked youth optimism about the future across 100 countries, with students from 41 countries providing sufficient responses, measuring how hopeful students feel about their personal futures, the economy, the environment, politics, and the future of society as a whole.\n\nThe study by Oxford summer school provider, Oxford Scholastica Academy, received responses from 1,433 students to discover where Gen Z are the most and least optimistic about their future.\n\nSri Lankan students are the most optimistic in the world, scoring 4.32 out of 5 overall, while young people in Kuwait are the least hopeful, with an overall score of just 2.92.\n\nSri Lanka leads the study with young people reporting the highest rates of optimism, with the highest environmental optimism score of any country in the study (4.62) and strong personal optimism (4.75). Despite significant economic turbulence in recent years, Sri Lankan students remain deeply hopeful about the future of their society (4.25).\n\nThe second most optimistic country is Kenya, with students surveyed scoring 4.31 overall. Kenyan students marked feeling optimistic about the future of society (4.43, joint highest in the study alongside Cyprus) and record the highest political optimism of any country surveyed (3.96).\n\nNepal achieves a perfect personal optimism score of 5.00 \u2013 the only country to do so and ranked third overall. Nepal also has the highest economic optimism (4.80), suggesting that despite its lower-income status, students feel strongly positive about their individual prospects.\n\nKazakhstan is the fourth most optimistic nation according to the students polled, students report high economic confidence (4.56) along with the third-highest political optimism in the study (3.76)."
  },
