    "md5": "c23df06ec81f7a494be735727a6dcdf1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "Manufacturers seek power and tax relief in Budget 2027\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s main industrial chamber called for relief on electricity tariffs and corporate taxes, saying Budget 2027 would expand access to credit for the small businesses while leaving the domestic factories under pressure from the operating costs.\n\nCeylon National Chamber of Industries (CNCI) Chairman Pradeep Kahawalage said the budget lacked \u201cbroad-based relief on industrial power tariffs or corporate taxes to ease the operational pressures on the domestic factories\u201d.\n\nThe CNCI also pointed to the absence of large industrial or construction projects that could attract private-sector investment and generate orders for local manufacturers. It called for tighter oversight of public investment, from planning through execution, to ensure the funds are used on time.\n\nKahawalage welcomed the allocation of Rs.67.7 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing, which the CNCI said was expected to unlock Rs.174 billion in concessional loans for entrepreneurs through state and private banks.\n\nHe said the financing would support the micro, small and medium enterprises, which the government has placed at the centre of its plans to expand production.\n\nThe CNCI also backed the plans to develop the Valachchenai Industrial Estate and fund feasibility studies and preliminary planning for an estate in Puliyankulam.\n\nThe budget seeks to accelerate work in Ingiriya, Dambulla, Raigama, Millaniya and Katunayake and upgrade the infrastructure at the existing estates. Kahawalage urged the officials to ensure timely spending across the government\u2019s wider investment programme including transport, highways, railways, water supply, irrigation, health, education, housing, urban development, energy, digital infrastructure, ports and regional projects.\n\nHe also backed the push to digitise public administration, saying it could improve the efficiency and transparency of the government operations."
