    "md5": "45c7f01c6af64dd3a91975228bd5a60e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s Budget 2027 faces a test of investment delivery: Fitch\n\nPrimary surplus could exceed 2.3% of GDP target, if capital spending falls short again\n\nBudget carries forward Rs.260bn in unspent Cyclone Ditwah reconstruction funds\n\nFitch says wider deficit is consistent with falling debt and budget broadly aligns with IMF programme\n\nSri Lanka could again beat its primary surplus target in 2027, if it falls short on public investment, Fitch Ratings said yesterday, pointing to a risk hanging over the government\u2019s spending plans after Rs.260 billion earmarked for cyclone reconstruction went unspent in 2026.\n\nFitch said Budget 2027 kept Sri Lanka on a debt reduction path, despite a wider deficit. It forecast that the interest payments would still absorb about 41 percent of government revenue this year, more than three times the median for the sovereigns in the \u2018B\u2019 rating category.\n\nThe government is targeting a primary surplus of 2.3 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 2027, down from an estimated 3.6 percent this year, as revenue moderates and capital spending rises. The overall deficit is projected to widen to 4.5 percent of GDP, from 3.7 percent.\n\nStronger revenue put the 2026 primary surplus well ahead of the government\u2019s 2.5 percent target and Fitch\u2019s earlier forecast of 2.6 percent. Fitch said the outperformance and the government\u2019s focus on reducing debt were key factors behind its September decision to upgrade Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating to \u2018B-\u2019, from \u2018CCC+\u2019, with a stable outlook.\n\nFitch said the budget assumptions appeared credible and were broadly in line with its September sovereign review. The government\u2019s primary surplus target is below Fitch\u2019s forecast of 2.8 percent of GDP because the planned spending is higher than the agency had assumed. Another shortfall in capital spending could push the surplus above the budget target, Fitch said."
  },
