    "md5": "d0a9c032655bf73871ffcbbf29f31f58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Fitch said the budget assumptions appeared credible and were broadly in line with its September sovereign review. The government\u2019s primary surplus target is below Fitch\u2019s forecast of 2.8 percent of GDP because the planned spending is higher than the agency had assumed. Another shortfall in capital spending could push the surplus above the budget target, Fitch said.\n\nThe 2027 spending plans include Rs.260 billion for the cyclone reconstruction from a supplementary budget approved in December 2025. The allocation was carried forward after it went unspent in 2026.\n\nRevenue is projected to ease to 15.8 percent of GDP in 2027, from 16.6 percent in 2026 and 16.7 percent in 2025. Fitch said the boost to the import duties from the pent-up demand for vehicles was fading as the imports normalised, while the efforts to widen the tax base and improve compliance were supporting the gains across other sources of revenue.\n\nFitch said the budget was broadly aligned with the parameters of Sri Lanka\u2019s International Monetary Fund (IMF) programme, which should support the seventh review of the Extended Fund Facility. The current programme is due to end in March 2027. The government has announced a committee to assess a possible follow-on arrangement, which Fitch said could help sustain policy continuity.\n\nOver the medium term, Fitch said maintaining the recent revenue gains and restraining the spending would be central to further debt reduction and the outlook for the sovereign rating. The government\u2019s aim of maintaining a primary surplus of 3 percent of GDP would require politically difficult spending choices to stay within the 13 percent expenditure ceiling.\n\nHigher public investment could help lift potential growth, which Fitch forecasts at 4.2 percent and support more stable public finances. The agency said those gains would depend on the government\u2019s ability to carry out and properly target capital spending as well as progress on broader structural reforms."
