    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Negligence-possible-poisoning-concerns-raised-in-Kawanthissa-death-inquiry/108-355257",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795058803.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailymirrorlk-f0ae70af-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a2644e474489315d962b39068139ff24",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "Negligence, possible poisoning concerns raised in Kawanthissa death inquiry\n\nLegal submissions made before the Galgamuwa Magistrate\u2019s Court in connection with the death of tusker Kawanthissa highlighted public concerns over alleged negligence by wildlife officers, possible poisoning and the veterinary treatment provided to the animal, particularly in light of an interim post-mortem report which had ruled out gunshot injuries as the cause of death and instead identified intestinal torsion (large intestinal volvulus), accompanied by severe toxaemia, as the cause.\n\nThe interim post-mortem report dated November 6, 2026 ruled out the gunshot injuries as the cause of death and identified intestinal torsion, or large intestinal volvulus, accompanied by severe toxaemia, as the cause.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Upul Jayasuriya, appearing on behalf of wildlife photographer Pathum Bandara, told court that public concerns had raised questions about the conduct of wildlife officers and whether they had neglected their duties after initially treating the tusker for an injury to its right front leg.\n\nHe submitted to court that the interim post-mortem report had identified three gunshot injuries in the lower abdominal region of Kawanthissa.\n\nHe further submitted that the report had recommended obtaining an analysis from the Government Analyst to determine the nature of the toxins detected in the animal\u2019s body, raising further questions about the need for a comprehensive investigation into its death.\n\n\u201cThe interim post-mortem report has revealed that these three injuries were not responsible for the death of the animal. Therefore, a comprehensive investigation should be conducted into the treatment provided to Kawanthissa."
  },
