    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailymirrorlk-e6a150c5-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2d83def438e7985d16aa060b0889c972",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "While Hakeem\u2019s arguments appear reasonable overall, doubts arise as to whether some of his points are valid and whether others are justified. In particular, the suspicion he raised that the government or ministers might have taken a commission for the Israeli drones is unjustified, as there is no credible evidence to support it.\n\nDuring the budget debate, Hakeem stated that these drones are being purchased to spy on Iranian ships around Sri Lanka\n\nOpposition parties have been talking about the release of 323 containers from the Colombo Port in January last year without physical inspection. However, the Department of Customs announced that these containers were released based on a decision made in June 2024\u2014when Ranil Wickremesinghe was President. According to that decision, containers of certain companies have been released without physical inspection on several prior occasions also, during times of severe port congestion, based on past trust, the department said.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake during his budget speech also stated that containers were released without physical inspection 13 times under the above decision, and those who kept their mouths shut during the previous 12 occasions are now talking about the 13th occasion, as if they have discovered something new. Nobody has contested this point so far.\n\nSimilarly, the import of substandard coal that took place last year was not the first time in history. Since Sri Lanka began importing coal in 2009, substandard coal has been imported on multiple occasions. An investigation regarding this was also conducted by the Auditor General\u2019s Department in 2016. Since 2020, fines have also been imposed on the coal suppliers for sending substandard coal to Sri Lanka."
  },
