    "md5": "69d53d9d382ccbc990ba3468f766efde",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1620,
    "chunk_text": "Similarly, the import of substandard coal that took place last year was not the first time in history. Since Sri Lanka began importing coal in 2009, substandard coal has been imported on multiple occasions. An investigation regarding this was also conducted by the Auditor General\u2019s Department in 2016. Since 2020, fines have also been imposed on the coal suppliers for sending substandard coal to Sri Lanka.\n\nNevertheless, his questions regarding the government\u2019s foreign policy are important. Hakeem\u2019s argument that the NPP makes decisions in foreign affairs out of fear of the United States, even if not always true, is largely a fact. However, the question also arises here as to whether this fear is entirely unjustified.\n\nHakeem stated that the government is responsible for the lives of more than a hundred naval personnel killed when an Iranian ship was attacked by the US navy in the sea near Sri Lanka. However, this is a complex issue, because if the United States had wanted to, it could have carried out that attack right inside a Sri Lankan port.\n\nIn the context of the atrocities taking place in Palestinian territories, including Gaza, if the main thrust of Hakeem\u2019s statement regarding the Israeli drones is that Israel must be isolated globally, that argument cannot be rejected.\n\nHowever, the contention that Sri Lanka is buying drones from Israel just to spy for the United States does not seem plausible. If Sri Lanka wanted to do that, it could have purchased the drones directly from the United States itself. The question then arises: if they had done so, would Hakeem have questioned it?"
