    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailymirrorlk-c1d41b65-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ec818892038e1aa7f00a1d1f3f585e4a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "Under Sri Lanka\u2019s Penal Code, sexual intercourse with a girl under 16 is rape whether or not she consents. The offence carries a sentence of 10 to 20 years\u2019 imprisonment. Following the incident, Minister of Women and Child Affairs, Saroja Savithri Paulraj, said that children should be taught sex education and the basics of the country\u2019s laws from an early age. She described this incident as a \u2018clear case of statutory rape.\u2019\n\nHowever, the discussion around sex education has always come to a standstill. It had faced severe backlash by religious and cultural groups. But it is ironical that many individuals in religious institutions have been accused of alleged sexual abuse particularly concerning minors. The victims include both girls and boys below consenting age. A major religious figure who opposed the introduction of sex education in school curricula claimed that topics such as same-sex relationships and birth control are \u201callegedly being funded and influenced by international agencies.\u201d\n\nEven though the incumbent had broader educational reforms as part of its elections manifesto, critics including the Opposition have raised concerns regarding the clarity of these reforms. According to Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya, discussions have been initiated to introduce age-appropriate sex education for children.\n\nBut sex education alone won\u2019t resolve this issue which has spread like a cancer, especially in rural areas of the country. Children need to be aware of the law and what it means to give consent. Parents need to be aware of their children\u2019s whereabouts. Many children are abandoned or neglected in rural settings as both parents go to work. They are left with their guardians such as grandparents or uncles and aunts. As a result, many children and adolescents and vulnerable to sexual abuse, rape and harassment most often done by a known individual."
  },
