    "chunk_size_bytes": 1632,
    "chunk_text": "Rs. 3.5 billion Indian chilli imports held up at port; exporters seek diplomatic intervention\n\nColombo, Nov. 19 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0As many as 120 containers of dry chillies valued at Rs. 3.5 billion, imported from India, have remained at the Colombo Port for nearly two months due to the consignments moisture content exceeding permissible levels under Sri Lankan standards, with local importers pressing for their immediate release and Indian exporters seeking diplomatic intervention to resolve the matter, an official said.\n\nThe situation has resulted in a surge in dry chilli prices in the local market, while chilli powder manufacturers are struggling to meet demand.\n\nThe Chillies Exporters Association of India has already informed its members, including chilli exporters, traders and other stakeholders, to suspend exports of red dry chillies to Sri Lanka until further notice.\n\nThe decision is intended to address the current concerns affecting the Indian red dry chilli trade and protect the interests of exporters, traders and other stakeholders in the industry. All members and associated exporters have been requested to take note of the decision and extend their cooperation. They have also been asked to refrain from entering into new export commitments to Sri Lanka during the suspension period, subject to their existing contractual obligations, according to a press release issued by the association last month.\n\nMeanwhile, the organisation has written to Andhra Pradesh Chief Minister N. Chandrababu Naidu, seeking his intervention to resolve the matter with the Sri Lankan authorities through diplomatic channels."
