    "md5": "110797efe3b5bc2fde6a40fce256e687",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2011,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, the organisation has written to Andhra Pradesh Chief Minister N. Chandrababu Naidu, seeking his intervention to resolve the matter with the Sri Lankan authorities through diplomatic channels.\n\n\u201cA large number of exporters and farmers from Andhra Pradesh are severely affected. We earnestly seek your strong support for urgent high-level diplomatic action to resolve this matter and ensure the smooth flow of agricultural trade between India and Sri Lanka,\u201d it added.\n\nAsked about the current status, Secretary of the Essential Food Commodity Importers and Traders Association P. Subramanium said his organisation had taken up the matter with the government, but without success.\n\nHe said samples were usually tested by the Sri Lanka Standards Institution and the Food Control Administration of the Health Ministry for moisture and aflatoxin levels.\n\nAccording to him, whole dried chillies are naturally hygroscopic and can absorb moisture during transit and prolonged storage under humid conditions.\n\n\u201cIn Sri Lanka, the humidity level is very high. It is challenging to meet the current specifications regarding moisture content under the weather conditions triggered by El Ni\u00f1o. The permissible level is set at 12 per cent. We are asking the authorities to increase it to at least 14 per cent,\u201d he said.\n\nMoisture content and aflatoxin are two distinct parameters and must be assessed separately, he stressed.\n\nExplaining the nature of the product, he said dried chilli was made from green chilli and was not entirely devoid of moisture, despite being classified as a dried product.\n\nAsked whether there were alternative markets from which Sri Lanka could source its imports, he said local consumers were accustomed to the taste of locally produced and Indian chillies.\n\n\u201cWe can import from China or Pakistan. But Sri Lankan consumers are not used to the taste. If we import from China, it has to be done in deep-freezer containers. Then, the moisture level will rise further,\u201d he said."
