    "chunk_text": "Influenza sweeps across Sri Lanka as doctors urge caution\n\nColombo, Nov. 19 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Influenza infections are spreading across Sri Lanka, with the public urged to remain cautious as more people experience fever, cough, sore throat, body aches and other respiratory symptoms, particularly among children, older adults and those with underlying health conditions.\n\nSenior Consultant Physician Dr Ananda Wijewickrama told the Daily Mirror that influenza, commonly known as the flu, is a viral respiratory infection that spreads mainly through droplets released when an infected person coughs, sneezes or talks.\n\nHe said the infection could also spread when people touch contaminated surfaces and subsequently touch their eyes, nose or mouth.\n\nThe most common symptoms include fever, chills, persistent cough, sore throat, runny or blocked nose, headaches, muscle aches and extreme tiredness. Some patients may also experience changes in their sense of taste or smell, although these are not typical symptoms of influenza.\n\nUnlike the common cold, influenza symptoms often develop suddenly and can leave patients feeling considerably unwell.\n\nAlthough most people recover within a week, the infection can sometimes lead to serious complications, including pneumonia and other respiratory problems, Dr Wijewickrama cautioned.\n\nHe said children below two years of age, adults aged 65 and above, pregnant women and people with underlying medical conditions such as diabetes, heart disease, asthma and kidney disease face a greater risk of developing severe complications.\n\nPeople with weakened immune systems, including those undergoing cancer treatment or taking immunosuppressive medication, are also particularly vulnerable."
