    "chunk_text": "Nigeria and Indonesia also featured among the most optimistic countries, scoring 4.08 and 4.07, respectively. Nigerian students recorded environmental optimism of 3.92, while Indonesian students expressed economic confidence, scoring 4.41.\n\nOxford Scholastica Academy Co-Director Lavinia Abell said young people faced numerous reasons for uncertainty and pessimism, including the rise of artificial intelligence, wars and the increasing cost of living, particularly as they prepare to enter the workforce.\n\nShe said the study highlighted how young people in different countries viewed these uncertainties, pointing to differences in attitudes towards the future across nationalities.\n\nAbell also noted that Italy's political optimism was particularly low compared with other European countries, while France and Poland, which also ranked near the bottom, showed slightly different patterns.\n\nThe findings suggest that despite economic and global challenges, young people in Sri Lanka remain particularly hopeful about their personal prospects and the future of the country."
