    "chunk_size_bytes": 1270,
    "chunk_text": "Bank impersonation scams hit Kandy: Over 12 complaints\n\nColombo, Nov. 19 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0More than 12 complaints have been lodged with the Kandy Police in recent days over a financial scam in which fraudsters impersonate officials of leading banks and financial institutions to steal money from customers' accounts.\n\nAn 80-year-old victim had Rs. 7 million fraudulently withdrawn from a bank account, police investigations have revealed.\n\nThe scammers reportedly use telephone calls, SMS messages and WhatsApp to deceive customers, sometimes making the official logos of banks appear on recipients' mobile phone screens to make their communications look genuine.\n\nUsing this tactic, they attempt to obtain confidential banking information, including account details, passwords and One-Time Passwords (OTPs), either through conversations or links sent via messages.\n\nPolice have warned the public not to disclose passwords or OTPs to anyone, stressing that legitimate banks do not request such confidential information through telephone calls or text messages.\n\nThey have also urged customers not to trust calls or messages solely because a bank's official logo appears on their mobile phone screens and to exercise caution when responding to suspicious communications."
  },
