    "chunk_text": "Moise\u2019s widow, Martine Moise \u2013 a key witness in the trial \u2013 was flown to Florida for treatment for bullet wounds she sustained in the attack. She has now registered as a presidential candidate for Haiti\u2019s 2027 general election, the first in more than 10 years.\n\nSanon, who remains in federal custody, showed little reaction as the jury verdict was announced. He faces up to life in prison and is set to be sentenced on February 10.\n\nUS prosecutors have charged more than 40 people with crimes related to the assassination, and a dozen have been convicted, including five who are serving life sentences.\n\nA new list of indictments released by US prosecutors in September included four people whose names were redacted.\n\nSeven more people have been charged for their roles in facilitating the funding of the assassination plot and helping those responsible evade justice after the killing, including Vitel\u2019homme Innocent, a gang leader tied to the kidnapping of 17 US and Canadian missionaries later in 2021. He has yet to be arrested.\n\nThose still to face trial include Joseph Felix Badio, a former government anti-corruption official whom other suspects\u2019 defence lawyers have accused of masterminding the operation, and 17 Colombian former soldiers flown in from Haiti in September.\n\nHaiti has not had a president since authorities said a squad of mostly retired Colombian soldiers impersonating US Drug Enforcement Administration agents killed Moise and injured his wife in a night-time raid on their home outside Port-au-Prince.\n\nA series of transitional governments followed, as armed gangs, widely believed to be aided by corrupt politicians, seized control of much of the capital and surrounding regions.\n\nThe United Nations Human Rights Office documented 4,595 people killed in armed violence between January 1 and October 31 this year.\n\nThe situation has caused economic, security and humanitarian crises, forcing nearly 1.5 million people to flee their homes, according to the UN."
