    "chunk_text": "Voyager 1 is now one light-day from Earth\n\nCNN - Voyager 1, NASA\u2019s celebrated deep-space probe, has reached one light-day from Earth \u2014 a historic milestone that no other human-made object has achieved.\n\nOne light-day is the distance at which a radio signal, moving at the speed of light, takes 24 hours to reach the spacecraft. That\u2019s equivalent to roughly 16 billion miles (26 billion kilometers), making Voyager 1 the farthest human-made object \u2014 a title it has held since 1998, when it surpassed another NASA probe called Pioneer 10.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s never happened before, and it may never happen again \u2014 it\u2019s that kind of extraordinary,\u201d said Kareem Badaruddin, Voyager mission manager at NASA\u2019s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, California.\n\nSince it lifted off in September 1977, Voyager 1 has racked up an impressive list of achievements. The probe and its twin spacecraft, Voyager 2, which launched a month earlier, were originally built to last just five years and explore Jupiter and Saturn. Both have extended their missions nearly 10 times longer than expected.\n\n\u201cThat they are still operating in a scientifically useful way after 49 years is a testament to the dedication and talent of those who designed and built them and those that continue to operate them,\u201d said William Kurth, a research scientist at the University of Iowa who started working on Voyager as a graduate student in 1974. He now leads the Voyager Plasma Wave Science investigation.\n\nThe Voyager probes are currently studying interstellar space, having left the heliosphere \u2014 a vast protective bubble generated by the sun\u2019s magnetic field \u2014 in 2012 and 2018, respectively.\n\n\u201cWe\u2019re having to figure out how to fly these machines to explore the interstellar medium, when they were really designed to explore the planets,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cWe\u2019re using very old machines to do something they weren\u2019t really designed to do.\u201d"
