    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe\u2019re having to figure out how to fly these machines to explore the interstellar medium, when they were really designed to explore the planets,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cWe\u2019re using very old machines to do something they weren\u2019t really designed to do.\u201d\n\nNASA is still able to send commands to and receive data from the spacecraft using the Deep Space Network, a collection of large ground-based radio antennas. But messaging isn\u2019t exactly snappy.\n\n\u201cOne way we can think about it is a phone call,\u201d Badaruddin said. If you called someone on the other side of the Earth, the phone signal \u2014 traveling at light speed \u2014 would take less than one-tenth of a second to reach them, he explained. With such an insignificant delay, \u201cyou can have a conversation as though they were in the same room.\u201d\n\nRinging up Voyager 1 is a much trickier long-distance call. \u201cOur signals to the spacecraft move at the same speed, the speed of light, but it literally takes 24 hours for them to get to it,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cThat\u2019s how far the spacecraft is from us.\u201d\n\nOnce a signal has reached Voyager 1, engineers must then wait another 24 hours for a response to travel all the way back to Earth. And that\u2019s in ideal conditions, when telecommunications and the spacecraft\u2019s instruments are working as expected.\n\n\u201cOperating a remote device with a round-trip communication time of two days requires a totally different mindset than any other operational scenario I can imagine,\u201d Kurth said in an email.\n\nJust a few weeks ago, NASA didn\u2019t hear from the probe for two days, stoking fears that Voyager 1 might have been lost.\n\nTo keep the Voyager spacecraft running well beyond their expected lifespan, NASA engineers have had to come up with some creative solutions over the years. \u201cI think of it like pirates trying to avoid the laws of the land,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cWe try to avoid the laws of physics as we continue our journey.\u201d"
