    "chunk_size_bytes": 1702,
    "chunk_text": "To keep the Voyager spacecraft running well beyond their expected lifespan, NASA engineers have had to come up with some creative solutions over the years. \u201cI think of it like pirates trying to avoid the laws of the land,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cWe try to avoid the laws of physics as we continue our journey.\u201d\n\nUnlike solar panels, commonly used on spacecraft, these generators don\u2019t require sunlight, but the power output decreases as the plutonium naturally breaks down over time and the hardware ages.\n\nAt launch, the generators onboard Voyager 1 supplied 470 watts of electrical power \u2014 roughly the amount of a small household appliance such as a blender. But the probe loses about 4 watts per year, which means it\u2019s currently producing roughly 210 watts.\n\nOnly two of the 10 scientific instruments onboard Voyager 1 are still running and returning data to Earth, as NASA has progressively switched off the others over the years. Those still operating are the magnetometer, which analyzes magnetic fields, and the plasma wave subsystem, which studies the density of charged particles. Both are suited to observe interstellar space, where the probe is currently traveling at 38,000 miles per hour (61,155 kilometers per hour).\n\nNASA recently performed a daring reshuffle of the power distribution onboard \u2014 simultaneously turning off some non-scientific hardware components while turning others on \u2014 to try to save electricity while maintaining essential heat. The computers have to be warm enough to work, and the propellant used to direct the probe must be kept from freezing, because that would prevent important maneuvers such as pointing the antenna toward Earth to exchange data."
