    "chunk_size_bytes": 2046,
    "chunk_text": "NASA recently performed a daring reshuffle of the power distribution onboard \u2014 simultaneously turning off some non-scientific hardware components while turning others on \u2014 to try to save electricity while maintaining essential heat. The computers have to be warm enough to work, and the propellant used to direct the probe must be kept from freezing, because that would prevent important maneuvers such as pointing the antenna toward Earth to exchange data.\n\nIt was a radical change, he said: \u201cWe called it the \u2018Big Bang,\u2019 because it was everything all at once.\u201d\n\nThis \u201cBig Bang\u201d fix will allow Voyager\u2019s remaining science instruments to work for longer than the previous configuration. Badaruddin said that, even after about a year of preparation to convince themselves that the test might work, Voyager team members were very nervous because \u201cthere were seven power commands that had never been used in flight.\u201d\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s going to save us 10 watts, so it bought us about two and a half years of power,\u201d he added. \u201cTo find 10 watts without having to turn off an instrument, that\u2019s just wonderful, wonderful savings. I call it pulling a rabbit out of a hat. So now we\u2019ve got our arm deep in the hat and we\u2019re looking around for another rabbit.\u201d\n\nBut in late October \u2014 just weeks after the daring instrument swap \u2014 Voyager 1 experienced a different issue: a temporary loss of communication. Even though it resolved itself after 48 hours, the incident was an alarming and poignant reminder that the probe is on borrowed time.\n\n\u201cIt was quite a surprise, and we\u2019re still trying to understand what happened,\u201d Badaruddin said. \u201cWe know that we are living in very thin margins, and episodes like that are probably going to become more frequent.\u201d\n\nIt\u2019s hard to predict exactly how much longer Voyager 1 can operate. For decades, mission managers have continued to estimate the mission would last three to five more years, Badaruddin said \u2014 \u201cunless we can find another rabbit in the hat,\u201d he added."
