    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "It\u2019s hard to predict exactly how much longer Voyager 1 can operate. For decades, mission managers have continued to estimate the mission would last three to five more years, Badaruddin said \u2014 \u201cunless we can find another rabbit in the hat,\u201d he added.\n\nIt will be a tough moment for astronomers and researchers when the Voyagers finally give up, Badaruddin said.\n\n\u201cWe fight with everything we have to keep these spacecraft flying. When ultimately that\u2019s not enough, it will be difficult to come to terms with,\u201d he said. \u201cAt the same time, the Voyager mission is one of the great things that humans have done, and it is meaningful to everyone on Earth. It\u2019s deeply humbling to be a part of that.\u201d\n\nThey will leave behind a legacy of major scientific discoveries, including new moons around Saturn and Jupiter, active volcanos on Jupiter\u2019s moon Io, and details about the atmosphere of Saturn\u2019s largest moon, Titan. Voyager 1 also took the famous \u201cPale Blue Dot\u201d photograph, which put into perspective Earth\u2019s place in the vastness of space.\n\nBoth probes are now gathering data about interstellar space in a way no telescope ever could.\n\n\u201cWe use telescopes to study the interstellar medium from Earth, but just like visiting a planet, there\u2019s a huge difference between having a device actually in the medium that you\u2019re studying versus studying it from a distance,\u201d Badaruddin explained.\n\nVoyagers 1 and 2 also carry the Golden Record, a 12-inch gold-plated copper disk that contains images, natural sounds, music and spoken greetings in 55 languages, as well as messages from former US President Jimmy Carter and former UN Secretary-General Kurt Waldheim.\n\nEven if they lose all power, the probes will continue traversing through space at the same velocity, carrying these cultural capsules with them. It will take about 40,000 years for Voyager 1 to make its closest approach to the star Gliese 445, passing within about 1.7 light-years of the distant object."
