    "chunk_size_bytes": 1869,
    "chunk_text": "Dozens missing after boat carrying 100 migrants sinks off Senegal\u2019s coast\n\nBBC - Senegalese authorities are continuing search operations after a boat reportedly carrying about 100 migrants capsized off the coast.\n\nInitial reports said that one person had died, but some local media have reported that at least four bodies have been recovered. Dozens of people are still missing.\n\nPersonal belongings were found on the badly damaged boat, which washed ashore near the village of Yenne, south of Dakar. Search operations continued throughout Wednesday and were due to resume on Thursday.\n\nThis comes less than two weeks after a shipwreck off the coast of the north-western town of Thieppe killed at least 39 people, in one of Senegal's deadliest migration disasters.\n\nOumar Sow, a representative of the village chief, said the latest tragedy happened around 02:00 local time (02:00 GMT).\n\n\"Cries could be heard coming from the pirogue that were audible even from the beach,\" the AFP news agency quoted him as saying.\n\nA survivor questioned by the police said there were about 100 people on the boat, including five women, the AFP reported.\n\nPolice recovered identity documents, several mobile phones and personal belongings from the wreckage, including a school enrolment document.\n\nAt a cabinet meeting on Wednesday, President Diomaye Faye expressed concern about a renewed increase in irregular departure by migrants hoping to reach Europe.\n\nHe instructed the interior ministry to prepare a report examining the factors driving the dangerous journeys and proposing measures to tackle the problem.\n\nFollowing last month's tragedy, Faye had urged Senegalese people not to risk their lives attempting the dangerous sea crossings.\n\nMany thousands of people from West Africa have died or gone missing trying to flee conflict, authoritarian rule or poverty in recent years."
