    "chunk_text": "Following last month's tragedy, Faye had urged Senegalese people not to risk their lives attempting the dangerous sea crossings.\n\nMany thousands of people from West Africa have died or gone missing trying to flee conflict, authoritarian rule or poverty in recent years.\n\nIt warned the figures were likely to be an underestimate, as many boats disappear without leaving survivors or a trace.\n\nAs it has become more difficult to reach Europe through North Africa, due to tighter controls and increased co-operation with European countries, migrants are increasingly targeting Spain's Canary Islands.\n\nThey often travel in wooden boats known as pirogues that are frequently overloaded and not suited to such long voyages across the Atlantic Ocean.\n\nMost migrants arriving in the Canary Islands this year are from Senegal, according to the UN.\n\nThey are driven to leave home by limited employment opportunities, family pressures and established migration networks, according to the IOM.\n\nOn 7 November, El Hierro, one of the Canary islands, declared a state of emergency following a surge in migrant arrivals.\n\nIt said more than 3,300 people had reached the island in about 30 boats since the beginning of the month. This amounts to almost a third of the island's population."
