    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "Fire set at home of former Bangladesh interim leader\n\nCrude bomb explosions rocked Bangladesh's capital before dawn, while former interim government chief Muhammad Yunus's ancestral home was torched in Chattogram. The unrest followed a Thursday (November 19, 2026) \"lockdown\" call by the banned Awami League, led by former Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina.\n\nPolice and witnesses said at least four people were wounded. Witnesses reported explosions of a series of improvised devices, arson attacks and vandalism in Dhaka despite stepped-up security.\n\nUnidentified arsonists set fire to Mr. Yunus's paternal home at Chattogram's Panchlaish area after several branches of his Grameen Bank in different parts of Bangladesh were set on fire.\n\nNewspaper reports said rival activists, presumably belonging to Prime Minister Tarique Rahman's Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP), overnight attacked or set ablaze the residences of two Awami League leaders in Dhaka.\n\nPolice said they stepped up security overnight, mobilising more personnel at major flyovers across the capital. At the same time, officers were seen checking vehicles at entry and exit points and questioning motorcyclists and drivers of suspicious vehicles.\n\nAwami League called the lockdown coinciding with the anniversary of a special tribunal verdict sentencing Ms. Hasina to death last year after trying her in absentia on charges of committing crimes against humanity.\n\nMs. Hasina, 79, has been living in India since fleeing Bangladesh on August 5, 2024, following a student-led protest called the July Uprising that toppled her government. Bangladesh has been seeking her extradition from India.\n\nOfficials said thousands of paramilitary Border Guard Bangladesh (BGB) troops were called out to maintain order in sensitive districts including southwestern Gopalganj district, Ms. Hasina's birthplace."
