    "chunk_id": "2026-11-19-dailymirrorlk-57b0ea24-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "397cf21fe26bff0f302f7e71604e2aa7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1869,
    "chunk_text": "Trump, Xi and Putin show different priorities for China summit\n\nWashington Times - President Trump joined Chinese President Xi Jinping and Russian President Vladimir Putin on Thursday for an unprecedented diplomatic summit aimed at reducing nuclear weapons and ending the wars in Ukraine and Iran.\n\nIn their opening statements before the actual closed-door meeting, the three men, each speaking in his native language, outlined quite different priorities, including a surprising mention of Ukraine by Mr. Putin, who had started the war in the first place.\n\nThe first-ever three-way summit between the leaders of the U.S., China and Russia \u2014 the world\u2019s three largest nuclear powers \u2014 took place on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation gathering in Shenzhen, China. It comes at a time of growing international tensions and uncertainty about the future of global security.\n\n\u201cNo problems were ever solved under the old school, refusing to talk about them. No peace was ever forged by declining opportunities for engagement and speech,\u201d Mr. Trump said. \u201cA historic summit offers us the chance to seek out new opportunities and approaches on a range of very important issues.\u201d\n\nMr. Trump made it clear that nuclear arms reduction should be the main focus of trilateral talks, though neither Mr. Putin nor Mr. Xi mentioned the topic explicitly.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s frankly a very hard thing to do because once you have the nuclear weapons, it\u2019s very hard not to have them. It\u2019s very hard to give them up as the leader of a country,\u201d Mr. Trump said.\n\nIn his opening remarks, Mr. Putin said the three leaders should work to solve the challenges of the time, including solving food security, energy security, and combating terrorism and international crime.\n\nHe noted that the settlement of the war in Ukraine was among their \u201cshared interests.\u201d"
