    "md5": "ca68b9e9bceafdd9762950a8dd04e451",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "In his opening remarks, Mr. Putin said the three leaders should work to solve the challenges of the time, including solving food security, energy security, and combating terrorism and international crime.\n\nHe noted that the settlement of the war in Ukraine was among their \u201cshared interests.\u201d\n\nAs host, Mr. Xi welcomed both leaders, saying their talks were critical to \u201cpeaceful coexistence and overall stability\u201d that will \u201cbenefit each other and the whole world.\u201d\n\nAlthough Mr. Trump has promoted the meeting as an opportunity to pursue sweeping agreements with America\u2019s two biggest geopolitical rivals, administration officials have cautioned that major breakthroughs are unlikely.\n\nThe president held separate meetings with Mr. Xi and Mr. Putin before the trilateral gathering which was delayed by more than two hours, emphasizing his personal relationships with both leaders and expressing optimism that their discussions could produce results.\n\n\u201cWonderful things can come out of this \u2014 or at least start,\u201d Mr. Trump said during his separate meeting with Mr. Putin, whom he described as \u201cmy friend.\u201d\n\nThe talks with Mr. Putin lasted approximately 40 minutes and marked their first face-to-face meeting since an August 2025 summit in Alaska failed to produce an agreement ending Russia\u2019s invasion of Ukraine.\n\nAccording to both the American and Russian sides, that was the dominant topic of their meeting.\n\nDuring his separate meeting with Mr. Xi, Mr. Trump emphasized the importance of keeping oil moving through the Strait of Hormuz while pressing Beijing to increase purchases of American agricultural products.\n\n\u201cI\u2019ll be asking you to do some more for our farmers,\u201d Mr. Trump told the Chinese leader ahead of their 13-minute meeting.\n\nNuclear arms control is expected to dominate the trilateral discussions following the expiration in February of New START, the last major nuclear weapons treaty between Washington and Moscow."
