    "md5": "4659152bac1bcdfa9dc5871d1b0b1c03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI\u2019ll be asking you to do some more for our farmers,\u201d Mr. Trump told the Chinese leader ahead of their 13-minute meeting.\n\nNuclear arms control is expected to dominate the trilateral discussions following the expiration in February of New START, the last major nuclear weapons treaty between Washington and Moscow.\n\nReaching such an agreement could prove difficult because Beijing maintains a substantially smaller nuclear arsenal than either Washington or Moscow and has resisted for decades any proposals to accept restrictions.\n\nChina possesses more than 600 nuclear warheads, compared with more than 5,000 apiece in the American and Russian stockpiles, according to estimates cited by the Associated Press.\n\nThe wars in Ukraine and Iran were also expected to feature prominently in the discussions.\n\nMr. Trump has repeatedly expressed confidence that he can negotiate an end to the fighting in Ukraine, but his diplomatic efforts have produced little progress toward a lasting settlement.\n\nAdministration officials initially suggested Ukraine would not be a central topic at the trilateral summit, arguing that negotiations with Moscow and Kyiv were proceeding separately.\n\nEven before Mr. Putin\u2019s opening statement, Kremlin officials had suggested the leaders would address the war.\n\nThe U.S. president also hopes to enlist Chinese and Russian cooperation in addressing the conflict involving Iran, which has disrupted global energy markets and international shipping.\n\nThe historic gathering offered Mr. Trump an opportunity to demonstrate his commitment to personal diplomacy, but analysts warned that the leaders\u2019 competing interests could limit progress.\n\nThe summit also raised concerns among Ukrainian and European officials that Washington could negotiate major security arrangements with Moscow and Beijing without consulting other nations whose interests would be directly affected."
