    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Riyadh\u2019s tallest skyscraper hit in Houthi strike\n\nRIYADH, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Riyadh's tallest skyscraper, headquarters of the Saudi sovereign wealth fund, was struck on \u200bThursday by a suspected Houthi missile in the latest blow in the Iran-backed Yemeni group's war with the kingdom.\n\nThe strike blew out \u200cwindows across seven to eight floors of PIF Tower, bending parts of its metal facade. It was the second time in less than two weeks that a Houthi attack damaged a landmark in Riyadh's financial district, with state oil giant Aramco's building hit in a November 8 attack.\n\nEntrances to the King Abdullah Financial District (KAFD) were closed and people were being evacuated, \u200bwhile ambulances and fire trucks were on the scene. There were no immediate reports of casualties.\n\nThree sources familiar with the matter said the \u200bbuilding had been struck by an incoming Houthi missile. The Saudi-led coalition fighting the Houthis said it had \"dealt with a \u2060missile threat\" launched by the Houthis towards Riyadh, and that its initial assessment showed debris had fallen and material damage had been reported.\n\nPIF, which stands \u200bfor the Saudi Public Investment Fund, acknowledged receiving a Reuters request for comment. The Houthis issued no immediate statement on the strike.\n\nThe Houthis have been firing \u200bat Saudi airports and oil infrastructure since Yemen's frozen civil war was reignited in July amid the US-Iran war. On the ground in Yemen, the group has seized swathes of territory from the Saudi-backed government, forcing it to stage a major retreat at the weekend.\n\nResidents of the Saudi capital were awoken by a loud boom in the early hours of Thursday.\n\nThe Houthi-run \u200bSaba news agency reported that Saudi aircraft had carried out airstrikes on the Houthi-controlled Yemeni capital Sanaa early on Thursday, without providing further details."
