    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "Urubokka to be developed as hub for traditional Southern food culture: Handunnetti\n\nColombo, Nov. 19 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0New Hela Bojun outlet aims to preserve local food heritage while creating opportunities for women and rural entrepreneurs\n\nUrubokka has the potential to become a major centre for promoting traditional Southern food culture, while creating new economic opportunities for rural communities, Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Minister Sunil Handunnetti said.\n\nSpeaking at the opening of a new Hela Bojun outlet in Urubokka, the Minister said the area had a rich agricultural and culinary heritage that could be developed into an important attraction by preserving its traditional food culture.\n\nRecalling Urubokka's former commercial importance, Handunnetti said the town was once a major agricultural trading hub, attracting businessmen from Colombo and other areas who travelled there to purchase black pepper, fruits, vegetables and bananas.\n\n\"Urubokka was once a significant commercial hub. Businessmen from Colombo used to travel here specifically to purchase produce such as black pepper, fruits, vegetables and, most notably, bananas. It was renowned across Sri Lanka as a major banana collection point,\" he said.\n\nThe Minister said lorries from Colombo and Ambalangoda would arrive in the evening and remain until dawn, while farmers from surrounding villages, including Ginnaliya and areas towards Kolonna, brought their harvests to the town.\n\n\"Much like Dambulla is today, Urubokka once functioned as a vibrant trading hub. It was a bustling marketplace, with farmers bringing their produce and traders arriving from different parts of the country,\" he said.\n\nHowever, he said that much of this commercial activity had gradually declined as successive generations moved away from agriculture, with many relocating to cities or migrating overseas."
