    "chunk_text": "\"Much like Dambulla is today, Urubokka once functioned as a vibrant trading hub. It was a bustling marketplace, with farmers bringing their produce and traders arriving from different parts of the country,\" he said.\n\nHowever, he said that much of this commercial activity had gradually declined as successive generations moved away from agriculture, with many relocating to cities or migrating overseas.\n\n\"We must revitalise our Sri Lankan food culture. It possesses unique characteristics. The cuisine of the South is distinct, particularly in the Matara area,\" he said.\n\nPointing to the traditional preparation of halapa, he said even familiar Sri Lankan food items had different flavours and preparation methods depending on the region.\n\n\"If you try a halapa in another part of Sri Lanka, it will be quite different from the halapa we make here in Urubokka. Even the method of preparation is different. We have a specific food culture here in the Southern region, particularly in Matara,\" he said.\n\nThe Minister said Urubokka could once again become an important destination by promoting its traditional cuisine and protecting the authenticity of local recipes and preparation methods.\n\n\"Urubokka can certainly become a hub for promoting that culture, provided, of course, that its authentic quality is preserved,\" he said.\n\nThe new Hela Bojun outlet was established in the Urubokka Grama Niladhari Division under the 2026 Decentralised Budget Programme, with the Pasgoda Divisional Secretariat implementing the project.\n\nThe initiative is also expected to create income-generating opportunities for women and rural entrepreneurs while promoting nutritious, locally prepared traditional food.\n\nThe outlet is intended to support the preservation of Sri Lanka's food heritage while strengthening rural livelihoods through traditional food production and sales."
