    "md5": "a8c97073b12f44370551b127bcdc8673",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "Court told of threat to wildlife from 13,207 state-issued firearms\n\nColombo, Nov. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The Court of Appeal was told that the iconic tusker Kawanthissa had died after being shot at with a firearm, highlighting the dangers posed to wildlife by the issuing of 13,207 firearms to members of the public, including farmers.\n\nAppearing for the Wildlife and Nature Protection Society of Sri Lanka (WNPS), President\u2019s Counsel Sanjeeva Jayawardena made these submissions when a writ petition challenging the issuing of 13,207 firearms to farmers to protect their crops from wild animals was taken up before a two-judge Bench of the Court of Appeal, comprising its President, Justice Rohantha Abeysuriya and Justice Priyantha Fernando.\n\nJayawardena told court that several elephants had died after being shot at, including Bhathiya and Kawanthissa, while the lives of many others remained at risk. He cautioned that any further increase in the number of firearms issued to the public would aggravate the threat to wildlife.\n\nThe President\u2019s Counsel informed court that firearms had previously been issued to farmers for crop protection, subject to a limit of five acres of cultivated land.\n\nHowever, he maintained that a previous government decision to reduce the minimum extent of cultivated land required for eligibility from five acres to two acres could result in an increase in the number of firearms issued.\n\nHe further submitted that the number of firearms issued, which stood at 305 in 2002, had increased to 490 by 2026.\n\nPresident of the Court of Appeal, Justice Rohantha Abeysuriya, expressed concern over the issue, stating in open court that elephants must be protected as a national asset.\n\n\"Tourists do not come to this country to look at buildings. The Court of Appeal is deeply concerned about wildlife and the environment,\" Justice Abeysuriya observed."
