    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f2ad3addc27d392ba045d5722d23f75d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "CM Vijay\u2019s ally visits LTTE ex-chief Prabhakaran\u2019s home in Sri Lanka. BJP slams, Congress stays mum\n\nINDIA TODAY - A visit to the former residence of slain chief of Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE), Velupillai Prabhakaran, by Viduthalai Chiruthaigal Katchi (VCK) president Thol Thirumavalavan has triggered a political controversy in Tamil Nadu, with the BJP accusing him of insulting former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi's memory. The Congress, which shares power with the VCK in Chief Minister C Joseph Vijay's government, has so far remained silent. Rajiv was the Congress President when he was killed by the LTTE on May 21, 1991.\n\nFor a rainbow coalition like the one in place in Tamil Nadu, there are certain no-go areas for its constituents. The former home of Prabhakaran in Sri Lanka is one such politically sensitive site. Thirumavalavan, a key ally of Vijay's government, visited the property associated with the former LTTE chief, whose organisation assassinated Rajiv Gandhi in 1991.\n\nOn Tuesday (November 17), VCK chief Thol Thirumavalavan visited the site of Prabhakaran's former residence in Valvettithurai, in Sri Lanka's Jaffna district. Videos shared by the Dalit leader showed him walking around the property where Prabhakaran spent his childhood.\n\nPrabhakaran's house, however, no longer exists. The Sri Lankan Army demolished Prabhakaran's ancestral home in 2010, a year after the end of the civil war. The property was subsequently levelled, and what remains today is largely an empty plot of land, with a board marking the site where the LTTE chief once lived.\n\nThirumavalavan was in Sri Lanka to attend the funeral of veteran Tamil politician MK Sivajilingam, held on November 16. He also visited the Chemmani memorial site in Jaffna, associated with the discovery of mass graves, to express solidarity with victims and their families."
  },
