    "md5": "ffe5670ae160c9990a2cbad12209257c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Thirumavalavan was in Sri Lanka to attend the funeral of veteran Tamil politician MK Sivajilingam, held on November 16. He also visited the Chemmani memorial site in Jaffna, associated with the discovery of mass graves, to express solidarity with victims and their families.\n\nNarayanan Thirupathy, chief spokesperson of the BJP in Tamil Nadu, strongly condemned Thirumavalavan's visit to Prabhakaran's Jaffna home in a post on X on Tuesday.\n\nHe called it a \"shameful and unpardonable act\", Thirupathy said Prabhakaran was not a freedom fighter but the head of a banned terrorist organisation responsible for the assassination of former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi.\n\n\"Any Indian political leader who chooses to visit his house is not showing solidarity with Tamils, he is insulting the memory of Rajiv Gandhi,\" he said.\n\nThe BJP spokesperson also questioned the Congress's continued association with the VCK in the Tamil Nadu government.\n\nPointing to the fact that the Congress lost its former Prime Minister to an LTTE suicide bombing, Thirupathy accused the party of sharing power with a political ally whose leader had visited the residence of the man responsible for the assassination.\n\nHe went a step further, demanding that the Congress either break its alliance with the VCK or carry the political burden of sharing power with a party whose leader, according to him, was glorifying Rajiv Gandhi's assassin.\n\nThe BJP's attack has put the Congress in a political catch 22 position. The party has so far not issued a public response to Thirumavalavan's visit or the allegations made by the BJP.\n\nVelupillai Prabhakaran founded and led the LTTE, an armed separatist organisation that fought for an independent Tamil state in Sri Lanka. The organisation was responsible for several attacks during Sri Lanka's civil war and was banned in India.\n\nIts most consequential association with Indian politics remains the assassination of former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi."
