    "md5": "957cb92defe95cf3c5559e003b87e67f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "Velupillai Prabhakaran founded and led the LTTE, an armed separatist organisation that fought for an independent Tamil state in Sri Lanka. The organisation was responsible for several attacks during Sri Lanka's civil war and was banned in India.\n\nIts most consequential association with Indian politics remains the assassination of former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi.\n\nFor the Congress, therefore, any public gesture involving Prabhakaran carries political sensitivities.\n\nA visit to the former residence does not establish that Thirumavalavan endorses the LTTE's armed campaign or its assassination of Rajiv Gandhi. His engagement with Sri Lankan Tamil issues has long involved questions of displacement, political representation, human rights and accountability for alleged wartime atrocities.\n\nHis attendance at Sivajilingam's funeral and visit to the Chemmani memorial also need to be viewed in that broader context.\n\nHowever, political symbolism cannot be separated from historical memory. A public visit to the former residence of the man who led the organisation responsible for Rajiv Gandhi's assassination inevitably invites political questions, particularly when the visitor belongs to an alliance that includes the Congress.\n\nThe controversy is also significant because of the political arithmetic underpinning Vijay's government.\n\nThe Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam (TVK) emerged as the single-biggest party in the 2026 Tamil Nadu Assembly election, winning 108 seats. However, it fell short of the 118 seats required for a majority in the 234-member Assembly.\n\nVijay needed the support of other parties to form the government. The Congress, Left parties, Indian Union Muslim League and VCK extended support, taking the alliance's numbers beyond the majority mark.\n\nThe VCK had only two MLAs, but its political importance was not limited to its legislative strength. Its support was part of the coalition arithmetic that helped Vijay cross the majority threshold."
  },
