    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "Vijay needed the support of other parties to form the government. The Congress, Left parties, Indian Union Muslim League and VCK extended support, taking the alliance's numbers beyond the majority mark.\n\nThe VCK had only two MLAs, but its political importance was not limited to its legislative strength. Its support was part of the coalition arithmetic that helped Vijay cross the majority threshold.\n\nHis visit, therefore, presents Vijay's government with a politically sensitive situation. While the chief minister might not be directly involved in the controversy, the actions of an important ally have given the opposition an opportunity to question the ideological and historical contradictions within the ruling coalition.\n\nMembers of the Nehru-Gandhi family have said they have forgiven Rajiv Gandhi's killers and bear no ill-will against anyone.\n\nIn 2018, Rahul Gandhi, then the Congress President, said at an event in Malaysia that he and his sister, Priyanka Gandhi, had \"completely forgiven\" their father Rajiv Gandhi's killers.\n\nHe reiterated that in 2021, saying, \"I don't have anger or hatred towards anybody. Of course, I lost my father and for me, it was a very difficult time.\"\n\nDespite the stance, the Congress finds itself in a delicate position.\n\nRajiv Gandhi's assassination remains a defining historical issue for the party. At the same time, its participation in Vijay's government requires it to maintain working relations with the VCK, which is an important constituent of the ruling arrangement.\n\nA public confrontation with Thirumavalavan could create friction within the coalition. However, remaining silent could give the BJP an opportunity to question the Congress's position on the LTTE and Prabhakaran.\n\nThe BJP has already sought to make the issue of the Congress's political consistency, rather than just Thirumavalavan's visit."
