    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ab55842448fafbcc4c438fcbdf8e0a63",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2022,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Court finds that Myanmar\u2019s security forces killed thousands of Rohingya \u2060\u2060civilians, perpetrated widespread acts \u2060\u2060of sexual violence as well as acts of \u2060\u2060extreme violence against children and \u2060\u2060attacked fleeing Rohingya \u2060\u2060as they sought to flee in large numbers to \u200c\u200cBangladesh,\u201d he added.\n\nIn specific cases that he referred to, he said Myanmar had acted with \u201cgenocidal intent\u201d.\n\nIt said Myanmar should have known that there was a risk of genocide when armed civilians participated in the military campaign. \u201cMyanmar thus violated its obligation to prevent genocide,\u201d it added.\n\nMyanmar is now obliged to provide reparations and restitution \u201cas a result of Myanmar\u2019s wrongful acts\u201d, Iwasawa said.\n\nThe court said appropriate restitution by Myanmar would include: Facilitating the safe and voluntary return of Rohingya; returning or replacing all collective property damaged; facilitating the search for the disappeared and ensuring proper burials; providing for the physical and psychological rehabilitation of those mentally injured; and ensuring the population\u2019s protection from persecution.\n\nThe court established that at least four genocidal acts have been proven, said Al Jazeera\u2019s Step Vaessen, reporting from the Hague. They are the mass killing of civilians; the forced displacement of hundreds of thousands of people; serious harm inflicted upon the group; and the prevention of birth.\n\nThe Rohingya have long been treated as second-class citizens, well before 2017, according to the judge, Vaessen said. They were also forced to take part in a birth control programme, she added.\n\n\u201cSo the judge really saw this as a very strong explanation that \u2026 a genocidal act was committed there.\u201d\n\nThe verdict is \u201cextremely important for the Rohingya,\u201d our correspondent said. \u201cThey\u2019ve been waiting for 10 years for any kind of justice, but it\u2019s also important for the whole international community because genocide is such a difficult act to prove.\u201d"
  },
  {
