    "md5": "b14eff929aefeba74dd0176f5d181898",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1368,
    "chunk_text": "The verdict is \u201cextremely important for the Rohingya,\u201d our correspondent said. \u201cThey\u2019ve been waiting for 10 years for any kind of justice, but it\u2019s also important for the whole international community because genocide is such a difficult act to prove.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe court is guided by the genocide convention and what the genocide convention states and it is a high threshold,\u201d Cadman told Al Jazeera.\n\nHuman rights organisations welcomed the judgement. \u201cWe hope this ruling marks the beginning of a long overdue process of accountability in Myanmar,\u201d Amnesty International said in a statement. It added that all crimes under international law \u201cmust be effectively investigated and those suspected of responsibility brought to justice\u201d.\n\nCadman said that the situation does not come to an end with the ICJ\u2019s ruling and a lot will depend on what comes next. This includes whether the issue goes before the UN Security Council and whether there are sanctions, or other remedies that the court can order to ensure it is enforced.\n\nMore than 1.3 million Rohingya remain displaced across South and Southeast Asia, including 1.2 million in sprawling refugee camps in Cox\u2019s Bazar in Bangladesh, according to the UN. Conflict, shrinking humanitarian aid and dangerous sea crossings continue to leave many members of the community displaced and vulnerable."
  },
