    "md5": "f0af45a8b9abbe84c1d8e33675e702c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "The session marked the final stage of consensus-building on the core investment parameters for the sector, namely electricity tariffs, land and water allocations, connectivity requirements, access to renewable energy and fiscal incentives, while also confirming the readiness of zone infrastructure and the responsibilities of each institution.\n\nOpening the proceedings, Hulangamuwa outlined the joint BOI\u2013MoDE initiative to position Sri Lanka as a competitive destination for data centre investment. Dr. Wijayasuriya highlighted the potential of the sector both to accelerate domestic digitalisation and to open new export opportunities, while emphasising that, given the central role of electricity, the industry should be developed in a measured and sustainable manner.\n\nParticipants reviewed the proposed national capacity targets of 25 MW by 2027 and 50 MW by 2028, with an indicative mix of 75% conventional compute and 25% AI compute, and an ambition to attract hyperscale data centre investments. Proposed investment terms were presented for both grid-supplied and renewable-integrated data centres, covering greenfield projects as well as expansions.\n\nBroadening the renewable energy pathway beyond co-located generation to include off-site generation through power wheeling and third-party power purchase agreements;\n\nTargeting the operational availability of open-access electricity arrangements by the end of 2027;\n\nDr. Jayawardena presented preliminary investment locations within BOI zones, comprising both grid-supplied sites and captive-power, renewable-integrated sites. The BOI will now work with the relevant utility and regulatory agencies to document site-specific infrastructure availability and readiness before the investment locations are formally published, ensuring that investors receive accurate, decision-ready information."
  },
