    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6a4f709762b9a8a4210b19e176719ec3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2027,
    "chunk_text": "Thingsnode, founded by Migara Amithodhana, is helping make renewable energy operations smarter through technology that monitors and optimises clean-energy assets. Its solutions already support over 500 MW of renewable energy capacity.\n\nNeuroFleets, a Jaffna-based startup co-founded by Lavanya Ratanbala and Robinroy Peter, is using edge AI and autonomous hardware to coordinate logistics fleets and eliminate downtime in third-party logistics warehouses.\n\nUber Sri Lanka Country Manager \u2013 Delivery Varun Wijewardane said: \u201cWhat stands out about this year\u2019s winners is the future-facing sectors they represent - from artificial intelligence and autonomous technology to clean energy. It is equally encouraging to see innovation emerging from beyond Colombo, reflecting the depth of entrepreneurial talent across Sri Lanka. Through Springboard, we remain committed to nurturing this talent, sharing Uber\u2019s global expertise and helping promising startups build businesses that can scale in Sri Lanka and beyond.\u201d\n\nUber Sri Lanka Country Manager \u2013 Mobility Kaushalya Gunaratne said: \u201cWhen an 18-year-old entrepreneur is already building technology for a global audience, it says something about the ambition and potential of Sri Lanka\u2019s next generation of innovators. Great ideas can come from anywhere, but turning them into successful businesses takes the right guidance and opportunity. We\u2019re proud to play a part in that journey and grateful to the Ministry of Digital Economy for partnering with us to help Sri Lankan entrepreneurs turn their ideas into innovations that can make an impact at home and globally.\u201d\n\nThe selected start-up founders will participate in structured mentorship sessions at Uber\u2019s Hyderabad Tech Centre, engaging with senior leaders on product development, technology and business growth. The third edition builds on Springboard\u2019s 2024 and 2025 programmes, reinforcing Uber\u2019s broader contribution to Sri Lanka\u2019s digital economy beyond its mobility and delivery businesses."
  },
