    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5636822d7141a31b5045890f4e92edd8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka launches Marine Industries Advisory Committee to position country as regional maritime hub\n\nHolds inaugural Marine-Based Services Advisory Committee meeting, appoints Kaushal Rajapaksa as Chairman and Ricky Barnett as Co-Chairman\n\nEDB brings public and private leaders together to tackle space shortages, boost marine tourism and drive export-led growth\n\nThe inaugural meeting of the Advisory Committee on Marine-Based Industries was held on 16 November 2026 at the Sri Lanka Export Development Board (EDB), bringing together key public and private sector stakeholders to discuss strategic priorities, industry challenges and emerging opportunities for the development of Sri Lanka\u2019s marine-based industries.\n\nThe meeting was attended by the EDB Chairman, Director General, and the Additional Director General (Development), underscoring the importance placed on the sector as a key component of Sri Lanka\u2019s export diversification and economic growth agenda.\n\nDuring the meeting, Kaushal Rajapaksa was appointed as Chairman of the Marine-Based Services Advisory Committee, while Ricky Barnett was appointed as Co-Chairman. Members expressed confidence in their leadership to drive the Committee\u2019s future initiatives and strengthen collaboration among industry stakeholders.\n\nEDB Chairman Mangala Wijesinghe delivered the main presentation, outlining the current status of the sector, emerging global opportunities, and the strategic priorities required to position Sri Lanka as a competitive regional hub for marine-based services. The presentation highlighted the sector\u2019s growth potential and the need for coordinated efforts to enhance value addition, innovation, skills development, and international market access."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-a2e1cb59",
    "num": "dailyftlk-a2e1cb59",
    "date_str": "2026-11-20",
    "description": "Sri Lanka launches Marine Industries Advisory Committee to position country as regional maritime hub",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Sri-Lanka-launches-Marine-Industries-Advisory-Committee-to-position-country-as-regional-maritime-hub/34-800338",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795128847.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-a2e1cb59-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "771841657fb2b9db0ceff42cf04b0667",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1714,
    "chunk_text": "EDB Chairman Mangala Wijesinghe delivered the main presentation, outlining the current status of the sector, emerging global opportunities, and the strategic priorities required to position Sri Lanka as a competitive regional hub for marine-based services. The presentation highlighted the sector\u2019s growth potential and the need for coordinated efforts to enhance value addition, innovation, skills development, and international market access.\n\nThe Committee also discussed opportunities to strengthen marine infrastructure and tourism-related activities, including the development of marinas, sheltered anchorages, breakwaters, boatyards and other marine tourism facilities. The potential to develop existing harbors, including Galle and other coastal locations, as marine tourism and boating hubs was highlighted. In this regard, the progress of the proposed Kapparathota Marina Development Project was discussed.\n\nAnother important area of discussion was the taxation structure and HS Code classification applicable to locally manufactured commercial boats supplied to domestic tourism operators. The Committee also emphasised the importance of fostering stronger public-private partnerships (PPPs) and creating an enabling environment that supports sustainable growth and increased export earnings from marine-based services.\n\nSpeaking at the meeting, Committee Chair Rajapaksa noted the significant opportunities available within the sector and stressed the importance of collective action to unlock its full potential. Some members of the committee highlighted the need for continuous engagement with industry stakeholders to ensure that the Committee\u2019s recommendations translate into tangible outcomes."
  },
