    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "968571f0799b3e0d22d263cc5be86bb3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1434,
    "chunk_text": "SDPs received Rs. 286 million in VAT relief in 2025, almost all of it in tourism, and Rs. 93 million in SSCL relief. Port City projects received Rs. 11 million in SSCL relief in 2025 and Rs. 13 million in the first half of 2026.\n\nThe Ministry expects these costs to grow. \u201cTax expenditure associated with Port City projects is expected to increase over the medium term as approved businesses commence operations and become eligible for incentives provided under the relevant legislative framework,\u201d the statement said.\n\nTo keep the cost in check, the Ministry has introduced ex-ante cost-benefit analyses and ex-post evaluations of incentives granted to SDPs, and technical analyses and ex-post evaluations of incentives granted under the Colombo Port City Economic Commission. \u201cThe Strategic Development Projects (Amendment) Act, No. 26 of 2025, and the Colombo Port City Economic Commission (Amendment) Act, No. 1 of 2026, have strengthened the tax incentive framework by providing for specified periods for the granting of tax incentives,\u201d it said.\n\nThe Ministry cautioned that the final bill for large projects is uncertain. \u201cFor major investment projects, including BOI projects, Strategic Development Projects, and Colombo Port City projects, the actual fiscal cost may depend on the implementation of investment commitments and the achievement of employment, production, export and other project outcomes,\u201d it said."
  },
