    "chunk_text": "Australian envoy highlights investment, development partnerships\n\nPledges open engagement with Sri Lankan media as new forum launched\n\nAustralian High Commissioner to Sri Lanka Matthew Duckworth yesterday briefed senior editors from leading Sri Lankan media houses on Australia\u2019s diplomatic priorities and bilateral initiatives, ahead of the 80th anniversary of diplomatic relations between the two countries next year.\n\nThe briefing was organised by the OzLanka Journalists\u2019 Consortium in partnership with Australia\u2019s Market Development Facility (MDF). Discussions covered Australian investment, development partnerships and Australia\u2019s contribution to Sri Lanka\u2019s economic growth.\n\n\u201cAustralia deeply values our longstanding partnership with Sri Lanka, and next year marks 80 years of our bilateral relations,\u201d Duckworth said. \u201cWe take this very seriously and look forward to telling this story more broadly next year. As neighbours in this region, we recognise each other as long-term partners and friends, and we want to continue supporting and building upon these ties.\u201d\n\nThe High Commissioner also reaffirmed Australia\u2019s support for press freedom and regional media cooperation.\n\n\u201cWe value independent and credible journalism as a public good. Therefore, we are committed to engaging with you regularly and operating as openly and transparently as possible,\u201d he said.\n\nHe pointed to the recently launched \u201cDigital Turtles\u201d program and exchange visits for media representatives as evidence of Canberra\u2019s commitment to skills development, information sharing and stronger ties between the two countries\u2019 media sectors.\n\nMDF Sri Lanka Country Director Maryam Piracha outlined the work of the Australian Government-funded program, which has operated in the country since 2015. MDF co-invests with private sector firms to promote sustainable, inclusive growth in high-value, export-oriented sectors, including tourism, specialty coffee and agriculture."
