    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "Risk of currency fragmentation in Sri Lanka\n\nWhen the United States of America froze the $ 300 billion. Reserve of Russia, it had weaponised and leveraged an instrument which for years textbooks have claimed to be neutral, driven by standards and market efficiency.\n\nEven though still 56.7% of allocated global foreign exchange reserves are accounted as US Dollars, it is observable as per the World Gold Council (WGC), the central bank net purchases hit a record-breaking 289 tons in the second quarter of 2026 alone, which is a dramatic increase in comparison to the number in the Q4 of 2021 which had shown a number of 76.8 tons which outlines a growth approximately of 376%, proving that Central Bank reserve diversification remains actively underway.\n\nAccumulating the trend a prediction has evolved that de-dollarisation is taking effect as BRICS and multiple other bodies and Central Banks move away from the Dollar. However, this de-dollarisation trend has been misread by multiple as an open opportunity to escape liquid availability of the Dollar constraint.\n\nHowever, that hypothesis has significantly not been accurate about the effect of such fragmentation.\n\nIn case of the fragmentation of currency, countries who import a considerable amount of necessary goods such as Sri Lanka would face multiple systemic complications and constraints.\n\nFinance charges would be increased due to Licensed Commercial Banks as profit maximising entities, institutions passing these high, multi-currency volatility risks directly onto businesses through inflated fees, driving up import prices that are ultimately absorbed by the end consumer. During foreign exchange stress cycles (such as 2022-2024), official trade statistics from the Central Bank of Sri Lanka showed that while raw import volumes of key commodities fluctuated, the financial service fees, exchange spreads, and trade credit charges incurred by importers doubled or tripled compared to baseline operational years."
