    "chunk_text": "Finance charges would be increased due to Licensed Commercial Banks as profit maximising entities, institutions passing these high, multi-currency volatility risks directly onto businesses through inflated fees, driving up import prices that are ultimately absorbed by the end consumer. During foreign exchange stress cycles (such as 2022-2024), official trade statistics from the Central Bank of Sri Lanka showed that while raw import volumes of key commodities fluctuated, the financial service fees, exchange spreads, and trade credit charges incurred by importers doubled or tripled compared to baseline operational years.\n\nAs Sri Lanka\u2019s domestic manufacturing sector generates roughly 15% to 20% of GDP, heavily concentrated in light manufacturing (apparel, rubber products, tea processing as confirmed by the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS), National Accounts Estimates of Sri Lanka. And if Sri Lanka ever relies heavily on critical imports from a trade partner operating outside the centralised Dollar network, any localised currency deficit or clearing failure within that fragmented bilateral corridor directly threatens the broader economy. Deprived of the deep, volatility absorbing liquidity buffer provided by global dollar rails, small open economies face immediate settlement bottlenecks. Forcing a sudden pivot toward domestic import substitution under such strain is an operational nightmare; domestic production capacity cannot rapidly scale to replace multi-billion-dollar global supply chains."
