    "chunk_text": "As Sri Lanka\u2019s domestic manufacturing sector generates roughly 15% to 20% of GDP, heavily concentrated in light manufacturing (apparel, rubber products, tea processing as confirmed by the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS), National Accounts Estimates of Sri Lanka. And if Sri Lanka ever relies heavily on critical imports from a trade partner operating outside the centralised Dollar network, any localised currency deficit or clearing failure within that fragmented bilateral corridor directly threatens the broader economy. Deprived of the deep, volatility absorbing liquidity buffer provided by global dollar rails, small open economies face immediate settlement bottlenecks. Forcing a sudden pivot toward domestic import substitution under such strain is an operational nightmare; domestic production capacity cannot rapidly scale to replace multi-billion-dollar global supply chains.\n\nFor Sri Lankan corporate treasurers and commercial banks, this means moving away from passive exposure to bilateral currency shifts. Businesses must actively hedge against emerging clearing bottlenecks rather than treating alternative trade corridors as a frictionless silver bullet.\n\nUltimately, global monetary fragmentation acts as a hidden tax on Sri Lanka\u2019s economy. Without smooth dollar-clearing channels, essential imports become pricier and our foreign debt obligations grow even more dangerous and threatening. Navigating such a fractured financial landscape will require clearheaded realism from policymakers building strong reserve buffers and managing risk long before the next global shock hits our shores.\n\nThis analysis is compiled from independent research into macro-financial trends, utilising data from the World Gold Council, the Asian Development Bank, the IMF, and the Central Bank of Sri Lanka, and was solely compiled and articulated."
