    "md5": "9191f9c71c3f826a920bdb7733e0c6c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "It is a quiet, disheartening tragedy to watch Chief Executive Officers (CEOs), even at large, powerful organisations, freeze when faced with basic finance and accounting questions. They often retreat behind the reflexive, rehearsed refrain: \u201cI\u2019ll have to check with my Chief Financial Officer (CFO and get back to you.\u201d Whilst it is disturbing to witness such slavish reliance on the CFO to respond to basic queries, it reveals a glaring void in foundational financial literacy where it ought to be. Seeing someone hold absolute strategic authority while lacking a basic grasp of his/her own balance sheet, cash flows, profitability indicators, et cetera, exposes an uncomfortable reality. It is essential that a CEO is in intimate touch with the core of the business. Running a multi-billion-rupee enterprise on pure vibes and financial blindness is not just dismal; it is the gateway to failure.\n\nThis disproportionate reliance on the CFO creates a dangerous organisational dynamic. When a CEO lacks foundational financial literacy, the CFO ceases to be a strategic partner and inadvertently becomes the sole arbiter of corporate viability. Strategic discussions about capital allocation, merger and acquisition valuations, debt structuring, or pricing models risk becoming one-sided. The CEO is left unable to challenge assumptions, stress-test financial projections, or ask the critical, uncomfortable questions that safeguard a business. Decision-making becomes reactive, driven by a blind trust in spreadsheet outputs rather than a deep, intuitive understanding of financial reality."
  },
