    "md5": "4ecd098e988dabc2fe7e583ebedb2b03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "This disproportionate reliance on the CFO creates a dangerous organisational dynamic. When a CEO lacks foundational financial literacy, the CFO ceases to be a strategic partner and inadvertently becomes the sole arbiter of corporate viability. Strategic discussions about capital allocation, merger and acquisition valuations, debt structuring, or pricing models risk becoming one-sided. The CEO is left unable to challenge assumptions, stress-test financial projections, or ask the critical, uncomfortable questions that safeguard a business. Decision-making becomes reactive, driven by a blind trust in spreadsheet outputs rather than a deep, intuitive understanding of financial reality.\n\nThe danger of this prevailing dilemma cannot be overemphasised, particularly in Sri Lanka\u2019s volatile economic environment. In a landscape marked by shifting interest rates, foreign exchange fluctuations, and tight liquidity constraints, financial illiteracy at the helm is an existential risk. Capital allocation errors, such as over-leveraging during economic upturns or misjudging cost structures during downturns, often stem directly from CEOs who cannot read the financial pulse of their own companies. Furthermore, this dynamic exposes organisations to severe governance risks. Without an independent financial lens at the executive level, oversight diminishes, and operational blind spots multiply.\n\nAdmittedly, in corporate leadership, the CEO, irrespective of his core expertise, is expected to be an integrator. He is not expected to understand the granular details of finance. Any attempt to do so will be a poor use of CEO bandwidth because it actively hampers strategic focus. There is, however, a fundamental difference between operational level \u2018meddling\u2019 and directorial/managerial questioning. Delegating the technical execution of finance and accounting to the CFO is logical and prudent but delegating their comprehension and application solely to the CFO is reckless."
  },
