    "chunk_size_bytes": 1371,
    "chunk_text": "Admittedly, in corporate leadership, the CEO, irrespective of his core expertise, is expected to be an integrator. He is not expected to understand the granular details of finance. Any attempt to do so will be a poor use of CEO bandwidth because it actively hampers strategic focus. There is, however, a fundamental difference between operational level \u2018meddling\u2019 and directorial/managerial questioning. Delegating the technical execution of finance and accounting to the CFO is logical and prudent but delegating their comprehension and application solely to the CFO is reckless.\n\nWorking alongside finance-savvy leaders, such as the late Anderson Mazoka, former CEO of Boart Longyear and Managing Director at Anglo American Corporation Central Africa (Anglo), Susantha Ratnayake, former Chairman/CEO of John Keells Holdings (JKH), and Ajit Gunewardena, former Executive Director of JKH and current Chairman of PickMe, Fintrex, and Teejay, and various CEOs/CFOs in the JKH Group, impressed on me the critical value of executive financial literacy. Their sharp, searching questions kept me on my toes. Having also served as CEO of Anglo following Anderson Mazoka\u2019s resignation to contest Zambia\u2019s presidency, and as Group Finance Director at both Anglo and JKH, I have experienced firsthand the extent of financial literacy a CEO must possess to lead effectively."
