    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "Working alongside finance-savvy leaders, such as the late Anderson Mazoka, former CEO of Boart Longyear and Managing Director at Anglo American Corporation Central Africa (Anglo), Susantha Ratnayake, former Chairman/CEO of John Keells Holdings (JKH), and Ajit Gunewardena, former Executive Director of JKH and current Chairman of PickMe, Fintrex, and Teejay, and various CEOs/CFOs in the JKH Group, impressed on me the critical value of executive financial literacy. Their sharp, searching questions kept me on my toes. Having also served as CEO of Anglo following Anderson Mazoka\u2019s resignation to contest Zambia\u2019s presidency, and as Group Finance Director at both Anglo and JKH, I have experienced firsthand the extent of financial literacy a CEO must possess to lead effectively.\n\nWhile the Balance Sheet is a snapshot of net wealth, the income statement records revenues, operational costs, and net margins over a specified reporting window. A CEO must look past top-line growth to evaluate fixed versus variable overhead and identify margin contraction early. Spotify built its audio platform on this level of scrutiny, balancing heavy content licensing fees and royalty costs against subscriber acquisition and retention to achieve sustainable profitability.\n\nThe cash flow statement tracks the actual movement of capital across operations, investing, and financing activities. Revenue and reported net income mean little if an enterprise cannot convert sales into cash. The dramatic crash of Carvana in 2022 serves as a case study in how growth-at-all-costs models, excessive leverage, and paper inventory assets can collapse when underlying macroeconomic tailwinds suddenly reverse.\n\nExtracting strategic intelligence from financial statements requires analysing key performance metrics across four primary areas: profitability, liquidity, solvency, and operational efficiency."
