    "chunk_text": "The cash flow statement tracks the actual movement of capital across operations, investing, and financing activities. Revenue and reported net income mean little if an enterprise cannot convert sales into cash. The dramatic crash of Carvana in 2022 serves as a case study in how growth-at-all-costs models, excessive leverage, and paper inventory assets can collapse when underlying macroeconomic tailwinds suddenly reverse.\n\nExtracting strategic intelligence from financial statements requires analysing key performance metrics across four primary areas: profitability, liquidity, solvency, and operational efficiency.\n\nLiquidity ratios, including the current and quick ratios, assess an organisation\u2019s capacity to cover short-term obligations using liquid assets. During the early supply chain shocks of 2020, retailers with deep cash reserves navigated severe disruptions, while under-capitalised chains faced sudden operational freezes. Solvency ratios, such as debt-to-equity and debt-to-asset ratios, evaluate long-term stability and reliance on borrowed capital. Efficiency ratios, such as inventory turnover and asset turnover, reveal how effectively company assets and working capital are deployed. Zara founder Amancio Ortega transformed fast fashion by driving exceptionally high inventory turnover, freeing up capital, and reducing markdown risks through tight, real-time supply chain feedback loops.\n\nA primary responsibility of executive leadership is determining where capital should be deployed. Evaluating strategic growth initiatives, whether internal research and development, physical expansion, or corporate acquisitions, requires a firm grasp of discounted cash flow mechanics."
