    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "CEOs are responsible for protecting corporate assets and maintaining the integrity of public reporting. This requires monitoring operational, market, and credit risks while establishing strict internal controls to prevent accounting errors or fraudulent activity. The collapse of Wirecard remains a cautionary example of failed governance. Under Markus Braun, aggressive off-balance-sheet misdirection and a total breakdown of internal controls obscured 1.9 billion euros in non-existent cash reserves, leading to complete insolvency and regulatory ruin.\n\nWhen an executive team operates without financial fluency, systemic vulnerabilities multiply rapidly. Strategic capital flows toward low-return initiatives, starving core growth drivers. Ill-considered debt structures expose the enterprise to default during downturns, while inaccurate forecasting damages market credibility and poor oversight invites operational blind spots, fraud, and legal liabilities.\n\nCEOs do not need to handle daily bookkeeping, but they must be capable of auditing the logic behind every financial proposal. Corporate finance fluency transforms quantitative data into a strategic framework, enabling CEOs to ask probing questions of their CFOs, make data-backed operational decisions, and articulate a coherent strategy to shareholders. Ultimately, financial literacy provides the clarity required to navigate economic uncertainty and build enduring enterprise value.\n\nA CEO without financial literacy is akin to Columbus sailing without a compass. He/she may command a magnificent vessel and inspire a brilliant crew yet would be steering blind. In modern business, vision alone cannot navigate the hidden reefs of cash flow crises and volatile markets. Leaving the numbers entirely to the finance team is not delegation; it is surrendering the wheel. True leadership demands reading the financial instruments, interpreting balance sheet currents, and turning hard data into bold strategy."
