    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond the cheque: What Sri Lanka\u2019s children need from business\n\nEarlier this year, I visited a temporary learning facility for children whose school had been relocated from their hill country estate after Cyclone Ditwah. What struck me was not only what they had lost, but what they were seeing for the first time. Instead of a short walk from their row house to school, the children now take a tuk-tuk down the hill into town. It is no exaggeration to say that their world view was being transformed by this experience. It reminded me that children\u2019s futures are shaped by the opportunities in front of them \u2013 and that too often, where a child is born still decides what those opportunities are.\n\nToday is World Children\u2019s Day, and it is worth remembering how far Sri Lanka has already come in widening those opportunities. In 1990, about 23 of every 1,000 children died before their fifth birthday. Today the figure is under six, thanks to free healthcare, a remarkable network of public health midwives and decades of steady public investment.\n\nBut recent years have shown how quickly gains can slip. According to the World Bank, poverty more than doubled after 2019, from 11.3% to 24.5% in 2025. Cyclone Ditwah affected more than 275,000 children last year, and the 2025 National Nutrition Month found that almost half of children under five had at least one growth-related problem. The burden falls hardest on children on the plantations, in disaster-hit districts and children with disabilities \u2013 those already furthest from opportunity.\n\nEvery one of these children has the right to grow, learn and be safe \u2013 not someday, but now. Government cannot close these gaps alone, and neither can UNICEF. Children in Sri Lanka need business too."
  },
