    "md5": "a28b2b9b44c4ca9061854c6db65b41e6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "Every one of these children has the right to grow, learn and be safe \u2013 not someday, but now. Government cannot close these gaps alone, and neither can UNICEF. Children in Sri Lanka need business too.\n\nThe good news is that care for children runs deep in Sri Lanka. The country made education free in 1945, long before most of its neighbours, and has kept it free ever since. WHO and UNICEF data show that almost every Sri Lankan baby receives their routine vaccines in the first year of life. And when Cyclone Ditwah struck, UN figures show that nearly two in three displaced people were taken in by host families and communities rather than staying in safety centers \u2013 and more than four months later, some 150,000 people were still being hosted. Families opened their homes to other people\u2019s children without being asked.\n\nSri Lankan businesses share that instinct. During the economic crisis, companies and individuals helped raise funds for children and families, and after Cyclone Ditwah, businesses gave generously to the recovery.\n\nThat generosity has mattered, and it will always be needed when disaster strikes. But much of it still goes to one school, one village or one cause close to a company\u2019s heart \u2013 valuable but limited to the children it happens to reach. The bigger opportunity is to work alongside Government to strengthen the systems every child relies on: schools, health services and social protection. When business backs a system rather than a single project, a good idea in one district can become the norm in all 25, and Sri Lanka as a whole benefits.\n\nThe early years are the place to start. In Uva and Central provinces, businesses have worked with provincial authorities and UNICEF to improve preschools \u2013 funding that strengthens the public system rather than sitting alongside it. With the education reform under way, multi-year commitments like these could help the new early childhood curriculum reach every classroom, not just the fortunate ones."
  },
