    "chunk_size_bytes": 2020,
    "chunk_text": "The early years are the place to start. In Uva and Central provinces, businesses have worked with provincial authorities and UNICEF to improve preschools \u2013 funding that strengthens the public system rather than sitting alongside it. With the education reform under way, multi-year commitments like these could help the new early childhood curriculum reach every classroom, not just the fortunate ones.\n\nBusiness also brings what money alone cannot: know-how in logistics, technology, finance and data \u2013 exactly what helps services reach the children who are hardest to reach. In Sri Lanka we are exploring private-sector-led models to bring solar power to schools, clinics and water systems \u2013 solutions that make commercial sense and change children\u2019s lives.\n\nAnd what gets measured gets done. A recent UNICEF review, \u2018Making Children Count\u2019, looked at 1,399 listed companies across nine Asian markets, including 52 in Sri Lanka. Nearly three in four had made human rights commitments, but only one in 20 named children as stakeholders. Sri Lanka already has sustainability reporting in place for its top 100 listed companies but in 20 of its 26 indicators, what companies reported achieving for children fell at least 50% short of what they pledged, and markets with mandatory reporting scored more than twice as high as those with voluntary or no guidance. Asking them to report on how they respect children\u2019s rights \u2013 in the workplace, in their supply chains and in their marketing \u2013 would be a low-cost way to make children visible and to turn goodwill into accountability.\n\nThis is also sound economics. Every dollar spent on childhood immunisation returns between $20 and $50 or more, and nutrition programs return more than $20. With an ageing population, where each generation of workers will be smaller than the last, Sri Lanka cannot afford for any child to fall behind. And as the country rebuilds after the economic crisis, investors are watching its people as closely as its balance sheet."
