    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Saint abroad, devil at home\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake delivering 2027 Budget speech in Parliament\n\nFor the people of Sri Lanka, development, as the President invoked at the start of his Budget speech, remains a longstanding but unachieved aspiration. Sadly, the 2027 Budget fails to lay the groundwork for fulfilling that aspiration. Worse than a mere failure, it hoodwinks people, as many past Budgets have done.\n\nThe 2027 Budget is best read not as a fiscal plan for development and wellbeing but as a chant of the IMF mantra: a primary surplus of 2.3%, Budget deficit below 5% and debt sustainability. As this article illustrates, a Government enchanted by the IMF mantra deceives people in three ways.\n\nFirst, it declares grand fiscal allocations but under-executes them. Second, it subordinates productive investments and social security to investor requirements. Third, it subjects life-sustaining infrastructure to a neoliberal grip. The result is a Budget that acts as a saint abroad and a devil at home.\n\nThe Government\u2019s claim that the Budget is transformative gains legitimacy from grand Budget allocations. But numbers on paper are not a reason for optimism. Not spending allocated capital expenditure has become a norm, constituting a strategy of fiscal consolidation and a new form of austerity.\n\nTo begin with the last Budget: the 2026 Budget had allocated Rs. 1,380 billion for capital expenditure. However, according to the Mid-Year Fiscal Position Report 2026 issued by the Treasury, only 16.1% had actually been spent by June 2026. According to the Budget, Economic and Fiscal Position Report issued in November, capital expenditure and net lending had increased to 31.6%, equivalent to Rs. 436 billion.\n\nIn 2025, the corresponding figure was 17.2%. The Mid-Year Fiscal Position Report 2025 attributes the fall in expenditure to \u201cdelayed execution of the capital Budget\u201d (p. 12)."
