    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "To begin with the last Budget: the 2026 Budget had allocated Rs. 1,380 billion for capital expenditure. However, according to the Mid-Year Fiscal Position Report 2026 issued by the Treasury, only 16.1% had actually been spent by June 2026. According to the Budget, Economic and Fiscal Position Report issued in November, capital expenditure and net lending had increased to 31.6%, equivalent to Rs. 436 billion.\n\nIn 2025, the corresponding figure was 17.2%. The Mid-Year Fiscal Position Report 2025 attributes the fall in expenditure to \u201cdelayed execution of the capital Budget\u201d (p. 12).\n\nThe conventional understanding of austerity is living within one\u2019s means or slashing of public expenditure. What the data show is something beyond that: Governments, including the NPP Government, have not spent even the funding allocated. The ADB and IMF analyses of underutilisation of funds point to limitations of the public sector in terms of capabilities, and governance (delays in procurement, land acquisition, feasibility assessment and inter-agency coordination), which could be overcome through more public-private partnerships. These analyses assume that private sector is better endowed with skills and expertise. However, what if under-execution is an outcome of austerity internalised into Government psyche through decades of austerity imposed on Governments by the IMF, ADB and World Bank?\n\nCross-country data, particularly for countries under IMF programs, show that under-utilisation of capital funds is a shared practice. According to an early IMF report, Economic Adjustment and Reform in Low-Income Countries (1999), one strategy to contain deficit was to adjust expenditure by cutting capital expenditure. An IMF report in 2026 confirms that cutting capital expenditure is the \u201cresidual adjustment tool when fiscal pressures emerge\u201d (IMF, May 13, 2026). The IMF recognises that such fiscal pressures emerge not only from revenue shortfalls but also from delays in external financing."
