    "chunk_size_bytes": 1425,
    "chunk_text": "Cross-country data, particularly for countries under IMF programs, show that under-utilisation of capital funds is a shared practice. According to an early IMF report, Economic Adjustment and Reform in Low-Income Countries (1999), one strategy to contain deficit was to adjust expenditure by cutting capital expenditure. An IMF report in 2026 confirms that cutting capital expenditure is the \u201cresidual adjustment tool when fiscal pressures emerge\u201d (IMF, May 13, 2026). The IMF recognises that such fiscal pressures emerge not only from revenue shortfalls but also from delays in external financing.\n\nCapital under-execution when revenue is not falling, as in Sri Lanka, could hint at dangerous policy moves. Under-investing in capital expenditure while subordinating fiscal policy to the IMF mantra could be interpreted as a purposeful policy to make lending to Sri Lanka attractive to foreign creditors, particularly those in international Bond markets. A primary surplus itself signals positively to creditors indicating that Government has surplus revenue after meeting all non-interest expenses (expenses on salaries, goods, services and capital projects). Under-spending on capital projects, therefore, leaves more money to spend on other things, such as debt servicing. The Government, expecting to return to the international Bond market in the future, is under-spending to enhance its credibility among creditors."
