    "chunk_size_bytes": 1545,
    "chunk_text": "Rewarding private investors is as much an overriding theme of the Budget as inspiring foreign creditors. The President\u2019s Budget speech interprets new legal and institutional reforms as a means of \u201cremoving shackles that bind our feet from reaching the economic transformation we envisage\u201d (p. 11). In rendering a new meaning to the word \u201cshackles\u201d, historically used in left literature as a rallying cry for working people, the 2027 Budget advocates pro-market reforms to set private investors free from binding State regulations or established communal practices.\n\nSimilarly, the Acts on Public-Private Partnerships (PPPs), and State-Owned Business Enterprises Management Act will carry forward the transfer of public assets and wealth into the hands of the private sector. Forthcoming labour law reforms, specifically eliminating protective clauses on hiring and firing in the Termination of Employment of Workmen Act, which the Chamber of Commerce has been advocating for years, would render workers completely submissive to the whims and fancies of employers.\n\nThe word \u201cresilience\u201d figures four times in the Budget speech. The repeated emphasis on resilience, by the Government as well as by the IMF, World Bank and ADB, however, is not a positive sign. Rather than recognising the need to invest in and strengthen community infrastructure that enables resilience and helps people preserve life during hard times, both the IFIs and the Government, under the IFI\u2019s spell, approach resilience as a resource pool to be grabbed."
