    "chunk_text": "Despite facing acute crises such as Cyclone Ditwah in November 2025 and spillovers from the war against Iran in 2026, the Sri Lankan Government failed to gain flexibility in how IMF targets are achieved, let alone concessions. What we have seen so far is the Government legitimising both the IMF program and the debt restructuring process, making it possible for the IMF to make a comeback as essential and useful mediator in instances of debt crises in the Global South. For the people on whose backs this success story has been written, the future is still bleak. A preliminary report issued by the Department of Census and Statistics on the Household Income and Expenditure Survey 2025 reveals that income inequality between the poorest and richest Sri Lankans has deepened. The poorest 20% earn an average income of Rs. 22,612 while the richest 20% earn Rs. 335,679 per month. The Budget 2027 lowered income tax that the richest 20% are likely to pay, while the poorest 20% received only marginal tax benefits.\n\nThe Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) in its Financial Stability Review 2026 cautions about household debt, illustrating how an increase in pawning in the first quarter of 2026 could indicate financial stress at the household level. Figure 2, taken from the CBSL report, illustrates how household consumption loans expanded. In 2026, pawning accounts for the largest source of household consumption loans."
