    "chunk_size_bytes": 1670,
    "chunk_text": "The Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) in its Financial Stability Review 2026 cautions about household debt, illustrating how an increase in pawning in the first quarter of 2026 could indicate financial stress at the household level. Figure 2, taken from the CBSL report, illustrates how household consumption loans expanded. In 2026, pawning accounts for the largest source of household consumption loans.\n\nWhat households in Sri Lanka need is not more debt but more enabling investment, in MSMEs, agriculture and fisheries sectors, to break the decades-long funding crisis and financial grip of both money lenders and the formal banks and finance companies. As a people striving to emerge from default, we need more public investment in education, health care, social security and livelihoods to ensure that no one is left behind. Yet, as World Bank human development indicators illustrate, the indicators that matter to ordinary people still lag behind pre-crisis levels.\n\nGovernments have chased macroeconomic stability indicators in a quest to stage a recovery attractive to foreign creditors and investors. The result is a Budget that presents Sri Lanka as a saint abroad while acting as a devil at home: under-executing capital investment, inundating productive sectors and households with more debt, and eroding the very community resilience that has helped people survive hard times. This promises not recovery but deepening crises.\n\n(The author (PhD, Hawai\u2018i) is a feminist political economist, a senior researcher at the Bandaranaike Centre for International Studies, and a member of the Movement for Climate and Economic Justice (MCEJ) and Yukthi Collective)"
