    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Lacklustre Budget for decisive year\n\nThat 2027 is an important year cannot be disputed. Sri Lanka graduates from the IMF EFF program, having completed many of its commitments on the fiscal front. This Budget was a test of the country\u2019s commitments to its new public financial guardrails. On that front, the Government has done well. There were no major tax concessions granted, although there was a lot of public pressure for it.\n\nMy assessment of Budget 2026 was that it was cautious where needed, but not bold enough where it could be. On Budget 2027, my assessment is that the Government\u2019s cautiousness continues while its sense of boldness seems to have vanished altogether.\n\nThings that are going right: Taxes, investment and digitalisation and trade\n\nWhile there was much talk about personal income tax relief and VAT relief at many political gatherings in the run up to the Budget, the Government only conceded a little. Raising the personal income tax threshold to Rs. 1.95 million per year and tweaking the tax bands. Books and printed materials have been exempted from VAT, but that is unlikely to cause a major dent in Government revenues. There was no reduction in the rate nor changes to the threshold.\n\nProponents of a VAT rate reduction pointed towards cost-of-living relief, but does reducing VAT lead to a reduction in the cost of living? Reducing VAT did not lead to a reduction in the cost of living in December 2019, when rates were almost cut in half. In fact, food prices even rose by 6.3%. More recently, exempting local dairy from VAT did not lead to the expected price drop in April 2025 either. It was a lose-lose situation \u2013 the Government lost revenues while consumers continued to pay the same prices. So, reducing VAT for cost-of-living relief is unlikely to have such a desirable outcome."
  },
