    "chunk_size_bytes": 1500,
    "chunk_text": "Proponents of a VAT rate reduction pointed towards cost-of-living relief, but does reducing VAT lead to a reduction in the cost of living? Reducing VAT did not lead to a reduction in the cost of living in December 2019, when rates were almost cut in half. In fact, food prices even rose by 6.3%. More recently, exempting local dairy from VAT did not lead to the expected price drop in April 2025 either. It was a lose-lose situation \u2013 the Government lost revenues while consumers continued to pay the same prices. So, reducing VAT for cost-of-living relief is unlikely to have such a desirable outcome.\n\nLast year, the Government announced the implementation of the National Tariff Policy. While I raised a concern about the fact that the Government\u2019s revenue numbers at the time didn\u2019t reflect this, this issue seems to have been rectified now. Revenues from para-tariffs like Ports and Airports Development Levy and Cess are indeed phasing out. However, surreptitiously, the revenues from the Special Commodity Levy (SCL) are rising. It is estimated to generate Rs. 170 billion in 2027, Rs. 187 billion in 2028 and Rs. 204 billion in 2029 \u2013 these are higher than what was projected in the 2026 Budget.\n\nThe SCL was a tariff brought about in 2007 as a composite levy that would ease administration of Sri Lanka\u2019s then-highly complex import tariff regime. It is currently exclusively levied on food items \u2013 dhal, onions, potatoes, sprats, dried fish, dairy, fresh fish, pulses and fruits."
