    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "The SCL was a tariff brought about in 2007 as a composite levy that would ease administration of Sri Lanka\u2019s then-highly complex import tariff regime. It is currently exclusively levied on food items \u2013 dhal, onions, potatoes, sprats, dried fish, dairy, fresh fish, pulses and fruits.\n\nThe imposition of SCL is done by Gazette notice, at the discretion of the Minister of Finance. So SCL may be expanded to cover things beyond food \u2013 things that the para-tariff phase out was supposed to help with. On the other hand, if SCL continues to be collected on food items, this is an extra burden to consumers \u2013 especially when the cost of living is already unaffordable to many.\n\nIn mid-2026, the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development announced that they would introduce new import standards among other measures to counterbalance the effects of the phase out. Coupling this with the projected SCL revenues, one could argue that it is essentially a continuation of the para-tariff regime in all but name.\n\nOn other aspects related to trade, there was mention of the intention to pursue trade agreements with China and India and even negotiate economic cooperation agreements with Oman and UAE. However, there has been no mention of establishing a dedicated office that can see such negotiations through. Negotiating free trade agreements typically take 1-3 years, but Sri Lanka usually takes much longer \u2013 5 or 6 years. Without an institution dedicated to such a feat, it is unlikely that such an ambitious plan can be executed.\n\nThis Budget is suspiciously absent on State-owned enterprise reforms. The much-delayed public commercial business management Bill was referred to in passing, but no mention of when it will be tabled in Parliament. The last year Budget also referred to the Bill and promised to present it to Parliament in the first 6 months of 2026. It has not been done so. It further pledged to restructure 44 and liquidate 13 non-commercial entities. Neither has this been done."
