    "md5": "cef44b61e6921d873dcfeef3416f87f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "This Budget is suspiciously absent on State-owned enterprise reforms. The much-delayed public commercial business management Bill was referred to in passing, but no mention of when it will be tabled in Parliament. The last year Budget also referred to the Bill and promised to present it to Parliament in the first 6 months of 2026. It has not been done so. It further pledged to restructure 44 and liquidate 13 non-commercial entities. Neither has this been done.\n\nIn a similar vein, public sector reforms too are absent in this Budget. While many new allowances and benefits have been announced for various civil servants, there is no mention of rationalising or right-sizing the public service. In fact, Budget estimates show that the Government spending on salaries and benefits and pensions will keep pace with Government revenue \u2013 at around 16% and 10% respectively. There is nothing on reforming the civil pension scheme which will be a huge fiscal burden in time to come.\n\nBudget week happened to coincide with the Department of Census and Statistic\u2019s decision to finally release at least preliminary results from the much-delayed Household Income and Expenditure Survey (HIES) 2025. The survey results are extremely concerning. From my reading of the numbers, my conclusions are that we may be poorer and more in debt than we think. Inequality has surged, and Sri Lanka\u2019s \u2018middle-class\u2019 seem to have vanished. Median household income is Rs. 75,882, and median household expenditure is Rs. 94,057 \u2013 a gap that is likely bridged with debt.\n\nThe poorest 40% of Sri Lankans live with an average household income of Rs. 36,564. On the other hand, the official poverty line was Rs. 16,417 per person per month and an average household of 3-4 people would need Rs. 55-65,000 per month to meet their basic needs. This means that at least 40% of Sri Lankan households do not earn sufficiently to be above the poverty line."
