    "chunk_size_bytes": 1447,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o and food inflation in  Sri Lanka: What history tells us\n\nHistorical evidence suggests that the effects on food prices may take time to emerge, making rainfall, crop conditions, and market supplies worth watching\n\nThe Department of Meteorology\u2019s outlook1 indicates that El Ni\u00f1o may persist into early 2027 and peak towards the end of 2026. Above-normal rainfall is possible during November\u2013December 2026, followed by a greater risk of drier conditions and higher temperatures between February and April 2027. On this background, this article examines three strong historical El Ni\u00f1o episodes identified by the Department of Meteorology using the Relative Oceanic Ni\u00f1o Index (RONI):2 1982/83, 1997/98 and 2015/16.\n\nAn important feature observed across the three historical El Ni\u00f1o episodes is that the drought conditions, which were generally identified during Jan\u2013Apr, were preceded by relatively heavy rainfall during Oct\u2013Dec of the preceding year. This highlights the importance of examining rainfall patterns not only during drought periods but also in the months leading up to them. This observation is consistent with Sri Lanka\u2019s seasonal rainfall pattern. On a national average,3\n\nrainfall is generally higher during Sep-Dec than during Jan-Apr and Mean temperatures also tend to be relatively high during Mar\u2013May. These seasonal patterns provide important context for understanding the potential effects of El Ni\u00f1o."
