    "chunk_text": "rainfall is generally higher during Sep-Dec than during Jan-Apr and Mean temperatures also tend to be relatively high during Mar\u2013May. These seasonal patterns provide important context for understanding the potential effects of El Ni\u00f1o.\n\nMonth-on-month (m-o-m) inflation is examined to identify short-term price movements around periods of rainfall deficiencies and assess how quickly these movements may be reflected in consumer prices.\n\nIn each episode, the highest m-o-m inflation within the period examined occurred one to two months after the month with the largest rainfall deficit. In 1982/83, m-o-m inflation reached 3.76% in March 1983, following the February largest rainfall deficit. In 1997/98, it reached 3.22% in May, following the March largest deficit. In 2015/16, it reached 1.80% in May, following the April largest rainfall deficit. M-o-m inflation also remained relatively elevated during the following three months after the drought, with average m-o-m inflation rates across the episodes ranging from about 1.3% to 1.6%.\n\nThe timing suggests a possible delay between rainfall deficits and price increases, but does not prove a direct causal link. Inflation is also affected by demand, fuel and other commodity prices, exchange rates, seasonal factors and wider economic conditions. Comparisons are further limited because the CCPI series use different base years and consumption baskets. Historical patterns therefore indicate a potential risk, not a definite prediction for 2026/27. The historical comparison is most useful for identifying timing and possible pressure points, rather than predicting a particular inflation rate.\n\nHeadline inflation covers a broad range of goods and services; food inflation focuses on food prices. Looking at food inflation separately helps show how food prices moved around periods of rainfall deficiency.\n\nGraph 03: CCPI-based food and non-food inflation (m-o-m) during the 1997/98 and 2015/16 episodes.