    "chunk_text": "Headline inflation covers a broad range of goods and services; food inflation focuses on food prices. Looking at food inflation separately helps show how food prices moved around periods of rainfall deficiency.\n\nGraph 03: CCPI-based food and non-food inflation (m-o-m) during the 1997/98 and 2015/16 episodes.\n\nThe food and non-food m-o-m inflation series for 1997/98 and 2015/16 suggest that food prices accounted for much of the increase following the identified peak drought months. Over the next three months, average m-o-m food inflation was 2.17% in 1998 and 1.57% in 2016, giving a combined average of 1.87% for the two episodes.\n\nThe magnitude of price increases differed between the two episodes. The impact on food prices would have depended on several factors, including the crops affected, the timing and duration of supply disruptions, and the availability of market stocks.\n\nHow did selected food prices respond to the 2015/16 El Ni\u00f1o episode?\n\nThe movements in selected food subgroups in the Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI) during the 2015/16 El Ni\u00f1o episode provide further insights into food-price developments during this period.\n\nSome of the graphs illustrate changes in the cost of fixed baskets of selected food subgroups, reflecting overall price movements within each group rather than changes in individual food items.\n\nThese movements may also have been influenced by other weather conditions and economic factors prevailing at the time. Accordingly, the analysis highlights possible food-price responses associated with the 2015/16 El Ni\u00f1o episode, rather than establishing a direct causal relationship.\n\nGraph 4: Potentially Quicker Price Responses: Vegetables, Fish and Meat.\n\n*Note: Expenditure values represent the estimated cost of the respective consumer baskets based on CCPI weights. These are not displayed, as the analysis focuses on price trends and relative movements over time."
