    "chunk_text": "*Note: Expenditure values represent the estimated cost of the respective consumer baskets based on CCPI weights. These are not displayed, as the analysis focuses on price trends and relative movements over time.\n\nThe increases observed from May to July 2016 were also larger or more persistent than those in the corresponding months of 2015 and 2017, suggesting that seasonal influences alone may not fully explain the price movements.\n\nFish prices recorded notable increases following the dry spell, with the movements being more pronounced in small fish than in large fish during the first three months after the identified drought period. This may partly reflect changes in marine environmental conditions associated with El Ni\u00f1o, which could affect fish availability. A recent bulletin issued by the Indian National Centre for Ocean Information Services (INCOIS) indicates that El Ni\u00f1o-related warming in the Indian Ocean could place marine ecosystems under thermal stress, potentially affecting the distribution and recruitment of small pelagic species, particularly sardines and mackerel, and thereby influencing catches.\n\nAlthough prices of both small and large fish typically show seasonal movements between May and July, the magnitude and pace of the increases observed in 2016 were noteworthy compared with the corresponding periods in 2015 and 2017.\n\nMeat prices also increased during the post-drought period, potentially reflecting a shift in demand towards meat as an alternative to fish. Particularly noteworthy was the sharp increase between June and July 2016, which was considerably higher than the movements observed during the corresponding months of 2015 and 2017. Higher animal feed costs and shortages of water may also have\n\nGraph 5: Potentially Delayed Price Responses: Rice, Coconut and Fresh Fruits"
