    "chunk_size_bytes": 1325,
    "chunk_text": "Meat prices also increased during the post-drought period, potentially reflecting a shift in demand towards meat as an alternative to fish. Particularly noteworthy was the sharp increase between June and July 2016, which was considerably higher than the movements observed during the corresponding months of 2015 and 2017. Higher animal feed costs and shortages of water may also have\n\nGraph 5: Potentially Delayed Price Responses: Rice, Coconut and Fresh Fruits\n\nCoconut prices showed an overall upward trend from December 2016 onwards, despite marginal setbacks during some months. This sustained increase, particularly during 2017, may reflect delayed effects of El Ni\u00f1o-related weather conditions or rainfall patterns preceding the event. Research on Sri Lanka\u2019s coconut sector also suggests that adverse weather conditions may affect coconut supply with a considerable time lag of around ten months to a year (Silva & Matsui, 2026).\n\nFresh fruit prices also recorded a considerable increase during the 2016 drought period. Notably, the magnitude of this increase was greater than the movements observed during the corresponding period in 2015 and 2017. This suggests that, beyond normal seasonal fluctuations, the dry conditions may have contributed to the price pressures observed in fresh fruits during this period."
