    "md5": "31bae4bd91b21a6c79f0db010b5f8e40",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Fresh fruit prices also recorded a considerable increase during the 2016 drought period. Notably, the magnitude of this increase was greater than the movements observed during the corresponding period in 2015 and 2017. This suggests that, beyond normal seasonal fluctuations, the dry conditions may have contributed to the price pressures observed in fresh fruits during this period.\n\nThe historical evidence highlights that food-price developments may be influenced not only by drought conditions but also by rainfall patterns in the preceding months. Food prices accounted for a significant part of the inflation response, although the timing and magnitude of the increases varied across commodities. These historical patterns highlight potential pressure points but do not imply that inflation will follow the same path during the 2026/27 episode.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic environment has also changed. Expenditure values are estimated to be around three times higher than in 2015/16, this does not necessarily indicate greater household purchasing power, as constrained budgets may limit households\u2019 ability to absorb further price increases.\n\nMonitoring rainfall forecasts alongside crop conditions, vegetable arrivals, fish landings and wholesale and retail prices can help identify food-supply pressures early. History offers lessons about possible risks, not a definitive prediction for 2026/27. The outcome will depend on the severity and duration of weather disruptions and on wider economic conditions and policy responses.\n\nThese weather-related risks are emerging at a time when headline inflation has already moved above the 5% inflation target due to several supply-side pressures, including adjustments to energy prices reflecting changes in international fuel prices, partly influenced by geopolitical conflicts, as well as other domestic and external cost pressures."
