    "chunk_size_bytes": 2035,
    "chunk_text": "Globally adored hippo Fiona dies after eating a visitor\u2019s hat at Cincinnati Zoo\n\nCNN - Fiona, the universally loved and internationally famous hippopotamus at Cincinnati Zoo, has died at age 9 after ingesting a knit hat that accidentally landed in her habitat, the zoo announced.\n\nThe social media darling had received round-the-clock care from the zoo\u2019s veterinary team and care staff, receiving various treatments, but did not survive. Her death has struck a chord with fans throughout Cincinnati and beyond, many of whom watched the hippo grow up.\n\n\u201cWe are in shock at the moment,\u201d said Cincinnati Zoo director Dave Jenike in a news release Thursday. \u201cWe did not expect this outcome. She fought until she couldn\u2019t anymore.\u201d\n\nSurgery had not been an option for the more than 2,000-pound animal, according to the Cincinnati Zoo, which had consulted with both \u201cveterinary and human specialists.\u201d\n\n\u201cDespite multiple attempts to move the hat out of her system, it was ultimately too much to overcome,\u201d the zoo said. \u201cThe team performed necessary supportive care today in an attempt to save her, but it was not successful and she did not recover.\u201d\n\nThe knit cap was tossed into the air in front of the habitat more than a week ago and caught by a ceiling fan that catapulted it through a 1-foot opening above the 12-foot-tall viewing windows, zoo communications director Michelle Curley said Tuesday.\n\nZoo Miami associate veterinarian and hippo expert Dr. Gabriella Flacke, who consulted on Fiona\u2019s case, said intestinal obstructions can occur when animals eat foreign objects.\n\nFiona previously underwent a colonoscopy, according to the zoo. An ultrasound showed her intestines were bunching, a sign that something was passing through her small intestine.\n\nWhen an object becomes stuck, \u201cthe intestines around that object will start to die, basically, and devitalize because of the pressure of the object in the blockage stretching the intestine to the point that the blood supply goes away,\u201d said Flacke."
  },
