    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "When an object becomes stuck, \u201cthe intestines around that object will start to die, basically, and devitalize because of the pressure of the object in the blockage stretching the intestine to the point that the blood supply goes away,\u201d said Flacke.\n\nFiona\u2019s care team may have decided against surgery partially because common hippos\u2019 weight and size can make it difficult for surgical incisions to heal, Flacke said. They also have unusually thick skin designed to insulate their bodies and offer protection.\n\n\u201cThey just are very specialized, which is great for them in their environment where they need all those adaptations to thrive and survive, but are not really geared toward a major surgery,\u201d Flacke said.\n\nFiona became an international celebrity when she was born six weeks prematurely in 2017, at a record-low weight of only 29 pounds. Hippos are typically born closer to 100 pounds, but Fiona managed to survive against all odds.\n\nSo small she could barely walk and unable to stand long enough to nurse from her mother Bibi, the baby animal had several zoo staff pressed into service to care for her, forming a care team they began to call \u201cTeam Fiona.\u201d They were joined by nurses from the nearby Cincinnati Children\u2019s Hospital, experts at finding veins and caring for tiny beings.\n\nBefore long, Team Fiona grew beyond a team and into a hashtag.\n\nAs Fiona put on weight and her health improved, the zoo\u2019s social media team began putting pictures and videos of the baby hippo online to let people follow her journey. The #TeamFiona hashtag began filling up social media with official updates from the zoo and requests and well wishes from fans all over the world.\n\nThe sporadic updates became daily as Fiona\u2019s fandom grew. Videos shared on social media showing keepers reading Fiona some of her fan mail or of her falling asleep in the shower went viral."
