    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "The community left pink and white flower bouquets and cards honoring Fiona leaning against a statue of the beloved hippo and her mother outside the zoo, video from CNN affiliate WKRC shows. A card \u2014 with a drawing of Fiona, a heart and childlike handwriting \u2014 read, \u201cHope that you get well soon friend.\u201d\n\n\u201cFiona was always my destination on those walks,\u201d he said. \u201cHer grit, how she went and would make herself available to her fans, her ear wiggle \u2026 Just the way that she held herself. She was the face of Cincinnati Zoo.\u201d\n\n\u201cFiona was such a gift,\u201d said Cincinnati Zoo Executive Chair of the Board Thane Maynard. \u201cI have never known an animal so beloved in all my life. From the day she was born, she was like lightning in a bottle. And, she will be remembered and beloved many years from now.\u201d\n\nVideo posted by the zoo Wednesday showed Fiona grunting and walking around her pen as she stuck her nose into the camera and wiggled her ears. This was an apparent improvement from video posted a day earlier showing the hippo sedated with a tube inserted in her nose and a towel covering her eyes.\n\nAlthough this brought hope to many, \u201crecovery isn\u2019t always a straight line,\u201d American Veterinary Medical Association president Dr. Jennifer Quammen told CNN.\n\nAnimals can show encouraging signs while still facing complications, she said. \u201cEven with careful monitoring, appropriate treatment, and the dedicated efforts of veterinary teams, some animals develop complications they cannot overcome.\u201d\n\nFiona\u2019s death, while seemingly the result of an accident, is a reminder for zoo visitors to secure their personal belongings, respect safety barriers and be mindful of animals\u2019 spaces, Quammen said. \u201cSomething that seems harmless to us can have devastating consequences for an animal.\u201d"
  },
