    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe reached the location within about half an hour. By then, the ground floor was already engulfed in flames, and the fire was spreading,\u201d Jayalal said.\n\nTwo fire engines were initially dispatched, but another was called in after firefighters learnt that four people could be trapped inside.\n\nHowever, what awaited them was far worse than they had anticipated.\n\nThe ground floor had become a mass of flames, while thick smoke from the burning business premises had risen to the upper floor. The building had no windows on the ground floor to provide adequate ventilation, making the situation even more difficult.\n\n\u201cWe were initially told that only one person was living in the building. But when we arrived, we learnt that an entire family of four were upstairs. We wanted to get inside immediately, but the flames were so intense that we could not even approach the building. Even with our protective clothing, it was impossible to get close,\u201d he recalled.\n\nAfter battling the fire, the firefighters finally managed to make their way upstairs. The upper floor was covered in thick black smoke.\n\nThey broke a window in an attempt to enter the premises. It was then that they spotted the little girl lying on the bed.\n\nThe discovery of the child, followed by the sight of the other family members, left the rescuers devastated.\n\n\u201cOur officers had to crawl along the floor to reach the child. We managed to get the little one out first. We had already arranged for 1990 Suwa Seriya ambulances to be on standby. The mother and father were lying on the floor. We then brought them down along with the other child,\u201d Jayalal said.\n\nThe parents had been working at the electrical appliance showroom and had moved into the upper floor of the building about three months earlier, police said.\n\nBut the building's layout and lack of emergency exits had made the rescue operation extremely difficult."
