    "md5": "786defebcd6b1d7e0a3be4f8a51b8d7b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "As the trilateral talks commenced yesterday, Xi described them as being of \u201cgroundbreaking significance\u201d, while Trump said the leaders were \u201creally making history\u201d. Meanwhile, Putin stated that Russia, China and the United States share a \u201cjoint responsibility\u201d for \u201cmaintaining international peace and security\u201d.\n\n\u201cFrom a Chinese perspective, they do not actually want the perception or the optics of a Yalta 2.0,\u201d Shullman told CNN.\n\nIt is interesting to note that while the United States, a long-time advocate of economic liberalism, is moving towards protectionism through the imposition of steep tariffs on both rich and poor countries, its socialist counterpart, China, is calling for greater openness in global trade. Of course, China stands to benefit from such a policy.\n\nAccording to the Chinese newspaper Global Times, China has put forward four proposals at the APEC summit for building an Asia-Pacific community and fostering shared prosperity in the region: upholding peace and stability, building bridges through openness and cooperation, promoting innovation-driven growth, and expanding opportunities for mutual benefit and win-win outcomes.\n\nAlthough Sri Lanka is not a member of the APEC forum, the decisions taken by the grouping have a bearing on the country, as it falls within the Asia-Pacific region both geographically and geopolitically. The Quadrilateral Security Dialogue (Quad) meeting that Trump attended in Manila during the US-ASEAN Summit, together with the leaders of Australia, India and Japan, may signal that Washington, after a period of apparent indifference to the Quad, is once again seeking to counter China\u2019s growing political influence in the Asia-Pacific region."
  },
