    "md5": "606917e4df99f6ae8f2c01dd1f6e3b43",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Lessons from \u00a0 Shenzhen summit  for Sri Lanka\n\nWhen Chinese President Xi Jinping, U.S. President Donald Trump, and Russian President Vladimir Putin convened in Shenzhen on the sidelines of the APEC summit, the optics alone signaled a profound message in global politics. For the first time in decades, the leaders of Washington, Beijing, and Moscow sat face-to-face not to ratify a universal rules-based charter, but to negotiate directly as supreme arbiters of a fractured world. For small nations watching from the periphery\u2014none more acutely than Sri Lanka\u2014this rare trilateral encounter is not merely diplomatic theater. It is a way of showing\u00a0 how the international system actually works. It indicates what is in store for the world.\n\nTo decode the Shenzhen summit, one must look past the surface-level headlines and examine the competing lenses of international relations theory. At first glance, the meeting appears to be a triumph of realism. Realists argue that international politics is an anarchic struggle for power where states\u2014especially great powers\u2014act solely in their self-interest.\n\nFrom a liberal perspective\u2014which emphasizes interdependence, international institutions, and shared norms\u2014the Shenzhen meeting exposes the erosion of multilateral guardrails. The post-1945 liberal order was built on the premise that trade and international law could take on great-power rivalry. Instead, we see institutions like the World Trade Organization and the United Nations bypassed in favor of ad-hoc \u201cBoards of Trade\u201d and bilateral tariff splits. However, liberalism is not dead; it is mutating. Even as Trump, Xi, and Putin disdain traditional multilateral constraints, the sheer fact that they must meet to manage economic entanglement, supply chains, and nuclear stability proves that deep interdependence still forces rivals to the negotiating table."
  },
