    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "From a liberal perspective\u2014which emphasizes interdependence, international institutions, and shared norms\u2014the Shenzhen meeting exposes the erosion of multilateral guardrails. The post-1945 liberal order was built on the premise that trade and international law could take on great-power rivalry. Instead, we see institutions like the World Trade Organization and the United Nations bypassed in favor of ad-hoc \u201cBoards of Trade\u201d and bilateral tariff splits. However, liberalism is not dead; it is mutating. Even as Trump, Xi, and Putin disdain traditional multilateral constraints, the sheer fact that they must meet to manage economic entanglement, supply chains, and nuclear stability proves that deep interdependence still forces rivals to the negotiating table.\n\nFor a small, strategically located island nation like Sri Lanka, the lessons from Shenzhen are urgent and existential. Situated astride the Indian Ocean\u2019s busiest sea lanes, Sri Lanka cannot afford to indulge in ideological nostalgia for a vanished unipolar order, nor can it passively drift into the orbit of a single patron.\n\nFirst, small states must abandon passive non-alignment in favor of active, principled hedging.\u00a0 When great powers strike grand bargains over the heads of smaller nations, neutrality without strategy becomes vulnerability. Colombo\u2019s recent calibrated balancing.\n\nSecond, domestic resilience is the ultimate foreign policy, particularly for a nation like Sri Lanka.\u00a0 Realist power politics punishes internal fragility. Sri Lanka\u2019s 2022 economic collapse demonstrated how swiftly debt distress translates into geopolitical leverage for external creditors. Sustaining macroeconomic stability, diversifying exports, and insulating national institutions from partisan capture are not merely domestic goals\u2014they are the prerequisites for diplomatic autonomy."
