    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "14ed23439513034ce2f6ec5c832c41da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1773,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo Port City spans 269 hectares of reclaimed land across 74 parcels designated for residential, commercial, hospitality and leisure uses. Approximately US $ 1.4 billion has been invested in land reclamation and horizontal infrastructure.\n\nThe SEZ\u2019s contribution should be assessed through realised foreign direct investment, foreign currency generation, employment, business activity and linkages with local suppliers. These economic benefits must be considered alongside the fiscal implications of incentives granted.\n\nInvestment incentives in Colombo Port City are governed by the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act, No. 11 of 2021, as amended and regulations and orders issued under the act. The law distinguishes between authorisation to operate in the SEZ and designation as a Primary or Secondary BSI.\n\nUnder Section 53, the CPCEC recommends eligible businesses for designation and incentives through the prescribed government process, culminating in a Cabinet-approved gazette order. Incentives therefore depend on the business category, applicable regulations and the terms of the relevant order and not on the SEZ registration alone.\n\nThe governance framework comprising the 2021 act, 2026 amendment and 2025 regulations, sets out eligibility criteria, designation procedures, incentive terms and oversight mechanisms. The 2026 amendment strengthens the statutory basis for investment thresholds, job creation criteria and performance evaluation.\n\nMonitoring is tied to the commitments, conditions, commencement dates and expiry provisions specified in each designation and gazette order. Assertions regarding monitoring practices, annual cost-benefit reporting or clawback mechanisms should be made only where supported by law or the relevant order."
  },
