    "md5": "3d706ac90b27860f13a4db8b117f6be8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "Monitoring is tied to the commitments, conditions, commencement dates and expiry provisions specified in each designation and gazette order. Assertions regarding monitoring practices, annual cost-benefit reporting or clawback mechanisms should be made only where supported by law or the relevant order.\n\nA predictable environment requires clear eligibility criteria, a defined designation process and transparent conditions attached to approved incentives. CPCEC Act Sections 52 and 53 read with the 2025 BSI Regulations establish the complete current framework.\n\nFiscal accountability and investor certainty are complementary. A rules-based, time-bound, time bound and transparent system enables investors to understand the conditions and duration of incentives while allowing the authorities to assess compliance against the statutory requirements.\n\nColombo Port City is designed to translate investment into tangible economic benefits for Sri Lanka, such as employment creation, workforce upskilling, increased foreign currency liquidity, knowledge transfer, new business opportunities and stronger domestic linkages.\n\nBy offering clear criteria, regulatory clarity, accountable oversight and streamlined investment facilitation, the CPCEC supports a competitive business environment that attracts strategic investment and drives long-term national growth.\n\nIn summary, Colombo Port City\u2019s incentive framework is designed to balance investor facilitation with fiscal responsibility. By grounding exemptions in clear criteria, statutory processes and measurable performance, the system ensures that the incentives are granted where they deliver tangible economic value. This disciplined approach supports both investor confidence and national development objectives."
