    "chunk_id": "2026-11-20-dailymirrorlk-b99ac19d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b3084f2fe3b73a3806836fc34fbc82b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Sungrow sees Galle battery plant as template for Sri Lanka\u2019s grid storage build-out\n\nSungrow says its 10 MW/40 MWh Galle plant offers a practical approach to grid flexibility as Sri Lanka prepares to add 560 MW of battery capacity in 2027\n\nThe company believes its PowerHarbor and PowerKeeper Lite systems can help homes and businesses store the surplus daytime solar that is now being curtailed\n\nChinese solar inverter and storage major Sungrow sees its 10 MW/40 MWh battery plant in Galle as a practical approach to strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s grid flexibility, as the country prepares to add 560 MW of new battery capacity next year to absorb its fast-growing solar output, the company told Mirror Business.\n\nDeveloped by WKV Hydro Technics on Sungrow\u2019s PowerTitan 2.0 platform, the battery energy storage system (BESS) entered commercial operation on September 10 and can discharge its full 10 MW output for four hours. The company noted in written responses that the project \u201cmarks an important milestone in Sri Lanka\u2019s development of grid-scale energy storage.\u201d\n\nSungrow explained that battery systems can rapidly adjust their charging and discharging power in response to grid requirements, which allows them to support frequency regulation when electricity supply and demand fall out of balance. They can also absorb surplus renewable energy when generation is abundant and release it when demand climbs, improving overall energy utilisation.\n\nThe company believes the October 17 island-wide blackout, which a preliminary National System Operator (NSO) report traced to the Norochcholai power plant, further highlighted the importance of power system resilience and flexibility. However, it was careful to point out that nationwide outages can involve multiple interconnected factors and cannot be prevented by a single storage project alone."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
