    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9b05bf5233a9a215144208c6e5a2336c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "The company believes the October 17 island-wide blackout, which a preliminary National System Operator (NSO) report traced to the Norochcholai power plant, further highlighted the importance of power system resilience and flexibility. However, it was careful to point out that nationwide outages can involve multiple interconnected factors and cannot be prevented by a single storage project alone.\n\nBudget 2027, presented last week, targets 560 MW of new battery capacity in 2027, the equivalent of 56 Galle-sized plants, before scaling up to 1,245 MW by 2029 to help lift renewables to 69 percent of power generation from about 61 percent in 2025. The NSO\u2019s tender for 150 MW/600 MWh of storage at existing solar plants, which is 15 times Galle\u2019s storage capacity, is also under way, with WindForce PLC and Vidullanka PLC among the successful bidders.\n\nMeanwhile, installed solar capacity reached 3,053 MW by October, exceeding the night-time peak demand of about 3,000 MW, as per the Sustainable Energy Authority (SEA). With little storage available to shift that output into the evening, the grid has been forced to curtail daytime generation. In August, NSO Chairman Dr. Pradeep Perera said commercial and industrial sites with solar installations above 200 kW were being asked to disconnect on Sundays, when demand falls while solar output keeps flowing.\n\n\u201cIn a solar-rich market like Sri Lanka, where rooftop PV installations continue to grow, storage can help consumers make better use of solar energy while gaining greater control over their energy supply,\u201d Sungrow pointed out."
  },
