    "md5": "9a373849b4b3a593d27fba73ce5e1af6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1047,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIn a solar-rich market like Sri Lanka, where rooftop PV installations continue to grow, storage can help consumers make better use of solar energy while gaining greater control over their energy supply,\u201d Sungrow pointed out.\n\nHouseholds investing in such systems will also benefit from the Budget\u2019s Rs.3.5 billion subsidy for rooftop solar with battery storage, which offers Rs.25,000 per kW up to a cap of Rs.250,000 per household, enough to support at least 14,000 homes at the maximum subsidy. The Ports and Airports Development Levy (PAL) on solar storage batteries will also be removed from January 1, 2027, lowering the upfront cost of storage.\n\nLooking ahead, Sungrow sees promising long-term opportunities in Sri Lanka and across South Asia. \u201cWhile Sri Lanka is a relatively compact market, its strategic value should not be measured by market size alone,\u201d the company stressed, adding that the need for flexible storage and smarter energy management on both the grid and consumer sides would grow as renewable capacity expands."
