    "md5": "06df89cd1c7f6a070e8e6aa39bbddf47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Nigeria moves to end jail terms for attempted suicide\n\nBBC - Nigerian President Bola Tinubu has asked parliament to decriminalise attempted suicide, proposing medical support instead of prosecution for those who survive.\n\nThe proposal seeks to shift Nigeria's approach from criminal punishment to treatment and rehabilitation.\n\nIt follows years of calls for reform from mental health professionals. A separate bill seeking to decriminalise attempted suicide has already passed its second reading in the Senate.\n\nUnder Nigeria's law, attempted suicide is a criminal offence with those convicted facing up to one year's imprisonment, a fine or both.\n\nThe Senate bill also seeks to replace criminal penalties for attempted suicide with medical treatment, rehabilitation and support.\n\nIt was sponsored by Senator Asuquo Ekpenyong, who has argued that prosecuting people who survive suicide attempts could discourage those experiencing suicidal thoughts from seeking help.\n\nEkpenyong has argued that prosecuting people who survive suicide attempts could discourage those experiencing suicidal thoughts from seeking help.\n\nHe also warned that families might conceal such incidents for fear of prosecution and social stigma.\n\n\"Anyone attempting suicide will ensure that the attempt is successful in order to avoid criminal charges, if he or she survives,\" he said.\n\nHe stressed that the bill is not aimed at legalising or encouraging suicide attempts but rather protecting the survivors.\n\nTinubu's proposal has been welcomed by Senate President Godswill Akpabio, who had previously suggested that people who survived suicide attempts should be sent to prison.\n\n\"We are saying that suicide is a crime for now. Therefore, if you attempt it, you better succeed. If you don't succeed, we will send you to prison,\" Akpabio had said.\n\nHowever, after receiving the Tinubu's proposal, he sought to clarify his earlier remarks, saying they did not apply to people experiencing mental health conditions."
  },
