    "md5": "68e31d3193a9863097f8fca370996f1a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1357,
    "chunk_text": "\"We are saying that suicide is a crime for now. Therefore, if you attempt it, you better succeed. If you don't succeed, we will send you to prison,\" Akpabio had said.\n\nHowever, after receiving the Tinubu's proposal, he sought to clarify his earlier remarks, saying they did not apply to people experiencing mental health conditions.\n\nHe added that punishing people whose actions were influenced by mental health conditions would be insensitive.\n\nTinubu's letter follows sustained calls from mental health professionals for the law to be changed.\n\nAt a parliamentary public hearing in May 2024, the then president of the Association of Psychiatrists of Nigeria, Professor Taiwo Obindo, urged lawmakers to remove criminal penalties for attempted suicide.\n\n\"Why do we, as a nation, then punish individuals who are ill and need medical attention rather than prosecution?\" he asked.\n\nDrawing on his experience as a psychiatrist, Obindo said most people who die by suicide have a history of mental illness.\n\nAkpabio has referred the president's amendment bill to the relevant senate committee for further consideration.\n\nNigeria is among several African countries where attempted suicide remains a criminal offence.\n\nGhana decriminalised attempted suicide in 2023, while Kenya's High Court ruled in January 2025 that the law criminalising it was unconstitutional."
