    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "SriLankan could break even by end 2026: Deputy Minister\n\nSriLankan Airlines could break even or turn a small profit by end 2026, Deputy Aviation Minister Ruwan Kodithuwakku told parliament yesterday, although Finance Ministry figures showed the national carrier incurring a loss of Rs. 8.9 billion in the first five months of its financial year.\n\nThe April\u2013August net loss would have reached Rs. 20.3 billion without a one-off reversal of Rs. 11.4 billion in withheld Rolls-Royce discounts and suspended credits, the ministry\u2019s figures showed.\n\nRevenue rose 35 percent to Rs. 154.6 billion from Rs. 114.6 billion a year earlier. Expenditure climbed 35.2 percent to Rs. 160.4 billion as aircraft fuel costs surged 78.2 percent, leaving the airline with a Rs. 5.8 billion operating loss. Foreign exchange losses of Rs. 11.9 billion and financing costs of Rs. 7.1 billion added to the pressure on the bottom line.\n\nThe ministry said tensions in the Middle East disrupted flights, restricted access to airspace and forced costly diversions. In the financial year ended March 2026, SriLankan\u2019s pre-tax loss had already tripled to Rs. 23.2 billion from Rs. 7.6 billion a year earlier, as financing and currency costs wiped out earnings before interest and tax of Rs. 26.4 billion.\n\nThe government has extended US$ 30 million, or about Rs. 10 billion, in short-term assistance to meet the airline\u2019s immediate operating and liquidity needs. It is also restructuring a US$ 175 million sovereign-guaranteed bond that matured in 2024 and pursuing a settlement of legacy debt. The Treasury guarantees a further Rs. 3.8 billion in obligations to the International Air Transport Association.\n\nKodithuwakku put the airline\u2019s outstanding loans at US$ 118 million to Bank of Ceylon and US$ 138 million to People\u2019s Bank. He also cited a US$ 70 million overdraft facility from Bank of Ceylon."
  },
