    "chunk_text": "The government has extended US$ 30 million, or about Rs. 10 billion, in short-term assistance to meet the airline\u2019s immediate operating and liquidity needs. It is also restructuring a US$ 175 million sovereign-guaranteed bond that matured in 2024 and pursuing a settlement of legacy debt. The Treasury guarantees a further Rs. 3.8 billion in obligations to the International Air Transport Association.\n\nKodithuwakku put the airline\u2019s outstanding loans at US$ 118 million to Bank of Ceylon and US$ 138 million to People\u2019s Bank. He also cited a US$ 70 million overdraft facility from Bank of Ceylon.\n\n\u201cSriLankan Airlines has become a burden on the General Treasury,\u201d he stressed.\n\nKodithuwakku said the government would decide how to restructure the carrier once it received the report of an expert committee. He said maintaining international air links was a consideration in that decision.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is an island and essentially needs connectivity with the world. The national carrier plays an important role when it comes to building connectivity with the world,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Cabinet appointed the committee in June under Presidential Senior Adviser on Digital Economy Dr. Hans Wijayasuriya, with International Finance Corporation as transaction adviser. The review covers the airline\u2019s corporate structure, operating model and potential partnerships, as well as options for raising capital, restructuring debt and allocating risk."
