    "md5": "d4bb36094f9d752f690e3d2c853f6d64",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1695,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo Port set to break 2025 record a month early as transshipment jumps 10%\n\nTransshipment volumes rose 10.0% year-on-year to 636,623 TEUs in October, accounting for 80.4% of total throughput\n\nCWIT\u2019s volume gain of 55,663 TEUs was larger than the port\u2019s entire increase for the month\n\nHaving handled 7.52mn TEUs in ten months, the port needs fewer boxes than it moved in October alone to surpass its 2025 record\n\nTransshipment volumes at the Port of Colombo rose 10.0 percent year-on-year to 636,623 TEUs in October, lifting their share of total container throughput to 80.4 percent from 77.7 percent a year earlier, while overall container handling grew at a slower 6.3 percent to 791,811 TEUs, as per Sri Lanka Ports Authority (SLPA) data.\n\nTransshipment added 57,693 TEUs during the month, well above the port\u2019s net gain of 47,019 TEUs, as domestic cargo continued to move in the opposite direction. Laden imports fell 15.5 percent to 51,898 TEUs from 61,428 TEUs, extending the decline recorded in August, although laden exports rose 5.7 percent to 28,128 TEUs. As a result, ten-month growth in laden imports has narrowed to 1.0 percent at 531,433 TEUs, down from 4.9 percent at the end of August, which suggests the slowdown in import demand has carried into the final quarter.\n\nOctober\u2019s throughput also marked an 8.2 percent month-on-month recovery from September, when the port handled an estimated 731,959 TEUs, 1.4 percent fewer than in the same month last year, based on calculations from SLPA\u2019s cumulative data. Transshipment grew a modest 2.0 percent to 596,755 TEUs in September, a month in which congestion at the port peaked and adverse weather disrupted operations."
  },
