    "md5": "9d304ad0fd798f76259db1fd9115dced",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1744,
    "chunk_text": "October\u2019s throughput also marked an 8.2 percent month-on-month recovery from September, when the port handled an estimated 731,959 TEUs, 1.4 percent fewer than in the same month last year, based on calculations from SLPA\u2019s cumulative data. Transshipment grew a modest 2.0 percent to 596,755 TEUs in September, a month in which congestion at the port peaked and adverse weather disrupted operations.\n\nFor the first ten months, the port handled 7,518,617 TEUs, 8.6 percent more when compared to the same period last year, with transshipment of 6,061,346 TEUs accounting for 544,568 TEUs, or 91.2 percent, of the 596,871 TEU increase. Over the same period, the three terminals other than CWIT collectively handled 415,140 fewer TEUs, with SAGT recording the steepest fall of 15.3 percent. Colombo now needs only 772,561 TEUs to surpass its 2025 record of 8,291,178 TEUs, fewer than it handled in October alone, placing the milestone within reach in November. Should the ten-month average of around 751,862 TEUs a month hold, the port would close the year at just over nine million TEUs, 8.8 percent higher than in 2025 and in line with the projection by SLPA Chairman Dr. Parakrama Dissanayake in September.\n\nTotal ship arrivals fell 6.3 percent to 345 in October, with container vessel calls declining 7.9 percent to 291. Nevertheless, the volume handled per container call rose 15.4 percent to around 2,721 TEUs, indicating larger exchanges per vessel rather than more ships. At CICT, calls dropped 18.8 percent to 91 even as volumes edged up, lifting its average exchange by 24.7 percent to around 3,085 TEUs. Total cargo handled rose 3.3 percent to 9.94 million tonnes in October and 1.0 percent to 98.89 million tonnes for the ten months."
