    "md5": "fac2dddf459b328c6d663c97934b4082",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "Fitch upgrades six Sri Lankan insurers after sovereign rating lift\n\nState-owned SLIC Life and SLIC General rise to AA-(lka), the highest ratings among the eight entities reviewed\n\nFitch says the stronger sovereign credit profile has reduced insurers\u2019 investment and liquidity risks\n\nContinental Insurance stays at A(lka) following a Rs. 654 million loss in 2025\n\nFitch Ratings raised the national financial strength ratings of six Sri Lankan insurers by one notch yesterday, saying the sovereign\u2019s stronger credit profile had reduced investment and liquidity risks for a sector heavily exposed to government securities and domestic financial institutions.\n\nState-owned Sri Lanka Insurance Corporation Life and Sri Lanka Insurance Corporation General were upgraded to AA-(lka) from A+(lka). HNB Life, HNB General Insurance and People\u2019s Insurance moved to A+(lka) from A(lka), while Co-operative Insurance rose to BB+(lka) from BB(lka). Fitch affirmed Continental Insurance Lanka at A(lka) and the Construction Guarantee Fund at BB(lka). All eight outlooks are stable.\n\nThe rating actions followed Fitch\u2019s October recalibration of its Sri Lankan national rating scale after it raised the sovereign\u2019s long-term local-currency rating to \u2018B-\u2019 from \u2018CCC+\u2019 on September 22. The national ratings rank financial strength relative to other issuers in Sri Lanka.\n\n\u201cWe believe the sovereign\u2019s credit profile improvement has reduced insurers\u2019 investment and liquidity risks, which are closely linked to those of the sovereign and domestic banks because insurers\u2019 investment portfolios are dominated by local-currency fixed-income government securities, corporate debt and deposits with financial institutions,\u201d Fitch said."
  },
