    "md5": "8c377b29e7d2bf0cc48743f9e4bcbc1f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "The 2027 Budget presented to the Parliament of Sri Lanka represents more than an annual exercise in estimating Government revenue and expenditure. It reflects the Government\u2019s broader economic vision and its policy direction towards transforming the national economy from a phase of economic stabilisation to a more sustainable, productive and growth-oriented development model. The important emphasis of the Budget is on strengthening domestic production, promoting investment, expanding export capacity, improving economic competitiveness and ensuring inclusive social development.\n\nFurthermore, the effectiveness of the proposed Budget will depend not just on the allocation of financial resources but also on the quality of implementation, institutional coordination, transparency and accountability. Improving the efficiency of State-owned enterprises, reducing unnecessary public expenditure, strengthening public sector governance and ensuring that development projects deliver measurable economic returns are equally important. In this regard, the Government must move beyond policy announcements and establish clear implementation mechanisms, measurable performance indicators and appropriate monitoring systems to ensure that Budgetary commitments translate into tangible economic outcomes.\n\nTherefore, the 2027 Budget should be evaluated not simply as an annual financial statement, but as a strategic economic policy framework that will influence Sri Lanka\u2019s future development path. Its ultimate success will depend on whether it can translate fiscal discipline into productive investment, economic recovery into sustainable growth and national economic progress into tangible improvements in the living standards of the people. The critical question is whether the policies and financial allocations proposed in the Budget are sufficiently practical, financially sustainable and effectively implementable to achieve the country\u2019s long term economic transformation."
  },
