    "md5": "c50d07f9c3c39eeff58b5e6950c0c576",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1818,
    "chunk_text": "Therefore, in addition to maintaining fiscal discipline, priority must be given to expanding domestic production capacity, diversifying exports, attracting foreign investment, fostering technological innovation and developing human resources. Ultimately, the success of the Budget is determined not only by achieving the stated financial targets, but by translating them into tangible economic outcomes, such as job creation, growth in public income and an improved standard of living.\n\nFrom a broader economic perspective, the key challenge facing the Government is not simply the reduction of the Budget deficit but the improvement of the quality and efficiency of public finances. Although increasing tax revenue is essential for fiscal consolidation, excessive reliance on indirect taxation could increase the cost of living, place additional pressure on businesses and discourage consumption and investment. At the same time, expenditure rationalisation should not come at the expense of essential development investments that generate future economic returns. The Government must therefore gradually shift its expenditure priorities from consumption-oriented spending towards productive capital investment, particularly in transport infrastructure, ports, logistics, energy, technology and export-oriented industries. In addition, improving the financial performance of State-owned enterprises, strengthening public procurement and introducing measurable performance targets for public expenditure are essential. Ultimately, the success of the 2027 Budget should be assessed not merely by its ability to achieve short-term fiscal targets, but by whether it can create a sustainable revenue base, reduce the debt burden and generate sufficient economic growth to improve the country\u2019s long-term fiscal position."
