    "chunk_text": "From a broader economic perspective, the key challenge facing the Government is not simply the reduction of the Budget deficit but the improvement of the quality and efficiency of public finances. Although increasing tax revenue is essential for fiscal consolidation, excessive reliance on indirect taxation could increase the cost of living, place additional pressure on businesses and discourage consumption and investment. At the same time, expenditure rationalisation should not come at the expense of essential development investments that generate future economic returns. The Government must therefore gradually shift its expenditure priorities from consumption-oriented spending towards productive capital investment, particularly in transport infrastructure, ports, logistics, energy, technology and export-oriented industries. In addition, improving the financial performance of State-owned enterprises, strengthening public procurement and introducing measurable performance targets for public expenditure are essential. Ultimately, the success of the 2027 Budget should be assessed not merely by its ability to achieve short-term fiscal targets, but by whether it can create a sustainable revenue base, reduce the debt burden and generate sufficient economic growth to improve the country\u2019s long-term fiscal position.\n\nThe 2027 Budget proposes an allocation of Rs. 95 billion for the modernisation of transport and road infrastructure. Key proposals under this initiative include expanding the metro bus service to major cities, providing the necessary facilities for 100 electric buses, procuring 200 small buses to strengthen rural transport, acquiring five railway carriages, and modernising railway stations. Furthermore, investments have been proposed for the expansion of the highway network, including the Central and Ruwanpura Expressway projects."
