    "md5": "7c5aec65829811cc434fc59ac79f32a1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "Chinese Ambassador and Pathfinder Founder discuss broadening relations\n\nChinese Ambassador Wei Huaxiang (third from left) with Pathfinder Foundation Founder Milinda Moragoda. At the meeting, the Chinese Embassy was also represented by Counsellor Mei Jing and Second Secretary Jin Enze. The Pathfinder Foundation was represented by its Chairman, Ambassador (Retd.) Bernard Goonetilleke; Director, Ambassador (Retd.) Ahmed Jawad, and Consultant, Strategic Communications G.S. Godakanda\n\nChinese Ambassador Wei Huaxiang (right) with Pathfinder Foundation Founder Milinda Moragoda\n\nThe Ambassador of the People\u2019s Republic of China to Sri Lanka, Wei Huaxiang, met the Pathfinder Foundation Founder Milinda Moragoda recently, to exchange views on how to further strengthen cooperation between China and the Pathfinder Foundation.\n\nThe Pathfinder Foundation\u2019s engagement with China dates back many years. A key milestone in this relationship was the establishment of the China-Sri Lanka Cooperation Studies Centre (CSLCSC) in October 2015.\n\nOver the years, this partnership has evolved through numerous initiatives, including international conferences, academic and scholarly exchanges, joint publications and collaborative research projects. At present the Pathfinder Foundation maintains 10 active Memoranda of Understanding (MoUs) with leading Chinese think tanks, universities, research institutions and other organisations.\n\nDuring the meeting, Moragoda briefed Ambassador Wei on the work the Pathfinder Foundation had undertaken in collaboration with numerous Chinese institutions, think tanks, educational and other institutions. Ambassador Wei expressed interest in upgrading Sri Lankan agricultural practices with Chinese know-how, concluding a China-Sri Lanka free trade agreement to facilitate the introduction of new technology and facilitate trade, providing know-how on artificial intelligence, and addressing human-elephant conflict using experience gained from Yunnan Province, among other areas."
  },
