    "chunk_text": "CA Sri Lanka Faculty of Taxation Chairman Saman Srilal observed that the Budget places considerable emphasis on productive sectors of the economy through investments in export-oriented manufacturing, tourism, agriculture, technology, digitalisation and SMEs. He welcomed several tax measures, including personal income tax relief, investment incentives to encourage export-oriented projects, and initiatives to strengthen tax administration and improve compliance processes.\n\nOffering an independent economic perspective, Verit\u00e9 Research Executive Director and Head of Research Dr. Nishan de Mel discussed the broader fiscal and economic implications of the Budget. He acknowledged the emphasis on the digital economy, renewable energy and export growth, while drawing attention to areas requiring continued policy focus, including labour force participation, higher education and public sector reform.\n\nRepresenting the Government\u2019s policy perspective, Finance Ministry Tax Policy Advisor Tanuja Perera provided insights into several tax proposals announced in the Budget, including revisions to personal income taxation, withholding tax measures and reforms relating to digital and cross-border taxation. She explained the policy rationale behind these proposals and how they are intended to help maintain fiscal sustainability while providing targeted relief to taxpayers.\n\nAddressing developments in tax administration, Inland Revenue Department Commissioner P. Nandan Kumara outlined ongoing efforts to modernise the tax system through digitalisation, improved taxpayer services and greater inter-agency integration. He highlighted plans to advance the Department\u2019s transition towards a more efficient, technology-driven tax administration framework."
