    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5c02bf13313dda2677a8c3d81cd2f068",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "And the first rung of the ladder is breaking. Stanford researchers find that since late 2022, employment of 22- to 25-year-olds in the occupations most exposed to AI has fallen steadily behind their peers, a gap that has now widened to 19%, mainly because firms are simply not hiring them. The World Bank ranks Sri Lanka among South Asia\u2019s most AI-exposed economies and last in jobs where AI complements workers rather than replaces them; nearly a quarter of our workforce is in exposed occupations. The clerical, call-centre and junior coding jobs our graduates queue for are the first to go. If Colombo cannot promise those first jobs, the regions must be free to create new ones, in work that needs local hands and local knowledge.\n\nAfter 1987\u201389, the Presidential Commission on Youth heard young people say they could bear hardship but not deprivation with injustice. Its recommendations were shelved. Every uprising we have had followed one pattern: a voice unheard, a conflict escalated. The street is where unheard arguments go.\n\nThe 1986 UN Declaration on the Right to Development calls the human person the central subject of development: its active participant, not merely its beneficiary. That is the test Sri Lanka has failed since independence. In more than seventy-five years, no government has asked the regions what their development priorities are. Colombo has always decided. Decentralised budgets went to MPs, not regions; the District Development Councils of 1981 were stillborn; Provincial Councils have been starved of money and power since 1988. The outcome to date is four uprisings: the JVP insurrections of 1971 and 1987\u201389, a war in the North and East that consumed a generation, and the Aragalaya of 2022. We ask citizens to bear the cost, yet never let them, or their regions, decide what comes next."
  },
