    "chunk_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-9f0a2478-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cb230e2dba7c9e80d2f634a8d8e8c37a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1661,
    "chunk_text": "The 1986 UN Declaration on the Right to Development calls the human person the central subject of development: its active participant, not merely its beneficiary. That is the test Sri Lanka has failed since independence. In more than seventy-five years, no government has asked the regions what their development priorities are. Colombo has always decided. Decentralised budgets went to MPs, not regions; the District Development Councils of 1981 were stillborn; Provincial Councils have been starved of money and power since 1988. The outcome to date is four uprisings: the JVP insurrections of 1971 and 1987\u201389, a war in the North and East that consumed a generation, and the Aragalaya of 2022. We ask citizens to bear the cost, yet never let them, or their regions, decide what comes next.\n\nThe 13th Amendment of 1987 gave nine Provincial Councils 37 subjects of their own, among them agriculture, irrigation, health, education and provincial planning, and a Finance Commission to balance development across regions. Its intent was a measure of self-direction. That intent has been honoured mostly in the breach. Councils have gone nearly nine years without elections, most provincial money pays salaries, and subjects have drifted back to ministries.\n\nThe Praja Shakthi program shows the bottom-up instinct is alive: community councils in some 14,000 villages now propose local projects. But village councils that answer to a ministry, with no elected provincial tier above them, are a ladder missing its middle rungs. The 13th Amendment supplies those rungs. Using it needs no new constitution, only elections and the will to let elected councils decide."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-9f0a2478",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9f0a2478",
    "date_str": "2026-11-20",
    "description": "Give citizens and regions chance, and Sri Lanka grows richer",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Give-citizens-and-regions-chance-and-Sri-Lanka-grows-richer/4-800403",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795198588.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-9f0a2478-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "870f8ee4229f4356e469ef17de2f7d83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1465,
    "chunk_text": "The Praja Shakthi program shows the bottom-up instinct is alive: community councils in some 14,000 villages now propose local projects. But village councils that answer to a ministry, with no elected provincial tier above them, are a ladder missing its middle rungs. The 13th Amendment supplies those rungs. Using it needs no new constitution, only elections and the will to let elected councils decide.\n\nIn the North, a charter might make the province the country\u2019s most water-efficient farming region, owned through a farmer-majority Growers Federation and exporting across 64 kilometres to Tamil Nadu and to diaspora markets abroad. In the hill country, it would begin with dignity: land titles, housing and a route from estate labour to ownership. In the East, shared mills and fish markets could make coexistence pay. Some will fail. A province that tries and misses learns faster than a nation that waits for permission.\n\nOthers show it can work. In 1996 Kerala handed 35 to 40% of its development budget to local bodies; today its Kudumbashree women\u2019s groups supply nearly a third of its local councillors.\n\nOur plans are also written for a climate we no longer have. After floods and Cyclone Ditwah, the Meteorology Department now forecasts drought from January to April 2027 as a strong El Ni\u00f1o plays out. Yet budgets still treat floods, droughts, saline intrusion and coastal erosion as emergencies to answer afterwards, not conditions to design for."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-9f0a2478",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9f0a2478",
    "date_str": "2026-11-20",
    "description": "Give citizens and regions chance, and Sri Lanka grows richer",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Give-citizens-and-regions-chance-and-Sri-Lanka-grows-richer/4-800403",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795198588.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-20-dailyftlk-9f0a2478-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "455500001e3d24311bbb87690c896d4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "Our plans are also written for a climate we no longer have. After floods and Cyclone Ditwah, the Meteorology Department now forecasts drought from January to April 2027 as a strong El Ni\u00f1o plays out. Yet budgets still treat floods, droughts, saline intrusion and coastal erosion as emergencies to answer afterwards, not conditions to design for.\n\nOpportunity needs guarantees. The Sri Lanka Resilience and Accountability Framework, published in the Daily FT on 12 June, would turn development promises into obligations. It sets measurable targets in law, such as social protection of at least eight% of the budget and ten doctors per 10,000 people in every district. And it creates an independent commission able to sanction those who miss targets. A promise without a penalty is only a press release. Lenders, who audit our books while no one audits their impact, should meet the same standard. A public district dashboard would let every province see whether it is being heard.\n\nFairness must also reach within regions, to estate families, female-headed households, oppressed castes, persons with disabilities and informal workers. Equality cannot simply be devolved; it must be guaranteed.\n\nOpportunity is a two-way bargain. Citizens create the jobs and revenue that fund every right, pay their taxes honestly, refuse bribes, and keep watch through the forums that write their province\u2019s charter. The State\u2019s side is to tax fairly, show results, and clear away the gatekeeping that strangles initiative.\n\nWill we not all be richer for it? The Western Province holds 28% of the people and produces 42% of output. The other eight provinces hold nearly three-quarters of the people, most of the land and water, and produce less than three-fifths. If they closed even half the gap in output per person with the West, the national economy would be roughly a quarter larger, without a rupee of new debt. That wealth will come not from Colombo but from people given the chance to build where they live."
  },
