    "md5": "a41136033867d489cc29cd6e50528671",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "What a beautiful feeling! It was something beyond ordinary words.\n\nBut the most wonderful experience of my journey was yet to come.\n\nThe Braj Raj: The adorable and tender touch of the sacred soil of Vrindavan\n\nThe tiny particles felt delicate, almost like flower petals. The texture reminded me of freshly prepared laddus, made with flour and ghee\u2014soft, delicate, and gentle to the touch. Every particle seemed to carry that tenderness. With every step, I felt as though the earth itself was receiving my feet. The soil seemed to make space for each step, welcoming me and allowing me to move forward. I did not feel that I was simply walking on the ground. It felt as though the land itself was lovingly accepting my presence, almost embracing my feet. For a moment, I felt connected to something much deeper than the physical surroundings. The sacredness of Braj Raj was no longer just a belief I had heard from others. It had become a personal experience of tenderness, humility, and surrender.\n\nI cannot fully explain that feeling. Perhaps the sacredness of a place is not always understood through explanations. Sometimes, it is experienced through the simplest contact with nature.\n\nThat night, I was physically tired and eventually fell asleep, but the experience remained with me.\n\nThe following day, I visited an ashram. I do not wish to mention its name. What matters here is not the identity of the place or the people, but what the experience made me reflect upon.\n\nWhen I entered, I saw a monk sitting peacefully on a raised platform built around a tree. His eyes were closed, and he held a Japamala (prayer beads) in his hands. His face appeared completely immersed in bliss. I stood there for a moment, observing the stillness around him. Suddenly, he opened his eyes and noticed my footwear. In a loud voice, he instructed me to remove my slippers, as wearing footwear inside the ashram, especially on the sacred soil of Vrindavan, was not permitted."
  },
