    "chunk_text": "When I entered, I saw a monk sitting peacefully on a raised platform built around a tree. His eyes were closed, and he held a Japamala (prayer beads) in his hands. His face appeared completely immersed in bliss. I stood there for a moment, observing the stillness around him. Suddenly, he opened his eyes and noticed my footwear. In a loud voice, he instructed me to remove my slippers, as wearing footwear inside the ashram, especially on the sacred soil of Vrindavan, was not permitted.\n\nLater, I was directed towards a place where Prasad was being distributed. It was around noon, and what I witnessed there was remarkable. Thousands of sadhus and other people were seated, waiting for food. Volunteers were serving meals to everyone. There was an abundance of food, and people were waiting patiently to receive it.\n\nWhile looking around, I noticed a person holding a Japamala inside a cloth covering commonly used while performing Japa. His index finger remained outside the covering. I remembered hearing that, according to certain traditions, the index finger should not touch the beads during Japa because it is associated with the ego.\n\nThis made me think about the symbolism behind the practice. If the entire body belongs to God, why is one finger considered unsuitable for touching something sacred?\n\nAt that very moment, I noticed the same person pointing his index finger towards another individual and shouting angrily:\n\n\u201cHey! Don't you know that using a mobile phone is prohibited here?\u201d\n\nThe contrast was striking. The finger being kept away from the sacred beads was being used to point angrily at another human being.\n\nWe are so careful about the way we hold a mala, but how careful are we about the words that come out of our mouths?\n\nWe protect the beads from our index finger, but do we protect our consciousness from ego?\n\nWe pay attention to the movements of our fingers during Japa, but are we equally attentive to the movements of our own ego?"
  },
