    "md5": "f2cd98bfcbb93f57733c82ffe8626d25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "We protect the beads from our index finger, but do we protect our consciousness from ego?\n\nWe pay attention to the movements of our fingers during Japa, but are we equally attentive to the movements of our own ego?\n\nAs I moved towards the exit, another unexpected encounter brought my thoughts back to the meaning of service.\n\nThe entrance to the food distribution area was closed, and a few monks were standing outside, waiting to enter and receive food. Among them, I recognised someone. He was the same monk whom I had encountered earlier during my walk, when I had asked for a little help with a lift. He had ignored my request and moved away. Now, I saw him standing outside the gate, politely requesting permission to enter. I watched him and smiled quietly. It was not about the lift or any personal grievance. What stayed with me was the nature of service. If someone is walking beside us and needs a little help, isn't that an opportunity to serve? If we believe that God exists in every living being, shouldn't our first response be kindness?\n\n\u201cIf you cannot recognise God in everyone, how can you claim to have found God?\u201d\n\nIt felt as though life itself was showing me a lesson through an unexpected situation.\n\nPerhaps wearing the identity of a sadhu is one thing, but living with the heart of a sadhu is an entirely different journey. A spiritual life should not be limited to a particular dress, a mala, a tilak, or a place of residence. It should be reflected in the way we treat others, especially when nobody is watching.\n\nAs I continued reflecting on these experiences, another observation from Vrindavan came to my mind. Throughout the city, I had seen people feeding monkeys, offering fruits to cows, and giving food to birds. It was beautiful to witness. People were buying fruits and distributing them among the animals. Perhaps they were doing it out of genuine love, devotion, or the belief that such acts would bring punya, or spiritual merit."
  },
