    "md5": "85ad1b9db89d6ef523928fb105c5301a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "As I continued reflecting on these experiences, another observation from Vrindavan came to my mind. Throughout the city, I had seen people feeding monkeys, offering fruits to cows, and giving food to birds. It was beautiful to witness. People were buying fruits and distributing them among the animals. Perhaps they were doing it out of genuine love, devotion, or the belief that such acts would bring punya, or spiritual merit.\n\nWhen we return to our homes, do we continue the same practices?\n\nDo we show the same compassion towards animals when they are no longer surrounded by the atmosphere of Vrindavan?\n\nWhy should a cow in Vrindavan be considered sacred, while a cow elsewhere becomes just another animal?\n\nWhy should a bird need to be in a holy place to receive our kindness?\n\nIf we believe that every living being carries the presence of God, shouldn't our compassion extend everywhere?\n\nI understand that we all have different intentions, and many perform these acts with genuine devotion. I do not wish to judge anyone. But I felt that sometimes we become so attached to the idea of performing good deeds in a sacred place that we forget to make compassion a part of our everyday lives.\n\nIt is almost as though we believe that something becomes spiritually valuable only when performed in a particular location. I wondered whether we were following the feeling of devotion or simply following what everyone around us was doing. Because genuine compassion should not depend on geography. A cow does not become sacred only when it enters Vrindavan. A bird does not become worthy of kindness only when it flies over a temple.\n\nIf our love is genuine, it should not need a sacred address.\n\nAll these experiences brought me to a deeper reflection on the rituals we perform in the name of devotion.\n\nWe offer flowers, light lamps, burn incense, and decorate deities. These traditions can be beautiful expressions of love and reverence. But I began looking at them from another perspective."
  },
  {
