    "md5": "61f8171f5449a81da67cd393ea661af4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1612,
    "chunk_text": "All these experiences brought me to a deeper reflection on the rituals we perform in the name of devotion.\n\nWe offer flowers, light lamps, burn incense, and decorate deities. These traditions can be beautiful expressions of love and reverence. But I began looking at them from another perspective.\n\nPerhaps this is where we need to look more deeply at our relationship with God. Do we sometimes limit our devotion to external offerings while overlooking the divine presence in everyday life?\n\nPerhaps God is already alive in everything around us\u2014in the soil beneath our feet, the air we breathe, the birds, the animals, and every human being. Maybe it is not God who needs awakening, but our own consciousness.\n\nWe worship love, yet struggle to love. We worship compassion, yet sometimes fail to show kindness. We offer flowers to the divine, yet may find it difficult to offer a gentle word to another human being.\n\nPerhaps the true purpose of rituals is not merely to perform them, but to allow them to transform the way we think, feel, and live.\n\nThe true expression of Bhakti is not simply how beautifully we worship the divine, but how genuinely we embody love, humility, and compassion towards every living being.\n\nI arrived in Vrindavan seeking an experience of God. I left with a deeper understanding that the divine is not confined to a temple or a sacred place. The soil, the air, the animals, the birds, and the people all reminded me to look beyond appearances.\n\nIf God is already alive in everything around us, is it really God who needs awakening by doing rituals, or is it our own consciousness?"
  },
