    "md5": "a039ed999d02fa3daccd4bf4c4fb218a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Phantom synagogues and tall tribe tales: Myths of Sri Lanka\u2019s Jewish past\n\nEver since the advent of the internet, accounts of Sri Lankan history have painted romantic tales of an ancient Jewish presence on the island. These narratives conjure images of biblical ships docking in Galle, Jewish ministers advising Lankan kings, and massive synagogues with men in hats walking to service in colonial era Colombo.\n\nYet, modern factual research reveals a narrative built littered with unverified myths, romantic hypotheses, and mistranslated texts. Both Sri Lankan and Israeli media frequently repeat these tall tales without basic fact-checking, playing a game of telephone.\n\nJews lived in many places and Sri Lanka has hosted people from many places, so naturally when people realise that there is no large, permanent Jewish community here like nearby India, the imagination runs wild. However, the verified history is one of a minor, transient merchant presence\u2014and is equally fascinating once you unlock the truth under the fog of rumours.\n\nThe most enduring legend links Galle to the biblical port of Tarshish, where King Solomon supposedly sourced gold, ivory, apes, and peacocks.\n\nSolomon the wise reigned around 970\u2013931 BCE, centuries before Sri Lanka\u2019s first recorded king, Vijaya, arrived in roughly 543 BCE. The Tarshish connection was just a romantic hypothesis popularized in 1859 by Sir James Emerson Tennent, former Colonial Secretary of Ceylon.\n\nTheories claiming that the rock inscriptions documented in local prehistoric cave art by Professor Raj Somadeva is Hebrew are highly anachronistic: During the era of King Solomon, the Hebrew language was written in a Paleo-Hebrew script that looks entirely different from the modern square alphabet used to make that comparison.\n\nFurthermore, simple shapes like straight lines and circles are universal basic motifs that appear across many cultures and cannot be used to prove a solid connection."
  },
