    "language": "en",
    "md5": "943dd79e2e1ab82b0ec3445b83ce7cb7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1792,
    "chunk_text": "Theories claiming that the rock inscriptions documented in local prehistoric cave art by Professor Raj Somadeva is Hebrew are highly anachronistic: During the era of King Solomon, the Hebrew language was written in a Paleo-Hebrew script that looks entirely different from the modern square alphabet used to make that comparison.\n\nFurthermore, simple shapes like straight lines and circles are universal basic motifs that appear across many cultures and cannot be used to prove a solid connection.\n\nSimilarly, the medieval logs frequently cited as proof of a Jewish community lack concrete evidence. A 12th-century\u00a0 itinerary by Benjamin of Tudela claimed that a few thousand Jews lived in the region, while the geographer al-Idrisi asserted that a Sinhalese king appointed four Jewish ministers to a royal council. Neither writer ever set foot on the island; their accounts were entirely based on bazaar gossip. Some online communities have even conflated this king with Kasyapa I who built the rock citadel Sigiriya - Al-Idrissi lived almost 700 years after Sigiriya was built.\n\nFurthermore, Benjamin of Tudela\u2019s original manuscript mentions Ibrig/Kulam, which in later translations was transcribed as \"Candig\". Later readers assumed this referred to Kandy\u2014a major anachronism, given that Kandy was not an established capital in the 12th century. Similar unverified claims made by Abu Zeid al-Hasan of Siraf remain equally debatable.\n\nArchaeological excavations at Tel Dor in Israel uncovered 3,000-year-old Phoenician ceramic flasks. Organic residue analysis revealed traces of cinnamaldehyde within these vessels. Although the 2013 study only showed that it wasn\u2019t African cinnamon and native to Asia. Further tests have to be conducted to confirm the Sri Lankan connection as fact."
  },
