    "md5": "3ac9ff8e827015cc8f3aecdf0eacc4a7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Archaeological excavations at Tel Dor in Israel uncovered 3,000-year-old Phoenician ceramic flasks. Organic residue analysis revealed traces of cinnamaldehyde within these vessels. Although the 2013 study only showed that it wasn\u2019t African cinnamon and native to Asia. Further tests have to be conducted to confirm the Sri Lankan connection as fact.\n\nWhile there are letters in the Geniza speaking of Middle Eastern Jews marrying Indian women, there is no record of marriage in Sri Lanka; rather they mostly left their wives behind at home.\n\nIn contrast, Arab Muslim traders left definitive proof of their voyages with a vibrant local Muslim community that claims descent from them. Local chronicles like the Mahavamsa, Culavamsa, palm-leaf manuscripts, and stone inscriptions are entirely silent when it comes to Jewish presence.\n\nEven the Cochin Jews of nearby India who have been there for over a 1,000 years and maintained a rich oral tradition of folk songs and poetry that stretches back centuries, make no mention of a sister community across the sea.\n\nIn one letter from the 1130s, a Middle-Eastern businessman named Joseph b. Abraham mentions a young business partner named Abu l-Faraj b. Moses al-Baghdadi, who absconded from India and ran away to Sri Lanka. Joseph tries to track him down in vain. So, it is possible that a Jewish individual remained a fugitive in Sri Lanka. Although nobody can truly verify if he even stayed in Sri Lanka or went elsewhere to disappear.\n\nConsequently, modern claims that the local Thattar or Rodiya communities possess Jewish ancestry are dubious. It is probable that these theories came about because Thattars work with gold (a trade frequently associated with European Jewish names like Goldsmith) and Rodiyas historically lived, as a marginalised group the way Jews were historically confined to ghettos in Europe."
  },
