    "chunk_size_bytes": 1744,
    "chunk_text": "Consequently, modern claims that the local Thattar or Rodiya communities possess Jewish ancestry are dubious. It is probable that these theories came about because Thattars work with gold (a trade frequently associated with European Jewish names like Goldsmith) and Rodiyas historically lived, as a marginalised group the way Jews were historically confined to ghettos in Europe.\n\nThere is no archaeological find yet showing ancient synagogues or other medieval Jewish graves or structures in Sri Lanka.\n\nA wave of Jewish individuals arrived centuries later during European colonial times. Some arrived disguised as \"New Christians\" (Conversos) alongside the Portuguese, and later, openly with the Dutch East India Company (VOC). However, these individuals largely assimilated into the broader Burgher (Eurasian) community over generations, and the majority of their descendants do not maintain the Jewish faith. While many Sinhalese and Tamil families carry Portuguese-derived surnames with some that can be traced back to Sephardic Jewish roots (such as Perera, Fonseka or De Silva), it remains unclear whether locals adopted these names directly upon conversion, intermarriage, or perhaps a combination of both.\n\nRobert Knox was an English sailor who spent over 19 years in captivity in the Kandyan Kingdom after being detained by King Rajasinghe II in 1660. Again, he made no mention of Jews among the king\u2019s subjects in his writings.\n\nHad the Portuguese encountered a pre-existing Jewish community on the island, they most likely would have documented it. For example, while exploring West Africa, they encountered griots or gewalas (oral storytellers) and mistakenly wrote that they were Jews. They made no such recordings in Sri Lanka."
  },
