    "md5": "0f93a2cb2f93b97da9bad4c6619a9c17",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "Had the Portuguese encountered a pre-existing Jewish community on the island, they most likely would have documented it. For example, while exploring West Africa, they encountered griots or gewalas (oral storytellers) and mistakenly wrote that they were Jews. They made no such recordings in Sri Lanka.\n\nMultiple interviews claiming to have seen Jewish men in hats and suits walking in Colombo stem from an alternative explanation: these memories were simply that of local elites or foreigners wearing formal suits and top hats heading out. Notably, the nearby Galle Face Hotel had a similar formal dress code. This serves as a classic example of the \"Mandela Effect\"\u2014a collective false memory similar to the widespread beliefs that Darth Vader said, \"Luke, I am your father,\" or that the Monopoly man wears a monocle, neither of which is true. The mind simply has a way of editing memories to fill gaps.\n\nTellingly, the 1911 and 1921 censuses both counted a mere eight Jewish residents across the entire island - barely sufficient to maintain a huge synagogue. While media reports often cite 18 Jewish graves in the Borella Kanatte cemetery and scattered elsewhere, the actual count may be lower. Furthermore, the articles claim that the tombstones feature inscriptions in both Hebrew and Yiddish (a Germanic language spoken by European Ashkenazi Jews), but verifiable searches show only Hebrew and English. There is one Indian Jewish soldier buried in the Commonwealth section. Even though most of the Jewish graves are of Ashkenazi origin, there is no evidence of Yiddish grave inscriptions in Sri Lanka."
  },
