    "chunk_size_bytes": 701,
    "chunk_text": "Tellingly, the 1911 and 1921 censuses both counted a mere eight Jewish residents across the entire island - barely sufficient to maintain a huge synagogue. While media reports often cite 18 Jewish graves in the Borella Kanatte cemetery and scattered elsewhere, the actual count may be lower. Furthermore, the articles claim that the tombstones feature inscriptions in both Hebrew and Yiddish (a Germanic language spoken by European Ashkenazi Jews), but verifiable searches show only Hebrew and English. There is one Indian Jewish soldier buried in the Commonwealth section. Even though most of the Jewish graves are of Ashkenazi origin, there is no evidence of Yiddish grave inscriptions in Sri Lanka."
