    "md5": "be8aa3d4c40b8b03365ced5f128d1423",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Is Sri Lanka clean? Plastics and plastic waste growth says otherwise\n\nA serious effort is needed from policy makers about the use of plastics and to explore and promote, with incentives, the use of alternative environmentally friendly local materials. Plastic is convenient but it enriches manufacturers in other countries while causing the after-effect damage in Sri Lanka, to our health and the environment\n\nPlastics and plastic waste pose major health risks because they break down into tiny particles (microplastics and nano plastics) and release thousands of toxic chemicals that enter the human body through air, food, water, and skin contact (https://www.genevaenvironmentnetwork.org/resources/updates/plastics-and-health/\n\nWhile exact global percentage of total human deaths caused directly by plastic does not exist because scientific tracking measures specific pathways rather than a unified global mortality rate, researchers estimate toll figures through specific environmental and chemical links:\n\nMismanaged Waste and Burning: Charities like Tearfund estimate that between 400,000 and 1 million people die annually in developing countries from diseases, respiratory issues, and floods linked to uncollected or burned rubbish.\n\nChemical Exposure (e.g., Phthalates and Bisphenols): Studies published in The Lancet and related journals tie specific plastic additives\u2014such as DEHP\u2014to hundreds of thousands of cardiovascular and heart-related deaths globally each year (for instance, over 356,000 heart disease deaths linked to DEHP in specific analyses).\n\nBroader Pollution Context: Overall global pollution accounts for roughly one in four premature deaths worldwide (about 9 million per year), though isolating the precise percentage uniquely attributable to plastics versus other industrial pollutants remains a major gap in global health data."
  },
