    "md5": "5baf948316b589a0d23c7dee0dcd03f7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "Broader Pollution Context: Overall global pollution accounts for roughly one in four premature deaths worldwide (about 9 million per year), though isolating the precise percentage uniquely attributable to plastics versus other industrial pollutants remains a major gap in global health data.\n\nJudging by the above common experience witnessed across the country, it does not appear that public health advisories warning against the common domestic habit of burning polythene bags, lunch sheets, and yoghurt cups to light firewood stoves etc., issued by the Health Ministry\u2019s Environmental and Occupational Health Directorate has had much effect. Although these guidelines highlight severe risks of lung cancer, stroke, low birth weight, and respiratory illnesses for women and children exposed to indoor plastic smoke, it is uncertain whether enough has been done to educate and increase public awareness of these dangers.\n\nEnvironment risks are longer term risks and not tangible risks other than being eyesores to some. Few seem to understand the damage plastic waste does to the environment either due to lack of knowledge about it or due to the very common general apathy and indifference towards it.\n\nHealth risks on the other hand are short-, medium- and long-term risks, and they are tangible.\u00a0 Even fewer people seem to know or understand the damage plastic can do to the health of human beings and other living beings.\u00a0 It is uncertain as to how well the health risk message is conveyed to the public.\u00a0 Health risks also result in a cumulative financial challenge as well, as the funds needed to attend to those falling sick from plastic related diseases increases substantially when more and more plastic enters the day-to-day life of the public.\n\nThe following are noted as to how exposure to damage from plastic happens:\n\nIngestion: Eating or drinking from plastic containers, bottled water, or food contaminated with microplastics."
  },
