    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Health risks on the other hand are short-, medium- and long-term risks, and they are tangible.\u00a0 Even fewer people seem to know or understand the damage plastic can do to the health of human beings and other living beings.\u00a0 It is uncertain as to how well the health risk message is conveyed to the public.\u00a0 Health risks also result in a cumulative financial challenge as well, as the funds needed to attend to those falling sick from plastic related diseases increases substantially when more and more plastic enters the day-to-day life of the public.\n\nThe following are noted as to how exposure to damage from plastic happens:\n\nIngestion: Eating or drinking from plastic containers, bottled water, or food contaminated with microplastics.\n\nSkin Contact: Absorbing chemical additives directly from personal care items or synthetic clothing\n\nThe most recent, verified scientific statistics and medical data connect plastic pollution directly to clinical health issues:\n\nA landmark study presented at the European Respiratory Society (ERS) Congress provided the most direct statistical link between inhaled plastics and cancer:\n\n66% of lung cancer patients were found to have detectable microplastics embedded in their lung fluids, compared to 46% of individuals without cancer.\n\nPatients diagnosed with lung cancer were twice as likely to carry a heavy load/concentration of microplastics in their respiratory tract compared to healthy controls.\n\nThe primary polymers found trapped in human lung tissue are polypropylene and polyethylene\u2014the exact materials used to make plastic bags, bottles, and synthetic carpets.\n\nThe American Lung Association and global environmental health registries highlight how heavily our respiratory systems are bombarded by airborne plastic:\n\n22,000,000 microplastics: The estimated number of invisible plastic particles a human inhales annually from the air, driven heavily by synthetic clothing fibres, indoor carpets, and degraded environmental trash."
  },
