    "md5": "df4f05e7202f88db7e39be1acf23d5fb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "22,000,000 microplastics: The estimated number of invisible plastic particles a human inhales annually from the air, driven heavily by synthetic clothing fibres, indoor carpets, and degraded environmental trash.\n\nInhaled plastic particles trigger oxidative stress and chronic inflammation in alveolar macrophages (the lung's defence cells), which medical researchers link directly to the development of asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and tissue scarring (pulmonary fibrosis).\n\nThe comprehensive Minderoo-Monaco Commission on Plastics and Human Health tracked the health hazards of plastics across their entire lifecycle\u2014from extraction to disposal:\n\nFamilies and children living in \"fenceline communities\" (adjacent to plastic production plants or open-air plastic waste incineration sites) show significantly elevated rates of childhood leukaemia, lymphomas, and respiratory disorders.\n\nWorkers inside plastic manufacturing and recycling facilities suffer highly elevated mortality rates from lung cancer, mesothelioma, and cardiovascular disease due to the inhalation of chemical additives and micro-polymers.\n\nPlastics are not just inert pieces of trash; they are chemical cocktails. Over 10,000 chemical additives are used to make plastics flexible or flame-retardant.\n\nChemicals like BPA and Phthalates easily leach from plastic containers into food and water.\n\nThese chemicals mimic human hormones. The World Health Organisation (WHO) and endocrine researchers link this internal chemical exposure to early-onset puberty, reproductive disorders, immune suppression, and an increased long-term risk of breast and colon cancers.\n\nThe following are noted as to how exposure to damage from plastic happens:\n\nIngestion: Eating or drinking from plastic containers, bottled water, or food contaminated with microplastics.\n\nInhalation: Breathing in synthetic dust, tire wear particles, or smoke from open-air waste burning."
