    "md5": "84b49bfe6d3c7cc30af9f0b82ff5ea42",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "Ingestion: Eating or drinking from plastic containers, bottled water, or food contaminated with microplastics.\n\nInhalation: Breathing in synthetic dust, tire wear particles, or smoke from open-air waste burning.\n\nSri Lanka imports about 500,000 metric tonnes of virgin plastic each year to manufacture local plastic products, driven by roughly 400 active processing and manufacturing companies. (https://www.mdpi.com/2313-4321/8/6/94). Other key statistics tracking the accumulation, consumption, and ecological proliferation of plastic across the country include:\n\nConsumption and Growth Rates: Plastic consumption in Sri Lanka increases by approximately 16% annually. [https://www.researchgate.net/publication/379607620_Present_Status_of_Microplastic_Pollution_Research_Data_in_Sri_Lanka_and_Microplastic_Risk_Mitigation_Solutions_Lessons_from_a_Global_Policy_Context]\n\nSingle-Use Plastics (SUPs): Single-use plastics make up roughly 70% of the country's total plastic stream. [https://www.globalplasticaction.org/resources/publications/sri-lanka%E2%80%99s-struggle-with-single-use-plastics:-why-bans-alone-are-not-enough/8f96263d14fe21188f84ad1764c110e5b76e7384]\n\nProduct-Specific Proliferation: Consumers utilise about 30 million yoghurt cups and 1,250 tons of PET bottles every month. [file:///C:/Users/Raj%20G/Downloads/UNEP-CHW-Norad-PrepMeeting-SriLanka-PRNT-2-20210315.English%20(3).pdf]\n\nCoastal and Marine Influx: The Marine Environment Protection Authority (MEPA) reports an annual influx of 1,500 metric tonnes of plastic directly into Sri Lanka's coastal waters. [https://www.defence.lk/Article/view_article/27501]\n\nForecasted Surplus: Research models project a 54% increase in plastic surplus in Sri Lanka, signaling a steep acceleration in future plastic accumulation and disposal. [https://repository.ou.ac.lk/bitstream/handle/94ousl/3333/Proceeding-Book-ICPES%20%281%29-129.pdf?sequence=1andisAllowed=y]"
  },
