    "language": "en",
    "md5": "890103f80c22be9134e77d996d5c9f92",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Forecasted Surplus: Research models project a 54% increase in plastic surplus in Sri Lanka, signaling a steep acceleration in future plastic accumulation and disposal. [https://repository.ou.ac.lk/bitstream/handle/94ousl/3333/Proceeding-Book-ICPES%20%281%29-129.pdf?sequence=1andisAllowed=y]\n\nSri Lankan researchers and health authorities have conducted several studies on plastic waste and microplastics. Most local research focuses on how plastics contaminate the food chain, drinking water, and air\u2014particularly following the 2021 MV X-Press Pearl maritime disaster.\u00a0 While clinical studies measuring microplastics directly inside Sri Lankan human tissue are still limited, local data clearly maps out major health exposure pathways across the island.\n\nSeafood and Food Chain Contamination: Studies on fish sold in local markets found microplastics in nearly all examined fresh fish and almost half of dried fish\u2014especially small species like anchovies and sardines that are eaten whole. High levels of plastic fibres were also documented in shrimp from the Negombo Lagoon and aquaculture farms in Puttalam (https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/16/3/447)\n\nToxic Smoke from Open Burning: A 2025 field study in rural Pallewela found that 97% of surveyed households openly burn their plastic waste. Local air quality tests near burn sites detected dangerous spikes in PM2.5, dioxins, furans, and carbon monoxide, directly linking plastic burning to higher local hospitalisation rates for asthma, COPD, and heart disease. (https://jphe.amegroups.org/article/view/11450/html)\n\nRiver and Drinking Water Pollution: Research along the entire 142 km length of the Deduru Oya river confirmed microplastic contamination from headwaters to the coast, raising concerns about drinking water treatment and contamination in commercial table salt. (https://iesl.lk/SLEN/62/Microplastics_invading.php)"
  },
