    "md5": "c807b12600e5f06dc268004686e820b2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "River and Drinking Water Pollution: Research along the entire 142 km length of the Deduru Oya river confirmed microplastic contamination from headwaters to the coast, raising concerns about drinking water treatment and contamination in commercial table salt. (https://iesl.lk/SLEN/62/Microplastics_invading.php)\n\nPublic Awareness Gaps: A cross-sectional study in the Colombo District revealed that while 96.7% of respondents knew plastics harm human health, fewer than half understood that microplastics can cross the human placenta or enter the food chain. (https://journals.sjp.ac.lk/index.php/ijms/article/view/8704/6088)\n\nBased on all of the above, it is clear that despite some efforts, the health and environment risks arising from the proliferation of plastic and widespread plastic waste generation and mismanagement continues without a vision and goals and objectives to address this issue along with strategies that are needed to address them. While much lip service is made about them, the actual efforts and outcomes have been far from satisfactory. There are insufficient programs in place to educate the public about the health hazards of plastics and the plastic waste and there is insufficient monitoring of whatever programs that may be in place. Addressing this serious issue needs serious strategies and the participation of the public in taking ownership of the cause and effects arising from the proliferation of plastic and the destructive methods used to get rid of plastic waste. Strategies must also include educational programs in schools, and amongst civil society entities, as well as in religious institutions, to apprise the public about the dangers arising to the health of humans and other living beings and to the environment, which in the end impacts on the health and well-being of all."
  },
