    "md5": "493c94adbddffaf8e2fa5c44f7bf4cc3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Budget blues, worker agitation and Minister Lal Kantha\n\nIt\u2019s just over a week since \u2018good\u2019 president Anura Kumara Dissanayake (AKD) presented his last budget on\u00a0 November 12 2026. Unfortunately,\u00a0 the president since he assumed power has unfailingly broken most of the promises he made prior to the presidential and general elections.\n\nCountry-wide,\u00a0 he has managed to make a mess of most of what he promised the masses. To the country in general, he promised to dish the IMF deal signed by his predecessor Ranil Wickremesinghe. That promise was made so long ago, the president now seems to have forgotten about it.\n\nDuring his 2027 budget presentation,\u00a0 he announced a high-level Cabinet-appointed committee to evaluate whether to seek a follow-up programme after the current 17th Extended Fund Facility (EFF) ends in March. Before the presidential and general elections the president and his party promised to scrap the IMF deal and bring down the cost of living.\n\nLal Kantha,\u00a0 the president\u2019s Minister of Agriculture and senior member of the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP),\u00a0 on Nov. 19 2026-\u00a0 acknowledged that the salary increase granted to public sector employees had not kept pace with the rising cost of living.\n\nHowever, without tackling the problem, he simply announced the issue would have to be considered in the NEXT budget. No further increase to bring public sector salaries in line with the cost of living has been provided for, in the 2027 Budget he announced\u2026\n\nThe private sector in our country is even worse off. The Ceylon United Business Alliance (CUBA) warned that the 2027 Budget lacks targeted tax, VAT, or Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL) relief. It argues this will accelerate closures in sectors like the cottage apparel industry. In fact,\u00a0 today many private sector companies do not pay wages in a timely manner."
  },
