    "md5": "2e3b5678c960ff439948a03fd789a66a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "The private sector in our country is even worse off. The Ceylon United Business Alliance (CUBA) warned that the 2027 Budget lacks targeted tax, VAT, or Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL) relief. It argues this will accelerate closures in sectors like the cottage apparel industry. In fact,\u00a0 today many private sector companies do not pay wages in a timely manner.\n\nA family of four (father, mother and two children) would therefore need over Rs. 30,000/- for just a single meal per day! This figure does not take into consideration cost of travel, clothes, medical costs etc. Yet in Mr. Lalkantha\u2019s mind our people must wait until next year\u2019s budget to expect an increase in wages.\n\nNo wonder the trade union movements are calling\u00a0 out for a strike. Doctors have warned of trade union action if their demands for a salary increase is not met. Seven major principals\u2019 associations and roughly 20 teacher trade unions plan a protest on November 23, 2026, demanding a proposed Rs. 25,000 interim allowance that was left out of the 2027 Budget.\n\nThe Government Executive Officers (20 Services) have called a one-day island-wide token strike for November 20, 2026. Following a one-day strike by non-academic university workers on November 18, 2026, Inter-University Trade Union Federation (IUTUF) has warned of a broader campaign to paralyse the university system itself.\n\nYet these are the more advantaged sections of our population. The weaker sections like those working in tea, rubber and coconut estates of the country are much worse off. An ordinary tea plucker on an estate receives a daily wage of Rs. 1,700/-. But does not\u00a0 receive work daily.\n\nYet the export of tea earned US$ 1,507.2 million. Rubber and rubber-based products earned US$ 945.7 million and coconut and coconut-based products earned US$ 1,233.0 million. These workers continue to live in single-roomed attached houses dating back to the British period."
