    "md5": "8b09b7268c5d5e910c7b50351732926f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1430,
    "chunk_text": "The Rising Tide of Aging uncovers the Silver Economy in Sri Lanka\n\nOriginally introduced in 1973 as Silver Seats for elderly passengers on Tokyo and Osaka lines, they were renamed and re-branded in the 1990s as priority seats\n\nSri Lanka is currently experiencing one of the most rapid demographic transitions in South Asia. In the early 2020s, approximately 12.3% of the population was aged 60 years or older, representing the highest proportion of older adults in the region, according to data compiled by the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs. Projections by the World Bank indicate that this share is expected to rise to nearly 25% within the next 15 years.\n\nThis shift signals a transformation from a predominantly youthful demographic structure to one increasingly characterised by older age groups, a phenomenon often described as a \u201csilver tsunami.\u201d While this demographic change presents significant socio-economic challenges, it simultaneously opens many avenues for innovation, entrepreneurship, and market diversification etc.\n\nThe identification of demographic changes will allow probable transitions that may rigour business development opportunities for new products and services in the market.\u00a0 Identifying and tailoring such products and services to fulfill the needs of older adults will enable strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s economic landscape and fostering an inclusive economic growth."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
