    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1f76dad7b10bba0e42be98481637863e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1699,
    "chunk_text": "The inclusive policies would improve the citizens of all walks of life that entrust and broaden the strategic approaches in managing human capital for better economic creation,\u00a0 thus ensuring that older adults\u00a0 too, are actively contributing to the economy without becoming an economic burden. In addition, it provides flexible retirement systems and provides a focus business market for eldercare products and infrastructure to expand the business platforms leveraging the silver economy.\n\nIt is evident that the silver transition is driven by the demographic shifts that are taking place towards ageing societies and in many western countries the older adult population engages actively in the workforce surpassing 50-60 years. Usually the retirement policies specify a \u201cminimum age\u201d rather than fixing a retirement age with an upper ceiling.\u00a0 Very surprisingly in Sri Lanka, the Minimum Retirement Age policy clauses are misunderstood by the majority of employers; the \u201cminimum retirement age\u201d is being treated as the \u201cmandatory retirement age\u201d misinterpreting the Employment Act of Minimum Age of Retirement and thereby limiting the opportunities for older adults contributing to the economy at a higher age limit of 60 years.\n\nSri Lanka also could draw lessons from Singapore, Japan and Belgium by re-introducing targeted tax incentives to active older employees aligning same to corporate tax deductions, corporate tax credit on qualifying older worker payroll cost, statutory contribution concessions for employers who recruit and retain older adult workers beyond a standard retirement age, designing a tax incentive model for corporate taxes creating an economic opportunity."
  },
