    "md5": "efeb74ebe1e70ed3fccaaabc86710ca2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka also could draw lessons from Singapore, Japan and Belgium by re-introducing targeted tax incentives to active older employees aligning same to corporate tax deductions, corporate tax credit on qualifying older worker payroll cost, statutory contribution concessions for employers who recruit and retain older adult workers beyond a standard retirement age, designing a tax incentive model for corporate taxes creating an economic opportunity.\n\nThe term \u201csilver economy\u201d was born to the world in the year 1970 when Japanese National Railways suggested \u201csilver seats\u201d to elderly citizens\n\nAccording to some available statistics,\u00a0 the status-quo of the older adults is unclear.\u00a0 It is required to enable the older adults with employment opportunities and they have the great potential to contribute to Sri Lanka\u2019s economy.\u00a0 The records revealed that 49% of 55-64 years older adults are economically inactive, and the labour force involvement rate in males and females recorded 36% and 11% respectively may be due to limited employment options.\n\nFor example, companies that retain or hire employees over the age of 60 beyond the statutory retirement age could qualify for a percentage reduction in their total corporate income tax liability, proportionate to the remuneration made to the older workers in their workforce when calculating taxable profits.\n\nSri Lanka stands at a pivotal juncture where the challenges of an aging population can be transformed into opportunities for sustainable growth. By strategically investing in social support systems such as healthcare, workforce participation, and digital inclusion,\u00a0 the nation can foster a robust silver economy. Such an economy would not only enhance the quality of life for older citizens,\u00a0 but also strengthen overall economic resilience by recognising older adults as vital contributors to Sri Lanka\u2019s human capital."
