    "md5": "694644a60166443bd3c248cf05fcc214",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "Justice for All urges President to withdraw anti-terror, organised crime Bills\n\nSays both Bills contain \u2018draconian\u2019 clauses that could sustain abuses seen under PTA\n\nWelcomes President\u2019s offer to consult rights community, seeks early meeting\n\nJustice for All has urged President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to withdraw the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill (PSTB) and the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POC), both now before Parliament. It argues that the two Bills threaten human rights and the rule of law.\n\nIn a letter dated 19 November and signed by 29 people, the organisation welcomed the President\u2019s recent announcement that he would consult the human rights community and other stakeholders on the two Bills. It said it was writing in that spirit.\n\n\u201cIt is in this spirit that we urge you to withdraw the two Bills as they both contain clauses that are detrimental to human rights and the rule of law in Sri Lanka,\u201d the letter said.\n\nJustice for All said it had demanded the repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA) for years. \u201cWe note that both Bills contain draconian clauses that can sustain the abuses witnessed under the PTA and have no place in a democracy,\u201d it said.\n\nThe organisation welcomed the President\u2019s offer to consult on law reform, said it looked forward to an open and transparent dialogue, and requested an early meeting with him to raise its concerns.\n\nThe letter was written following a decision at a meeting held on 16 November. Justice for All had announced the meeting to the President in a letter dated 9 November, inviting him to the discussion at ICES in Colombo 8, or to nominate a representative if he could not attend. It said it had also invited other members of the Government and others involved in its 2022-2023 campaign."
  },
