    "md5": "e2549dfeb004c71fefe2e3c238d5fbd3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Organic Farming Is the Future,  but It Requires Patience and Sustainability- Priyambada Jayakumar\n\nPriyambada Jayakumar, a historian, columnist, and the bestselling author of M.S. Swaminathan: The Man Who Fed India, speaks to Daily Mirror on the rights of farmers, the future of farming and food security.\n\nShe was raised in India, specifically in Kolkata. Born to a Bengali mother and a Tamil father, she completed her schooling at La Martiniere for Girls, Kolkata. The capital city of Delhi soon beckoned, where she received her BA (Hons) degree in History from St. Stephen\u2019s College, University of Delhi. A Tripos in Social and Political Sciences followed thereafter from the University of Cambridge. Subsequently, she attended Harvard University for a further period of study.\n\nThe \u2018Man who fed India\u2019 a biography of Prof M.S. Swaminathan, her uncle and the \u2018Father of the Green Revolution in India\u2019, the 2024 Bharat Ratna awardee and India\u2019s founding father, tallest agricultural scientist, technocrat and scientific diplomat, is published by HarperCollins in his centennial year (2025-2026). The book has been widely covered by the mainstream national and international media and has consistently featured on national bestselling lists in the non-fiction category.\n\nQ Sri Lanka had a terrible experience in 2022 in attempting to introduce organic farming. What do you actually mean by eco-friendly agriculture?\n\nNow, farming is, whether you live in America, India, or Sri Lanka, the ability to feed yourself means one thing and only one thing. It means you\u2019re free.\n\nThe minute you become unable to feed yourself and have to import fertiliser, food or other agricultural inputs, your food security is threatened. Your national sovereignty and national security get threatened.\n\nNow, the biggest problem facing humanity today, in the wake of the conclusion of COP 31, is the fact that our world has already lost time to turn back the clock when it comes to climate change."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
