    "chunk_id": "2026-11-21-dailymirrorlk-6e55ad5d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a93d411ed208ff6c7de6743dbd3e36cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1847,
    "chunk_text": "The minute you become unable to feed yourself and have to import fertiliser, food or other agricultural inputs, your food security is threatened. Your national sovereignty and national security get threatened.\n\nNow, the biggest problem facing humanity today, in the wake of the conclusion of COP 31, is the fact that our world has already lost time to turn back the clock when it comes to climate change.\n\nHere I talk about M.S. Swaminathan, who gave India a green revolution in the 1960s, but was aware that the green revolution itself wasn\u2019t sustainable because it was done with the help of importing fertilisers. It was a water and a fertiliser guzzler.\n\nThe Green Revolution worked, and the yields per hectare tripled and quadrupled. India produced more grain in four years than it had done in 4,000 years of human history, simply because more fertilisers and water were added.\n\nOver time, over fertilisation, over watering and depletion of the water table mean rising salinity and chemicals in the food system. You have carcinogens that get dumped into the ground. We needed the Green Revolution in the 1960s. Otherwise, Mrs. Gandhi was at the U.S.\u2019s mercy for food security, because we were importing food under the public law, PL 480 food scheme. We were not able to grow our own crops. Like Sri Lanka, India was also a colony.\n\nAnd under the British crown, agriculture wasn\u2019t a huge priority. We built civil services, railways and the rest of it, but agriculture wasn\u2019t given national importance. India, when it was free in 1947, was born in hunger. We were still a hungry nation. From 1947 to 1967, at the time when India was described as a people who sat with a begging bowl, it was Professor Swaminathan\u2019s conviction that we had to go from the begging bowl to the breadbasket. The only way to do that was the Green Revolution."
  },
