    "md5": "0f0d9612af0c319872425214b7228e7a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "And under the British crown, agriculture wasn\u2019t a huge priority. We built civil services, railways and the rest of it, but agriculture wasn\u2019t given national importance. India, when it was free in 1947, was born in hunger. We were still a hungry nation. From 1947 to 1967, at the time when India was described as a people who sat with a begging bowl, it was Professor Swaminathan\u2019s conviction that we had to go from the begging bowl to the breadbasket. The only way to do that was the Green Revolution.\n\nSo M.S. Swaminathan in 1968 advocated for something called the evergreen revolution, which was production in perpetuity without additional ecological harm. That is when your question arises: how important is organic farming or what is the meaning of ecological farming?\n\nAncient agricultural wisdom and modern science must work together to promote sustainable farming\n\nQ The ecological aspect is compromised when you use more and more chemicals. Isn\u2019t it?\n\nYes, the ecological aspect is compromised. In a world where you have too many mouths to feed, don\u2019t forget India, Sri Lanka and even China are countries with small landowners.\n\nWe don\u2019t have massive landholdings like in America. To get so much more from a small plot of land means chemicals are added to the ground to top up the yield.\n\nBut that comes at an ecological cost. With climate change, that is a huge problem. Climate change makes you realise just how much we\u2019ve exploited nature without giving anything back.\n\nNature is cyclical. If you take anything out of it, you should actually put it back in. It is the cyclic form of ecology, the blend of ecological awareness and economic sustainability, which underlines organic farming or an organic ecological system.\n\nNow, the problem with that is that anything organic tends to be much slower. That is when the profits get eaten away. When the profits get eaten away, you don\u2019t have the kind of harvest you want."
