    "md5": "bfc1a4e1c43701ee000230a1c8f95009",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Nature is cyclical. If you take anything out of it, you should actually put it back in. It is the cyclic form of ecology, the blend of ecological awareness and economic sustainability, which underlines organic farming or an organic ecological system.\n\nNow, the problem with that is that anything organic tends to be much slower. That is when the profits get eaten away. When the profits get eaten away, you don\u2019t have the kind of harvest you want.\n\nWhen I talk about organic [farming}, I just don\u2019t mean non-fertilisers, non-chemical. I also mean ancient wisdom \u2013 how our ancestors grew food. They did not have access to chemical fertilisers.\n\nThe population, of course, is a different matter altogether. The population intensity is much higher. The population expectation and the population explosion are that much higher.\n\nBut it doesn\u2019t take away from the fact that ancient wisdom, agroecology or agro-biodiversity. In the past, we were able to grow food without exploiting or poisoning nature. I think it is time to go back, take a step back and look at our ancient systems of wisdom.\n\nThose ancient systems offer many answers. We saw this in Indonesia in the 1980s, where Swaminathan worked with the government to phase out pesticides and adopt natural methods of pest control. It worked. You need patience for any ancient system. You need patience for an organic system. The reason why organic systems don\u2019t work is also because people don\u2019t have patience. In a highly commercialised economy where food is a commodity traded all across, patience is a slipping virtue. The thrust on organic farming is a genuine need. But it is not going to take off so easily.\n\nIt is not going to be adapted so quickly by countries because profit margins are important. But the climate crisis forces us to take a step back."
  },
