    "md5": "8789f34e16ac727d2f995a9e9db42705",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "Those ancient systems offer many answers. We saw this in Indonesia in the 1980s, where Swaminathan worked with the government to phase out pesticides and adopt natural methods of pest control. It worked. You need patience for any ancient system. You need patience for an organic system. The reason why organic systems don\u2019t work is also because people don\u2019t have patience. In a highly commercialised economy where food is a commodity traded all across, patience is a slipping virtue. The thrust on organic farming is a genuine need. But it is not going to take off so easily.\n\nIt is not going to be adapted so quickly by countries because profit margins are important. But the climate crisis forces us to take a step back.\n\nYou have to be ecologically aware and economically sustainable as well. The two sometimes marry. That marriage is a long one.\n\nIt\u2019s a marriage which doesn\u2019t give you instant children. It gives you children in the next 20 years.\n\nQ What do you think of scientific agriculture? Can processed organic manure, using modern agricultural technology, supply the same level of nutrients as chemical fertilisers such as urea? Is this already being practised in India or elsewhere?\n\nYes, it does. I mean that is called village science- village wisdom. It\u2019s something which has happened in India for years.\n\nBut again, farming has become a commercial activity. And when you have a commercial activity takes over, then profit margins come in. The farmer is then not willing to wait.\n\nThat is where I think you need to look at the long-term benefits. Government support is also very important. In agriculture, especially in India, the farmer looks towards the government for a lot of support and a lot of respite.\n\nScientists have good ideas. Traditional ancient systems of wisdom tell us that these are good things to do. But until governments come and give you that kind of support, poor farmers are going to do what they can to provide food on the table for their children and their families."
  },
