    "chunk_text": "Scientists have good ideas. Traditional ancient systems of wisdom tell us that these are good things to do. But until governments come and give you that kind of support, poor farmers are going to do what they can to provide food on the table for their children and their families.\n\nAt my book launch recently, we were talking about how nobody wants to really farm. Farming in general, as an occupation, is finding absolutely no takers. A farmer in India doesn\u2019t want his children to be farmers, citing no economic viability.\n\nQ At the same time, as farming becomes increasingly commercialised, the number of people engaged in agriculture is declining. However, a new category of agro-entrepreneurs is emerging, moving towards large-scale commercial farming. What are your views?\n\nBut they are few and far between. There is an imbalance. You have agro-entrepreneurs and agro-people, but their number is much smaller than the number of people who actually actively give up farming.\n\nQ Actually, how serious is it for a country like India? Because India has to feed its own population?\n\nQ Besides, a lot of other countries in the region also depend on India for food security. Now, for example, Sri Lanka depends on India for big onions and even sugar?\n\nFood security anywhere is a problem. Economic viability of farming today is a big question mark. It\u2019s not just a problem for India but a problem for Sri Lanka.\n\nHow do you make farming attractive to the population so that the next generation of farmers is secure? If farming is not made economically viable, who\u2019s going to grow the food? Who\u2019s going to feed the nation? In this context, issues such as land ownership and access to credit become much more significant.\n\nIn India, the greater trouble is sharecropping by tenant farmers. These are huge problems.\n\nThat\u2019s where farming debt in India is. The great problem we have is farmer suicides. Especially in the western region, in the Baramati region in Maharashtra, at least 8 farmers commit suicide."
