    "md5": "9f4fd87aef690766684c711dbdcf06bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "In India, the greater trouble is sharecropping by tenant farmers. These are huge problems.\n\nThat\u2019s where farming debt in India is. The great problem we have is farmer suicides. Especially in the western region, in the Baramati region in Maharashtra, at least 8 farmers commit suicide.\n\nAt the same time, they are in abject poverty they commit suicide. We look at farmers through the eyes of pity. We look at farmers as people who depend on the state for handouts. We do not celebrate the farmer. We do not respect his wisdom or his work. That itself is a problem.\n\nThe minute you start feeling that a farmer is an object of pity, you lose sight of the fact that your essential food security depends on this humble farmer, the breadwinners of the world, to be treated so badly. That is the premise of my book. The National Commission of Farmers, which Professor Swaminathan chaired from 2004 to 2006, only looked at one thing: how to make farming economically viable and farmers socially acceptable.\n\nMaking agriculture economically viable is essential to attracting the next generation of farmers\n\nQ What is the solution you advocate at this juncture? Is that scientific agriculture? Some countries like Israel and the Netherlands have been successful in scientific agriculture.\n\nWhen you talk about Israel and the Netherlands, I think the greater question to answer is size. India is much larger than any of these countries. The larger your country, the more problems you have.\n\nGeographically, we are much more disparate. Every part of India has a different geography, a different topography, a different climate and different agricultural needs. Smaller countries are able to do things much faster because size is in their favour.\n\nUnless we make farming remunerative, we cannot attract and retain people in agriculture. The C2+50% formula, recommended by the National Commission on Farmers, provides for a minimum support price at 50% above the comprehensive cost of production."
