    "md5": "3504dd9849238e313e6f11b1441d5e19",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1639,
    "chunk_text": "Unless we make farming remunerative, we cannot attract and retain people in agriculture. The C2+50% formula, recommended by the National Commission on Farmers, provides for a minimum support price at 50% above the comprehensive cost of production.\n\nFarming has historically been one of the areas that has connected India and Sri Lanka. In India, too, farmers encounter wild animals and face the risk of being injured or otherwise harmed. Nature offers us its bounty, but that very bounty, which ensures our food security and puts food on our tables, reflects two things: nature\u2019s generosity and the farmer\u2019s contribution. We must be grateful for both.\n\nThe Tamil equivalent of Thanksgiving is Pongal, which is celebrated here. We have festivals that honour farmers, agricultural produce and nature\u2019s bounty. However, sadly, we do not have a corresponding legal framework to protect farmers\u2019 rights. I think that is where the problem lies.\n\nUnless nations and governments take it upon themselves to safeguard farmers\u2019 rights, these issues will remain unresolved. Look at the National Commission on Farmers, whose recommendations were drafted by Professor M.S. Swaminathan between 2004 and 2006, during the Manmohan Singh government. They were unable to translate those recommendations into law. Subsequently, Prime Minister Narendra Modi also failed to turn them into legislation.\n\nAgain, I return to the fact that our forefathers believed farmers and nature\u2019s bounty should be celebrated and protected. However, today\u2019s governments and administrations do not seem to share that belief. I think that is where the problem lies."
  },
