    "md5": "7fb5e983ccaa27ed4a6e369adab6b264",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1583,
    "chunk_text": "The FBI was hacked again, this time the breach targeted its crime database\n\nCNN- The FBI is investigating an apparent breach of its nationwide criminal information database that serves as a lifeline between federal, state and local law enforcement, two people briefed on the investigation said.\n\nThe intrusion of the National Crime Information Center by apparent hackers is the second such breach in recent months.\n\nThe NCIC database is used by law enforcement nationwide to share information on warrants and background checks.\n\nThe discovery of the latest intrusion in recent days comes as the Trump administration has sought to assuage public concern about the nation\u2019s cybersecurity amid a series of high profile outages affecting banks, the National Security Agency and the FAA\u2019s air traffic control system.\n\nThe FBI didn\u2019t immediately respond to a request for comment.\n\nOn Thursday, after bureau officials learned of the latest cybersecurity incident, FBI Director Kash Patel appeared on Fox News to tout the FBI\u2019s cybersecurity work.\n\nPatel used the appearance to discuss an operation completed months ago aimed at urging companies to update their systems and prevent hackers from getting to their sensitive data.\n\nPatel referred to Operation Winter Shield as if it was still in the works, and touted the current administration for making updates to key systems to protect the nation\u2019s critical infrastructure.\n\n\u201cOther administrations could have made these updates, but they didn\u2019t,\u201d he said. \u201cWe are staying ahead of the curve and making up for lost time.\u201d"
