    "chunk_id": "2026-11-21-dailymirrorlk-7bd8306e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3110ba0bd32c1a371197bfc7e22d1a44",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1581,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka hits 2mn tourists for 3rd conservative year\n\nArrivals 1.4% behind 2025 as March-April slump erases early gains\n\nBeating last year\u2019s record needs about 8,740 arrivals a day to year-end\n\nSri Lanka crossed two million tourist arrivals for the third consecutive year on November 20, three days later than in 2025, with a sharp slump in March and April, still leaving the cumulative arrivals behind last year\u2019s pace, despite the strong growth from India, the official data showed.\n\nThe island\u2019s two millionth visitor of the year was part of a Russian family of three, Sergei, Nataliya and their daughter Anna, according to the Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism Ministry.\n\nThe milestone was reached on November 17 last year and only on December 26 in 2024, when Sri Lanka first returned to the two million mark, after a six-year gap following 2018.\n\nAddressing Parliament, Tourism Deputy Minister Prof. Ruwan Ranasinghe described the achievement as remarkable, amid the Middle East conflict, disruptions to international flight routes, rising aviation fuel prices and global economic challenges, while also recalling the setback the industry faced after Cyclone Ditwah struck on November 28, 2025.\n\nHe stressed that crossing two million was \u201cnot merely a numerical achievement\u201d but a reflection of the industry\u2019s resilience and international appeal.\n\nHowever, the cumulative arrivals stood at 1,986,666 as of November 18, 1.4 percent or 28,746 visitors, below the same period last year, as per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) data."
  },
  {
