    "md5": "7486319524782729db67523f9b333a3a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1879,
    "chunk_text": "However, the cumulative arrivals stood at 1,986,666 as of November 18, 1.4 percent or 28,746 visitors, below the same period last year, as per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) data.\n\nThe arrivals of 1,862,304 in the first 10 months were also 1.2 percent below 1,885,045 recorded in the same period of 2018, which shows how close the island remains to where it stood eight years ago. Even last year\u2019s record total of 2,362,521 exceeded the 2018 figure of 2,333,796 by just 1.2 percent.\n\nMeanwhile, the composition of the island\u2019s visitors has shifted noticeably. The Indian arrivals climbed 18 percent to 499,625 in the first 10 months when compared to the same period last year, adding 76,220 visitors and lifting India\u2019s share of total arrivals to 26.8 percent, from 22.4 percent.\n\nIndia also accounted for three in every 10 arrivals in the first 18 days of November.\n\nChina and Australia grew as well, by 10 percent to 123,740 and by 12 percent to 95,498, respectively.\n\nRussia, home to the milestone family, moved the other way, with the arrivals down 32.8 percent to 89,741 in the 10 months, a drop of 43,899 visitors. The Russian arrivals averaged 676 a day in the first 18 days of November, compared with 832 a day across November 2025, which suggests the anticipated winter surge from the market is yet to show up in the numbers.\n\nGermany, France and Poland declined 9.8 percent, 10.1 percent and 25.6 percent, respectively, while the United Kingdom, the second largest source market, eased 1.0 percent to 173,129.\n\nNovember itself has offered mixed signals. The ministry highlighted a 4.9 percent rise in average daily arrivals since November 10, though the first 18 days of the month delivered 124,362 arrivals, virtually unchanged from 124,725 in the same period last year, as a 7.8 percent drop over the first nine days offset the later gains."
  },
