    "chunk_size_bytes": 1137,
    "chunk_text": "November itself has offered mixed signals. The ministry highlighted a 4.9 percent rise in average daily arrivals since November 10, though the first 18 days of the month delivered 124,362 arrivals, virtually unchanged from 124,725 in the same period last year, as a 7.8 percent drop over the first nine days offset the later gains.\n\nThat goal is a step down from the 2.5 million arrivals that SLTDA Chairman Suranjith Wevita told Mirror Business in August he expected by year-end, a figure that would now demand close to 11,940 arrivals a day. Even the lowest of the SLTDA\u2019s revised scenarios, at 2,377,370, would require about 9,090 a day.\n\nProf. Ranasinghe credited the year\u2019s progress to new international airlines, expanded direct flight connections, entry into new markets and stronger promotion. He added that air connectivity, international promotion and new market development would be further strengthened to meet the target.\n\nThe ministry is also revising how it estimates tourism revenue, in line with the international standards, to reflect the changes in the exchange rates and tourist spending patterns since 2018. (NF)"
