    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "CBSL springs rate surprise\n\nOvernight Policy Rate raised by 25 basis points to 9%, first increase since May\n\nGovernor sees inflation nearing 9% this month, with a return to 5% target projected for second half of 2027\n\nBond yields climb and Colombo stocks open lower after markets had expected a hold\n\nThe Central Bank sprang a surprise yesterday, raising its policy rate by 25 basis points to 9 percent, after the earlier increases in energy and food costs spread to other prices and Sri Lankans began to expect higher inflation over the coming year.\n\nThe move ended a six-month pause, following May\u2019s full percentage-point increase.\n\nIn September, the Monetary Policy Board had been prepared to hold at 8.75 percent, even though it expected inflation to remain in high single digits through the first quarter of 2027.\n\nYesterday\u2019s decision reflected a growing risk that the price pressures would persist, even after the initial energy shock eased.\n\n\u201cInflation is going to remain elevated in the near term, at higher than expected levels compared to the last review,\u201d Central Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe said at a briefing yesterday.\n\nHe said Colombo inflation could approach 9 percent by end-November, from 8.3 percent in October and stay around that level in the near term.\n\nThe Central Bank now expects inflation to return to its 5 percent target in the second half of 2027.\n\nThe rise in core inflation helps explain why the board moved, even though the global energy prices had eased somewhat. The earlier cost increases were still working their way through the economy and higher expectations for inflation over the next year raised the risk of further price increases.\n\nThe medium-term expectations, however, stayed around the 5 percent target. That gave the board room to act in quarter-point increments while May\u2019s larger increase continued to feed through to the borrowing costs."
  },
