    "time_ut": 1795256819.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-21-dailymirrorlk-642e2f51-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aa00a355f8337691063b51135cd14210",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "Dayasiri challenges mandatory remand powers in Namal\u2019s case\n\nOpposition MP Dayasiri Jayasekara has questioned the mandatory remand provisions under Section 149 of the Anti-Corruption Act, raising concerns over judicial independence and the possibility of using criminal investigations against political opponents.\n\nSpeaking in Parliament, Jayasekara, a lawyer by profession, questioned whether magistrates had sufficient discretion to examine the grounds for detention when a certificate issued by the Director-General of the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) was presented under the provision.\n\nReferring to proceedings involving Opposition MP Namal Rajapaksa, he said the Fort Magistrate had sought clarification in September on whether the Director-General had submitted such a certificate in the relevant proceedings.\n\nJayasekara said Section 149 of the Anti-Corruption Act, enacted in 2023, provides for the remand of suspects in certain bribery-related offences when a certificate issued by the CIABOC Director-General is presented, while allowing bail only in exceptional circumstances.\n\nHe questioned whether suspects had a meaningful opportunity to challenge the factual basis of such certificates, warning that prolonged remand could affect individuals and their families even if they were subsequently acquitted.\n\nCiting Article 13(5) of the Constitution, he said the presumption of innocence must be protected and stressed that investigations should be guided by evidence rather than political considerations.\n\n\u201cAn honest investigation follows the evidence, wherever it leads and whoever may be implicated,\u201d he told Parliament.\n\nThe MP called on the Justice Minister to disclose the number of certificates issued under Section 149 annually, the period suspects had spent in remand under the provision and the number of cases that had proceeded to trial."
