    "md5": "4cf80336a8249f36418d58fef6b297b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "The MP called on the Justice Minister to disclose the number of certificates issued under Section 149 annually, the period suspects had spent in remand under the provision and the number of cases that had proceeded to trial.\n\nJayasekara also raised concerns over the extension of the retirement ages of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges through the 22nd Amendment, arguing that the changes could have been applied to future appointments rather than judges already serving.\n\nHe further called for greater transparency in the appointment of judicial benches in politically sensitive cases, saying the publication of principles governing bench assignments would strengthen public confidence in the judiciary.\n\nReferring to the 1937 Mark Bracegirdle case, he recalled how the judiciary had challenged the colonial administration's deportation order and secured Bracegirdle's release.\n\nHe also referred to the decision by the J.R. Jayewardene administration in 1980 to strip former Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike of her civic rights, preventing her from contesting the 1982 presidential election.\n\nWarning the Government against using criminal law to restrict political competition, Jayasekara said such action would undermine the people's right to choose their representatives.\n\n\u201cIf you use the criminal law to put an opponent out of the electoral contest, you are doing to that opponent what J.R. did to Sirimavo,\u201d he said.\n\nHe urged the Government to face its political opponents at elections rather than use legal mechanisms to weaken them.\n\n\u201cHave the courage to face your opponents at the polls. Leave the choice to the people,\u201d he said.\n\nRecalling Bandaranaike's return to power as Prime Minister in 1994, Jayasekara concluded: \u201cDo not forget, Honourable Speaker. Sirimavo had the last laugh.\u201d"
