    "description": "Parliamentary Committee calls for proper recycling and disposal of electric vehicle batteries",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Parliamentary-Committee-calls-for-proper-recycling-and-disposal-of-electric-vehicle-batteries/108-355480",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795258108.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-21-dailymirrorlk-05b85454-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aefa9016e38cbbd26d24e8c225bd4c72",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1782,
    "chunk_text": "Parliamentary Committee calls for proper recycling and disposal of electric vehicle batteries\n\nColombo, November 21 (Daily Mirror)- The Committee on Ways and Means has stressed the importance of establishing a proper system to ensure that importers and manufacturers take responsibility for the environmentally safe disposal and recycling of electric vehicle (EV) batteries at the end of their lifespan.\n\nThe matter was raised during a recent meeting of the Committee held at Parliament under the chairmanship of MP Wijesiri Basnayake.\n\nDuring the discussion, the Committee reviewed the number and value of vehicles imported into the country following the relaxation of vehicle import restrictions, along with the tax framework applicable to vehicle imports. Attention was also drawn to the complexities involved in calculating import taxes, which depend on several factors, including engine capacity, Cost, Insurance and Freight (CIF) value, and excise duty. Concerns regarding Customs valuation procedures were also discussed.\n\nThe Committee further examined delays in the clearance of imported goods arising from the need to secure approvals from multiple government institutions in addition to Sri Lanka Customs. Officials highlighted that the absence of an integrated digital system connecting relevant institutions, including the Department of Import and Export Control, has created coordination challenges and delayed the approval process.\n\nThese delays have resulted in containers being held at ports for extended periods, increasing costs for importers through additional storage and related charges. Accordingly, the Committee emphasised the need to improve coordination among government agencies and streamline procedures to facilitate faster clearance of imported goods."
