    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Titanic survivor\u2019s engagement ring sells for \u00a3150,000 at auction\n\nINDEPENDENT - A diamond ring that belonged to one of the most famous survivors of the Titanic\u2019s sinking has fetched \u00a3150,000 at auction.\n\nLucy, Lady Duff Gordon was wearing the solitaire diamond ring when the liner went down on April 15, 1912, on its maiden voyage, resulting in around 1,500 deaths.\n\nIt was the only piece of jewellery of significance that she owned that didn\u2019t go down with the ship. The family knew it as The Titanic Ring, and it was believed to have been her engagement ring.\n\nThe ring went under the hammer at Henry Aldridge & Son in Devizes, Wiltshire on Saturday, sold via a direct descendant. It blew past estimates after being expected to sell for up to \u00a380,000 at auction.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s exceptionally rare for jewellery related to a Titanic survivor to come to market but almost unprecedented for such a personal item from such a high-profile passenger to be sold at auction via direct descent,\u201d Andrew Aldridge, of Henry Aldridge & Son, said.\n\nThe ring had been given to Lady Duff Gordon's grandson, John, the 3rd Earl of Halsbury, a British crossbencher peer and scientist.\n\nAfter his death, it stayed within the family and has been treasured ever since.\n\nLady Duff Gordon had travelled to the US in 1912 with her husband, Sir Cosmo, as first class passengers on board the Titanic.\n\nThe Duff Gordons and their secretary, Laura Mabel Francatelli, escaped the sinking in Lifeboat No 1.\n\nThe boat was designed to hold 40 people, but it was lowered with only 12 aboard, seven of them crew.\n\nOn board RMS Carpathia, the Cunard liner that rescued Titanic survivors, Sir Cosmo gave each of the men from Lifeboat No 1 a cheque for \u00a35.\n\nThis was to help them after the White Star Line stopped their wages when the Titanic sank.\n\nIt led to gossip that the Duff Gordons had bribed their lifeboat's crew not to return to save swimmers out of fear the vessel would be swamped."
  },
