    "md5": "9a4dbc378eefa09d254a9ac73867ec6f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1630,
    "chunk_text": "This was to help them after the White Star Line stopped their wages when the Titanic sank.\n\nIt led to gossip that the Duff Gordons had bribed their lifeboat's crew not to return to save swimmers out of fear the vessel would be swamped.\n\nThe ticket allowed the mother of a second class passenger to watch the liner's departure. The card bears the White Star burgee and is addressed to the officer in charge.\n\nIt states: \u201cPlease allow Mr Talbot and 2 friends to view the S.S. Titanic.\u201d\n\nIt is stamped \u201csecond-class\u201d and is dated April 10, 1912. A handwritten note on the reverse says: \u201cMrs Dibden plus two sisters.\u201d\n\nEliza Dibden, the mother of 18-year-old William Dibden, from the New Forest, Hampshire, carried the ticket onto the Titanic. Her son died in the disaster.\n\nAccompanying the ticket is her son's first communion certificate, dated Easter 1908.\n\nA postcard written by the Titanic's senior wireless operator while the liner was docked at Southampton also went under the hammer, selling for \u00a332,000.\n\nJack Phillips, 25, wrote the card, which features a photograph of the Titanic on the front, to his sister Elsie Phillips on April 6, 1912.\n\nPhillips, described by auctioneer Andrew Aldridge as \u201cone of the true heroes of the disaster\u201d, sent wireless messages enlisting other ships to assist with rescuing passengers and crew as the Titanic was sinking.\n\nThe postcard, addressed to Miss Elsie Phillips of Godalming, Surrey, features a photo of the Titanic at Southampton.\n\nIt reads: \u201cThanks very much for your letter. Having glorious weather, went to Cowes yesterday. Will write later before we sail.\u201d"
  },
