    "md5": "f6faab86f8cc0eca59f131352020f0fe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "IMF to consider Sri Lanka\u2019s seventh programme review on December 2\n\nColombo, Nov. 22 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Executive Board of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) is scheduled to consider Sri Lanka\u2019s Article IV Consultation and Seventh Review under the Extended Fund Facility (EFF) programme on December 2, potentially paving the way for the release of approximately US$345 million in funding.\n\nThe review also includes requests for waivers of non-observance of performance criteria, modifications to performance criteria and a financing assurances review.\n\nThe IMF reached a staff-level agreement with Sri Lanka on the seventh review in October. However, the release of funds remains subject to approval by the Executive Board.\n\nThe Board's consideration comes as Sri Lanka approaches the scheduled conclusion of its current US$2.9 billion IMF-supported programme in March 2027, with discussions continuing over a possible follow-up arrangement.\n\nAnalysts have stressed the importance of continued engagement with the IMF to safeguard the country's economic recovery and prepare for substantial foreign debt repayments due in the coming years.\n\nSri Lanka's foreign debt repayments are estimated at nearly US$3 billion in 2027 and US$3.6 billion in 2028, when repayments to international bondholders and bilateral creditors, including Japan, China and India, are scheduled to resume.\n\nFitch Ratings Senior Director Jeremy Zook has said a follow-up IMF programme could provide a supportive policy framework and a financing backstop amid volatile global conditions.\n\nHe also highlighted the importance of maintaining credible fiscal policies and healthy primary budget surpluses to ensure debt sustainability and support future credit rating improvements.\n\nA committee has reportedly been appointed to examine whether Sri Lanka should enter into another IMF programme after the current arrangement expires."
  },
