    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "He also highlighted the importance of maintaining credible fiscal policies and healthy primary budget surpluses to ensure debt sustainability and support future credit rating improvements.\n\nA committee has reportedly been appointed to examine whether Sri Lanka should enter into another IMF programme after the current arrangement expires.\n\nOptions under consideration could include a funded Stand-By Arrangement or a Policy Coordination Instrument, he noted.\n\nMeanwhile, IMF Deputy Director of the Asia and Pacific Department Sonali Jain-Chandra said last month that the Fund stood ready to support Sri Lanka in any appropriate way the authorities wished.\n\nSpeaking at a press conference during the IMF meetings in Bangkok, she said Sri Lanka's macroeconomic situation had improved considerably, with strong fiscal performance and debt restructuring largely completed.\n\nShe also highlighted progress in structural reforms, including improvements in governance, and confirmed that the IMF would continue engaging with Sri Lankan authorities beyond the current review through a possible new financial arrangement, policy advice, surveillance and capacity development.\n\nJain-Chandra further indicated that another IMF review mission would take place before the existing programme expires in March 2027.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake's third budget, presented in Parliament on November 12, outlined the Government's plans to strengthen the economy following the 2022 financial crisis.\n\nSri Lanka's foreign reserves have since recovered to approximately US$7 billion, compared with the severe reserve shortages experienced during the crisis.\n\nThe country has projected economic growth of 4% in 2027 and an average of 4.2% over the next three to five years. However, analysts have pointed out that growth of 6% or higher would be necessary to make a more substantial impact on the country's debt burden."
