    "md5": "b5af4e1bc4bdaac9216fb25be5e87e29",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Government Dental Service must focus more on prevention: PM\n\nColombo, Nov. 22 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya has called for a shift towards preventive dental care, saying the Government Dental Service must focus not only on treatment but also on prevention, early detection and equal access to services.\n\nShe made these remarks at a ceremony held at the Bandaranaike Memorial International Conference Hall (BMICH) yesterday (22) to mark the centenary of the Government Dental Service in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe Prime Minister said the service, which began on a small scale at the Colombo General Hospital in 1926, had expanded into a nationwide network of 600 treatment centres, employing around 1,350 dental surgeons, including 106 specialists.\n\nShe identified the establishment of the Dental College in 1943 and the graduation of the first batch of locally trained dentists in 1947 as major milestones in the development of dental services.\n\nShe also highlighted the contribution made by the Faculties of Dentistry at the Universities of Peradeniya and Sri Jayewardenepura towards strengthening dental education in the country.\n\nExpressing her appreciation to dental surgeons, specialists, school dental therapists, laboratory technicians, dental assistants and other staff, she said their dedication had helped build the service over the past 100 years.\n\nThe Prime Minister said that oral health went beyond treating toothaches and extracting teeth, as it directly affected nutrition, speech, self-confidence and overall quality of life.\n\nShe pointed out that the prevalence of tooth decay among 12-year-old children had declined from around 67 per cent in earlier national surveys to 30.4 per cent in the 2015-2016 survey, demonstrating the impact of school dental services and preventive programmes.\n\nHowever, she said greater public awareness was needed about oral cancer and the risks associated with tobacco, betel, areca nut and harmful alcohol consumption."
