    "chunk_size_bytes": 1296,
    "chunk_text": "She pointed out that the prevalence of tooth decay among 12-year-old children had declined from around 67 per cent in earlier national surveys to 30.4 per cent in the 2015-2016 survey, demonstrating the impact of school dental services and preventive programmes.\n\nHowever, she said greater public awareness was needed about oral cancer and the risks associated with tobacco, betel, areca nut and harmful alcohol consumption.\n\nShe also said the need to bring basic dental services closer to communities and ensure that access to quality treatment was not determined by income, geographical location or social status.\n\nSpecial attention, she said, should be given to economically disadvantaged communities, persons with disabilities and older people.\n\nThe Prime Minister further highlighted that modern developments such as digital dentistry should be made equally accessible to all sections of society.\n\nA commemorative stamp and first-day cover marking the centenary of the Government Dental Service were also launched at the ceremony with the participation of the Prime Minister and Minister of Health and Mass Media Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa.\n\nDeputy Director General of Health Services (Dental Services) Dr. Chandana Gajanayake and several medical and dental professionals also attended the event."
