    "md5": "1b14ab974a8d2cc9bee1fca3b5fb8547",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "Immigration Bill clears Parliamentary Committee, armed forces to get limited border enforcement powers\n\nNew legislation to replace two existing laws, modernise immigration controls and enable E-Gates\n\nBiometric data collection to be subject to Personal Data Protection Act safeguards\n\nThe proposed Immigration Bill, which seeks to overhaul Sri Lanka\u2019s immigration and emigration framework, has received the approval of the Parliamentary Sectoral Oversight Committee on Governance, Justice and Civil Protection, subject to amendments at the Committee Stage in line with the Supreme Court\u2019s determination.\n\nThe Bill was considered by the Committee at a meeting on 18 November chaired by its Chairman, Dr. Najith Indika, with officials from the Public Security Ministry, Department of Immigration and Emigration, Attorney General\u2019s Department and Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department participating.\n\nThe proposed legislation is intended to replace both the Immigrants and Emigrants Act (Chapter 351) and the Passports and Exit Permits Act No. 53 of 1971, bringing the country\u2019s immigration framework in line with contemporary international standards and technological developments.\n\nOfficials informed the Committee that amendments required by the Supreme Court following a legal challenge to the Bill had been incorporated, including safeguards to ensure the collection and storage of biometric information comply with the Personal Data Protection Act.\n\nAmong the significant provisions is the authority for the President, through a Gazette notification issued at the request of the relevant Minister, to assign limited immigration enforcement powers to members of the three Armed Forces and the Coast Guard to prevent illegal entry into the country."
  },
