    "description": "Dayasiri questions judicial independence, warns against weaponising criminal justice system",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Dayasiri-questions-judicial-independence-warns-against-weaponising-criminal-justice-system/56-800433",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395012.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-5535de65-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1c0025d7caaa2c8f43c2c50daf093b5c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Dayasiri questions judicial independence, warns against weaponising criminal justice system\n\nCalls for disclosure of Anti-Corruption Act remand certificates, transparency in judicial appointments and bench assignments\n\nMP Dayasiri Jayasekara on Saturday in Parliament raised concerns over the independence of the judiciary and the potential misuse of criminal law against political opponents, warning that the credibility of Sri Lanka\u2019s justice system depends on Courts retaining the authority to question the State and scrutinise the grounds for arrest and detention.\n\nSpeaking in Parliament, he, questioned provisions of the Anti-Corruption Act that require Magistrates to remand suspects upon the submission of a certificate by the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) Director-General, subject to statutory conditions.\n\nHe said Section 149 of the 2023 Act gives significant weight to such certificates in determining remand, with bail being granted only in exceptional circumstances and with reasons recorded by the Magistrate. He questioned whether citizens have a meaningful opportunity to challenge the factual basis of a certificate that could result in their continued detention.\n\nReferring to proceedings involving MP Namal Rajapaksa, he said the Fort Magistrate had sought clarification in September on whether the Director-General had submitted such a certificate in related proceedings. \u201cOur duty in this House is to ask what opportunity any citizen has to test the factual basis of a certificate that can keep him behind bars. A Magistrate who must sign the remand order must have a meaningful opportunity to examine the grounds on which a person loses his liberty,\u201d he stressed."
  },
