    "md5": "d57c860a8a12b037d6ed8199d5326816",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "Referring to proceedings involving MP Namal Rajapaksa, he said the Fort Magistrate had sought clarification in September on whether the Director-General had submitted such a certificate in related proceedings. \u201cOur duty in this House is to ask what opportunity any citizen has to test the factual basis of a certificate that can keep him behind bars. A Magistrate who must sign the remand order must have a meaningful opportunity to examine the grounds on which a person loses his liberty,\u201d he stressed.\n\nJayasekara also cautioned against investigations in which individuals were allegedly targeted first, with investigators subsequently seeking offences to fit them. Such practices, he argued, undermined the presumption of innocence guaranteed under Article 13(5) of the Constitution and risked turning legal proceedings into instruments of punishment before guilt was established.\n\n\u201cAs a lawyer and a Member of this House, I am deeply concerned about what happens to that citizen when the decision to pursue him is taken first and the search for an offence begins afterwards. An honest investigation follows the evidence, wherever it leads and whoever may be implicated. Once those in authority select a person and ask investigators to find an offence that will fit, they have already decided whom to punish. Everything that follows carries the appearance of a legal process. That is precisely what makes the abuse so dangerous,\u201d he said.\n\nHe also questioned the Government\u2019s decision under the 22nd Amendment to raise the retirement age of Supreme Court judges from 65 to 67 and Court of Appeal judges from 63 to 65, arguing that the revised limits could have been applied only to future appointments rather than extending the tenure of serving judges."
