    "md5": "66b44bdb4b4afe8841be743db2b18fd9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1732,
    "chunk_text": "He also questioned the Government\u2019s decision under the 22nd Amendment to raise the retirement age of Supreme Court judges from 65 to 67 and Court of Appeal judges from 63 to 65, arguing that the revised limits could have been applied only to future appointments rather than extending the tenure of serving judges.\n\nWhile affirming the Chief Justice\u2019s constitutional authority to constitute benches, Jayasekara proposed that the judiciary publish the principles governing bench assignments and maintain a public record of the benches appointed to hear cases of significant public interest. Such disclosure, he said, would help address concerns over repeated assignments of the same judges to politically sensitive cases while strengthening public confidence in the judiciary.\n\nDrawing on Sri Lanka\u2019s judicial history, he cited the 1937 Mark Bracegirdle case, in which Chief Justice Sir Sidney Abrahams and two other judges ordered the release of Bracegirdle after examining the legality of the colonial Governor\u2019s deportation order. He said the episode demonstrated that even the most powerful executive authority must be required to justify the detention of an individual.\n\nJayasekara also warned that using the criminal justice system to prevent political opponents from contesting elections would undermine the franchise and deny voters their right to choose.\n\n\u201cA Government that weaponises the criminal justice system to disqualify an opponent betrays its own lack of confidence in facing the people at the next election. If you believe you have earned a second mandate, go before the country, defend your record and ask for it. What right have you to narrow the people\u2019s choice before a single vote has been cast?\u201d"
  },
