    "md5": "7f7ae969ce71c5d8d57c1055782d5715",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?\n\nWhy Sri Lanka needs a simpler, fairer and more transparent electricity tariff that protects vulnerable consumers, encourages productive electricity use and ensures the financial sustainability of the power sector\n\nElectricity is no longer merely a household necessity. It is a fundamental requirement for economic development, industrial competitiveness and improved living standards. As the world increasingly moves towards electric transportation, cooking and other technologies, access to affordable electricity has become more important than ever.\n\nDespite achieving almost universal electricity access, Sri Lanka has relatively low per-capita electricity consumption compared with several countries in the region. According to World Bank data for 2024, Sri Lanka\u2019s annual electricity consumption was approximately 695 units per person, compared with 1,223 units in India, 3,080 units in Thailand and 5,418 units in Malaysia.\n\nThis raises an important question: Are Sri Lankans consuming less electricity because they do not need it, or because they cannot afford it?\n\nAlthough income levels, industrial development and economic activities influence electricity consumption, affordability is undoubtedly an important consideration. For many Sri Lankan families, the monthly electricity bill can represent a substantial financial burden, limiting their ability to enjoy the benefits of modern electrical appliances and technologies.\n\nUnfortunately, Sri Lanka\u2019s complicated electricity tariff structure, with its multiple consumption blocks, steep increases in charges and cross-subsidies, can discourage electricity consumption rather than promote improved living standards.\n\nElectricity should be treated as an essential driver of economic development, not as a luxury to be discouraged through excessive pricing."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8329e4f759744f41c5a3443ac13e03b0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "Unfortunately, Sri Lanka\u2019s complicated electricity tariff structure, with its multiple consumption blocks, steep increases in charges and cross-subsidies, can discourage electricity consumption rather than promote improved living standards.\n\nElectricity should be treated as an essential driver of economic development, not as a luxury to be discouraged through excessive pricing.\n\nConsider the domestic tariff effective from 11 May, 2026. A household consuming 180 units during a standard 30-day billing period pays Rs. 6,420, including the fixed charge. However, consuming just one additional unit increases the bill to Rs. 8,450.\n\nIn other words, a single additional unit of electricity increases the monthly bill by Rs. 2,030.\n\nThis occurs because crossing the 180-unit threshold changes not only the rate for additional electricity but also the charges applicable to the first 180 units and the monthly fixed charge.\n\nA household consuming 350 units pays Rs. 25,350, equivalent to an average of Rs. 72.43 per unit. This is approximately twice the recent estimated national average cost of supplying electricity, which has varied around Rs. 30-37 per unit.\n\nAlthough the national average cost does not necessarily represent the precise cost of supplying each consumer, such substantial differences raise legitimate questions about whether the existing tariff structure fairly reflects supply costs.\n\nA large family, a household caring for elderly people or one with greater electricity requirements may easily fall into a higher consumption category without necessarily having a higher income.\n\nMeanwhile, a wealthy household equipped with rooftop solar may purchase relatively little electricity from the grid and consequently qualify for a lower tariff category. Similarly, a luxury holiday bungalow occupied for only a few days each month may benefit from a low-consumption tariff despite the owner\u2019s financial capacity."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c3328495c73434de2945061df29c8e1d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, a wealthy household equipped with rooftop solar may purchase relatively little electricity from the grid and consequently qualify for a lower tariff category. Similarly, a luxury holiday bungalow occupied for only a few days each month may benefit from a low-consumption tariff despite the owner\u2019s financial capacity.\n\nFurthermore, high electricity charges can discourage families from using appliances that improve comfort, health and living standards. They may also discourage the transition from fossil-fuel-based cooking and transportation towards electricity.\n\nSri Lanka should encourage efficient and productive electricity consumption as part of national development rather than penalise consumers merely for using more electricity.\n\nCross-subsidisation is another important concern. Under this arrangement, certain consumer categories pay higher tariffs to help compensate for lower tariffs charged to others.\n\nAlthough the intention is often to protect low-income households or support important industries, the benefits are not necessarily received by those who genuinely need financial assistance.\n\nThe scale of this issue is significant. In June 2022, the Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka (PUCSL) reported that approximately 3.14 million domestic consumers, representing around 50% of domestic electricity connections at that time, consumed fewer than 60 units per month.\n\nWhile many of these consumers genuinely required assistance, low electricity consumption alone could not establish their financial circumstances. The present distribution of consumers should be reassessed using updated data.\n\nWhen tariffs are set below the actual cost of supplying a consumer category, the resulting revenue shortfall must ultimately be recovered through higher charges on other consumers, Government funding or losses incurred by the electricity utilities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
