    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c4f366d686309c34017fb7b139732960",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1785,
    "chunk_text": "When tariffs are set below the actual cost of supplying a consumer category, the resulting revenue shortfall must ultimately be recovered through higher charges on other consumers, Government funding or losses incurred by the electricity utilities.\n\nThe fundamental problem is the assumption that low electricity consumption necessarily indicates poverty, while high consumption indicates wealth.\n\nSocial welfare and industrial development are legitimate national objectives. However, financing these objectives should primarily be the responsibility of the Government rather than an obligation imposed indirectly on other electricity consumers.\n\nSingapore provides a useful example. Eligible households receive Government-funded U-Save rebates credited directly to their utility accounts, based on defined eligibility criteria.\n\nSri Lanka could adopt a similar arrangement through the existing Aswesuma welfare framework, supported by appropriate verification of household income and circumstances. Eligible households could receive a Government-funded monthly electricity allowance, either through direct payments or credits to their electricity bills.\n\nLikewise, if certain industries deserve assistance because of their contribution to employment, exports or national development, such support should be provided through transparent and targeted Government incentives rather than hidden cross-subsidies.\n\nSri Lanka already provides Government funding within its tariff arrangements. For example, the May 2026 tariff determination recognised a Government subsidy of Rs. 15 billion to protect selected consumer categories from tariff increases. However, eligibility was based principally on tariff categories and consumption thresholds rather than individual financial circumstances."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "92f5138b54f800c5f34fea9af1e0282e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka already provides Government funding within its tariff arrangements. For example, the May 2026 tariff determination recognised a Government subsidy of Rs. 15 billion to protect selected consumer categories from tariff increases. However, eligibility was based principally on tariff categories and consumption thresholds rather than individual financial circumstances.\n\nSri Lanka should progressively replace its complicated consumption-block structure with a simpler tariff based on the efficient cost of supplying electricity.\n\nUnder an average-cost-based approach, consumers could pay a transparent energy charge reflecting the cost of electricity supply, together with a reasonable fixed or capacity charge to recover the cost of maintaining the network and providing a reliable connection.\n\nTariffs should primarily reflect factors that genuinely influence supply costs, including voltage level, contracted capacity, network requirements and time of consumption.\n\nFor example, supplying electricity to an industrial consumer at 11 kV or 33 kV involves different distribution costs from supplying a household at 230 V. Similarly, maintaining a high-capacity connection imposes infrastructure costs even when relatively little electricity is consumed.\n\nThese differences should be reflected transparently in electricity tariffs rather than through excessive reliance on the purpose of consumption or arbitrary consumption thresholds.\n\nMalaysia provides a useful regional example through its revised tariff structure, which places greater emphasis on supply voltage and identifiable cost components.\n\nA simplified tariff would allow consumers to understand what they are paying for while enabling utilities to recover the reasonable cost of the services they provide.\n\nHowever, the transition should be gradual, with appropriate protection for vulnerable consumers and measures to avoid sudden increases in electricity bills."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "79cc66d38bf1378b097672a92bbfd469",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "A simplified tariff would allow consumers to understand what they are paying for while enabling utilities to recover the reasonable cost of the services they provide.\n\nHowever, the transition should be gradual, with appropriate protection for vulnerable consumers and measures to avoid sudden increases in electricity bills.\n\nElectricity generation costs are highly sensitive to rainfall, reservoir storage, hydropower availability, fuel prices, exchange rates, electricity demand and generating-plant availability.\n\nThese factors can change considerably within a short period, making accurate forecasting difficult.\n\nAn examination of recent PUCSL tariff determinations indicates that the average allowable cost of supplying electricity to consumers was approximately Rs. 32.63 per unit in 2024 and Rs. 29.55 per unit in 2025, while estimates for the middle of 2026 were higher, at around Rs. 37 per unit. These are overall supply-cost estimates or regulatory benchmarks, including generation, transmission, distribution and other approved costs, rather than generation costs alone.\n\nImportantly, these figures demonstrate how electricity supply costs can vary considerably between periods.\n\nRegulatory records also show significant differences between forecast and actual expenditure. For example, actual generation-energy expenditure during the first quarter of 2025 was approximately Rs. 9 billion below the amount initially forecast by PUCSL.\n\nThese variations highlight the importance of giving greater weight to verified actual expenditure rather than relying predominantly on uncertain future projections.\n\nA more transparent approach would be to use verified actual costs from the preceding comparable period as the principal reference, supplemented by seasonal comparisons and clearly justified adjustments for expected changes."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "66173afbf83de3cc2441f7f879ad5642",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "A more transparent approach would be to use verified actual costs from the preceding comparable period as the principal reference, supplemented by seasonal comparisons and clearly justified adjustments for expected changes.\n\nMalaysia\u2019s Automatic Fuel Adjustment mechanism offers a useful example. It allows monthly adjustments to reflect variations in fuel-related generation costs, resulting in additional charges or rebates depending on prevailing conditions.\n\nSri Lanka could adopt a similar transparent adjustment mechanism while maintaining appropriate financial safeguards for the utilities.\n\nForecasting cannot be eliminated entirely, particularly in a power system heavily influenced by hydropower and fuel-price variations. However, forecasting differences should be openly reconciled, and consumers should receive the benefits when actual costs are lower.\n\nImportantly, inefficient operations, unnecessary expenditure and avoidable losses should not automatically be passed on to consumers merely because utilities have incurred those costs.\n\nTariff determination should be based on efficiently incurred and justifiable expenditure, supported by independent regulatory scrutiny.\n\nRooftop solar has become an important national energy resource, attracting private investment, reducing dependence on conventional generation and contributing to energy security.\n\nHowever, its continued development should not compromise the financial sustainability of electricity utilities, which must maintain the electricity network and ensure reliable supply even when solar generation is unavailable.\n\nCountries such as Malaysia, Australia and the United Kingdom allow consumers to use their own solar electricity and export surplus generation to the grid. Consumers receive payments or credits for exported electricity while continuing to contribute towards the cost of maintaining the electricity network."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0007",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "586c4bb427dfa72c5d6e546edb64f606",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Countries such as Malaysia, Australia and the United Kingdom allow consumers to use their own solar electricity and export surplus generation to the grid. Consumers receive payments or credits for exported electricity while continuing to contribute towards the cost of maintaining the electricity network.\n\nSri Lanka should adopt a similarly balanced approach rather than restricting new rooftop solar consumers exclusively to the Net Plus arrangement.\n\nConsumers should have the option to use their own generation, store surplus energy in batteries and export excess electricity at a reasonable price reflecting the value of that electricity to the power system.\n\nAt the same time, a fair connection or capacity charge could ensure that solar consumers contribute towards maintaining the grid without transferring an unreasonable financial burden to other consumers.\n\nAs smart-metering infrastructure improves, higher payments could be offered for electricity exported during peak-demand periods, encouraging battery investment and reducing dependence on expensive conventional generation.\n\nSuch arrangements must also respect technical limitations, including voltage control, network capacity and grid stability.\n\nThe guiding principle should be simple: Consumers should pay fairly for using the electricity network, while utilities should pay fairly for the electricity consumers supply.\n\nThis would encourage rooftop solar investment while protecting both electricity utilities and non-solar consumers.\n\nThe cost of supplying electricity varies considerably throughout the day. During periods of abundant solar generation and relatively low demand, electricity can be less expensive, whereas during the evening peak, more costly thermal generation may be required.\n\nElectricity tariffs should recognise these differences and encourage consumers to shift flexible electricity consumption towards lower-cost periods."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Electricity tariffs in Sri Lanka: Are consumers paying fair price?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Electricity-tariffs-in-Sri-Lanka-Are-consumers-paying-fair-price/4-800434",
    "lang": "en",
