    "time_ut": 1795395397.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-2bfd3b6d-0008",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "48f474b06cc46b616cc41e11bedd23b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1884,
    "chunk_text": "The cost of supplying electricity varies considerably throughout the day. During periods of abundant solar generation and relatively low demand, electricity can be less expensive, whereas during the evening peak, more costly thermal generation may be required.\n\nElectricity tariffs should recognise these differences and encourage consumers to shift flexible electricity consumption towards lower-cost periods.\n\nMore advanced arrangements are available in Singapore and the United Kingdom, where participating consumers can choose electricity pricing that varies every half-hour.\n\nSri Lanka already offers optional TOU tariffs for domestic, commercial and industrial consumers. Therefore, the immediate priority should be to improve these existing arrangements rather than to introduce sophisticated real-time pricing prematurely.\n\nCurrent TOU periods should be reviewed against actual generation costs, particularly to recognise the benefits of abundant daytime solar electricity.\n\nAttractive tariffs during lower-cost periods could encourage electric-vehicle charging, water pumping, battery storage and other flexible electricity uses, helping to reduce evening peak demand.\n\nAs smart-metering infrastructure expands, more advanced dynamic tariffs could be introduced voluntarily, initially for high-consumption households, commercial establishments and consumers equipped with solar and battery systems.\n\nHowever, reliable metering, transparent pricing, advance information and protection against excessive price volatility would be essential. Consumers who prefer predictable electricity bills should retain the option of conventional tariffs.\n\nThis gradual approach would improve the utilisation of existing generating capacity, accommodate greater renewable energy penetration and potentially postpone unnecessary investment in additional generation and network infrastructure."
  },
