    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bb2402c6d0eb330402065bbea82ec02c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2011,
    "chunk_text": "This gradual approach would improve the utilisation of existing generating capacity, accommodate greater renewable energy penetration and potentially postpone unnecessary investment in additional generation and network infrastructure.\n\nHowever, Sri Lanka cannot simply replicate systems adopted by countries with more advanced electricity markets. Reforms must reflect the country\u2019s economic circumstances, institutional arrangements and existing infrastructure.\n\nThe immediate priority should be an independently reviewed cost-of-service study to determine the efficient cost of supplying electricity at different voltage levels and to different consumer categories.\n\nThis should be accompanied by regular publication of actual electricity supply costs, transparent tariff calculations and clear reconciliation of forecasting differences.\n\nThe complicated consumption-block structure and hidden cross-subsidies should then be progressively removed, while direct Government-funded assistance is established for eligible low-income households and strategically important industries.\n\nSimpler tariffs based on supply voltage, capacity and electricity consumption should be introduced gradually, with appropriate safeguards against sudden increases in household bills.\n\nIn parallel, rooftop solar consumers should receive fair choices for self-consumption, storage and export, while improved TOU tariffs should encourage electricity use during lower-cost periods.\n\nMore advanced dynamic pricing arrangements can follow as smart-metering and other supporting infrastructure become available.\n\nThroughout this transition, independent regulation will remain essential to protect consumers, ensure reliable electricity supply and prevent inefficient or unjustified costs from being passed on to the public.\n\nConclusion: Electricity should be an engine of development, not a luxury\n\nSri Lanka needs an electricity tariff structure that is fair, transparent, affordable and supportive of national economic development."
