    "md5": "e2d8f5a35e67499086302faba1f7e4a5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1522,
    "chunk_text": "Conclusion: Electricity should be an engine of development, not a luxury\n\nSri Lanka needs an electricity tariff structure that is fair, transparent, affordable and supportive of national economic development.\n\nElectricity consumption should not be discouraged through punitive block tariffs, nor should consumers be expected to finance social welfare and industrial policies through hidden cross-subsidies.\n\nLow-income households and deserving industries should receive transparent, Government-funded assistance. Tariff calculations should give greater importance to verified actual costs, with justified adjustments and proper reconciliation. Rooftop solar investment and flexible electricity consumption should be encouraged through fair and practical arrangements.\n\nThese reforms should be introduced progressively, protecting vulnerable consumers and avoiding sudden financial difficulties for electricity utilities.\n\nThe guiding principle should be straightforward: Electricity utilities should recover the efficient cost of the services they provide; the Government should finance the subsidies it wishes to grant; and consumers should be free to use electricity productively without being unfairly penalised.\n\nA fair electricity tariff is not necessarily the cheapest. It is one that consumers can understand, utilities can sustain and the country can rely upon to improve living standards and accelerate economic development.\n\n(The author is a former Chief Operating Officer of Electricity Distribution Lanka Ltd.)"
