    "chunk_size_bytes": 1692,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027\u2019s enhanced capital allowance: A 200% promise needs 100% clarity\n\nBudget 2027 offers enterprises approved by the Board of Investment (BOI) an enhanced capital allowance of 200% for investing at least $ 3 million in export-oriented manufacturing or tourism development. The stated aim is to encourage private investment, promote export-oriented manufacturing and strengthen tourism. The Budget also sets itself a welcome test: every tax concession will be assessed on the economic and social benefits it generates.\n\nThe direction is right. An allowance tied to capital actually spent rewards investment that has actually happened. But read alongside the Inland Revenue Act, No. 24 of 2017 (IRA) and its latest amendment, the proposal raises a basic question that the Budget leaves unanswered: who exactly is this incentive meant to help? The answer will be found not in the Budget Speech, but in the drafting of the law.\n\nSection 16 of the IRA is the gateway. Capital allowances are granted on depreciable assets and computed under the Second, Fourth or Sixth Schedule. They must be claimed in the year they arise and cannot be deferred.\n\nGeneral capital allowance. The Fourth Schedule provides allowances on a straight-line basis for depreciable assets owned and used in the production of income. Plant, machinery, computers, vehicles and furniture are written off over five years; buildings over twenty years, at 5% a year.\n\n(a) is employed in the production of income from a business and is likely to lose value because of wear and tear, obsolescence or the passing of time; but\n\n(b) excludes goodwill, an interest in land, a membership interest in an entity and trading stock."
