    "chunk_text": "(a) is employed in the production of income from a business and is likely to lose value because of wear and tear, obsolescence or the passing of time; but\n\n(b) excludes goodwill, an interest in land, a membership interest in an entity and trading stock.\n\nEnhanced capital allowance (ECA). That leaves the Second Schedule as the principal ECA regime. A person who makes a new investment in Sri Lanka is granted an allowance computed on expenses incurred on depreciable assets, other than intangibles, in addition to the general allowance. Expansions of an existing business qualify only under a BOI agreement and with specific BOI approval. The tiers are summarised below.\n\nTechnical Note 1.2 to the Budget allows BOI-approved enterprises investing at least $ 3 million in export-oriented manufacturing or tourism development to opt for an enhanced allowance of 200% of qualifying capital expenditure.\u00a0 In the case of manufacturing and tourism (other than hotels), investment should be made within 3 years. For hotels, investment should be made within 5 years. An enterprise that opts in forgoes both the general allowance under the Fourth Schedule and the ECA under the Second Schedule.\n\nThe first question the law must answer is whether the 200% is for new projects, expansions, or both. The Budget refers simply to \u201centerprises operating under agreements with the BOI\u201d, which on its face includes existing BOI factories and hotels. At post-Budget seminars, however, officials indicated that the intention is to cover new undertakings. If that is the policy, it must be written into the law, because the consequences for existing businesses are significant.\n\nUnder the current Second Schedule, an existing company is already at a disadvantage. The $ 250,000 tier introduced in 2026 is confined to new undertakings. An existing BOI exporter expanding by $ 2.9 million therefore receives no ECA at all, while a newly formed undertakings investing"
