    "chunk_text": "Under the current Second Schedule, an existing company is already at a disadvantage. The $ 250,000 tier introduced in 2026 is confined to new undertakings. An existing BOI exporter expanding by $ 2.9 million therefore receives no ECA at all, while a newly formed undertakings investing\n\nIf the proposed 200% is also confined to new undertakings, the gap widens. Businesses with an established track record, existing markets and the balance sheet to invest are left with the general allowance, while incentives flow to new entities. That is the reverse of what an investment-led recovery needs.\n\nIt also raises a question about the policy maker\u2019s choice of words. If the shift from \u201cinvestment in depreciable assets\u201d to \u201cqualifying capital expenditure\u201d was intended to bring the refurbishment and modernisation of existing hotels and factories within the incentive, that intention sits uneasily with a scheme confined to new undertakings. A new undertaking has nothing to refurbish. Both cannot be true: either the wider base is meant to reach existing businesses, in which case the law must say so, or the incentive is for new undertakings only, in which case the change in wording does little for the hotels and factories that most need to reinvest.\n\nThe issue is sharpest in tourism. Much of what existing hotels need to spend today is on refurbishment: upgrading rooms, renewing interiors and restoring properties damaged by the recent cyclone. None of this qualifies for ECA. The Act excludes the replacement of an existing depreciable asset from the definition of \u201cexpansion\u201d, and an existing hotel is not a new undertaking. Refurbishment spending is instead relieved in one of three ways, depending on its nature."
