    "chunk_text": "The issue is sharpest in tourism. Much of what existing hotels need to spend today is on refurbishment: upgrading rooms, renewing interiors and restoring properties damaged by the recent cyclone. None of this qualifies for ECA. The Act excludes the replacement of an existing depreciable asset from the definition of \u201cexpansion\u201d, and an existing hotel is not a new undertaking. Refurbishment spending is instead relieved in one of three ways, depending on its nature.\n\nA hotel refurbishment is therefore not left without relief, but that relief is limited to 100% of cost and, for structural improvements to an older building, can take well over a decade to recover. The real gap is not repairs, which are deducted immediately, nor loose equipment, written off in five years. It is capital improvements to hotel buildings. If qualifying capital expenditure were defined to include such improvements, a 200% allowance would bring the relief forward and double it, a gain in both timing and quantum.\n\nIf the objective is to strengthen the tourism sector, as the Budget states, a well-defined refurbishment window for existing hotels, with a minimum spend, a defined completion period and a clawback, may deliver faster and more visible results than incentives for new projects alone.\n\n\u201cQualifying capital expenditure\u201d: A wider base, or a new label?\n\nThe Second Schedule allowance is anchored to depreciable assets within the Fourth Schedule classes. The Budget instead proposes 200% of \u201cqualifying capital expenditure\u201d, a term found nowhere in the Act. Capital expenditure is a wider concept, capable of reaching land development, lease premiums, pre-operational costs and intangibles."
