    "chunk_text": "The Second Schedule allowance is anchored to depreciable assets within the Fourth Schedule classes. The Budget instead proposes 200% of \u201cqualifying capital expenditure\u201d, a term found nowhere in the Act. Capital expenditure is a wider concept, capable of reaching land development, lease premiums, pre-operational costs and intangibles.\n\nOn a like-for-like base, the headline is less dramatic than it appears. A new manufacturer investing $ 10 million in depreciable assets outside the Northern Province already receives a 100% ECA plus the general allowance, adding up to around 200% of cost over time. A proposed 200% allowance in place of both delivers broadly the same total. The real gain is in timing.\n\nThat gain is clearest for new hotels, which invest mainly in buildings written off over twenty years; a front-loaded 200% would bring much of that relief forward. For manufacturers, whose plant is already written off over five years, the benefit is modest.\n\nFor new projects above $ 100 million, or in the Northern Province, current law already gives 150% or 200% plus the general allowance. \u201c200% in place of everything\u201d would leave these investors worse off, which is presumably why the allowance is optional. But investors can only exercise an option they can price, and they cannot price it until the law is written. Meanwhile, existing exporters expanding below $3 million, existing hotels refurbishing and non-BOI companies sit outside the frame entirely, unless the law is drafted to include them."
