    "chunk_text": "4. How is the $ 3 million measured? For an expansion, is the test the expansion alone or the company\u2019s total investment? Which exchange rate and which date apply when spending is in rupees over three to five years?\n\n5. When does the clock start? Must the investment commence after the amending law is enacted, and how do the three- and five-year windows align with the existing implementation period?\n\n7. How does the election (option to choose) work? When should such election be made ? Should it be in writing and is it subject to approval ? Is it irrevocable, and how does it apply to BOI enterprises part-way through a Second Schedule project?\n\n8. What happens on sale or failure? A specific recapture rule is needed if the allowance covers expenditure that is not a depreciable asset, together with a clear clawback where the threshold or sector conditions are not met.\n\nThese questions are not exhaustive, but answering them would give investors the clarity and certainty the incentive needs.\n\nThe policy logic holds: an allowance tied to capital actually spent is a better tool than a profit-based holiday, and it sits more comfortably with today\u2019s global tax standards. But Sri Lanka has introduced, expired, extended and re-tiered its enhanced capital allowances repeatedly in recent years. Investors committing $ 3 million or more over a three- to five-year horizon need certainty more than generosity."
