    "chunk_size_bytes": 1466,
    "chunk_text": "The policy logic holds: an allowance tied to capital actually spent is a better tool than a profit-based holiday, and it sits more comfortably with today\u2019s global tax standards. But Sri Lanka has introduced, expired, extended and re-tiered its enhanced capital allowances repeatedly in recent years. Investors committing $ 3 million or more over a three- to five-year horizon need certainty more than generosity.\n\nThe Tax Expenditure Statement 2025/2026, published by the Ministry of Finance on 12 November 2026, recognises enhanced and accelerated capital allowances as a form of tax expenditure. Yet its inventory neither lists nor estimates the cost of the Second Schedule ECA, and the\n\n$ 250,000 tier introduced in 2026 is not disclosed as a new tax expenditure. If every concession is to be judged on the benefits it generates, the starting point must be to measure what existing allowances cost and what they have delivered. The 200% option proposed in the Budget 2027 should be costed and reported from the outset, so that its value for money can be tested rather than assumed.\n\nA 200% allowance is a strong signal to investors. Whether it becomes a strong incentive, and for whom, depends entirely on the drafting and its implementation.\n\n(The views and opinions expressed in this article are those of the author in her personal capacity)\n\n(Reference has been made to the Inland Revenue Act No 24 of 2017 and related amendments and the Budget Speech 2027.)"
