    "md5": "3c3948a26846b807b1e5481fd3d02250",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "A decade after Paris Agreement: Financing farms and natural capital\n\nSri Lanka should use climate finance to protect farms and the soils, water, forests and biodiversity that sustain them. A decade after the Paris Agreement entered into force, success should be judged by whether investment reduces crop losses, secures water and protects livelihoods. Farmers need timely advice as rainfall becomes less predictable; families need reliable food supplies at affordable prices.\n\nAdopted in December 2015, opened for signature in April 2016 and brought into force in November that year, the Agreement created a shared framework to limit warming, strengthen adaptation and support developing countries. It seeks to keep warming well below 2\u00b0C while pursuing efforts towards 1.5\u00b0C above pre-industrial levels. Its anniversary invites scrutiny of both achievements and unfinished work.\n\nThe Paris Agreement established a continuing process through which countries submit climate commitments, strengthen them over time and report progress. These Nationally Determined Contributions, or NDCs, have brought climate concerns into national planning. Common reporting arrangements and periodic global reviews allow countries\u2019 collective progress to be assessed.\n\nYet these institutional achievements have not secured a safe climate pathway. The Emissions Gap Report (2026) of the United Nations Environment Program (UNEP) estimates that delivering national commitments and continuing similar efforts would limit warming this century to about 2.6\u00b0C. The Paris Agreement has organised the global response, but commitments and implementation remain inadequate. The Adaptation Gap Report (2025) of UNEP has estimated the annual adaptation needs for developing countries in 2035 would cost around\n\n$ 310\u2013365 billion, compared with international public adaptation finance of $ 26 billion in 2023. Although the years differ, the figures illustrate the scale of the financing challenge."
  },
