    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "Yet these institutional achievements have not secured a safe climate pathway. The Emissions Gap Report (2026) of the United Nations Environment Program (UNEP) estimates that delivering national commitments and continuing similar efforts would limit warming this century to about 2.6\u00b0C. The Paris Agreement has organised the global response, but commitments and implementation remain inadequate. The Adaptation Gap Report (2025) of UNEP has estimated the annual adaptation needs for developing countries in 2035 would cost around\n\n$ 310\u2013365 billion, compared with international public adaptation finance of $ 26 billion in 2023. Although the years differ, the figures illustrate the scale of the financing challenge.\n\nSpending priorities matter too. Reducing emissions, or mitigation, must proceed alongside adaptation that helps people cope with changing conditions. Farmers need support before harvests fail. Governments and funding agencies should assess projects by timely disbursement, lasting benefits and the share reaching those facing the greatest risks.\n\nDifficulties in the agricultural sector have emanated due to several causes, for example: Input costs, policy decisions, markets and land degradation interact with weather. Hence, attributing every production decline to climate change would weaken the argument. Finance should address demonstrated climate risks while improving farmers\u2019 ability to respond.\n\nThe NDC 3.0 of Sri Lanka covers the period 2026\u20132035 and includes agriculture in both mitigation and adaptation segments. Priorities include climate advice, resilient varieties and breeds, better soil and water management, efficient input use and reduced postharvest losses. These provide a direction for investment."
