    "chunk_size_bytes": 1869,
    "chunk_text": "The NDC 3.0 of Sri Lanka covers the period 2026\u20132035 and includes agriculture in both mitigation and adaptation segments. Priorities include climate advice, resilient varieties and breeds, better soil and water management, efficient input use and reduced postharvest losses. These provide a direction for investment.\n\nNational commitments alone do not demonstrate greater resilience. Government agencies and project implementers should establish whether households experience fewer losses, more dependable water and more stable incomes. Interventions must remain affordable and effective after external funding ends, with clear responsibilities and national budgets for continued operation.\n\nThe Government should make natural capital assessment part of agricultural and climate investment planning. Natural capital comprises soils, water, forests, wetlands and biodiversity. These assets support farming through water regulation, erosion control and pollination. Assessing their condition helps identify where degradation threatens production and where protection offers lasting benefits.\n\nSuch assessments should identify the assets, their condition, the people who depend on them and their exposure to climate risks. Existing surveys, satellite information and local knowledge need field verification. Priority assessment areas could include upper watersheds supplying reservoirs, degraded agricultural slopes and vulnerable tank catchments.\n\nNatural capital assessment should begin with physical measures such as soil loss, water quality, habitat condition and seasonal water availability. Economic valuation can then estimate selected benefits, including avoided sediment removal or crop losses, while explaining uncertainty. Ecological and cultural importance cannot always be expressed in rupees; valuation should not imply that every ecosystem is replaceable."
