    "chunk_text": "Natural capital assessment should begin with physical measures such as soil loss, water quality, habitat condition and seasonal water availability. Economic valuation can then estimate selected benefits, including avoided sediment removal or crop losses, while explaining uncertainty. Ecological and cultural importance cannot always be expressed in rupees; valuation should not imply that every ecosystem is replaceable.\n\nConservation costs and benefits must also be shared fairly. Upstream farmers may benefit from healthier soils while bearing costs for land management changes that also help downstream water users. Technical support or payments for verified stewardship could make participation affordable. Assessments can inform contributions from public agencies, utilities and businesses, with clear land rights and benefit-sharing arrangements.\n\nOver time, natural capital accounts could track changes in these assets alongside economic indicators. The findings should inform budgets, land-use decisions and lending. Assessments deliver value when they lead to action and subsequent monitoring.\n\nThe Government should prioritise reliable water management from upper catchments to farms in its climate investment program. Rehabilitating a tank without protecting its catchment leaves it exposed to sedimentation. Programs should connect watershed restoration, tank cascades, irrigation scheduling, drainage and maintenance, with farmers involved in water allocation.\n\nSoil protection belongs in the same package. Suitable contour measures, ground cover, organic matter management and appropriate nutrient use can protect productive land. Evidence on erosion, water stress and livelihoods should guide site selection. Success should reflect soil condition, water reliability and production stability, alongside hectares treated."
  },
