    "chunk_size_bytes": 1677,
    "chunk_text": "Finance should support food production with fewer avoidable losses and lower environmental costs. Better fertiliser timing and placement, soil testing and integrated pest management need practical training and evidence of farm benefits. Suitable paddy water management can reduce methane emissions, provided irrigation is controllable and yields are safeguarded.\n\nFinancing must match the investment. Grants and public budgets suit shared services such as climate information and catchment protection. Affordable loans, guarantees and private investment may suit viable storage, processing and renewable energy businesses. Smallholders should not face unaffordable debt for investments whose benefits extend across society.\n\nThe Government should develop an investment program, coordinated by the Ministry of Finance, linking agricultural resilience with natural capital protection. Each proposal needs a climate rationale, baseline, responsible institution, maintenance budget and measurable outcomes. Farmers, including women and tenants, should help design access arrangements. Implementing agencies should disclose expenditure, beneficiaries and results, provide a complaints process and commission independent evaluation. Universities can help establish baselines and assess outcomes.\n\nPriority should be investments that farmers can sustain and public agencies can maintain. Connecting resilient farming with healthy watersheds and soils would give climate commitments practical meaning: more dependable harvests, secure livelihoods and reliable food supplies.\n\n(The author is Senior Professor in Crop Science, Faculty of Agriculture, University of Peradeniya, Sri Lanka)"
