    "chunk_size_bytes": 1504,
    "chunk_text": "IPS\u2019s State of the Economy 2026: More worm\u2019s eye analysis than bird\u2019s eye analysis\n\nIPS \u2018State of the Economy\u2019 report is far too precious a document to be ignored by those entrusted with shaping the\n\nThose who read my previous week\u2019s article in this newspaper, titled \u2018Bird\u2019s Eye View of the Economy: \u201cOk, Stable and Growing\u201d; Worm\u2019s Eye, \u201cNot That Rosy\u201d\u2019, will find a timely companion in the newly released annual flagship report by the Institute of Policy Studies (IPS).\n\nThis publication skillfully mirrors that dual perspective, initialising its coverage with a four-chapter macroeconomic analysis\u2014a high-level evaluation of national economic metrics like inflation, total output, and trade balances\u2014before guiding readers through a much grimmer reality across the remaining ten chapters. This extensive second section serves as a \u201cworm\u2019s-eye\u201d analysis, focusing on microeconomic conditions to explain how individual households, small businesses, and workers are actually faring on the ground.\n\nEstablished in the late 1980s as an independent State apparatus, IPS was specifically created to provide autonomous policy advice, filling critical gaps in the less comprehensive reporting historically produced by the Central Bank and the Ministry of Finance. For nearly four decades, the institute has published this State of the Economy report every November, delivering crucial data just in time to assist the Ministry of Finance with its upcoming budget planning."
  },
