    "md5": "eef662d7081fd8485299c5b8c8721a19",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2008,
    "chunk_text": "Established in the late 1980s as an independent State apparatus, IPS was specifically created to provide autonomous policy advice, filling critical gaps in the less comprehensive reporting historically produced by the Central Bank and the Ministry of Finance. For nearly four decades, the institute has published this State of the Economy report every November, delivering crucial data just in time to assist the Ministry of Finance with its upcoming budget planning.\n\nIn the opening chapter, titled \u2018Policy Perspectives\u2019, IPS synthesises its institutional wisdom on how Sri Lanka should craft strategic policies to sustain economic growth once the safety net of the IMF is gone. However, the report candidly admits that the State does not possess total freedom to design a completely independent policy package just yet. It explicitly notes that \u201cgrowth under the guardrails is the operating reality of 2026 and will remain so for the duration of the IMF program and beyond.\u201d This means that the strict fiscal limits and structural benchmarks agreed with international lenders will dictate national budgeting for years to come.\n\nCrucially, IPS reminds policymakers that an IMF program is essentially an emergency toolkit designed strictly to achieve macroeconomic stability\u2014the stabilisation of national balances, such as curbing hyperinflation and steadying the currency. True, durable growth relies on an entirely different set of foundations. According to IPS as outlined in the State of the Economy Report for 2026, long-term economic expansion requires boosting the economy\u2019s potential output through advanced technology, a more highly skilled workforce, and robust capital investment. It also demands careful mitigation of environmental damage, a steady elevation of living standards, and deep social inclusion\u2014a development model ensuring that vulnerable or marginalised groups are not left behind but are given equal access to economic opportunities and fruits of economic advancement."
  },
