    "md5": "31a160b1514eace35b1571fa5affcfb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1652,
    "chunk_text": "Crucially, IPS reminds policymakers that an IMF program is essentially an emergency toolkit designed strictly to achieve macroeconomic stability\u2014the stabilisation of national balances, such as curbing hyperinflation and steadying the currency. True, durable growth relies on an entirely different set of foundations. According to IPS as outlined in the State of the Economy Report for 2026, long-term economic expansion requires boosting the economy\u2019s potential output through advanced technology, a more highly skilled workforce, and robust capital investment. It also demands careful mitigation of environmental damage, a steady elevation of living standards, and deep social inclusion\u2014a development model ensuring that vulnerable or marginalised groups are not left behind but are given equal access to economic opportunities and fruits of economic advancement.\n\nSri Lanka learnt this lesson at a catastrophic cost in 2022. On that occasion, ordinary people, pushed to the absolute brink by unendurable financial burdens, revolted against the State, culminating in the incumbent President fleeing the country under the cover of darkness and paving the way for a brand-new administration to set in. Fresh with the trauma of that hazardous memory, IPS is understandably desperate to avoid a repeat of history. It is precisely this fear of renewed instability that drives the institute to advocate for social inclusion as an indispensable goal of modern economic policy. It ends the chapter with a wise counsel in this regard. It says that Sri Lanka can and must grow, but it must grow differently to achieve its ambitious socio-economic targets."
