    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka learnt this lesson at a catastrophic cost in 2022. On that occasion, ordinary people, pushed to the absolute brink by unendurable financial burdens, revolted against the State, culminating in the incumbent President fleeing the country under the cover of darkness and paving the way for a brand-new administration to set in. Fresh with the trauma of that hazardous memory, IPS is understandably desperate to avoid a repeat of history. It is precisely this fear of renewed instability that drives the institute to advocate for social inclusion as an indispensable goal of modern economic policy. It ends the chapter with a wise counsel in this regard. It says that Sri Lanka can and must grow, but it must grow differently to achieve its ambitious socio-economic targets.\n\nAchieving this safety net demands tough structural changes, including comprehensive tariff and labour reforms to boost competitiveness, alongside deep investment in modern infrastructure. Crucially, IPS stresses that this does not just mean pouring concrete into traditional physical projects like highways, but rather building digital infrastructure to modernise transactions, governance, and trade. National economic policies must be firmly geared toward this modernisation, yet the hurdles remain monumental.\n\nThe report reveals a grim reality when looking at individual sectors: between 2015 and 2025, Sri Lanka\u2019s agriculture completely stagnated, suffering a below-zero compound annual growth rate (CAGR)\u2014a metric that measures the smoothed, steady annual growth rate of an industry over a specific period, showing that real farming output actually shrank over the decade. Over the same 10-year period, industrial growth averaged a miniscule 0.5%,while the services sector averaged just 2%. These low numbers, as revealed by IPS, contributed to a very low real economic growth over that entire decade hovering barely above zero."
