    "chunk_size_bytes": 1627,
    "chunk_text": "The report reveals a grim reality when looking at individual sectors: between 2015 and 2025, Sri Lanka\u2019s agriculture completely stagnated, suffering a below-zero compound annual growth rate (CAGR)\u2014a metric that measures the smoothed, steady annual growth rate of an industry over a specific period, showing that real farming output actually shrank over the decade. Over the same 10-year period, industrial growth averaged a miniscule 0.5%,while the services sector averaged just 2%. These low numbers, as revealed by IPS, contributed to a very low real economic growth over that entire decade hovering barely above zero.\n\nUnder the strict guidance of IMF, Sri Lanka\u2019s fiscal policy\u2014the way the Government manages its public taxation and spending\u2014has focused intensely on achieving immediate fiscal stabilisation. However, IPS warns that while balancing the books is an absolute necessity to create a stable economic environment, it is not enough on its own to drive genuine national development. Instead of simply squeezing more taxes out of the economy, the institute highlights a critical need to maintain tax revenues while simultaneously rationalising, or streamlining, State spending.\n\nThis is known as a balanced fiscal stance, an approach that carefully balances incoming revenue against outgoing expenses to ensure the Government\u2019s financial position actively supports national progress. In contrast, the standard IMF blueprint prescribes a rigid strategy of revenue-based fiscal consolidation, which means narrowing the budget deficit purely by driving up Government income through higher taxes and duties."
