    "chunk_text": "This is known as a balanced fiscal stance, an approach that carefully balances incoming revenue against outgoing expenses to ensure the Government\u2019s financial position actively supports national progress. In contrast, the standard IMF blueprint prescribes a rigid strategy of revenue-based fiscal consolidation, which means narrowing the budget deficit purely by driving up Government income through higher taxes and duties.\n\nThis is broken down into a fivefold plan: directing State investment into highly productive economic zones, channeling significant resources into human capital development\u2014which means upgrading the skills, health, and knowledge of the workforce through better education and healthcare services\u2014and building physical infrastructure strong enough to withstand severe climate shocks.\n\nIt also demands aggressive funding for technological innovation and digital transformations, alongside actions to improve productivity across every single sector. Crucially, this modern framework has absolutely no room for inflating recurrent expenditure\u2014the everyday, ongoing costs of running the Government\u2014such as expanding expensive fuel subsidies or maintaining a bloated, inefficient public service. These are exceptionally bold policy recommendations that challenge long-standing political habits.\n\nWith this comprehensive overview complete, IPS shifts its focus to a detailed \u201cworm\u2019s-eye\u201d analysis, examining the ground realities faced by ordinary households. At first glance, the data appears promising: Sri Lanka\u2019s unemployment rate has fallen to approximately 4%, returning to the baseline observed before the 2022 economic collapse."
