    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "With this comprehensive overview complete, IPS shifts its focus to a detailed \u201cworm\u2019s-eye\u201d analysis, examining the ground realities faced by ordinary households. At first glance, the data appears promising: Sri Lanka\u2019s unemployment rate has fallen to approximately 4%, returning to the baseline observed before the 2022 economic collapse.\n\nMaking matters worse, this low participation is exacerbated by a severe brain drain\u2014the mass emigration of highly educated and skilled professionals seeking better opportunities abroad. As IPS bluntly notes, this flight has removed the country\u2019s most mobile, skilled workers, making unskilled workers to occupy jobs which demand high levels of skills. At the same time, a collapse in the supply of skilled workers needed by the information and communications technology (ICT) and professional service sectors has pushed those industries to hire less qualified workers to fill the vacancies. The result of this unwarranted development is the loss of competitiveness, on one side, and low growth in productivity, on the other.\n\nThe IPS report turns a compassionate yet analytical lens onto the plight of Sri Lanka\u2019s rapidly ageing population, often referred to as the silvering people. While popular misconception often paints senior citizens as an economic burden who merely consume the goods produced by the younger workforce, the report vigorously corrects this bias, documenting the highly productive contributions they continue to make. In countless households, older family members look after the young, transfer invaluable traditional and professional knowledge to the next generation, and actively engage in trade and community services essential to a functioning society. Consequently, a vast market known as the silver economy has emerged around them."
