    "chunk_text": "The IPS report turns a compassionate yet analytical lens onto the plight of Sri Lanka\u2019s rapidly ageing population, often referred to as the silvering people. While popular misconception often paints senior citizens as an economic burden who merely consume the goods produced by the younger workforce, the report vigorously corrects this bias, documenting the highly productive contributions they continue to make. In countless households, older family members look after the young, transfer invaluable traditional and professional knowledge to the next generation, and actively engage in trade and community services essential to a functioning society. Consequently, a vast market known as the silver economy has emerged around them.\n\nThese products are increasingly classified as age-brands, and the specialised field dedicated to developing them is formally known as gerontechnology\u2014or more colloquially, age-tech\u2014which uses technological solutions to improve the health, independence, and daily lives of older individuals. With Sri Lanka\u2019s demographic shift accelerating rapidly, ensuring the wellbeing of this massive group through dedicated economic planning must become a core national priority.\n\nIn my view, the IPS \u2018State of the Economy\u2019 report is far too precious a document to be ignored by those entrusted with shaping the economic future of this country. It is an indispensable roadmap, yet it suffers from one persistent, recurring vulnerability: it continues to cater almost exclusively to technically competent academics and data analysts. Not every decisionmaker, particularly within the volatile political arena, possesses the specialised training required to instantly digest its complex macroeconomic messaging and dense policy equations."
