    "md5": "1b5d074ec453b81fffc17ff54ceab43f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "Proposals for development of tourism infrastructure and facilities in Eastern coastal region\n\nUnder the current practice, a 1% levy is allocated to the SLTD and 1% to the relevant Local Authorities. In this regard, the new Budget proposal to remove the SLTD\u2019s 1% levy is a welcome and commendable initiative, as it will provide further support to the tourism sector.\n\nThe Eastern coastal region, home to popular destinations such as Pasikuda, Arugam Bay, Nilaveli and Trincomalee, has significant potential to contribute further to the country\u2019s tourism industry. However, gaps in infrastructure, public amenities, safety and environmental management continue to hold the region back. The following 10 proposals set out the key areas that require attention. The new Budget also offers welcome relief to the sector. Under the current practice, a 1% levy is allocated to the SLTD and 1% to the relevant Local Authorities. In this regard, the new Budget proposal to remove the SLTD\u2019s 1% levy is a welcome and commendable initiative, as it will provide further support to the tourism sector.\n\nA proper and sustainable beach cleaning and maintenance mechanism should be established in popular tourist destinations along the Eastern Coast, particularly Pasikuda, Arugam Bay and Nilaveli. These areas are among the country\u2019s prominent tourism destinations and attract a considerable number of local and foreign tourists. The relevant Local Government Authorities should be given responsibility and adequate resources to ensure regular beach cleaning, proper waste management and the maintenance of a clean and attractive coastal environment.\n\nChina Bay Airport and Batticaloa Airport should be further developed and upgraded to improve domestic air connectivity and, where feasible, facilitate international tourism. Such development would improve accessibility to the Eastern Province, encourage more tourists to visit the region and contribute to the overall growth of the country\u2019s tourism industry."
