    "md5": "a000150ce3bdc0c0f7354bbd92543ca7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "China Bay Airport and Batticaloa Airport should be further developed and upgraded to improve domestic air connectivity and, where feasible, facilitate international tourism. Such development would improve accessibility to the Eastern Province, encourage more tourists to visit the region and contribute to the overall growth of the country\u2019s tourism industry.\n\nTrincomalee Harbour, recognised as one of the world\u2019s finest natural harbours, has significant potential to become an important destination for passenger and tourism vessels. Facilities should be developed to accommodate passenger ships, cruise vessels and other tourism-related maritime activities. This would create new opportunities to attract international tourists, promote coastal tourism and strengthen the tourism economy of the Eastern Province.\n\nAllocation of a portion of Pigeon Island Boat Service revenue for local environmental facilities\n\nAt present, the Department of Wildlife Conservation operates boat services for tourists visiting Pigeon Island National Park in Trincomalee. However, adequate sanitary facilities and other basic public amenities are lacking in the surrounding area. It is therefore proposed that a portion of the revenue collected from these boat services be allocated to the relevant Local Government Authority to establish and maintain sanitary facilities, improve waste management and maintain clean beaches. This would ensure better environmental protection while improving the facilities available to tourists and local communities.\n\nAdequate Tourist Police facilities should be established in major tourist destinations across the Eastern Province. At present, there is a lack of properly equipped Tourist Police posts with even basic facilities. Therefore, the necessary infrastructure, accommodation, communication facilities and other essential requirements should be provided to enable Tourist Police officers to ensure the safety and security of tourists effectively."
  },
