    "md5": "c12bf556071ce95abdb61f56215310db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "The Pottuvil, Lahugala and Thirukkovil Divisional Secretariat Divisions should be officially identified, developed and declared as Tourism Zones, considering their tourism potential, natural attractions, coastal resources and cultural heritage. This initiative would help attract public and private sector investment, encourage the development of tourism-related infrastructure and facilities, create employment opportunities for local communities and promote new tourism destinations in the Eastern Province. It would also facilitate better coordination among relevant Government institutions in planning and implementing tourism development activities in these areas.\n\nAppropriate coastal protection measures should be implemented in Arugam Bay and Whisky Point to address the increasing threat of sea erosion. Geo-bags have already been installed along a 150-metre stretch in the area. However, it is urgently necessary to extend this protection by a further 450 metres along the vulnerable coastal stretches. This would help minimise further coastal erosion, protect tourism-related infrastructure and safeguard the coastal environment, which is essential for sustaining tourism activities in the area.\n\nA suitable seaplane landing facility should be developed in Arugam Bay to facilitate emergency and urgent air transportation between Arugam Bay and Colombo. This facility would be particularly beneficial for tourists who require urgent transportation due to medical emergencies or other unforeseen circumstances. It would also improve regional accessibility, enhance tourist safety and contribute to the further development of Arugam Bay as an international tourism destination.\n\n(The author is President of the Chamber of Tourism and Industry)"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-f5669dda",
    "num": "dailyftlk-f5669dda",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Why revenue dip amidst rising tourist arrivals is not necessarily a bad thing",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Why-revenue-dip-amidst-rising-tourist-arrivals-is-not-necessarily-a-bad-thing/4-800440",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795396641.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailyftlk-f5669dda-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "25f923578fc792d51cd35c0669ab46fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "Why revenue dip amidst rising tourist arrivals is not necessarily a bad thing\n\nNote: 2026 figure is an estimation as per the September 2026 SLTDA report\n\nAnnual tourist arrivals are expected to hit an all-time high, at 2.4 million by the end of 2026. However, the revenue numbers tell a different story. Per tourist spending has reduced dramatically. The average spending for 2026 is expected to be roughly the same.\n\nIf we pass the 2.4 million mark in 2026, it will be an all-time high.\n\nThe Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) in its External Sector Performance Report in February 2026, mentioned how the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority has changed their estimations based on Departure Surveys.\n\nNow, the SLTDA takes tourist arrivals, average stay period and average spending per day into account.\n\nThey have also considered the seasonality of average stay per tourist. Therefore, average spending per day is revised from August 2025.\n\nThese revisions will give a more accurate, rather low figure of spending than previous.\n\nLKR/USD Exchange Rate depreciated fast and service quality dropped due to rising inflation\n\nTherefore, fewer dollars are needed to purchase goods in Sri Lanka. The country was already grappling with many structural issues, mounting debt and the exchange rate ballooned out of control in 2022.\n\nAs the products get \u2018cheaper\u2019 more budget travelers would opt to visit. Also, the imported goods fetch a very high price, then the quality of service begins to drop, foreign service providers, travel operators pull out their investment due to uncertainty. \u2018High-end\u2019 tourism will no longer be supported.\n\nInformal sector transactions are very difficult to trace the flow. Most are cash and ledger books are not maintained. No records are kept. The services do not get captured in the GDP."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
