    "md5": "81cf14a7d5b92e17a7c662d55dd3fbbf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "As the products get \u2018cheaper\u2019 more budget travelers would opt to visit. Also, the imported goods fetch a very high price, then the quality of service begins to drop, foreign service providers, travel operators pull out their investment due to uncertainty. \u2018High-end\u2019 tourism will no longer be supported.\n\nInformal sector transactions are very difficult to trace the flow. Most are cash and ledger books are not maintained. No records are kept. The services do not get captured in the GDP.\n\nFor Indian and other Asian tourists, the cultural experience, the climate and surroundings are familiar to their home country. Therefore, the spending could be mostly on key, planned visit, or pre-determined at a package rate.\n\nHowever, \u2018unplanned\u2019 sporadic spending can be expected from the Western tourists, outside their basic needs, owing to the different cultural experience.\n\nThe money could be actually flowing into local communities, smallholder establishments\n\nAnother view is that the tourists are opting for smaller, informal service establishments/vendors. Their transactions are off record and mostly in cash. While State Revenue may not benefit, the local value chains could be thriving on this. However, there is little data to confirm this fact.\n\nIf the sector needs systematic, resilient growth, promoting digital payment methods is a must. It is not always about being captured for tax purposes, but also for transparency and safety of both service providers and the tourists.\n\nRather than solely relying on Tourists Surveys, a closer look of the characteristics of the service providers, amenities, surrounding can give a better perspective. And to cross-reference the survey data. Such a data driven exercise can give insights as to the quality of one of Sri Lanka\u2019s main export.\n\n(The author is an independent researcher in development and economic policy. The views expressed here are her own and do not necessarily reflect those of any organisation she is affiliated with)"
  },
  {
