    "chunk_text": "Hemas Holdings appoints Harikrishnan Sundaram to Mobility Sector Board\n\nFormer CWIT CEO and Adani Ports executive brings 24 years of ports, maritime and logistics leadership\n\nHemas Holdings PLC recently announced the appointment of Harikrishnan Sundaram to the Board of Directors of its Mobility Sector, with effect from 1 November 2026.\n\nThe appointment supports the Group\u2019s plan to grow its Mobility Sector, which covers shipping, logistics, maritime services and aviation. Hemas intends to do this through clear strategic direction, business development and the creation of new ventures.\n\nSundaram has built and run port infrastructure in Sri Lanka and across the region, and he has strong relationships throughout the global shipping community.\n\n\u201cHarikrishnan has built and operated port infrastructure at scale, including here in Colombo, and he has negotiated the kind of international partnerships we intend to pursue,\u201d said Hemas Holdings PLC Chairman-Mobility Sector Imtiaz Esufally. \u201cHis experience in governance, cross-border trade and supply chain connectivity will be invaluable as we sharpen our strategic direction and build lasting value across the maritime and logistics ecosystem,\u201d he added.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is well placed to become South Asia\u2019s leading transhipment and logistics hub. Hemas has the heritage, relationships and ambition to play a meaningful part in that growth, and I look forward to helping shape it,\u201d said Sundaram.\n\nSundaram has over 24 years of senior leadership experience in global ports, maritime and logistics. His main areas are strategic business development, commercial strategy, infrastructure investment and international operations. Most recently he was Business Head, Container Business at Adani Ports and Special Economic Zone Ltd. Before that he was Chief Executive Officer of Colombo West International Terminal (CWIT). He has led large infrastructure projects and business expansion across South Asia, the Middle East and East Africa."
  },
