    "md5": "a8df56129000841a0a2aa1508788ced3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1740,
    "chunk_text": "Illicit cigarettes cost Rs. 29.5 b in lost taxes in 2025: Oxford Economics\n\nIllicit share of cigarette market falls to 20% from 32% peak in 2024\n\nLegal cigarette value chain paid Rs. 181 b in taxes, 3.6% of Govt. tax revenue\n\nBeedi, outside the excise net, accounts for 73% of tobacco sticks smoked\n\nIllicit cigarettes cost the Government an estimated Rs. 29.5 billion in lost tax revenue in 2025, equal to 17% of the legal industry\u2019s direct tax contribution, even as the illicit share of the market fell to 20%, according to a report by Oxford Economics commissioned by Ceylon Tobacco Company PLC (CTC).\n\nThe report, \u2018The Economic Impact of the Cigarette Industry in Sri Lanka\u2019, estimates the loss at Rs. 24.6 billion in sales taxes, comprising Rs. 18.6 billion in excise duty, Rs. 5.4 billion in Value Added Tax (VAT), and Rs. 600 million in Social Security Contribution Levy (SSCL), plus Rs. 4.9 billion in corporate and labour income taxes. Had illicit products been unavailable, smokers would have bought an extra 303 million duty-paid cigarettes, the report said, based on what illicit smokers told a survey they would switch to.\n\nIllicit Factory Made Cigarettes (FMC), mostly smuggled into the country, accounted for an estimated 470 million sticks, or 20% of FMC consumption, in 2025, down from a peak of 32% in 2024, according to research by consultancy PepperCube. The illicit share stood at 15% in 2018, when it first emerged as a significant problem. The report attributed the decline to a sharp increase in illicit cigarette prices and a growing number of retailers unwilling to stock them due to Government enforcement, as well as a more moderate 5.9% excise increase in 2025, after hikes of 44% in 2023 and 14% in 2024."
  },
