    "chunk_text": "Illicit Factory Made Cigarettes (FMC), mostly smuggled into the country, accounted for an estimated 470 million sticks, or 20% of FMC consumption, in 2025, down from a peak of 32% in 2024, according to research by consultancy PepperCube. The illicit share stood at 15% in 2018, when it first emerged as a significant problem. The report attributed the decline to a sharp increase in illicit cigarette prices and a growing number of retailers unwilling to stock them due to Government enforcement, as well as a more moderate 5.9% excise increase in 2025, after hikes of 44% in 2023 and 14% in 2024.\n\n\u201cContinued efforts to reduce illicit trade could, therefore, help protect an important source of Government revenue,\u201d the report said.\n\nThe legal FMC value chain contributed an estimated Rs. 181 billion in taxes in 2025, equal to 3.6% of all Government tax revenue. CTC\u2019s own contribution was Rs. 177 billion, including Rs. 112 billion in excise duty, Rs. 33.4 billion in VAT, Rs. 24.7 billion in corporate tax, Rs. 3.7 billion in SSCL, and Rs. 3.2 billion in Withholding Tax on dividends. Taxes accounted for 82% of the average retail price of cigarettes. The report noted corporate tax on the sector was raised to 45% from 40% in 2024, which it described as one of the highest rates in the world for the tobacco sector.\n\nCigarette-related sales taxes accounted for 2.7% of Sri Lanka\u2019s total tax and non-tax revenue, against 1.8% in Pakistan, 1.4% in India, and a global median of 1%, based on World Health Organisation\n\nThe report said this revenue matters given the fiscal outlook. The Budget deficit narrowed to 2.3% of GDP in 2025 from 11.7% in 2021, but Oxford Economics projects it to widen to 5.2% in 2026 and 5.8% in 2027, following Cyclone Ditwah in late 2025 and the early 2026 Middle East conflict. \u201cAgainst this background, an unexpected decline in cigarette-related revenues could make fiscal consolidation more difficult,\u201d it said."
