    "chunk_size_bytes": 1559,
    "chunk_text": "The report said this revenue matters given the fiscal outlook. The Budget deficit narrowed to 2.3% of GDP in 2025 from 11.7% in 2021, but Oxford Economics projects it to widen to 5.2% in 2026 and 5.8% in 2027, following Cyclone Ditwah in late 2025 and the early 2026 Middle East conflict. \u201cAgainst this background, an unexpected decline in cigarette-related revenues could make fiscal consolidation more difficult,\u201d it said.\n\n\u201cStrengthening enforcement and compliance measures could help bring beedies into the tax net and provide an additional source of revenue to the Government,\u201d the report said.\n\nThe industry\u2019s total contribution to GDP was estimated at Rs. 74 billion, or 0.2% of national output, comprising a direct contribution of Rs. 61.2 billion, Rs. 6.5 billion from Rs. 8.1 billion in domestic procurement, and Rs. 6.3 billion from wage-funded spending. The direct contribution is comparable to that of cereals (Rs. 59.5 billion) and rubber (Rs. 65.6 billion).\n\nThe value chain supported 81,400 jobs, or 1% of national employment. These include 62,700 jobs across 74,600 retail outlets, 14,600 leaf farmers and associated workers, 1,100 jobs in 11 distribution companies, and 301 CTC employees. CTC, the country\u2019s sole legal cigarette manufacturer, produced 1.92 billion cigarettes in 2025 using 1.5 million kilograms of tobacco leaf sourced from 884 contracted farmers and 5,610 sub-growers.\n\n\u201cThe analysis does not consider the broader social and health impacts of tobacco consumption, whether legal or illicit,\u201d the report said."
