    "md5": "53b7f0dc9fbb542094539e5ee0f3765a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Global climate summit collapses as countries fail to agree on moving away from fossil fuels\n\nCNN - The COP31 climate conference in Antalya, Turkey, collapsed Sunday as countries failed to agree to an overarching deal after more than two weeks of contentious negotiations. The breaking point was over the very cause of climate change itself: fossil fuels.\n\nThe annual UN climate talks, which stretched deep into overtime over the weekend, hold the dubious honor of being the longest COP in history and the first major breakdown since COP15 in Copenhagen in 2009.\n\nExperts criticized the conference as a stunning failure of leadership at a time when the climate crisis is accelerating with increasingly deadly consequences. \u201cThis has been the worst ever COP and its collapse needs to be a wake up call for the world,\u201d said Mohamed Adow, director of the energy think tank Power Shift Africa.\n\nAt the final meeting intended to approve the so-called Antalya Pact on Sunday, it became clear there would not be the necessary consensus between the more than 190 countries at the talks to adopt the deal. These did not include the US, which, under President Donald Trump, has pulled out of international climate action.\n\nDeep divisions proved impossible to bridge over key issues \u2014 most importantly, including language that the world would need to move away from fossil fuels, a phrase that did not end up in the agreement, and increasing financial support for countries already grappling with climate catastrophes. Petrostates, including Saudi Arabia, have long opposed the inclusion of any reference to fossil fuels.\n\nThe European Union, Panama, the Marshall Islands and Norway were among those that rejected it. Darragh O\u2019Brien, Ireland\u2019s climate minister who is helping lead negotiations for the EU, said the bloc would not endorse a deal \u201cthat does not even mention fossil fuels.\u201d\n\n\u201cThis COP, I believe, was hijacked by some oil states,\u201d he later told Reuters news agency."
