    "md5": "95d6275cdf5bddabde5f23ba23288f02",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "The European Union, Panama, the Marshall Islands and Norway were among those that rejected it. Darragh O\u2019Brien, Ireland\u2019s climate minister who is helping lead negotiations for the EU, said the bloc would not endorse a deal \u201cthat does not even mention fossil fuels.\u201d\n\n\u201cThis COP, I believe, was hijacked by some oil states,\u201d he later told Reuters news agency.\n\nChina, which is the world\u2019s biggest climate polluter but also a clean energy superpower, called the lack of consensus a \u201cdeep disappointment.\u201d Chinese delegation head Li Gao said the outcome shouldn\u2019t \u201clead to a loss of confidence\u201d in countries\u2019 ability to work together to address climate change, Chinese state media Xinhua reported.\n\nThe talks come at a critical time for the planet. While attention is diverted by geopolitical turbulence \u2014 including the Iran war, which has caused the world\u2019s largest energy supply shock \u2014 the climate crisis is accelerating.\n\nA sobering recent UN report confirmed the world will overshoot its target of limiting global warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels. On current policies temperatures are expected to rise to a potentially catastrophic 2.8 degrees. The planet is currently at around 1.4 degrees of warming and the impacts have been starkly clear, from catastrophic flooding in Nepal to deadly heat in Europe.\n\nYet, COP31 laid bare the enormous gap between those seeking ambitious action, often the most climate-vulnerable countries, and the oil-rich nations pushing back.\n\nIn common with previous COPs, the biggest sticking point came down to two words: fossil fuels.\n\nAt COP28 in Dubai in 2023, countries agreed to an unprecedented call to \u201ctransition away from fossil fuels.\u201d Since then, this has neither been repeated nor built upon as oil-producing nations balk at any reference to the driving force of the climate crisis."
  },
  {
