    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b7dde154d837b276befdd2c42d24ab2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2026,
    "chunk_text": "At COP28 in Dubai in 2023, countries agreed to an unprecedented call to \u201ctransition away from fossil fuels.\u201d Since then, this has neither been repeated nor built upon as oil-producing nations balk at any reference to the driving force of the climate crisis.\n\nBut all mentions of fossil fuels were stripped out of every subsequent draft, despite a push by more than 100 countries to keep them in. \u201cYou cannot solve a fossil fuel crisis by pretending we don\u2019t need to phase out fossil fuels,\u201d said Harjeet Singh, COP negotiations veteran and founding director of Satat Sampada Climate Foundation.\n\nBritain\u2019s lead climate negotiator Kate Hughes said the proposed agreement \u201cmoves us backwards,\u201d describing it as \u201ca lowest common denominator, where it should have been instead presenting bold, collective ambition on climate action.\u201d\n\nMeanwhile, Amnesty International\u2019s Climate Justice policy adviser, Ann Harrison, said countries were \u201cright to reject\u201d what she called a \u201cpolluters\u2019 charter designed to protect corporate profits.\u201d\n\nCountries and climate groups were also concerned by the agreement\u2019s reference to increasing carbon capture technologies, which would allow for a continuation of fossil fuel production provided its carbon pollution is captured before it reaches the atmosphere.\n\nWhile many scientists say the amount of carbon pollution means this technology will be needed to some extent, it remains expensive, unproven at scale and criticized as a dangerous distraction from climate policies that tackle the root cause of the crisis.\n\n\u201cRelying on carbon capture without phasing down fossil fuels is like trying to quit smoking without putting down the cigarettes,\u201d said Matt Webb, associate director for global clean power diplomacy at E3G, a climate think tank.\n\nAnother huge issue of contention was finance. Poorer, climate-vulnerable countries have long called for more money to help them adapt to climate disasters they disproportionately face and yet did little to cause."
  },
