    "md5": "a098a565e2f4d4b5e46131be1b15893a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1044,
    "chunk_text": "Another huge issue of contention was finance. Poorer, climate-vulnerable countries have long called for more money to help them adapt to climate disasters they disproportionately face and yet did little to cause.\n\n\u201cRich countries refused to put money on the table to help communities prepare for and recover from climate disasters, and handed polluters a free pass instead,\u201d Singh said. \u201cNo deal is better than a deal that locks in failure.\u201d\n\nCOP31 president of negotiations, Chris Bowen, said the result was very disappointing but there were \u201ca lot of good things\u201d to come out of the talks, including more than 90 countries signing a pledge to ramp up the electrification of their economies.\n\nStill, many experts expressed bitter disappointment at the world\u2019s failure to meet the urgency of the climate crisis.\n\n\u201cRecognition of the dangers of overshoot should have shaken leaders into action, instead COP31 saw a shameful lack of urgency and leadership,\u201d said Mary Robinson, the former president of Ireland and a COP veteran."
