    "md5": "c0f4b98a888c4533f4a5ef5592393d72",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1867,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis midterm election showed a politics of human rights that includes Palestinians is a winning platform,\u201d said Institute for Middle East Understanding policy project executive director Margaret DeReus. \u201cFor millions of Americans, holding Israel accountable for genocide and supporting Palestinian freedom are the litmus test.\u201d\n\n\u201cIt felt like being pro-Palestinian was the most important thing to run on.\u201d\n\nKawas\u2019s parents were uprooted from their homes in Palestine during the mass expulsions. Her father was uprooted in 1948 and her mother in 1967. They grew up as neighbours in Jordan before falling in love, marrying and moving to the US with their infant son in the 1990s. They later had three daughters. Aber is the third child of the four, sandwiched between her two sisters. \u201cWhich is why I have a lot of politician skills,\u201d she said, laughing.\n\nBut when she was in the seventh grade, her father was detained by US Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), which her mother described as a regular occurrence in New York\u2019s post-9/11 Muslim community. His detention meant weekend visits to prison, and it left the family struggling.\n\n\u201cI had to work whether I wanted to or not; you have to become both man and woman, father and mother,\u201d her mother, Manal Abu Samaha, told Al Jazeera.\n\nWhen Kawas was a teenager, she said she travelled to Albany to tell her family\u2019s story, hoping to expand opportunities for undocumented students like her older brother, who was ineligible for a college scholarship. Lawmakers seemed uninterested, Kawas recalls.\n\nThe state senator-elect credits the experience with launching her advocacy career and shaping her goals as a politician. \u201cIt\u2019s not upending systems, but it\u2019s meeting real, immediate material needs for people,\u201d she said. \u201cThat\u2019s maybe a little kernel of what got me to run.\u201d"
  },
