    "chunk_text": "The state senator-elect credits the experience with launching her advocacy career and shaping her goals as a politician. \u201cIt\u2019s not upending systems, but it\u2019s meeting real, immediate material needs for people,\u201d she said. \u201cThat\u2019s maybe a little kernel of what got me to run.\u201d\n\nKawas recalled that, while Mamdani was warned that supporting Palestine would end his political career, the stance instead helped him earn voters\u2019 trust. Seeing this, she said, made her less wary that entering politics would require her to compromise her beliefs.\n\n\u201cI voted for Aber Kawas because Palestinian liberation is not separate from the everyday survival and basic needs that Americans and New Yorkers struggle with,\u201d said community organiser Doreen Mohammed. \u201cAber Kawas\u2019s vocal support for Palestine is what we need more of everywhere.\u201d\n\nKawas told Al Jazeera she believes her stance on Palestine helped her earn trust from local leaders. Whether it can change US policy is a harder question.\n\n\u201cMany of us are rightfully cautious, if not outright sceptical, when it comes to electoral politics,\u201d said Samee Sulaiman, an anthropologist at Columbia University who studies the Middle East. \u201cCan we build and use this nascent power to finally end American support of Israel in every way? This will be the real test.\u201d\n\nKawas acknowledged that a lot will depend on the political will of legislative leaders.\n\n\u201cThe challenge, truly, is going to be the political will of the leadership,\u201d she said. \u201cWe [the DSA] are still a minority in the legislature.\u201d\n\nHer first priority is reviving the Not on Our Dime! bill, which she drafted with then-Assembly member Zohran Mamdani in 2023 to prohibit New York non-profits from supporting illegal Israeli settlement activity in the occupied Palestinian territory. Rejected at the time, it is now back before the New York state Senate.\n\nKawas said she remains convinced change is possible, pointing to the activists who helped bring an end to apartheid in South Africa."
