    "md5": "ee3d58e20d49c9a7bc8377ef9ec0d9a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka must learn to save its rain\n\nSri Lanka has never been short of rain. What it has repeatedly lacked is the foresight to save enough of it for everyone.\n\nEvery year, the monsoons arrive, rivers swell and reservoirs fill. In some areas, rain falls in torrents, causing floods and sending water rushing towards the sea. Yet, when the dry months arrive, families in several districts queue for water, farmers watch crops fail and authorities send bowsers to communities whose wells have run dry. This should shame a country whose agriculture and civilisation have depended on water for centuries.\n\nWater is fast becoming one of the world\u2019s most critical resources. Climate change is making rainfall less predictable, droughts more severe and competition for freshwater more intense. Population growth is adding pressure. For Sri Lanka, this is not a distant warning. The question is no longer whether Sri Lanka should invest in rainwater harvesting. It is why the country has failed to make it a national priority.\n\nMore than half of Sri Lanka\u2019s annual rainfall ultimately flows into the sea without productive use, according to the country\u2019s climate planning documents. Not every drop should be captured; rivers and ecosystems need water. But allowing so much seasonal rainfall to pass through the country without making better use of it, while other communities face shortages, is a failure of planning.\n\nSri Lanka has had policies on paper for years. A National Rainwater Harvesting Policy and Strategies was adopted in 2005, while later building regulations require harvesting facilities in many new developments within designated urban areas. Community initiatives also exist. In May this year, the Government announced a pilot scheme\u00a0 to install systems in at least 100 state institutions in Colombo, initially for non-drinking uses."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-409acb51",
