    "md5": "83c260b1585fc186c8f2b9f77edc2fee",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has had policies on paper for years. A National Rainwater Harvesting Policy and Strategies was adopted in 2005, while later building regulations require harvesting facilities in many new developments within designated urban areas. Community initiatives also exist. In May this year, the Government announced a pilot scheme\u00a0 to install systems in at least 100 state institutions in Colombo, initially for non-drinking uses.\n\nDistricts that repeatedly experience drought must be at the centre of that plan. Northern, North Central, North Western, Eastern and Uva communities know what it means to live with uncertain rainfall and depleted wells. In September, drought had affected more than 169,000 people across 10 districts, with water bowsers supplying large numbers of households. Rainwater harvesting can be expanded at several levels. Homes, schools, hospitals, offices, factories and apartments should collect roof water in properly designed tanks. Public buildings could use this water for cleaning, sanitation and gardening. In rural areas, household tanks and community storage can provide a reserve through dry periods.\n\nAgriculture must be part of the same strategy. Small village reservoirs, restored tanks, farm ponds, check dams and better catchment management can retain water and recharge groundwater. Existing tanks and irrigation networks must be maintained. Farmers need support to adopt water-efficient irrigation and plan cultivation around reliable water availability. Harvested rainwater can supplement irrigation, protect crops during dry spells and reduce dependence on emergency deliveries.\n\nCollecting water is only part of the task. Storage must be safe and suited to local conditions. Water intended for drinking must be tested and treated appropriately. Authorities must map rainfall, identify suitable sites and protect downstream communities and ecosystems. The expertise exists. What is missing is the political commitment to coordinate it."
  },
