    "md5": "9261cf56a6c9f49e24e48251437975b2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "Price of Looking Powerful: Trump\u2019s Uneasy Summit in Shenzhen\n\n(L-R) Russian President Vladimir Putin, Chinese President Xi Jinping and US President Donald Trump at the Shenzhen Summit (Photo credit -- Vyacheslav Prokofyev/Sputnik/ via Reuters).\n\nYet defenders must answer a key question: what is the point of such talks if they bring no pressure on the other side, no concessions in return and no drop in danger? Personal friendships cannot replace national interests\n\nAnd did Washington keep its technology lead while dealing with China? These questions matter more than the words used to describe the summit\n\nDonald Trump came back from Shenzhen calling his meeting with Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin \u201chistoric\u201d. In one way, he was right. The leaders of the United States, China and Russia had never before sat around the same table. The photographs were striking, the meaning was plain, and the chance for talks was significant. Yet photographs do not make history, and a meeting is not a success just because everyone was in the room.\n\nThe more important question is what, if anything, came out of it. On what we know so far, the answer is disappointing. There was no breakthrough on Ukraine, no real agreement on nuclear weapons and no announced settlement of the wider tensions over Iran. A declaration towards peace used familiar words about cooperation and calm, but offered little that can be checked against firm promises. For a president who put so much political weight on this meeting, the gain looks small.\n\nThat does not mean the summit was worthless. When three nuclear-armed countries are led by men with sharply different aims, keeping the lines of talk open is valuable in itself. A mistake can be more dangerous than a quarrel, and talks often begin with conversations that settle nothing straight away. But opening a door is one thing. Letting others walk through it carrying gains for themselves is another."
  },
