    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4dc8fcb4aeae7e9d8794b6f7417eaec4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "That does not mean the summit was worthless. When three nuclear-armed countries are led by men with sharply different aims, keeping the lines of talk open is valuable in itself. A mistake can be more dangerous than a quarrel, and talks often begin with conversations that settle nothing straight away. But opening a door is one thing. Letting others walk through it carrying gains for themselves is another.\n\nPutin wanted something different. Russia\u2019s invasion of Ukraine has cost it heavily in lives and money, while Western governments have tried to shrink Moscow\u2019s standing in the world. Yet there was Putin, seated beside the leaders of the two richest and strongest countries. The picture let the Kremlin say that Russia was neither alone nor stripped of its place among the great powers. The danger is mistaking being seen for achieving something. Putin may have gained respect without giving up much. If so, the result is troubling: Russia gets recognition while Ukraine keeps suffering from a war it did not start.\n\nEurope has good reason to feel uneasy. While the Shenzhen meeting was going on, European leaders met in Berlin to discuss how to respond to a changing world. Their worry was not simply that Trump was talking to Putin. It was that Washington might strike a deal with Moscow and then expect Europe and Ukraine to live with the results.\n\nAccording to reports of Trump\u2019s call with European leaders, he described Putin as tired of the war and keen for a deal. That may be true. But hopeful talk should not be mistaken for proof that Russia has changed course. Putin\u2019s public words gave little sign that Moscow has dropped its main demands. Indeed, his insistence that European officials must come to Moscow and win him over if they want a part in future talks hardly suggests a man ready to meet European worries."
  },
