    "md5": "bf9942ac41d163e6c1fd22a45650c9c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1614,
    "chunk_text": "According to reports of Trump\u2019s call with European leaders, he described Putin as tired of the war and keen for a deal. That may be true. But hopeful talk should not be mistaken for proof that Russia has changed course. Putin\u2019s public words gave little sign that Moscow has dropped its main demands. Indeed, his insistence that European officials must come to Moscow and win him over if they want a part in future talks hardly suggests a man ready to meet European worries.\n\nTechnology adds another side to Shenzhen. America\u2019s lead over China goes beyond military and economic size to include advanced computer chips, artificial intelligence and key public networks. Limits on advanced American chips are therefore not just trade rules; they are tools of national security.\n\nAny decision to ease those limits needs close checking. If Washington lets China get technology that could strengthen its military or speed up its plans, it may be swapping a long-term edge for short-term profit or goodwill. But claims about Nvidia and American chip sales must be proven with care. Which products are involved? What rules have changed? What safeguards remain? These are questions for evidence, not slogans.\n\nTrump\u2019s defenders have a fair argument. Personal diplomacy can achieve what formal talks cannot. A president willing to speak straight to rivals may find openings that career diplomats, tied to fixed positions, cannot easily explore. Also, there is no public proof that Washington gave anything away at Shenzhen. Critics should not mistake an awkward photograph or a friendly remark for proof of surrender."
