    "chunk_size_bytes": 1749,
    "chunk_text": "Trump\u2019s defenders have a fair argument. Personal diplomacy can achieve what formal talks cannot. A president willing to speak straight to rivals may find openings that career diplomats, tied to fixed positions, cannot easily explore. Also, there is no public proof that Washington gave anything away at Shenzhen. Critics should not mistake an awkward photograph or a friendly remark for proof of surrender.\n\nThis is the central puzzle of the Shenzhen summit. Trump wants to show strength by bringing powerful leaders together. But looking influential is not the same as using influence. A president can hold the world\u2019s attention and still leave a meeting with nothing of lasting value.\n\nThe weeks ahead will show what really happened. Did Ukraine receive real promises of protection? Were European governments truly included in talks that shape their future? Did Russia show willingness to compromise, rather than simply demand more? And did Washington keep its technology lead while dealing with China? These questions matter more than the words used to describe the summit. Words such as historic, successful and unprecedented come easily to politicians; real gains are harder to prove.\n\nThe real test of diplomacy is not who smiled, hosted or stood beside whom, but who gained, who gave way and what dangers were lessened. If Trump won talks without giving up American interests, he may have achieved something worthwhile. But if Xi and Putin gained respect, influence and a free hand while Washington got little beyond applause and photographs, the picture will look different. In Shenzhen, three leaders met, and the world watched. The final verdict will depend not on the show, but on the price America, Europe and Ukraine may pay for it."
