    "chunk_text": "U.S. scrutiny of Sri Lanka likely if GSP renewal is taken up \u2013 Blake\n\nColombo, Nov. 23 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Former U.S. Ambassador to Sri Lanka Robert O. Blake Jr. said that Sri Lanka will be under renewed scrutiny by the Democrats -controlled U.S. Congress if it decides to take up GSP renewal, as many Democrats would be likely to push stronger worker rights provisions and potentially add eligibility criteria covering issues like environmental issues and human rights. However, he said it would be lower down the priority list, in his opinion.\n\nAlso, he proposed the appointment of a new ambassador toe the U.S. as soon as possible since engagement is needed with new members of the Congress who know nothing about Sri Lanka.\n\nDelivering remarks online during a webinar organized by Pathfinder Foundation on the recently conducted U.S. midterm elections titled \u2018America Votes, South Asia Adjusts\u2019, the former ambassador said he visited Sri Lanka last month as part of an Asia Foundation board delegation and met Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya , Foreign Minister Vijitha Herath and a wide range of other people.\n\nHe said that some Sri Lankan friends asked him about prospects for the next Congress to renew GSP, which has lapsed since 2020.\n\n\u201cAs many of your listeners may know, GSP provides duty-free treatment for thousands of products imported from designated developing countries, including Sri Lanka. And the answer to the question is, it's not clear how much of a priority this will really be in the next Congress.\n\nMy guess is it'll be lower down the list of priorities. One argument in favour is it would help lower costs for consumers by bringing in cheaper imports and therefore it would help the affordability agenda of the Democrats. However, if the Democrats do decide to take up GSP renewal, many Democrats would be likely to push stronger worker rights provisions and potentially add eligibility criteria covering issues like environmental issues and human rights."
  },
