    "chunk_text": "My guess is it'll be lower down the list of priorities. One argument in favour is it would help lower costs for consumers by bringing in cheaper imports and therefore it would help the affordability agenda of the Democrats. However, if the Democrats do decide to take up GSP renewal, many Democrats would be likely to push stronger worker rights provisions and potentially add eligibility criteria covering issues like environmental issues and human rights.\n\nHe said as Sri Lanka now prepares for the new Congress, he would recommend several steps to improve prospects for garnering support from the new Congress.\n\nFirst, it's really important for the government to appoint a new ambassador. I think Mahinda Samarasinghe did a great job representing his country and was very active both in the executive branch and on the Hill. And so we'll need somebody who is prepared to act with similar vigour to represent Sri Lanka's interests both on the Hill and in the executive branch. And of course, it'll be very, very important to engage a lot of these new members, many of whom know absolutely nothing about Sri Lanka.\n\nSecond, while President Dissanayake has made important progress on addressing corruption, I think the new Democratic Congress is likely to scrutinise the human rights records of trading partners much more closely than the last Congress did. And so I think there are steps that Sri Lanka could take to sort of improve its standing and improve its prospects for eligibility for GSP. And these are steps that I think are politically feasible, given the strong support that President Dissanayake continues to enjoy,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said that the number one on the list should be the repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act."
