    "chunk_text": "Second, while President Dissanayake has made important progress on addressing corruption, I think the new Democratic Congress is likely to scrutinise the human rights records of trading partners much more closely than the last Congress did. And so I think there are steps that Sri Lanka could take to sort of improve its standing and improve its prospects for eligibility for GSP. And these are steps that I think are politically feasible, given the strong support that President Dissanayake continues to enjoy,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said that the number one on the list should be the repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act.\n\nAnd I hope that that will help to reduce a lot of the extraordinary powers that those security services now enjoy. I do think it will be important to take some steps on reconciliation. And so that would include possibly things like demilitarising the North and East, returning some of the seized lands, and then work towards devolution of power.\n\nAgain, these are all longstanding issues that have been promised and shouldn't be that difficult to do. And again, President Dissanayake has quite important support still in the parliament. Yeah, let me stop right there,\u201d he said."
