    "md5": "b5f41014898c202a71a663fcbe1b3e9e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "Smartphones linked to concerns over sleep and productivity\n\nColombo, Nov. 23 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Smartphones have become an increasingly unavoidable part of everyday life, but spending too much time on these devices is emerging as a growing concern, particularly among younger generations, with many adults admitting they struggle to cut back on their screen time.\n\nAccording to a survey conducted by the Pew Research Center in May and June 2026, just over half of adults in the United States said they spend too much time on their smartphones.\n\nAround one-third said their usage was about right, while only 3% believed they spent too little time using the devices.\n\nYounger adults were more likely to express concerns about their smartphone habits, with 70% of those under 30 saying they spent too much time on their devices. The figure was around two-thirds among adults aged 30 to 49, approximately half among those aged 50 to 64 and a quarter among those aged 65 and above.\n\nThe findings also showed that 45% of adults had attempted to reduce their smartphone use over the previous 12 months, while 46% had not made any such effort.\n\nAmong those who felt they spent too much time on their smartphones, 62% said they had tried to cut back.\n\nHowever, reducing screen time has not been easy for everyone. Only 25% of those who attempted to limit their smartphone use said they were extremely or very successful. Another 52% described their efforts as somewhat successful, while 23% said they had been unsuccessful.\n\nAdults under 50 were considerably more likely to have attempted to limit their smartphone use than older age groups.\n\nDespite concerns over excessive usage, smartphones remain a constant presence in daily life, with around one in four adults saying they use their devices almost constantly. Another 60% said they use their smartphones several times a day."
  },
