    "chunk_size_bytes": 1397,
    "chunk_text": "Despite concerns over excessive usage, smartphones remain a constant presence in daily life, with around one in four adults saying they use their devices almost constantly. Another 60% said they use their smartphones several times a day.\n\nThe survey also examined how smartphone use affects different aspects of people\u2019s lives, including their ability to stay informed, maintain relationships, productivity, mood and sleep.\n\nAround three-quarters of adults said smartphones helped them stay informed, while approximately half said the devices helped them feel connected to others.\n\nViews were more divided on productivity and mood, with respondents reporting both positive and negative effects.\n\nSleep, however, emerged as the area most negatively affected by smartphone use. Around 41% said their smartphones had a negative impact on the amount of sleep they received, compared with just 5% who said the devices helped their sleep. Nearly half said smartphones had no impact.\n\nThe impact on sleep was particularly evident among younger adults, with 62% of those under 30 saying smartphones negatively affected their sleep. Around half of those aged 30 to 49 also reported a similar effect.\n\nYounger adults were also more likely to say their smartphone use negatively affected their productivity, while those under 50 were more likely than older adults to report an adverse impact on their mood."
