    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Balancing Security and Rights:  The Evolution of Sri Lanka\u2019s Anti-Terrorism Framework",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Balancing-Security-and-Rights-The-Evolution-of-Sri-Lankas-Anti-Terrorism-Framework/172-355526",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795412028.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-93c557cf-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "eed2195037a8de660e24594e8479218c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "Balancing Security and Rights:  The Evolution of Sri Lanka\u2019s Anti-Terrorism Framework\n\nWhile the memory of the 2019 Easter Sunday attacks drives strong support for heightened security, there is an equally strong demand for the protection of democratic values (Photo credit Ishara S. Kodikara AFP).\n\nAs Sri Lanka navigates the complex landscape of national security in a modern, digitised world, the legal mechanisms designed to combat terrorism are undergoing a pivotal transformation. The transition from the long-standing Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA) to the proposed Anti-Terrorism Act (PSTA) represents a critical juncture in the nation\u2019s pursuit of a framework that effectively addresses extremist threats while upholding the fundamental pillars of human rights and the rule of law.\n\nFor decades, the PTA has served as the primary instrument for addressing terrorism, yet it has been frequently criticised for its broad, often vague definitions of terrorist offenses. These ambiguities have historically provided authorities with significant discretionary power, leading to concerns regarding arbitrary application.\n\nThe proposed PSTA aims to rectify these flaws by introducing a more precise delineation of terrorism-related offenses. By focusing on acts with a clear impact on national security and public order, the PSTA seeks to minimise the potential for misuse. Crucially, the new framework recognises the realities of the digital age, incorporating provisions to address cyberterrorism and online radicalisation, thereby modernising the state\u2019s response to contemporary challenges.\n\nPerhaps the most significant departures from the status quo lie in the procedural changes regarding detention and confession admissibility. The PTA has long been criticised for allowing detention for up to 18 months without trial, a practice that has fostered a culture of uncertainty."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-93c557cf",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-93c557cf",
    "date_str": "2026-11-23",
    "description": "Balancing Security and Rights:  The Evolution of Sri Lanka\u2019s Anti-Terrorism Framework",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Balancing-Security-and-Rights-The-Evolution-of-Sri-Lankas-Anti-Terrorism-Framework/172-355526",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795412028.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-93c557cf-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3df6f20531c272089777c5b2efbf1fa5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Perhaps the most significant departures from the status quo lie in the procedural changes regarding detention and confession admissibility. The PTA has long been criticised for allowing detention for up to 18 months without trial, a practice that has fostered a culture of uncertainty.\n\nJudicial oversight is similarly strengthened. Where the PTA allowed for significant law enforcement latitude without prior court approval, the PSTA requires judicial authorization for key actions, including surveillance and detention. This systemic change is aimed at restoring public trust by ensuring that security measures remain subject to independent legal review.\n\nThe debate surrounding the PSTA is not merely technical\u2014it is deeply philosophical. Proponents argue that the modernisation of these laws is essential for preemptive action against evolving threats. By streamlining intelligence gathering and inter-agency coordination, the PSTA promises a more proactive security posture.\n\nHowever, civil liberties advocates remain cautious. The expansive powers granted to the state, even with improved oversight, raise concerns about potential infringements on privacy and freedom of expression. There is a palpable fear that without robust checks and balances, these new tools could be used to suppress dissent rather than to counter genuine threats. Legal experts emphasise that the success of the PSTA will depend not just on the text of the law, but on its implementation and the prevention of discriminatory enforcement, particularly against marginalised communities.\n\nPublic opinion remains divided. While the memory of the 2019 Easter Sunday attacks drives strong support for heightened security, there is an equally strong demand for the protection of democratic values. The media and civil society organisations have become vital conduits for this national conversation, pushing for a narrative that views safety and freedom as complementary rather than mutually exclusive."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-93c557cf",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-93c557cf",
