    "chunk_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-93c557cf-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "851371c69958aedfb14017ec8e01ceb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "Therefore, they should consider how to improve the legal system to address the challenges of terrorism without introducing separate acts like the PSTA. Several countries have transitioned away from standalone emergency or special terrorism legislation, opting instead to integrate terrorism-related offenses into their broader criminal codes to ensure consistency with standard rule-of-law principles. Here are some examples:\n\nCanada:\u00a0 Following the expiration of the Anti-terrorism Act (which was enacted post-9/11 as a temporary measure), the Canadian government integrated permanent counter-terrorism provisions directly into the Criminal Code of Canada. By doing so, Canada ensured that terrorism offenses are prosecuted within the same judicial framework as other major crimes, subjecting them to the same constitutional standards, rules of evidence, and procedural safeguards provided by the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.\n\nAustralia: Australia has moved toward consolidating its security laws. While it maintains a robust set of counter-terrorism offenses, the legislative trend has been to ensure that these offenses sit within the Criminal Code Act 1995. This integration allows the judiciary to apply established principles of criminal liability, such as the requirement of mens rea (intent), in a manner consistent with non-terrorism related criminal prosecution.\n\nNew Zealand: After the 2019 Christchurch attacks, New Zealand reviewed its counter-terrorism framework. Rather than creating a sprawling, independent \u201cspecial\u201d act that exists outside the standard legal system, the government focused on amending the Terrorism Suppression Act 2002 to better interface with the Crimes Act 1961. This approach ensures that when the state prosecutes terrorism, it does so use the standard, transparent procedures of the general criminal legal system, thereby preventing the creation of a \u201cparallel\u201d justice system."
  },
