    "chunk_size_bytes": 1317,
    "chunk_text": "New Zealand: After the 2019 Christchurch attacks, New Zealand reviewed its counter-terrorism framework. Rather than creating a sprawling, independent \u201cspecial\u201d act that exists outside the standard legal system, the government focused on amending the Terrorism Suppression Act 2002 to better interface with the Crimes Act 1961. This approach ensures that when the state prosecutes terrorism, it does so use the standard, transparent procedures of the general criminal legal system, thereby preventing the creation of a \u201cparallel\u201d justice system.\n\nAll these examples prove that the improvements made to existing criminal litigation frameworks allow countries to litigate terrorism effectively. However, if a country has a hidden agenda to suppress its political opposition, that is the main reason for introducing such anti-terrorism legislation.The pursuit of democracy, the rule of law, and good governance is essential for a stable society. Many advocates for these principles argue that a transparent and accountable government is the most effective framework for addressing complex security challenges, such as terrorism and organised crime.\n\n(The Writer is a Human Rights Activist and Strategy Advisor to International Organisations on Protection of Human Rights\u00a0 and Right Based Development Approaches)."
