    "md5": "2b7e122f5af8e92fbb2e46e13aec38e2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "Saman Srilal, Chairman of the CA Sri Lanka Faculty of Taxation, observed that the Budget places considerable emphasis on productive sectors of\u00a0 the\u00a0 economy\u00a0 throughinvestments in export-oriented manufacturing,\u00a0 tourism,agriculture, technology, digitalisation and SMEs. He welcomed several tax measures including personal income tax relief, investment incentives to encourage export-oriented projects, and initiatives aimed at strengthening tax administration and improving compliance processes.\n\nDelivering the budget highlights, Sulaiman Nishtar, Partner and Head of Tax at EY Sri Lanka, provided a detailed overview\u00a0 of\u00a0 the\u00a0 Budget proposals and the legislative process.\n\nRepresenting\u00a0 \u00a0 the Government\u2019s\u00a0 policy perspective, Tanuja Perera, Tax Policy Advisor at the Ministry of Finance, provided insights into several tax proposals announced in the reforms relating to digital and cross-border taxation.\n\nAddressing developments in tax administration, P. Nandan Kumara, Commissioner of the Inland Revenue Department, outlined ongoing efforts to modernise the tax system through\u00a0 \u00a0digitalisation, improved taxpayer services and greater inter-agency integration.\n\nThe panel discussion further featured contributions from Nisreen Rehmanjee, Vice President and Head of Corporate Finance, Group Tax and Social Entrepreneurship at John Keells Holdings PLC, who shared practical perspectives from the corporate sector on tax compliance, taxpayer facilitation and broadening the tax base."
  },
