    "chunk_text": "Democrats win largest House majority since 1992 after flipping North Carolina seat\n\nBBC - The Democratic Party has won the largest majority in the US House of Representatives since 1992, after Democrat Chuck Hubbard unseated Republican Representative Virginia Foxx in North Carolina.\n\nThe race was called on Sunday, with Hubbard beating Foxx by 330 votes with 99% counted. It was one of the closest contests of the recent midterm elections, in which Democrats also secured the Senate.\n\nThe party has now won at least 258 House seats in the midterms compared to Republicans, who hold at least 173 seats, with four races still yet to be called.\n\nNeither party had won as many House races since 1992, when Bill Clinton won the presidency and Democrats won a total of 258 seats.\n\nSunday's result came after Foxx, who has represented North Carolina's 5th Congressional District since 2005, requested a recount. This is allowed under state law when the margin of victory is under 1%.\n\n\"I think the American people are just sick of what's going on and they wanted a change. They voted for change,\" Hubbard told the BBC News Channel after his victory. He pledged to work alongside his fellow lawmakers to hold President Donald Trump accountable.\n\nEarlier, he wrote on X that he had spoken to Foxx, \"who graciously conceded following the results of the recount process\".\n\nHubbard added: \"I deeply appreciate her commitment to a smooth transition of the office and constituent casework. We may not always agree, but we both love North Carolina and the people of the Fifth District.\"\n\nFoxx, meanwhile, said in a statement: \"After running a campaign focused on my track record of commonsense solutions and conservative values, I'm humbled and grateful that so many North Carolina voters responded by supporting my candidacy, even if we came up just a few votes short.\""
