    "chunk_text": "Belihuloya: Hill-country gateway  Sri Lanka keeps driving past\n\nFor generations, Belihuloya has been a place Sri Lankans pass through rather than linger in. The travellers on the A4 between Colombo and the hills would pull in at the old rest house, eat rice and curry within an earshot of the Belihul Oya tumbling over its boulders and press on towards Haputale, Bandarawela or Ella, before the afternoon mist settled over the ridges.\n\nEKHO Hotels and Resorts, the hotel brand of Ceylon Hotels Corporation PLC (CHC), is betting that the pause can become a stay. The rest house has been relaunched as EKHO Belihuloya and the team behind it is working to turn the surrounding valley into a destination in its own right rather than a lunch stop.\n\n\u201cMost of the tourists who come here are passing through from the Bandarawela side,\u201d EKHO naturalist Gayathri Tissera observed.\n\n\u201cThis location has not yet become a tourism pinpoint. It has not developed enough as a destination.\u201d\n\nCHC Marketing and Communications Manager Sherin Hamid conceded that Belihuloya rarely features on the itineraries of foreign holidaymakers.\n\n\u201cAt the moment, a lot of foreign travellers don\u2019t know Belihuloya as a destination; so, most of the time it doesn\u2019t come under the itinerary,\u201d she noted.\n\nHamid sees part of the opening in Ella\u2019s own success, which has turned the hill town into one of the island\u2019s most sought-after stops.\n\n\u201cElla is now sort of too crowded; so, we want to promote Belihuloya in terms of experiences,\u201d she explained.\n\n\u201cThere are a lot of operators who are focusing on Belihuloya at the moment; so, I think it could be a great start for the destination.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe travel agents who spoke to me said that if we promote the activities here properly, this can become a destination,\u201d she remarked.\n\nShe agreed that the valley holds the kind of promise Ella once did, provided it is not overrun in the same way."
