    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe travel agents who spoke to me said that if we promote the activities here properly, this can become a destination,\u201d she remarked.\n\nShe agreed that the valley holds the kind of promise Ella once did, provided it is not overrun in the same way.\n\nThe waterfalls come first. A short drive up the lane beside the rest house leads to Pahanthuduwa Ella, named for the lamp-like shape of its base. Bambarakanda Ella, at 263 metres, the island\u2019s tallest waterfall, plunges down a forested cliff about 18 kilometres away.\n\nSmaller cascades such as Surathali Ella and Papulagala Ella are scattered across the surrounding hills.\n\n\u201cAs far as I have learned, there is a line of waterfalls running all along this area and there is rock climbing too,\u201d Tissera pointed out, adding that the operators with climbing equipment are based in nearby Bandarawela.\n\nAbove the valley rise the ridgelines. Hawagala, a summit of roughly 1,330 metres a few kilometres from town, rewards a rough three-kilometre climb with views across Samanala Wewa towards Horton Plains.\n\nHigher still, the road through Nonpareil Estate winds for 11 kilometres, climbing more than 900 metres, to Baker\u2019s Bend. This horseshoe-shaped viewpoint sits at about 1,530 metres and according to local lore, takes its name from a colonial-era estate manager whose horse slipped to its death on the bend. On clear mornings, the Samanala and Udawalawe reservoirs glint on the horizon.\n\nThen there is the Devil\u2019s Staircase, an old estate road of hairpin bends climbing from the Bambarakanda area to the road between Ohiya and Horton Plains. The 22-stage Pekoe Trail also runs nearby, with its Horton Plains stages crossing the plateau above.\n\nYet, several of these routes have quietly shifted from footpaths to jeep tracks, a gap that a hotel built around experiences is well placed to fill."
