    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Then there is the Devil\u2019s Staircase, an old estate road of hairpin bends climbing from the Bambarakanda area to the road between Ohiya and Horton Plains. The 22-stage Pekoe Trail also runs nearby, with its Horton Plains stages crossing the plateau above.\n\nYet, several of these routes have quietly shifted from footpaths to jeep tracks, a gap that a hotel built around experiences is well placed to fill.\n\n\u201cWe have a couple of excursions, which I have to explore myself before I take guests out.\u201d\n\nTissera, a self-taught wildlife photographer, who previously worked at Aitken Spence, was handed a clear brief.\n\n\u201cWhen my CEO came here, he told me to put together all the activities that can be done here if we are to make this a tourism destination,\u201d she recalled.\n\n\u201cI don\u2019t usually like to do things the way they are written in a book. I like to try them practically because what is in a book is someone else\u2019s experience,\u201d Tissera reflected.\n\nThat philosophy extends to the property\u2019s reptilian residents. Tissera handles snakes herself and has asked the garden staff not to kill any they find but to call her, so that she can move them.\n\n\u201cNobody taught me. I just watched and from that day I have been catching animals,\u201d she said.\n\n\u201cMore than calling it eco, we must develop it on the sustainable side,\u201d Tissera stressed.\n\n\u201cBut I see many places now making sustainability a marketing point. It has to be done genuinely.\u201d\n\nThe property itself straddles past and present. EKHO Belihuloya now has 16 rooms, all in the deluxe category, after the old staff quarters upstairs were converted into guest rooms. The larger rooms on the far side interconnect for families, Dias noted. The grounds stretch across more than five acres."
