    "chunk_text": "SRR hike to cost banks about Rs.12bn a year in forgone interest\n\nEXTRA PERCENTAGE POINT TO PARK ABOUT RS.140BN OF BANK FUNDS WITH CBSL AT ZERO INTEREST FROM 9 DEC\n\nRaising the Statutory Reserve Ratio (SRR) to 3 percent from 2 percent will cost commercial banks roughly Rs.12 billion a year in forgone interest income, as the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) locks a larger share of their rupee deposits in non-remunerated accounts, First Capital Research (FCR) estimated.\n\nRupee deposits subject to the SRR stood at about Rs.13.7 trillion at end-August 2026, the research house pointed out, so the additional percentage point would park around Rs.140 billion with the Central Bank from 9 December. Valued at the Standing Deposit Facility Rate (SDFR) of 8.50 percent, those funds would have earned close to Rs.12 billion a year before tax. On the same deposit base, the total required reserves banks must hold would rise by half, from about Rs.274 billion to around Rs.411 billion.\n\nThe hike is the first change to the ratio since August 2023, when the Central Bank halved it from 4 percent to 2 percent, a move expected to release around Rs.200 billion into the money market at the time. According to First Capital Research, it is also the first increase since September 2021, when the ratio was raised from 2 percent to 4 percent.\n\n\u201c You can think of this as SRR normalisation,\u201d Central Bank Senior Deputy Governor Dr. Chandranath Amarasekara told reporters at the post-policy press briefing on Friday.\n\nHe explained that the ratio had been lowered in 2023 to support banking sector liquidity, which has since recovered on the back of continued forex purchases."
