    "chunk_text": "\u201c You can think of this as SRR normalisation,\u201d Central Bank Senior Deputy Governor Dr. Chandranath Amarasekara told reporters at the post-policy press briefing on Friday.\n\nHe explained that the ratio had been lowered in 2023 to support banking sector liquidity, which has since recovered on the back of continued forex purchases.\n\nMeanwhile, excess liquidity, the funds left over once banks meet the SRR, has climbed to record highs above Rs.475 billion, the research house noted, meaning the Rs.140 billion to be absorbed would mop up less than a third of that surplus. It expects outstanding market liquidity to ease towards Rs.200 billion to Rs.300 billion by year-end, though it cautioned that liquidity would stay elevated for some time.\n\nFirst Capital also read the move as an indirect signal that the Central Bank intends to keep buying dollars from the market, having now dealt with a by-product of those purchases. The Central Bank bought around US$ 1.7 billion this year up to October, with purchases continuing in November, helping lift gross official reserves to US$ 7.1 billion by end-October from US$ 6.9 billion at end-August."
