    "chunk_text": "LankaPay discloses LankaSign portal  breach six weeks before data protection  duties kick in\n\nPersonal details of people who registered for LankaSign, the digital signature service run by national payment network operator LankaPay, may have been exposed after a portal used to collect registration data suffered a security incident, according to a notice the company sent to registrants. In the notice, signed by LankaPay Deputy Chief Executive Officer Dinuka Perera, the company stressed that the incident had not spread beyond the registration portal into its core systems.\n\n\u201cThis incident is confined only to the above data collection portal, and no other systems including payment systems or digital signature platforms have been affected or impacted,\u201d the notice read.\n\n\u201cWe have taken all appropriate steps to contain the matter and are closely monitoring the situation,\u201d the company told registrants. It directed those with concerns to its Data Protection Officer, Lakmal Uyanahewage.\n\nHowever, the notice did not disclose how many registrants were affected, when the incident took place or was detected, or how the portal came to be compromised.\n\nLankaPay launched LankaSign in May 2009 as Sri Lanka\u2019s first certification authority under the Electronic Transactions Act No. 19 of 2006. It remains the only Certification Service Provider authorised by the National Certification Authority. In May this year, LankaPay widened the service\u2019s reach through a remote digital signature solution developed with eMudhra, which allows organisations and individuals to sign documents from anywhere.\n\nLankaPay is owned by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) and 24 licensed commercial banks, and the Central Bank holds the largest single stake at 19.59 percent, according to the company. The two state banks, People\u2019s Bank and Bank of Ceylon, hold a further 27.80 percent between them, which takes the combined state-linked holding to 47.39 percent. The company\u2019s Chairman is appointed by the CBSL Governor."
