    "chunk_text": "Foreign-linked loan apps continue to prey  on crisis-hit Sri Lankans from regulatory  black hole: CSF\n\nMore than five years after the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) began cautioning the public against them, a web of foreign-linked loan apps continues to prey on crisis-hit households, rebranding and reshuffling to stay in business within a regulatory black hole, a study by the Centre for a Smart Future (CSF) found. The lenders were thrust into the spotlight after the 2022 economic crisis, as their harassment of defaulters spilled into public view and pushed some borrowers to the brink of suicide. Drawing on 39 borrower interviews, the study found that such harassment caused severe psychological distress and, in several cases, suicidal ideation. One borrower, whose employer, husband and children\u2019s teachers had all been contacted by agents, told researchers she had decided to end her life before her family intervened.\n\nRather than retreating, several operators have resurfaced under new guises. A service raided by the Computer Crimes Investigation Division in 2024 now leads to a loan comparison site. The branding of an early Croatian-backed entrant that stopped lending now fronts a comparison site promoting other lenders. Others have switched developers or redirect applicants to sister brands, the study observed.\n\nThe study reviewed seven prominent services, all registered locally. One is offered by a Kyiv-based finance company and another is part of a Latvia-based fintech group. Three other brands share a single Latvian address, while a fourth lists an Estonian address that leads to a car park.\n\nThe Digital Alternative Lending Association (DALA) bills itself as the sector\u2019s only self-regulatory body and counts the developers of the leading apps among its members, yet it has no Sri Lankan leadership beyond its local secretaries. As per DALA\u2019s March 2026 change-of-directors filing cited by the study, all its directors are nationals of Portugal, Latvia and Russia."
