    "chunk_text": "The Digital Alternative Lending Association (DALA) bills itself as the sector\u2019s only self-regulatory body and counts the developers of the leading apps among its members, yet it has no Sri Lankan leadership beyond its local secretaries. As per DALA\u2019s March 2026 change-of-directors filing cited by the study, all its directors are nationals of Portugal, Latvia and Russia.\n\n\u201cTaking loans from these companies is like holding a tiger by its tail, you will not have any respite,\u201d one borrower lamented.\n\nHowever, the lenders remain outside the CBSL\u2019s purview, as it regulates only deposit-taking institutions. The Microfinance and Credit Regulatory Authority Act, passed in March, provides for the regulation of digital money lending. Even so, the Authority is still to be established, and the Act is reportedly being revised before it has even been operationalised.\n\nCSF urged the government to fast-track the Authority with explicit powers over app-based lenders. It warned that the network\u2019s cross-border structure makes it difficult for any single regulator to rein in."
