    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4061f7bd00ab9694f55eba740e10e392",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "With CHC running just three other rest houses with accommodation, in Dambulla, Pussellawa and Ambepussa, even a full conversion would lift the brand to only 10 hotels. The balance is set to come from properties EKHO will operate under management contracts, an asset-light route that lets the brand scale without carrying the full cost of ownership.\n\nThe conversions are aimed squarely at a younger audience. \u201cThe reason for us to convert these rest houses into the EKHO brand is basically to gain more traction with the younger market and the younger audience,\u201d Hamid explained.\n\nYet CHC is careful not to erase the memories that made these properties landmarks on the island\u2019s highways, where families once pulled in for breakfast and a rice and curry lunch.\n\n\u201cWhile keeping that nostalgia going, we wanted to just give it a refreshed look,\u201d Hamid added.\n\nEKHO\u2019s chief asset, she pointed out, is its footprint. \u201cThe main selling point we have is the location. We have amazing locations all across the country,\u201d Hamid remarked.\n\nThat footprint underpins a destination-led approach, in which EKHO markets the surrounding region as much as the hotel itself. \u2018 \u201cThrough our channels, we want to promote the destination, and along with that, we promote our property,\u201d she noted.\n\nMeanwhile, the clearest expression of the repositioning is a new EKHO Adventure cluster. It draws together the hiking trails of Ella, the waterfalls and river valleys of Belihuloya and the white-water rapids of Kithulgala, and CHC is pitching it to travel agents specialising in adventure travel.\n\n\u201cWe\u2019re looking at EKHO as a different repositioning, if you will,\u201d Hamid stated.\n\nFor now, Belihuloya has yet to draw the market the brand is courting.\n\n\u201cRight now we don\u2019t really get the younger crowd coming over here,\u201d Hamid admitted, adding that CHC is working closely with the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA), which has its own plan to uplift Belihuloya as a destination."
