    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Card fraud victims foot  the bill as region shifts  burden to banks\n\nRS. 430,000 GONE ON A HONG KONG TAP WHILE CARDHOLDER WAS IN MALAYSIA\n\nFRAUD COMPLAINTS UP 80% AS REGIONAL REGULATORS SHIFT LOSSES TO BANKS\n\nAt a time when regulators across the region are shifting card fraud losses onto banks, Sri Lankan cardholders defrauded abroad can still be left to carry the loss until they report it, even when their bank\u2019s own systems catch the fraud within minutes, correspondence seen by Mirror Business showed.\n\n\u201cThe cardholder remains responsible for transactions performed using the card until the loss or theft is formally reported to the Bank and the card is blocked,\u201d a private commercial bank, which this year completed its takeover of a foreign bank\u2019s local retail operations, stated in a November 12 letter to Fintechnology Asia Pacific (FINAP) CEO Dr. Kutila Pinto, a career banker.\n\nDr. Pinto\u2019s Mastercard debit card was used for a HK$ 10,000 contactless purchase at a Hong Kong jeweller on October 19, nine minutes after his flight landed in Johor Bahru, Malaysia. No PIN was requested, though the amount was ten times Hong Kong\u2019s HK$ 1,000 limit for contactless payments without a signature or PIN.\n\nThe bank\u2019s fraud system blocked the card 18 minutes later, yet the full Rs.429,698 was drawn from his savings account and has not been returned. The bank told him the transaction \u201cdid not meet any of these blocking criteria and was therefore processed through the normal authorisation flow\u201d.\n\nDr. Pinto reported the fraud by phone the same evening, lodged a formal written dispute the next day and wrote again on October 26 to say he was overseas and needed the funds. On October 28, he put ten specific questions to the bank in a 13- page letter copied to CBSL\u2019s Financial Consumer Relations Department (FCRD) and the Financial Ombudsman. The November 12 reply answered none of them, he noted, reaffirming only that \u201cthe Bank\u2019s position remains the same\u201d."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
