    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a7e3844974fd854e442049d34cd00155",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Dr. Pinto reported the fraud by phone the same evening, lodged a formal written dispute the next day and wrote again on October 26 to say he was overseas and needed the funds. On October 28, he put ten specific questions to the bank in a 13- page letter copied to CBSL\u2019s Financial Consumer Relations Department (FCRD) and the Financial Ombudsman. The November 12 reply answered none of them, he noted, reaffirming only that \u201cthe Bank\u2019s position remains the same\u201d.\n\n\u201cYou are the Bank\u2019s fraud prevention department. Not the Bank,\u201d Dr. Pinto told Mirror Business, contending that a cardholder agreement cannot override Mastercard\u2019s Zero Liability policy, which protects cardholders who take reasonable care and report promptly.\n\nMany such disputes are unlikely to travel beyond the bank, but Dr. Pinto, with a career in banking behind him, is pressing ahead, lodging formal complaints with the FCRD and the Financial Ombudsman and escalating the matter to Mastercard Asia Pacific. Even then, CBSL rules give banks 21 days to resolve a complaint, extendable to three months, and the central bank 90 days to deliver a determination once a complaint reaches it.\n\nThe country\u2019s two largest state banks both publish terms that hold cardholders liable for the use of a lost or stolen card until the bank is notified, with one stating it is not liable to refund such transactions. At least two private banks carry comparable clauses.\n\nComplaints on frauds and scams lodged with the FCRD against banks and finance companies rose by about 80 percent between 2023 and 2025, according to FCRD officials.\n\nMeanwhile, Visa data shows Malaysia\u2019s card-not-present fraud rate climbed 44 percent in 2025, even as the Asia Pacific average fell by 15 percent."
