    "chunk_text": "UK teacher who had sex with pupil 30 times faces sentencing after court no-show\n\nINDEPENDENT - A chemistry teacher convicted of sexual relationships with two pupils at an all-boys school in the late 1980s is set to be sentenced after failing to appear at court.\n\nSally-Anne Bowen, 65, was found guilty earlier this year on seven counts of indecent assault and was scheduled to face sentencing at Harrow Crown Court on Friday.\n\nShe was in her 20s when she had sex with one pupil up to 30 times and kissed a second, when the boys were aged about 14 to 15.\n\nHer trial heard she wore short, tight skirts and flirted in class, with one former pupil comparing her behaviour to \"a Sabrina Carpenter video\".\n\nBowen failed to appear while on bail on Friday morning, prompting an arrest warrant. Untraceable at her Hemel Hempstead home, police eventually located and arrested her in Hampshire.\n\nShe was remanded into custody and will appear at the same court for sentencing on Monday.\n\nA Metropolitan Police spokesperson said: \u201cOn Friday November 20, officers were made aware that a 65-year-old woman had failed to appear at Harrow Crown Court for sentencing.\n\n\u201cA warrant was issued for her arrest and officers promptly launched inquiries to locate her.\n\n\u201cAt around 1500, officers from a neighbouring force found the woman at a location in Hampshire and arrested her on suspicion of failing to appear at court. She was taken into police custody.\n\n\u201cOn Saturday November 21 she appeared before Uxbridge Magistrates\u2019 Court where she was remanded to appear at Harrow Crown Court on Monday November 23.\u201d\n\nHer trial heard that Bowen was in her mid-20s when she taught at Christ\u2019s College in Finchley, north London.\n\nOne victim told jurors that he had sex with her \u201cabout 20 or 30 times\u201d over several months, regularly visited her home and considered her his girlfriend.\n\nHe had fallen in love but kept their relationship secret because Bowen had \u201cdrummed it\u201d into him that he must not tell anyone, the court heard."
