    "md5": "585d9cac8c8a0e737ca5b496c4133f52",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1515,
    "chunk_text": "Houthis claim attack on Saudi oil refinery\n\nASIA TODAY - Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi rebels have claimed to have attacked a major oil refinery on Saudi Arabia's Red Sea coast. As military tensions rise across the Middle East amid the war between the United States and Iran, Saudi energy infrastructure is also coming under threat.\n\nAccording to Reuters on November 22 (local time), the Houthis said they had carried out a military operation targeting the Rabigh oil refinery on Saudi Arabia's western Red Sea coast and had directly hit the facility. The extent of any damage and whether operations at the facility have been disrupted remain unconfirmed. Saudi authorities have not commented on the claim.\n\nThe Rabigh area is home to Petro Rabigh, an integrated refining and petrochemical complex developed by Saudi state oil company Aramco and Japan's Sumitomo Chemical. The complex also has facilities for exporting petroleum products through a deepwater port. Located on the Red Sea coast, approximately 165 kilometers north of Jeddah, it is a major industrial hub for both oil refining and petrochemical production. Any confirmed strike would therefore cause significant damage to Saudi Arabia's energy industry.\n\nThe Houthis have claimed a series of attacks on Saudi energy facilities in recent months. Reported targets include the country's East-West Pipeline and refineries in Jazan in the south and Ras Tanura in the east. However, Saudi authorities have yet to officially confirm attacks on these refineries."
