    "chunk_text": "Police have recorded dozens of crude bomb blasts and incidents of arson targeting vehicles \u200cin at \u2060least 28 districts over the past 10 days, with several police officers and civilians injured, according to local media.\n\nKhalid Mahmud Chowdhury, a senior Awami League official, said the current political establishment was responsible for the violence.\n\nThe minister said there had been no official discussions with India about sending an aircraft to bring Hasina back, adding that Dhaka wanted balanced ties with New \u200bDelhi that did not compromise Bangladesh's \"dignity, \u200bself-respect and interests.\"\n\n\"India needs to \u2060decide what kind of foreign policy they need to adopt for Bangladesh,\" Obaed said.\n\nDhaka is also stepping up diplomatic pressure on neighbouring Myanmar over the return of Rohingya refugees after the UN's highest court ruled that \u200bthousands of killings and widespread sexual violence by Myanmar's security forces against the Muslim minority in 2017 \u200bamounted to state-sponsored genocide.\n\nMyanmar \u2060has criticised the ruling by the International Court of Justice said Rohingya would only be able to return \"when situations permit\".\n\n\"Now, this verdict gives us, Bangladesh, the strength, the Rohingya community the strength to fight,\" Obaed said, adding that they deserved to return \"with dignity and safety\".\n\nAbout 1.2 million Rohingya live in \u2060Bangladesh after repeated \u200brepatriation attempts failed. Their return has been complicated by Myanmar's civil war and the \u200bArakan Army militia's control of nearly all of Rakhine State, which is home for most Rohingya.\n\nObaed called for greater international pressure on Myanmar and the Arakan Army.\n\n\"Bangladesh did not create \u200bthis crisis. Myanmar created this crisis, so Myanmar has to end it,\" she said."
  },
