    "md5": "35aa42d45db553ad6e8c7a5b0cf5b9ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan marine biologist Asha de Vos named Fellow of UK Marine Biological Association\n\nColombo, Nov. 23 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lankan marine biologist Dr. Asha de Vos has been appointed a Fellow of the Marine Biological Association (MBA) in the United Kingdom in recognition of her contributions to marine biology, conservation and public understanding of the ocean.\n\nThe appointment, announced by the MBA on November 18, recognises her decades of research on marine mammals, particularly blue whales in the Northern Indian Ocean, as well as her work in marine conservation, education and science communication.\n\nUnder the Royal Charter of the MBA, Fellowship is awarded to individuals who have made outstanding and impactful contributions to marine biology.\n\nThe association described de Vos as an internationally renowned marine biologist, ocean educator, author and pioneer in long-term blue whale research in the Northern Indian Ocean. She is also recognised as Sri Lanka\u2019s first deep-sea explorer and aquanaut.\n\nHer research has contributed to scientific understanding of marine mammals in the waters around Sri Lanka, while her efforts to promote ocean conservation have helped make marine science accessible to wider audiences through public lectures, books, documentaries and social media.\n\nDe Vos has authored three books on the ocean aimed at readers of different age groups.\n\nEducated at the University of St Andrews, the University of Oxford and the University of Western Australia, she later left academia to establish Oceanswell, recognised as Sri Lanka\u2019s first marine conservation research and education organisation.\n\nThrough Oceanswell, she has worked to strengthen marine science capacity in Sri Lanka by training and mentoring a new generation of marine scientists.\n\nThe MBA said her Fellowship recognised not only her individual achievements but also the growing contribution of Sri Lanka\u2019s marine science community."
  },
