    "md5": "27163430dcd75a885b81dc17f229beed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Schools to be reorganised with transport facilities, says PM\n\nPrime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya has assured that transport facilities will be provided as a mandatory requirement when schools are consolidated under the school reorganisation programme.\n\nShe said there were no obstacles to introducing measures based on the specific needs and challenges of individual areas. The current Budget also includes provisions to provide transport facilities for students with disabilities.\n\nThe Prime Minister made these remarks at a meeting of the Colombo District Education Sub-Committee held at the Parliament complex on November 23.\n\nDuring the meeting, officials revealed that student attendance had increased significantly in certain areas following the introduction of school nutrition programmes.\n\nThe need to establish a mechanism to recruit security personnel to protect school property was also discussed. Such personnel could be recruited either at school level or from the local community, subject to the necessary approval from the Provincial Council.\n\nThe meeting also discussed providing funds for security services through daily payment schemes based on the requirements of individual schools or through budgetary allocations. The importance of following proper criteria when obtaining external services was also highlighted.\n\nThe shortage of Tamil-medium schools in the Colombo District was identified as a key reason for school dropouts and poor attendance in certain areas. Accordingly, it was decided to introduce Tamil-medium classes in schools in affected areas and work towards establishing a trilingual school in the future.\n\nThe Prime Minister stressed that every child has the right to receive an education in their mother tongue and that ensuring this right is the Government's responsibility.\n\nThe meeting also discussed difficulties faced by students who enter schools without birth certificates and later encounter problems obtaining the necessary documents."
  },
