    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "The Prime Minister stressed that every child has the right to receive an education in their mother tongue and that ensuring this right is the Government's responsibility.\n\nThe meeting also discussed difficulties faced by students who enter schools without birth certificates and later encounter problems obtaining the necessary documents.\n\nMeanwhile, officials noted that a formal policy on the transfer of principals within the Western Province had been formulated and approved by the Public Service Commission, ending the previous absence of a clear policy.\n\nAs a result, the transfer process is expected to be implemented systematically from next year.\n\nThe importance of principals' commitment and leadership, particularly in managing schools with limited facilities and those located in difficult areas, was also emphasised. The meeting noted that decisions concerning schools should take into account the specific conditions and requirements of each institution.\n\nDiscussions were also held on improving the training of preschool teachers. It was proposed to provide them with practical training kits in addition to their formal training to help them carry out classroom activities more effectively.\n\nThe Prime Minister further said next year's teacher training programmes should place greater emphasis on the meaning of a student-centred approach and how it could be effectively implemented in classrooms.\n\nDeputy Minister of Mass Media Kaushalya Ariyaratne, Deputy Minister of Urban Development Eranga Gunasekara, Deputy Minister of Industries Chathuranga Abesinghe, Colombo District Coordinating Committee Chairman Lakshman Nipunaarachchi, MP Samanmalee Gunasinghe and Colombo Additional District Secretary Kanchana Gunawardena attended the meeting, along with officials from the Ministry of Education."
