    "md5": "12f32f753d27443e8c5d9a74d568babf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "Animal group seeks action over alleged dumping of airport dogs\n\nColombo, November 23 (Daily Mirror) - The Animal Welfare Forum (AWC) has initiated legal and administrative action over allegations that dogs removed from the Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA) and Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport (MRIA) are being abandoned in forests and garbage dumps.\n\nIn a series of complaints submitted today (23), the organisation called on authorities to investigate reports that dogs captured from the airport premises were being transported and released in forest areas in Weerawila and Thanamalwila in the Hambantota District, as well as at the Kerawalapitiya garbage dump.\n\nThe organisation said reports and photographs circulating on social media suggested that a team had been capturing dogs near SriLankan Airlines and Sri Lanka Air Force facilities at Mattala since early morning, allegedly with plans to release them in nearby forest areas later in the day.\n\nHowever, following an inquiry by Attorney-at-Law Sajeewa Gamage, Mattala Airport authorities had denied that the dogs or the removal operation originated from the airport, the organisation claimed.\n\nThe forum has therefore sought official information to establish where the dogs were captured, who authorised their removal and where they were taken.\n\nAn application under the Right to Information Act was submitted to Airport and Aviation Services (Sri Lanka) today (23), with copies sent to its Chairman and the Director General of the Civil Aviation Authority of Sri Lanka.\n\nThe request covers the period from January 2024 to November 23, 2026, and seeks details of wildlife hazard management plans, airport perimeter inspections, the number of dogs captured and removed, contractors involved, locations where the animals were released and public funds spent on such operations."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-23-dailymirrorlk-7450b355",
