    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1dcdbc95ba21f9b8d6810566afe0feaf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "The request covers the period from January 2024 to November 23, 2026, and seeks details of wildlife hazard management plans, airport perimeter inspections, the number of dogs captured and removed, contractors involved, locations where the animals were released and public funds spent on such operations.\n\nThe forum has also lodged a formal complaint with the Director General of the Department of Wildlife Conservation, requesting immediate action to prevent dogs from being released in wildlife areas.\n\nIt has called for wildlife officers in Weerawila, Lunugamvehera, Bundala, Thanamalwila and Hambantota to conduct patrols and road inspections. It has also requested investigations and legal action against those responsible if dogs have already been abandoned.\n\nIn a separate communication, the organisation urged airport authorities, SriLankan Airlines and the Sri Lanka Air Force to immediately stop the alleged practice and adopt proper measures to manage animals within airport premises.\n\nThe forum also raised concerns over an incident in which a mother dog was allegedly separated from her five nursing puppies and abandoned at the Kerawalapitiya garbage dump. The mother and puppies were later located and rescued, while a witness had agreed to provide evidence for legal action, it said.\n\nThe organisation acknowledged that keeping dogs away from airport runways and operational areas was essential for aviation safety. However, it said authorities should address the problem by improving airport security infrastructure rather than transporting and abandoning animals in forests or garbage dumps.\n\nIt warned that abandoning domestic dogs in wildlife habitats could expose them to starvation, attacks by wild animals and infectious diseases, including rabies, canine distemper and parvovirus."
