    "md5": "2dab3892700090581b0c5832a1ee5fb7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1884,
    "chunk_text": "The organisation acknowledged that keeping dogs away from airport runways and operational areas was essential for aviation safety. However, it said authorities should address the problem by improving airport security infrastructure rather than transporting and abandoning animals in forests or garbage dumps.\n\nIt warned that abandoning domestic dogs in wildlife habitats could expose them to starvation, attacks by wild animals and infectious diseases, including rabies, canine distemper and parvovirus.\n\nCiting animal welfare and wildlife protection laws, the forum said the alleged abandonment of dogs could violate the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Ordinance, the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance and other relevant legislation.\n\nIt also pointed to the 2006 National Policy prohibiting the culling of dogs, which promotes scientific population management through Catch, Neuter, Vaccinate and Release programmes.\n\nAs a long-term solution, the organisation has proposed repairing airport perimeter fences, securing drainage channels and installing animal-proof barriers at vehicle and pedestrian entrances.\n\nIt has also recommended CCTV monitoring, proper waste management and sterilisation and vaccination programmes within a one-to-two-kilometre radius of airports.\n\nDogs currently living within airport premises should be documented, given medical treatment and sterilised before being placed in suitable homes or transferred to officially recognised shelters, it proposed.\n\nThe forum has requested written confirmation within seven days that the alleged abandonment of dogs has been stopped and called for a meeting with relevant authorities within 14 days to discuss implementing the proposed measures.\n\nIt warned that it would pursue criminal proceedings and lodge complaints with national and international aviation regulatory bodies if the alleged practices continued."
