    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5094d8fe3a4ae1b6b73257da98eb93d4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "For somewhere beyond the boundaries of party, class, caste, creed and ethnicity, there are men and women who have waited all their lives for this journey to begin. They may never have sung your songs, carried your banners or embraced your political philosophy. They may have opposed you yesterday and may question you tomorrow. But in the deepest chambers of their conscience, they too have heard the same ancient summons.\n\nIt is the summons of a nation yearning to become worthy of its own people.\n\nThat promise has survived the splendor of kingdoms and the ruin of empires. It has survived foreign conquest, colonial subjugation, communal suspicion, political betrayal and the bitter tragedies of civil conflict. It has lived in the calloused hands of the farmer, the weary eyes of the plantation worker, the patient endurance of the fisherman, the uncelebrated sacrifices of the teacher and the quiet desperation of the mother who sends her child across oceans in search of a future denied at home.\n\nThe task before you is not to become another celebrated occupant of the presidential chair. It is to help make that chair less powerful than the institutions of the Republic, less important than the rights of its citizens, and less enduring than the principles by which the country must be governed. Let that be the measure of your achievement. For Mother Lanka has waited long enough\n\nIt has lived in the sorrow of the North and the East, in the frustrations of the South, in the neglected villages of the dry zone and the crowded streets of Colombo. It has waited in every home where hope was repeatedly born, repeatedly betrayed, yet never entirely extinguished.\n\nAnd now, perhaps, the hour has arrived when that ancient promise demands more than another speech, another election, another government or another procession of triumphant politicians.\n\nNot merely a change of rulers, but a change in the very meaning of rule."
  },
