    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "There will be dark mornings. There will be betrayal and disappointment. There will be moments when the burden of expectation weighs more heavily than the machinery of government itself. The applause of the multitude may fade, and the solitude of leadership may become almost unbearable.\n\nBut history has never reserved its finest pages for those who chose comfort over conviction.\n\nShe has buried too many sons, mourned too many daughters, watched too many promises dissolve into the polluted waters of political expediency. Her soil has absorbed the tears of the innocent and the blood of the forgotten. Her children have inherited not merely the glory of an ancient civilization but the unresolved wounds of a modern nation.\n\nShe asks for a generation courageous enough to make saviors unnecessary.\n\nTake it not as the leader of a victorious party, but as the temporary custodian of a permanent national aspiration.\n\nTake it knowing that those who begin the journey may not live to witness its destination; that the seeds planted today may blossom beneath skies unseen by their planters; that the noblest victories of public life are often those for which no individual can claim exclusive credit.\n\nAnd when the familiar voices grow faint, when the road bends beyond the horizon, when yesterday\u2019s certainties no longer illuminate tomorrow\u2019s choices, remember that the true compass of this odyssey is neither party doctrine nor personal popularity.\n\nFor somewhere in the depths of this ancient island, beneath the ruins of abandoned promises and the weight of unfulfilled dreams, the heartbeat of a different Sri Lanka can still be heard.\n\nNot for a king. Not for a conqueror. Not for another name to be carved upon the monuments of political vanity.\n\nIt is calling for justice. For reconciliation. For decency. For a republic in which the humblest citizen may stand upright without fear before the mightiest institution of the State."
