    "language": "en",
    "md5": "28a98676bd23241c9abb446549836f97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Friends with all, subservient to none: Why Sri Lanka must guard its national interest\n\nWhether it is a warship in distress, a tariff set in Washington, an investment from Beijing or a caf\u00e9 in Arugam Bay, the question before us is the same: Are we protecting the interests of Sri Lanka and its people?\n\nThere was a time when Sri Lanka stood taller in the world than its size or its wealth would suggest. Under the late Sirimavo Bandaranaike, this country practiced what I believe was the finest non-aligned foreign policy in our history. The idea of the Indian Ocean as a zone of peace was first raised by Sri Lanka at the Non-Aligned summit in Cairo in 1964. In 1971, it was Sri Lanka, later joined by Tanzania, that took the proposal to the United Nations, and on 16 December that year the General Assembly adopted Resolution 2832, declaring the Indian Ocean a Zone of Peace. In August 1976, Colombo hosted the Fifth Summit of the Non-Aligned Movement, welcoming leaders from across Asia, Africa and Latin America, and Mrs. Bandaranaike assumed the chair of the Movement. A small island helped to set the agenda for the great powers.\n\nThat was not luck. It was the product of three qualities: clarity, consistency and credibility. We knew who we were, we said the same thing to every capital, and we were believed. As our country faces one of the most complicated external environments in its modern history \u2013 with a war in the Middle East spilling into our waters, volatile trade policy in our largest market and intense competition for influence in our ports \u2013 those three qualities are worth recalling.\n\nForeign policy is unlike any other area of government. In domestic policy, a government can experiment, make a mistake and correct it at the next Budget. In foreign policy, a careless word, a hasty decision or a single port call can follow a nation for a generation. Foreign policy is not something to be played with for political applause at home."
  },
