    "chunk_size_bytes": 1611,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign policy is unlike any other area of government. In domestic policy, a government can experiment, make a mistake and correct it at the next Budget. In foreign policy, a careless word, a hasty decision or a single port call can follow a nation for a generation. Foreign policy is not something to be played with for political applause at home.\n\nDuring the Cold War, the rivalry between the superpowers was largely conducted directly between them. Since then, geopolitics has changed. The contest is no longer fought mainly between the great powers themselves; it is fought in other countries: in their ports, their sea lanes, their infrastructure, their debts and their politics. Sri Lanka, sitting beside some of the busiest shipping lanes in the Indian Ocean, has become geopolitical turf. We must be mindful of that every single day.\n\nWe have learnt this the hard way. Many will remember 4 June 1987. Shortly after our Navy had turned back a flotilla of boats carrying supplies, parippu and other provisions were dropped from the air over Jaffna, and we were powerless to prevent it. Within weeks, the Indo-Sri Lanka Accord had been signed. We will remember, too, that in the years before that, those who took up arms against this State received training on foreign soil. I do not raise these matters to reopen old wounds with a neighbour with whom our relations today are close and valued. I raise them as lessons. When the interests of larger nations are engaged, a small nation must know its actual strength, and it must ask itself honestly whether it can take other countries on in the global arena."
  },
