    "chunk_text": "We have learnt this the hard way. Many will remember 4 June 1987. Shortly after our Navy had turned back a flotilla of boats carrying supplies, parippu and other provisions were dropped from the air over Jaffna, and we were powerless to prevent it. Within weeks, the Indo-Sri Lanka Accord had been signed. We will remember, too, that in the years before that, those who took up arms against this State received training on foreign soil. I do not raise these matters to reopen old wounds with a neighbour with whom our relations today are close and valued. I raise them as lessons. When the interests of larger nations are engaged, a small nation must know its actual strength, and it must ask itself honestly whether it can take other countries on in the global arena.\n\nWe cannot leave our export earnings so heavily dependent on a handful of markets, however friendly. Trade agreements with emerging markets in Asia, the Middle East and Africa must be pursued with urgency, and our exporters, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises, must be given the market intelligence, finance and diplomatic support to reach them. Our missions abroad should be judged, in part, by the new markets they open. That, too, is foreign policy\n\nLet me be clear: Sri Lanka must never compromise on its humanitarian and human rights obligations. A sailor in distress is a human being first; his nationality comes second. International maritime law requires us to render assistance, and our own values, rooted in every one of our faiths, demand it. The President was right to say that every life is as precious as our own. I commend the Sri Lanka Navy, the Air Force, the doctors and nurses in Galle and Colombo, and everyone who responded so swiftly and professionally."
