    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "Let me be clear: Sri Lanka must never compromise on its humanitarian and human rights obligations. A sailor in distress is a human being first; his nationality comes second. International maritime law requires us to render assistance, and our own values, rooted in every one of our faiths, demand it. The President was right to say that every life is as precious as our own. I commend the Sri Lanka Navy, the Air Force, the doctors and nurses in Galle and Colombo, and everyone who responded so swiftly and professionally.\n\nNowhere is this balance more important than in our economy. Consider the facts. Our exports go primarily to the West. The United States alone is our largest single export market, taking more than a fifth of our merchandise exports, and together with the European Union it accounts for nearly half of all we sell abroad; add the United Kingdom, and it is more than half. Apparel dominates our shipments to America, and the industry employs more than 300,000 workers directly, many of them women from our rural villages.\n\nWhen tariffs are pushed up in Washington, it is our apparel workers in Katunayake, in Biyagama and in our villages who feel it first. Over the past two years, we have watched the duty on our goods swing wildly. In April 2025, 44% reciprocal tariff was announced on Sri Lankan exports; it was later cut to 30% and then to 20%. In February this year, the United States Supreme Court struck down the legal basis for those tariffs, and a temporary 10 per cent duty was imposed in their place. In July, a new regime set our rate at 10%, sparing us a higher bracket for now. Our exporters have lived with every twist of that uncertainty, and each adverse shift has pushed our economy downhill. Planning a factory, a production line or a season\u2019s orders in such conditions is extraordinarily difficult, and it is the worker on the factory floor who carries the risk."
