    "chunk_size_bytes": 1956,
    "chunk_text": "When tariffs are pushed up in Washington, it is our apparel workers in Katunayake, in Biyagama and in our villages who feel it first. Over the past two years, we have watched the duty on our goods swing wildly. In April 2025, 44% reciprocal tariff was announced on Sri Lankan exports; it was later cut to 30% and then to 20%. In February this year, the United States Supreme Court struck down the legal basis for those tariffs, and a temporary 10 per cent duty was imposed in their place. In July, a new regime set our rate at 10%, sparing us a higher bracket for now. Our exporters have lived with every twist of that uncertainty, and each adverse shift has pushed our economy downhill. Planning a factory, a production line or a season\u2019s orders in such conditions is extraordinarily difficult, and it is the worker on the factory floor who carries the risk.\n\nWhen the present Government assumed office, there was considerable concern that it would abandon the IMF program, and that it would engage only with the East and ignore the West. In fairness, that has not happened. The Government has continued with the program agreed in March 2023; the IMF Executive Board completed the combined fifth and sixth reviews in May this year, and staff reached agreement on the seventh review in October. The Government has engaged Washington on tariffs and Beijing on investment, and has, so far, managed to strike a balance. Credit must be given where it is due.\n\nA word of caution, however. Remaining within the program is one thing; being ruled by the dictates of the IMF is quite another. The program is a means to recovery, not a substitute for our own economic policy. As Parliament debates the 2027 Budget, on which the next IMF tranche depends, the pensioner, the small trader and the daily-wage earner must see that the decisions taken in their name are made here, by our own Parliament, for their benefit, and that the burden of reform is shared fairly."
