    "chunk_text": "A word of caution, however. Remaining within the program is one thing; being ruled by the dictates of the IMF is quite another. The program is a means to recovery, not a substitute for our own economic policy. As Parliament debates the 2027 Budget, on which the next IMF tranche depends, the pensioner, the small trader and the daily-wage earner must see that the decisions taken in their name are made here, by our own Parliament, for their benefit, and that the burden of reform is shared fairly.\n\nYet all of this balancing rests on something that, at present, we lack, our own ambassadors in the two capitals that matter most to us. Sri Lanka\u2019s ambassadorial posts in Washington DC and in Beijing both stand vacant. Our Ambassador to China took formal leave of the Chinese Foreign Ministry in April, and our Ambassador to the United States ended his term and returned home in July. In August, by contrast, both the United States and China sent new ambassadors to Colombo, and each presented credentials to the President on the same day, 25 August. They are here, in our capital, advancing the interests of their countries, as they are entitled to do. We have no one of equal rank in theirs.\n\nSome foreign nationals have arrived in Sri Lanka and taken jobs that are meant for our own citizens. Some have set up businesses, including guesthouses, surf schools, caf\u00e9s and restaurants, and some of these establishments do not even welcome locals. In our own country, Sri Lankans are being made to feel unwelcome in places run by visitors. How can this happen? Who issued the approvals? Who is checking the visas? Who is collecting the taxes?"
