    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "Some foreign nationals have arrived in Sri Lanka and taken jobs that are meant for our own citizens. Some have set up businesses, including guesthouses, surf schools, caf\u00e9s and restaurants, and some of these establishments do not even welcome locals. In our own country, Sri Lankans are being made to feel unwelcome in places run by visitors. How can this happen? Who issued the approvals? Who is checking the visas? Who is collecting the taxes?\n\nI acknowledge that the authorities have acted. According to the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority, 75 foreign nationals were arrested and 15 deported last year for doing business while on tourist visas. But treating these cases merely as individual visa violations is not enough. The problem is structural, and it requires a structural response. Other destinations in our region have faced the same challenge and have learnt that occasional raids, without proper registration, licensing and tax enforcement, simply move the problem from one beach to the next.\n\n(The author is a President\u2019s Counsel, a Member of Parliament, former Minister of Sports and former Deputy Minister of Tourism)\n\nLet me also be clear that we welcome every tourist. Visitors who respect our laws, our culture and our communities are our honoured guests. Genuine foreign investors who come through the proper channels, create jobs for Sri Lankans and pay their taxes are welcome too. This is not about any nationality, and it must never become so. It is about the rule of law. The unregulated way in which some of these businesses operate is a direct threat to a vibrant and inclusive tourism industry \u2013 an industry that must, above all, benefit the people of Sri Lanka.\n\nI have therefore called upon the Government to take four steps:\n\n1. Carry out a comprehensive, district-by-district audit of foreign-run tourism businesses, so that we know the true scale of the problem."
  },
