    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s trade and diplomatic ties under fresh scrutiny post US midterm election\n\nI hope that as the most important and most powerful superpower of the world, America does discover the geography of South Asia, does discover the geography of the Indian Ocean and does discover the importance of a genuine mutually beneficial friendship with India\n\nIt is important for the Government to appoint a new Ambassador to Washington, who is prepared to represent its interests to engage with many of the new members of Congress, a number of whom know very little about Sri Lanka\n\nThe best way to say where we stand right now is that we feel to be at the beginning of the end of the Trump era\n\n- Henry Stimson Centre Senior fellow in the South Asia program Dr. Daniel\n\nThe US did not provide us with offensive weapons, but it did provide intelligence, the war would probably have continued for many more years\n\n- Former Foreign Secretary and Sri Lankan Ambassador to the US Ravinatha Aryasinha\n\nThe Americans has delivered its verdict at the US midterm elections. According to political analysts with Democrats gaining control of both the House of Representatives and the Senate, President Donald Trump is likely to face influences in his policy approaches in different sectors including trade, foreign policy, security and the relationship with the Global South.\n\nThe changing political landscape in the US indicates significant implications for Sri Lanka for its relationship with the US, which is the largest export partner, accounting for nearly a quarter of the country\u2019s exports.\n\nAt the webinar organised by the Pathfinder Foundation titled \u2018America Votes, South Asia Adjusts: Implications for Sri Lanka and the Region\u2019 former diplomates and policy experts looked into the implications of Washington\u2019s political shift for South Asia, with trade access, human rights, international relations and regional security as key concerns"
