    "chunk_text": "At the webinar organised by the Pathfinder Foundation titled \u2018America Votes, South Asia Adjusts: Implications for Sri Lanka and the Region\u2019 former diplomates and policy experts looked into the implications of Washington\u2019s political shift for South Asia, with trade access, human rights, international relations and regional security as key concerns\n\nReferring to possible foreign relations changes of the US with South Asia and China\u2019s growing influence in South Asia and the Indian Ocean, Goonatileke said the question was whether a change in Washington would give regional countries more room to pursue their interests or bring new pressures to choose sides.\n\n\u201cThe Indian Ocean also deserves particular attention. Securing maritime trade routes and chokepoints, maritime security, freedom of navigation, port infrastructure and undersea communications are becoming increasingly important\u201d, he said adding that those matters have direct implications for Sri Lanka and the wider Indian Ocean region.\n\nThe speakers highlighted the possible implications of the changing political balance in Washington for US relations with India, Pakistan, China and other South Asian countries, while highlighting the economic and strategic considerations facing Sri Lanka.\n\nFormer Indian Minister of State for External Affairs M.J. Akbar stressed that the United States could not overlook India\u2019s strategic importance in maintaining security and stability in West Asia, South Asia and the wider Indo-Pacific.\n\nAkbar is a veteran journalist and a politicians who has written several books, including \u2018The Past and Future of Pakistan\u2019.\n\nHe said India\u2019s role in the changing geopolitical landscape would become increasingly significant, particularly as conflicts stretching from Ukraine to West Asia and the Indian Ocean became more interconnected."
