    "chunk_text": "He said India\u2019s role in the changing geopolitical landscape would become increasingly significant, particularly as conflicts stretching from Ukraine to West Asia and the Indian Ocean became more interconnected.\n\n\u201cCan you remove India from the architecture of security and stability of West Asia, of the Indo-Pacific and of Asia?\u201d he asked.\n\nAkbar emphasised that India\u2019s independent foreign policy should not be mistaken for opposition to international partnerships or cooperation with the United States.\n\n\u201cIndependence is not a contrarian position. It is a position in which we offer partnership for a world order but through mutual cooperation, through an understanding of bilateral and even multilateral interests,\u201d he said.\n\nDescribing India as a pivotal power in South Asia and Asia, Akbar said the country would become an increasingly influential force in regional and international affairs.\n\n\u201cIndia is a pivotal power in South Asia, it is a pivotal power in Asia and it will become an increasingly powerful force for the good of the region and for the good of the world,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cWhat I have described as the war of the five seas, searching for the sixth. The war in Ukraine began with Crimea in 2014. The five seas are the Mediterranean, Caspian, Red, Black and Arabian seas, with the conflict almost reaching the sixth sea \u2013 the Indian Ocean,\u201d he said giving the focus to the Indian Ocean.\n\nHe identified Taiwan and the Malacca Strait as important flashpoints in the evolving US-China strategic rivalry and stressed the importance of India\u2019s naval presence in the region.\n\nAkbar also cautioned that the election outcome could influence Washington\u2019s approach towards West Asia, including its policy on Iran, and warned of the possibility of renewed military escalation.\n\n\u201cThere is a possibility that, because he does not want to be entre history books as a loser, he could actually reignite the conflict,\u201d he said."
