    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe U.S. remains Sri Lanka\u2019s most important export market,\u201d he, who is the Senior Managing Director at McLarty Associates, where he leads the Climate and Sustainability and Southeast Asia practices, said.\n\nBlake noted that Democrats would have opportunities to challenge some of President Donald Trump\u2019s tariff measures when they assume control of the House of Representatives and Senate in January 2027.\n\nAmbassador Blake said that the GSP scheme, which provides duty-free access to the US market for thousands of products from designated developing countries, including Sri Lanka, had lapsed in 2020.\n\nHe said its renewal was unlikely to be an immediate priority for the incoming Congress. However, if Democrats decided to revive the program, they could seek stronger provisions relating to workers\u2019 rights, environmental protection and human rights.\n\n\u201cThe latter in particular could bring renewed scrutiny on Sri Lanka,\u201d he warned.\n\nBlake stressed that Sri Lanka needed to strengthen its engagement with the new Congress, particularly as newly elected lawmakers might have limited knowledge of the country and its economic interests.\n\nSuggesting several key steps to improve prospects of securing support for Sri Lanka to get effectively engaged with the new Congress, he stressed the important of appointing a new Ambassador to Washington have an active diplomatic representation.\n\n\u201cI think Mahinda Samarasinghe did a great job representing Sri Lanka and was very active in engaging both the executive branch and Capitol Hill,\u201d he noted.\n\nCommending President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s efforts in addressing corruption, he said that he believes the new Democratic Congress was likely to scrutinise the human rights records of trading partners much more closely than the previous Congress did."
