    "chunk_text": "Commending President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s efforts in addressing corruption, he said that he believes the new Democratic Congress was likely to scrutinise the human rights records of trading partners much more closely than the previous Congress did.\n\n\u201cWe have been talking about repealing of the PTA for 20 years since I was an ambassador there, and it\u2019s long outlived its usefulness,\u201d he said.\n\nHe added that repealing the legislation could also improve Sri Lanka\u2019s prospects of securing the renewal of the European Union\u2019s GSP Plus trade facility.\n\nBlake further called for tangible progress on reconciliation, including the demilitarisation of the Northern and Eastern provinces, the return of seized land and steps towards the devolution of power.\n\n\u201cThese are all longstanding issues that have been promised and shouldn\u2019t be that difficult to do,\u201d he said.\n\nHe noted that the Government had an opportunity to advance these measures while President Anura Kumara Dissanayake continued to enjoy significant parliamentary support.\n\nBlake\u2019s observations highlighted the importance of proactive diplomacy and progress on domestic policy issues as Sri Lanka prepares to engage with a new US Congress, where trade access and human rights could receive greater attention.\n\nFormer Foreign Secretary and Sri Lankan Ambassador to the United States Ravinatha Aryasinha explained the changing US political landscape from Sri Lanka\u2019s foreign policy and economic perspectives.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka had significantly recalibrated its foreign policy following the 2022 economic crisis, moving towards a more balanced position that enabled it to maintain constructive relations with major powers.\n\nAryasinha, who is the Executive Director of the Regional Centre for Strategic Studies, stressed the importance of preserving the diplomatic space created through this approach, particularly as Sri Lanka approaches the conclusion of its current International Monetary Fund (IMF) program in March 2027."
