    "chunk_text": "Aryasinha, who is the Executive Director of the Regional Centre for Strategic Studies, stressed the importance of preserving the diplomatic space created through this approach, particularly as Sri Lanka approaches the conclusion of its current International Monetary Fund (IMF) program in March 2027.\n\nHe noted that international stakeholders had supported Sri Lanka\u2019s economic stabilisation partly because of their economic and strategic interests in the country.\n\nAccording to Aryasinha trade, investment and regional security as three major areas requiring careful attention in managing relations with Washington.\n\nCommenting on trade, he expressed concern over relatively flat growth in Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to the United States, citing uncertainty surrounding Trump\u2019s tariff policies, the absence of a trade agreement and potentially weaker consumer demand in Western markets.\n\nHe observed that Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel industry had remained competitive primarily because of the quality of its products rather than preferential trade arrangements.\n\n\u201cSri Lankan apparel manufacturers compete successfully because they produce good-quality products. However, if tariffs increase or consumers reduce their purchases, the quality of the products alone will not be enough. This is something we need to watch closely and try to protect,\u201d he said.\n\nAmbassador Aryasinha acknowledged that attracting substantial foreign direct investment had remained a longstanding challenge, even during favourable economic periods.\n\nHe identified China\u2019s established presence in Sri Lanka as a strategic consideration in US investment decisions but cautioned Washington against allowing its competition with Beijing to constrain engagement with Colombo.\n\n\u201cI am not telling the US that they must ignore their red lines,\u201d he said, adding that demanding exclusive strategic alignment from Sri Lanka would be counterproductive."
