    "chunk_text": "He identified China\u2019s established presence in Sri Lanka as a strategic consideration in US investment decisions but cautioned Washington against allowing its competition with Beijing to constrain engagement with Colombo.\n\n\u201cI am not telling the US that they must ignore their red lines,\u201d he said, adding that demanding exclusive strategic alignment from Sri Lanka would be counterproductive.\n\nAryasinha also referred to the regional implications of tensions involving Iran and the wider Middle East, stressing that Sri Lanka should retain the flexibility to make decisions based on its national interests.\n\nHe said the changing composition of the US Congress could bring renewed emphasis on human rights, reconciliation and democratic governance in Washington\u2019s relations with Sri Lanka.\n\nWhile acknowledging the importance of addressing these concerns meaningfully, he cautioned against using human rights as an instrument of political pressure.\n\nHaving served as Sri Lanka\u2019s Ambassador in Geneva from 2012 to 2018, Aryasinha said human rights were used more as a means of keeping Sri Lanka under scrutiny.\n\n\u201cThe US did not provide us with offensive weapons, but it did provide intelligence, the war would probably have continued for many more years,\u201d he commended the US assistance during the final stages of Sri Lanka\u2019s ar.\n\nDespite differences over human rights, Aryasinha noted that bilateral relations had remained relatively stable through difficult periods.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka had so far managed the changing international environment without suffering major diplomatic setbacks, although it might not have secured all the gains it could have achieved.\n\nConcluding his remarks, he stressed that Sri Lanka should remain responsive to US concerns while expecting the US to recognise the importance of Sri Lanka\u2019s independent decision-making.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka does best when it is allowed to kind of come to these decisions on its own,\u201d he said."
