    "chunk_text": "Concluding his remarks, he stressed that Sri Lanka should remain responsive to US concerns while expecting the US to recognise the importance of Sri Lanka\u2019s independent decision-making.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka does best when it is allowed to kind of come to these decisions on its own,\u201d he said.\n\nHe described the Democratic gains as a significant political setback for the Trump administration, although he cautioned against interpreting the results as an outright rejection of its foreign policy.\n\n\u201cThe best way to say where we stand right now is that we feel to be at the beginning of the end of the Trump era,\u201d Dr. Markey said, noting that President Trump would nevertheless retain considerable executive power during the remaining two years of his term.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and Nepal, Markey said the future of relations with Washington would depend heavily on the strength of American diplomatic and government institutions rather than solely on decisions taken by the White House.\n\n\u201cThe principal area of concern with dealing with the United States is process and institutions,\u201d Dr. Markey, whose expertise are mainly focused on international relations and U.S. policy in Asia, said.\n\nHe highlighted the importance of professional diplomats and officials in the State Department, Defence Department and National Security Council in sustaining regional cooperation.\n\nMarkey questioned whether Washington would return to a more institutionalised approach to international relations or continue with a more personalised and transactional foreign policy.\n\nCommenting on India-US relations, he said the election outcome was likely to be viewed positively in New Delhi, particularly following two years of friction over trade, the Iran war, Russia and Washington\u2019s relations with Pakistan.\n\n\u201cThe elections for India will in general terms be perceived as a welcome development,\u201d he said."
