    "chunk_text": "Commenting on India-US relations, he said the election outcome was likely to be viewed positively in New Delhi, particularly following two years of friction over trade, the Iran war, Russia and Washington\u2019s relations with Pakistan.\n\n\u201cThe elections for India will in general terms be perceived as a welcome development,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said the incoming Congress could provide further support for this cooperation.\n\nHowever, Washington\u2019s changing approach towards China remained a major concern for India.\n\nMarkey noted that for nearly two decades, US policy towards the Indo-Pacific had been shaped partly by strategic competition with China, making India an important regional partner.\n\n\u201cIndia was perceived by Washington as being very important, sometimes of principal importance,\u201d he said.\n\nHe observed that the Trump administration had appeared to move away from this approach at various points, raising questions about Washington\u2019s long-term commitment to the Indo-Pacific.\n\n\u201cNone of these things provide the same confidence about U.S. commitment to its place of global leadership and especially not its commitment to the Indo-Pacific,\u201d he said.\n\nDr. Markey said the next US administration could take a different direction on trade, alliances, climate change and multilateral cooperation, although a complete return to the Biden administration\u2019s approach should not be assumed.\n\nThe webinar provided an opportunity to exchange views on how the changing political landscape in the United States could influence international relations and the strategic and economic environment of South Asia."
