    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7ff0d05f9e2047ceb3e11ec6389576a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget: From economic stabilisation to economic transformation\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake presents the 2027 national Budget to Parliament, pivoting the national agenda from post-crisis stabilisation to long-term economic transformation\n\nThe Sri Lankan 2027 Budget, presented on 12 November 2026 by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake in his dual role as the Finance Minister, positions 2027 as the critical next phase of the country\u2019s recovery and economic transformation, following the sovereign debt default and deep economic crisis of 2022. It prioritises macroeconomic stability, continued reform under the IMF-supported program and the creation of a more productive, export-oriented economy. The Budget seeks to balance fiscal discipline with growth, aiming to raise productivity, accelerate the digital economy, attract investment, create jobs and support a long-term national economic renaissance while maintaining social equity. This represents significant progress for Sri Lanka, but key policy areas require deepening in the months ahead \u2013 particularly regarding future engagement with the IMF \u2013 alongside the accelerated implementation of free trade agreements (FTAs) and streamlined business regulations for trading firms.\n\nOverall direction of the Budget: From stabilisation to economic transformation\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s national Budget for 2027 marks a strategic pivot. While the 2025 Budget focused on economic stability and 2026 targeted production, investment, digitalisation and institutional reforms, the 2027 Budget zeroes in on the economic transformation required for sustained, inclusive growth. The proposals were widely welcomed by business bodies, including the apex Ceylon Chamber of Commerce and the Hotel Association of Sri Lanka."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-55618b93",
    "num": "dailyftlk-55618b93",
    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s 2027 Budget: From economic stabilisation to economic transformation",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Sri-Lanka-s-2027-Budget-From-economic-stabilisation-to-economic-transformation/4-800480",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795462036.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-55618b93-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8dd77e1636cdf610a626027ca07daa0e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s national Budget for 2027 marks a strategic pivot. While the 2025 Budget focused on economic stability and 2026 targeted production, investment, digitalisation and institutional reforms, the 2027 Budget zeroes in on the economic transformation required for sustained, inclusive growth. The proposals were widely welcomed by business bodies, including the apex Ceylon Chamber of Commerce and the Hotel Association of Sri Lanka.\n\nTo permanently exit the shadow of the 2022 debt crisis, Sri Lanka must foster a more outward-oriented, productive and inclusive economy. This requires capitalising on opportunities in tourism, the digital economy, niche manufacturing and agriculture. The 2027 Budget aims to make that leap, though transformation will require sustained political will to overcome historical domestic distortions, weak institutions and previous policy missteps.\n\nSupporting cost-effective public and private investment with high fiscal multipliers is the critical first step. The Government announced a substantial LKR 2 trillion (4% of GDP, $ 6 billion) public investment program. The Budget seeks to provide tax relief through revised tax slabs, increase capital expenditure and stimulate growth, all while maintaining strict fiscal discipline as Sri Lanka approaches the end of its 17th IMF Extended Fund Facility (EFF) in March 2027.\n\nHow to support growth and economic transformation: Alignment between the ODI Global/CEPA report and the 2027 Budget\n\nThe 2027 Budget strongly aligns with the broader trajectory of the ODI Global and CEPA growth report, which outlined 30 proposals to sustain growth through 2030. While the Budget embraces many of the report\u2019s recommendations on macroeconomic stability, productivity, export promotion, investment, digitalisation and State capacity, greater specificity is needed regarding trade integration, labour-market reform, working with the IMF and capacity-building within the State."
  },
