    "chunk_size_bytes": 1660,
    "chunk_text": "The 2027 Budget strongly aligns with the broader trajectory of the ODI Global and CEPA growth report, which outlined 30 proposals to sustain growth through 2030. While the Budget embraces many of the report\u2019s recommendations on macroeconomic stability, productivity, export promotion, investment, digitalisation and State capacity, greater specificity is needed regarding trade integration, labour-market reform, working with the IMF and capacity-building within the State.\n\nA key recommendation from our recent research is to evolve the current goods-only India-Sri Lanka FTA into a deeper, comprehensive agreement. Encouragingly, the Budget signals a renewed commitment to regional integration: \u201cWe expect to update the existing Free Trade Agreements and enter into new trade agreements with more countries in order to connect Sri Lanka to the global trade network\u2026An officials\u2019 committee has already been appointed by the Cabinet of Ministers to expand the existing Free Trade Agreement with India.\u201d The Budget also prioritises the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP), a new FTA with China, and Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreements with the United Arab Emirates and Oman.\n\nWhile this is good progress, the global trade landscape is shifting rapidly. With world trade projected to outperform IMF and WTO forecasts in 2026, and India aggressively pursuing FTAs with the EU and the UK, Sri Lanka must inject urgency into its trade agenda and rapidly build its institutional negotiation capacity. Our forthcoming work includes suggestions for how a deeper FTA with India can unlock substantial economic dividends for Sri Lanka."
