    "md5": "4f2fa5c31a54b78b8c719db21443036c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "The 2027 Budget rightly positions the digital economy as a major pillar of Sri Lanka\u2019s future growth model, moving beyond recovery towards a knowledge- and technology-based economy. To remain competitive, Sri Lanka must transition from relying on low wages and natural resources to competing on skills, innovation, technology adoption and digital connectivity.\u00a0 As highlighted in a recent ODI Global / SLASSCOM report, many trading firms already leverage e-commerce for global reach,\u00a0 but critical gaps in customs logistics, cross-border payments, cybersecurity and digital literacy require urgent, targeted policy intervention.\n\nDrawing on insights from our recent high-level ODI Global roundtable, convened on the margins of the IMF-World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok this October, the blueprint for maintaining growth under IMF program is clear. The roundtable brought together senior officials \u2013 including the Deputy Governor of the Central Bank of Tanzania and the Zambian High Commissioner to the UK \u2013 alongside think tank leaders and representatives from the IMF and World Bank to distil lessons from Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Thailand, Tanzania and Zambia.\n\nKey takeaways for Sri Lanka and other debt-distressed nations include:\n\n1. Tailored IMF programs: IMF program have evolved from a one-size-fits-all template to highly customised frameworks covering adjustment, financing and structural reforms. Countries need to prepare better.\n\n2. Stabilisation success: In the Asian and African contexts analysed, IMF programs have successfully tackled balance of payments crises, supported positive growth trajectories, curbed inflation, mobilised revenue and rebuilt foreign exchange reserves.\n\n3. Complex creditor coordination: A multi-creditor landscape complicates debt restructuring, demanding patient diplomacy and sophisticated coordination."
