    "md5": "6453c6e1c267a7a5c770591609d6206a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "2. Stabilisation success: In the Asian and African contexts analysed, IMF programs have successfully tackled balance of payments crises, supported positive growth trajectories, curbed inflation, mobilised revenue and rebuilt foreign exchange reserves.\n\n3. Complex creditor coordination: A multi-creditor landscape complicates debt restructuring, demanding patient diplomacy and sophisticated coordination.\n\n5. Proactive future planning: Debt-distressed countries should actively explore early options for follow-on IMF engagement, whether through full-disbursement programs or surveillance-only arrangements.\n\nDelivering this ambitious reform agenda requires a fundamental shift in State capacity and political consensus. Sri Lanka\u2019s economic history is defined by the partial and inconsistent implementation of policies, a cycle that has repeatedly constrained growth. To break this pattern, two urgent institutional reforms are required:\n\nn Establish an independent growth commission:\u00a0 Drawing on successful models from South Korea, Malaysia, Singapore, Vietnam and India, this body would formulate and insulate a long-term national growth plan.\n\nn Elevate public sector training: Sri Lanka must establish a world-class public policy and administration training school for civil servants and parliamentarians, delivered in partnership with a leading international university. Furthermore, existing bodies, such as the Sri Lanka Institute of Development Administration, should be twinned with elite international counterparts in\u00a0 Malaysia and Australia.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economy has successfully stabilised following a deep crisis. However, insulating the country from future shocks requires a relentless commitment to economic transformation. The 2027 Budget sets a strong, forward-looking direction that echoes several core ODI/CEPA proposals. Yet, against a backdrop of global economic volatility, the window for action is narrow."
