    "description": "Shanakiyan questions Govt.\u2019s rights record as HRCSL warnings on Bills go unheeded",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Shanakiyan-questions-Govt-s-rights-record-as-HRCSL-warnings-on-Bills-go-unheeded/56-800491",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795480792.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c2d6fea9-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5bbacb383df4471d4d492315e7a77758",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "Shanakiyan questions Govt.\u2019s rights record as HRCSL warnings on Bills go unheeded\n\nSays Govt. cannot claim domestic accountability works while ignoring its own Human Rights Commission\n\nHRCSL found PTA-like provisions in Organised Crime Bill, called for its withdrawal\n\nSays ITAK will pursue justice for Chemmani victims through Courts, not political theatrics\n\nUrges Election Commission to hold Provincial Council polls, postponed for years, at least in 2027\n\nSeeks beach-cleaning machinery for Batticaloa and a raise in the Rs. 750 parliamentary staff allowance, unchanged for a decade\n\nIlankai Tamil Arasu Katchi (ITAK) Parliamentary Group Leader MP Shanakiyan Rasamanickam has questioned the Government\u2019s commitment to human rights and accountability. He said it was going ahead with two security-related Bills despite serious objections from the Human Rights Commission of Sri Lanka (HRCSL).\n\nSpeaking in Parliament, Rasamanickam asked how the Government could keep telling the international community that domestic mechanisms were enough to deliver justice and accountability while it disregarded the recommendations of its own independent Commission.\n\nHe said the HRCSL had written to the Minister of Justice and National Integration about the Prevention of Organised Crime Bill. The Commission identified provisions resembling those in the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), warned that the proposed law threatened fundamental rights and freedoms, and called for the Bill\u2019s withdrawal.\n\nIt had raised similar concerns about the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill, warning that provisions introduced under the pretext of protecting the State could seriously undermine citizens\u2019 fundamental rights and freedoms.\n\n\u201cHow can the Government proceed with these Bills without addressing the concerns raised by its own Human Rights Commission? This is a serious issue that raises fundamental questions about the Government\u2019s commitment to human rights and accountability,\u201d Rasamanickam said."
  },
