    "md5": "64897afbd0563f88db4ab001df82cb58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2019,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cHow can the Government proceed with these Bills without addressing the concerns raised by its own Human Rights Commission? This is a serious issue that raises fundamental questions about the Government\u2019s commitment to human rights and accountability,\u201d Rasamanickam said.\n\n\u201cIf the Government is unwilling to listen to its own independent Human Rights Commission, how can the Tamil people or the international community be expected to place confidence in domestic accountability mechanisms?\u201d he asked.\n\nTurning to the Northern and Eastern Provinces, Rasamanickam said long-standing problems of land occupation, restrictions on fundamental freedoms, intimidation and harassment remained unresolved. The continued failure to return land to its rightful owners was depriving affected communities of their fundamental rights and preventing them from rebuilding their lives, he said.\n\nCivilians, journalists and people advocating for justice and accountability also continued to face intimidation and harassment. He urged the HRCSL to investigate these matters thoroughly and said he was concerned that such allegations kept emerging under the present Government despite its promises of democratic reform and respect for fundamental rights.\n\nOn the Chemmani mass grave investigation, Rasamanickam responded to remarks the Minister of Justice made in Parliament about a report submitted by archaeologist Prof. Somadeva. The Minister had described the document as merely a report and indicated that the Court had taken a similar position. Rasamanickam said ITAK remained committed to pursuing justice through the judicial process.\n\nHe noted that ITAK General Secretary President\u2019s Counsel M. A. Sumanthiran had appeared before the Court in person and had submitted affidavits on behalf of individuals willing to undergo DNA testing, to help further investigations.\n\n\u201cWe are pursuing justice for the victims of Chemmani through the Courts. We have no intention of engaging in cheap political theatrics,\u201d he said."
  },
