    "md5": "230dd0d978ee658104cce6e7a1cec8af",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1846,
    "chunk_text": "He noted that ITAK General Secretary President\u2019s Counsel M. A. Sumanthiran had appeared before the Court in person and had submitted affidavits on behalf of individuals willing to undergo DNA testing, to help further investigations.\n\n\u201cWe are pursuing justice for the victims of Chemmani through the Courts. We have no intention of engaging in cheap political theatrics,\u201d he said.\n\nRasamanickam noted that the HRCSL\u2019s report on the Northern and Eastern Provinces had also addressed the Chemmani issue. He said justice for the victims and their families required credible investigations, forensic evidence and meaningful judicial action rather than political statements.\n\nRasamanickam also called on the Judicial Service Commission (JSC) to resolve an administrative problem in the Batticaloa District without further delay. Fines collected by the Police within the jurisdiction of the Valaichchenai Pradeshiya Sabha were being credited to another local authority, he said.\n\nThe matter had been brought to the JSC\u2019s attention and raised repeatedly with the Minister of Justice, but it had remained unresolved for about a year. The Minister had indicated that it required the JSC\u2019s intervention. Rasamanickam also highlighted a shortage of staff in the Court Registrars\u2019 Service, which he said was contributing to delays in judicial proceedings.\n\nHe urged the Election Commission to ensure Provincial Council elections are held at least in 2027. He noted that while the Commission had most recently conducted the Local Government elections, the provincial polls had been postponed for several years. The absence of elected Provincial Councils deprived people of their democratic right to representation at the provincial level, he said, and he urged the authorities to use the funds allocated for elections to hold the polls next year."
  },
