    "md5": "731fe7e94512a64b2d288c6195330297",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "NCE renews call for clear timeline on Sri Lanka\u2019s accession to Madrid System\n\nThe National Chamber of Exporters of Sri Lanka (NCE) has warned that Sri Lanka\u2019s accession to the World Intellectual Property Organisation\u2019s (WIPO) Madrid System is still awaiting the completion of the required legislative amendments.\n\nThe National Intellectual Property Office of Sri Lanka (NIPO) has reviewed the amended Bill and communicated the required corrections to the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department, and is awaiting the final Bill before it can be submitted to the Attorney General\u2019s Department for legal clearance. No confirmed timeline has yet been provided for the country\u2019s accession to the system.\n\nThe NCE has brought the matter to the attention of NIPO and the Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Co-operative Development Minister multiple times, highlighting the need for clarity on the remaining steps and a practical timeframe for completion. The Chamber has also offered to provide private-sector input to support the process.\n\nAccording to the latest update, the accession process has reached an advanced stage. The Intellectual Property (Amendment) Bill, incorporating the provisions required for Sri Lanka\u2019s accession to the Madrid Protocol, had been forwarded to the Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Co-operative Development Ministry Secretary. Following a review of the amended draft, observations submitted by NIPO had been incorporated, subject to clarifications on two sections identified by the Legal Draftsman.\n\nThe required corrections were communicated to the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department, with a request for the final Bill. Once the final Bill is received, the Ministry will submit it to the Attorney General\u2019s Department for legal clearance, after which it will proceed through the standard legislative process in Parliament. Despite the lack of a definitive date at this stage, the process is moving forward."
  },
