    "md5": "aa11a47dea81a022c0aa2e8149c1099e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "The required corrections were communicated to the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department, with a request for the final Bill. Once the final Bill is received, the Ministry will submit it to the Attorney General\u2019s Department for legal clearance, after which it will proceed through the standard legislative process in Parliament. Despite the lack of a definitive date at this stage, the process is moving forward.\n\nEstimates gathered by the NCE from member companies indicate that registering a trademark in an individual overseas market can cost from a few hundred US dollars to several thousand US dollars. These estimates include official filing fees, professional charges and other associated expenses, with the final cost depending on the country and the brand.\n\nThe expense can increase when a business seeks protection in several markets or across different classes of goods and services. This can be especially difficult for small and medium-sized exporters that are building their brands overseas and have to account for the cost of registration in each market.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s accession to the Madrid System would provide a more straightforward route to international trademark registration. The system allows an eligible applicant to submit a single international application through the country\u2019s own intellectual property office and request protection in any or all of the member countries. This replaces the need to submit separate applications to each participating country, while providing a centralised process for managing international registrations.\n\nThe reach of the system has also grown. WIPO\u2019s Madrid System currently has 117 members covering 133 countries and representing more than 80% of global trade. In 2025, an estimated 64,150 international trademark applications were filed through the system, with Asia accounting for 25.6% of the total."
  },
