    "md5": "e6f47f8e3b6a58c931f10e9004f588dc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "The reach of the system has also grown. WIPO\u2019s Madrid System currently has 117 members covering 133 countries and representing more than 80% of global trade. In 2025, an estimated 64,150 international trademark applications were filed through the system, with Asia accounting for 25.6% of the total.\n\nThe system is especially significant to Sri Lankan businesses whose products and services are entering international markets under local brands. This includes exporters in sectors such as tea, apparel, spices, gems and jewellery, processed foods and IT services. As companies develop value-added products and build recognition in overseas markets, protecting their names and trademarks becomes an essential part of their export plans.\n\nWithout registration in a particular market, for instance, a business may face difficulties if another party registers the same or a similar brand there. Resolving such disputes can be costly and time-consuming, and may affect the local company\u2019s ability to use its brand in that market. A more accessible international registration process would therefore be crucial to companies expanding into several export destinations.\n\nTrademark protection is also part of the intellectual property environment that overseas investors consider when assessing a country for business and investment. Access to an established international registration system can provide greater clarity for foreign companies looking at partnerships, manufacturing operations, franchise arrangements and other commercial activities involving Sri Lankan businesses.\n\nThe NCE has been collecting cost information from member companies and consulting sectoral representatives to support its work on the issue. The Chamber considers Sri Lanka\u2019s accession to the Madrid System relevant to the country\u2019s larger export ambitions, especially as local businesses seek to build internationally recognised brands and increase the value of their products and services."
  },
