    "md5": "ac94aa2b147dd041ab3ff3010cc27db0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "From Colombo\u2019s floods to climate finance: Wake-up call for Sri Lanka\n\nMaldivian President Dr. Mohammed Muizzu\u2019s call for fairer climate finance at COP31 in Antalya, Turkey, comes at a particularly relevant time for Sri Lanka. With Colombo still grappling with the aftermath of recent flooding and the country recovering from the devastation caused by Cyclone Ditwah, the growing cost of extreme weather is becoming increasingly difficult to ignore.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the challenge is not only to protect lives, livelihoods and infrastructure from future disasters, but also to find ways of financing these efforts without adding further pressure to an already fragile economic recovery.\n\nOn the 6 November 2026, heavy rain brought parts of Colombo to a standstill. Roads disappeared beneath floodwater, motorists were stranded, shops struggled to protect their goods and sections of the National Hospital were inundated. For thousands of people trying to get home from work, run a business or reach essential services, the disruption was immediate. The flooding also exposed problems that have troubled the capital for years. Blocked canals, inadequate drainage, unauthorised construction and the steady loss of open spaces have made it increasingly difficult for rainwater to escape. Following the floods, the Colombo Municipal Council identified 60 locations requiring urgent intervention. For a shopkeeper whose stock has been damaged, or a worker who loses a day\u2019s income because transport has stopped, these are not abstract infrastructure problems. They have real financial consequences.\n\nThe events in Colombo provide a timely backdrop to the intervention made by Maldivian President Dr. Mohammed Muizzu at COP31 in Antalya, Turkey on the 11 November 2026. Dr. Muizzu questioned why countries highly vulnerable to climate impacts should be expected to borrow heavily to protect themselves against a crisis they have contributed relatively little to creating."
  },
