    "chunk_text": "The events in Colombo provide a timely backdrop to the intervention made by Maldivian President Dr. Mohammed Muizzu at COP31 in Antalya, Turkey on the 11 November 2026. Dr. Muizzu questioned why countries highly vulnerable to climate impacts should be expected to borrow heavily to protect themselves against a crisis they have contributed relatively little to creating.\n\nThe destruction caused by Cyclone Ditwah in November 2025 demonstrates how quickly a natural disaster can become a national economic problem. The World Bank\u2019s initial rapid assessment estimated direct physical damage at $ 4.1 billion, equivalent to approximately 4% of Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP. This figure did not include lost income, disrupted production or the full cost of recovery and reconstruction. In the transport sector alone, the World Bank subsequently estimated recovery requirements of $ 1.31 billion following extensive damage to provincial and rural roads and bridges. Behind these figures are communities whose everyday lives depend on functioning infrastructure.\n\nGiving greater consideration to climate vulnerability in international financing decisions could help address this imbalance. Such recognition should not mean weaker accountability. Projects must still meet appropriate standards for procurement, environmental protection, technical design and long-term maintenance. The challenge is to make climate finance more accessible without compromising the quality of the investments it supports\n\nWhen a rural road is washed away, a farmer may be unable to transport vegetables to market. A small business may lose customers because deliveries cannot get through. Children may miss school, while patients face longer journeys to reach medical care. The damage to the road is visible. The wider economic losses are often harder to measure."
  },
