    "md5": "8b2f640300e922e862b658ffabd2893c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "When a rural road is washed away, a farmer may be unable to transport vegetables to market. A small business may lose customers because deliveries cannot get through. Children may miss school, while patients face longer journeys to reach medical care. The damage to the road is visible. The wider economic losses are often harder to measure.\n\nOne of the strongest points in Dr. Muizzu\u2019s argument concerns how the international financial system values investments that prevent future losses. A renewable energy project can earn revenue by selling electricity. A flood protection scheme strengthened embankment or improved drainage network generally does not generate income in the same way. Yet the benefits can be considerable.\n\nConsider Colombo\u2019s recent flooding. Better drainage, properly maintained canals, and protected water-retention areas could reduce the disruption caused by intense rainfall. Businesses would be less likely to lose trading days, households would face fewer repair bills and public services could continue operating. The value of these investments lies in the damage they help avoid. That creates a financing challenge.\n\nGovernments are often expected to borrow for infrastructure projects even when the benefits are widely shared and cannot be recovered through user charges. For countries already managing substantial debt obligations, this can make essential adaptation investments difficult to finance.\n\nDr. Muizzu\u2019s call for greater grant support is therefore particularly relevant to projects that protect public welfare without generating direct commercial returns. Concessional loans, with affordable interest rates and long repayment periods, can also play a useful role where borrowing remains sustainable. Private investment may be appropriate for projects capable of generating reliable revenue. What matters is choosing financing arrangements suited to the project, rather than treating all climate investments as commercial opportunities."
  },
