    "md5": "b4ef864fde35b166930f6e9a857c3c2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1834,
    "chunk_text": "Dr. Muizzu\u2019s call for greater grant support is therefore particularly relevant to projects that protect public welfare without generating direct commercial returns. Concessional loans, with affordable interest rates and long repayment periods, can also play a useful role where borrowing remains sustainable. Private investment may be appropriate for projects capable of generating reliable revenue. What matters is choosing financing arrangements suited to the project, rather than treating all climate investments as commercial opportunities.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s circumstances are different, but coastal communities face their own pressures. Around a quarter of the population lives within one kilometre of the coastline. Fishing settlements, tourism facilities, agricultural land and freshwater supplies are exposed to coastal erosion, flooding and saltwater intrusion. For a fishing family, the loss of a landing site or damage to a home can threaten a livelihood built over generations. For coastal businesses, repeated flooding can make investment and recovery increasingly difficult. The concern is not only about protecting physical assets. It is also about preserving communities and the economic activities that sustain them.\n\nThe scale of the global adaptation challenge adds urgency to Dr. Muizzu\u2019s appeal. According to the United Nations Environment Program\u2019s 2026 Adaptation Gap Report, developing countries will require an estimated $ 305 billion annually for adaptation by 2035. Even if the COP30 aspiration to triple international public adaptation finance by that year is achieved, UNEP estimates that the resulting funding would meet only 20% to 29% of developing countries\u2019 needs. This leaves governments facing difficult choices about which investments to prioritise and which risks they can afford to address."
