    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "The scale of the global adaptation challenge adds urgency to Dr. Muizzu\u2019s appeal. According to the United Nations Environment Program\u2019s 2026 Adaptation Gap Report, developing countries will require an estimated $ 305 billion annually for adaptation by 2035. Even if the COP30 aspiration to triple international public adaptation finance by that year is achieved, UNEP estimates that the resulting funding would meet only 20% to 29% of developing countries\u2019 needs. This leaves governments facing difficult choices about which investments to prioritise and which risks they can afford to address.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the immediate challenge is particularly visible in damaged roads, flood-prone neighbourhoods, vulnerable coastal settlements and communities exposed to landslides.\n\nEven when international climate funds are available, securing support is not always straightforward. Funding applications require detailed project proposals, feasibility studies, technical assessments and financial planning. Institutions must demonstrate that projects are properly designed, environmentally sound and capable of delivering lasting benefits. These requirements are important, but preparing credible proposals takes time, money and specialist expertise. Sri Lanka has taken steps to improve its access to international climate finance.\n\nDFCC Bank has been accredited to seek funding directly from the Green Climate Fund since 2023. The Maldives achieved a similar milestone in October 2026, when Maldives Finance and Leasing Company became its first nationally accredited institution. Such accreditation allows national institutions to submit funding proposals without depending entirely on international intermediaries. It can also strengthen local involvement in deciding which projects deserve priority."
