    "md5": "0ad941b9abeecf2d50cfde4545a0a628",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1629,
    "chunk_text": "DFCC Bank has been accredited to seek funding directly from the Green Climate Fund since 2023. The Maldives achieved a similar milestone in October 2026, when Maldives Finance and Leasing Company became its first nationally accredited institution. Such accreditation allows national institutions to submit funding proposals without depending entirely on international intermediaries. It can also strengthen local involvement in deciding which projects deserve priority.\n\nDeveloped with support from UNDP and the United Kingdom, the strategy seeks to incorporate climate priorities into fiscal planning and investment decisions while identifying additional sources of finance. Its success will depend on how effectively national priorities are translated into practical projects, supported by coordination between Government agencies, financial institutions and development partners.\n\nDr. Muizzu has also questioned the reliance on income indicators when determining countries\u2019 eligibility for concessional financing. Speaking at COP31 on the 12 November 2026, he argued that income alone does not adequately reflect the vulnerabilities facing small states such as the Maldives.\n\nThe concern has relevance for Sri Lanka. A country\u2019s income classification may indicate its broad level of economic development, but it says relatively little about the damage a major flood, cyclone or landslide could cause. Nor does it fully reflect the pressure that repeated disasters place on public budgets. A country may be making progress in rebuilding its economy while remaining highly exposed to events capable of reversing those gains."
