    "chunk_size_bytes": 1719,
    "chunk_text": "The concern has relevance for Sri Lanka. A country\u2019s income classification may indicate its broad level of economic development, but it says relatively little about the damage a major flood, cyclone or landslide could cause. Nor does it fully reflect the pressure that repeated disasters place on public budgets. A country may be making progress in rebuilding its economy while remaining highly exposed to events capable of reversing those gains.\n\nColombo\u2019s recent floods also underline the importance of measures that do not necessarily require large construction projects. Timely warnings, reliable communication and clear emergency procedures can help people protect themselves and their belongings before conditions deteriorate. At COP31, Dr. Muizzu highlighted improved forecasting and community preparedness as essential components of climate resilience. Sri Lanka is taking steps in this direction.\n\nIn November, an EU-supported initiative was announced to pilot disaster preparedness measures in Badulla and Matara. The program will work with local authorities, schools and communities to improve warning communication, prepare response plans and conduct simulation exercises. These measures can be especially valuable in areas where residents have limited time to respond to flash floods or landslides. An early warning is useful only when it reaches people in time and they know what action to take. Equally important is maintaining existing infrastructure. Colombo\u2019s flooding demonstrated how blocked waterways and weaknesses in urban planning can worsen the effects of intense rainfall. Addressing such problems requires sustained attention, not only emergency clean-up operations after each disaster."
