    "chunk_text": "In November, an EU-supported initiative was announced to pilot disaster preparedness measures in Badulla and Matara. The program will work with local authorities, schools and communities to improve warning communication, prepare response plans and conduct simulation exercises. These measures can be especially valuable in areas where residents have limited time to respond to flash floods or landslides. An early warning is useful only when it reaches people in time and they know what action to take. Equally important is maintaining existing infrastructure. Colombo\u2019s flooding demonstrated how blocked waterways and weaknesses in urban planning can worsen the effects of intense rainfall. Addressing such problems requires sustained attention, not only emergency clean-up operations after each disaster.\n\nSri Lanka must also make a stronger case for climate financing that recognises the wider economic and social benefits of adaptation, supported by an appropriate mix of grants, concessional loans and investment. While the Maldives faces the threat of rising seas, Sri Lanka must contend with recurring floods, damaged infrastructure and disrupted livelihoods. Both countries share an interest in financing arrangements that reflect their vulnerabilities. Ultimately, the success of climate finance should be measured not merely by funds mobilised, but by the communities, livelihoods and development gains it helps protect."
