    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II\n\nIn the previous article (https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-I/4-800073), we explored the background factors feeding in to Colombo\u2019s recent stormwater stress. Today, we continue to examine features of a national plan to mitigate the devastating impact of flash floods in and beyond our beleaguered commercial capital.\n\nMay we make so bold as to present a recommended policy framework under the proposed title of Sri Lanka National Flood Resilience and Water Security Program (2027\u20132040).\n\nIts policy objective is to substantially reduce the human, economic, environmental and infrastructural consequences of flooding through integrated water management, climate-resilient infrastructure, ecological restoration and accountable governance.\n\nAnd its geographical scope would be national, with initial priority given to Greater Colombo and the Kelani, Kalu, Gin, Nilwala and Attanagalu Oya river basins.\n\nThis comes with implementation horizons of immediate measures (0\u201312 months), medium-term reforms (1\u20135 years) and structural transformation (5\u201314 years).\n\nThis is the highest ecological priority. The first principle should be to retain as much rainfall as possible within the natural landscape before it reaches urban drainage systems. This requires a national wetland and floodplain protection program.\n\nConduct a comprehensive satellite-based inventory of wetlands, marshes, paddy fields, retention ponds, floodplains and natural drainage paths.\n\nLegally protect designated flood-retention areas against reclamation and inappropriate development.\n\nRestore degraded wetlands, including those in the Greater Colombo basin.\n\nCreate additional retention ponds and urban lakes where hydrological studies demonstrate their suitability."
  },
