    "md5": "db4670f0bbb75ebf55eed905b133a5e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Legally protect designated flood-retention areas against reclamation and inappropriate development.\n\nRestore degraded wetlands, including those in the Greater Colombo basin.\n\nCreate additional retention ponds and urban lakes where hydrological studies demonstrate their suitability.\n\nAny development that removes or reduces an existing flood-retention area should be prohibited unless an independently verified assessment demonstrates that equivalent or greater storage capacity will be provided within the same hydrological catchment.\n\nPreferably, development should avoid destroying the original wetland altogether. Replacement storage elsewhere may not provide the same protection to the affected neighbourhood.\n\nThis matters enormously because wetlands provide flood protection without requiring constant pumping or mechanical intervention. They also provide biodiversity, improve water quality, reduce urban heat and contribute to carbon storage.\n\nThe World Bank\u2019s Colombo wetland assessment demonstrates the considerable hydrological value of these ecosystems.\n\nColombo requires a comprehensive reassessment of its entire stormwater network. Simply enlarging individual drains will not necessarily solve the problem. The system must be examined from the point where rainfall reaches the ground to the final point where water is discharged into the river or sea.\n\nReplace or enlarge undersized drains based on updated rainfall-intensity modelling.\n\nOne particularly important technical issue is the interaction between rainfall, river levels and tidal conditions.\n\nDrainage systems should be designed to function during compound events in which intense rainfall coincides with high Kelani River levels or unfavourable tides.\n\nThis is not theoretical. Research on Colombo\u2019s drainage network has documented the reduction in outfall capacity when high Kelani River levels necessitate closure of particular gates."
  },
