    "md5": "5afa58a70e33cadd83bcdb689018a482",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Drainage systems should be designed to function during compound events in which intense rainfall coincides with high Kelani River levels or unfavourable tides.\n\nThis is not theoretical. Research on Colombo\u2019s drainage network has documented the reduction in outfall capacity when high Kelani River levels necessitate closure of particular gates.\n\nThe audit should establish actual capacity of each major drainage channel; current condition and maintenance requirements; storage capacity available in wetlands and canals; pumping and outfall capacity; locations of recurrent bottlenecks; rainfall thresholds at which different parts of the system fail.\n\nThe results should be publicly accessible. The reported 30 mm/hour design capacity should be investigated in particular. And it should not automatically be assumed that every component of Colombo\u2019s drainage network has the same design standard.\n\nUrban planning must become an instrument of flood prevention. Sri Lanka should introduce mandatory flood-risk assessments for all significant development proposals.\n\nThese should apply particularly to residential housing schemes; commercial and industrial developments; roads and transport infrastructure; large-scale land reclamation; Government construction projects; and developments near rivers, wetlands and drainage reservations.\n\nA developer should demonstrate that a proposed project will not increase flood risk for neighbouring properties or communities.\n\nThe following measures are important (as are their intended outcomes):\n\nMeanwhile, Singapore\u2019s experience with integrated urban water management provides useful lessons, particularly its approach to combining drainage engineering, natural waterways, public spaces and water-sensitive urban design.\n\nSri Lanka should adapt such approaches to its own geography, rainfall patterns and financial circumstances rather than simply importing expensive engineering solutions."
  },
