    "md5": "d7fde9294101fcd1575aae1c4e7eaf23",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1608,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, Singapore\u2019s experience with integrated urban water management provides useful lessons, particularly its approach to combining drainage engineering, natural waterways, public spaces and water-sensitive urban design.\n\nSri Lanka should adapt such approaches to its own geography, rainfall patterns and financial circumstances rather than simply importing expensive engineering solutions.\n\nMay we recommend establishing a Greater Colombo Flood Resilience Authority (GCFRA), initially through legislation or an appropriate statutory framework.\n\nIt should coordinate the work of the Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation; Colombo Municipal Council; Urban Development Authority; Road Development Authority; Irrigation Department; Department of Meteorology; the National Disaster Management Centre; and relevant neighbouring local authorities.\n\nIts responsibilities should include basin-wide flood planning; drainage infrastructure coordination; wetland and retention-area protection; maintenance standards, flood-risk mapping; emergency coordination; capital investment planning; and public reporting and performance monitoring.\n\nThe authority should not necessarily absorb every existing institution or duplicate their technical expertise. Instead, it should establish a single accountable framework for outcomes that currently depend on multiple agencies.\n\nIts governing board should include independent engineering, hydrological, environmental and urban-planning expertise. And it should publish an annual report documenting progress, expenditure, infrastructure condition and flood-risk reduction."
