    "chunk_text": "Its governing board should include independent engineering, hydrological, environmental and urban-planning expertise. And it should publish an annual report documenting progress, expenditure, infrastructure condition and flood-risk reduction.\n\nFor the rest of the country, similar coordination arrangements should be established at river-basin level.\n\nSri Lanka should invest in a modern, integrated hydro-meteorological forecasting system.\n\nThis should combine Doppler weather radar; satellite rainfall observations; automated rain gauges; river-level sensors; reservoir-level monitoring; tide and coastal water-level observations; numerical weather prediction; and flood-inundation modelling.\n\nThe objective should be to provide increasingly accurate warnings at neighbourhood and river-basin level, rather than relying primarily on general warnings covering large geographical areas.\n\nFor Colombo, a useful system would be capable of identifying areas likely to receive extreme rainfall; expected rainfall intensity over the next one to six hours; locations where drains are approaching capacity; neighbourhoods likely to experience flash flooding; and whether river or tidal conditions will impede drainage.\n\nWarnings should be delivered through mobile networks, public broadcasting, local authorities and community communication networks.\n\nThe system should also distinguish between three types of warning: a) heavy rainfall expected; b) localised flash flooding imminent; and c) major river or basin flooding anticipated. These are different hazards and require different responses.\n\nAn important opportunity is for Sri Lanka\u2019s meteorological authorities to investigate whether MJO-informed sub-seasonal forecasting can improve preparedness during the October\u2013November second inter-monsoon period. The scientific evidence provides a basis for developing this capability."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
