    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2bfa4f3243e33ae700b9800e4dc4eb44",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "An important opportunity is for Sri Lanka\u2019s meteorological authorities to investigate whether MJO-informed sub-seasonal forecasting can improve preparedness during the October\u2013November second inter-monsoon period. The scientific evidence provides a basis for developing this capability.\n\nRecommended measures include reliable household waste collection; separate collection of recyclable materials; more effective management of construction debris; regular mechanical cleaning of drains; CCTV monitoring at recurrent illegal dumping locations; enforcement against commercial and industrial dumping; public reporting of blocked drains; and community participation in protecting local waterways.\n\nA useful innovation would be a publicly accessible digital reporting system through which residents can photograph and report blocked drains, illegal dumping and unauthorised structures. Each complaint should receive a reference number and a defined response period. And municipal authorities should publish monthly performance statistics.\n\nIllegal construction must be addressed, but enforcement should be fair and technically informed.\n\nIt is essential to distinguish between structures that directly obstruct drainage channels; developments that occupy designated flood-retention land; buildings that are exposed to flood risk but do not obstruct water movement; and informal settlements that have developed because of inadequate affordable housing. These categories should not be treated identically.\n\nWhere removal is necessary, affected residents must be provided with suitable alternative accommodation, secure tenure where appropriate and access to employment, education and public transport.\n\nConversely, commercial developers and other property owners who unlawfully obstruct waterways should not receive preferential treatment."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-II/4-800498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795482652.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5042fc89fb839a7a5b14c0c9a4e1a0c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "Where removal is necessary, affected residents must be provided with suitable alternative accommodation, secure tenure where appropriate and access to employment, education and public transport.\n\nConversely, commercial developers and other property owners who unlawfully obstruct waterways should not receive preferential treatment.\n\nFlood mitigation should extend beyond drainage. Hospitals, schools, power stations, telecommunications infrastructure, water-supply installations and transport networks must be designed to remain operational during floods.\n\nMeasures should include raising critical electrical and mechanical equipment above expected flood levels; protecting underground electrical installations; designing road drainage to minimise traffic disruption; providing emergency access routes; introducing flood-resistant construction standards; protecting drinking-water supplies against contamination; and requiring emergency continuity plans for essential public services.\n\nThe objective should be to prevent a local flood from becoming a national economic disruption.\n\nFlood resilience requires sustained investment. Sri Lanka should establish a dedicated, multi-year flood-resilience financing program, rather than depending on annual emergency allocations.\n\nPossible sources include Government capital expenditure; reprioritisation of existing urban-development projects; multilateral development financing; climate-adaptation funding; public-private partnerships (PPP) for suitable infrastructure; and environmental levies on developments that create additional runoff, subject to appropriate safeguards.\n\nA useful economic principle is that the cost of preventing flood damage should be compared with the expected recurring cost of inaction.\n\nThe World Bank has documented the considerable economic exposure associated with flooding in Greater Colombo. Its assessment of the May 2016 floods estimated approximately US$310 million in economic damage in Colombo."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-II/4-800498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795482652.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104-0007",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4936b7166c5068194d767943f229492e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1795,
    "chunk_text": "The World Bank has documented the considerable economic exposure associated with flooding in Greater Colombo. Its assessment of the May 2016 floods estimated approximately US$310 million in economic damage in Colombo.\n\nA policy is only as good as its implementation arrangements. The following is a proposed sequence.\n\nPhase I: Immediate intervention - 0\u201312 months (Nov. 2026\u2013Nov. 2027)\n\nExpected result: reduced vulnerability to foreseeable flooding and improved emergency response.\n\nExpected result: a measurable reduction in flood frequency, depth and duration in priority urban locations.\n\nExtend the program to vulnerable secondary cities and river basins.\n\nExpected result: a nationally integrated system capable of managing increasingly extreme rainfall with substantially reduced social and economic consequences.\n\nThe following proposed allocation of responsibilities may come to serve:\n\nA central requirement should be that each institution has clearly defined responsibilities, budgets and performance indicators. The public should not have to navigate a maze of agencies to discover who is responsible for a blocked canal or a failed drainage facility.\n\nWhat should Sri Lanka actually achieve? It is important not to define success simply as the absence of flooding. No responsible Government can guarantee that an exceptionally severe rainfall event will never inundate a city. And instead, the program should be assessed against measurable targets.\n\n(The writer is the Editor-at-large of LMD and is a senior journalist with a Post-graduate Diploma in Politics and Governance)\n\nMay we recommend the following proposed indicators and targets. These are policy targets, not existing Government commitments.\n\nRecurrent flood locations (Annual reduction in flood depth and duration)"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-II/4-800498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795482652.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104-0008",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "38c1c53a4c392b415686dd661d89dd2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "May we recommend the following proposed indicators and targets. These are policy targets, not existing Government commitments.\n\nRecurrent flood locations (Annual reduction in flood depth and duration)\n\nThere are several tempting responses that could prove expensive or counterproductive.\n\nFirstly, building bigger drains everywhere. Enlarging drains without improving storage and downstream discharge capacity may simply transfer flooding from one neighbourhood to another.\n\nSecondly, treating wetlands as expendable. Wetlands cannot be replaced by concrete drainage infrastructure alone. Their ecological and hydrological functions are too important.\n\nThirdly, responding with periodic canal-cleaning campaigns. Cleaning is essential, but it is not a substitute for adequate infrastructure, enforcement and permanent maintenance budgets.\n\nFourthly, relocating vulnerable communities without a social policy. Moving people away from flood-prone locations without providing viable housing and livelihoods can create a different social disaster.\n\nFifthly, treating every flood as an isolated event. The meteorological trigger may differ from one year to another, but the underlying vulnerabilities are often remarkably similar.\n\nSixthly, spending without independent evaluation. Every major drainage and flood-control project should undergo independent technical and financial assessment, including an evaluation of whether it actually reduces flood risk elsewhere in the basin.\n\nThere is potential for reconnecting Sri Lanka with its hydraulic civilisation, which is a larger national development opportunity. The island nation with its network of rivers, lakes and tanks possesses an extraordinary historical inheritance in water management.\n\nIts ancient civilisation developed sophisticated systems for collecting, storing, regulating and distributing water. The tank cascades of the dry zone demonstrate an understanding of hydrological interdependence, landscape management and community stewardship."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "num": "dailyftlk-c7524104",
    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-II/4-800498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795482652.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104-0009",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1d5008bb9e2e368c76b5ea551633d071",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1898,
    "chunk_text": "Its ancient civilisation developed sophisticated systems for collecting, storing, regulating and distributing water. The tank cascades of the dry zone demonstrate an understanding of hydrological interdependence, landscape management and community stewardship.\n\nA tank is connected to its catchment. A canal is connected to its outfall. A wetland is connected to its surrounding landscape. A river is connected to the communities upstream and downstream. This systemic understanding is precisely what modern flood management requires.\n\nThere is also an opportunity to reconnect environmental protection with economic development. Restored wetlands can become recreational spaces, biodiversity reserves and environmental education centres.\n\nBetter drainage can improve property values, public health and commercial productivity. More reliable flood forecasting can protect agricultural production and infrastructure investment.\n\nFlood resilience should therefore be understood as an investment in national prosperity.\n\nWe could draw five principal conclusions from this policy schema.\n\nFirst: Meteorological extremes are becoming an increasingly important planning challenge. Sri Lanka must prepare for rainfall events that exceed historical expectations. The November 2026 Colombo inundation demonstrates the consequences of designing infrastructure around insufficient rainfall-intensity assumptions.\n\nSecond: Human activity has amplified the consequences of natural hazards. Wetland destruction, unplanned development, inadequate drainage, blocked canals and poor land-use enforcement have reduced our country\u2019s capacity to absorb extreme rainfall.\n\nThird: The institutional issue is as important as the engineering problem. Multiple public agencies share responsibility for drainage, waterways, development and emergency management. Effective coordination and accountability are indispensable."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104",
