    "date_str": "2026-11-24",
    "description": "From monsoon to management: Colombo\u2019s heavy weather and building a flood-resilient Sri Lanka \u2013 Part II",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-monsoon-to-management-Colombo-s-heavy-weather-and-building-a-flood-resilient-Sri-Lanka-Part-II/4-800498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795482652.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailyftlk-c7524104-0010",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "16b6594b9be09311e46a1543f9677bc2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1903,
    "chunk_text": "Third: The institutional issue is as important as the engineering problem. Multiple public agencies share responsibility for drainage, waterways, development and emergency management. Effective coordination and accountability are indispensable.\n\nFifth: Prevention must become an economic and political priority. The recurring cost of flood damage, emergency expenditure, lost working days, disrupted commerce, damaged infrastructure and displaced households is likely to outweigh much of the investment required to improve resilience.\n\nIf the Government could concentrate on just six (shall we say) major interventions, perhaps it would do well to prioritise these:\n\n1. Protect the wetlands: Legally secure Colombo\u2019s remaining natural flood-retention areas.\n\n2. Rebuild drainage intelligently: Conduct a basin-wide engineering audit and upgrade the most critical bottlenecks.\n\n3. Unify responsibility: Establish an accountable Greater Colombo Flood Resilience Authority.\n\n4. Control development: Prevent further construction on floodplains and obstructed drainage reservations.\n\n5. Modernise forecasting: Introduce integrated rainfall, river-level and neighbourhood flood-warning systems.\n\n6. Fund maintenance permanently: Ensure that infrastructure remains functional long after the political attention surrounding a disaster has disappeared.\n\nSri Lanka cannot command the clouds \u2013 no matter how just or righteous its rulers are or claim to be \u2013 but it can certainly determine what happens when the rain falls.\n\nIt can preserve the land that absorbs water, build infrastructure that manages excess rainfall, plan settlements around natural drainage systems, and create institutions capable of anticipating and responding to disasters.\n\nThe real challenge is not simply to prevent the next flood. It is to ensure that the next exceptional rainfall event does not become the next national disaster."
  },
