    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "Supply constraints hit essential food commodity market\n\nEssential food commodities, both imported and locally produced, are facing tightening supply conditions, prompting traders to shift sourcing markets, according to suppliers.\n\nDried chillies and coriander have come under sharp price pressure, while the availability of imported onions and potatoes from India and Pakistan has also varied, according to the latest weekly price list issued by the Essential Food Commodities Importers and Traders Association (EFCITA).\n\nAccording to the wholesale price schedule submitted by EFCITA yesterday, dried chillies were quoted at Rs. 2,000 per kilogram, with traders reporting a shortage and warning that prices could rise further. Deluxe-grade dried chillies, a premium quality category, were priced at Rs. 2,500 per kilogram.\n\nThe price increase follows recent delays in customs clearance at the Colombo Port involving Indian chilli consignments over moisture-content specifications, an importer said. The issue has been attributed to high humidity and El Ni\u00f1o-related weather conditions. Coriander was similarly priced at Rs. 500 per kilogram amid a shortage linked to moisture-related issues.\n\nIn the vegetable import market, Indian and Chinese potatoes were unavailable on the wholesale price list, leaving Pakistani potatoes as the main imported variety, at Rs. 160\u2013170 per kilogram. Local potatoes traded at significantly higher prices of Rs. 370\u2013380 per kilogram, while WMDA stocks were quoted at Rs. 320 per kilogram.\n\nWith no local big onions available in the market, Indian big onions traded at Rs. 270\u2013300 per kilogram for non-AC stocks and Rs. 220 for AC stocks. Holland-variety Indian onions fetched Rs. 230 per kilogram. Indian red onions were quoted at Rs. 650\u2013660 per kilogram, while Bangalore 'Chipla' red onions were priced at Rs. 300. Local Jaffna red onions traded between Rs. 400 and Rs. 500 per kilogram."
