    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "With no local big onions available in the market, Indian big onions traded at Rs. 270\u2013300 per kilogram for non-AC stocks and Rs. 220 for AC stocks. Holland-variety Indian onions fetched Rs. 230 per kilogram. Indian red onions were quoted at Rs. 650\u2013660 per kilogram, while Bangalore 'Chipla' red onions were priced at Rs. 300. Local Jaffna red onions traded between Rs. 400 and Rs. 500 per kilogram.\n\nLocal rice varieties remained relatively stable, with Nadu trading at Rs. 180\u2013185 per kilogram, White Raw at Rs. 175\u2013180, Red Raw at Rs. 175\u2013185, Samba at Rs. 215\u2013220 and Keeri Samba at Rs. 245\u2013250. Araliya Keeri Samba was quoted at Rs. 330 per kilogram. Imported Ponni and Paal Ponni were priced at Rs. 220 and Rs. 275 per kilogram, respectively.\n\nWhite sugar traded at Rs. 240\u2013245 per kilogram following a recent Rs. 10 per kilogram increase in the Special Commodity Levy, while red sugar stood at Rs. 285. Prima wheat flour was quoted at Rs. 188\u2013190 per kilogram, while imported flour was priced at Rs. 185. Red dhal ranged between Rs. 240 and Rs. 255 per kilogram.\n\nAmong other commodities, garlic traded at Rs. 320\u2013340 per kilogram, tamarind at Rs. 650, mustard at Rs. 450 and goraka at Rs. 380. Indian green moong was priced at Rs. 650 per kilogram, with split green moong at Rs. 550, while urid traded at Rs. 765\u2013780. Local groundnuts were quoted at Rs. 550.\n\nIn the edible oil market, local coconut oil and palm oil traded at Rs. 850\u2013860 per kilogram, while sunflower oil was priced at Rs. 810\u2013825.\n\nIndustry representatives have cautioned that adverse weather conditions in key source markets, coupled with higher international freight costs and rupee depreciation, could pose further risks to supplies and prices of imported commodities, particularly onions and potatoes, as the country heads into December. However, potato supply is steady from Pakistan, where the crop season has begun."
