    "chunk_text": "Sheikh Hasina seeks to return to Bangladesh on December 16 on state carrier\n\nNEW DELHI, Nov 24 (Reuters) - Ousted Bangladeshi Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina plans to return home from more than two years of \u200cexile in India on December 16, the anniversary of the end of her country's 1971 liberation war with Pakistan, her spokesperson told Reuters on Tuesday, though the government said she can only return through extradition.\n\nIt is the first time Hasina, 79, has set a specific \u200bdate for her return, potentially raising political and legal tensions in a country where her party is \u200bbanned and where violence has risen since she sought a new passport and other documents earlier this \u2060month.\n\nIn addition to seeking the paperwork, cancelled months after she fled to Delhi following the uprising against her, Hasina \u200bhas urged the Bangladeshi government to arrange her return on flag carrier Biman Bangladesh Airlines.\n\n\"Since Sheikh Hasina was flown to \u200bIndia on a Bangladesh aircraft on August 5th, 2024, she now calls upon the government to arrange for her air transport from Delhi back to Dhaka,\" the spokesperson, Syed Mozammel Ali, said in a statement.\n\n\"Having exiled her contrary to the Constitution under such circumstances, \u200bit is now the responsibility of the government to make possible her return to Bangladesh on Victory Day on \u200bDecember 16th.\"\n\nThe government, however, reiterated that she can only be extradited by India, having been sentenced to death by a Bangladeshi court \u200clast year \u2060in her absence for ordering a deadly crackdown on a student-led uprising. Hasina has denied the charges from exile.\n\n\"Hasina is a convicted criminal; a fugitive terrorist,\" Humaiun Kobir, Bangladesh's minister of state for foreign affairs, told Reuters.\n\n\"She can only return home by extradition treaty; in handcuffs, and under strict surveillance.\""
