    "description": "India to test all bottled water brands after Bisleri sample found unsafe",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/international/India-to-test-all-bottled-water-brands-after-Bisleri-sample-found-unsafe/107-355603",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795502512.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailymirrorlk-51ebae03-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9b3ccbc3430ac04a068f688dc908a2ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1675,
    "chunk_text": "India to test all bottled water brands after Bisleri sample found unsafe\n\nChannel News Asia: India's food safety regulator has found a sample of Bisleri International's packaged drinking water unsafe in a major setback for the country's biggest and best-known bottled water brand, and a government source said samples from all companies will now be tested.\n\nIn light of the findings, the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) plans to test more Bisleri samples from across the nation, as well as those of other companies, a government source said.\n\nBisleri competes with PepsiCo's Aquafina and Coca-Cola's Kinley in India's packaged drinking water market, valued at US$3.6 billion in 2025, according to Persistence Market Research.\n\n\"Drinking water safety is a priority for Bisleri. We maintain stringent quality controls,\" Bisleri said, adding it will cooperate with the regulators.\n\nThe eponymous brand Bisleri, introduced in India in 1965, is a household name in the country where poor water quality often prompts people and tourists to buy packaged water both for homes and at restaurants.\n\nA second government source said the batches linked to the unsafe sample had been recalled, and subsequent testing of products showed no issues with water quality.\n\nThe sample, collected from Bisleri's premises, did not meet prescribed limits on bromate, a chemical formed as a by-product of certain disinfection processes, the FSSAI said in a social media post on Sunday (Nov 22).\n\nAfter Bisleri appealed the finding, the government regulator still found the sample unsafe but instead said it had detected polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons, or PAHs, the FSSAI said."
