    "chunk_text": "The sample, collected from Bisleri's premises, did not meet prescribed limits on bromate, a chemical formed as a by-product of certain disinfection processes, the FSSAI said in a social media post on Sunday (Nov 22).\n\nAfter Bisleri appealed the finding, the government regulator still found the sample unsafe but instead said it had detected polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons, or PAHs, the FSSAI said.\n\nBisleri said in its statement that the sample collected from its Goa facility last year showed bromate levels of 12 micrograms per litre against the prescribed limit of 10 micrograms per litre, but further independent testing arranged by the company showed the chemical was within prescribed limits.\n\nIndia's food regulator has stepped up enforcement against major brands this year, including legal action against Nestle over a baby food sample it found substandard in September."
