    "md5": "d0f54cf63586703f6b3b7e09c6064c29",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1620,
    "chunk_text": "From this Buddhist perspective, the death penalty is unacceptable because killing can produce negative karmic consequences. According to Buddhism, a person\u2019s present circumstances may be connected to actions performed in past existences.\n\nThe Buddha then explained that a person who takes the lives of living beings, is cruel, has blood-stained hands, is inclined towards killing or destruction, and lacks compassion may, because of those actions, be reborn in miserable existences. Even if such a person is reborn as a human being, the Buddha explained that taking life and lacking compassion can lead to a short life\n\nAn example given in Buddhist tradition is the Venerable Moggalana. Venerable\u00a0 Moggalana was one of the Buddha\u2019s foremost disciples and was known for possessing great supernatural powers. Despite this, he was killed by a group of robbers because of an action committed in one of his previous existences, when he had killed his parents.\n\nAccording to the traditional account, the consequences of this past action remained with him even though he had become an Arahant. According to the Buddha\u2019s teaching, killing and lacking compassion produce bad karma and negative results. Therefore, from this perspective, there is no justification for deliberately taking another person\u2019s life. Punishment, however, is important for maintaining order in human society. There is no argument against the need to protect the public from criminals and to rehabilitate offenders. Deterrence is also an important purpose of punishment. There are many other reasons why societies punish people who commit crimes."
