    "md5": "145c35c46d9a9c0a148b98f14e97ab3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1711,
    "chunk_text": "According to the traditional account, the consequences of this past action remained with him even though he had become an Arahant. According to the Buddha\u2019s teaching, killing and lacking compassion produce bad karma and negative results. Therefore, from this perspective, there is no justification for deliberately taking another person\u2019s life. Punishment, however, is important for maintaining order in human society. There is no argument against the need to protect the public from criminals and to rehabilitate offenders. Deterrence is also an important purpose of punishment. There are many other reasons why societies punish people who commit crimes.\n\nThe story of Angulimala provides an important example. Angulimala, whose name literally means \u201cGarland of Fingers\u201d, was a murderer and robber who had killed many people. The local people were terrified of him. Nevertheless, when the Buddha was staying in the area, he decided to travel along the road where Angulimala was believed to be hiding. Angulimala saw the Buddha and pursued him with the intention of killing him. However, the Buddha eventually managed to transform Angulimala, who became a monk. This story demonstrates the Buddhist belief that even a person who has committed serious crimes can change. It also illustrates the importance of rehabilitation rather than simply destroying the life of the offender. At the same time, the king and his army were searching for Angulimala. When the king met the Buddha, he explained the situation. The Buddha then showed him the reformed Angulimala, who was living peacefully as a monk. The king was amazed by the transformation. This clearly demonstrates the Buddhist notion of rehabilitation"
