    "chunk_text": "The story of Angulimala provides an important example. Angulimala, whose name literally means \u201cGarland of Fingers\u201d, was a murderer and robber who had killed many people. The local people were terrified of him. Nevertheless, when the Buddha was staying in the area, he decided to travel along the road where Angulimala was believed to be hiding. Angulimala saw the Buddha and pursued him with the intention of killing him. However, the Buddha eventually managed to transform Angulimala, who became a monk. This story demonstrates the Buddhist belief that even a person who has committed serious crimes can change. It also illustrates the importance of rehabilitation rather than simply destroying the life of the offender. At the same time, the king and his army were searching for Angulimala. When the king met the Buddha, he explained the situation. The Buddha then showed him the reformed Angulimala, who was living peacefully as a monk. The king was amazed by the transformation. This clearly demonstrates the Buddhist notion of rehabilitation\n\nAccording to the Digha Nikaya Brahmajala Sutta, translated from Pali by T. W. Rhys\u00a0 Davids in 1899: \u201cPutting away the killing of living things, Gautama, the recluse, holds aloof from the destruction of life. He has laid the cudgel and sword aside, and, shunning roughness, is full of mercy, compassion and kindness towards all creatures that have life.\u201d This teaching further emphasises the Buddhist commitment to non violence and compassion. Tenzin Gyatso, the Fourteenth Dalai Lama, has also expressed opposition to the death penalty. He argued that although capital punishment may serve a preventive function, it is also a form of revenge and is especially severe because it is final. The Dalai Lama has argued that, regardless of how horrible an individual\u2019s actions may have been, everyone has the potential to improve and correct themselves."
