    "chunk_text": "According to the Digha Nikaya Brahmajala Sutta, translated from Pali by T. W. Rhys\u00a0 Davids in 1899: \u201cPutting away the killing of living things, Gautama, the recluse, holds aloof from the destruction of life. He has laid the cudgel and sword aside, and, shunning roughness, is full of mercy, compassion and kindness towards all creatures that have life.\u201d This teaching further emphasises the Buddhist commitment to non violence and compassion. Tenzin Gyatso, the Fourteenth Dalai Lama, has also expressed opposition to the death penalty. He argued that although capital punishment may serve a preventive function, it is also a form of revenge and is especially severe because it is final. The Dalai Lama has argued that, regardless of how horrible an individual\u2019s actions may have been, everyone has the potential to improve and correct themselves.\n\nNo person is perfect until attaining Nirvana or enlightenment. Until then, people remain influenced by ignorance, greed, hatred, jealousy and other negative mental states. From this perspective, wrongdoing can be understood as arising from harmful tendencies within a person. Instead of simply destroying the individual, it may be more appropriate to help that person overcome these negative vcharacteristics. Buddhist teaching therefore emphasises compassion and assistance in developing a calm and wholesome state of mind. According to the law of karma, no one can escape the consequences of his or her actions. If a person deliberately kills or injures another person, that person creates negative karma through the action. Therefore, if the state kills a murderer, the person carrying out the execution is still participating in an act of killing. Although the act may be legally permitted by society, it does not escape the consequences of karma from a Buddhist perspective.\n\nThe Buddhist understanding of death also provides another perspective on the death penalty. According to the Abhidhamma, there 