    "chunk_text": "No person is perfect until attaining Nirvana or enlightenment. Until then, people remain influenced by ignorance, greed, hatred, jealousy and other negative mental states. From this perspective, wrongdoing can be understood as arising from harmful tendencies within a person. Instead of simply destroying the individual, it may be more appropriate to help that person overcome these negative vcharacteristics. Buddhist teaching therefore emphasises compassion and assistance in developing a calm and wholesome state of mind. According to the law of karma, no one can escape the consequences of his or her actions. If a person deliberately kills or injures another person, that person creates negative karma through the action. Therefore, if the state kills a murderer, the person carrying out the execution is still participating in an act of killing. Although the act may be legally permitted by society, it does not escape the consequences of karma from a Buddhist perspective.\n\nThe Buddhist understanding of death also provides another perspective on the death penalty. According to the Abhidhamma, there are four kinds of death\n\n2. Death due to the expiration of productive karmic force (Kammakkhaya marana).\n\n3. Death due to the expiration of both the age limit and karma\n\n4. Death caused by destructive karma (Upacchedaka-marana), such as sudden death resulting from an accident or suicide.\n\nFrom a Buddhist perspective, the final moments of life are particularly important.\n\nWhen a person is approaching death, it is important that wholesome mental states, or Javana Citta, arise. If wholesome thoughts prevail until the final breath, the being may be reborn in a happy existence. Conversely, if unwholesome mental states dominate before death, the person may be reborn in a woeful existence."
