    "chunk_text": "When a person is approaching death, it is important that wholesome mental states, or Javana Citta, arise. If wholesome thoughts prevail until the final breath, the being may be reborn in a happy existence. Conversely, if unwholesome mental states dominate before death, the person may be reborn in a woeful existence.\n\nEverything is constantly changing, including human beings. Criminals are also subject to this nature of impermanence. A person who commits a crime may not remain the same person psychologically or morally throughout his or her life. If a person can change, then permanently terminating that person\u2019s life removes the possibility of further transformation\n\nThe Buddha also explained the Dasa Raja Dhamma, or the Ten Royal Virtues, which are important qualities for leaders to develop to create a peaceful society based on happiness, security and the welfare of others. These qualities include\n\nAvihimsa, meaning non-harm, and Akrodaya, meaning non-ill will, require leaders to put aside anger, hatred, jealousy, envy, fear and resentment. Kings and leaders should therefore act with kindness and compassion towards other beings. If leaders possess these qualities, they are less likely to promote violence and conflict. From this perspective, Buddhism does not provide legitimacy for the death penalty"
