    "md5": "542ecff3a5bc6dcd18ce77ce117def20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1626,
    "chunk_text": "Avihimsa, meaning non-harm, and Akrodaya, meaning non-ill will, require leaders to put aside anger, hatred, jealousy, envy, fear and resentment. Kings and leaders should therefore act with kindness and compassion towards other beings. If leaders possess these qualities, they are less likely to promote violence and conflict. From this perspective, Buddhism does not provide legitimacy for the death penalty\n\nAccording to Buddhism, no one becomes perfect until attaining Nirvana or enlightenment. Until that point, people remain influenced by ignorance, greed and hatred, which can produce jealousy, competition and other harmful behaviour. Human decisions and actions may therefore be imperfect. This raises questions about whether human judgments and legal decisions can ever be completely accurate or fair. Justice can also be affected by differences in wealth, power and social status. Wealthier people may have greater access to experienced lawyers and legal organisations and may therefore be better able to use the appeals system. In contrast, poorer people may have fewer resources and may have little choice but to accept decisions made by lower courts. The possibility of unequal access to justice is particularly important when considering an irreversible punishment such as the death penalty. According to Buddhism, a Buddhist should follow the Four Brahma Viharas: Metta, Karuna, Mudita and Upekkha. Upekkha, or equanimity, means maintaining an impartial mind and not giving people special treatment based on wealth, caste or social status. From this perspective, justice should not favour the rich over the poor."
