    "md5": "c9fbd9fbf0d5d0e8b2dca0e6501d2120",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1366,
    "chunk_text": "Temporary punishment may cause suffering, but the death penalty is fundamentally different because it permanently separates a person from his or her family, children and loved ones. The consequences also affect people who have not committed the crime, but who may suffer emotionally because of the execution.\n\nJapan still retains the death penalty today, although some justice ministers have refused to sign death warrants, citing their Buddhist beliefs as a reason. Buddhist majority countries have also adopted different approaches. Bhutan has abolished the death penalty, while Thailand retains it, despite Buddhism being highly influential in both countries.\n\nThere are also traditional expressions within Japanese Buddhist traditions that connect the sword with both taking and preserving life. In these circumstances, it can be argued that the use of lethal force may be permissible when it is intended to prevent greater suffering.\n\nHowever, Buddhist groups in different countries have reached different conclusions. In countries such as Japan, Korea and Taiwan, Buddhist groups have generally tended to oppose the death penalty, while in Thailand, Sri Lanka and Bhutan, where Buddhism has had significant political influence, attitudes have varied. Nevertheless, almost all Buddhist groups oppose the use of the death penalty purely as a form of retribution."
