    "chunk_size_bytes": 1751,
    "chunk_text": "However, Buddhist groups in different countries have reached different conclusions. In countries such as Japan, Korea and Taiwan, Buddhist groups have generally tended to oppose the death penalty, while in Thailand, Sri Lanka and Bhutan, where Buddhism has had significant political influence, attitudes have varied. Nevertheless, almost all Buddhist groups oppose the use of the death penalty purely as a form of retribution.\n\nFrom the Buddhist perspective presented above, the death penalty is fundamentally difficult to justify because it involves the intentional taking of a human life. The first Buddhist precept calls upon people to abstain from killing, while Buddhist teachings on karma emphasise that intentional actions have consequences. Buddhism also places great importance on compassion, rehabilitation and the possibility of change.\n\nThe story of Angulimala demonstrates that even someone who has committed extremely serious crimes may be capable of transformation. The death penalty, however, removes that possibility permit not completely uniform on this issue. Some Buddhist interpretations have considered exceptional forms of violence or lethal action through the principle of preventing greater suffering. Historically, Buddhist societies have also differed in their use of capital punishment.\n\nNevertheless, when the death penalty is considered specifically as a form of retribution, it appears to conflict strongly with the central Buddhist principles of non harm, compassion, rehabilitation and the rejection of hatred. From this perspective, protecting society and addressing crime should be pursued through methods that allow offenders the possibility of reform rather than through the irreversible act of taking their lives."
