    "chunk_text": "The Elephant  Doesn\u2019t Need Us\u2026  We Need the Elephant\n\nIt is estimated that around 65\u201370% of Sri Lanka\u2019s wild elephants range outside protected areas\n\nMore than 400 wild elephant deaths recorded in some recent years, and around 100\u2013150 human lives lost\n\nMuch has been written about why we need to save the elephant. We speak about its intelligence, complex social behaviour, and close family bonds and, of course, its iconic status as one of the world\u2019s most magnificent and charismatic wild animals. In Sri Lanka, the elephant has an even deeper significance, woven into our culture, religion, history and, more recently, tourism.\n\nBut perhaps there is another, more fundamental reason why the elephant matters. It is not merely another large animal inhabiting our forests. It is an animal actually helping to shape and maintain the very ecosystem in which it lives.\n\nElephants are mega-herbivores and are the largest mammals living on earth. They are also considered an important keystone and flagship species. An adult Asian elephant may spend 17\u201319 hours a day feeding and consume around 150 kg or more of fresh vegetation each day.1\n\nElephants are hindgut fermenters, which means that much of the fermentation of the plant material they consume takes place in the large intestine, unlike in a complex multi-chambered stomach such as that of cattle and other ruminants. Their digestive efficiency is relatively low, so this allows them to process very large quantities of vegetation quite rapidly. An elephant\u2019s seemingly endless search for food is an inevitable consequence of being such a massive animal, living predominantly on vegetation of relatively low nutritional value.2"
