    "chunk_size_bytes": 1774,
    "chunk_text": "Elephants are hindgut fermenters, which means that much of the fermentation of the plant material they consume takes place in the large intestine, unlike in a complex multi-chambered stomach such as that of cattle and other ruminants. Their digestive efficiency is relatively low, so this allows them to process very large quantities of vegetation quite rapidly. An elephant\u2019s seemingly endless search for food is an inevitable consequence of being such a massive animal, living predominantly on vegetation of relatively low nutritional value.2\n\nStudies involving Asian elephants in Sri Lanka have estimated that about half of the seeds dispersed by elephants were transported more than one kilometre from where they were consumed, while maximum dispersal distances in the study approached six kilometres.4\n\nThus, elephants, because of their huge food intake, long gut-passage time and extensive movements, can transport large numbers of seeds across considerable distances.\n\nThe seed is then deposited in piles of moist elephant dung containing organic matter and nutrients. It is well known that seeds of many species remain viable after passing through an elephant, while in some cases passage through the digestive tract can enhance germination.\n\nSo as an elephant moves across the landscape feeding, walking and defecating, it is effectively \u2018planting the forest as it walks along\u2019.\n\nThere is an interesting and very appropriate term sometimes used by scientists to describe elephants in this role as \u201cmega-gardeners of the forest\u201d.5 The elephant does not consciously plant anything, of course, but over its lifetime it may transport enormous quantities of seeds from one part of the landscape to another. In doing so it can contribute to forest regeneration."
