    "md5": "e3244b1666b5732f5ff57b9c5195f626",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1711,
    "chunk_text": "There is an interesting and very appropriate term sometimes used by scientists to describe elephants in this role as \u201cmega-gardeners of the forest\u201d.5 The elephant does not consciously plant anything, of course, but over its lifetime it may transport enormous quantities of seeds from one part of the landscape to another. In doing so it can contribute to forest regeneration.\n\nAt first sight some of this can appear to be a rather destructive method of gardening. But ecosystems are not gardens that need to remain neat and tidy, and in nature, sometimes disruption is an important ecological process. By opening dense vegetation, elephants can create gaps through which sunlight reaches the ground, encouraging new plant growth and changing the structure of the vegetation.\n\nTheir movements also create and maintain pathways used by other animals. Around water sources, their repeated movements can modify river banks and access points, while their digging and disturbance of soil can alter small patches of habitat. Their dung, produced in very large quantities, returns partly digested plant material and nutrients to the environment and provides food and habitat for an entire community of insects and other small organisms.\n\nThe elephant therefore does not simply live in an ecosystem. To a great extent, it helps create and continuously modify that ecosystem.\n\nThis is what ecologists mean when they describe the elephant as an ecosystem engineer.6 It physically changes its environment in ways that influence other species. Elephants achieve this over larger landscapes, through their cumulative effect of feeding, trampling, digging, breaking vegetation, dispersing seeds and transporting nutrients."
