    "md5": "bc3cff789a679ee8b0fbf46d2bd3b074",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1815,
    "chunk_text": "This is what ecologists mean when they describe the elephant as an ecosystem engineer.6 It physically changes its environment in ways that influence other species. Elephants achieve this over larger landscapes, through their cumulative effect of feeding, trampling, digging, breaking vegetation, dispersing seeds and transporting nutrients.\n\nThis is why fragmentation is such a serious problem for elephant conservation. When a road, village settlement, agricultural scheme, fence or other development suddenly cuts across an elephant\u2019s traditional range, the problem is not simply that a few hectares of habitat have been lost. The connectivity of the landscape itself may have been disrupted.\n\nIt is in this wider ecological context the human-elephant conflict (HEC) takes on a different perspective. Sri Lanka suffers from the highest annual elephant mortality rate globally relative to its small population, alongside the second-highest human death toll from elephant encounters. With more than 400 wild elephant deaths recorded in some recent years, and around 100\u2013150 human lives being lost annually due to HEC, this is indeed a crisis of very large proportions. In fact, HEC is said to be Sri Lanka\u2019s most acute socio-environmental crisis. Unfortunately, despite all the discussion and interventions over the years, there still seems to be no focused, overarching solution to the problem in sight.\n\nA farmer whose crop is destroyed in a single night suffers a very real economic loss, and a family that loses someone to an elephant attack is a tragedy that cannot be dismissed with ecological arguments. Equally, hundreds of elephants are being killed through shootings, electrocution, explosive devices, poisoning, train collisions and other causes associated directly or indirectly with the conflict."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
