    "md5": "4578811d66a3631bc70c2cc9b09ac795",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1836,
    "chunk_text": "A farmer whose crop is destroyed in a single night suffers a very real economic loss, and a family that loses someone to an elephant attack is a tragedy that cannot be dismissed with ecological arguments. Equally, hundreds of elephants are being killed through shootings, electrocution, explosive devices, poisoning, train collisions and other causes associated directly or indirectly with the conflict.\n\nBut the elephant did not suddenly decide to enter into conflict with humans. In many instances, it is we who have gradually moved agriculture, settlements and infrastructure into landscapes that elephants were already using, thereby restricting their ability to move elsewhere.\n\nFor most of that evolutionary history, elephants did perfectly well without wildlife departments, conservationists, management plans, electric fences or national parks. They responded to droughts, climatic changes, changing vegetation and other natural pressures through movement, adaptation and natural selection.\n\nToday we speak constantly about the need to \u201cmanage elephants\u201d. We translocate them, fence them in or fence them out, drive them away from settlements, construct barriers across their ranges and debate endlessly about how many elephants a particular area should contain. Some intervention has, of course, become unavoidable because the landscapes in which elephants now survive, have been so drastically altered by people.\n\nBut this \u201cmanagement\u201d has now become necessary because we have changed the landscape.\n\nThe irony becomes even greater when we consider what healthy natural ecosystems provide for us. Forests and grasslands help regulate water, protect soils, store carbon, recycle nutrients and sustain biological diversity, and are part of the natural life-support systems, on which human societies and economies depend."
  },
