    "md5": "b696de46440877acf30116714991fd2f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "The irony becomes even greater when we consider what healthy natural ecosystems provide for us. Forests and grasslands help regulate water, protect soils, store carbon, recycle nutrients and sustain biological diversity, and are part of the natural life-support systems, on which human societies and economies depend.\n\nThe irony is that ecologically, the elephant does not need mankind. Given sufficient habitat, food, water and the freedom to move, wild elephants would probably manage perfectly well without us. Indeed, their ancestors did so for millions of years before modern humans transformed their landscapes.\n\nWe, on the other hand, need functioning natural ecosystems. We need forests, water catchments, fertile soils, biodiversity and climatic stability. And elephants, where they naturally occur, are one of the many species helping to maintain the ecological processes that sustain those systems.\n\nSo perhaps we need to change the way we look at elephant conservation. Saving elephants is not simply about being kind to a magnificent animal, nor is it only about preserving an iconic species for our children to see. It is about understanding that the elephant is part of an intricate ecological machinery that has evolved over an immense period of time, and that dismantling pieces of that machinery inevitably will have serious consequences.\n\nPerhaps therein lies one of the great contradictions of elephant conservation today. We speak constantly about \u201cmanaging elephants\u201d, when in reality, much of what we are trying to manage are problems that we ourselves have created.\n\nMaybe it is time we started managing ourselves a little better.\n\n2. Clauss, M., Steuer, P., M\u00fcller, D. W., Codron, D., & Hummel, J. (2013). \u201cHerbivore body size and digestive efficiency: The role of dietary quality and availability.\u201d Journal of Zoology, 291(4)\n\n3. Perera, M., Vandercone, R.P.G., (2020) Some Aspects of Seed Dispersal by Asian Elephants (Elephas maximus ) in Kaudulla National Park, Sri Lanka"
