    "md5": "2dd8c09221db641115f52b9ba2060013",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "India\u2019s \u2019cockroach\u2019 party leader says he was stopped from flying to UK\n\nBBC- A leader of India's \"cockroach\" party has said that immigration officials at Delhi airport stopped him from flying to the UK, where he was set to attend a series of events.\n\nSaurav Das said he was supposed to board a flight on Tuesday at 01:00 local time (19:30 GMT on Monday) but was told by immigration officers that his passport had been \"flagged\".\n\nIt is not clear yet why Das's passport was flagged. The BBC has reached out to the federal home ministry, Delhi police and airport officials for comment.\n\nDas, who rose to prominence in India earlier this year after Gen Z-led protests, said that he had been invited by the Oxford Union to speak about the movement.\n\nHe said that he was also scheduled to speak at other universities in Cambridge and Edinburgh.\n\n\"Indian students put their heart and soul to organise the events but this anti-intellectual [government] was scared of me going to these prestigious institutions,\" he wrote on X.\n\nA federal home ministry spokesperson directed the BBC to airport authorities and the Delhi police for comment on Das's allegations.\n\nRatna Singh, another leader of the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP), said in a post on X that she and Das had been invited by the Oxford Union but that her visa had been rejected a few days ago.\n\n\"The manner in which Saurav was not allowed to board today raises deeply troubling questions about the state of freedom of expression and the space being allowed for young voices in this country,\" she wrote.\n\nDas, a former journalist, became one of the faces of the CJP - a Gen Z-led movement that began as satire but has since emerged as a youth-led pressure group.\n\nIn July, protests spearheaded by the CJP over exam paper leaks led to the resignation of India's Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan ."
  },
