    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "First opened in 1984 as the Galadari Meridien, the hotel has spent more than four decades as a fixture of Colombo\u2019s commercial and social life, a legacy General Manager Marko Janssen placed at the heart of his video address to guests. \u201cOver the years, this hotel has been part of Colombo\u2019s story.\n\nIt hosted weddings, business deals, farewell dinners, and first dates,\u201d he noted, adding that a hotel \u201cis only as alive as the memories it creates. And this one has plenty.\u201d\n\nAccommodation ranges from Deluxe Rooms of 32 square metres with city or sea views to Executive Rooms and Suites, a two-bedroom suite of 128 square metres and a sea-view Presidential Suite, with connected rooms available for families. Guests in the Executive Rooms and Suites also enjoy an in-room espresso machine and access to the Executive Lounge.\n\nThe dining line-up has been rebuilt around an all-day dining restaurant, specialty Indian and Lebanese restaurants, a lobby bar and caf\u00e9, a pool bar and a separate caf\u00e9 with outdoor access. The leisure offering includes an outdoor pool and a spa with five treatment rooms for massages, facials, body wraps and aromatherapy, along with a fitness centre, sauna and steam room.\n\nThe hotel is also making a strong play for the meetings and weddings market. It has seven event venues, led by the Blu Grand Ballroom, which spans 580 square metres and can seat up to 800 guests theatre-style, and the 520-square-metre Blu Sapphire, which accommodates up to 500, complemented by five smaller rooms including the ballroom\u2019s VIP lounge.\n\nReflecting on the journey, Janssen admitted that \u201ca closed hotel is a quiet, strange place\u201d, but maintained that \u201ca building can be closed while its spirit is not\u201d. He acknowledged that the road to the grand opening brought delays, difficult decisions and plans that had to be rewritten more than once. Each setback, he argued, pushed the team to ask what the hotel could become rather than how to restore what it once was."
