    "description": "Bring online gaming into Sri Lanka\u2019s tax net, Advocata urges",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Bring-online-gaming-into-Sri-Lankas-tax-net-Advocata-urges/215-355632",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795509173.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailymirrorlk-bd66f4c3-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a9a49c080df3b5b249e53cea6481b5dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1585,
    "chunk_text": "Bring online gaming into Sri Lanka\u2019s tax net, Advocata urges\n\nProposes single levy on gross gaming revenue for online operators and physical casinos\n\nSays current casino tax structure creates scope for expense shifting\n\nCalls for independent regulator funded by licence fees or a dedicated levy\n\nSri Lanka should bring online gaming operators into the tax net under its Budget 2027 review of gambling taxes, the Advocata Institute said, calling for a single levy on gross gaming revenue across online platforms and physical casinos.\n\nThe think tank said a uniform levy would allow the government to collect revenue it says is being lost from unregulated online gaming. It recommended setting the rate in line with competing gaming jurisdictions. The institute gave no estimate of the revenue forgone and did not detail how the levy would be collected from offshore platforms serving Sri Lankan customers.\n\nThe proposed levy would replace the current casino tax structure, which Advocata said combines an 18 percent levy on gross gaming revenue with a 45 percent corporate income tax on profits. The institute said the two-tier system complicates tax administration and gives operators scope to lower their tax bills by classifying non-gaming expenses as gaming costs. It said the system also creates an incentive to shift costs into categories such as rent to take advantage of tax holidays under the Strategic Development Projects Act. Taxing gross gaming revenue alone would remove the benefit of shifting expenses to reduce taxable profit under the proposed regime, Advocata said."
  },
  {
