    "md5": "82ef86c206f41402f2939206f6bf9267",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1562,
    "chunk_text": "The proposed levy would replace the current casino tax structure, which Advocata said combines an 18 percent levy on gross gaming revenue with a 45 percent corporate income tax on profits. The institute said the two-tier system complicates tax administration and gives operators scope to lower their tax bills by classifying non-gaming expenses as gaming costs. It said the system also creates an incentive to shift costs into categories such as rent to take advantage of tax holidays under the Strategic Development Projects Act. Taxing gross gaming revenue alone would remove the benefit of shifting expenses to reduce taxable profit under the proposed regime, Advocata said.\n\nAdvocata said reliance on Treasury allocations had left the authority struggling to secure office space and recruit technical staff. It proposed allowing the regulator to retain operator licence fees or charge a dedicated regulatory fee to fund its work.\n\nThe institute said an independent regulator would be better placed to assess the appropriate size of the market and address money laundering, terrorism financing, problem gambling and consumer protection risks. It also proposed using the term \u201cgaming\u201d across tax and regulatory laws, arguing that inconsistent definitions and tax treatment across casinos, betting and lotteries could distort the market.\n\nAdvocata issued the recommendations in response to Budget 2027\u2019s commitment to align gaming taxation with international practices. The proposals build on two policy papers published by the institute in 2024 and 2025."
  },
