    "md5": "91ddd5982ec578d8e4dd76c700092a76",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "ADB\u2019s US$ 200mn package to ease credit barriers for small businesses in SL\n\nUS$ 100mn for investment loans, including US$ 40mn for green projects\n\nReforms to expand credit guarantees, improve credit reporting and curb predatory lending\n\nAt least one in five firms receiving climate-related support to be owned or led by women\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s small businesses could find it easier to borrow for expansion under a US$ 200 million financing package approved by the Asian Development Bank (ADB), which will fund new investment loans and back reforms to widen access to credit guarantees.\n\nHalf the package will fund investment loans for micro, small and medium-sized enterprises (MSMEs) through participating financial institutions, with US$ 40 million earmarked for green lending. The remaining US$ 100 million is a policy-based loan to improve how lenders assess borrowers and strengthen oversight of the microfinance sector. MSMEs contribute more than 52 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s gross domestic product and account for 45 percent of employment, according to the ADB. Limited access to finance and low adoption of digital tools hold back the sector, while climate-related disasters leave businesses vulnerable to disruptions.\n\n\u201cThis program will help the Government of Sri Lanka build a stronger, more inclusive financial ecosystem that enables MSMEs to grow, innovate, and withstand economic shocks and disasters related to the changing climate,\u201d ADB Country Director for Sri Lanka Shannon Cowlin said. The programme will support legal reforms to establish the Microfinance and Credit Regulatory Authority, strengthen oversight and curb predatory lending. It will also strengthen credit reporting so lenders have better information to assess borrowers and expand guarantees that help lenders share the risk of lending to smaller firms."
  },
