    "chunk_id": "2026-11-24-dailymirrorlk-1c44dc34-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e69bd1c2e329e3fa8c226b4c9b27e8cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka temporarily allows travelers to board flights without an ETA\n\nDue to the temporary unavailability of its Electronic Travel Authorization (ETA) system, Sri Lanka is allowing travelers of most nationalities to board flights without obtaining prior approval until further notice.\n\nEligible passengers will be able to complete their entry formalities upon arrival in the country.\n\nIn a circular dated November 23, 2026, bearing reference number DIE/VISA/01/121/ETA Resto, Sri Lanka\u2019s Department of Immigration and Emigration announced that its ETA application portal was temporarily unavailable due to ongoing system maintenance.\n\nThe announcement was shared on Tuesday, November 24, 2026, by several Sri Lankan diplomatic missions around the world, including the embassies in the Philippines, Japan, Sweden, and T\u00fcrkiye, as well as the high commissions in Australia and Singapore.\n\nTo minimize disruption for international travelers, Sri Lankan authorities have temporarily adjusted the country\u2019s entry procedures.\n\nAuthorities have confirmed that eligible travelers are temporarily permitted to board flights to Sri Lanka without obtaining prior ETA approval.\n\nUpon arrival at Colombo\u2019s Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA) or another relevant port of entry, passengers will be able to obtain their Electronic Travel Authorization or Visit Visa directly from immigration authorities.\n\nAirlines and their ground handling agents have been asked to make the necessary arrangements to facilitate boarding for passengers covered by this temporary measure.\n\nCitizens of the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and European Union countries are among those eligible for the temporary relaxation of entry requirements."
  },
