    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "Growth deadlock: Diagnosing Sri Lanka\u2019s detour from knowledge economy and charting the way forward - Part I\n\nPresidential Address delivered at the Annual International Conference 2026 of the Sri Lanka Economic Association (SLEA), held on 21 November, 2026\n\nThe theme of the Annual International Conference 2026 of the Sri Lanka Economic Association (SLEA) is not just relevant but absolutely vital for our nation's survival and future prosperity: \"Paving the Way for Sri Lanka's Knowledge Economy\". The country has been caught in the lower-middle-income trap for the last three decades. What went wrong for Sri Lanka? My answer can be encapsulated in two terms - \"low productivity\" and \"closed economy\".\n\nThe Sri Lankan economy's low productivity is due to the diminishing returns on capital. In 1960, we required only three units of capital to produce one additional unit of output or GDP. Today we have to invest six units to yield the same output increase of one unit. This implies diminishing marginal returns on investment.\n\nLand, labour and capital have been the major drivers of Sri Lanka's GDP growth so far. We have invested heavily in factories, machinery and other physical capital only to gain diminishing marginal returns. As a result, the economy remains at a low equilibrium level. The only way to expand the country's production possibility frontier is to introduce knowledge inputs into our growth model.\n\nLow productivity, coupled with inward-looking economic strategies, has eroded export competitiveness, and Sri Lanka has now become a more closed economy. Its exports-to-GDP ratio came down from 27% in 1960 to just 14% in 2025. This means we are getting more and more isolated from the rest of the world.\n\nThe resulting low GDP growth not only prevents improvement in people's quality of life but also disturbs macroeconomic stability, which is the very foundation of economic prosperity."
  },
