    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "Low productivity, coupled with inward-looking economic strategies, has eroded export competitiveness, and Sri Lanka has now become a more closed economy. Its exports-to-GDP ratio came down from 27% in 1960 to just 14% in 2025. This means we are getting more and more isolated from the rest of the world.\n\nThe resulting low GDP growth not only prevents improvement in people's quality of life but also disturbs macroeconomic stability, which is the very foundation of economic prosperity.\n\nKnowledge-driven growth is rooted in Endogenous Growth Theory, developed by Paul Romer in 1990. It treats human capital, innovation and technological progress as internal drivers of economic growth, overcoming the diminishing returns of physical capital through non-rival public goods. Unlike physical machines or land, knowledge can be used simultaneously by multiple people and firms without wearing out or depleting. This creates increasing marginal returns rather than diminishing returns to scale.\n\nThe older neoclassical models articulated by Robert Solow in 1956 asserted that technology fell 'from the sky' as an exogenous shock. In contrast, new growth models internalise research and development (R&D), education and spillovers as deliberate economic choices. Accordingly, long-run growth mainly depends on internally generated technological change by means of the key drivers - human capital, knowledge and R&D.\n\nThe present era of the 5th Industrial Revolution (5IR or Industry 5.0) is a value-driven system that prioritises the synergy between human intelligence and advanced technology. While the 4th Industrial Revolution (4IR) focused on automation and efficiency, the 5IR shifts the focus back to human-centricity, sustainability and resilience. In this context, a knowledge economy that creates, disseminates and uses knowledge is essential to accelerate Sri Lanka's export-led growth and to escape the middle-income trap."
  },
