    "chunk_size_bytes": 1588,
    "chunk_text": "The present era of the 5th Industrial Revolution (5IR or Industry 5.0) is a value-driven system that prioritises the synergy between human intelligence and advanced technology. While the 4th Industrial Revolution (4IR) focused on automation and efficiency, the 5IR shifts the focus back to human-centricity, sustainability and resilience. In this context, a knowledge economy that creates, disseminates and uses knowledge is essential to accelerate Sri Lanka's export-led growth and to escape the middle-income trap.\n\nEconomic and Institutional Regime: A supportive economic environment and legal framework\u2014such as strong intellectual property protection, good governance and open trade\u2014that encourages investment and entrepreneurship;\n\nEducation and Human Resources: An educated, skilled and literate population capable of creating, sharing and effectively using knowledge;\n\nInformation and Communication Technology (ICT) Infrastructure: A dynamic and accessible communication network (such as the internet and modern telecommunications) that allows the fast and reliable processing and sharing of information; and\n\nInnovation Systems: A connected network of research centres, universities, private companies, think tanks and civil society groups that turn global and local knowledge into new products, services and solutions.\n\nLet us now look at the growth performance of Sri Lanka. Since the economic liberalisation policies adopted in late 1977, the annual average growth rate has hovered around 4%. Overall, GDP growth shows a declining trend during the period from 1977 to date."
