    "chunk_text": "Let us now look at the growth performance of Sri Lanka. Since the economic liberalisation policies adopted in late 1977, the annual average growth rate has hovered around 4%. Overall, GDP growth shows a declining trend during the period from 1977 to date.\n\nThe growth rate was in the negative region during 2019-2023 (except in 2021) amidst the COVID-19 pandemic and the economic crisis. The economic reforms adopted since 2022 helped the economy recover, as reflected in the moderate growth rate of 5% in 2024 and 2025. This recovery appears to be short-lived. For this year, the growth rate is expected to decline to 3.2%, according to official forecasts.\n\nSri Lanka's growth performance is dismal in comparison with the fast-growing East Asian countries. Between 1960 and 2025, the economic landscape of these countries underwent a dramatic transformation, in contrast to Sri Lanka. While Japan and the Four Asian Tigers (South Korea, Taiwan, Singapore and Hong Kong) achieved extraordinary leaps in per capita wealth, Sri Lanka followed a volatile, low-growth trajectory.\n\nAt the time of its independence, Sri Lanka (then Ceylon) had a higher per capita income than South Korea and was widely seen as a prospective economic model. However, differing choices in industrialisation strategy, political stability and total factor productivity led to a profound developmental divergence over the next six decades.\n\nSince the early 1960s, Japan and the Four Asian Tigers have transformed from war-torn, developing agrarian societies into high-income global powerhouses. Their per capita GDP skyrocketed from a range of $ 150 to $ 520 in 1960 to high-income levels ranging from $ 36,000 to nearly $ 100,000 today.\n\nJapan pioneered the \"East Asian Developmental Model\", using State-directed capitalism, aggressive export-oriented manufacturing and massive investment in technology to resurrect its economy."
