    "chunk_text": "Since the early 1960s, Japan and the Four Asian Tigers have transformed from war-torn, developing agrarian societies into high-income global powerhouses. Their per capita GDP skyrocketed from a range of $ 150 to $ 520 in 1960 to high-income levels ranging from $ 36,000 to nearly $ 100,000 today.\n\nJapan pioneered the \"East Asian Developmental Model\", using State-directed capitalism, aggressive export-oriented manufacturing and massive investment in technology to resurrect its economy.\n\n$ 36,227 by 2025. Today, Sri Lanka's per capita GDP is only $ 5,000.\n\nSingapore and Hong Kong capitalised on their strategic maritime locations to transition into world-class trade, logistics and financial centres. By 2025, Singapore surged to lead Asia with a per capita GDP close to $ 100,000, while Hong Kong reached $ 56,983.\n\nSri Lanka, which had a comparable or higher economic baseline than some of its East Asian peers in the 1950s and early 1960s, experienced slower, volatile growth marred by a multitude of negative factors, including dogmatic political ideologies, prolonged ethnic conflict, civil unrest, inconsistent economic policies, structural bottlenecks, politically affiliated trade union protests, labour indiscipline and macroeconomic imbalances. Sri Lanka's per capita GDP crawled from $ 146 in 1960 to a mere $ 5,002 in 2025.\n\nSri Lanka transitioned from a low-income country to a lower-middle-income country in 1997 and has remained in this category, except on two brief occasions. It first graduated to upper-middle-income status in 2019, but the achievement was short-lived as it was placed back in the lower-middle-income category the following year against the backdrop of the economic crisis."
