    "md5": "795220f028ca061dbfe83379ce17e7e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1660,
    "chunk_text": "Anuradhapura: Protecting our ancient water civilisation, archaeological heritage and national land\n\nWe are guardians and not owners: the wisdom of King Devanampiya Tissa, the principles articulated by Justice C.G. Weeramantry and the landmark reasoning of Justice A.R.B. Amerasinghe convey a common message. Our ancestors did not build reservoirs, canals, monasteries and sacred monuments merely for their own generation. They created a civilisation to be preserved and passed on\n\nThe wisdom of Justices A.R.B. Amerasinghe and C.G. Weeramantry directing decisions affecting the sacred city of Anuradhapura\n\nAnuradhapura is replete with historical marvels; some of the marvels identified by the generations of yesteryear require national and public attention to protect and preserve them for the generations yet unborn.\n\nOne of those marvels is the Kalawewa National Park in Kekirawa, a sanctuary for elephants, particularly tuskers, which are rare in the country. In the dry season, the water levels of the reservoirs recede, revealing green grasslands that attract elephants. The Kala Wewa and Balalu Wewa reservoirs, built in the 5th century, are central to the park's ecosystem. The massive Kumbuk trees that line the park are a habitat for numerous birds, both resident and migratory.\n\nAnother marvel is the Yoda Ela, an 87 km (54 mi) long single-banking water canal constructed in 459 AD, carrying excess water from the Kala Wewa reservoir in Anuradhapura to the Tissa Wewa reservoir. The Yoda Ela is acclaimed for its low gradient of 6 inches per mile which, even with modern-day survey equipment, would be a difficult feat to achieve with such accuracy."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-25-dailyftlk-ec2ca59c",
    "num": "dailyftlk-ec2ca59c",
    "date_str": "2026-11-25",
