    "md5": "556a3791f25e02ae200f828fbf74321c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "The Court considered the potential consequences for the ancient irrigation systems of Rajarata, the water and soil conservation ecosystems, underground aquifers and agricultural lands, the archaeological and cultural heritage and the livelihoods of local communities.\n\nJustice Amerasinghe drew attention to the fact that there was no adequate provision for protecting a cultural-heritage landscape such as the Yoda Wewa, as distinct from an individual archaeological monument.\n\nJustice Amerasinghe went on to say that protecting an individual dagoba or monument is not necessarily sufficient if the surrounding ancient irrigation system, landscape, waterways and archaeological setting are damaged.\n\nThe judgment also emphasised the precautionary approach towards sustainable development and the principle that environmental damage must be borne by those responsible for causing it. The judgment deemed that if no plans are publicly available, there is no way to assess the damage that could possibly be caused by the project; in these circumstances, a project is not to be proceeded with.\n\nJustice Amerasinghe's judgment says that Sri Lanka's law cannot be circumvented by secret agreements or by some type of understanding arrived at by two parties to the contract.\n\nThe judgment went on to say that the authority that will determine whether a project is suitable for the country in respect of its heritage, civilisation, environment, etc., is the authority that has been delegated such powers by the country's sovereign law.\n\nIn 1997, Justice C.G. Weeramantry, a former Vice-President of the International Court of Justice (ICJ), made a similar observation in a case that came up before him at the ICJ. Sri Lanka's ancient water wisdom was internationally recognised."
