    "md5": "b69e4016ba5e3205d3dcc1255e29438d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "In 1997, Justice C.G. Weeramantry, a former Vice-President of the International Court of Justice (ICJ), made a similar observation in a case that came up before him at the ICJ. Sri Lanka's ancient water wisdom was internationally recognised.\n\nThe eminent judge referred to the ancient principle that not even a small quantity of rainwater should flow to the sea without benefiting the people.\n\nJustice Weeramantry also referred to the sermon of Arahat Mahinda to King Devanampiya Tissa, explaining that animals and birds have an equal right to live and move within the land.\n\nJustice Weeramantry's central message was that development and environmental conservation must go together. He recognised that sustainable development was not a newly invented international concept: Sri Lanka had practiced these principles for centuries.\n\nAn important point is that Justice Amerasinghe's Eppawela judgment referred to Justice Weeramantry's international jurisprudence.\n\nTheir contributions provide a powerful legal and historical foundation for the principle that development and environmental conservation must go hand in hand.\n\nJustice Amerasinghe and Justice Weeramantry both outline the doctrine of \u2018Public Trust\u2019, nationally and internationally.\n\nThis doctrine carries an intergenerational responsibility: the responsibility to protect natural resources.\n\nThe two eminent judges speak of sustainable development, the need to protect the Yoda Wewa cultural landscape, the ancient irrigation wisdom, the precautionary approach to environmental damage, harmony between development and nature, including the fauna, the flora and the majestic elephants that roam the land, and the shared responsibility to protect them all for future generations.\n\nTogether, the wisdom of the honourable judges provides an important framework for examining development projects in Anuradhapura and elsewhere."
