    "md5": "2308b30665d30a4caf94b29ccf711275",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "The two eminent judges speak of sustainable development, the need to protect the Yoda Wewa cultural landscape, the ancient irrigation wisdom, the precautionary approach to environmental damage, harmony between development and nature, including the fauna, the flora and the majestic elephants that roam the land, and the shared responsibility to protect them all for future generations.\n\nTogether, the wisdom of the honourable judges provides an important framework for examining development projects in Anuradhapura and elsewhere.\n\nThe protection of heritage should not be confined to individual monuments while the wider historic landscape and the habitat of elephants and other animals and birds are altered.\n\nThe following laws should be examined together: the Antiquities Ordinance No. 9 of 1940 (protection of antiquities, ancient monuments and archaeological reserves); the National Environmental Act No. 47 of 1980 (environmental approvals and assessment, where applicable); the State Lands Ordinance No. 8 of 1947 (legal administration of State land); and the Mahaweli Authority of Sri Lanka Act No. 23 of 1979 (where applicable, to designated areas and lands).\n\nAlso important are Article 27(14) of the Constitution (State responsibility to protect, preserve and improve the environment) and Article 28(f) (the duty of every person to protect nature and conserve its riches). The Eppawela judgment is particularly important when applying these laws to development proposals affecting interconnected archaeological and environmental resources.\n\nCan a Government undertake tourism and infrastructure development in Anuradhapura without first establishing, through proper archaeological, hydrological and environmental studies, that the ancient water systems, archaeological landscape and sacred character will be protected?\n\nThe answers must be provided by the legal principles already established by our courts."
