    "chunk_size_bytes": 1294,
    "chunk_text": "Can a Government undertake tourism and infrastructure development in Anuradhapura without first establishing, through proper archaeological, hydrological and environmental studies, that the ancient water systems, archaeological landscape and sacred character will be protected?\n\nThe answers must be provided by the legal principles already established by our courts.\n\nOur ancestors did not build reservoirs, canals, monasteries and sacred monuments merely for their own generation. They created a civilisation to be preserved and passed on.\n\nAnuradhapura is not simply land available for development. It is a national inheritance held in trust for generations yet unborn.\n\nIt is the Government's duty to preserve its archaeological integrity, ancient water civilisation, sacred character and historical landscape. Development must serve this heritage, not destroy or diminish it.\n\n(The author is a Senior Attorney-at-Law who has worked with Justice A.R.B. Amerasinghe.)\n\nThe Eppawela Supreme Court judgment, Bulankulama and others vs. Freeport-McMoRan and others: https://elaw.org/resource/sri-lanka-bulankulama-v-min-industrial-development-eppawala-case-sc-application-no-88499-fr\n\nJustice Weeramantry's \u2018International Environmental Jurisprudence and Sri Lanka's Ancient Irrigation Wisdom\u2019"
