    "md5": "f0375c0f6eee47a3c3f7e929047be98b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1693,
    "chunk_text": "Making aspiration affordable again: Notes from alternative Budget\n\nAs you read this, Sri Lankans are living through a historic redistribution of wealth. Evidence shows that since 2019 Sri Lanka has witnessed a redistribution of income away from the bottom and towards the top. An Institute of Policy Studies (IPS) analysis of Department of Census and Statistics data found that between 2019 and early 2025, the real household income of the poorest 20% fell by 19.6%, while that of the richest 20% increased by 6.8%. The difference is visible across the income distribution. Real median household income fell by 11.1%, compared with a decline of only 1.7% in the average household income.\n\nAs a consequence, the distance between the top and bottom widened sharply: in 2019, the average household in the richest 20% earned around 11.2 times as much as the average household in the poorest 20%; by early 2025, that ratio had risen to 14.8 times. Budget 2027 seems likely to accelerate this upward redistribution of wealth, there is little relief for working people, other than raising the income tax-free threshold from 150,000 to 162,500. Sri Lanka\u2019s long-suffering middle class received little or no attention.\n\nAn SJB alternative Budget should demonstrate what would change under its administration, restoring purchasing power to working and middle-income households would be a central objective. The SJB and Sajith Premadasa more specifically, has criticised the current program\u2019s over-emphasis on fiscal consolidation, its failure to distinguish GDP growth from external-sector resilience, and the trajectory towards rebuilding a significant International Sovereign Bond (ISB) portfolio."
