    "chunk_size_bytes": 1963,
    "chunk_text": "An SJB alternative Budget should demonstrate what would change under its administration, restoring purchasing power to working and middle-income households would be a central objective. The SJB and Sajith Premadasa more specifically, has criticised the current program\u2019s over-emphasis on fiscal consolidation, its failure to distinguish GDP growth from external-sector resilience, and the trajectory towards rebuilding a significant International Sovereign Bond (ISB) portfolio.\n\nThe data is clear: the Sri Lankan middle class is significantly diminished since the 2019 economic crisis saw 70% inflation and interest rates as high as 32% alongside a rapidly depreciating currency. Today, a salaried executive will struggle to afford rent in an urban enclave close to the main city, many skilled workers have already moved abroad, SMEs struggle under a pile of debt that has kept accumulating under moratoriums that came and went. Dynamic entrepreneurs have largely retreated into what\u2019s left of the corporate sector. Beyond the economic indictors of stability lies the real cost of the economic crisis: the loss of aspiration, the surrendering of dreams.\n\nA basic family car should not be treated fiscally in the same way as a Land Cruiser or Mercedes Benz. Sri Lanka already differentiates vehicle excise according to engine capacity. A 1,301-1,500cc petrol hybrid currently attracts Rs. 3,450 per cc, compared with Rs. 9,650 per cc for a 2,751-3,000cc hybrid. Vehicles also face customs duty, customs surcharge, VAT and, where applicable, luxury tax. The alternative Budget would create a tightly defined Affordable Family Vehicle category:\n\nThis is a deliberately narrow category, aimed primarily at the lower-middle and middle-income family-car market rather than providing a concession across the entire vehicle market. Examples of vehicles in the Sri Lankan small-car market likely to fall within the category, subject to individual CIF values, include:"
