    "md5": "eab7ca8f901643185eabea354a5c6604",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "This is a deliberately narrow category, aimed primarily at the lower-middle and middle-income family-car market rather than providing a concession across the entire vehicle market. Examples of vehicles in the Sri Lankan small-car market likely to fall within the category, subject to individual CIF values, include:\n\nPrices would be reduced gradually over three years by lowering excise duties and customs duty and removing the 50% customs duty surcharge on qualifying affordable family vehicles. VAT and SSCL would remain unchanged, preserving a significant tax contribution while reducing the exceptionally high upfront cost of basic vehicle ownership.\n\nVehicle taxes remain a major revenue source, generating Rs. 387 billion in the first half of 2026, including Rs. 138 billion from sub 1,000cc petrol cars. As pent-up import demand subsides, the phased concession is estimated to cost Rs. 50-60 billion annually, partly offset by higher import volumes as affordable vehicle prices fall.\n\nPart of the revenue loss would be recovered from the opposite end of the vehicle market. Existing import taxation should become progressively heavier for vehicles above 2,000cc, high-powered EVs, luxury SUVs. An Annual Luxury Vehicle Levy might also be considered or a narrow band of high-end vehicles where demand is likely to be inelastic.\n\nProperty has traditionally been one of the main ways Sri Lankan families build wealth but increasingly, middle-class Sri Lankans are being priced out of the neighbourhoods in which they grew up. Colombo\u2019s price-to-income ratio is estimated at 55.3 in 2026, according to Numbeo\u2019s Property Price Index; while this measure is distorted due to the urban/rural divide in property prices, it still indicates a lack of affordability. Official and market data show that since 2019, Colombo asking prices have risen approximately 73% for land, 93% for houses and 119% for condominiums while construction costs have also risen by approximately 91%."
