    "chunk_text": "Property has traditionally been one of the main ways Sri Lankan families build wealth but increasingly, middle-class Sri Lankans are being priced out of the neighbourhoods in which they grew up. Colombo\u2019s price-to-income ratio is estimated at 55.3 in 2026, according to Numbeo\u2019s Property Price Index; while this measure is distorted due to the urban/rural divide in property prices, it still indicates a lack of affordability. Official and market data show that since 2019, Colombo asking prices have risen approximately 73% for land, 93% for houses and 119% for condominiums while construction costs have also risen by approximately 91%.\n\nThe alternative Budget would establish a First Home programme through Bank of Ceylon, People\u2019s Bank, National Savings Bank and the Housing Development Finance Corporation. The treasury would explicitly fund an interest rate subsidy for first time home buyers through the State banks to cover mortgages on concessional rates, subsidising the first 3% or 4% of the mortgage interest rate for say, the first 5 to 7 years. Support would decline as household income rises.\n\nA first-home mortgage could apply to properties valued at up to Rs. 30-35 million and the subsidised mortgage would cover up to 80% of the purchase price, subject to a maximum subsidised loan of Rs. 20 million. Buyers purchasing properties above Rs. 25 million would therefore need to finance the additional amount through their own funds or borrowing at normal commercial rates. Repayment periods could extend up to 25 years. Eligibility would be restricted to the purchase of a first residential property, with an indicative household income ceiling of Rs. 600,000-750,000 per month. Similar policies have been in place in many countries including India, Malaysia and Indonesia."
