    "chunk_size_bytes": 1817,
    "chunk_text": "A first-home mortgage could apply to properties valued at up to Rs. 30-35 million and the subsidised mortgage would cover up to 80% of the purchase price, subject to a maximum subsidised loan of Rs. 20 million. Buyers purchasing properties above Rs. 25 million would therefore need to finance the additional amount through their own funds or borrowing at normal commercial rates. Repayment periods could extend up to 25 years. Eligibility would be restricted to the purchase of a first residential property, with an indicative household income ceiling of Rs. 600,000-750,000 per month. Similar policies have been in place in many countries including India, Malaysia and Indonesia.\n\nThe Government\u2019s principal contribution need not be a large cash subsidy. Well-located State land around railway stations and major transport corridors could be leased for medium-density housing, with private developers selected through competitive tenders; keeping land under public ownership would remove a major component of development costs and reduce apartment prices.\n\nMortgage and rent-to-own programs must also increase housing supply, subsidising demand without expanding supply risks simply pushing up property prices. Subsidised units should therefore be developed alongside new housing and carry temporary resale restrictions to prevent speculation. The objective is to expand affordable middle-class housing and create a realistic path from renting to home ownership.\n\nBudget 2027 projects that direct taxes would contribute only 24% to total tax revenue while indirect taxes would account for 76%; a ratio that has consistently hovered between 80:20 and 70:30, always in favour of indirect taxes like VAT and import duties. Indirect taxes are regressive by nature, ie, they impact low income groups disproportionately."
