    "chunk_size_bytes": 1688,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 projects that direct taxes would contribute only 24% to total tax revenue while indirect taxes would account for 76%; a ratio that has consistently hovered between 80:20 and 70:30, always in favour of indirect taxes like VAT and import duties. Indirect taxes are regressive by nature, ie, they impact low income groups disproportionately.\n\nThat is the economic space in which aspiration withers. This is where opposition to AKD and the NPP Government will converge, joining farming communities including those that held a protest on the 2nd of November in Anuradhapura or the Government Service Engineers\u2019 Association that held a nationwide strike on the 13 of November. The Main Opposition SJB is uniquely placed to take advantage of a potential middle-class revolt against the Government, given its strong middle-class bases in electorates such as Dehiwala, Kandy, Galle, Matara, Kurunegala, Badulla, Bandarawela and so on.\n\nAny alternative Budget operating within the present debt sustainability framework would have to retain the fiscal anchor: Sri Lanka is committed to returning to a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP from 2027. The alternative Budget would not therefore abandon fiscal consolidation but it would alter the composition of revenue and expenditure within the fiscal anchor.\n\nThe objective should be broadly revenue neutral middle-class relief. The alternative Budget would match the 2027 Budget\u2019s Rs.162,500 monthly tax-free threshold, but provide substantially greater relief immediately above the threshold through lower marginal income tax rates. This avoids narrowing the tax base further while reducing the burden on lower and middle-income earners."
