    "chunk_text": "The objective should be broadly revenue neutral middle-class relief. The alternative Budget would match the 2027 Budget\u2019s Rs.162,500 monthly tax-free threshold, but provide substantially greater relief immediately above the threshold through lower marginal income tax rates. This avoids narrowing the tax base further while reducing the burden on lower and middle-income earners.\n\n* Note: these are marginal rates: higher rates apply only to income within each specific band\n\nRelief is concentrated on lower and middle-income taxpayers and declines as incomes rise. The 40% and 42% top rates are transitional, with a three-year review allowing thresholds or rates to be adjusted if growth and revenue performance permit.\n\nIncome tax not collected at source is generally settled through self-assessment, including quarterly instalments and a final payment. Advance Personal Income Tax (APIT) on employment income is deducted directly by employers and remitted to the IRD, making it one of the more reliable methods of collecting personal income tax.\n\nUnder the alternative Budget, APIT withholding tables would be adjusted to reflect the new marginal rates; the proposed 40% and 42% rates on the highest bracket of employment incomes would therefore be collected progressively through payroll during the year. APIT is not an additional tax so amounts withheld are credited against the taxpayer\u2019s final income-tax liability, improving collection certainty while reducing the risk of unpaid tax and creating no additional taxes on the tax payer."
