    "chunk_size_bytes": 1339,
    "chunk_text": "Under the alternative Budget, APIT withholding tables would be adjusted to reflect the new marginal rates; the proposed 40% and 42% rates on the highest bracket of employment incomes would therefore be collected progressively through payroll during the year. APIT is not an additional tax so amounts withheld are credited against the taxpayer\u2019s final income-tax liability, improving collection certainty while reducing the risk of unpaid tax and creating no additional taxes on the tax payer.\n\nThe higher withholding remains creditable against final PIT liability and should not automatically be counted as additional permanent revenue; its main purpose is to collect a larger proportion of the eventual tax liability earlier and reduce collection risk. The Rs.10 million threshold would have to apply to aggregate interest income across financial institutions rather than separately at each bank.\n\nThe alternative Budget would be an attempt to rebuild the ladder of middle-class asset ownership that has been severely damaged by the economic crisis: income becomes savings; savings become a deposit; a deposit becomes a home; work makes a family car attainable; and rising income once again produces a material improvement in living standards. That is what restoring aspiration must look like, and the national Budget must reflect this."
