    "chunk_text": "Stop financial bleeding before expanding SriLankan Airlines\n\nAn independent investigation into financial leakages, unnecessary expenditure and management accountability must be central to the national carrier\u2019s five-year recovery plan\n\nSriLankan Airlines has once again announced an ambitious five-year plan aimed at restoring profitability, expanding its fleet and improving operational efficiency.\n\nThe plan was discussed before Parliament\u2019s Sectoral Oversight Committee on Infrastructure and Strategic Development, where attention was given to aircraft shortages, technical delays, mounting debt and procurement difficulties.\n\nThese are undoubtedly serious matters requiring immediate attention. However, there is another equally important question that deserves national discussion.\n\nBefore expanding the airline, have we properly identified and stopped the financial leakages, unnecessary expenditure, poor management practices and potential irregularities that may be contributing to its continuing losses?\n\nImagine running a small shop that continuously loses money. Would the first solution be to open another five branches, purchase additional equipment and employ more staff? Certainly not.\n\nAny responsible business owner would first examine the accounts, identify where money is being lost, investigate unnecessary expenses and take immediate corrective action.\n\nOnly after bringing the existing business under control would expansion become a sensible option. Why should this basic principle be any different for SriLankan Airlines?\n\nAn airline is undoubtedly more complex than a small shop. Aircraft availability, network connectivity and operational reliability are essential to generating revenue. Nevertheless, expanding a business that is already making substantial losses without properly addressing its underlying weaknesses could increase the financial burden rather than resolve it."
  },
