    "md5": "8ea6e6d3c470a5929a46934356bb2a39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "An airline is undoubtedly more complex than a small shop. Aircraft availability, network connectivity and operational reliability are essential to generating revenue. Nevertheless, expanding a business that is already making substantial losses without properly addressing its underlying weaknesses could increase the financial burden rather than resolve it.\n\nThe same report identified substantial increases in fuel expenditure, foreign exchange losses and continuing structural financial difficulties.\n\nMeanwhile, the President has reportedly informed Parliament that the airline\u2019s accumulated losses between 2008 and 2025 reached approximately Rs. 528 billion. These figures should concern every Sri Lankan citizen.\n\nThe airline\u2019s financial problems cannot simply be attributed to corruption or management failures. Fuel prices, exchange rates, aircraft maintenance, financing costs and international disruptions all have significant consequences.\n\nHowever, these external challenges should not prevent a thorough investigation into expenditure that management can actually control. Why are overseas stations and GSA contracts not receiving greater attention?\n\nOne area that deserves particular scrutiny is SriLankan Airlines\u2019 overseas station operations, including General Sales Agent (GSA) agreements, ground handling arrangements, staffing structures and other contracted services.\n\nAcross international stations, expenditure is often incurred in foreign currencies such as Singapore dollars, US dollars and euros.\n\nWhen converted into Sri Lankan rupees, even relatively small amounts of avoidable expenditure can become substantial.\n\nAre all overseas GSA arrangements still commercially necessary? Are the services being billed actually delivered? Are staffing levels justified by the workload? Are contracts being regularly reviewed against market rates? Could some activities be managed more efficiently through alternative arrangements?"
  },
