    "md5": "74e0b4e33d5c18832db6ca420ca0c87f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Are all overseas GSA arrangements still commercially necessary? Are the services being billed actually delivered? Are staffing levels justified by the workload? Are contracts being regularly reviewed against market rates? Could some activities be managed more efficiently through alternative arrangements?\n\nHowever, every agreement involving the airline\u2019s money should be independently examined to establish whether it represents genuine value.\n\nWhere there are credible allegations of overbilling, unsupported invoices, conflicts of interest or services not delivered, they should be investigated using contracts, invoices, duty rosters, operational reports and payment records.\n\nAny wrongdoing established through proper investigation must lead to accountability and, where legally possible, the recovery of funds.\n\nThe airline should commission a genuinely independent financial and operational audit, prioritising areas of greatest risk across its major departments and overseas operations.\n\nThis examination should include aircraft leasing, maintenance expenditure, procurement, crew accommodation, transportation, station administration, staffing, GSA contracts and ground handling agreements.\n\nThe objective must not simply be to produce another report that remains on a shelf.\n\nThe findings should identify unnecessary expenditure, weaknesses in internal controls, commercially unjustified contracts and opportunities for immediate savings.\n\nThe Government should also establish a clear process for referring suspected criminal conduct to the appropriate independent authorities.\n\nAn investigation must be based on evidence, conducted transparently and remain free from political interference.\n\nThe proposal to increase the fleet from 23 aircraft to at least 29 by 2029 may be operationally justified.\n\nAircraft shortages can cause delays, cancellations, missed connections, additional accommodation expenses and serious damage to customer confidence."
