    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "The proposal to increase the fleet from 23 aircraft to at least 29 by 2029 may be operationally justified.\n\nAircraft shortages can cause delays, cancellations, missed connections, additional accommodation expenses and serious damage to customer confidence.\n\nTherefore, every aircraft acquisition should be supported by a transparent business case demonstrating expected revenue, route profitability, operating costs and financial risks.\n\nThe airline must also review routes that consistently perform poorly, aircraft utilisation, scheduling efficiency, maintenance planning, cargo opportunities and revenue management.\n\nThe objective should be a financially sustainable airline, not simply a bigger airline.\n\nSriLankan Airlines has experienced numerous leadership changes and restructuring proposals over the years.\n\nEach new leadership team arrives with expectations of improvement, yet the underlying financial difficulties remain.\n\nThe time has come to move beyond announcements and establish measurable accountability.\n\nEvery department should have clearly defined financial and operational targets.\n\nSenior management should be required to explain significant budget overruns, recurring inefficiencies and failures to implement agreed corrective measures.\n\nA confidential and independently supervised reporting mechanism should also protect employees and former employees who provide credible evidence of financial irregularities or misconduct.\n\nThose raising legitimate concerns should not face retaliation merely for requesting an investigation.\n\nA credible recovery plan should begin with immediate action rather than waiting several years for results.\n\nWithin the first 90 days, the Government should initiate independent reviews of expenditure presenting significant financial risks, major overseas contracts and areas where credible financial irregularities have been reported."
  },
