    "md5": "44a50ac8fbe647e2975c423815e7f115",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "Within the first 90 days, the Government should initiate independent reviews of expenditure presenting significant financial risks, major overseas contracts and areas where credible financial irregularities have been reported.\n\nWithin 12 months, management should publish measurable results showing savings achieved, operational improvements, route performance and progress towards financial sustainability.\n\nThe longer-term fleet and network strategy should proceed alongside these measures, but every major investment must be justified by independently reviewed financial projections.\n\nParliament should receive regular progress reports, with sufficient information made public to demonstrate that genuine corrective action is taking place.\n\nSri Lanka is a country where many families continue to struggle with the cost of living.\n\nPublic resources are precious, and every rupee committed to supporting a State enterprise that continues to make losses carries an opportunity cost.\n\nThat money could otherwise support healthcare, education, infrastructure and essential public services.\n\nSriLankan Airlines remains an important national institution, with experienced employees, international connections and the potential to contribute meaningfully to tourism and the economy.\n\nIts dedicated workforce should not be unfairly blamed for failures beyond its control.\n\nBut protecting the airline must also mean protecting public money.\n\nThe Government, Parliament, Finance Ministry and airline leadership have a responsibility to ensure that recovery is built on transparency, financial discipline, operational efficiency and accountability.\n\nThe five-year plan may contain worthwhile proposals. But the public deserves to know whether it includes a sufficiently independent investigation into existing expenditure, contracts and financial controls.\n\nBefore approving further major expansion, Parliament should demand clear answers."
