    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Sri-Lanka-s-Budget-2027-From-economic-recovery-to-better-livelihoods/4-800519",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795559199.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-25-dailyftlk-453a6e6d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8663a6d46516a129d4a2674e1e5e22cb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "Presented under the theme \u201cA Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life\u201d, Budget 2027 seeks to strengthen economic growth while addressing the expectations of citizens who continue to experience the consequences of the economic crisis. Its success, however, will ultimately depend not on the scale of financial allocations announced, but on the effectiveness with which these resources are converted into measurable economic and social outcomes.\n\nThis represents a significant commitment to capital expenditure at a time when the country continues to face considerable fiscal constraints. The Government has established a medium-term objective of achieving economic growth of 7% or higher, maintaining revenue at not less than 16% of GDP and gradually increasing it towards 20% in the longer term. These objectives are important, but their achievement requires a careful balance between revenue mobilisation, expenditure management, debt sustainability and economic expansion.\n\nGovernment revenue is estimated at approximately 15.8% of GDP in 2027. Although this represents a substantial improvement from the levels experienced during the crisis, achieving the medium-term revenue target remains challenging.\n\nAt the same time, interest payments continue to absorb a significant portion of public resources. The estimated allocation for interest payments is Rs. 2,643 billion, highlighting the continuing burden of public debt on fiscal space. The policy challenge is therefore twofold: increasing revenue without unnecessarily burdening productive economic activity, and improving expenditure efficiency so that public resources generate greater economic and social returns."
  },
