    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b1400acfe7a08a601bcd7ebcaf90bffa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "At the same time, interest payments continue to absorb a significant portion of public resources. The estimated allocation for interest payments is Rs. 2,643 billion, highlighting the continuing burden of public debt on fiscal space. The policy challenge is therefore twofold: increasing revenue without unnecessarily burdening productive economic activity, and improving expenditure efficiency so that public resources generate greater economic and social returns.\n\nThe proposed personal income tax revisions represent an important step towards providing relief to individual taxpayers. From 1 April 2027, the annual tax-free threshold is proposed to increase from Rs. 1.8 million to Rs. 1.95 million, equivalent to a monthly threshold of Rs. 162,500. The revised progressive tax structure introduces a 12% tax band and widens several existing income brackets. The maximum marginal tax rate remains 36%, but the income level at which it applies is increased. These changes provide additional relief to salaried employees and other individual taxpayers, particularly those in the middle-income categories. They may also help improve disposable income and ease some household financial pressures.\n\nHowever, the long-term sustainability of personal income tax relief depends on broadening the tax base, improving compliance and bringing more economic activity into the formal system. The Government must therefore strengthen digital tax administration, improve information-sharing between institutions and reduce opportunities for tax evasion, while ensuring that compliant taxpayers are treated fairly.MA more transparent, predictable and efficient tax system would strengthen taxpayer confidence and improve the environment for private investment."
  },
