    "md5": "2f7769eb2311c40afc52dd65082accbd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "One of the notable proposals in Budget 2027 is the \u201cA Share for Everyone\u201d initiative, which seeks to introduce eligible students aged 18 and above to investment in the Colombo Stock Exchange through a share allocation valued at Rs. 10,000. This initiative has the potential to promote financial literacy, investment awareness and long-term savings among young people.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, developing a broader investment culture and strengthening capital market participation are important elements of financial inclusion and economic modernisation.\n\nMicro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) remain among the most important contributors to employment, regional economic activity and household income. Budget 2027 allocates approximately Rs. 67.7 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing arrangements intended to facilitate around Rs. 174 billion in concessionary financing for MSMEs through State and private banking institutions. These financing arrangements are expected to support working capital requirements, investment in machinery, technology improvements, business expansion and employment creation.\n\nThe effectiveness of these programs, however, depends on more than the availability of funds. Entrepreneurs must be able to access financing through transparent, simplified and practical procedures. Credit assessment should recognise the realities of small businesses while maintaining appropriate safeguards against excessive lending and non-performing loans.\n\nFinancial assistance should also be complemented by business development services, accounting and financial management support, digitalisation, market access and export readiness. Particular attention should be given to women-led enterprises, rural businesses, young entrepreneurs and enterprises operating in sectors with significant employment potential. The objective should be to transform MSMEs from survival-oriented businesses into productive, competitive and sustainable enterprises."
  },
