    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "Economic growth cannot be concentrated exclusively in Colombo and other major urban centres. The allocation of Rs. 50 billion for district development programs provides an opportunity to strengthen regional infrastructure, improve local economic activity and address disparities in development.\n\nSri Lanka must also address the growing importance of technical education, digital skills, vocational training and industry-oriented qualifications. The education system should increasingly connect learning with employment opportunities, entrepreneurship and the skills required by emerging industries. Improving public health services, educational access and the quality of human resources would strengthen the country's ability to participate in higher-value economic activities.\n\nAgriculture and fisheries remain important sources of livelihood, particularly in rural and coastal communities. Budget 2027 allocates Rs. 6.2 billion for fisheries sector development, including fishery harbour infrastructure, research and inland fisheries development.\n\nThe Government has also set an objective of increasing fisheries export earnings from $ 300 million to $ 600 million by 2030. Achieving this target requires more than financial allocations. The sector needs modern technology, improved cold-chain facilities, quality assurance, export certification, sustainable resource management and stronger market access. Similarly, agricultural development should increasingly focus on productivity, value addition, technology adoption, storage facilities, efficient distribution and reducing post-harvest losses.\n\nThe objective must be to move beyond subsistence-oriented production towards commercially viable, competitive and market-oriented agriculture. Rural economic development should therefore be integrated with transport infrastructure, financial services, digital connectivity and entrepreneurship."
