    "md5": "5d69d4a88aed0e26da1778bc90aabbab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1640,
    "chunk_text": "The public should be able to assess not only how much money has been allocated, but also what has been achieved through those allocations. Regular reporting on project progress, expenditure performance, employment outcomes and economic benefits would strengthen public confidence and improve fiscal accountability.\n\nBudget 2027 provides opportunities to strengthen economic recovery through investment, entrepreneurship, infrastructure development, social protection and digital transformation.\n\nSri Lanka must also ensure that economic growth is inclusive. Vulnerable households, low-income earners, persons with disabilities, elderly citizens and communities affected by economic or natural disasters require effective and targeted support. Social protection programs should be designed to protect those who genuinely need assistance while strengthening opportunities for economic participation and self-reliance. At the same time, fiscal discipline, efficient public expenditure and responsible debt management must remain central to the country's economic policy.\n\nSri Lanka's economic recovery has created an opportunity to move towards a more productive, investment-oriented and inclusive economy. Budget 2027 seeks to support this transition through personal income tax relief, MSME financing, investment incentives, housing, infrastructure, health, education, agriculture, fisheries and digitalisation. The challenge now is implementation. Government institutions must translate Budget proposals into practical programs, while the private sector must respond through investment, innovation, entrepreneurship and employment creation."
