    "md5": "ba5462bf70b6e0c1d33444939e082be2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1736,
    "chunk_text": "Deloitte Sri Lanka examines Budget 2027 as nation looks beyond stabilisation towards sustainable growth\n\nAs Sri Lanka moves beyond economic stabilisation towards its next phase of growth, attention is turning to how fiscal reforms, investment priorities and policy initiatives can translate into meaningful economic progress. Bringing together perspectives from Government, industry and economic policy, Deloitte Sri Lanka hosted its Budget 2027 webinar, \u201cBeyond Stabilization: Building for Growth,\u201d on 12 November, 2026, following the presentation of the National Budget in Parliament.\n\nThe webinar provided a platform for policymakers, economists and business leaders to examine the Budget through two complementary discussions, \u201cFiscal Perspective\u201d and \u201cTrade & Economy.\u201d Moderated by Partner and Head of Tax Charmaine Tillekeratne and Head of Strategy, Risk & Transactions Ruvini Fernando, respectively, the sessions explored the implications of fiscal measures, investment, trade, energy and economic reforms. The discussions highlighted the importance of moving beyond policy announcements towards effective implementation and tangible outcomes for businesses and the wider economy.\n\nBudget 2027 reflects Sri Lanka\u2019s continued emphasis on fiscal discipline while seeking to create greater opportunities for investment and economic expansion. According to Budget 2027, the Government targets a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP and an overall fiscal deficit of 4.5% in 2027, with tax revenue projected at 14.6% of GDP. Total budgeted revenue stands at Rs. 6.15 trillion against an expenditure of Rs. 7.93 trillion, reflecting the continued importance of balancing fiscal commitments with national development priorities."
  },
