    "md5": "e8cf8ad8bddf7d918e87b1e3c9a7d277",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027 reflects Sri Lanka\u2019s continued emphasis on fiscal discipline while seeking to create greater opportunities for investment and economic expansion. According to Budget 2027, the Government targets a primary surplus of 2.3% of GDP and an overall fiscal deficit of 4.5% in 2027, with tax revenue projected at 14.6% of GDP. Total budgeted revenue stands at Rs. 6.15 trillion against an expenditure of Rs. 7.93 trillion, reflecting the continued importance of balancing fiscal commitments with national development priorities.\n\nOther notable measures include the proposed expansion of capital gains tax coverage to certain digital assets and commercial rights, legal recognition of electronic VAT invoices and changes to VAT refund eligibility for new exporters. The proposed abolition of the Tourism Development Levy from April 2027 also forms part of the Budget\u2019s revenue-related measures.\n\nBeyond taxation, public investment and private-sector development feature prominently in the Government\u2019s growth agenda. Budget 2027 allocates Rs. 2 trillion for public investment, with substantial provisions for highways, railways, healthcare, irrigation and water supply. Support for micro, small and medium enterprises also remains a priority, with Rs. 67.69 billion allocated towards interest subsidies and bank refinancing to support Rs. 174 billion in concessionary lending.\n\nThe importance of these measures was reflected in Deloitte\u2019s webinar discussions, where panellists examined the challenges of improving access to finance, strengthening investment facilitation and reducing administrative barriers. The conversations highlighted that financial allocations alone may not be sufficient without stronger institutional capabilities and practical mechanisms to support implementation."
  },
