    "chunk_text": "The domestic economy faced mounting cost pressures, with fuel and energy expenditure rising sharply alongside a global fertiliser shortage, and the potential impact of severe El Ni\u00f1o conditions on the agriculture sector. Over the quarter, the Rupee was observed to depreciate against the US Dollar increasing pressures on reserves. Furthermore, year-on-year headline inflation, measured by the National Consumer Price Index (NCPI) reached 8.1% in August, exceeding the Central Bank\u2019s target range for a second consecutive month.\n\nFurther reinforcing investor confidence, Fitch Ratings upgraded Sri Lanka\u2019s credit rating to \u2018B-\u2019 with a Stable Outlook, citing stronger-than-expected fiscal and external sector performance. This marked a notable improvement from the \u2018CCC+\u2019 rating assigned since November 2020, signaling progress in the country\u2019s economic recovery.\n\nAgainst this mixed macroeconomic backdrop, Sri Lanka\u2019s cement industry moved to strengthen market resilience by maintaining uninterrupted supply and stable market prices despite significant increases in raw material, freight, insurance and fuel costs. Cement consumption continued its upward trajectory during the quarter, supported by favourable weather conditions and the availability of construction materials. The Sri Lanka Purchasing Managers\u2019 Index for Construction (PMI-Construction) also indicated continued industry expansion with new orders stemming from State-led development projects, although supply chain disruptions in the Middle East constrained the availability of bitumen, required for road construction activities.\n\nTokyo Cement Group further strengthened its market position during the quarter, gaining market share as coastal shipping improved access to the Western Province and beyond. The expanded market reach supported higher utilisation of the Group\u2019s enhanced production capacity, driving greater operational efficiency."
