    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Key economic sectors contract; Opposition raises alarm\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Joint Opposition criticized the Government over the contraction in several key economic sectors, including paddy cultivation, fisheries, ornamental fish exports and tea, saying the Budget has failed to adequately address the declining performance of sectors vital for employment generation and income growth.\n\nSpeaking at the weekly press conference, its convener former minister G.L. Peiris said these sectors were needed for the country\u2019s development and employment generation.\n\nNow when you compare the second quarter of last year with the second quarter of this year, all the sectors that need growth have contracted. Paddy cultivation has contracted by 15 percent, fisheries by 10 percent, aquarium fisheries for export has contracted by 61% and tea by two percent, according to him.\n\nHe highlighted that unemployment was the biggest issue the country was facing.\n\n\u201cWe have 40,000 to 50,000 unemployed graduates, and the situation is likely to worsen in the near future. Sri Lanka has a population of 1.8 million in the 15\u201319 age group and five million in the 10\u201324 age group. These young people will gradually enter the labour market, but there is insufficient employment opportunities for them,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said 50 percent of educated youths seek to migrate because there are no jobs available in this country.\n\n\u201cThe largest number of people leaving the country were doctors. There are 7000 vacancies in the health sector. This is going to drastically impact on the quality of health care,\u201d he said.\n\nAsserting that the same was true of the higher education sector, quoting the Federation of University Teachers\u2019 Association (FUTA), he said 12,000 academics were required for universities to operate and there were 6,000 vacancies."
