    "md5": "e7975e51372f0c84f3bb0efbba3d8550",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "UN and the Govt. of Sri Lanka strengthen dialogue to build trust and support inclusive recovery\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The United Nations and the Government of Sri Lanka concluded a two-year joint initiative aimed at fostering social cohesion and supporting an inclusive economic recovery by strengthening dialogue across public institutions, private workplaces, and local communities.\n\nA central milestone of the \u2018Social Dialogue for Peace and Crisis Prevention in Sri Lanka\u2019 project was the adoption of a Declaration on Social Dialogue in the Public Service, which establishes South Asia\u2019s first national framework for structured dialogue within the state sector.\n\nLaunched in response to the socio-economic pressures of Sri Lanka\u2019s economic crisis, where higher taxes, rising living costs, and reduced purchasing power have strained households, the initiative sought to prevent disputes from escalating by bringing government officials, employers, workers, and community members to the same table.\n\nAt a closing event at Hotel Cinnamon Grand on Friday, state representatives, private sector leaders, trade unions,[1] civil society, and development partners, stakeholders reviewed the programme\u2019s outcomes and outlined strategies to sustain the mechanisms long-term.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka\u2019s recovery must deliver better public services, fairer conditions at work and greater opportunity for communities to shape their future,\u201d said Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche, UN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka. \u201cThis initiative puts dialogue at the heart of that effort, bringing people together to resolve concerns before they deepen into divisions. The responsibility now is to turn listening into action, so that people see their concerns addressed and have reason to trust the institutions that serve them.\u201d"
  },
