    "md5": "8d374c6778ce6ba9a9da82d125396cea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cSri Lanka\u2019s recovery must deliver better public services, fairer conditions at work and greater opportunity for communities to shape their future,\u201d said Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche, UN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka. \u201cThis initiative puts dialogue at the heart of that effort, bringing people together to resolve concerns before they deepen into divisions. The responsibility now is to turn listening into action, so that people see their concerns addressed and have reason to trust the institutions that serve them.\u201d\n\nFunded by the UN Sri Lanka SDG Fund, the project was jointly implemented by the International Labour Organisation (ILO), the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO), and the United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA).\n\nWorking alongside the Ministry of Public Administration, the initiative focused on giving a voice to Sri Lanka\u2019s roughly 1.4 million public service workers through a new three-tier escalation ladder:\n\nWorkplace Level: 87 institutional forums were created to address immediate operational issues, ranging from promotion processes to workplace safety. Sectoral Level: Seven sectoral forums were established to handle complex disputes that cannot be resolved at the institutional level. National Level: The newly launched National Public Service Dialogue Forum addresses cross-sectoral issues requiring central government intervention.\n\nIn the private and plantation sectors, project partnered with key industry and labour bodies, including the Employers\u2019 Federation of Ceylon (EFC), the UN Global Compact Network Sri Lanka, the National Chamber of Commerce of Sri Lanka (NCCSL), and the National Labour Advisory Council (NLAC), to advance gender equality and workplace safety."
