    "chunk_text": "Budget offers no plan to lift working people out of poverty- Communist Party\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) - The Budget for 2027, presented to Parliament on 12 November 2026, is a status-quo budget which meant to manage the economy within the IMF framework, yet without any plan to lift people out of poverty, the Communist Party of Sri Lanka said.\n\nThe party said the budget manages the economy within the IMF framework and continues the liberal reform agenda, but it offers no plan to lift working people out of poverty or to transform the economy. It was attempted to draw a beautiful picture by presenting macro-economic indicators, yet ordinary people of the country are hard hit and unable to meet the minimum daily living.\n\nThe party in a statement signed by its General Secretary Dr. G. Weerasinghe, said, \u201cThe economy has stabilised even from the previous government\u2019s time. Growth reached 5 per cent in 2025 and the deficit has narrowed. But these gains were paid for by workers, farmers and the poor. The budget does not say who will now share in the recovery.\n\nThe Budget Speech offers almost no analysis of the world in which Sri Lanka must make its way. It mentions the war in West Asia mainly as a cause of higher fuel prices.\n\nYet the world is in upheaval. The wars in Ukraine, Palestine, Iran and Sudan are killing hundreds of thousands and disrupting the global trade in food, fuel and fertiliser. By the government\u2019s own account, the closure of the Strait of Hormuz alone cost Sri Lanka more than Rs. 147 billion.\n\nThe Speech is silent on the rise of unilateralism and protectionism in the Global North, where tariffs, sanctions and export controls are now used as weapons. It is equally silent on the shift of the world economy\u2019s centre of gravity to Asia. Trade agreements with China and India and an application to join RCEP are listed as separate items, not as a strategy."
