    "chunk_text": "The Speech is silent on the rise of unilateralism and protectionism in the Global North, where tariffs, sanctions and export controls are now used as weapons. It is equally silent on the shift of the world economy\u2019s centre of gravity to Asia. Trade agreements with China and India and an application to join RCEP are listed as separate items, not as a strategy.\n\nA large section of our people lives in poverty and cannot afford food or medicine. Inflation rose from 2.1 per cent in December 2025 to 8.3 per cent in October 2026, eating into wages and pensions. Yet:\n\n-Social protection, including Aswesuma, receives Rs. 230 billion, against Rs. 2,643 billion for interest payments.\n\nCuts in the levy on sprats and Maldive fish are positive, but insignificant. What is missing is a real poverty alleviation programme, with targets, a timeline and a clear account of who is poor and how they will be lifted out.\n\nThe budget continues the path of IMF stabilisation and liberal reform. Its central targets, a primary surplus of 2.3 per cent of GDP and a cap on primary spending, are the targets of austerity. Interest still takes about 43 per cent of government revenue and together with principal payments, it adds up to 78.5% of the same.\n\nThe reform agenda is familiar: a Public-Private Partnership Act, a holding company for state enterprises, an Investment Protection Bill, labour law reform, private partners for the Sapugaskanda refinery and Mattala airport, and tariff cuts on 800 product lines.\n\nStability is necessary, but it is not a development strategy. The Speech admits that the external sector has not been strengthened. The current account swung from a surplus of USD 1,853 million in the first nine months of 2025 to a deficit of USD 420 million in the same period of 2026. Trade deficit for the first nine months of 2026 was 7.9 billion USD compared to 5.2 billion USD for the same period in 2025. Opening the economy further, before building domestic production, will widen the gaps."
