    "chunk_text": "Stability is necessary, but it is not a development strategy. The Speech admits that the external sector has not been strengthened. The current account swung from a surplus of USD 1,853 million in the first nine months of 2025 to a deficit of USD 420 million in the same period of 2026. Trade deficit for the first nine months of 2026 was 7.9 billion USD compared to 5.2 billion USD for the same period in 2025. Opening the economy further, before building domestic production, will widen the gaps.\n\nWe welcome the rise in public investment to Rs. 2,000 billion, including about Rs. 100 billion for rural roads and Rs. 80 billion for railway electrification. But roads and railways alone will not industrialise the country. Public investment must be tied to domestic production through local content rules, local manufacture of buses, rolling stock and energy equipment, and development finance for chosen sectors. The Asian economies that industrialised did so through planning, directed credit and the protection of new industries, not by cutting tariffs and waiting for investors.\n\nIndirect taxes make up about 76 per cent of tax revenue. Every family pays VAT and SSCL on what it buys, whatever its income. Yet more than half of the budget\u2019s Rs. 28.4 billion in tax concessions is income tax relief for those earning over Rs. 150,000 a month, from which the poor gain nothing. Business gains the abolition of the Tourism Development Levy and new tax allowances.\n\nIndirect taxes on the poor should be reduced. The tax net should be widened to reach the wealthiest, especially in the informal sector, where many pay little or nothing. With revenue set to fall to 15.8 percent of GDP, below the government\u2019s own floor of 16 percent, the money for relief must come from taxing wealth.\n\n1.Adopt a foreign economic policy for a multipolar world, based on non-alignment and South\u2013South cooperation.\n\n2.Launch a national poverty alleviation programme and bring