    "chunk_text": "\"I leaned forward to look a little and it terrified me. It was before sleeping\", Francis recounted. \"Into hell one slips as the most natural thing. It is not the first time in my life that I have had this experience, but this time it was of a devouring intensity. It is dangerous to approach the gates of hell because the flames can come out and burn you.\"\n\nFern\u00e1ndez has tried to explain what the Pope's temptations might have been, whom he describes as a \"mystic\".\n\n\"Pagan temptation is to reject the true God in order to worship idols, or worse still to worship oneself. The strongest temptation is power badly managed\", the cardinal added, citing the pontiff.\n\n\"The temptation of power that places you above others, that induces you to subject others to your projects and to treat them like puppets, for him became terrible\", Fern\u00e1ndez explained.\n\nBergoglio was tormented by accusations he had received in Argentina of having been excessively harsh with subordinates and of having somehow abused his power during the ecclesiastical rise that led him to become provincial superior of the Jesuits (from 1973 to 1979) and Archbishop of Buenos Aires (from 1998 to 2013, when he was elected Pope).\n\nA political power from which he also had to defend himself physically, according to the Argentine cardinal, who said that Francis never visited Argentina also because of intelligence information indicating that \"the far right\" would \"organise something against him\", since he was considered an \"enemy\".\n\nYet, tensions also existed within the Vatican and the Curia, against which he took several decisions while avoiding others. Among the innovations, partly moderated by his successor, Robert Prevost, the current Leo XIV, who said he was a \"great shepherd and an irreproachable man\", there was also the possibility of ordaining married men as priests."
