    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "US court convicts China\u2019s Huawei for fraud, conspiring to steal trade secrets\n\nCNN - Chinese telecommunications equipment maker Huawei was convicted by a jury in New York City on Tuesday in a sprawling US criminal case on charges that it deceived banks about its business in Iran and stole trade secrets from American companies.\n\nThe verdict came in a criminal case that began in 2018, with the US government the following year placing Huawei on a trade blacklist that restricted its access to US technology amid American concerns that Huawei posed national security risks.\n\nThe jury in Brooklyn federal court found Huawei guilty on 10 of the 12 criminal counts the company faced, including bank fraud, wire fraud, sanctions violations and conspiring to steal trade secrets. The jury acquitted Huawei of conspiring to obstruct justice and racketeering conspiracy, a charge alleging the company had acted as a criminal enterprise.\n\nThe trial lasted more than two months, with prosecutors calling more than 60 witnesses and the defense calling 15. It was not immediately clear what penalties Huawei will face when US District Judge Ann Donnelly sentences it on September 14, 2027.\n\nReuters stories in 2012 and 2013 helped expose Huawei\u2019s activities in Iran and were discussed during the trial.\n\n\u201cAfter the media started questioning Huawei\u2019s operations in Iran, so did Huawei\u2019s banks,\u201d prosecutor Meredith Arfa told jurors during closing arguments in the case last week.\n\nBrian Heberlig, a lawyer for Huawei, said the company was disappointed by the convictions but said the acquittal on the racketeering conspiracy charge vindicated its \u201cgood-faith compliance culture.\u201d\n\nUS Attorney Joseph Nocella said in a statement: \u201cFew crimes are as serious as Huawei\u2019s decades-long pattern of endangering US national security, violating the sanctity of US commerce, and impeding the enforcement of US laws.\u201d China\u2019s embassy in Washington did not immediately respond to a request for comment."
  },
