    "md5": "852f01991147a672e6cdaab803bb5534",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "US Attorney Joseph Nocella said in a statement: \u201cFew crimes are as serious as Huawei\u2019s decades-long pattern of endangering US national security, violating the sanctity of US commerce, and impeding the enforcement of US laws.\u201d China\u2019s embassy in Washington did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\n\nExperts have said the case did not undermine Huawei\u2019s business, as the company diversified and emerged as a key player in China\u2019s push for artificial intelligence.\n\nIn December 2018, Meng Wanzhou, Huawei\u2019s chief financial officer and daughter of the company\u2019s founder, was detained in Canada on a US warrant, prompting a diplomatic crisis involving the United States, China and Canada.\n\nMeng had been secretly indicted on US charges of misleading an HSBC banker about Huawei\u2019s business in Iran, so the company could move dollars in and out of Iran in violation of American sanctions. Meng fought extradition to the US for nearly three years, before entering a deferred prosecution agreement and flying home to China to a hero\u2019s welcome. The charges against her were dropped.\n\nMeng did not testify at trial, though an HSBC banker she met with in 2013 did testify.\n\nThe 2018 indictment alleged that Huawei misled HSBC, Standard Chartered and BNP Paribas about its business dealings in Iran. Prosecutors added new charges in 2020, including allegations that Huawei conspired to steal trade secrets from US technology companies over two decades.\n\nDuring the trial, prosecutors sought to prove that Huawei stole source code from Cisco Systems for its routers, stole wireless telecommunications company Quintel\u2019s antenna technology, and hired a professor to gain access to a circuit board developed by semiconductor company CNEX.\n\nJurors watched a video that, according to prosecutors, showed a Huawei employee slipping a piece of a T-Mobile testing robot into his backpack, and heard about a Huawei engineer sneaking into a Fujitsu booth at a trade show to photograph a device."
