    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ae1ae2b7881accf1e09fcb6efc5b7e96",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Destructors\u2019 take different forms in different countries\n\nWe studied a short story called \u2018The Destructors\u2019 by British author Graham Greene as an Advance Level text. Our English teacher told us while discussing the text that there is no such word in English, and Greene in fact invented it.\n\nBut an internet search shows me that the word entered the English language in the 17th century, derived from Latin. Over time, it came to signify physical devices such as refuse incinerators or an explosive self-destruct mechanism in rocketry or defense.\n\nWhat my English teacher meant perhaps was that Greene (1904-1991) brought back its original meaning, which meant a person or force that destroys. But this column is not about the origin or meaning of \u2018destructor.\u2019 It\u2019s rather about the people\u00a0 described in his story as destructors. They are all British teenagers, members of a gang.\n\nI remember my teacher laughing as he reached the end of the story. He was about the same age as Mr. Thomas, the\u00a0 pensioner whose life is pretty much destroyed by the young hooligans. But he found it funny. It is very bleak humour.\n\nThe setting is a poverty-stricken neighbourhood known as Wormsley Common, still bearing scars of German bombing during World War II. The street gang has a new leader called Trevor, from a family fallen from middle class status to poverty. Trevor resents Mr. Thomas\u2019 beautiful 18th century house which miraculously escaped the bombing.\n\nMr. Thomas,\u00a0 called \u2018Old Misery\u2019 by the teenage gang, lives alone. He\u00a0 is mocked and hated by them. When he goes away during a weekend, the gang begin to strip the house of all its fittings. They find Mr. Thomas\u2019 life savings (no more than seventy pounds) and burn the bank notes simply for fun.\n\nWhen the pensioner returns, the gang locks him inside the outdoor toilet. They then tie the remaining load-bearing struts to the house to a lorry. When the driver pulls away, the entire house collapses into a heap of rubble."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-25-dailymirrorlk-453a29dc",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-453a29dc",
    "date_str": "2026-11-25",
    "description": "\u2018Destructors\u2019 take different forms in different countries",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Destructors-take-different-forms-in-different-countries/172-355683",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795589395.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-25-dailymirrorlk-453a29dc-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dd95a10559f1ab530787f269b083d289",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2027,
    "chunk_text": "When the pensioner returns, the gang locks him inside the outdoor toilet. They then tie the remaining load-bearing struts to the house to a lorry. When the driver pulls away, the entire house collapses into a heap of rubble.\n\nGreene was commenting on the sense of nihilism and moral emptiness in post-war England. The gang revel in the violence because purely for the perverse pleasure of it. Their motive is not hate or theft, though envy of someone better off than them is certainly part of it.\u00a0 In 1972, Stanley Kubrik\u2019s film \u2018A Clockwork Orange\u2019 painted a grimmer picture by portraying a gang which indulges in physical violence and rape.\n\nEurope recovered quickly from the destruction of World War II, but youth malaise and unrest continued. It took the form of political protest against heavy-handed governance (French students against France\u2019s Charles de Galle, or Czech students against Soviet invasion forces), and non-political hooliganism of the kind Graham Greene described in \u2018Destructors\u2019 took second place.\n\nThis student unrest is now reflected in the recent wave of youth-led revolts from Sri Lanka and South Asia to currently Serbia and Albania. But there is plenty of non-political youth violence and vandalism in the world.\n\nA You Tube video on youth violence in the American state of New Mexico prompted me to write this column,\n\nA couple were badly beaten by five youths\u00a0 outside a cafeteria --\u00a0 three boys and two girls, all of them teenagers except the oldest male, 21,\u00a0 with a criminal record. The youngest girl was 12 and the other was 14.\n\nLike the gang members in Graham Greene\u2019s story, they didn\u2019t steal anything. Instead of vandalising\u00a0 property, they beat up the couple. The woman was pregnant. The culprits were quickly apprehended by the police.\n\nBut, in this real life case as in Greene\u2019s fictitious one (perhaps based on a real life story known to him), police can\u2019t establish a motive. It\u2019s nothing personal, and the impersonal quality of the act is truly chilling."
  },
