    "md5": "6b2d6b9ed70342b8e72b73d81cc7f00a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "According to a BBC report, the police believe she was held captive, tortured and ravaged. More than 10 days after they found her body, the police checked the list of some 8,000 missing children and couldn\u2019t identify her. \u201cThere was no sign of struggle at the spot where the body was found,\u201d the local police chief said.\n\nAnalysts say\u00a0 rape is increasingly used as an instrument to assert power and intimidate the powerless in India. They say this is not surprising in a hierarchical, patriarchal and increasingly polarised society, where hate is being used to divide people and harvest votes.\n\nAn awful sex ratio imbalance - largely because of illegal sex-selection abortions - means India has 112 boys born for every 100 girls, which is against the natural sex ratio of 105 boys for every 100 girls. The northern state of Haryana, which records the highest number of gang rapes in India, has the worst sex ratio in the country. In January alone, a 50-year-old man was held for mutilating a 10-year-old girl, a 15-year-old boy allegedly raped a three-and-a-half-year-old girl and a minor\u2019s girl\u2019s brutalised body was found in the fields. Eight Hindu men have been charged with the Kashmir gang-rape and murder of a Muslim girl.\n\nOne Indian social media video recently showed police and citizens nabbing four men who kidnapped a girl in broad daylight and sped away in a car. They were caught after a tyre burst and they were forced to stop.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, sexual violence, while not as acute as in India, remains a serious issue. But gang violence in the Western or the traditional Hong Kong sense is largely absent. Even in the roughest parts of Colombo, citizens are not threatened and shops are not looted by street gangs, and female workers\u00a0 and\u00a0 security guards can be seen walking home after work even late at night. Rape seems to be more a rural\u00a0 problem than urban in Sri Lanka."
  },
