    "md5": "962278a5fe6c09eac96a29b7fee0462b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1169,
    "chunk_text": "Anuradhapura: Protecting our ancient water civilisation,  archaeological heritage and national land\n\nAnuradhapura is replete with historical marvels; some of the marvels identified by the generations of yesteryear require National and People\u2019s attention to protect and preserve these marvels for the generations yet unborn.\n\nOne of those marvels is the Kalawewa National Park in Kekirawa, a sanctuary for elephants, particularly tuskers which are rare in the country. In the dry season,\u00a0 the water levels of the reservoirs recede, revealing green grasslands that attract elephants. The Kala Wewa and Balalu Wewa reservoirs, built in the 5th century, are central to the park\u2019s ecosystem. The massive Kumbuk Trees that line the park, are a habitat for numerous birds, resident and migratory.\n\nAnother marvel is the Yoda Ela, an 87 km (54 miles) long single-banking water canal constructed in 459 AD carrying excess water from the Kala Wewa reservoir in Anuradhapura to the Tissa Wewa reservoir. The Yodha Ela is acclaimed for its low gradient of 6 inches per mile which, even with modern day survey equipment, would be a difficult feat to achieve with such accuracy."
  },
