    "md5": "20bde9524260a5c7bac9aef3d99ff4cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "Another marvel is the Yoda Ela, an 87 km (54 miles) long single-banking water canal constructed in 459 AD carrying excess water from the Kala Wewa reservoir in Anuradhapura to the Tissa Wewa reservoir. The Yodha Ela is acclaimed for its low gradient of 6 inches per mile which, even with modern day survey equipment, would be a difficult feat to achieve with such accuracy.\n\nIn this landmark judgment, Justice A.R.B. Amerasinghe examined the proposed phosphate mining project in the Anuradhapura District.\n\nThe Court considered the potential consequences for the ancient irrigation systems of Rajarata, the Water and soil conservation ecosystems, underground aquifers and agricultural lands, the archaeological and cultural heritage and the livelihoods of local communities. One particularly important observation concerned the inadequacy of existing legal protection for the cultural-heritage landscape. Justice Amerasinghe drew attention to the fact that there was no adequate provision for protecting the cultural-heritage landscape such as the Yoda Wewa, as distinct from an individual archaeological monument. Justice Amerasinghe went on to say that protecting an individual dagoba or monument is not necessarily sufficient if the surrounding ancient irrigation system, landscape, waterways and archaeological setting are damaged. The judgement also emphasised the precautionary approach towards sustainable development and towards the principle that environmental damage must be borne by those responsible for causing it; the judgement deemed that if no plans are publicly available there is no way to assess the damage that could possibly be caused by the project; in these circumstances a project is not to be proceeded with.\n\nThe Justice Amerasinghe judgement says that Sri Lanka\u2019s law cannot be circumvented by secret agreements or by some type of understanding arrived at by two parties to the contract."
