    "md5": "6d9ca3bcae19185ee44afcee1999d19e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "In 1997, Justice C.G. Weeramantry, a former Vice-President of the International Court of Justice made a similar observation in a case that came up before him at the ICJ.\u00a0 Sri Lanka\u2019s ancient water- wisdom was internationally recognised.\n\nThe eminent judge referred to the ancient principle that not even a small quantity of rainwater should flow to the sea without benefiting the people.\n\nJustice Weeramanthry\u00a0 also referred to the sermon of Arahat Mahinda to King Devanampiya Tissa, explaining that the animals and birds have an equal right to live and move within the land. Justice Weeramanthry\u2019s central message was that development and environmental conservation must go together; he recognised that sustainable development was not a newly invented international concept: Sri Lanka had practised these principles for centuries.\n\nAn important point is that Justice Amerasinghe\u2019s Eppawela judgment referred to Justice Weeramantry\u2019s international jurisprudence. Their contributions provide a powerful legal and historical foundation that development and environmental-conservation must go hand-in-hand.\n\nJustice Amerasinghe and Justice Weeramantry both outline the doctrine of \u2018Public Trust\u2019, nationally and internationally. In this doctrine, there is an intergenerational responsibility, the responsibility to protect natural resources. The two eminent judges speak of sustainable development, the need to protect the Yoda Wewa cultural landscape, the ancient irrigation-wisdom, the precautionary approach to environmental damage, harmony between development and nature which includes the fauna and the flora and the majestic elephants that roam the land and the shared responsibility to protect them all for future generations.\n\nTogether, the wisdom of the honourable judges provides an important framework for examining development projects in Anuradhapura and elsewhere."
  },
