    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "Justice Amerasinghe and Justice Weeramantry both outline the doctrine of \u2018Public Trust\u2019, nationally and internationally. In this doctrine, there is an intergenerational responsibility, the responsibility to protect natural resources. The two eminent judges speak of sustainable development, the need to protect the Yoda Wewa cultural landscape, the ancient irrigation-wisdom, the precautionary approach to environmental damage, harmony between development and nature which includes the fauna and the flora and the majestic elephants that roam the land and the shared responsibility to protect them all for future generations.\n\nTogether, the wisdom of the honourable judges provides an important framework for examining development projects in Anuradhapura and elsewhere.\n\nThe following authorities should be examined together: the Antiquities Ordinance No. 9 of 1940 \u2013 protection of antiquities, ancient monuments and archaeological reserves, the National Environmental Act No. 47 of 1980 \u2013 environmental approvals and assessment where applicable, the State Lands Ordinance No. 8 of 1947 \u2013 legal administration of State land, the Mahaweli Authority of Sri Lanka Act No. 23 of 1979 \u2013 where applicable, to designated areas and lands.\n\nImportantly, Article 27(14) of the Constitution \u2013 State responsibility to protect, preserve and improve the environment, Article 28(f) \u2013 the duty of every person to protect nature and conserve its riches, the Eppawela judgement is particularly important when applying these laws to development proposals affecting interconnected archaeological and environmental resources.\n\nCan a Government undertake tourism and infrastructure development in Anuradhapura without first establishing, through proper archaeological, hydrological and environmental studies, that the ancient water systems, archaeological landscape and sacred character will be protected?"
