    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan refugee dies months after forced UK resettlement in Eswatini\n\nBBC - The UK forcibly resettled a refugee and convicted sex offender in an African country with which he had no connection, in a deal not disclosed to the UK Parliament or the local community.\n\nThe man, who was from Sri Lanka and had a history of severe mental illness and suicide attempts, took his own life months after arriving in Eswatini.\n\nThe BBC has obtained a copy of the draft deal between the two governments, in which the UK pledges to cover the costs of his resettlement for up to three years.\n\nThe UK government was finalising plans for 35-year-old Kishanth Peramalya to live there permanently when he died.\n\nMigration experts told the BBC this was the first known case of the UK government forcibly resettling in such a manner a refugee permanently overseas.\n\nIn 2024, the Labour government scrapped a controversial plan to send asylum seekers with pending claims to Rwanda. Now it wants to send failed asylum seekers overseas to third countries when they cannot be sent to their home countries, but is yet to announce any deals. Last week, five EU countries reached a deal to send people whose claims have been refused to Rwanda.\n\nThe Eswatini deal raises questions about how much the UK government should disclose about such arrangements, and how it protects both the people it sends overseas and the communities that receive them.\n\nThe UK government told the BBC that protecting the British public was its priority and that it \"continue[s] to work with the Eswatini authorities in relation to this case\".\n\nPeramalya's family is demanding answers about his treatment, while critics in Eswatini have accused Britain of using their country as a \"dumping ground\".\n\nIn 2021, the Royal Navy rescued Peramalya in the Indian Ocean, along with dozens of other Sri Lankan asylum seekers, as they tried to sail from India to Canada."
