    "md5": "9229f831a305e1313a9b979e4fa68c8e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "The High Court upheld the UK's refusal to admit him, in part because of the risk he posed and because ministers were making genuine efforts to find somewhere else safe for him to live. Court documents, seen by the BBC, show the UK approached more than 20 countries and could not secure an agreement.\n\nThen, in February 2026, the US began using its military base on Diego Garcia in its war with Iran. The island authorities began withdrawing non-essential personnel.\n\nAt the time of his death, the British and Eswatini governments had been finalising the agreement for Peramalya to live there permanently, with the UK to pay for the \"reasonable costs\" of his accommodation, healthcare, and other support for up to three years.\n\nThe document does not specify a total cost or explain how the two governments would manage the risks Peramalya posed to himself and others. But it explicitly acknowledged he required support that would meet his basic needs and protect his physical and mental health, and wellbeing.\n\nPeramalya's family has told the BBC he was initially placed in an isolated guesthouse on his own.\n\nSpeaking from India, his sister said the family had not been aware of the plan to make his transfer permanent, and that his mental health had worsened while in the country.\n\nShe said he would call her four times a day. \"My brother did not want to go there at all,\" she said. \"He couldn't manage being there alone, he was very lonely.\" The family also said it had been unaware of sexual offences committed by him.\n\nHe initially saw a psychiatrist weekly, she said. Later, after his mental health declined, he had daily appointments, and an attendant was brought in to prepare his food.\n\nThe UN's refugee agency, which had previously raised concerns about Peramalya's condition on Diego Garcia, said it was \"deeply saddened\" by his death and stressed that refugees being transferred to other different countries must be protected."
