    "md5": "cb33a33755b780d72c73840ea6e9036d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "The UN's refugee agency, which had previously raised concerns about Peramalya's condition on Diego Garcia, said it was \"deeply saddened\" by his death and stressed that refugees being transferred to other different countries must be protected.\n\nPeter Walsh, of Oxford University's Migration Observatory, described the case as \"highly unusual\".\n\n\"In most cases, people with criminal convictions who cannot be returned to their home country would simply remain in the UK. Third countries are generally quite reluctant to accept foreign national offenders,\" he said.\n\nIt is unclear how Britain chose Eswatini, but the kingdom had already agreed to accept US deportees under President Trump's wider programme of sending migrants to countries across Africa and Latin America. In 2025, the Trump administration paid Eswatini $5.1m (\u00a33.9m) to accept up to 160 people.\n\nMelusi Simelane, of the Southern African Litigation Centre, who is challenging the US deal in court, said migrant transfers were putting pressure on his country's limited resources.\n\n\"Our healthcare system is crumbling, not to mention our mental healthcare, so it is a big ask to bring people into our society who need a lot of support,\" he said.\n\n\"Africa is becoming a dumping ground for the world's unresolved problems.\"\n\nZakithi Sibandze, of the Eswatini Rural Women's Assembly, which campaigns to protect women and girls, said it was \"very painful\" that a sex offender had been relocated to her country.\n\n\"Why did they think it was okay to send him to Africa?\" she asked. \"The UK seems to be saying it is fine for people to commit crimes against women in poor countries.\"\n\nEarlier this year, the UN's special rapporteur on migrants' rights, Gehad Madi, urged governments to publish the terms of their migration arrangements with other countries and subject them to parliamentary and public oversight. \"Strengthening transparency is essential to reduce human rights risks, ensure accountability and uphold the rule of law,\" he said."
