    "chunk_text": "SLID-Frankfurt School of Finance & Management executive programme on Dec. 1\n\nRecognising the importance of strengthening risk oversight at board level, the Sri Lanka Institute of Directors (SLID), in collaboration with Frankfurt School of Finance & Management, will present \u2018Beyond the Obvious: Risk Management\u2019, an executive programme for directors and senior professionals.\n\nThe programme will take place on December 1, 2026, from 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., at Ivy Room, Cinnamon Grand Colombo.\n\nIt will be led by Dr. Joachim Bald, Frankfurt School\u2019s Senior International Advisor and Lead Consultant at its Competence Centre in Risk Management.\n\nMarking an inaugural initiative in the SLID\u2019s collaboration with Frankfurt School of Finance & Management, this programme establishes a partnership with one of Germany\u2019s leading business schools, bringing international expertise and perspectives in risk management to Sri Lanka\u2019s corporate and professional community.\n\nWith extensive advisory experience across Europe, Asia, Africa and the Middle East, Dr. Bald has worked with leading financial institutions and development organisations, including the European Investment Bank, European Bank for Reconstruction and Development, KfW, Development Bank Ghana, RawBank, Alex Bank and Banque Misr.\n\nHe also serves on the Supervisory Boards of KMF Bank in Kazakhstan and Bailyk Finance in Kyrgyzstan.\n\nAt Frankfurt School, Dr. Bald is Academic Director of the Certified Expert programmes in Risk Management, Treasury and Markets and Climate Risk Management. His expertise spans quantitative analytics, machine learning and generative AI, supported by his background in software development and statistics, a doctorate in economics, a German MBA and the US Certified Management Accountant qualification.\n\nThe programme will explore how the directors and senior executives can move beyond conventional approaches to risk identification and incorporate analytical insights into strategic decision-making."
