    "md5": "727af1c573e16497911ec0668a6b590e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1727,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka tea industry performance \u2013 January to September 2026\n\nDuring the period January to September 2026, Sri Lanka\u2019s tea industry recorded declines in both production and export volumes compared with the corresponding period in 2025, while the national tea sale average increased.\n\nTotal tea production declined by 6.54 million kilogrammes (Mn/Kgs), falling from 199.16 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 192.62 Mn/Kgs in 2026.\n\nTotal tea export volume also decreased by 8.91 Mn/Kgs, from 198.33 Mn/Kgs to 189.41 Mn/Kgs.\n\nMeanwhile, the national tea sale average increased by Rs.9.64 per kilogramme, rising from Rs.1,160.56 in 2025 to Rs.1,170.20 in 2026.\n\nIn terms of export destinations, T\u00fcrkiye emerged as the largest importer of Ceylon Tea during the first nine months of 2026, recording a substantial increase in imports from 15.26 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 35.71 Mn/Kgs in 2026, an increase of 20.44 Mn/Kgs.\n\nIn contrast, Iraq recorded a significant decline of 11.64 Mn/Kgs, with the imports falling from 30.07 Mn/Kgs to 18.43 Mn/Kgs. Russia maintained a relatively stable demand, with the imports increasing marginally by 0.22 Mn/Kgs to 16.92 Mn/Kgs.\n\nAzerbaijan and Jordan also recorded a notable growth, with the imports increasing by 2.29 Mn/Kgs and 2.15 Mn/Kgs, reaching 9.03 Mn/Kgs and 6.36 Mn/Kgs, respectively.\n\nHowever, several other key markets experienced declines. Libya recorded a reduction of 9.33 Mn/Kgs, while Chile and China declined by 2.28 Mn/Kgs and 0.80 Mn/Kgs, respectively. Saudi Arabia also recorded a marginal decrease of 0.16 Mn/Kgs.\n\nOverall, Sri Lanka\u2019s tea industry experienced lower production and export volumes during January to September 2026, despite the improvements in the national tea sale average."
  },
