    "md5": "dec214211080be6c6f54df585ae9ad59",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "However, several other key markets experienced declines. Libya recorded a reduction of 9.33 Mn/Kgs, while Chile and China declined by 2.28 Mn/Kgs and 0.80 Mn/Kgs, respectively. Saudi Arabia also recorded a marginal decrease of 0.16 Mn/Kgs.\n\nOverall, Sri Lanka\u2019s tea industry experienced lower production and export volumes during January to September 2026, despite the improvements in the national tea sale average.\n\nThe total tea production for the January to September 2026 period recorded a decline compared with the corresponding period in 2025, with the production decreasing across all three elevations. Total tea production declined by 6.54 Mn/Kgs, falling to 192.62 Mn/Kgs in 2026, from 199.16 Mn/Kgs in 2025.\n\nThe high-grown tea production declined by 1.21 Mn/Kgs, decreasing to 41.89 Mn/Kgs during January to September 2026, compared to 43.11 Mn/Kgs recorded during the corresponding period of 2025. The medium-grown teas experienced a more significant reduction of 3.07 Mn/Kgs, with the production falling to 33.55 Mn/Kgs, from 36.62 Mn/Kgs.\n\nThe low-grown teas, which continued to account for the largest share of national tea production at approximately 60.83 percent, recorded a decline of 2.26 Mn/Kgs. Production decreased to 117.17 Mn/Kgs during January to September 2026, compared with 119.43 Mn/Kgs in the corresponding period of 2025.\n\nThe decline in tea production during the period January to September 2026 reflects reduced output across all three elevations. The medium- grown teas experienced the most significant decline in volume, followed by the low-grown and high-grown teas. Despite the low-grown teas continuing to account for the largest share of total production, the combined reductions across all elevations contributed to the overall decline in Sri Lanka\u2019s tea production compared with the corresponding period of 2025."
  },
