    "chunk_text": "The decline in tea production during the period January to September 2026 reflects reduced output across all three elevations. The medium- grown teas experienced the most significant decline in volume, followed by the low-grown and high-grown teas. Despite the low-grown teas continuing to account for the largest share of total production, the combined reductions across all elevations contributed to the overall decline in Sri Lanka\u2019s tea production compared with the corresponding period of 2025.\n\nThe high-grown CTC production recorded a decline of 0.17 Mn/Kgs, decreasing from 3.84 Mn/Kgs in January to September 2025 to 3.67 Mn/Kgs in the corresponding period of 2026.\n\nIn contrast, the medium-grown CTC production recorded a significant increase of 0.91 Mn/Kgs, rising from 6.67 Mn/Kgs to 7.58 Mn/Kgs. This category was the main contributor to the overall growth in the CTC tea production.\n\nThe low-grown CTC production recorded a marginal decline of 0.05 Mn/Kgs, decreasing from 7.63 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 7.58 Mn/Kgs in 2026.\n\nOverall, the CTC tea production increased from 18.15 Mn/Kgs during January to September 2025 to 18.84 Mn/Kgs in the corresponding period of 2026. The increase was primarily driven by the strong growth in the medium- grown CTC production, which more than offset the declines recorded in the high-grown and low-grown categories.\n\nThe comparison of Sri Lanka tea sale averages for the period January to September 2026 against the corresponding period in 2025 indicates a marginal improvement in the overall average price in Sri Lankan rupee terms, while the US dollar values declined across all elevation categories.\n\nIn the high-grown category, the average price increased by Rs.21.33, rising from Rs.1,083.91 in 2025 to Rs.1,105.24 in 2026. However, in US dollar terms, the average declined by US $ 0.20, from US $ 3.62 to US $ 3.42."
