    "chunk_text": "In the high-grown category, the average price increased by Rs.21.33, rising from Rs.1,083.91 in 2025 to Rs.1,105.24 in 2026. However, in US dollar terms, the average declined by US $ 0.20, from US $ 3.62 to US $ 3.42.\n\nIn the low-grown category, which continued to command the highest average price among the elevation categories, the rupee value increased by Rs.19.16, from Rs.1,230.94 in 2025 to Rs.1,250.10 in 2026.\n\nHowever, the US dollar average declined by US $ 0.25, from US $ 4.12 to US $ 3.87.\n\nOverall, the national tea sale average for January to September 2026 increased by Rs.9.64, rising from Rs.1,160.56 in 2025 to Rs.1,170.20 in 2026. In contrast, the average in US dollar terms declined by US $ 0.25, from US $ 3.88 to US $ 3.63. This reflects an improvement in the average sale price in rupee terms, despite a decline in the corresponding US dollar value across all elevation categories.\n\nThe comparison of Sri Lanka\u2019s tea export performance for the period January to September 2026 against the corresponding period in 2025 indicates a decline in total export volume, alongside an increase in total export value in Sri Lankan rupee terms.\n\nTotal tea export volume decreased by 8.91 Mn/Kgs, from 198.33 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 189.41 Mn/Kgs in 2026.\n\nDespite this reduction, the total export value increased by approximately Rs.2.23 billion, rising from Rs.347.96 billion in 2025 to Rs.350.18 billion in 2026. Consequently, the average FOB price per kilogramme increased from Rs.1,754.46 to Rs.1,848.78, reflecting an improvement of Rs.94.32 per kilogramme.\n\nAmong the different export categories, tea in packets recorded the highest export volume at 84.55 Mn/Kgs, followed by tea in bulk at 79.16 Mn/Kgs."
