    "chunk_text": "Despite this reduction, the total export value increased by approximately Rs.2.23 billion, rising from Rs.347.96 billion in 2025 to Rs.350.18 billion in 2026. Consequently, the average FOB price per kilogramme increased from Rs.1,754.46 to Rs.1,848.78, reflecting an improvement of Rs.94.32 per kilogramme.\n\nAmong the different export categories, tea in packets recorded the highest export volume at 84.55 Mn/Kgs, followed by tea in bulk at 79.16 Mn/Kgs.\n\nTea in bags recorded a decline in export volume, decreasing from 19.67 Mn/Kgs in 2025 to 19.05 Mn/Kgs in 2026. However, its export value increased from Rs.54.81 billion to Rs.57.79 billion, while the average FOB price rose from Rs.2,786.73 to Rs.3,033.91 per kilogramme.\n\nInstant tea exports increased in volume from 2.54 Mn/Kgs to 3.12 Mn/Kgs, with the export value rising from Rs.9.26 billion to Rs.10.97 billion. However, its average FOB price declined from Rs.3,646.05 to Rs.3,512.32 per kilogramme. The green tea exports recorded a decrease in volume from 3.74 Mn/Kgs to 3.54 Mn/Kgs, while the export value increased from Rs.14.81 billion to Rs.15.38 billion. Its average FOB price also increased from Rs.3,959.64 to Rs.4,347.30 per kilogramme.\n\nIn US dollar terms, the approximate total export value declined from US $ 1.16 billion in the January to September 2025 period to US $ 1.08 billion in the corresponding period of 2026. The average FOB price in US dollar terms also decreased from US $ 5.87 per kilogramme to US $ 5.73 per kilogramme.\n\nOverall, Sri Lanka\u2019s tea exports during January to September 2026 generated higher earnings in rupee terms, despite a reduction in export volume. The increase in average FOB prices across most export categories, particularly tea in bags and green tea, contributed to the growth in the export earnings."
