    "chunk_text": "The comparative analysis of Sri Lanka\u2019s average FOB prices for the period January to September 2026 against the corresponding period in 2025 indicates an overall improvement in the export prices, with the FOB prices remaining higher in every month of 2026.\n\nThe year commenced with a strong increase in January, when the average FOB price rose from Rs.1,722.78 in 2025 to Rs.1,821.71 in 2026, reflecting a gain of Rs.98.93/kilogramme.\n\nDuring March, the average FOB price continued to improve, reaching Rs.1,813.84, an increase of Rs.60.68 over Rs.1,753.16 recorded in March 2025. In April, the prices remained relatively stable, rising marginally by Rs.0.64, from Rs.1,760.31 in 2025 to Rs.1,760.95 in 2026.\n\nIn May, the average FOB price increased by Rs.17.52, from Rs.1,783.18 in 2025 to Rs.1,800.70 in 2026.\n\nThe upward trend strengthened in June, with the FOB price rising to Rs.1,871.14, from Rs.1,714.08, representing an increase of Rs.157.06/kilogramme.\n\nThe positive trend continued during the third quarter. In July, the average FOB price increased to Rs.1,921.95, from Rs.1,766.51 in 2025, recording a gain of Rs.155.44. In August, the price rose to Rs.1,910.27, compared with Rs.1,805.35, an increase of Rs.104.92. September recorded the highest monthly FOB price of the period at Rs.1,928.62, compared with Rs.1,741.45 in 2025, reflecting the largest year-on-year increase of Rs.187.17.\n\nOverall, the January to September 2026 period demonstrated a consistently stronger FOB price performance compared with the corresponding period in 2025. The most significant year-on-year increases occurred in September, June and July, highlighting the strengthening of the average export prices during the latter part of the period. These higher FOB prices indicate improved export price realisation in Sri Lankan rupee terms."
