    "chunk_size_bytes": 1834,
    "chunk_text": "Overall, the January to September 2026 period demonstrated a consistently stronger FOB price performance compared with the corresponding period in 2025. The most significant year-on-year increases occurred in September, June and July, highlighting the strengthening of the average export prices during the latter part of the period. These higher FOB prices indicate improved export price realisation in Sri Lankan rupee terms.\n\nDuring the period under review, a total of 193.01 Mn/Kgs of tea was sold from January to September 2026, compared with 204.96 Mn/Kgs during the corresponding period in 2025, reflecting a decline of approximately 11.95 Mn/Kgs.\n\nOn a monthly basis, the average auction prices during the first quarter of 2026 remained below the corresponding levels in 2025, with the largest year-on-year decline recorded in March, when the average price decreased by approximately Rs.39.53/kilogramme.\n\nHowever, the prices recovered during the second quarter, with April, May and June recording year-on-year increases of Rs.12.95/kilogramme, Rs.33.24/kilogramme and Rs.50.22/kilogramme, respectively. This positive price trend continued into July, when the average increased by Rs.48.50/kilogramme compared with the corresponding month in 2025.\n\nAlthough August recorded a marginal decline of Rs.7.81/kilogramme, the prices improved again in September, reaching Rs.1,202.74/kilogramme, an increase of Rs.11.12/kilogramme year-on-year.\n\nThe contrasting movement between the lower auction volumes and improved average prices suggests that the relatively tighter tea supplies helped support the market prices despite the reduction in the quantities sold. The supply constraints, variations in production levels and sustained demand for Ceylon Tea may have contributed to the overall price resilience observed during the period."
