    "chunk_text": "The contrasting movement between the lower auction volumes and improved average prices suggests that the relatively tighter tea supplies helped support the market prices despite the reduction in the quantities sold. The supply constraints, variations in production levels and sustained demand for Ceylon Tea may have contributed to the overall price resilience observed during the period.\n\nThe comparison of the monthly average exchange rates for the period January to September 2026 against the corresponding period in 2025 indicates an initial depreciation of the Sri Lankan rupee against the US dollar, followed by a gradual recovery during the third quarter.\n\nThe average exchange rate increased from Rs.296.18/US dollar in January 2025 to Rs.309.65/US dollar in January 2026. Although the rate marginally declined to Rs.309.36/US dollar in February 2026, it resumed its upward trend in March, reaching Rs.311.91/US dollar and continued to increase steadily throughout the second quarter, reaching Rs.334.43/US dollar in June 2026, compared with Rs.299.90/US dollar in June 2025.\n\nThe exchange rate remained relatively stable during the first nine months of 2025, fluctuating within a narrow range of Rs.295.91 to Rs.302.20 per US dollar. In contrast, 2026 experienced considerable exchange rate volatility, with the Sri Lankan rupee weakening progressively during the first half of the year.\n\nThe year-on-year difference widened from Rs.13.47 in January to a peak of Rs.34.90 in July 2026, indicating increased depreciation pressure during the second quarter.\n\nHowever, the exchange rate recorded a gradual appreciation during the third quarter, declining from Rs.336.02/US dollar in July to Rs.332.91/US dollar in August and further to Rs.329.54/US dollar in September 2026. Despite this improvement, the exchange rate remained considerably higher than the corresponding 2025 levels, with the year-on-year difference standing at Rs.27.34 in September."
