    "md5": "591b5fcf30751bce4226fbcfbb7b5111",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Tokyo Cement profit falls 13% despite 28% sales jump\n\nThe Tokyo Cement group\u2019s profit fell 13 percent in the September quarter, as the higher raw material costs and currency volatility squeezed the margins, even as the rising cement demand and price increase lifted the sales.\n\nThe group profit after tax fell to Rs.870 million in the three months ended September 30, from Rs.1.003 billion a year earlier. Revenue rose 28 percent to Rs.20.94 billion, from Rs.16.32 billion, while the net profit margin narrowed to 4.2 percent, from 6.1 percent.\n\nTokyo Cement attributed the rise in revenue to the higher sales volumes and price increase from the preceding quarter. The group said it gained the market share as the coastal shipping improved its access to the customers in the Western province and beyond, helping it make a greater use of its expanded production capacity.\n\nAcross the industry, the cement supply remained uninterrupted and the market prices held steady during the quarter, despite the rising raw material, freight, insurance and fuel costs, the company said.\n\nThe cement consumption increased, helped by the favourable weather and availability of construction materials.\n\nThe public development projects generated new orders, supporting the continued expansion in the Construction Purchasing Managers\u2019 Index. The Middle East supply disruptions, however, constrained the supplies of bitumen used in roadbuilding.\n\nTokyo Cement said the rupee depreciation and higher global costs pushed up the prices for the locally produced and imported inputs, making the private developers and individual investors more cautious and threatening to slow the public projects.\n\nThe group said the volatility in the global fuel prices and risk of diesel supply disruptions dented the consumer confidence during the quarter. The higher energy costs, a global fertiliser shortage and the potential impact of severe El Ni\u00f1o conditions on agriculture added to the uncertainty."
  },
