    "chunk_size_bytes": 1220,
    "chunk_text": "The group said the volatility in the global fuel prices and risk of diesel supply disruptions dented the consumer confidence during the quarter. The higher energy costs, a global fertiliser shortage and the potential impact of severe El Ni\u00f1o conditions on agriculture added to the uncertainty.\n\nThe company pointed to the rising gross official reserves and continued foreign exchange inflows from the exports and worker remittances. The tourism earnings showed signs of improving ahead of the peak season, although the recovery was slower than expected.\n\nFitch Ratings\u2019 upgrade of Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating to \u2018B-\u2019, with a stable outlook, could help attract a wider range of foreign currency inflows, Tokyo Cement said.\n\nThe group maintained a conservative short to medium-term outlook, citing uncertainty over the Middle East conflict, energy prices and supply chains, alongside the expectations of the slower private-sector credit growth in the coming quarters.\n\nIt said the regional infrastructure projects already underway were supporting the construction activity but sustaining that momentum would depend on getting larger public investments off the ground and encouraging private-sector participation."
