    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "New direct rivals chip away at SriLankan\u2019s UK, Australian strongholds\n\nSriLankan\u2019s Australian share falls to 38.4%, from 51.3%, as Jetstar takes 19%\n\nUK share slips to 38.9%, with British Airways flying for just 8 days\n\nSriLankan Airlines is losing its grip on two of its most important long-haul markets, with its share of tourists from Australia and the United Kingdom falling sharply in October, as the new direct competitors Jetstar and British Airways moved onto the routes the national carrier once flew nonstop on its own, as per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) data.\n\nSriLankan\u2019s share of Australian tourists dropped to 38.44 percent in October, from 51.25 percent a year earlier or roughly 3,270 passengers against 4,050, a 19 percent decline that came even as the Australian arrivals grew 7.7 percent to 8,509.\n\nJetstar, which launched thrice-weekly Melbourne-Colombo flights, with Boeing 787s on 25 August, carried 19.03 percent of the Australian arrivals or around 1,620 tourists and appeared as a named carrier in the SLTDA\u2019s airline breakdown for the first time.\n\nJetstar\u2019s footprint was, however, already visible a month earlier, when 18.78 percent of Australian tourists were recorded under \u201cother\u201d airlines, against just 1.78 percent in October 2025.\n\nSriLankan had pushed back a planned increase in its Melbourne frequency from seven to 10 weekly flights from August 02 to November 15, allowing Jetstar to enter the route without a capacity response from the incumbent. The national carrier has since lifted its Australia services to 14 weekly flights.\n\nIn the UK, Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest market, SriLankan\u2019s share slipped to 38.87 percent, from 42.55 percent, equal to about 4,820 passengers against 5,500. That 12.4 percent fall was three times steeper than the 4.1 percent dip in the UK arrivals to 12,402."
