    "chunk_text": "In the UK, Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest market, SriLankan\u2019s share slipped to 38.87 percent, from 42.55 percent, equal to about 4,820 passengers against 5,500. That 12.4 percent fall was three times steeper than the 4.1 percent dip in the UK arrivals to 12,402.\n\nThe thrice-weekly service operates only during the winter season and flies from Gatwick, while SriLankan serves Heathrow.\n\nAlthough the SLTDA does not yet list British Airways by name, the share of the UK tourists on carriers outside its named list jumped to 9.88 percent or roughly 1,225 passengers, from 1.93 percent a year earlier. The full impact of a complete month of operations is likely to surface in November\u2019s data.\n\nSriLankan\u2019s share of French tourists also fell to 14.82 percent, from 17.57 percent, as Qatar Airways and Turkish Airlines gained ground.\n\nHowever, the national carrier strengthened its hold on Bangladesh, lifting its share to 46.20 percent, from 32.84 percent and edged up in Germany, the United States and China.\n\nThe low-cost long-haul carrier French Bee will add to the pressure in France when it launches flights from Paris Orly on December 19, challenging SriLankan, currently the only nonstop operator between Paris and Colombo.\n\nFrench Bee will initially operate two weekly triangular rotations, flying Paris-Mal\u00e9-Colombo-Paris, before adding dedicated direct Paris-Colombo services later in the season. As it lacks fifth freedom rights, the airline cannot sell the Mal\u00e9-Colombo leg on its own, so the travellers must pair it with a Paris sector. The seasonal schedule runs until May 2 on Airbus A350-900s carrying 411 seats each, with introductory return fares to Colombo starting at \u20ac599."
