    "chunk_text": "French Bee will initially operate two weekly triangular rotations, flying Paris-Mal\u00e9-Colombo-Paris, before adding dedicated direct Paris-Colombo services later in the season. As it lacks fifth freedom rights, the airline cannot sell the Mal\u00e9-Colombo leg on its own, so the travellers must pair it with a Paris sector. The seasonal schedule runs until May 2 on Airbus A350-900s carrying 411 seats each, with introductory return fares to Colombo starting at \u20ac599.\n\nYet, IndiGo captured the bigger slice of that growth, flying about 6,560 more Indian tourists than a year earlier or 46.5 percent of the 14,098 additional arrivals, compared with 35.4 percent for SriLankan. Air India\u2019s share shrank to 11.40 percent, from 13.76 percent.\n\nIndiGo, which resumed daily Delhi and Bengaluru services on October 1, lifted its overall share of arrivals to 17.66 percent, from 15.05 percent in September.\n\nWith a fleet of 23 aircraft, the national carrier has already requested two more A330 wide-bodies, SriLankan Airlines Acting CEO Vipul Misra said in October, adding that the fleet is due for replacement around 2031-32, as its aircraft types are no longer manufactured.\n\nMisra has also flagged Perth, China and a revived Africa route, all of which would require aircraft sourced from outside.\n\nAs per the Finance Ministry figures, the airline posted a net loss of Rs.8.9 billion in the five months to August, despite a 35 percent rise in revenue to Rs.154.6 billion, weighed down by a 78.2 percent surge in aircraft fuel costs and Rs.11.9 billion in foreign exchange losses.\n\nThe government has extended US $ 30 million, about Rs.10 billion, in short-term support, following a Rs.25.2 billion capital infusion in December 2025.\n\nDeputy Aviation Minister Ruwan Kodithuwakku told Parliament last week that the carrier could break even or post a small profit by end-2026, stressing an island nation\u2019s dependence on air connectivity."
