    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "Inflation climbs to 8.5% in October on higher transport costs\n\nAnnual inflation picks up after holding at 8.1% for 2 months\n\nNon-food inflation reaches 9.5%, with petrol and bus fares driving transport costs\n\nFood inflation rises to 7.2%, while monthly increase in overall prices quickens to 0.41%\n\nThe nationwide inflation accelerated to 8.5 percent in October, its highest reading this year, with the higher petrol prices and bus fares driving up the transport costs and households paying more for vegetables and milk powder, the official data showed.\n\nThe annual inflation had held at 8.1 percent in August and September, after climbing from 1.6 percent in February. The October rate was more than three times the 2.7 percent recorded a year earlier, according to the Census and Statistics Department.\n\nThe non-food inflation rose to 9.5 percent, from 9.1 percent in September and accounted for more than three-fifths of the annual increase in consumer prices. Transport was the largest single contributor, adding 2.45 percentage points to headline inflation. Petrol alone added 1.21 points, followed by the bus fares at 0.36 points, diesel at 0.20 points and school and preschool transport at 0.19 points.\n\nHousing and utilities were the next-largest non-food contributor at 1.17 percentage points, reflecting higher rents, maintenance costs, cooking gas prices and electricity bills.\n\nRestaurants and hotels added 0.52 points, while education contributed 0.39 points, mostly due to the higher tuition fees.\n\nThe health costs also rose, with the fees at private medical practices accounting for the largest share of that category\u2019s contribution.\n\nThe food inflation rose to 7.2 percent, from 6.9 percent in September, more than double the 3.3 percent recorded in June. Vegetables made the largest contribution to the annual rise in food prices, followed by fresh fish, dried fish and big onions."
  },
