    "chunk_text": "The food inflation rose to 7.2 percent, from 6.9 percent in September, more than double the 3.3 percent recorded in June. Vegetables made the largest contribution to the annual rise in food prices, followed by fresh fish, dried fish and big onions.\n\nThe monthly figures showed a reversal in the transport costs. The consumer prices rose 0.41 percent from September, up from 0.11 percent the previous month.\n\nTransport added 0.19 percentage points to the October increase after subtracting 0.15 points in September. Petrol accounted for most of the turnaround, while the vehicle servicing charges and three-wheeler fares also rose. The airfares fell over the month.\n\nThe non-food items contributed 0.33 percentage points to the monthly increase, with the tuition fees and restaurant and hotel prices adding to the pressure from transport. Food contributed 0.08 points as higher prices for vegetables, limes, milk powder and coconuts outweighed the declines in fresh fish, green chillies, fruit, eggs, rice and chicken.\n\nThe National Consumer Price Index rose to 225.1, from 224.2 in September. The cost of the 485-item basket used to calculate the index increased by Rs.466 from September and Rs.8,951 from a year earlier. The 12-month average inflation climbed to 5.0 percent, from 4.5 percent."
