    "md5": "2d780d7504dea5f02ea9a91c1681e9b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1756,
    "chunk_text": "Orange the World Dutch ambassador urges  men  to join  fight on violence against women\n\nAmbassador of the Netherlands Wiebe de Boer responds to questions from the Daily Mirror on \u2018Orange the World\u2019 programme, a Dutch initiative to create global awareness on gender-based violence\n\nThe Netherlands urges stronger law enforcement and greater public awareness to tackle gender-based violence, including online harassment and AI-generated abuse\n\nCalls for men to challenge harmful gender norms, while highlighting the need for better protection and support for survivors\n\nQ In what ways has the annual Orange the World campaign produced tangible results?\n\nOrange the World is a 16- day long world-wide campaign to prevent Gender Based Violence. Every year it starts on November 25, the International Day for the Elimination of Violence against Women, and runs until 10 December, the International Human Rights Day.\n\nThe objective of Orange the World is to raise awareness, foster support and build networks to prevent women and girls being subjected to harassment and violence. I think Orange the World campaign has contributed a lot to making Gender-Based Violence a global movement that is instrumental in pushing policy and legal changes.\n\nIts tangible results are mostly indirect - it creates political pressure, funding, and space for local groups to act. Also, freedom to address the problem without stigma is crucial.\n\nAll this has produced, around the world, better laws to protect women and better implementation of these laws. Is it enough? Certainly not so. Therefore, we need everybody to participate in this campaign. Especially the role of men is crucial.\n\nQ What are the biggest weaknesses in Sri Lanka\u2019s response to gender-based violence?"
