    "md5": "4282262b650214d7659e1f39db1c5c0b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "All this has produced, around the world, better laws to protect women and better implementation of these laws. Is it enough? Certainly not so. Therefore, we need everybody to participate in this campaign. Especially the role of men is crucial.\n\nQ What are the biggest weaknesses in Sri Lanka\u2019s response to gender-based violence?\n\nQ Does Sri Lanka have adequate laws to protect women, or is the real problem weak enforcement?\n\nLike in most countries, including mine, laws and enforcement are both not optimal. As I mentioned, Sri Lanka has some good laws, such as the Prevention of Domestic Violence Act, but implementation is hard for victims and the police or other agencies involved in countering Gender-Based Violence. The National Commission on Women, which was established recently under the Women Empowerment Act, also has powers to monitor Gender-Based Violence.\n\nWhile some of these have limitations in comprehensively addressing GBV, some other laws, such as personal laws in Sri Lanka, also sometimes pose challenges in countering GBV. There is a tendency among law enforcement officers to still consider domestic violence as family issues rather than a crime. It is easy to look the other way. Police, like everywhere, face capacity challenges.\n\nImplementation of laws and safeguards is always difficult. That is why it is so important to work on the prevention of GBV by changing mindsets in society.\n\nQWhat lessons can the Netherlands offer to Sri Lanka for the prevention of violence against women?\n\nI am not sure whether the Netherlands is a perfect model in this case, as femicide takes place in my country every eight days. And 41% of women over the age of 15 have experienced physical or sexual violence. This is above the EU average of 31%."
