    "md5": "d2f10c1410bea656b518c2af36a70e4c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1540,
    "chunk_text": "I am not sure whether the Netherlands is a perfect model in this case, as femicide takes place in my country every eight days. And 41% of women over the age of 15 have experienced physical or sexual violence. This is above the EU average of 31%.\n\nInstead of victims going to separate and different places seeking help and justice, the system \u201cSafe at Home\u201d provides one national contact number available around the clock to help victims and those who are at risk, with regional centres offering assistance. Anyone- victim, neighbour, teacher, or doctor- can call and \u201cSafe at Home\u201d does risk assessment, safety planning, and referral.\n\nMayors of cities in the Netherlands have power and can also issue restraining orders temporarily removing the perpetrator from their home if there is an imminent danger to the victim. Sri Lankan law requires a woman to go to court to get a protection Order, which takes time, money, and courage. The Netherlands also has a five-step code for professionals to follow regarding domestic violence and child abuse, making violence not seen as a matter. It works, but it is not enough.\n\nThe Netherlands is a strong advocate for international collaboration on eliminating violence against women and girls. Combatting violence against women is a priority in the Dutch foreign policy and development cooperation. Through diplomacy and collaboration with civil society organisations, the Netherlands contributes to the prevention of violence against all women and girls and supports survivors of violence."
