    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5137a5c9afe638412111776997287d11",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "The Netherlands is a strong advocate for international collaboration on eliminating violence against women and girls. Combatting violence against women is a priority in the Dutch foreign policy and development cooperation. Through diplomacy and collaboration with civil society organisations, the Netherlands contributes to the prevention of violence against all women and girls and supports survivors of violence.\n\nThis has added more pressure to states in countering GBV in digital platforms. The EU wants to target the platforms to take more responsibility.\n\nQ How should governments tackle the growing problem of online harassment and digital violence against women?\n\nCountering TFGBV requires more collective efforts rather than pressing only the authorities to act. I am not saying it\u2019s less value; indeed it\u2019s the key, yet other stakeholders also need to get together in tackling online harassments against women and girls. This is one of the areas our embassy has focused on more recently. We provided a comprehensive training, possibly for the first time in Sri Lanka to women journalists.\n\nThe training was \u2018Enhancing understanding and knowledge of AI among women in the media\u2019, where women journalists were empowered with necessary knowledge and skills to counter weaponising AI against women and to get the best out of AI for their daily work. We are also implementing a project countering hate speech and empowering women to counter GBV in the North. Similarly, I think digital literacy and AI literacy needs to be widened, necessary laws needs to be updated and more importantly a robust implementation of rules and regulations are keys to counter TFGBV.\n\nQ As a male advocating women\u2019s rights, how do you see men play their role in challenging patriarchal attitudes and preventing violence against women?"
