    "md5": "35f485c40079d834fc7f8b5b6627eb6b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "The training was \u2018Enhancing understanding and knowledge of AI among women in the media\u2019, where women journalists were empowered with necessary knowledge and skills to counter weaponising AI against women and to get the best out of AI for their daily work. We are also implementing a project countering hate speech and empowering women to counter GBV in the North. Similarly, I think digital literacy and AI literacy needs to be widened, necessary laws needs to be updated and more importantly a robust implementation of rules and regulations are keys to counter TFGBV.\n\nQ As a male advocating women\u2019s rights, how do you see men play their role in challenging patriarchal attitudes and preventing violence against women?\n\nQ Is the Netherlands prepared to expand its support for Sri Lankan organisations working to protect women\u2019s rights?\n\nOne of our three priorities in Sri Lanka is human rights. Under Dutch Foreign policy, human rights is a priority for us. In Sri Lanka, we have three human rights priorities: equality for women and girls, equal rights for LGBTIQ+ community and freedom of expression. We have been doing several projects in different parts of the country to ensure gender equality and women\u2019s empowerment. We currently have ongoing projects specifically addressing GBV in specific districts as well as more generally across the country.\n\nWe recently concluded some projects addressing female genital mutilation, and are continuing programmes on legal and counselling support to victims of GBV, providing digital skills, vocational skill support to women in tourism, and political empowerment of women. We also work with the National Commission on Women. We look at supporting making more structural changes to make a real change. One example is the project we are currently implementing with the Family Planning Association of Sri Lanka to Integrate Gender Based Violence (GBV) Screening in Antenatal Care in Sri Lanka."
