    "md5": "06f47e23d6fdd000d942e896973e1fcc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "Hambantota Port provides refuge to disabled cargo vessel\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Hambantota International Port (HIP) has provided refuge to a disabled cargo vessel after flooding in its engine room left it unable to operate, with the ship now safely berthed at the port for repairs.\n\nThe Cyprus-flagged general cargo vessel MV AAL Gibraltar was towed to Sri Lanka by salvage tug SPM Arjun after being declared a dead ship. Its departure date remains unconfirmed.\n\nThe 159.99-metre vessel, which is reportedly carrying approximately 23,000 tonnes of cargo, is currently at a lay-up berth, where its local agent, Ceyline Maritime Services (Pvt) Ltd, will coordinate the necessary repairs.\n\nFollowing a no-objection from the Director General of Merchant Shipping and recommendations from the Harbour Master and the Marine Environment Protection Authority (MEPA), HIP deployed four tugs \u2013 Waruna, Vasabha, Ravana and Pandukabhaya \u2013 to assist SPM Arjun in manoeuvring the disabled vessel from its anchorage to the berth.\n\nBefore the vessel was granted clearance to enter the port, HIP also coordinated three offshore assistance operations while it remained at sea.\n\nOn September 23, tug Ravana transported surveyors, technicians and provisions to the vessel, approximately 12 nautical miles offshore.\n\nOn October 13, tugs Pandukabhaya and Waruna provided assistance for around 14 hours while the towing tug refuelled. Later, on October 23, tug Waruna delivered spare parts to the vessel approximately 12 nautical miles from the port.\n\nThe berthing operation has provided the vessel with shelter and access to shore-based support following several weeks offshore.\n\nHIP said its marine services include afloat repairs, underwater services, surveys, provisions, bunker supplies and lay-up facilities, subject to availability.\n\nThe port also maintains firefighting and rescue tugs on round-the-clock standby, supported by a dedicated Emergency Response Unit and equipment to handle oil and chemical spills."
