    "md5": "e48ee92d69873639d2729765a646ecbe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1810,
    "chunk_text": "The jury also acquitted Huawei of conspiring to obstruct justice, according to Reuters. The trial lasted more than two months, with prosecutors calling over 60 witnesses and the defence presenting 15 witnesses.\n\nJurors also watched footage that prosecutors said showed a Huawei employee taking part of a T-Mobile testing robot and placing it in a backpack. Another allegation involved a Huawei engineer photographing a device at a Fujitsu trade show booth.\n\nUS District Judge Ann Donnelly is scheduled to sentence Huawei on September 14, 2027. The penalties the company could face were not immediately clear, Reuters reported.\n\nHuawei has rejected the charges on which it was convicted, calling them without \u201clegal or factual merit\u201d. The company said it would review the verdicts and take necessary legal action to defend its rights and interests.\n\n\u201cThe five trade secret-related disputes cited by the government were all civil matters that were resolved over the past two decades. No court ever found Huawei liable for willful intellectual property misappropriation, and the company was never ordered to pay damages for trade secret misappropriation,\u201d Huawei said in a statement.\n\nHuawei also welcomed its acquittal in the racketeering case, saying the decision confirmed that the allegations lacked a factual basis and vindicated its position.\n\nHuawei lawyer Brian Heberlig said the company was disappointed by the convictions but maintained that its acquittal on the racketeering conspiracy charge vindicated its \u201cgood-faith compliance culture\u201d.\n\n\u201cWe fully expect to appeal,\u201d Heberlig told reporters, according to Reuters. He also said it was too early to comment on the potential financial penalties, adding that the acquittal on the racketeering charge could make a significant difference."
