    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe fully expect to appeal,\u201d Heberlig told reporters, according to Reuters. He also said it was too early to comment on the potential financial penalties, adding that the acquittal on the racketeering charge could make a significant difference.\n\nThe case drew international attention in December 2018, when Huawei CFO Meng Wanzhou, the daughter of the company's founder, was detained in Canada on a US warrant. She was accused of misleading an HSBC banker about Huawei's Iran business. After nearly three years of fighting extradition, Meng entered a deferred prosecution agreement and returned to China; the charges against her were later dropped. Her detention triggered a diplomatic crisis involving the US, China and Canada.\n\nDuring the trial, Huawei's defence argued that HSBC and other banks knew about the company's Iran business and had continued competing for its business. Huawei lawyer Brian Heberlig also accused prosecutors of using separate incidents to build their case for a two-decade conspiracy, Reuters reported."
