    "description": "Making Sri Lanka Water Positive by 2030",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Making-Sri-Lanka-Water-Positive-by-2030/108-355727",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795606534.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-25-dailymirrorlk-ac234115-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "870680686502c52c0de998ae94132a22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "Making Sri Lanka Water Positive by 2030\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0More than two thousand years ago, Sri Lanka built one of the world\u2019s most sophisticated water civilisations. Its kings and communities created vast networks of reservoirs, canals and village tank cascades that caught the monsoon, recharged the ground and fed the rice fields of the dry zone. Each tank fed the next, so water was used, returned and reused across an entire landscape.\n\nKing Parakramabahu the Great captured this spirit in a resolve that is still quoted today: no drop of rain should reach the sea without first serving the people. Long before the phrase existed, Sri Lanka was practising what the world now calls water stewardship.\n\nThat legacy is more than history. It is proof that a nation can live in balance with its water, and a foundation on which Sri Lanka can build a water-positive future. Today\u2019s challenge\n\nThe same island now faces a very different water story. A changing climate brings longer droughts in the dry zone and more intense floods elsewhere, often in the same year. Many ancient tanks have silted up or fallen out of use. Growing towns release untreated wastewater into rivers, lagoons and the ground. Water quality concerns in parts of the dry zone, and the rising cost of supplying safe water, add urgency.\n\nSri Lanka does not lack rain or skill. What it needs is a way to bring its heritage, modern science and new sources of finance together, around one clear national goal. What \u201cWater Positive\u201d means\n\nTo be Water Positive is to return more water to nature and to people than is taken. For a nation, it rests on three tests:\n\nQuantity: more water recharged, reused and restored than is consumed, basin by basin.\n\nQuality: water returned clean enough to sustain people, farms and ecosystems."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-25-dailymirrorlk-ac234115",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-ac234115",
    "date_str": "2026-11-25",
    "description": "Making Sri Lanka Water Positive by 2030",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Making-Sri-Lanka-Water-Positive-by-2030/108-355727",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795606534.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-25-dailymirrorlk-ac234115-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a5bb7e581adfbbb86d517e4e6ecfe587",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "To be Water Positive is to return more water to nature and to people than is taken. For a nation, it rests on three tests:\n\nQuantity: more water recharged, reused and restored than is consumed, basin by basin.\n\nQuality: water returned clean enough to sustain people, farms and ecosystems.\n\nA national mission of this scale needs a clear structure. I propose five pillars, each led by the Government of Sri Lanka around its own priorities, with partners in support.\n\nRevive the cascades. Restore village tanks and cascade systems so they once again capture the monsoon, recharge groundwater and sustain farming communities. This is heritage put back to work.\n\nRecharge the aquifers. Use managed aquifer recharge, rainwater harvesting and watershed restoration to rebuild groundwater in the most stressed districts.\n\nReuse every drop of wastewater. Treat sewage close to where it is produced, using decentralised, nature-based systems, and return clean water to irrigation, recharge and urban greening. Treated wastewater is a reliable source that does not depend on the rain.\n\nMeasure, verify and record. Track every project against a common standard, with metering at source, satellite checks and independent verification, so that progress is real, comparable and trusted.\n\nFinance what works. Bring impact funds, ESG funds, corporate water stewardship, development banks, climate funds and blended finance behind verified outcomes, so that money flows to the projects that deliver.\n\nThe last two pillars make the first three bankable. They are where the ecosystem we have built at AquaKraft, and the Water Parliament, can make the greatest contribution. A complete ecosystem for water positivity\n\nOver the years, my colleagues and I have built an ecosystem that offers Sri Lanka a single chain, from the project on the ground to the verified result recorded for the world."
  },
