    "md5": "332e46d16c7dcd39efe1c2c492cf1488",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "For Sri Lanka, this means a partner that brings not a single technology, but an integrated system, and my lifelong commitment to water sustainability. The Water Parliament: a home for every conscious stakeholder\n\nNo nation can become water positive alone, and no single company, bank or university can make it happen. Water positivity is a shared ambition, and it needs a shared platform.\n\nThe Water Parliament brings together everyone who aspires to be water positive:\n\nCompanies pursuing water stewardship commitments across their operations and supply chains;\n\nCivil society and communities who live with water challenges every day.\n\nIt holds no treaty or regulatory mandate, and acts in any country only with the consent of its national authority. Its role is to aggregate, align and amplify: to give these stakeholders one common language, a trusted record of progress, and a forum for dialogue.\n\nThe Water Parliament, in association with the Centre for Social Value Creation and Khalifa University, is leading an open consultation on its Unified Water Protocol, inviting views from all stakeholders.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, joining as one of the Water Parliament\u2019s early member countries would mean a seat at the centre of this movement, and a global stage on which to announce its water-positive ambition. I would be delighted to welcome Sri Lanka in that role.\n\nWe propose that the journey to 2030 follow five clear steps, each building on the last:\n\nAnnouncement. A national announcement of the Water Positive Sri Lanka mission in December 2026, setting the goal, the common standard and the institutions that will lead it.\n\nPilots funded by the private sector. In 2027, the first pilots in priority districts, funded by the private sector, so that the approach is proven on the ground with a measured baseline before public funds are committed at scale."
