    "md5": "df2cfbfd7caea98e35c30e836aa34e4d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Then there is the elderly English gentleman who was advised that he could run his guest house and employ foreign nationals without SLTDA approval. And so the list goes on.\n\nBut first, let us focus on why they are here, in this beautiful country.\n\nNow, in your mind's eye, picture \"Frank\". Frank is German. As you read this, he is sitting at his laptop in a cafe on Hiriketiya Bay, answering emails and meeting deadlines. As he peers over the top of his screen, shielding his eyes from the glare of the sun, he can see swimmers, surfers, and those simply trying to remain on their boogie boards dotting the water.\n\nHe is not one of the two million tourists who visited Sri Lanka this year. He may well have been at one point, but he is no longer a tourist. Like thousands of other foreign nationals who have settled in Sri Lanka, he lives here.\n\nEvery foreign resident has a story about moving here: the anxiety of making such a life-changing decision, the anticipation of a new life and new experiences, and, in many cases, the angst of navigating visa applications. Too often, that process is placed in the hands of agents more invested in obtaining a visa at any cost than in securing the correct visa through the proper channels.\n\nThe reality is that a legitimate stay in Sri Lanka, and the peace of mind that comes with it, depend on following the correct procedures. These procedures are designed to protect Sri Lanka's interests as well as those of its foreign residents. Any agent who fails to explain, for example, that employment visas require recommendations from the relevant line ministry, that tourism-related businesses employing foreign nationals should be registered with the SLTDA, which bank accounts are required for an investor visa, or the difference between an IIA account and a VPFCA account, undermines this framework and compromises the stability of the foreign national's stay in the country."
  },
