    "chunk_text": "Solomon Islands signs security deal with Australia\n\nALJAZEERA - Solomon Islands and Australia have signed a sweeping treaty affirming that the small Pacific country will allow no foreign military bases on its territory.\n\nThe deal, signed on Wednesday, is seen as a blow to the regional ambitions of China, which had been moving closer to the Solomon Islands for years.\n\n\u201cSolomon Islands has been for a number of years a source of uncertainty and doubt in the region. Those days are gone,\u201d Prime Minister Matthew Wale told journalists alongside his Australian counterpart Anthony Albanese.\n\nThe treaty establishes Australia as the island nation\u2019s \u201cprimary security partner\u201d and will see Canberra provide 740 million Australian dollars ($490m) over the next 10 years to fund free education and other programmes in Solomon Islands. Solomon Islands has a gross domestic product (GDP) of $1.75bn, according to the World Bank.\n\nIn exchange, Solomon Islands will allow no foreign military or security bases on its soil, and the country\u2019s \u201csensitive\u201d infrastructure will \u201cremain free from militarisation, foreign interference and unauthorised access\u201d.\n\nThat includes ports, airports, energy and telecommunications, as well as road and bridge construction \u2013 areas China has dominated in the islands through its Belt and Road Initiative. The deal will not apply retroactively.\n\nSolomon Islands will also have to consult Australia before making deals involving its critical infrastructure or security cooperation unless it is with members of the Pacific Islands Forum regional bloc, Albanese said.\n\nSince being elected in May, Wale has upended Solomon Islands\u2019 diplomatic moves towards China and realigned with Australia and the United States.\n\nAsked about reports he had ended a secretive security pact with Beijing, Wale told journalists that discussions with China were \u201congoing\u201d."
