    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Israel\u2019s Eisenkot secures first coalition partner in bid to form government\n\nALJAZEERA - The party of Israeli opposition leader Gadi Eisenkot has announced an agreement to unite with a right-wing party in a bid to form the next government following October\u2019s elections.\n\nThe centrist Yashar party said on Tuesday that it agreed to sign a coalition pact with Israel Our Home party after a meeting with its leader Avigdor Lieberman, the first alliance of its kind since the election.\n\nAfter no bloc managed to secure a 61-seat majority, Israel\u2019s parliament was sworn in on November 10 without a new government.\n\nPresident Isaac Herzog tasked Eisenkot with forming \u2060a government, while Likud party leader Benjamin Netanyahu remains caretaker prime minister despite the October 27 election stripping him of his majority.\n\nEisenkot has until December 6 to form a government under a 28-day mandate, though he could ask for a 14-day extension. If Eisenkot fails, Netanyahu would have an opportunity to do so. If no one is able to form a government, another election will be held next year.\n\nThe right-wing Likud\u2019s alliance with far-right and ultra-Orthodox parties holds 53 of the Knesset\u2019s 120 seats.\n\nEisenkot\u2019s centre-right Yashar party and patchwork coalition partners, including Lieberman, hold 48 seats. But they have potential allies in the parties representing Israel\u2019s Palestinian minority, which hold 19 seats and \u2060are aligned with Eisenkot in their vehement opposition to Netanyahu.\n\nLieberman had once served as foreign minister under a previous Netanyahu government but has since become a strong critic.\n\nTalks are also ongoing between Eisenkot, some of his \u201cchange bloc\u201d partners and Likud on forming a unity government.\n\nThe left-wing Democrats party, which won eight parliament seats, separately said it was suspending talks with Eisenkot after the Yashar party leader\u2019s talks with Netanyahu\u2019s Likud on a possible unity government."
