    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "US announces new tariffs of up to 20% on China, the EU, and 12 other nations\n\nyahoo finance - The Trump administration on Wednesday proposed new tariffs of up to 20% on China, the European Union, and 12 other major US trading partners to take effect next year, following an 8-month investigation into excess manufacturing capacity.\n\nThe duties could be a shock to the global economy, with China a main target.\n\nThe proposed 20% tariff on many Chinese goods was described by the Trump administration as a \"deal rate\" that could go higher depending on how ongoing talks progress. The new tariff would be stacked on top of at least some preexisting tariffs, resulting in a total rate over 40% on some goods.\n\nThe new tariffs won't go into effect until after additional hearings and a public comment period and \"probably will be in January at the earliest,\" according to a US official who briefed reporters ahead of the announcement.\n\nAn existing trade truce with China is set to expire on Jan. 10.\n\nSeveral other Asian nations are also in line for higher duties, with a new 20% tariff proposed for Vietnam. Cambodia, Indonesia, and Malaysia are in line for 18% rates. India would have a 17% rate but only, the administration said, if ongoing talks produce a deal.\n\nThe US official said the rates could go even higher depending on how the countries respond, and added that the investigation had concluded that a new 40% tariff on China and a 35% rate on Vietnam was \"warranted,\" even as a lower rate is being proposed for now due to ongoing talks\n\nOn Wednesday, the White House listed an array of \"Proposed Rates\" that were higher across the board, ranging from 20% to 40%.\n\nThe effects elsewhere could be more muted, with new duties designed not to overstep rates negotiated in recent trade deals. That meant trading partners, including the European Union, South Korea, and Japan would all see tariff rate of 15%, which is in line with what they negotiated with the Trump administration in recent months."
