    "chunk_text": "The effects elsewhere could be more muted, with new duties designed not to overstep rates negotiated in recent trade deals. That meant trading partners, including the European Union, South Korea, and Japan would all see tariff rate of 15%, which is in line with what they negotiated with the Trump administration in recent months.\n\nThe investigation was conducted under the authority of Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974 on Bangladesh, Cambodia, China, the European Union, India, Indonesia, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, Singapore, Switzerland, Taiwan, Thailand, and Vietnam.\n\nNorway and Mexico were also under investigation. Those findings are still being reviewed.\n\nThe new duties are formally aimed at what the Trump administration charges are illegal subsidies by foreign nations to create excess manufacturing capacity that the US found \"to be unreasonable and burden or restrict US commerce.\"\n\n\"This is not a new issue, unfortunately,\" the US official said. \"It's created severe imbalances globally, and it's harmed US commerce in particular.\"\n\nThe duties are also meant to replace tariffs struck down by the US Supreme Court in February.\n\nIn some cases, the new tariffs will supplant a previous set of tariffs that went into effect in July of 10% to 12.5% on 60 of the top US trade partners over the issue of forced labor.\n\nThe excess capacity duties, which are higher, will supplant the forced labor tariffs in nations that were investigated for both issues.\n\nThe Yale Budget Lab and other trade trackers didn't offer an immediate read on how the new tariffs would affect overall tariff levels, but the group calculated earlier this month that the effective US tariff rate sits at about 11.3% across all trading partners. That same measure was 13.86% just before the Supreme Court struck down Trump's blanket tariffs in February and was above 15% for much of 2025."
