    "md5": "5014e848f1f1536cd1859d7f638fcc9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s children under threat: NCPA\n\nColombo, Nov. 25 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka has recorded more than 9,600 complaints involving child abuse and other child-related issues in just nine months, exposing the disturbing scale of threats faced by children across the country.\n\nThe National Child Protection Authority (NCPA) received 9,613 complaints between January 1 and September 30 this year, including 51 teenage pregnancies, 169 cases of child rape and 114 cases of grave sexual abuse.\n\nThe alarming figures also include 364 complaints of sexual harassment and 196 cases of cyberbullying involving threats using nude photographs, raising serious concerns about the safety of children both at home and online.\n\nMeanwhile, nine child marriages were reported during the period, while 1,889 complaints of cruelty against children and 2,387 complaints involving children in need of care and protection were recorded.\n\nChairman of the Medical and Civil Rights Doctors' Trade Union Alliance, Dr. Chamal Sanjeewa, said the latest NCPA statistics revealed an alarming situation that required urgent national intervention.\n\nCalling for a comprehensive national child protection policy, he stressed that children under 18 needed stronger institutional and legal protection.\n\nHe said Colombo, Gampaha, Kurunegala, Ratnapura, Anuradhapura and Galle were among the districts reporting comparatively high numbers of child abuse incidents.\n\nDr. Sanjeewa warned that the actual number of incidents could be several times higher than the reported figures, as many cases remained hidden due to fear, social stigma and other barriers preventing victims from seeking help.\n\nHe also pointed out that the NCPA had received 9,613 complaints by the end of September this year, highlighting the urgent need for stronger measures to protect children.\n\nThe figures show that child abuse is not limited to sexual offences. There were also 153 complaints of child labour and 1,592 complaints relating to compulsory education."
  },
