    "md5": "300561b068f1ad117324f7ef9ebce387",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "He also pointed out that the NCPA had received 9,613 complaints by the end of September this year, highlighting the urgent need for stronger measures to protect children.\n\nThe figures show that child abuse is not limited to sexual offences. There were also 153 complaints of child labour and 1,592 complaints relating to compulsory education.\n\nThe statistics under the miscellaneous category paint an equally worrying picture. They include 719 requests for counselling and psychosocial support, 99 drug abuse complaints, 46 domestic violence complaints and 19 cases involving attempted suicide.\n\nThe situation has also remained concerning over the past two years.\n\nAccording to Dr. Sanjeewa, 53 teenage pregnancies were reported in 2024, along with 500 cases of sexual assault, three cases of child abortion, 42 cases of child rape and 321 cases of child sexual abuse.\n\nIn 2025, the NCPA recorded 46 teenage pregnancies, 545 cases of sexual assault and 98 cases of child sexual abuse, among other incidents.\n\nDr. Sanjeewa said urgent measures were needed to raise public awareness, expand school-based education programmes and improve sexual literacy among children and parents.\n\nHe also called for a stronger legal framework and rehabilitation programmes that would protect the privacy and dignity of victims while ensuring that affected children received the necessary support.\n\nSeptember recorded the highest number of complaints this year, with 1,554 cases, followed by July with 1,375 and June with 1,239.\n\nDistrict-wise figures show that Colombo recorded the highest number of complaints at 991, followed by Gampaha with 821 and Kurunegala with 623.\n\nHowever, the NCPA statistics contain a discrepancy. While the overall complaints table records 9,613 complaints, the district-wise table gives a total of 7,867. The main child abuse and related offences category alone records 7,622 complaints."
