    "md5": "44c24d88817b267cdd528748896d6cf6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1602,
    "chunk_text": "Dengue: A persistent national  threat demanding year-round action\n\nDengue has become an all-too-familiar part of life in Sri Lanka. Each year, particularly with the arrival of the monsoon rains, rising case numbers prompt renewed public concern, mosquito-control campaigns and calls for greater vigilance. Yet, as the immediate threat subsides, public attention often diminishes, only to return with the next outbreak.\n\nThis recurring pattern raises an important question: Have we begun to accept dengue as an inevitable part of life, rather than a preventable public health threat that demands sustained national action?\n\nFor decades, dengue has remained one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most persistent public health challenges. Despite considerable progress in disease surveillance, clinical management and vector control, it continues to impose a substantial burden on individuals, families, healthcare institutions and the national economy.\n\nThe magnitude of this challenge is evident in our epidemiological experience. In 2017, Sri Lanka experienced its largest recorded dengue epidemic, with 186,101 suspected cases and 440 reported deaths. Nearly a decade later, the country continues to face significant outbreaks, demonstrating that dengue remains far from being brought under sustainable control.\n\nThe situation in 2026 is particularly concerning. According to surveillance figures reported in November, Sri Lanka had recorded 110,431 dengue cases and 82 deaths by November 2026. The Western Province continued to bear a disproportionate burden, while transmission remained a concern across the country."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-3dc37480",
    "num": "dailyftlk-3dc37480",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "Dengue: A persistent national  threat demanding year-round action",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Dengue-A-persistent-national-threat-demanding-year-round-action/4-800588",
