    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795645036.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-3dc37480-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6bb95d839ffd18dbba67512b8b71054a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "The situation in 2026 is particularly concerning. According to surveillance figures reported in November, Sri Lanka had recorded 110,431 dengue cases and 82 deaths by November 2026. The Western Province continued to bear a disproportionate burden, while transmission remained a concern across the country.\n\nEarly recognition and timely medical intervention are therefore critical. Dengue commonly presents with fever, headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea and sometimes a rash. However, the most dangerous period may begin as the fever subsides, when some patients enter the critical phase of illness.\n\nWarning signs such as severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleeding, marked lethargy, restlessness or signs of circulatory compromise require urgent medical assessment. Patients with suspected dengue should seek timely medical advice, maintain appropriate hydration and avoid medications such as aspirin and ibuprofen because of the risk of bleeding. Early identification, careful monitoring and appropriate fluid management can substantially reduce the risk of death.\n\nImportantly, dengue is no longer a concern confined to Colombo or other densely populated urban centres. Although the Western Province accounts for a substantial proportion of cases, the disease affects communities across Sri Lanka. Rapid urbanisation, inadequate waste management, construction activities, population movement, rainfall patterns and environmental conditions all contribute to the persistence and spread of mosquito breeding sites.\n\nChanging weather patterns and increasing climatic variability further complicate dengue prevention. However, rainfall alone does not explain the problem. Poor environmental management, neglected premises, improperly stored water and inadequate community participation create conditions that allow mosquito populations to thrive."
  },
  {
