    "md5": "c67df0b506f5d8dc4e156b75f9028aeb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "Changing weather patterns and increasing climatic variability further complicate dengue prevention. However, rainfall alone does not explain the problem. Poor environmental management, neglected premises, improperly stored water and inadequate community participation create conditions that allow mosquito populations to thrive.\n\nSri Lanka has developed considerable expertise in dengue management, supported by dedicated healthcare professionals, public health teams and national surveillance systems. The National Dengue Control Unit and other health authorities have played an important role in coordinating prevention and response activities. Nevertheless, the continuing burden of disease demonstrates that healthcare-sector interventions alone cannot provide a lasting solution.\n\nEvery household should routinely inspect its surroundings and eliminate potential mosquito breeding sites. Water-collecting containers, discarded tyres, blocked gutters, flowerpots, construction materials and improperly managed waste require particular attention. Water-storage containers must be securely covered and regularly cleaned. Personal protection through appropriate clothing, mosquito repellents and measures to prevent mosquito bites during the day should also be encouraged.\n\nHowever, individual responsibility, while essential, is insufficient without supportive public systems.\n\nLocal authorities must ensure effective solid-waste management, drainage maintenance and environmental sanitation. Schools and workplaces should adopt regular, structured dengue-prevention programmes rather than limiting activities to periods of heightened transmission. Construction sites abandoned buildings and public spaces require systematic monitoring and accountability."
  },
  {
