    "language": "en",
    "md5": "04b314f0d215b7d46c6f63c69757e829",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "Local authorities must ensure effective solid-waste management, drainage maintenance and environmental sanitation. Schools and workplaces should adopt regular, structured dengue-prevention programmes rather than limiting activities to periods of heightened transmission. Construction sites abandoned buildings and public spaces require systematic monitoring and accountability.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s National Dengue Review in August 2026 reinforced the importance of evidence-based interventions, stronger operational coordination, sustained preparedness and active community participation. These priorities should now translate into measurable action at national, provincial, district and community levels.\n\nA more proactive approach is essential. Surveillance data, mosquito breeding indices, weather information and local epidemiological trends should be used to identify high-risk areas and guide targeted interventions before outbreaks escalate. Public communication must also move beyond occasional warnings towards sustained education that encourages practical behavioural change.\n\nUltimately, successful dengue control should not be measured solely by the number of emergency campaigns conducted or premises inspected during an outbreak. It should be judged by sustained reductions in transmission, preventable hospitalisations and deaths, supported by healthier and safer environments.\n\nSri Lanka has demonstrated its capacity to achieve remarkable public health outcomes through strong preventive services, community engagement and coordinated national programmes. The same commitment must now be directed towards dengue.\n\nThe goal should not merely be to respond effectively to the next dengue outbreak, but to prevent that outbreak from occurring wherever possible.\n\nDengue does not respect administrative boundaries, socioeconomic divisions or seasonal public health priorities. Our response must therefore be continuous, coordinated and inclusive."
