    "chunk_text": "The goal should not merely be to respond effectively to the next dengue outbreak, but to prevent that outbreak from occurring wherever possible.\n\nDengue does not respect administrative boundaries, socioeconomic divisions or seasonal public health priorities. Our response must therefore be continuous, coordinated and inclusive.\n\nDengue may be a year-round threat but preventing it must become a year-round national commitment.\n\n(The author is Consultant Paediatrician, President-Elect, Sri Lanka College of Paediatricians and Immediate Past President, Sri Lanka Medical Association)\n\nIn 2017, Sri Lanka experienced its largest recorded dengue epidemic, with 186,101 suspected cases and 440 reported deaths. Nearly a decade later, the country continues to face significant outbreaks, demonstrating that dengue remains far from being brought under sustainable control. The situation in 2026 is particularly concerning. According to surveillance figures reported in November, Sri Lanka had recorded 110,431 dengue cases and 82 deaths by November 2026"
