    "description": "From policy to protection: Why justice must be a right, not a privilege",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-policy-to-protection-Why-justice-must-be-a-right-not-a-privilege/4-800589",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795645360.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-7035a9d8-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2c61d3781a56b44c72ede83dadea4796",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1414,
    "chunk_text": "From policy to protection: Why justice must be a right, not a privilege\n\nSri Lanka has made commendable progress. We must recognise these achievements, build upon them and find the collective courage to address what remains unfinished. Success will be defined when fewer women experience violence, when survivors seek help without fear, and when every woman and girl lives with uncompromised dignity and freedom\n\nAs we mark the beginning of the 16 Days of Activism Against Gender-Based Violence on 25 November, I find myself reflecting on a simple yet heartbreaking question: What does a woman or girl truly endure when she takes the courageous step to seek justice after facing gender-based violence (GBV)? What do safety and freedom really feel like when the very spaces meant to protect her fail to do so?\n\nFor far too many women, the ordeal begins right in their own home: the very place where they ought to feel safest becomes a place of fear and violence. Meanwhile, the family and community they call their own and rely on for protection prevent them from seeking justice and sanction them for doing so. When violence strikes at home, they confront overwhelming familial and social pressures and coercion from family and community members to stay silent and settle matters privately, and they are overwhelmed with anxiety about the wellbeing of their children, their careers and their family\u2019s reputation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-7035a9d8",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7035a9d8",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "From policy to protection: Why justice must be a right, not a privilege",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-policy-to-protection-Why-justice-must-be-a-right-not-a-privilege/4-800589",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795645360.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-7035a9d8-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6d78f424e5a54c2d0ec98a76e4e43fd3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1476,
    "chunk_text": "For far too many women, the ordeal begins right in their own home: the very place where they ought to feel safest becomes a place of fear and violence. Meanwhile, the family and community they call their own and rely on for protection prevent them from seeking justice and sanction them for doing so. When violence strikes at home, they confront overwhelming familial and social pressures and coercion from family and community members to stay silent and settle matters privately, and they are overwhelmed with anxiety about the wellbeing of their children, their careers and their family\u2019s reputation.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s 2019 National Women\u2019s Wellbeing Survey reveals the true gravity of these challenges. One in five ever-partnered women (20.4%) has experienced physical and/or sexual violence by an intimate partner in her lifetime, while two in five (39.8%) have endured physical, sexual, emotional or economic violence, or controlling behaviours. Most heartbreaking of all, nearly half (49.3%) of women who survived sexual violence by a partner never sought formal help.\n\nBehind these numbers is a mother, daughter or sister who feared being blamed, judged or disbelieved. They are women who worry about their children\u2019s future, financial survival and community backlash, and women who endured the abuse because it had been normalised for generations.\n\nWhat must we do to dismantle these barriers and make seeking justice a seamless, empowering path for every survivor?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-7035a9d8",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7035a9d8",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "From policy to protection: Why justice must be a right, not a privilege",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/From-policy-to-protection-Why-justice-must-be-a-right-not-a-privilege/4-800589",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795645360.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-7035a9d8-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e44763f84545cf7193d8369e9e58e2e6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "Behind these numbers is a mother, daughter or sister who feared being blamed, judged or disbelieved. They are women who worry about their children\u2019s future, financial survival and community backlash, and women who endured the abuse because it had been normalised for generations.\n\nWhat must we do to dismantle these barriers and make seeking justice a seamless, empowering path for every survivor?\n\nHowever, legislation and policy only result in meaningful progress and benefit society if they are effectively enforced or implemented. Sri Lanka already possesses a critical legal framework, including the Prevention of Domestic Violence Act, the Victims and Witnesses Protection Act, police support units and specialised survivor services. Our immediate task is ensuring these mechanisms operate seamlessly together, receive adequate resources and remain accessible to every woman, creating safer spaces everywhere.\n\nWe must also identify and acknowledge remaining legal and practical gaps, ensuring robust protection extends to all forms of abuse, especially technology-facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV), which increasingly targets women and girls online. Equally vital is prevention. Violence begins long before a physical act: entrenched harmful social norms perpetuate and normalise abuse, taking root in everyday attitudes that excuse digital harassment, coercion and unequal power dynamics.\n\nEnding violence requires connecting Government commitments to a powerful public narrative. Sri Lanka possesses deeply ingrained cultural values of respect, protection, care and dignity for women. We must draw on these values to nurture children who understand consent, build communities that firmly reject violence, and engage men and boys as active partners in positive change. No woman should ever have to alter how she dresses, travels, works or lives simply to stay safe and free from violence."
  },
  {
