    "chunk_text": "Third perspective reveals awful truth of Budget 2027, Govt. strategy\n\nAs Niccolo Machiavelli observed, only those at the base can see what the apex is like. Only the ruled know what the rulers are like. Centuries later, Marxism elaborated the point philosophically and epistemologically. Capitalism can be understood as a totality only by those who view it, not necessarily from the lived personal experience, but from the standpoint and perspective of the working classes\u2014the majority within the system, at the base\n\nWhenever President AKD and his Cabinet colleagues scoff in Parliament and at public meetings at the notion of a debt burden/repayment problem, saying it is all under control, they simply aren\u2019t telling the truth.\n\nOne of our most eminent economists, ANU\u2019s Prof Emeritus Prema-chandra Athukorala blew the whistle, calling the repayment agreement \u201ca sword of Damocles\u201d.\n\n\u2018\u2026He attributed this to a restructuring that relied on maturity extensions and coupon (interest rate) cuts rather than haircuts, or write-downs, on International Sovereign Bonds (ISBs). Ghana, by contrast, secured a haircut of about 37% from its bondholders in 2024. Sri Lanka\u2019s upfront debt service relief of about $ 3 billion could fall to $ 1.8 billion under the new macro-linked bonds if growth exceeds the IMF baseline, or rise to $ 4.3 billion if it falls short.\n\n\u201cParadoxically, therefore, stronger-than-anticipated economic growth could itself increase the debt-servicing burden,\u201d he said.\n\nAnnual debt service payments (principal and interest) have climbed from $ 2.8 billion in 2023 to $ 4.3 billion in 2024 and $ 5.1 billion in 2025, with the Government accounting for 47% of the 2025 total.\n\nThe debt service ratio, debt service as a share of exports, rose from 15.9% to 24.9% over the same period.\n\nProf. Athukorala said the ratio had already reached the pre-crisis level as repayment of past due interest (PDI) bonds began in 2025. It would rise further once ISB coupon payments resume after 2027."
  },
