    "chunk_text": "As for university students in the 1960s, they rebelled violently twice, clashing with the Police and the Army at Colombo and Peradeniya. I doubt my old friend, Economics student at Peradeniya in the mid-1960s, N. Shanmugaratnam, currently Professor Emeritus of Development Studies at a Norwegian university, ever regarded Rostow \u2018a hero\u2019.\n\nThe 1960s paradigm was, quite rightly, not \u2018Growth\u2019 per se, but \u2018Development\u2019. The centre of intellectual inspiration for the English-speaking policy intelligentsia\u2014French and Spanish speaking policy intellectuals were way ahead of the Commonwealth ones\u2014was the Institute of Development Studies (IDS), Sussex. It was Dudley Seers, Michael Lipton and Richard Jolly who were most respected by Ceylonese economists.\n\nWhen Asia had its economic miracle, LSE\u2019s Prof Robert Wade was its most outstanding theoretical interpreter with \u2018Governing the Market: Economic Theory and the Role of Government in East Asia\u2019s Industrialisation\u2019 (Princeton 1990). The East Asian miracle bore no resemblance to Walt Rostow\u2019s\u00a0 \u2018Stages of Economic Growth\u2019.\n\nNobel Prize winners Joe Stiglitz and Paul Krugman never mention Rostow\u2019s book approvingly.\n\nBudget 2027 gave rise to two expected sets of responses: on the one hand that of the Establishment, i.e., the President and Government, top corporates, neoliberal economists, and international investment \u2018experts\u2019, and on the other hand, the Parliamentary Opposition.\n\nThe economic policy elite which for the first time since 1948 overlaps with the actual economic elite, commended the President and Govt for the Budget, but urged expeditious movement along the same trajectory, to free or minimise exchange controls and liberalise labour and land laws. (https://www.ft.lk/top-story/Sri-Lanka-must-prove-recovery-is-more-than-a-cycle-Hosking/26-800322)"
