    "chunk_text": "Corporate income tax holidays, on the other hand, which cost a mammoth Rs. 190 billion in 2024/25 according to the Ministry of Finance Tax Expenditure Report, continue unabated.\n\n85% of the burden of the expected tax revenue increase falls squarely on the general public.\n\nThe statement sets out the cumulative negative consequences of Budget 2027 policies.\n\n\u2018Trade Unions and People\u2019s Power\u2019 recommend alternative policies which would bring greater benefits.\n\nShifting towards a higher direct-to-indirect tax ratio by revoking corporate income tax breaks and enforcing strict tax compliance would ease the cost of living, curb inflation, and lift families out of poverty and child malnutrition, while improving fiscal stability.\n\nIt would drive down interest rates and hence, Government debt-servicing costs, while stimulating real investments, creating a virtuous cycle that further expands direct tax revenue through rising aggregate incomes.\n\nYet, the current tax policy remains entirely geared towards speculative and commercial capital that prefers shifting assets and surpluses abroad or into real estate rather than expanding industry.\n\nThat the Government has eschewed these options reveals the socioeconomic interests that the AKD-JVP-NPP administration primarily serves.\n\nGoing beyond the parliamentary Opposition, the TU Statement argues that the current tax policy actually \u2018locks in economic backwardness\u2019 and \u2018structural stagnation\u2019.\n\nConsequently, the tax structure locks in economic backwardness. This is underscored by Sri Lanka\u2019s Economic Complexity Index rank remaining stagnant at 83rd globally, with its value trapped in negative territory (-0.045) since the inception of the index in the mid-1990s.\n\nThe 2027 Budget will ensure that this structural stagnation in the economy persists.\n\nThe \u2018Movement\u2019 statement then proceeds, as the parliamentary Opposition does not, to unmask what the Government is actually doing: the deliberate \u2018manufacture\u2019 of \u2018artificial\u2019 budget surpluses."
