    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "This isn\u2019t to do with the class origins of the leaders/ members. One of the most refined African minds of the 20th century, Amilcar Cabral, founder-leader of the Guinea-Bissau national liberation movement, assassinated by the Portuese colonial secret service, famously said that the liberation movement, composed as it was of the petty-bourgeoisie, must \u2018commit class suicide\u2019 and be \u2018reborn\u2019 as the proletariat, i.e., with the class consciousness of the proletariat.\n\nGiven the lucid diagnostics of the 38-Trade Union statement, what are the political implications and conclusions that can be derived?\n\nFirstly, the crisis will begin to mount, not in 2028-2029, but in 2027, intensifying in 2028-2029.\n\nSecondly, the Government will turn openly repressive and authoritarian, if not dictatorial. The Trade Union statement gives a material explanation for the impending intrusive-coercive legislation:\n\n\u2018The horrendously repressive Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill (POCA) have been deliberately introduced by the NPP regime to stamp out future public dissent, silence opposing voices against the increasingly unbearable living costs and crumbling state of essential services...\u2019\n\nThe JVP-NPP is erecting an autocratic politico-legal \u2018superstructure\u2019 to pre-empt/contain the social fallout of its project at the material \u2018base\u2019 or \u2018substructure\u2019, the economy.\n\nThirdly, there is no longer any excuse to whitewash the JVP-NPP as somehow better, more progressive than the mainstream Opposition parties consisting of those previously in Government. How can a social safety-net slashing/shrinking, poverty-proliferating JVP-NPP Government be favourably compared with, say, the Premadasa administration which implemented the Janasaviya program, built and assisted the building of houses, and gave free mid-day meals and free school uniforms to schoolchildren?"
