    "md5": "f6c30b88e3d3142d7eb5bbdf17f26ae3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1791,
    "chunk_text": "What\u2019s in a name? Why NMRA must hold the line on unlabelled filgrastim\n\nThe NMRA\u2019s decision will reach far beyond one consignment. If a missing product name can be patched with stickers in a warehouse, every supplier learns that labelling is negotiable once the stock has landed. If the regulator holds the line, suppliers learn to get it right before they ship. Sri Lanka is also building a local pharmaceutical industry that aims to export, and that ambition rests on the credibility of its regulator. Overseas buyers and inspectors judge a country\u2019s medicines by how its regulator behaves under pressure\n\nUmpire Ross Emerson tested the character of Arjuna Ranatunga\u2019s captaincy in 1999. In 2026, quarantined consignment of temperature-sensitive (2\u20138 \u00b0C) injectable biological medicine is testing the character of Sri Lanka\u2019s regulatory authority.\n\nIn a cold store in Sri Lanka, a large Government-tender consignment of filgrastim pre-filled syringes sits in quarantine. Filgrastim helps protect patients on chemotherapy from dangerous, sometimes fatal, infections by shortening severe neutropenia (a dangerously low white blood cell count).\n\nThe outer cartons of this consignment are fully labelled but the syringes inside are not. Each syringe carries its strength, batch number, manufacturing date and expiry date, but not the name of the medicine or the name of its maker. Now this might not seem worth all the fuss to the average consumer. I know I\u2019ve bought groceries plenty of times not knowing the brand or maker as long as I knew the expiry date and ingredients. But this is not a supermarket; these aren\u2019t groceries; and the accountability of manufacturers when it comes to biological medicines is little more essential than when it comes to your jam and butter."
