    "chunk_text": "The outer cartons of this consignment are fully labelled but the syringes inside are not. Each syringe carries its strength, batch number, manufacturing date and expiry date, but not the name of the medicine or the name of its maker. Now this might not seem worth all the fuss to the average consumer. I know I\u2019ve bought groceries plenty of times not knowing the brand or maker as long as I knew the expiry date and ingredients. But this is not a supermarket; these aren\u2019t groceries; and the accountability of manufacturers when it comes to biological medicines is little more essential than when it comes to your jam and butter.\n\nHistorically I have been very candid and very critical of the NMRA, its actions and its rationale for those actions. But this time, its caution and adherence to protocol deserves support and appreciation.\n\nBoth global and domestic standards require these to be labelled on both the outer box and the inner container. Clear identification on every syringe is also basic to pharmacovigilance; it lets adverse reactions be reported and traced quickly, and lets the medicine\u2019s use and quality be monitored: the WHO\u2019s good manufacturing practice (\u2018GMP\u2019) principles require every finished medicine to be labelled with its name; Article 55 of the European Union\u2019s Directive 2001/83/EC requires the name even on the smallest immediate container, where nothing else will fit; and the NMRA\u2019s own registration of this product makes it mandatory on the primary pack.\n\nThe reason is practical, not bureaucratic. A carton protects the syringes in a warehouse. In a ward, it is opened, and the syringes go into trays, fridges and nurses\u2019 hands. From that moment, the syringe must speak for itself. A nurse preparing an injection at 2 AM reads the syringe, not the box it came in. A syringe that says only \u2018300 mcg\u2019 and a batch code could be mistaken for any injection of a similar strength. Medication mix-ups like this are exactly what labelling rules exist to prevent."
