    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "The reason is practical, not bureaucratic. A carton protects the syringes in a warehouse. In a ward, it is opened, and the syringes go into trays, fridges and nurses\u2019 hands. From that moment, the syringe must speak for itself. A nurse preparing an injection at 2 AM reads the syringe, not the box it came in. A syringe that says only \u2018300 mcg\u2019 and a batch code could be mistaken for any injection of a similar strength. Medication mix-ups like this are exactly what labelling rules exist to prevent.\n\nThe proposed fix carries more risk than the fault it is meant to cure. Labelling a sterile biological medicine is not clerical work. It is a manufacturing step. The WHO\u2019s GMP principles treat labelling as part of production, and anyone who does it as a manufacturer. That work belongs in a licensed site, with validated procedures, trained staff, full batch records and temperature control. An importer\u2019s warehouse is none of these.\n\nThe practical risks are serious. The first is the cold chain. Filgrastim is a protein that must stay between 2\u00b0C and 8\u00b0C, and handling tens of thousands of syringes outside a controlled packaging line invites temperature excursions that may leave no visible trace. Every syringe taken out and put back also risks a cracked barrel, a loosened cap, or a compromised seal.\n\nThen there are the labels themselves. The consignment spans five batches. Hand-applying loose labels across five batches is a classic recipe for the wrong batch number on the wrong syringe, which is why GMP prefers roll-fed labels to loose cut ones. A licensed site would also reconcile every label issued, used and destroyed. Recreating that discipline in a temporary set-up is hard, and any gap undermines confidence in every syringe. Supervision cannot substitute for a qualified facility. An inspector can watch a process. An inspector cannot validate it."
