    "chunk_text": "Then there are the labels themselves. The consignment spans five batches. Hand-applying loose labels across five batches is a classic recipe for the wrong batch number on the wrong syringe, which is why GMP prefers roll-fed labels to loose cut ones. A licensed site would also reconcile every label issued, used and destroyed. Recreating that discipline in a temporary set-up is hard, and any gap undermines confidence in every syringe. Supervision cannot substitute for a qualified facility. An inspector can watch a process. An inspector cannot validate it.\n\nFirst, test independently. Samples from every batch should be tested by a qualified laboratory before a single syringe reaches a patient. Second, labelling should be done at a licensed site, or replace the stock. If the syringes are sound, the manufacturer should relabel them at its own licensed facility; if not, they should be exported back and replaced. Third, bridge the gap in the meantime with emergency procurement of correctly labelled, registered stock (now, here is the right occasion to bring medicine down using emergency regulations).\n\nFinally, close the procurement gap. The manufacturer says the Government-specific artwork was approved by the procurement side before production. If so, tender artwork must in future be checked against the NMRA-registered labelling before anything is made. The NMRA has already told the State Pharmaceuticals Corporation (\u2018SPC\u2019) that sticker labels are not acceptable in line with its own labelling guideline, GL-006, which allows over-stickers only for the name in national languages and the price, subject to NMRA pre-approval. That rule should be in every tender.\n\nThe NMRA\u2019s decision will reach far beyond one consignment. If a missing product name can be patched with stickers in a warehouse, every supplier learns that labelling is negotiable once the stock has landed. If the regulator holds the line, suppliers learn to get it right before they ship."
