    "md5": "8150e498259bcda0b2e144df53110310",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "Less goods, not just more money: Why tighter credit alone could make our inflation worse\n\nThe Central Bank has raised interest rates and locked away more of the banks\u2019 deposits. The diagnosis behind these moves is that too much money is chasing too few goods. But our inflation is coming mainly from fuel, transport and food. Treat a supply problem as a money problem, and we risk shutting down the very businesses that produce what we buy\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s Financial Stability Review 2026 was an alarm. Credit is growing far faster than deposits, and much of it is flowing into consumption rather than production. A Central Bank must act early rather than clean up later. It has now acted.\n\nOn 20 November, the Central Bank raised its Overnight Policy Rate by 25 basis points to 9%, its second increase this year after a 100 basis point rise in May. It also raised the Statutory Reserve Ratio from 2% to 3%, effective from 9 December. The Governor expects inflation, already 8.5% in November, to reach about 9% by December.\n\nI welcome a Central Bank that acts rather than waits. But in medicine, the treatment is only as good as the diagnosis. Give a patient the wrong medicine and the illness can get worse. That is my concern today.\n\nThe two measures work in different ways, but they aim at the same thing: less money in circulation and slower credit.\n\nA higher policy rate makes borrowing more expensive across the economy. Banks will pass it on to lending rates immediately, including the existing loans, and in time to deposit rates.\n\nA higher Statutory Reserve Ratio forces banks to keep one more rupee in every hundred of their rupee deposits at the Central Bank, earning nothing. That money can no longer be lent."
  },
