    "md5": "8cbaa8600dd68350f1c5ad75e8f0292c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "A higher policy rate makes borrowing more expensive across the economy. Banks will pass it on to lending rates immediately, including the existing loans, and in time to deposit rates.\n\nA higher Statutory Reserve Ratio forces banks to keep one more rupee in every hundred of their rupee deposits at the Central Bank, earning nothing. That money can no longer be lent.\n\nWe all learned the textbook definition: inflation is too much money chasing too few goods. We tend to remember only the first half. But the definition has two halves, and either one can drive prices up. Prices rise when money grows faster than goods. They also rise when goods become scarce or more expensive to produce, even if money does not grow at all.\n\nTransport prices are up 17.6% in a year. Restaurants and hotels are up 13.4%. Food is up 8.8%, driven by big onions, fresh fish, eggs, bread, wheat flour, chicken, milk powder and coconuts. Yet core inflation, which strips out food, energy and transport, is only 5.6%.\n\nIn other words, most of our inflation is not coming from money. It is coming from fuel, freight and food.\n\nThe Central Bank itself says as much. It points to the Middle East conflict and high global energy prices, the rising cost of food, and the second-round effects of earlier energy price increases. These are supply shocks. They begin outside our banking system, and no amount of tightening inside it will produce a liter of fuel or a kilo of onions.\n\nThe trend over the year tells the same story. Until March, headline and core inflation moved together, at around 2%. In April, as the energy shock hit, prices of non-food items jumped and headline inflation leapt ahead. It has stayed well above core inflation ever since. If too much money were the main cause, core inflation would be rising just as fast.\n\nI do not dismiss the demand side. The Central Bank\u2019s own Financial Stability Review sets out the numbers, and they are stark."
