    "chunk_size_bytes": 1537,
    "chunk_text": "The trend over the year tells the same story. Until March, headline and core inflation moved together, at around 2%. In April, as the energy shock hit, prices of non-food items jumped and headline inflation leapt ahead. It has stayed well above core inflation ever since. If too much money were the main cause, core inflation would be rising just as fast.\n\nI do not dismiss the demand side. The Central Bank\u2019s own Financial Stability Review sets out the numbers, and they are stark.\n\nSo we have both problems at once: a supply shock pushing prices up, and a consumer credit boom adding fuel to the fire. The question is not whether the Central Bank should act. It is whether the tools it has chosen hit the right target.\n\nOn the same day the Central Bank raised rates and reserves, the Governor told reporters that the Central Bank has no target for credit growth. Credit growing at more than 20% was, in his words, \u201ca very good provision of credit to the economy\u201d. He rejected suggestions that people should delay taking loans: at current rates, he said, \u201cone should definitely take a loan\u201d, provided it is backed by a genuine need and the capacity to repay. As a banker I understand the point. Credit backed by repayment capacity is the lifeblood of an economy. But the message to the market is mixed. The Central Bank is locking away deposits to slow lending, while telling the public that borrowing is a good idea. Banks will hear the first message through their costs. Borrowers will hear the second through the headlines."
