    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "On the same day the Central Bank raised rates and reserves, the Governor told reporters that the Central Bank has no target for credit growth. Credit growing at more than 20% was, in his words, \u201ca very good provision of credit to the economy\u201d. He rejected suggestions that people should delay taking loans: at current rates, he said, \u201cone should definitely take a loan\u201d, provided it is backed by a genuine need and the capacity to repay. As a banker I understand the point. Credit backed by repayment capacity is the lifeblood of an economy. But the message to the market is mixed. The Central Bank is locking away deposits to slow lending, while telling the public that borrowing is a good idea. Banks will hear the first message through their costs. Borrowers will hear the second through the headlines.\n\nA Central Bank with no view on the direction of credit is leaving that choice to the collateral.\n\nThe Governor says the Central Bank watches how borrowing affects aggregate demand, particularly for imports. That is the right lens. But a loan that finances an imported car and a loan that finances a local onion farmer have very different effects on imports and on prices. The lens is right. It needs to look at where the credit goes, not only how much of it there is.\n\nFrom 9 December, every bank must lock away an additional 1% of its rupee deposits at the Central Bank. Banks are profit driven hence will find the money, but the way they find it matters.\n\nThey will run down their buffers first. Banks still hold large liquid buffers, mostly in Government securities. Selling or not renewing Treasury bills will meet the requirement, but it will push up the Government\u2019s borrowing costs.\n\nThey will compete harder for deposits. With a Credit-to-Deposit Ratio of 74% and deposits growing slowly, banks will raise deposit rates to attract funds.\n\nThey will raise lending rates. Idle reserves earn nothing, and dearer deposits cost more. Both costs will be passed to borrowers."
