    "md5": "bfb1bc680d2d4a7688b2d41535cea4e8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "They will compete harder for deposits. With a Credit-to-Deposit Ratio of 74% and deposits growing slowly, banks will raise deposit rates to attract funds.\n\nThey will raise lending rates. Idle reserves earn nothing, and dearer deposits cost more. Both costs will be passed to borrowers.\n\nHere lies the danger. The burden of this tightening will not fall evenly.\n\nMost SME loans are linked to the lending rate and reprice immediately. Many consumer and vehicle loans carry fixed rates and do not. Gold-backed pawning loans are short-term, fully secured and remain highly profitable for banks. So the borrowers the Central Bank most wants to slow down, consumers buying cars and pawning gold, will feel the least. The borrowers it should most want to protect, the manufacturers, farmers and small traders who produce goods, will feel the most.\n\nWe already know how fragile this sector is. The Department of Census and Statistics found that 204,100 micro, small and medium enterprises had closed permanently by the end of 2022, with another 59,100 closed temporarily. The SME sector has never fully recovered from that blow, and it now faces higher rates again.\n\nIf the tightening falls hardest on producers, the consequences will follow one another:\n\nSMEs cut output or close. Higher interest costs, on top of high energy and transport costs, squeeze margins that were already thin.\n\nSupply falls further. Fewer local producers mean fewer goods, more imports and higher prices. The supply shock deepens.\n\nHousehold incomes fall. SMEs are among our largest employers. When they close, jobs and family incomes go with them.\n\nDeposits fall further. Families with lower incomes save less and borrow more to get by. The Credit-to-Deposit Ratio worsens, not improves.\n\nThe Central Bank tightens again. Facing higher inflation and weaker deposits, it raises rates once more, and the circle begins again."
