    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Deposits fall further. Families with lower incomes save less and borrow more to get by. The Credit-to-Deposit Ratio worsens, not improves.\n\nThe Central Bank tightens again. Facing higher inflation and weaker deposits, it raises rates once more, and the circle begins again.\n\nMeanwhile, the pressures from outside have not eased. The US Federal Reserve is raising rates, the rupee lost 6.8% against the dollar between January and October, and gross official reserves stood at about $ 7.1 billion at the end of October. The IMF program ends in March 2027. Every one of these makes the margin for error smaller.\n\nThe 2027 Budget allocates about Rs. 68 billion for interest subsidies and refinancing to support\u00a0 \u00a0 Rs. 174 billion of concessionary lending to micro, small and medium enterprises. That is welcome. But a subsidy designed at one level of interest rates loses value with every increase. The Government should review these schemes now, so that they still reach the businesses they were meant for.\n\nThe Central Bank is right to act. But it should use sharper tools, and the Government must deal with the supply side, which monetary policy cannot reach.\n\nTarget consumer credit, not all credit. A ceiling on consumer borrowing, with education and health exempt, slows the credit boom without starving producers. Tighter loan-to-value limits on pawning and vehicle loans should continue.\n\nReward banks that lend to producers. The Central Bank could reduce the reserve requirement for banks whose SME, agriculture and export lending exceeds a set share of their loan book. China\u2019s central bank has used targeted reserve requirement cuts in just this way, to steer credit towards small businesses and farmers.\n\nProtect SME borrowers from sudden repricing. Encourage banks to offer SME loans with longer rate-reset periods, so that every policy move does not hit their cash flow overnight."
