    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Reward banks that lend to producers. The Central Bank could reduce the reserve requirement for banks whose SME, agriculture and export lending exceeds a set share of their loan book. China\u2019s central bank has used targeted reserve requirement cuts in just this way, to steer credit towards small businesses and farmers.\n\nProtect SME borrowers from sudden repricing. Encourage banks to offer SME loans with longer rate-reset periods, so that every policy move does not hit their cash flow overnight.\n\nEnergy first. Fuel and transport are the largest single source of today\u2019s inflation. Accelerate rooftop and utility solar, and review how fuel taxes and pricing formulas pass global shocks on to consumers.\n\nGrow more of what we eat. Big onions, eggs, fish, chicken, milk and coconuts are driving food inflation. Working capital, seeds, feed and fertiliser for local producers will do more to bring these prices down than any interest rate.\n\nLet essential goods flow. Restricting imports of essential consumer goods and raw materials cuts supply and raises prices. Restrictions belong on vehicles and luxuries, not on food and inputs.\n\nCut the cost of doing business. Logistics, port charges and electricity tariffs for producers and exporters all feed into prices. Time-bound relief for the sectors that earn foreign exchange protects both prices and the rupee.\n\nCut State waste, not State investment. Every rupee the State does not borrow is a rupee the banks can lend to producers.\n\nThree further measures should be prepared now, ready to be triggered without delay: restricting bank financing of vehicle imports, a phased withdrawal of the Rs. 5,000 note, and long-term certificates of deposit to draw idle money back into the banks. They matter even more now that deposits are scarce.\n\nThere is a further complication that has received little attention. While the Central Bank is trying to slow the flow of money, the Treasury is preparing to release a