    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Three further measures should be prepared now, ready to be triggered without delay: restricting bank financing of vehicle imports, a phased withdrawal of the Rs. 5,000 note, and long-term certificates of deposit to draw idle money back into the banks. They matter even more now that deposits are scarce.\n\nThere is a further complication that has received little attention. While the Central Bank is trying to slow the flow of money, the Treasury is preparing to release a great deal of it.\n\nWhether this spending adds to inflation depends on three things.\n\nHow it is financed. Spending paid for by taxes largely moves money around. Spending financed by borrowing from the banks adds to the money in circulation and competes with private borrowers for the same scarce deposits. Spending financed by the Central Bank is money printing, the road that led us to 2022.\n\nWhen it is spent. If the unspent capital budget is released in a rush at the end of the year, the Treasury will be pumping rupees into the economy in the very weeks the Central Bank is absorbing them through a higher reserve requirement. One foot will be on the accelerator and the other on the brake.\n\nWhat it is spent on. Large projects need imported machinery, fuel and steel, adding to import demand and pressure on the rupee. In the short term, that adds to inflation. But investment in energy, rail, logistics and irrigation lowers the cost of producing and moving goods. Over two or three years, the right projects are the most powerful cure for supply-driven inflation.\n\nUnderspending continues. Little extra inflation, but growth falls short of target and the supply-side investments never arrive.\n\nFull spending, financed at home. More money in circulation, higher interest rates as the State competes with business for funds, SMEs crowded out, more imports and a weaker rupee."
