    "num": "dailyftlk-26cad371",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "Less goods, not just more money: Why tighter credit alone could make our inflation worse",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Less-goods-not-just-more-money-Why-tighter-credit-alone-could-make-our-inflation-worse/4-800600",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795649214.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailyftlk-26cad371-0008",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b4893c5fe6ccd340fa046b055334c772",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1962,
    "chunk_text": "Underspending continues. Little extra inflation, but growth falls short of target and the supply-side investments never arrive.\n\nFull spending, financed at home. More money in circulation, higher interest rates as the State competes with business for funds, SMEs crowded out, more imports and a weaker rupee.\n\nSpending focused on energy, transport and food supply. Some pressure in the short term, but lower costs and lower inflation within two to three years. This is the outcome we should be planning for.\n\nI have found no published assessment that brings the Treasury\u2019s investment plans and the Central Bank\u2019s tightening into a single picture. The Central Bank publishes its inflation projections. The Treasury publishes its fiscal position. But what happens to prices, interest rates and the rupee if both succeed at the same time?\n\nThe Treasury plans to spend Rs. 2 trillion. The Central Bank is trying to slow the flow of money. There is no visible joint plan for what happens when both succeed.\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s Economic Research Department should now model the supply side as closely as it models money and credit. What happens to output if SME borrowing costs rise by two percentage points? How many businesses close, and how many jobs are lost? How much does that reduce deposits? If a measure designed to cut inflation reduces production, it may end up raising the very prices it was meant to control.\n\nThese assessments must reach the Monetary Policy Board before decisions are made, not after. And the Central Bank and the Treasury must act together. Monetary policy can slow demand. Only fiscal and sectoral policy can restore supply.\n\nStewardship means taking over something that is not yours and leaving it in better shape than you found it. Our banking system is stronger today than it has been for years. The task now is to cool the credit boom without breaking the businesses that keep this economy producing, employing and exporting."
  },
