    "md5": "0e2d0d1559d8d61fab51d1e5329d6d9b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "UN-Government initiative strengthens social dialogue to build trust and prevent conflict\n\nTwo-year project establishes 82 workplace forums, strengthens gender equality and gives communities a greater voice in decision-making\n\nFrom left: UN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche, Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government Ministry Secretary S. Alokabandara, UNESCO Regional Office for South Asia Chief of Sector and Program Specialist Eunsong Kim (Moderator), Uva Workers Development Foundation Founder Vadival Anthony, and MAS Holdings Gender Equality Head Thanuja Jayawardene\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic crisis brought more than financial hardship. It also placed pressure on workplaces, public institutions and relationships within communities, highlighting the need for stronger dialogue to address grievances, prevent disputes and rebuild trust.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, a two-year initiative by the United Nations and the Government of Sri Lanka has sought to make social dialogue a practical tool for strengthening institutions, improving workplace relations and promoting social cohesion.\n\nThe Social Dialogue for Peace and Crisis Prevention in Sri Lanka project, implemented by the International Labour Organisation (ILO), the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO) and the United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA), with funding from the UN Sri Lanka SDG Fund, concluded its activities at Cinnamon Grand Colombo yesterday.\n\nThe initiative brought together Government, employers, trade unions, civil society organisations and communities, creating opportunities for people to raise concerns and have a say in decisions affecting their lives.\n\nUN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche said the project had helped move relationships between workers, the State and communities away from confrontation towards constructive dialogue."
  },
