    "chunk_text": "UN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche said the project had helped move relationships between workers, the State and communities away from confrontation towards constructive dialogue.\n\nOne of the project\u2019s major achievements was the establishment of a structured national framework for dialogue covering approximately 1.4 million public-sector employees. Described as the first framework of its kind in South Asia, it operates through 82 workplace forums, seven sectoral forums and a National Public Sector Dialogue Forum.\n\nFranche said more than half of the disputes raised through workplace forums had been resolved locally without escalation. He also highlighted a reduction in public-sector protests from 51 in 2022 to eight in 2025, alongside the wider economic recovery.\n\nA wage dispute at Sri Lanka Railways, resolved through dialogue before developing into a strike, was cited as an example of how early engagement could prevent disruptions to essential services.\n\nHe stressed that industrial disputes affect not only workers and employers but also public services and economic activity, potentially affecting investor confidence. As Sri Lanka continues its recovery and public-sector reforms, trusted channels for discussing issues such as wages, working conditions and administrative changes will become increasingly important.\n\nGender equality and workplace safety were also important areas of the initiative. More than 1,400 public officials across 72 workplace forums in six ministries received training on addressing gender equality, safety and dignity at work.\n\nThe Postal Department is developing a gender-responsive action plan, while the Ministry of Transport has introduced mandatory gender and safety training for public transport workers."
  },
