    "language": "en",
    "md5": "839bce45053de903fe3718acc3030614",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Gender equality and workplace safety were also important areas of the initiative. More than 1,400 public officials across 72 workplace forums in six ministries received training on addressing gender equality, safety and dignity at work.\n\nThe Postal Department is developing a gender-responsive action plan, while the Ministry of Transport has introduced mandatory gender and safety training for public transport workers.\n\nThe initiative also focused on strengthening grievance mechanisms, preventing workplace sexual harassment and encouraging women\u2019s participation in leadership.\n\nDiscussions at the closing event highlighted why gender equality requires more than policies against discrimination and harassment. Organisations need to examine recruitment, promotions, training and leadership opportunities to understand the barriers that continue to limit women\u2019s progress.\n\nAddressing unconscious bias, providing regular training and creating safe channels for employees to raise concerns were identified as important steps. Workplace policies, participants stressed, must be supported by action, open communication and management commitment.\n\nThe project also extended its work to communities, particularly groups that are often left out of decision-making.\n\nWith UNESCO working alongside the Centre for Poverty Analysis, the Coalition for Inclusive Impact and World Vision Lanka, 33 community forums were supported across Gampaha, Badulla and Batticaloa.\n\nMore than 180 forum members received training to better understand policymaking processes and communicate community priorities to district-level officials.\n\nThe forums enabled people from fisheries, plantation and agricultural communities to raise concerns directly with public authorities. In Batticaloa and Jaffna, discussions also brought attention to longstanding issues, including land rights and language access affecting Tamil-speaking communities."
  },
  {
