    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b749574b00b903f4ba6ce757291bb38b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "The forums enabled people from fisheries, plantation and agricultural communities to raise concerns directly with public authorities. In Batticaloa and Jaffna, discussions also brought attention to longstanding issues, including land rights and language access affecting Tamil-speaking communities.\n\nA key highlight of the closing event was a panel discussion titled \u2018Dialogue in Action: Reflecting on the Impact of Social Dialogue\u2019, moderated by UNESCO Regional Office for South Asia Chief of Sector and Program Specialist Eusong Kim.\n\nThe panel featured UN Resident Coordinator Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche, Public Administration Ministry Secretary S. Aloka Bandara, Vadivel Anthony and MAS Holdings Women Go Beyond Head Thanuja Jayawardena, bringing together Government, UN and private-sector perspectives.\n\nThe discussion focused on how social dialogue could support public-sector reforms, improve workplace cooperation and strengthen social cohesion.\n\nParticular attention was given to ensuring that public servants have opportunities to express their concerns and participate in decisions affecting their working conditions, especially as administrative reforms continue.\n\nThe private-sector perspective highlighted the need to address workplace attitudes and unconscious bias that can limit women\u2019s opportunities, while strengthening systems through which employees can report concerns and seek fair solutions.\n\nFranche said differences of opinion should not automatically be treated as a problem. In a diverse society, disagreements are inevitable, but institutions must provide spaces where people can express different views, challenge prejudice and work towards solutions.\n\nHe also pointed to the need for wider public consultation in policymaking, including the national budget process, stressing that meaningful participation should go beyond formal parliamentary discussions."
  },
  {
