    "md5": "c4f74b2fe18806c2915f2d967142d31d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "He also pointed to the need for wider public consultation in policymaking, including the national budget process, stressing that meaningful participation should go beyond formal parliamentary discussions.\n\nLooking ahead, Franche identified the forthcoming expiry of plantation company estate leases, covering approximately one-fifth of Sri Lanka\u2019s arable land, as an opportunity to address longstanding inequalities and improve employment opportunities for plantation workers.\n\nHe also stressed the importance of ensuring that women and young people remain involved in decision-making and that community platforms and institutional mechanisms continue beyond the project\u2019s lifespan.\n\nAnother important message from the closing remarks was that the initiative\u2019s contribution went beyond the forums, training programs and activities established over two years. The trust, relationships and shared understanding developed between Government, employers, workers and communities were equally important.\n\nThe speakers expressed appreciation to the Public Administration Ministry for its leadership and coordination, as well as to the trade union partners, development organisations, participating institutions and communities.\n\nAlthough the project has formally concluded, its work is far from over. Its success will ultimately depend on whether the dialogue mechanisms become part of everyday institutional practice and continue to give people a meaningful voice.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the challenge now is to keep those conversations going. Lasting peace is not built simply by avoiding disagreements but by creating the trust and understanding needed to address them together."
