    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Bollywood union asks Modi to bar Netflix\u2019s Pakistani series in India\n\nReuters\u00a0- A prominent Bollywood \u200cworkers' union has asked Prime Minister Narendra Modi's government to ban a forthcoming Pakistani series on Netflix (NFLX.O) in India, while seeking a law prohibiting content from its foe from being shown in the country.\n\nThe first part of \"Jo Bachay \u200bHain Sang Samait Lo\" (All the Love Our Hearts Carry) is scheduled to air on Netflix \u200bon December 4 and is the global streaming giant's first Urdu-language series. Several top \u2060Pakistani actors feature in it.\n\n\"No Pakistani face, voice or story has a place on Indian screens,\" \u200bthe All Indian Cine Workers Association (AICWA), a union of film workers, said in a letter addressed to \u200bModi late on Tuesday.\n\nNetflix and a Modi spokesperson did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\n\nNew Delhi has for decades accused Islamabad of aiding, abetting and orchestrating violent attacks by Islamist militant groups across India, including one \u200bon Hindu tourists in Indian Kashmir last year that killed 26 people and led to the worst fighting \u200bbetween the neighbours in years. Pakistan denies India's accusations.\n\nIndia has asked all streaming platforms to stop screening Pakistani shows, many \u200cof \u2060which enjoy a huge fan following in the country, after the fighting, and AICWA said Netflix would be in violation of the advisory if it showed the series.\n\nPakistani content is not screened on streaming platforms in India, though several series are available on YouTube.\n\nSeveral of the actors in the series had \u200bspoken out against India \u200bduring times of tension \u2060between the two countries, AICWA, which says it is the largest union of film workers, said in the letter.\n\n\"We have also demanded a strong, permanent \u200blaw with the severest penalties and we appeal to Netflix India and \u200ball OTT platforms \u2060never to show any Pakistani content in India,\" it said."
