    "md5": "e068cf47209050b961270ae964a1b9e5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1826,
    "chunk_text": "HIV infections rise 48% in Sri Lanka as youth face growing risk\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka has recorded a 48% increase in new HIV infections, despite a significant decline in infections worldwide, raising concerns over the growing number of young people at risk and the country's efforts to contain the spread of the virus.\n\nSince the first HIV case was reported in Sri Lanka in 1987, a total of around 8,111 cases had been identified by the end of the third quarter of 2026. An estimated 8,700 people are currently living with HIV in the country.\n\nMen account for nearly 87% of reported cases, while 87% of HIV-positive men are men who have sex with men, according to the latest figures.\n\nThe increasing number of infections among young people aged 15 to 24 is another major concern. HIV transmission through injecting drug use is highest in this age group, highlighting the need for greater awareness and prevention measures.\n\nIn contrast, new HIV infections worldwide declined by 42% in 2025 compared with 2010. Sri Lanka, however, has recorded a 48% increase during the same period.\n\n\u201cThe increase in HIV infections among young people aged 15 to 24 is particularly worrying. We are also seeing drug use associated with sexual activity, which can increase the risk of HIV transmission. It is important for people to understand these risks and take necessary precautions,\u201d said Dr. Madhavi Gunathilake, Specialist in Community Medicine.\n\nShe stressed that early testing and awareness were essential to prevent further transmission of the virus.\n\nPeople can obtain HIV tests through government sexual health clinics or by visiting **knowforsure.lk**. HIV self-testing kits are also available through the website and can be delivered to people's homes, allowing them to check their HIV status privately."
  },
