    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-bd704069-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cbfd163f8867e25d5cf3ff625fd363ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1711,
    "chunk_text": "After years of waiting, WhatsApp can finally send your messages on a timer\n\nWhile messaging apps like Telegram and Discord have had the ability to schedule messages for a long time, the app with the largest global user base \u2013 WhatsApp \u2013 has surprisingly been behind. That changes now, as the developers have finally announced message scheduling.\n\nThe scheduled message announcement was shared via the official blog, and it is one of WhatsApp\u2019s most requested features. As the name suggests, it allows users to pick a time for when to send a particular message. According to the blog, a message can be scheduled anywhere from 10 minutes to 14 days later. It is not limited to one-on-one messages, either, as it works in group chats as well.\n\nFurthermore, texts, photos, or videos can all be scheduled, but WhatsApp doesn\u2019t mention documents or contacts. It should theoretically be possible to schedule those as well, but the blog doesn\u2019t particularly mention them.\n\nA scheduled message will look like any other message to the receiver, but the sender will be able to see the scheduled time and also be able to delete it if they so choose. Editing scheduled messages before they are sent should be possible as well, though once again, the blog doesn\u2019t mention it. Scheduled messages, like other messages, remain end-to-end encrypted.\n\nThe functionality has been integrated into the send button. Holding the send button will bring up a menu that allows the sender to choose the exact time \u2013 hours and minutes \u2013 and then schedule the message. Of course, you\u2019ll have to type the message before the send button appears, since by default, WhatsApp shows the record button in place of the send button."
  },
