    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka calls for regional action against drug trafficking, maritime threats\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Defence Secretary Air Vice Marshal Sampath Thuyacontha (Retd) has called for stronger regional cooperation to address shared security challenges, including drug trafficking, illegal fishing, threats to critical maritime infrastructure and natural disasters.\n\nHe made these remarks while addressing the 8th National Security Advisors\u2019 (NSA) Meeting of the Colombo Security Conclave (CSC), held in Mal\u00e9, Maldives, yesterday (25).\n\nThe Defence Secretary highlighted the importance of strengthening cooperation among regional countries in an increasingly interconnected and complex security environment, noting that the Colombo Security Conclave had evolved into an important platform for addressing common security concerns through dialogue, cooperation and collective action.\n\nEmphasising the Conclave\u2019s five pillars of cooperation, he called for enhanced collaboration in maritime safety and security, countering terrorism and radicalisation, combating trafficking and transnational organised crime, cybersecurity and the protection of critical infrastructure and technology, and humanitarian assistance and disaster relief.\n\nHe also outlined Sri Lanka\u2019s \u201cRatama Ekata\u201d National Mission, aimed at combating drug trafficking through enhanced law enforcement, disruption of supply networks, preventive measures, public awareness and rehabilitation initiatives.\n\nAddressing maritime security concerns, Thuyacontha stressed the need for greater regional cooperation to combat illegal, unreported and unregulated (IUU) fishing through information sharing, coordinated surveillance and effective enforcement.\n\nHe also highlighted the importance of protecting submarine communication cables, describing them as critical infrastructure requiring enhanced maritime domain awareness and coordinated regional responses."
