    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan households earn more but afford less\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lankan households at the midpoint of the income distribution lost nearly 40 percent of their purchasing power between 2019 and the first quarter of 2025, preliminary official figures show, exposing a deep deterioration in living standards despite higher rupee incomes.\n\nThe Department of Census and Statistics\u2019 revised Household Income and Expenditure Survey bulletin showed monthly median household income rose to Rs.75,882 from Rs.53,333 in 2019. However, after adjusting for inflation, it fell 39.2 percent, indicating that income growth fell well short of compensating for higher living costs.\n\nThe findings place household purchasing power and consumption capacity at the centre of the economic policy challenge, with families receiving more in nominal rupees while having less capacity to purchase goods and services.\n\n\u201cThe decline was greater for median household income than for mean household income,\u201d the department said.\n\nMedian income divides households into two equal groups, with half receiving less and half receiving more. It provides a useful counterpoint to average income, which is influenced by households with very high earnings.\n\nAverage monthly household income increased to Rs.120,266 in the first quarter of 2025 from Rs.76,414 in 2019. Yet its inflation-adjusted value declined 32.8 percent over the same period.\n\nMeasured at constant 2016 prices, median monthly household income fell to Rs.28,444 from Rs.46,829 in 2019, while average income dropped to Rs.45,081 from Rs.67,096. Both were also below their 2016 levels.\n\nThe deterioration extended to household expenditure, providing evidence of weaker real consumption and raising questions about the strength of purchasing capacity available to support businesses dependent on domestic demand."
