    "chunk_size_bytes": 1974,
    "chunk_text": "The deterioration extended to household expenditure, providing evidence of weaker real consumption and raising questions about the strength of purchasing capacity available to support businesses dependent on domestic demand.\n\nMedian monthly expenditure similarly increased in nominal terms to Rs.94,057 from Rs.47,544. Adjusted for inflation, it fell approximately 15.5 percent to Rs.35,257 from Rs.41,746.\n\nThe distinction matters for businesses assessing consumer demand: higher expenditure in rupee terms can coexist with a decline in the goods and services households can afford.\n\nThe survey also showed an estimated reversal in the earlier decline in poverty.\n\nThe national poverty headcount ratio rose to 16 percent in the first quarter of 2025 from 14.3 percent in 2019, returning to the level recorded in 2016. Approximately 3.42 million people were estimated to be living below the official poverty line.\n\nThe monthly poverty threshold stood at Rs.16,417 per person, compared with Rs.6,966 in 2019, reflecting the higher cost of meeting a fixed minimum standard of food and essential non-food needs.\n\nThe threshold is based on expenditure sufficient to meet a daily dietary energy requirement of 2,030 kilocalories, together with an allowance for necessities such as housing, clothing, transport and healthcare. It measures absolute poverty against a defined basic-needs standard rather than the full range of contemporary household spending requirements.\n\nIncome disparities were substantial. The richest fifth of households received average monthly incomes of Rs.335,679, nearly 15 times the Rs.22,612 recorded for the poorest fifth.\n\nThe middle 60 percent of households recorded average monthly income of Rs.81,067, substantially below the national average of Rs.120,266.\n\nConditions also varied across residential sectors. Average monthly household income was highest in urban areas at Rs.178,455, followed by Rs.110,613 in rural areas and Rs.72,558 in estates."
