    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "ASG says medical report found no torture, Sallay\u2019s counsel disputes prosecution\u2019s interpretation of findings\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0While Additional Solicitor General Dileepa Peeris told the Fort Magistrate\u2019s Court that a psychiatric report had not established that former SIS Director Suresh Sallay had been subjected to torture, Sallay\u2019s Counsel Bhanuka Malawiarachchi disputed the prosecution's interpretation of the medical findings, saying the report had not reached a final conclusion and had instead recommended further investigations.\n\nThe submissions were made when the case against three suspects, including retired Major General Suresh Sallay, over allegedly misleading investigations into the 2019 Easter Sunday terror attacks was taken up before Fort Magistrate Pasan Amarasena yesterday.\n\nMeanwhile, the Magistrate Amarasena directed the Criminal Investigation Department to expedite investigations into whether former President Gotabaya Rajapaksa should be named as a suspect in connection with the 2019 Easter Sunday terror attacks and report its findings to court.\n\nThe court also rejected a request made by former President Gotabaya Rajapaksa, through his lawyers, seeking the temporary lifting of the overseas travel ban imposed on him in connection with the case.\n\nASG Peeris, submitting a further report to court that the report, comprising 311 pages and 1,185 paragraphs, had not established that Sallay had been subjected to torture.\n\nReferring to the medical report, the Additional Solicitor General stated,\n\n\"Your Honour, the psychiatric report prepared by the medical specialists concerning the third suspect, Suresh Sallay, has been received. It consists of 311 pages and 1,185 paragraphs. There is no finding in the report that the third suspect had been subjected to torture. Sallay himself has made 95 statements, while approximately 30 statements have been provided by his wife."
  },
