    "chunk_size_bytes": 445,
    "chunk_text": "Diana Gamage pleads guilty to visa offence\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Former MP Diana Gamage today (26) pleaded guilty before the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court to a charge of remaining in Sri Lanka without a valid visa.\n\nWhen the case was taken up before Colombo Chief Magistrate Asanga S. Bodaragama, Attorney-at-Law Mohan Seneviratne, appearing on behalf of Gamage, informed the court that his client was pleading guilty to the charge."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-5824a1e5",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5824a1e5",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "Expose the Names of cheating rogue shops",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Expose-the-Names-of-cheating-rogue-shops/172-355771",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795674677.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-5824a1e5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f32909f6ded6ffa4bc4ce6524b5c6f8a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1527,
    "chunk_text": "Expose the Names of cheating rogue shops\n\nDaily, we find news reports of\u00a0 cheating shops\u00a0 and\u00a0 fines imposed\u00a0 by\u00a0 courts.\u00a0 \u00a0But names of shops are never given in these reports.\n\nWhy are the names of shops raided by the Central Environmental Authority and fined by the courts hidden from media reports?\n\nShops are raided by the Central Environmental Authority for offences such as polluting the environment, trading without a licence, and deceiving the public by selling goods. Later, fines are imposed on them by the courts. Yet, in newspapers and television news, the names of those shops or their owners are not mentioned.\n\nThis is a major problem. After a wrong doing has been committed and proven in court with a fine imposed, the public must be informed about it. It is not just a punishment for one person. It is a lesson for the whole business\u00a0 community.\n\nWhen names are hidden in reports, that same shop commits the same offence again. Other shops also commit the same offence without any fear. In the end, it is the innocent consumer and the environment that suffer the loss. Recent report of shops selling poisonous boondi\u00a0 is alarming as it may lead to serious health issues in litttle kids.\n\nOnce punished by law, publishing names is not defamation. It is publishing the truth. Therefore, we request that the names of shops proven guilty by the court, along with the amount of the fine, be reported publicly. Only then will the law have real value. Only then the public and environment would be protected."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "An uncertain ruling party and a confused opposition",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/An-uncertain-ruling-party-and-a-confused-opposition/172-355772",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795674849.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b16bb34925addb06354e2bf309bbb29e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1217,
    "chunk_text": "An uncertain ruling party and a confused opposition\n\nBut now, when Namal Rajapaksa is brought to court and taken back to prison, only a few supporters show up at the court premises (DM photo)\n\nThere is no sign that the government will take action soon to conduct the provincial council elections. The argument of the government leaders is that the law has to be changed to hold the election.\n\nTrue, the United National Party (UNP) led government in 2017 has created an impasse preventing the provincial council election from being held without amending the Provincial Council Elections Act.\n\nThe government appointed a parliamentary select committee last February for this purpose, yet it has not come out with any proposal acceptable to all political parties. Not only the government, but the opposition parties also do not seem to be in a hurry regarding this matter.\n\nHowever, conducting the elections is the responsibility of the government. Therefore, a perception is growing that the government is not interested in holding the election in fear of a possible defeat. That cannot be completely rejected either. Similarly, if the government is in fact afraid to face the election, it is not without justification."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "An uncertain ruling party and a confused opposition",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/An-uncertain-ruling-party-and-a-confused-opposition/172-355772",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795674849.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7c6908f4f4f625771a9316448b1cf29c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1785,
    "chunk_text": "However, conducting the elections is the responsibility of the government. Therefore, a perception is growing that the government is not interested in holding the election in fear of a possible defeat. That cannot be completely rejected either. Similarly, if the government is in fact afraid to face the election, it is not without justification.\n\nThe prime reason for the setback suffered by the NPP during the last local government elections was the non-fulfillment of the excessive expectations that the people had about the party. For several decades, the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), and since 2019, the NPP, heavily criticised the ruling parties and created a perception in people\u2019s minds that the problems faced by the public could be solved very easily.\n\nHowever, no matter how honest a government is, a country\u2019s problems cannot be solved so easily. Many thought that a revolutionary change would take place in the country as soon as the NPP came to power. They thought that fuel prices and electricity tariffs would drop significantly, the cost of living would decrease, and the country\u2019s development would take place dynamically.\n\nYet, even if the leaders wish for it, in a country that has faced an unprecedented economic crisis, reached bankruptcy, and is surviving on an International Monetary Fund (IMF) bailout package, such a massive change is not possible in a short period. The government has failed to bring about many administrative changes that people desired to see under an NPP rule mainly because of the negative mindset of the officialdom. Hence, in spite of the considerable change in the political culture, the overall picture remained unchanged and that led to the major setback of the NPP in the local government elections held in May last year."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
