    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7649d0dd7967e8d3f9228e714c76e3a2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1531,
    "chunk_text": "Yet, even if the leaders wish for it, in a country that has faced an unprecedented economic crisis, reached bankruptcy, and is surviving on an International Monetary Fund (IMF) bailout package, such a massive change is not possible in a short period. The government has failed to bring about many administrative changes that people desired to see under an NPP rule mainly because of the negative mindset of the officialdom. Hence, in spite of the considerable change in the political culture, the overall picture remained unchanged and that led to the major setback of the NPP in the local government elections held in May last year.\n\nEven without such an economic situation, the United National Party-led government that came to power in the 2015 election was rejected by the people very soon. It can be attributed to a systematic campaign against that government by the opposition. Within six weeks of the Maithripala Sirisena \u2013 Ranil Wickremesinghe government coming to power, an opposition movement named \u201cMahinda Sulanga\u201d (Mahinda Wind), launched under the leadership of Mahinda Rajapaksa loyalists, Wimal Weerawansa and Udaya Gammanpila, advanced successfully.\n\nWithin a short period of time, that protest wave transformed into one with a nationalist/racist tone. The attempts by the Maithri\u2013Ranil government to enact a new constitution and the willingness of the then-government to co-sponsor resolutions passed in the UN Human Rights Council (UNHRC) on wartime human rights violations in Sri Lanka facilitated this."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f239d85a",
    "date_str": "2026-11-26",
    "description": "An uncertain ruling party and a confused opposition",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/An-uncertain-ruling-party-and-a-confused-opposition/172-355772",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1795674849.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-26-dailymirrorlk-f239d85a-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1c3adfe06c0762af849b8d0f946224ed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Within a short period of time, that protest wave transformed into one with a nationalist/racist tone. The attempts by the Maithri\u2013Ranil government to enact a new constitution and the willingness of the then-government to co-sponsor resolutions passed in the UN Human Rights Council (UNHRC) on wartime human rights violations in Sri Lanka facilitated this.\n\nFinally, three years later, the Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) led by Mahinda Rajapaksa captured power in majority of local authorities at the elections held in February 2018. That victory became a trendsetter and turned into a massive wave, facilitating Gotabaya Rajapaksa to become President in the 2019 presidential election. In the 2020 general election, the SLPP won 145 seats.\n\nHowever, the current opposition parties do not seem to be gaining such growing public support. The leadership of the SLPP is facing multiple court cases involving high profile corruption. Initially, when Namal Rajapaksa and Gotabaya Rajapaksa were summoned for investigations, huge crowds gathered in front of the Bribery or Corruption Investigation Commission. But now, when Namal is brought to court and taken back to prison, only a few supporters show up at the court premises.\n\nThe main opposition party, the Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB), does not seem to want to join hands with other opposition parties to launch any struggle against the government. The leaders of that party seem to think that other parties will gain political mileage from their party\u2019s strength. At the same time, SJB is not a fighting force like the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna or Wimal Weerawansa\u2019s National Freedom Front.\n\nThe issues chosen by the current opposition parties to fight against the government also do not provoke public interest. Some issues they centered their protests around are completely incomprehensible to the general public and they hardly felt their relevance."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
