    "md5": "04cf1e0e80f4b4a134d6583016f545ec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "Most of the profit revision stems from the intra-group fair value gains booked by First Capital Holdings PLC on its stakes in Janashakthi Insurance PLC and Janashakthi Finance PLC, which were not eliminated on consolidation and inflated the 2024/25 profit by Rs.413.5 million.\u00a0 A 2017/18 consolidation entry had also been recorded twice, overstating the non-controlling interests by about Rs.1.4 billion in every year since, although correcting it lifted the equity attributable to the shareholders.\n\nThe March 25 prospectus carried the original figures and the 500 million-share offer closed on its April 9 opening day, with the shares rising 37 percent on their April 29 debut.\n\nKPMG signed off on the restated accounts, issuing an unmodified opinion with an emphasis of matter, only on November 16, more than seven months after the year-end and three days after the group\u2019s interim accounts had labelled the March 2026 figures as audited.\n\nHowever, Janashakthi Finance had already acknowledged in its 2025/26 annual report, released in September, that the year-end adjustments pushed its total capital ratio to 11.86 percent, below the 12.5 percent minimum, leaving a regulatory capital shortfall of about Rs.167.9 million.\u00a0 By September 30, both ratios were below the minimums, with the Tier I capital at 7.99 percent of risk-weighted assets, against 8.5 percent and the total capital at 10.72 percent, the finance company disclosed on November 11.\n\nThe parent\u2019s interim accounts, signed two days later, make no reference to the shortfall, with the executive summary instead stating that the finance company \u201cdemonstrated resilience\u201d and that \u201cthe group and its subsidiaries are strongly capitalised to absorb these uncertainties\u201d.\n\nIt also put the finance company\u2019s profit after tax at Rs.162 million, a figure matching its total comprehensive income rather than its Rs.198.6 million profit after tax."
