    "md5": "7562be94e1fecec011ffa38be382f087",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1924,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka holds 21st in Asia Power Index as tourism lifts cultural clout\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s standing in Asia strengthened this year to its best level since the 2022 economic crisis, as the improved tourism indicators lifted its cultural influence, although the island stayed 21st among 27 countries and territories and continued to wield less influence than its resources would suggest, the Lowy Institute\u2019s Asia Power Index 2026 showed.\n\nThe Sydney-based think tank placed Sri Lanka\u2019s comprehensive power score at 8.2 out of 100, up 0.4 points or 5.1 percent when compared to 7.8 points in the 2025 edition.\u00a0 This year\u2019s gain alone exceeded the 0.3 points the island added across the two previous editions combined, when its score crept up from 7.5 to 7.7 and then to 7.8. However, the improvement was not enough to lift Sri Lanka up the table, leaving it 21st for a fourth consecutive edition. The island slipped from 20th to 21st in the 2023 edition after its score fell 1.1 points to 7.5, its lowest in any edition, a decline the institute linked to the economic and political crises of 2022.\n\nThe latest score is still 0.4 points below the 8.6 points Sri Lanka recorded in the 2021 edition, before that fall.\n\nThe institute credited the uplift chiefly to cultural influence, noting that the island\u2019s tourism indicators had improved. Sri Lanka\u2019s cultural influence score climbed 1.0 point or 14.9 percent to 7.7, lifting it four places to 16th. That made it the only one of the index\u2019s eight measures in which the island changed rank and it is now Sri Lanka\u2019s strongest measure.\n\nThe index uses cultural influence to capture how far a country can sway international opinion through its cultural appeal and people-to-people contact.\n\nDiplomatic influence recorded the island\u2019s largest score increase, rising 1.1 points to 22.3, yet Sri Lanka remained 22nd on the measure, its lowest ranking across all eight."
