    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "The index uses cultural influence to capture how far a country can sway international opinion through its cultural appeal and people-to-people contact.\n\nDiplomatic influence recorded the island\u2019s largest score increase, rising 1.1 points to 22.3, yet Sri Lanka remained 22nd on the measure, its lowest ranking across all eight.\n\nDefence networks improved 0.9 points to 5.5 and resilience gained 0.7 points to 25.3, with the island holding 20th and 18th place on the two measures, respectively.\n\nIts scores for economic capability, military capability, future resources and economic relationships were unchanged at 1.9, 6.3, 2.6 and 2.5, leaving it 21st, 19th, 19th and 21st on those measures. The index\u2019s power gap measure, which compares the influence a country exerts with what its resources would predict, put Sri Lanka at minus 2.6, an improvement on minus 2.9 a year earlier.\u00a0 \u201cIts influence still falls short of its resources, though the shortfall narrowed slightly,\u201d the think tank observed. Meanwhile, Sri Lanka closed some of the distance to Bangladesh, which held the 20th place, with 8.9 points, down from 9.0 points. The gap between the two South Asian neighbours narrowed to 0.7 points, from 1.2 points a year earlier.\n\nSri Lanka also widened its margin over the country ranked directly below it. Myanmar, which climbed to 22nd on 7.2 points, trails the island by 1.0 point, when compared to the 0.6-point lead Sri Lanka held over 22nd-placed Laos in 2025.\n\nElsewhere in South Asia, Pakistan held the 16th place after its score rose 0.6 points to 15.0, just clearing the 15-point threshold the 2026 edition sets for middle powers. The institute put the gain down to shuttle diplomacy that gave Islamabad a brief role in the US-Iran ceasefire talks, though it cautioned the boost was unlikely to last.\u00a0 India, ranked third, consolidated its major power status with 40.5 points, up 0.5 points."
