    "chunk_text": "Elsewhere in South Asia, Pakistan held the 16th place after its score rose 0.6 points to 15.0, just clearing the 15-point threshold the 2026 edition sets for middle powers. The institute put the gain down to shuttle diplomacy that gave Islamabad a brief role in the US-Iran ceasefire talks, though it cautioned the boost was unlikely to last.\u00a0 India, ranked third, consolidated its major power status with 40.5 points, up 0.5 points.\n\nThe dependence runs largely one way, as Sri Lanka made up just 0.5 percent of India\u2019s trade, although that was still the highest reliance on Sri Lanka among the region\u2019s countries. At the top of the table, the United States remained Asia\u2019s most powerful country, with 78.9 points, though China, on 73.6 points, narrowed the gap between the two to 5.3 points, from 6.8 points a year earlier.\u00a0 Launched in 2018, the index ranks 27 countries and territories, using 131 indicators across eight measures."
