    "chunk_text": "Oil rises on supply concerns as Iran claims mine strikes on ships\n\nReuters\u00a0- Crude oil prices rose on Thursday, extending gains from the previous session on supply concerns after Yemen's Houthis said they had hit Saudi Arabia's largest oil \u200brefinery and at least two ships hit Iranian mines in the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nBrent \u200ccrude futures were 10 cents, or 0.1%, higher at $94.71 a barrel by 0640 GMT. US West Texas Intermediate crude futures rose 31 cents, or 0.4%, to $83.99 a barrel. There will be no settlement for WTI on Thursday due \u200bto the US Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nPrices rose for the third straight session on Thursday and \u200bare up about 5% this week, after plunging by around a quarter in \u2060the three weeks since the beginning of November. Middle East exports have been increasing despite continued \u200battacks on shipping, which triggered expectations of a global oil surplus this year.\n\nBrent finished 3.5% higher in the \u200bprevious session, while WTI climbed 2% after Yemen's Iran-aligned Houthis said they had attacked Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura oil refinery and Riyadh's financial district.\n\nThe refinery, Saudi Arabia's largest, is part of a complex that also serves as a major \u200bexport terminal for Saudi crude, and analysts expect the latest Houthi attacks to increase the geopolitical \u200brisk premium.\n\nAt the same time, shipping has been disrupted through the Strait of Hormuz, where oil supply had \u200cbeen recovering \u2060to near pre-war levels.\n\nAn oil tanker hit a naval mine and caught fire in a \"restricted zone\" in the strait on Wednesday, the semi-official Fars news agency cited an Iranian Army statement as saying. That report came after state media said two oil tankers struck mines and exploded in the strait \u200bon Wednesday.\n\n\"The broadening of the \u200bMiddle East conflict \u2060threatens to halt the recovery in oil flows in recent weeks. Persian Gulf crude exports rose last week but remain on a declining trend,\" said \u200bANZ analyst Daniel Hynes."
