    "md5": "3156742a1ffb544aac4d7c908dc7aefc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "October exports jump 11.6% YoY\n\nMerchandise exports rise 9.7% to US $ 1.26bn despite declines in apparel and tea\n\nTransport and logistics account for nearly 70% of October\u2019s rise in services earnings\n\nIndia overtakes UK as Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest single export market\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s exports climbed 11.6 percent to US $ 1.62 billion in October, with the gains in the rubber products, spices and electronics making up for the weaker apparel and tea earnings, while a surge in transport and logistics lifted the services exports.\n\nThe October gains took the earnings for the first 10 months to US $ 15.28 billion, up 6 percent from a year earlier, according to the Export Development Board (EDB).\n\n\u201cMoving forward, our priority must be to strengthen export competitiveness, diversify products and markets, enhance value addition and accelerate innovation and technology adoption to create greater opportunities for Sri Lankan businesses,\u201d EDB Chairman and Chief Executive Mangala Wijesinghe said.\n\nHe said implementation of the National Export Development Plan for 2026-2030 would be central to reaching the country\u2019s target of US $ 36 billion in annual export earnings by 2030.\n\nThe merchandise exports rose 9.7 percent to US $ 1.26 billion in October. The rubber and rubber-based products gained 24.8 percent to US $ 100 million on higher earnings from tyres and gloves, while the electrical and electronic components rose 47.8 percent to US $ 57.3 million, helped by the stronger exports of insulated wires and cables.\n\nThe exports of spices, essential oils and oleoresins climbed 76.9 percent to US $ 67.9 million. The pepper earnings nearly quadrupled to US $ 21 million, with India accounting for US $ 18.3 million, while the cinnamon exports rose 35.1 percent to US $ 35.1 million, supported by the higher shipments to Mexico.\n\nApparel and textiles, the largest merchandise export category, fell 3.8 percent to US $ 412.8 million, with the earnings down in the US, European Union and UK."
  },
