    "md5": "c2df78d89a8da71e3a248bbc62bbc627",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1628,
    "chunk_text": "The exports of spices, essential oils and oleoresins climbed 76.9 percent to US $ 67.9 million. The pepper earnings nearly quadrupled to US $ 21 million, with India accounting for US $ 18.3 million, while the cinnamon exports rose 35.1 percent to US $ 35.1 million, supported by the higher shipments to Mexico.\n\nApparel and textiles, the largest merchandise export category, fell 3.8 percent to US $ 412.8 million, with the earnings down in the US, European Union and UK.\n\nThe transport and logistics earnings increased 24.7 percent to US $ 197.9 million, accounting for nearly 70 percent of the month\u2019s year-on-year increase in the services earnings.\n\nThe ICT and business process management exports rose 10.7 percent to US $ 148.3 million.\n\nOver the first 10 months, the merchandise exports grew 5.7 percent to US $ 12.01 billion and the services earnings rose 7.2 percent to an estimated US $ 3.27 billion.\n\nThe electrical and electronic components led the growth among the major goods categories, rising 59.6 percent to US $ 566.1 million, while spices and concentrates grew 35.4 percent to US $ 498.4 million. The apparel and textile earnings fell 5.5 percent to US $ 4.20 billion and tea declined 6.8 percent to US $ 1.20 billion.\n\nIndia overtook the UK as Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest single export market, with the shipments rising 14.2 percent to US $ 989.7 million in the 10 months through October. The exports to the UK fell 8.7 percent to US $ 723.4 million.\n\nThe US remained the largest single market at US $ 2.52 billion, up 0.8 percent, while the exports to the EU bloc slipped 0.9 percent to US $ 2.59 billion."
