    "md5": "255e946faf23d6f0ad795b1f851002b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "Software takes centre stage as Sungrow wires AI into every layer of energy storage\n\nPoint Sungrow\u2019s artificial intelligence (AI) assistant at a factory on a satellite map and, within 30 seconds, it returns a tailor-made design for a solar and battery storage plant, including the technical layout, installation plan and financial calculations. Once the equipment reaches the site, Sungrow claims the same assistant can complete commissioning in three minutes, a process that has long relied on manual parameter setting and painstaking checks.\n\nThe assistant, nicknamed Bubbly, was one of the clearest signals at Sungrow\u2019s Global Energy Pioneer Summit (GEPS) 2026 in Hefei that the Chinese inverter and storage major sees the next leap in energy storage coming as much from software as from hardware. Across its new residential and commercial and industrial (C&I) systems, the company is embedding AI into design, installation, dispatch and maintenance, betting that smarter software will decide how much value each kilowatt-hour delivers.\n\nBubbly runs five intelligent agents covering plant design, commissioning, energy management, operation and maintenance, and comes built into the newly launched PowerStack 3.0 C&I storage series. Its design agent combines satellite imagery with 3D modelling to produce a tailor-made proposal, which Sungrow says can reduce on-site surveys and help developers assess a project\u2019s feasibility before committing capital.\n\nIntroducing the system on stage, Sungrow C&I Energy Storage Product Line General Manager Dr. Cao Wei pointed out that installers previously had to visit each site to work out its configuration, which often involved a huge amount of work. The commissioning agent now sets grid parameters automatically, runs communication checks and validates settings, cutting the risk of errors that come with manual verification and easing the training burden on service teams."
  },
