    "md5": "a4161458cb524bc5d26b508a1e634be3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1424,
    "chunk_text": "Netherlands to return two historic Kandyan guns to Sri Lanka\n\nColombo, Nov. 26 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0The Government of the Netherlands has decided to return two historic 18th-century Sri Lankan firearms, known as Maha Thuwakku (rampart guns), which were taken from the Kingdom of Kandy during the Dutch colonial period.\n\nThe two weapons, dating back more than 250 years, are currently housed at the Rijksmuseum, the Netherlands\u2019 national museum in Amsterdam.\n\nThe decision was approved by Dutch Minister of Education, Culture and Science Rianne Letschert and announced by Dutch Ambassador to Sri Lanka Wiebe de Boer.\n\nThe repatriation follows joint provenance research conducted by Rijksmuseum specialists and Sri Lankan scholars, which established that the firearms originated in Sri Lanka.\n\nResearchers identified the weapons as traditional Sri Lankan Maha Thuwakku and found historical evidence suggesting that they were captured during the 1765 war between the Dutch East India Company (VOC) and the Kingdom of Kandy before being taken to the Netherlands.\n\nOne of the firearms also bears inscriptions associated with the King of Kandy, further supporting its historical connection to the island.\n\nThe two weapons share similarities with two historic firearms previously returned to Sri Lanka by the Netherlands in 2023.\n\nArrangements for the formal return of the two cultural heritage artefacts will be made in due course."
  },
