    "chunk_text": "Whither justice as UN marks solidarity day with Palestinians?\n\nA drawing shared by children in Gaza as part of the \u201cGaza We Want\u201d initiative launched by UNICEF.\n\nDiplomatic coaxing and arm-twisting reached fever pitch at the United Nations headquarters in New York in the days leading up to a crucial vote on November 29, 1947, to partition historic Palestine, a land inhabited by various peoples long before the biblical exodus of the Israelites from Egypt in\n\nWhile all the major post-World War II powers supported the partition of Palestine, some countries saw a colonial agenda behind the move. They were reluctant to back United Nations General Assembly Resolution 181, which was based on the majority recommendation of the United Nations Special Committee on Palestine (UNSCOP). There was no Arab voice, let alone a Palestinian one, on the committee. The United States, one of the principal proponents of the resolution, threatened countries such as Liberia and the Philippines with aid cuts if they failed to vote in its favour. The resolution was eventually adopted, with 33 countries voting in favour and 13 against. Ten countries abstained, while one was absent.\n\nSignificantly, the Soviet Union, then regarded as the citadel of socialism, also voted in favour of the resolution and did not view the partition of Palestine and the creation of Israel as a colonial project driven by an imperialist agenda. It was only much later that the Soviet Union came to support the Palestinians\u2019 struggle for self-determination, perhaps more within the context of the Cold War than out of any commitment to peace and justice.\n\nIn contrast, the Fourth International, led by Leon Trotsky\u2019s followers after their break with Joseph Stalin\u2019s brand of socialism, rejected the partition plan."
