    "md5": "17ba98935b83fa032dc201b7f475b1d7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "What happens when  agitators become  administrators?\n\nToday, the placards have been traded for ministerial portfolios, and the tune has changed entirely\n\nThere is an undeniable, almost poetic irony unfolding across Sri Lanka\u2019s public sector as trade unions mobilise over the national budget. As teachers, principals, doctors, university staff, and state administrators threaten to bring essential public services to a standstill, the government finds itself staring directly into a mirror of its own making.\n\nFor decades, the political forces now occupying the seats of power built their brand on the streets, mastering the art of relentless agitation. Now, seated behind desks with the burden of statecraft upon their shoulders, they are confronting a truth they once routinely dismissed: reality cannot be negotiated away at a picket line.\n\nWhen in opposition, the playbook was straightforward and politically lucrative. No union demand was ever deemed too steep, no fiscal constraint was accepted as a valid excuse, and every work stoppage\u2014regardless of its crippling impact on schools, hospitals, or the broader economy\u2014was championed as a righteous struggle against an uncaring establishment.\n\nEconomic orthodoxy was routinely derided as a heartless talking point; basic arithmetic was treated as an adversary of the working class. During years of economic turmoil and high inflation, promises were made with reckless abandon. Voters and state employees were led to believe that decades-old salary anomalies could be wiped out with the stroke of a pen that allowances could effortlessly match the rising cost of living, and that fiscal discipline was merely a pretext used by corrupt elites to deny workers their due.\n\nToday, the placards have been traded for ministerial portfolios, and the tune has changed entirely."
  },
