    "chunk_text": "Economic orthodoxy was routinely derided as a heartless talking point; basic arithmetic was treated as an adversary of the working class. During years of economic turmoil and high inflation, promises were made with reckless abandon. Voters and state employees were led to believe that decades-old salary anomalies could be wiped out with the stroke of a pen that allowances could effortlessly match the rising cost of living, and that fiscal discipline was merely a pretext used by corrupt elites to deny workers their due.\n\nToday, the placards have been traded for ministerial portfolios, and the tune has changed entirely.\n\nThis collision between past rhetoric and present reality exposes a profound crisis of political maturity\u2014one that implicates both the ruling administration and the trade union movement itself.\n\nFirst, the government is paying the inevitable price for populist overpromising. In politics, weaponising unrealistic expectations is an effective way to win elections, but it is a disastrous foundation for governance. By assuring public servants that relief was merely a matter of political will rather than economic capacity, the party boxed itself into an impossible corner. Now, even prudent, responsible fiscal management is viewed by its own core voter base not as statesmanship, but as betrayal. While an honest acknowledgment in Parliament that public sector salaries have still not caught up with the cost of living is welcome, candour after taking office does little to pacify unions that were explicitly told such hurdles did not exist."
