    "chunk_text": "First, the government is paying the inevitable price for populist overpromising. In politics, weaponising unrealistic expectations is an effective way to win elections, but it is a disastrous foundation for governance. By assuring public servants that relief was merely a matter of political will rather than economic capacity, the party boxed itself into an impossible corner. Now, even prudent, responsible fiscal management is viewed by its own core voter base not as statesmanship, but as betrayal. While an honest acknowledgment in Parliament that public sector salaries have still not caught up with the cost of living is welcome, candour after taking office does little to pacify unions that were explicitly told such hurdles did not exist.\n\nIf the state caves in to every sectoral demand simply to buy industrial peace, the resulting fiscal blowout will quickly erase whatever nominal salary gains workers secure on paper.\n\nGovernance is not an extension of an election campaign, and a national budget cannot be treated as a political wishlist. For Sri Lanka to break free from its recurring cycles of economic crisis and social unrest, both sides must grow into their responsibilities. The government must own the consequences of the expectations it stoked and public sector unions must recognise that economic reality is non-negotiable."
