    "md5": "2f1f42f1e5dc66095a825a3437f1ab63",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka celebrate double cricket success as both teams reach new heights\n\nNov.27 (Mirror Sports) -\u00a0Sri Lanka A stormed into the Asia Cup Rising Stars final after producing a remarkable batting display to defeat India A by eight wickets in the first semi-final in Doha.\n\nChasing a challenging target of 318, Sri Lanka A reached 318 for two in just 41.3 overs, with Lasith Croospulle and Sineth Jayawardena leading the charge with outstanding centuries.\n\nIndia A had earlier posted 317 for eight after being sent in to bat. Abhishek Sharma top-scored with 78 off 63 deliveries, while captain Tilak Varma made 53 off 66 balls. Shahbaz Ahmed added an unbeaten 54 from 48 deliveries to strengthen India's total.\n\nVaibhav Sooryavanshi also contributed 40, while Janith Liyanage, Nipun Dhananjaya and Ravindu Fernando claimed two wickets each for Sri Lanka A.\n\nSri Lanka A made an aggressive start to the chase, reaching 85 without loss in the first 10 overs as the opening pair put the Indian bowlers under immediate pressure.\n\nJayawardena smashed 105 off just 70 balls, including 10 fours and six sixes, while Croospulle produced an equally impressive 118 from 107 deliveries, with 12 fours and three sixes.\n\nTheir opening stand of 174 runs effectively put Sri Lanka A in control of the chase.\n\nFollowing Jayawardena's dismissal, Nishan Madushka maintained the momentum with an unbeaten 58 off 56 balls.\n\nCroospulle departed in the 36th over after taking Sri Lanka A beyond the 250-run mark, but captain Pavan Rathnayake ensured a comfortable finish with an unbeaten 31 off just 16 deliveries, including three sixes and a four.\n\nMadushka and Rathnayake added an unbeaten 53-run partnership to complete a dominant victory and book Sri Lanka A's place in the final.\n\nSri Lanka's women's team crowned a remarkable unbeaten campaign by defeating Ireland by six wickets in the final to win the ICC Women's Emerging Nations Trophy 2026 in Ajman, United Arab Emirates."
