    "chunk_text": "Madushka and Rathnayake added an unbeaten 53-run partnership to complete a dominant victory and book Sri Lanka A's place in the final.\n\nSri Lanka's women's team crowned a remarkable unbeaten campaign by defeating Ireland by six wickets in the final to win the ICC Women's Emerging Nations Trophy 2026 in Ajman, United Arab Emirates.\n\nStand-in captain Harshitha Samarawickrama praised the collective effort of her players, highlighting the importance of every squad member understanding and fulfilling their role throughout the competition.\n\nVishmi Gunaratne was one of Sri Lanka's standout performers, finishing as the tournament's leading run-scorer with 324 runs.\n\nSpinner Sathya Sandeepani also enjoyed an outstanding campaign, claiming 17 wickets to share the tournament's leading wicket-taker award with Ireland's Cara Murray.\n\nSandeepani played a decisive role in the final, taking four wickets for just 13 runs to earn the Player of the Match award and help Sri Lanka secure the trophy.\n\n\"I'm very happy about our team and I think this is a complete team effort,\" Samarawickrama said after the victory.\n\nShe also praised the players for their confidence and consistency, noting how well they had performed after understanding their individual responsibilities.\n\nFor Sandeepani, the tournament represented a significant personal achievement. Having previously played just one international match in 2020, she made the most of her opportunity after earning a call-up following impressive performances for Sri Lanka A.\n\nReflecting on her breakthrough campaign, Sandeepani credited her family and coaches for their support.\n\nDespite the defeat, Ireland captain Gaby Lewis acknowledged that her side had not performed to its potential in the final but remained positive about the lessons gained from the tournament.\n\nSri Lanka's title-winning campaign marks an impressive achievement for the women's team, who maintained their unbeaten record throughout the competition."
