    "md5": "85b3be6e27f7dee3a5ea49a80ee09326",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1994,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka secures first national GI certificate for Ceylon Cinnamon\n\nEDB receives landmark certification following EU recognition; industry targets $ 1 b exports by 2030\n\nAuthorities pursuing national GI branding for local products with export potential including Ceylon Tea, pepper, kithul, pineapple, king coconut, blue sapphire and Dumbara mats\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Ceylon Cinnamon industry has secured another milestone with the National Intellectual Property Office (NIPO) issuing the country\u2019s first Geographical Indication (GI) certificate, formally recognising the product\u2019s national identity and strengthening efforts to position it as a premium global export.\n\nThe certificate was presented to the Sri Lanka Export Development Board (EDB) yesterday, in the presence of Trade Minister Wasantha Samarasinghe and representatives of the public and private sectors, following nearly a decade of collaboration among stakeholders to secure international recognition for the country\u2019s signature spice.\n\nThe development builds on the European Union\u2019s (EU) recognition of Ceylon Cinnamon as a GI on 2 February 2022, making it the first Sri Lankan product to secure GI registration in the EU. The national certification is expected to reinforce protection of the product\u2019s geographical identity, distinguish authentic Sri Lankan cinnamon from substitutes and support its positioning in premium international markets.\n\nSri Lanka earned around $ 250 million from cinnamon exports in 2025, with the industry now targeting expansion into a $ 1 billion export sector by around 2030, supported by GI recognition, improved quality, value addition and wider market access.\n\nThe GI designation is intended to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s competitive advantage by ensuring that products marketed as Ceylon Cinnamon meet specified quality and geographical origin requirements. It is also expected to support higher prices, export diversification, increased production and employment across the value chain."
  },
