    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "The GI designation is intended to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s competitive advantage by ensuring that products marketed as Ceylon Cinnamon meet specified quality and geographical origin requirements. It is also expected to support higher prices, export diversification, increased production and employment across the value chain.\n\nThe achievement follows sustained efforts by the EDB, with technical assistance from the EU Delegation to Sri Lanka, the United Nations Industrial Development Organisation (UNIDO) and the World Trade Organization (WTO). Financial support was provided by the Netherlands-based Common Fund for Commodities, while the International Finance Corporation (IFC) supported promotional activities and industry training.\n\nThe EDB also worked with the Trade and Foreign Affairs Ministries, Department of Commerce, Department of Export Agriculture, NIPO, the Spice Council and the Spices and Allied Products Producers' and Traders' Association (SAPPTA) to secure the recognition, despite objections raised by competing countries before the European Commission.\n\nEstablished with effect from 1 September 2023, the Cinnamon Development Department is also tasked with elevating cinnamon from a relatively minor export crop into a major commercial plantation industry by improving production, quality and export performance.\n\nMeanwhile, authorities are pursuing national GI branding for other Sri Lankan products with export potential, including Ceylon Tea, pepper, kithul, pineapple, king coconut, blue sapphire and Dumbara mats, as part of broader efforts to strengthen the country\u2019s product identity and expand value-added exports.\n\nThose present at the certificate presentation included Ministry of Trade Secretary K.A. Vimalenthirarajah, NIPO Acting Director General Himali Hettihewage, EDB Chairman and CEO Mangala Wijesinghe and EDB Director General Erandika Dissanayake."
