    "md5": "306262ccf842d052069f5bd4adffbb89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1459,
    "chunk_text": "Budget 2027: Sri Lanka is running out of time. Trade agreements alone won\u2019t save the economy\n\nBudget 2027 has announced a committee to drive trade agreements across multiple countries\n\nWHILE Sri Lanka debates trade agreements, the rest of Asia is building the future. India is racing towards a $ 5 trillion economy. Vietnam exports more than $ 370 billion annually. Bangladesh's apparel exports now exceed $ 50 billion. ASEAN is becoming the centre of global manufacturing growth. Regional supply chains are being redrawn, investment is shifting across Asia, and countries are competing aggressively for technology, talent and export market share.\n\nSri Lanka, meanwhile, continues to debate whether it should embrace economic openness at all. That debate belongs to another era. As the country debates Budget 2027, the real question is not whether Sri Lanka needs more Free Trade Agreements (FTAs). It is whether Sri Lanka can become competitive enough to benefit from them before the opportunity passes. The greatest threat to the economy is no longer protectionism, inflation or even debt. It is irrelevant.\n\nThe Government points to encouraging economic indicators. Inflation has stabilised. Tourism has rebounded but stalled due to the Middle Eastern crisis. Remittances have improved significantly. Export earnings reached a record $ 17.25 billion in 2025, growing by 5.6%. Sri Lanka is poised to reach $ 20 billion this year. Which are positive signs."
  },
