    "md5": "72aa90f0a5097d18bc46bad8ab142abf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1227,
    "chunk_text": "The Government points to encouraging economic indicators. Inflation has stabilised. Tourism has rebounded but stalled due to the Middle Eastern crisis. Remittances have improved significantly. Export earnings reached a record $ 17.25 billion in 2025, growing by 5.6%. Sri Lanka is poised to reach $ 20 billion this year. Which are positive signs.\n\nFor years, Sri Lanka's economic debate has centred on whether FTAs are good or bad. The evidence from Asia suggests this is the wrong question. Vietnam did not become an export powerhouse because it signed trade agreements. It became successful because it simultaneously invested in industrial zones, logistics infrastructure, technical education, foreign investment attraction and export competitiveness.\n\nBangladesh did not become the world's second-largest apparel exporter because of preferential market access. It built factories, skills and scale. South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore did not become advanced economies because of trade liberalisation alone. They invested relentlessly in productivity, technology, innovation and human capital. Trade agreements created opportunities. Competitiveness converted opportunities into growth. Sri Lanka continues to confuse the two."
