    "md5": "b7e98c135d0cb34a94247ff1f07821fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "Bangladesh did not become the world's second-largest apparel exporter because of preferential market access. It built factories, skills and scale. South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore did not become advanced economies because of trade liberalisation alone. They invested relentlessly in productivity, technology, innovation and human capital. Trade agreements created opportunities. Competitiveness converted opportunities into growth. Sri Lanka continues to confuse the two.\n\nSri Lanka exports far less because it has not developed comparable industrial capabilities. The gap is not geographic. The gap is competitive. And it is widening. While India is investing billions of dollars through Make in India, Production Linked Incentive programs, logistics corridors, semiconductor manufacturing and digital infrastructure, Sri Lanka is still arguing about whether trade agreements themselves are beneficial. The rest of Asia settled that debate years ago.\n\nCritics frequently point to Sri Lanka's trade deficit with India as evidence that the Indo-Lanka FTA has failed. That interpretation is politically convenient but economically misleading. Nearly 90% of imports from major trading partners such as India and China occur outside FTA concessions, with importers paying full duties and choosing those products anyway because they remain competitive on price, quality and reliability. That single statistic reveals an uncomfortable reality.\n\nCompetitiveness is driving trade, not tariff concessions. Likewise, Sri Lanka's export challenges are not primarily caused by a lack of agreements. They are caused by: Low productivity, High production costs, Weak innovation, Limited economies of scale, Skills shortages, Inadequate industrial sophistication In other words, Sri Lanka has a competitiveness problem disguised as a trade policy problem."
