    "chunk_size_bytes": 1352,
    "chunk_text": "Competitiveness is driving trade, not tariff concessions. Likewise, Sri Lanka's export challenges are not primarily caused by a lack of agreements. They are caused by: Low productivity, High production costs, Weak innovation, Limited economies of scale, Skills shortages, Inadequate industrial sophistication In other words, Sri Lanka has a competitiveness problem disguised as a trade policy problem.\n\nAny economy whose export performance can be significantly disrupted by a policy decision in a single market has failed to diversify sufficiently. Sri Lanka's dependence on a limited number of products and destinations is becoming a strategic economic risk. Diversification is no longer desirable. It is urgent.\n\nThe world economy is increasingly centred in Asia. The RCEP bloc accounts for roughly 30% of global GDP and trade. East Asia's middle class expanded from approximately 834 million consumers in 2016 to more than 1.1 billion by 2022. By 2030, one in every three middle-class consumers globally is expected to live in East Asia. This is where future demand will come from. This is where future investment will flow. This is where future supply chains will be built, just like what we saw in the recent Nvidia debacle. The critical question is whether Sri Lanka will be part of that growth story. Or whether it will watch from the sidelines."
