    "chunk_text": "The world economy is increasingly centred in Asia. The RCEP bloc accounts for roughly 30% of global GDP and trade. East Asia's middle class expanded from approximately 834 million consumers in 2016 to more than 1.1 billion by 2022. By 2030, one in every three middle-class consumers globally is expected to live in East Asia. This is where future demand will come from. This is where future investment will flow. This is where future supply chains will be built, just like what we saw in the recent Nvidia debacle. The critical question is whether Sri Lanka will be part of that growth story. Or whether it will watch from the sidelines.\n\nThe debate is no longer between protectionism and liberalisation. That is yesterday's argument. The real choice facing Sri Lanka is between competitiveness and irrelevance. Trade agreements can open doors. They cannot create productive factories. They cannot build innovative companies. They cannot develop skilled workers. They cannot substitute for economic reform. Only competitiveness can do that. And that is why Budget 2027 matters.\n\nThe window for reform is still open. But it is narrowing quickly as India, Vietnam, Indonesia and other Asian economies accelerate ahead. Sri Lanka's challenge is no longer whether it should join the race. The challenge is that the race has already started. The longer the country delays, the harder it will become to catch up. Budget 2027 must therefore be more than a fiscal statement. It must be a competitive Budget. Otherwise, Sri Lanka risks becoming a spectator in Asia's economic rise rather than a participant in it.\n\n(The author is the only Sri Lankan to have been the Chairman of the Sri Lanka Export Development Board, and Sri Lanka Tourism and to be appointed as the Commissioner General of World Expo - Milan, Italy. Currently, he serves as the World Rural Tourism Council (WRTC) Vice Chairman. He is an alumnus of Harvard University)."
