    "md5": "d77fe9114bb182ed0a4cac451d43a5c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1107,
    "chunk_text": "Five reasons Sri Lanka should go for another IMF program\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake chairs a meeting with an IMF delegation during its visit to Colombo in July 2026\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery in terms of macroeconomic indicators has been a remarkable story of success and resilience. From a nation posting negative growth of around 7%, inflation at nearly 70% and market interest rates at over 30%, Sri Lanka has largely stabilised thanks to its fiscal policies and the IMF. But currently Sri Lanka is facing external shocks such as the Mideast war, Russia-Ukraine war, rising global interest rates, El Nino and trade pattern changes globally. The most devastating impact has been the Mideast war, which is leading to a high fuel import bill, severely hampering Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to build gross reserves.\n\nBut as Sri Lanka\u2019s reserves are not meeting projections, global interest rates are high, the IMF program is coming to an end and debt repayment levels are increasing from 2028, a critical question is, \u201cShould Sri Lanka go for another IMF program\u201d? Let me give the case for it."
  },
