    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "55118d3a9f21ac2406da337fd1a0bd5d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1479,
    "chunk_text": "But as Sri Lanka\u2019s reserves are not meeting projections, global interest rates are high, the IMF program is coming to an end and debt repayment levels are increasing from 2028, a critical question is, \u201cShould Sri Lanka go for another IMF program\u201d? Let me give the case for it.\n\nMany people in Sri Lanka dismiss the IMF as saying it\u2019s just $3 billion and if we can get an investment of such a figure, we would not need the IMF. I have explained this many times before, the IMF is much more than the $3 billion. The IMF is like a verification for the Sri Lankan economy. It is like an auditor where investors, international bond markets, bilaterals and other multilaterals trust the IMF to move forward with a country in most cases. At this juncture especially when Sri Lanka is facing turbulence due to external shocks, having the IMF on board is much better.\n\nAs US 10 year Treasury yields hit 5.35% in October this year, borrowing costs are currently high due to high debt levels in developed countries, increasing inflation and financing being diverted to AI related spending. This will also mean that IMF borrowing costs also get higher but borrowing from the IMF is cheaper than issuing international sovereign bonds. Rather than issue a sovereign bond next year, 2027 (as projected by the IMF), Sri Lanka could borrow from the IMF. The other benefit is that borrowing from the IMF comes with technical assistance and IMF reviews of the economy at least twice a year."
  },
