    "chunk_size_bytes": 1397,
    "chunk_text": "As US 10 year Treasury yields hit 5.35% in October this year, borrowing costs are currently high due to high debt levels in developed countries, increasing inflation and financing being diverted to AI related spending. This will also mean that IMF borrowing costs also get higher but borrowing from the IMF is cheaper than issuing international sovereign bonds. Rather than issue a sovereign bond next year, 2027 (as projected by the IMF), Sri Lanka could borrow from the IMF. The other benefit is that borrowing from the IMF comes with technical assistance and IMF reviews of the economy at least twice a year.\n\nTable 1 shows how much we have to pay back the IMF each year from 2026-2030.\n\nAs seen in Table 1, Sri Lanka will be paying $2.194 billion back to the IMF from 2026 to 2030. There is a saying which goes as \u201cBorrowing from Peter to pay Paul\u201d. I am saying, \u201cLet us borrow from Peter to pay back Peter\u201d. In the next 4 years (2027-2030), Sri Lanka is expected to pay back the IMF a sum of $1.848 billion. Another program can help neutralise this to some extent. This is not something new. Sri Lanka keeps borrowing from the World Bank and at the same time, Sri Lanka is paying back the World Bank for past loans. We can do a similar process with the IMF.\n\n(The author is a Sri Lankan Economist. The views expressed in this article are strictly the author\u2019s own personal opinions)"
