    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "Budget blues\n\nThe national Budget for 2027 which was announced by President and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake in a stoic speech in parliament, was met with contrasting responses. The essence of a national Budget can be distilled by observing the reactions it provokes as much as by the numbers attached to Budget lines and the rhetoric in the Budget speech.\n\nEconomists, business leaders, professional bodies, think tanks and rating agencies were quick to endorse the economic policies crafted by the National People\u2019s Power (NPP). Their commentaries mainly raised concerns about the implementation of the policies and urged the Government to reform the public sector, which they saw as being the main obstacle to progress.\n\nApart from some reservations voiced about the dependence on a volatile market to deliver on investments and export-oriented growth, the ideological consensus between NPP\u2019s Budget for 2027 and business leaders was apparent. The Opposition parties in parliament were disarmed by the convergence of policy interests across the aisles to effectively challenge its contents.\n\nIn contrast, the usual buzz around Budget announcements was largely absent among ordinary Sri Lankans. Households who were hopeful about receiving some relief on taxes of essential items, tariffs on basic utilities and household debt were disappointed.\n\nSri Lanka is now ranked one of the lowest in terms of minimum wage when adjusted for purchasing power. Year-on-year headline inflation has increased to above 8% pinching on household Budgets that had already suffered due to significant price adjustments and wage repression in the aftermath of the economic crisis, the burden of which is disproportionately borne by women. Addressing the cost-of-living pressures should have been an obvious policy priority in the 2027 Budget."
  },
