    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka is now ranked one of the lowest in terms of minimum wage when adjusted for purchasing power. Year-on-year headline inflation has increased to above 8% pinching on household Budgets that had already suffered due to significant price adjustments and wage repression in the aftermath of the economic crisis, the burden of which is disproportionately borne by women. Addressing the cost-of-living pressures should have been an obvious policy priority in the 2027 Budget.\n\nThe Government, along with the business and economic experts, have repeatedly highlighted the 2027 Budget as hailing the transformation from, \u201ceconomic stability to economic growth.\u201d However, economic stability cannot be judged by fiscal discipline or macro data alone. The Budget does not convey a clear plan for how foreign debt and reserves will be managed in the future. Therefore, it is not possible to assess the soundness of the Government\u2019s promise of macro-economic stability.\n\nAny mention of achieving economic stability rings hollow in the ears of the working people as their lives have been destabilised by harsh austerity policies continued by the NPP Government, climate disasters and volatility caused by the wars abroad. The recent release of preliminary data from HIES reveal a troubling picture of poverty and households mired in debt.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s debt sustainability agreement negotiated by the previous Ranil Wickremesinghe Government and accepted by the NPP without renegotiations includes macro-linked bonds which is determined by economic growth rates. Higher economic growth in the consequent years will only increase the country\u2019s debt burden rather than result in benefits for the people. Furthermore, economic growth alone will not result in redistribution without economic policies to facilitate it. Budget 2027 does not include a vision for redistribution."
  },
