    "md5": "b85c65548438aebeb9c84ee79aa6733b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s debt sustainability agreement negotiated by the previous Ranil Wickremesinghe Government and accepted by the NPP without renegotiations includes macro-linked bonds which is determined by economic growth rates. Higher economic growth in the consequent years will only increase the country\u2019s debt burden rather than result in benefits for the people. Furthermore, economic growth alone will not result in redistribution without economic policies to facilitate it. Budget 2027 does not include a vision for redistribution.\n\nIn a preemptive special statement before the Budget announcement, the Deputy Minister of Finance Anil Jayantha Fernando reportedly urged the public to evaluate the Budget on its broader economic direction rather than on welfare benefits or tax concessions.\n\nThe Budget is thin on welfare benefits, with a mere 0.6% of GDP allocation, sufficient to cover only a fraction of the most vulnerable sections of our society \u2013 low-income families, disabled, those with chronic illness and the elderly, while leaving many households out.\n\nBudget 2027 provides no clear vision for how the NPP Government hopes to address poverty or growing inequalities. Although the poverty level has increased to what it was 10 years ago, the Budgetary amounts for low-income families have remained unchanged.\n\nThe Budget fails to adequately provision for extremities caused by the El Nino effect on the country\u2019s fragile food system, livelihoods, health and education, even though the main factor contributing to why half the population are multidimensionally vulnerable is the inability to withstand climate events (UNDP).\n\nEducation and health sectors are given priority in the expenditure heads, however, the low GDP allocations of 1.91% and 1.87% respectively, relegates Sri Lanka to one of the lowest spending countries for these sectors."
  },
