    "chunk_size_bytes": 1682,
    "chunk_text": "Education and health sectors are given priority in the expenditure heads, however, the low GDP allocations of 1.91% and 1.87% respectively, relegates Sri Lanka to one of the lowest spending countries for these sectors.\n\nApart from continuing housing projects and Rs. 200 allowance which is riddled with implementation challenges, no substantial provisions to address the neglect and exploitation of the plantation workers are included in the Budget.\n\nThe percentage (43%) of unemployment among educated youth which is the highest in the region is alarming (IMF Asia Pacific Outlook April 2026). Yet the Budget does not propose a clear plan for job creation, including to absorb the job losses from a flailing IT industry. Meanwhile, in a questionable proposal, 18-year-olds are being lured into investing in risky capital markets by providing share portfolios.\n\nIn terms of tax concessions, the relief provided to income (Rs. 16 billion) and import taxes (Rs. 10 billion) is large compared to the relief on Value Added Tax of only Rs. 2.9 billion. A significant portion of the public and private sector workers\u2019 monthly salaries fall below Rs. 100 000. Although gradual Budgetary increases on salaries were granted over three years, discrepancies exist in public sector salary scales.\n\nThe longstanding salary issues of the biggest group of the public sector cadre, of 225 000 professionals in teachers\u2019 service, remains unresolved. Teacher unions have now called for a token strike if the Government does not agree to grant the remaining two-thirds of the salary adjustments promised according to the Subodini Report and in the interim a cost-of-living allowance of Rs. 25 000."
