    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "Concerns on civil liberties, digitalisation, and AI\n\nJVP, at the core of this Government, knows better than any party in this country what emergency and counter-terror powers did\n\nA Government legislating in the shadow can easily stop distinguishing between securing the State and surveilling its citizens\n\nDr.Sanjana Hattotuwa, who researches social media, artificial intelligence and their implications for human rights, political communication and democratic integrity, in this interview highlights concerns on civil liberties, digitisation and Artificial Intelligence (AI) arising from several new legislations introduced by the Government. Here are excerpts;\n\nQ: You are concerned of a \u2018gap\u2019 that exists with what you refer to as the manner in which each of the proposed laws comes under a \u2018reasonable-sounding\u2019 name such as online safety, anti-corruption and efficient public services, leaving room for opposition to them on their own terms. How serious a concern is this to the civil liberties thus exercised in the country?\n\nA: It\u2019s among the most serious concerns I have, because the name of a law settles public opinion about it long before anyone has read, let alone tested in court, what its clauses actually permit. Online safety, anti-corruption, the recovery of stolen assets, data protection, cybersecurity, counter-terrorism, organised crime and efficient public services are each very hard to argue against, and in a country that went bankrupt in 2022, whose public money was looted for decades and which lived through the Easter Sunday attacks in 2019, they are meant to be. Each instrument then invites its own consultation and its own critique, one at a time, so that lawyers take on the Online Safety Act, NGOs the NGO Bill, a handful of us the cybersecurity draft, and economists cheer digitalisation, and while every one of these responses is accurate, each deals with a single law in isolation."
