    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "A: It\u2019s among the most serious concerns I have, because the name of a law settles public opinion about it long before anyone has read, let alone tested in court, what its clauses actually permit. Online safety, anti-corruption, the recovery of stolen assets, data protection, cybersecurity, counter-terrorism, organised crime and efficient public services are each very hard to argue against, and in a country that went bankrupt in 2022, whose public money was looted for decades and which lived through the Easter Sunday attacks in 2019, they are meant to be. Each instrument then invites its own consultation and its own critique, one at a time, so that lawyers take on the Online Safety Act, NGOs the NGO Bill, a handful of us the cybersecurity draft, and economists cheer digitalisation, and while every one of these responses is accurate, each deals with a single law in isolation.\n\nOpposing them on their own terms is hard precisely because those terms were chosen well, since nobody wants to be seen defending corruption, terrorism or organised crime, and so the critic is cast as an apologist for all three while the conversation never reaches the clause that lets a Deputy Inspector General obtain your call records and phone tower locations without a court order, or the one that makes failing to inform on a friend punishable by up to seven years in prison. Parliament\u2019s own record bears this out, since in a study of 173 Hansards from January 2025 to September 2026 I found MPs arguing over cost, procurement and implementation, while human rights impact assessment, remedy and safeguards against surveillance barely featured at all. If those elected to scrutinise these laws debate the labels, it\u2019s hardly surprising that citizens do too.\n\nQ: In effect the laws that have been under the NPP Government\u2019s express agenda threaten various aspects of basic rights such as speech, funding and also a right to organise?"
