    "chunk_size_bytes": 1632,
    "chunk_text": "Opposing them on their own terms is hard precisely because those terms were chosen well, since nobody wants to be seen defending corruption, terrorism or organised crime, and so the critic is cast as an apologist for all three while the conversation never reaches the clause that lets a Deputy Inspector General obtain your call records and phone tower locations without a court order, or the one that makes failing to inform on a friend punishable by up to seven years in prison. Parliament\u2019s own record bears this out, since in a study of 173 Hansards from January 2025 to September 2026 I found MPs arguing over cost, procurement and implementation, while human rights impact assessment, remedy and safeguards against surveillance barely featured at all. If those elected to scrutinise these laws debate the labels, it\u2019s hardly surprising that citizens do too.\n\nQ: In effect the laws that have been under the NPP Government\u2019s express agenda threaten various aspects of basic rights such as speech, funding and also a right to organise?\n\nOn speech, the Online Safety Act\u2019s section 12 still makes a \u201cfalse statement\u201d that threatens national security, public health or public order punishable by up to 5 years in prison, with a Commission the President appoints deciding what is false before any court does. The Act remains unrepealed and unamended despite repeated promises, its first prosecution concluded in April 2026, and it isn\u2019t the state\u2019s alone, since in April 2025 a private Bank obtained orders under it against websites and named Facebook, LinkedIn and X accounts discussing the bank\u2019s own data breach."
