    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "On speech, the Online Safety Act\u2019s section 12 still makes a \u201cfalse statement\u201d that threatens national security, public health or public order punishable by up to 5 years in prison, with a Commission the President appoints deciding what is false before any court does. The Act remains unrepealed and unamended despite repeated promises, its first prosecution concluded in April 2026, and it isn\u2019t the state\u2019s alone, since in April 2025 a private Bank obtained orders under it against websites and named Facebook, LinkedIn and X accounts discussing the bank\u2019s own data breach.\n\nOn funding, the quieter architecture of delay, disclosure and dependency already does more damage than any spyware. A survey of one hundred organisations recorded 69% facing difficulties accessing foreign funding, 50% struggling to open bank accounts, 57% reporting frequent banking problems and 21% postponing activities outright, and that is before the Anti-Corruption and Proceeds of Crime Acts, which allow accounts to be frozen and banking, phone and device records obtained on ex parte orders, are turned on a politically visible organisation.\n\nOn organising, the draft NGO law would catch any group of five or more people formed for non-profit purposes, make non-registration itself an offence, allow suspension without a judicial order on undefined grounds of sovereignty and national security, and have registration lapse every three years, while the POC Bill treats three people acting together as a possible criminal group and lets an undefined \u201csuspicious association\u201d count towards membership.\n\nThe chilling effect of all this begins well before anyone is charged, and that is why it matters so much now, while the architecture is still being built.\n\nQ: You are also concerned about the Government\u2019s creation of a national digital identity system with a central population register and a national AI strategy. What given the country\u2019s chequered political history raises serious concerns in you?"
