    "md5": "adde4e48a08c0f5a2be5b8ba49c05911",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1789,
    "chunk_text": "Q: You are also concerned about the Government\u2019s creation of a national digital identity system with a central population register and a national AI strategy. What given the country\u2019s chequered political history raises serious concerns in you?\n\nBeyond the horror, I keep returning to the instrument, a paper register compiled for an unrelated purpose, held by the state and turned to targeting within days. Paper imposed friction, since a pogrom needed people to cross-reference lists by hand. A central digital identity resolves the same query in milliseconds, from anywhere, for every home at once, and it can run again and again. The mobs of 1983 needed no computer to act on their intent, though a digital register would have removed the friction that limited how far that intent could reach.\n\nConsider what is being built. The Sri Lanka Unique Digital Identity, funded by a grant and built on face, fingerprint and iris biometrics, is due to issue its first identities by the end of this year, alongside a national data exchange whose \u201cAsk Once\u201d principle means what you give one agency becomes available to others. The Department for Registration of Persons describes its central population register as one that supports national security and exchanges information with other organisations, which is its own language and not mine.\n\nGovTech\u2019s chief executive has spoken of biometric registration at birth and a country that is paperless, cashless and presence-less, which to me reads like a blueprint for what I\u2019ve called surveillance by default, if not by design. Over Rs. 30 billion in the 2026 Budget went to biometric identification, revenue tracking, immigration, police data systems and financial transaction monitoring without any scrutiny of who controls the data."
