    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "GovTech\u2019s chief executive has spoken of biometric registration at birth and a country that is paperless, cashless and presence-less, which to me reads like a blueprint for what I\u2019ve called surveillance by default, if not by design. Over Rs. 30 billion in the 2026 Budget went to biometric identification, revenue tracking, immigration, police data systems and financial transaction monitoring without any scrutiny of who controls the data.\n\nNot a single document, page or slide explaining the program exists in Sinhala or Tamil, which makes Sri Lanka, to my knowledge, the only country digitising this comprehensively without addressing most of its own citizens in their own languages. I\u2019ve called it skyscrapers on a quagmire.\n\nGiven our history, negligence of this kind is dangerous, since the PTA, enacted in 1979 as a temporary measure, was used for decades against Tamils, Muslims, journalists and dissenters. Pandemic contact tracing saw military intelligence reach into the phone records of patients, social media was blocked in 2018 and 2019, and in August 2025 the Counter Terrorism Investigation Department summoned the president of the Mullaitivu Press Club over posts documenting excavation at the Chemmani mass grave. Our institutions have never distributed suspicion equally, and it\u2019s silly to believe that digital systems designed and run by the same institutions will magically defend rights.\n\nQ: It is fact that no one has seen the Cyber Security Bill the 3rd draft of which is with the AGs department. Why do you think the Government is functioning with such secrecy?\n\nA: The secrecy around the Cybersecurity Bill fits a pattern. The Deputy Minister of Digital Economy told Parliament that a third draft went to the Attorney General in October and should become law in early 2027, yet the last public version dates from July 2023, and no one outside Government (and, I suspect, few within it) has read what replaced it."
