    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "A: The secrecy around the Cybersecurity Bill fits a pattern. The Deputy Minister of Digital Economy told Parliament that a third draft went to the Attorney General in October and should become law in early 2027, yet the last public version dates from July 2023, and no one outside Government (and, I suspect, few within it) has read what replaced it.\n\nSecrecy also carries a procedural cost, since, as with the Online Safety Act, citizens may first read the Bill only once it is gazetted, with a very short window to challenge it in the Supreme Court, and nothing at all once it is law. Sri Lanka needs a cybersecurity law, and an authority able to make institutions fix what they are told to fix. However, a Bill nobody outside Government has read can\u2019t claim public consent, and a Government elected on the promise of system change should find that argument easy to accept.\n\nQ: Can you draw comparisons of other global scenarios where such a wide-reaching surveillance mechanism through such a series of laws passed without any consultation from the rest of society has led to the concerns that you speak of coming true?\n\nA: I would start closer to home, and with the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), which was passed in 1979 as a temporary law, with little debate, to deal with a specific threat. It stayed on the books for over four decades, was used against Tamils, Muslims, journalists and dissenters far beyond anything its sponsors claimed, and has drawn repeated UN calls for repeal ever since. Section 3 of the ICCPR Act of 2007, a law meant to protect rights, was later turned against the writer Shakthika Sathkumara, the social media commentator Ramzy Razeek and the comedian Natasha Edirisooriya. Sri Lanka doesn\u2019t need to look abroad, or to speculate, to know what happens when broad powers are granted on the promise of narrow use."
