    "chunk_text": "A: I would start closer to home, and with the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), which was passed in 1979 as a temporary law, with little debate, to deal with a specific threat. It stayed on the books for over four decades, was used against Tamils, Muslims, journalists and dissenters far beyond anything its sponsors claimed, and has drawn repeated UN calls for repeal ever since. Section 3 of the ICCPR Act of 2007, a law meant to protect rights, was later turned against the writer Shakthika Sathkumara, the social media commentator Ramzy Razeek and the comedian Natasha Edirisooriya. Sri Lanka doesn\u2019t need to look abroad, or to speculate, to know what happens when broad powers are granted on the promise of narrow use.\n\nIn each of these cases, harm arrived through ordinary administration, and fell first on those with the least power to contest it. Sri Lanka\u2019s case is more dangerous still, because these instruments arrive together, are routed to a single office, the godlike Executive Presidency, and enter a country with a documented history of violently turning civic records against its own minorities.\n\nQ: What meaningful safeguards should the Government secure before moving to implement such laws in the country?"
