    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "In each of these cases, harm arrived through ordinary administration, and fell first on those with the least power to contest it. Sri Lanka\u2019s case is more dangerous still, because these instruments arrive together, are routed to a single office, the godlike Executive Presidency, and enter a country with a documented history of violently turning civic records against its own minorities.\n\nQ: What meaningful safeguards should the Government secure before moving to implement such laws in the country?\n\nThe single correction that would do most work across every one of these laws is to require a judge\u2019s approval before any intrusion takes place. Every device search and data request, including the call, subscriber and location data a Deputy Inspector General can obtain under the POC Bill without one, should need a judicial order naming the account, device, dates and type of data sought. Independent reviewers should filter journalistic, legal, medical, survivor and research material out of what investigators ever see, the person affected should be told once an investigation no longer requires secrecy, and the state should publish annual figures on applications, approvals, refusals and urgent actions, which would compromise no investigation at all.\n\nThe triggers for these powers need narrowing. The POC Bill\u2019s power to compel any provider of \u201clocking or encryption services\u201d to unlock what it holds should go, with partners saying plainly that they will not support measures that weaken encryption for everyone. The Online Safety Act\u2019s false statement offence should be repealed now, rather than await a replacement that has yet to appear. The PSTA\u2019s offence on information about police and military activity should be confined to genuinely secret material whose disclosure causes demonstrable harm, and both the PSTA and POC Bills need an express saving clause for journalism, research, satire, election observation, industrial action, protest and peaceful advocacy."
