    "chunk_text": "The triggers for these powers need narrowing. The POC Bill\u2019s power to compel any provider of \u201clocking or encryption services\u201d to unlock what it holds should go, with partners saying plainly that they will not support measures that weaken encryption for everyone. The Online Safety Act\u2019s false statement offence should be repealed now, rather than await a replacement that has yet to appear. The PSTA\u2019s offence on information about police and military activity should be confined to genuinely secret material whose disclosure causes demonstrable harm, and both the PSTA and POC Bills need an express saving clause for journalism, research, satire, election observation, industrial action, protest and peaceful advocacy.\n\nThe World Bank, India, UNDP and the European Union should make an independent human rights impact assessment, trilingual publication and bars on function creep conditions of disbursement, with a standing power to pause finance when safeguards fail\n\nQ: You are especially concerned that the Anti-Corruption Act and Proceeds of Crime Act, allows for access to a citizen\u2019s financial records without his knowledge. How crucial a concern is this? And what safeguards do you believe should be in place before such laws are in place?\n\nA: I want to be clear at the outset, since this is often misread, that nothing I\u2019ve written argues against investigating corruption or recovering stolen assets, which this country badly needs, and the Anti-Corruption Act had strong civil society backing for good reason. Neither Act is an NGO surveillance law, and describing them that way would be wrong. My concern lies in what the two Acts authorise along the way, and in who else is swept up."
