    "md5": "3eaf9aa17d8253d45b99f38caf4d1bae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "A: I want to be clear at the outset, since this is often misread, that nothing I\u2019ve written argues against investigating corruption or recovering stolen assets, which this country badly needs, and the Anti-Corruption Act had strong civil society backing for good reason. Neither Act is an NGO surveillance law, and describing them that way would be wrong. My concern lies in what the two Acts authorise along the way, and in who else is swept up.\n\nUnder the Anti-Corruption Act, banks, the Inland Revenue Department and the Registrar of Companies must hand over account histories, transaction details and tax records on demand, telecommunications companies and internet providers can be required to hand over subscriber and usage data without a judicial warrant, section 59 lets authorities order any provider of encryption services to unlock or decrypt, and section 161 declares that the Act overrides any conflicting law.\n\nThe Proceeds of Crime Act lets police demand passwords and force the unlocking of devices, defines a \u201cdocument\u201d to cover virtually any electronic data, and makes it a crime to tip someone off that they are under investigation. Late last year a police spokesperson confirmed on video that the police now have legal authority to tap phones, including WhatsApp, and to extract data forensically from seized handsets.\n\nIt matters a great deal, because financial investigation by its very nature maps relationships. Provider records, transfers and device contents reveal the sources, colleagues, donors, family members and partner organisations around whoever is under investigation, so a journalist\u2019s confidential source can be identified from call records without anyone reading a single article, and people never suspected of anything find their privacy gone because they once sent money to, or called, someone who is."
