    "chunk_size_bytes": 1528,
    "chunk_text": "It matters a great deal, because financial investigation by its very nature maps relationships. Provider records, transfers and device contents reveal the sources, colleagues, donors, family members and partner organisations around whoever is under investigation, so a journalist\u2019s confidential source can be identified from call records without anyone reading a single article, and people never suspected of anything find their privacy gone because they once sent money to, or called, someone who is.\n\nQ: You expressed serious concerns that the draft\u2019s failure to address input on AI-enabled surveillance and repression or discrimination and the algorithmic bias that would deepen it or more importantly the lack of accountability if and when the system failed a \u2018performative consultation\u2019?\n\nA: Five working days were bad enough, though the consultation was performative above all because of what it was set up to hear. A document that would shape how AI enters tax, health, education, policing and public administration was put out in English alone, which guaranteed that the people most likely to be harmed would never read it, let alone respond, and its silences on surveillance, discrimination and redress were exactly the ones critical input would have exposed. Those omissions matter more now than they did in 2024, because a framework that ignores surveillance, bias and accountability at the outset is implemented without them, and the burden of retrofitting safeguards then falls on the people already harmed."
