    "chunk_text": "A: Five working days were bad enough, though the consultation was performative above all because of what it was set up to hear. A document that would shape how AI enters tax, health, education, policing and public administration was put out in English alone, which guaranteed that the people most likely to be harmed would never read it, let alone respond, and its silences on surveillance, discrimination and redress were exactly the ones critical input would have exposed. Those omissions matter more now than they did in 2024, because a framework that ignores surveillance, bias and accountability at the outset is implemented without them, and the burden of retrofitting safeguards then falls on the people already harmed.\n\nIn February 2026, the Public Security Ministry described Police use of facial recognition and telephone-data analysis at Katunayake airport without any account of authorisation, accuracy, error rates, retention or how a mistaken identification could be challenged. The PDPA\u2019s protection against automated decisions applies only to decisions made solely by a machine with an irreversible and continuous impact, which leaves out the far more common case of an official rubber-stamping an algorithm\u2019s recommendation they may not understand."
