    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3db80652b7718fdcc768b46a7814c604",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1621,
    "chunk_text": "Beneficial Ownership registration:  When compliance becomes a burden on businesses\n\nDelays, repeated visits, technical difficulties and heavy penalties are making it hard for companies to comply while they wait for Government approval\n\n\u200bA few days ago, while sitting in a coffee shop, I heard an advertisement promoting its online ordering app. \u201cGrab it and Go\u201d, it went on \u201c, Don\u2019t wait in line. Join online and walk away with your order,\u201d it proclaimed. I smiled at the clever message, but it made me think about our own experience with online systems introduced by regulatory institutions.\n\nIn many cases, going online doesn\u2019t eliminate queues. We struggle through complicated digital procedures, only to end up visiting the relevant offices and standing in physical queues to complete the process.\n\nIt seems we have perfected a new form of digital administration: First, join the queue online, and then join it again in person.\n\nThis is a concern worth examining as Sri Lanka moves towards mandatory digital compliance, particularly with the introduction of Beneficial Ownership registration.\n\nBeneficial Ownership (BO) registration, introduced by the Registrar of Companies (ROC), is an important step towards transparency and accountability in the corporate sector. Knowing who ultimately owns or controls a company supports good governance and helps prevent companies from being misused for illegal activities, such as conmen hiding behind corporate structures.\n\nThe other main aspect is identifying an individual as a beneficial owner who is not a shareholder legally recognised as a natural person."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-27-dailyftlk-9b209bed",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9b209bed",
    "date_str": "2026-11-27",
    "description": "Beneficial Ownership registration:  When compliance becomes a burden on businesses",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beneficial-Ownership-registration-When-compliance-becomes-a-burden-on-businesses/4-800663",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795742939.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-27-dailyftlk-9b209bed-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a4f117d09cb99249dfbfeab450eb8627",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "Beneficial Ownership (BO) registration, introduced by the Registrar of Companies (ROC), is an important step towards transparency and accountability in the corporate sector. Knowing who ultimately owns or controls a company supports good governance and helps prevent companies from being misused for illegal activities, such as conmen hiding behind corporate structures.\n\nThe other main aspect is identifying an individual as a beneficial owner who is not a shareholder legally recognised as a natural person.\n\nOn 28 October, the Registrar issued a notice described as a final warning on BO compliance. It states that non-compliance could attract a fine of Rs. 1 million or imprisonment for up to 10 years.\n\nNobody argues that companies may ignore their legal responsibilities. But an authority that threatens such penalties must also provide a system through which those responsibilities can be fulfilled. Companies and their secretaries are still struggling with the online system, waiting for approvals and making repeated visits for help.\n\nThe system also assumes a level of digital skill that many directors may not have. A Government survey has reportedly found that only 38% of the population can use a computer independently, and many of them are schoolchildren and young adults. Directors from older generations and small businesses often need professional help, which adds cost.\n\nThe most unfair consequence is financial. Companies must file Annual Returns and other statutory documents within set periods, and late filing brings fines. Yet a company secretary cannot proceed with the Annual Return until the BO registration is approved. A company that uploads everything on time can still miss its deadline because the Registrar\u2019s process is incomplete, and penalties begin to accumulate."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-27-dailyftlk-9b209bed",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9b209bed",
    "date_str": "2026-11-27",
    "description": "Beneficial Ownership registration:  When compliance becomes a burden on businesses",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Beneficial-Ownership-registration-When-compliance-becomes-a-burden-on-businesses/4-800663",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
