    "language": "en",
    "md5": "252c574fea285a1cc2fb299fb0783f73",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "The most unfair consequence is financial. Companies must file Annual Returns and other statutory documents within set periods, and late filing brings fines. Yet a company secretary cannot proceed with the Annual Return until the BO registration is approved. A company that uploads everything on time can still miss its deadline because the Registrar\u2019s process is incomplete, and penalties begin to accumulate.\n\nnWaive or extend deadlines:\u00a0 If the Registrar\u2019s office cannot process applications in time, the Government should announce a temporary waiver or extension for companies that have submitted their documents online.\n\nIt is unreasonable to expect companies to pay penalties for delays beyond their control. Compliance must be a two-way responsibility.\n\nDocuments are uploaded online, but the process does not move automatically. A first-level officer must check and approve them, then pass them to a second level, and then a third, before any further action can be taken.\n\nOfficers are understood to have around 35,000 files to attend to. That may not be the number of pending files, but it shows the scale of the workload. If the system depends on a limited number of officers to examine every submission, delays are inevitable.\n\nProfessionals who have uploaded all the required documents still report visiting the Registrar\u2019s office repeatedly to follow up, sometimes as many as five times. Regularly visiting government offices helps build rapport with officials, making them more sympathetic and willing to help move matters forward more quickly. Whether that is true or not, a statutory service should work through a clear, transparent process that treats every applicant the same.\n\nMoving Government services online should save time and money for everyone. With the BO website, professionals report the opposite, and several IT professionals who have examined it have questioned its design and user-friendliness."
  },
  {
