    "md5": "8d3b3a690dc973acfe47ac03be713be9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "Moving Government services online should save time and money for everyone. With the BO website, professionals report the opposite, and several IT professionals who have examined it have questioned its design and user-friendliness.\n\nMeaningful error messages. When a document is rejected, the applicant should be told exactly what is wrong and how to correct it.\n\nVisible status. When an application is pending, the user should be able to see which officer or level is holding it, and why.\n\nThe ability to save and return. Company secretaries report that the site doesn\u2019t appear to let users save a partially completed application. Even a small change to one field may mean re-entering everything. When there is more than one BO and one person needs a correction, all records must be changed and re-entered. Why should a one-word correction mean hours of repeated work?\n\nReal human support. Telephone and online help should connect users with people who can actually solve their problems.\n\nA system that sends users back to the office to find out what is happening to documents already submitted online has missed one of the main purposes of digitalisation.\n\nNone of this means Sri Lanka should hold back on technology or AI, which can bring enormous benefits. But an online system does not understand the stress a company secretary feels when facing an official deadline. Technology should help people, not leave them feeling helpless.\n\nComplicated ownership structures make matters worse. A company secretary recently explained one case using a diagram, which can be simplified as follows:\n\nConsider a company that holds a 12% stake in a publicly listed company.\n\nThe company itself is owned by several corporate shareholders, each holding more than 10%.\n\nTwo of these shareholders are, in turn, owned by other companies, each also holding more than 10%."
  },
  {
