    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c4c0233490a9fc60fcb86c87977e3165",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "Small companies, with annual turnover below a specified threshold, which may need simplified reporting.\n\nGuarantee companies, the Registrar of Companies gives verbal instructions to indicate the Board of Directors, while the e-ROC system has such information directly available.\n\nThis does not mean automatic exemption from transparency requirements. But the Government should ask whether every category needs the same procedures and compliance costs. A risk-based approach, with simplified procedures or legally justified exemptions, would be more practical than a one-size-fits-all procedure.\n\nA Company Secretary recently brought to my attention a case involving an international organisation seeking to establish its presence in Sri Lanka. The registration process has reportedly remained pending for nearly six months because the Registrar of Companies (ROC) requires the organisation to use a rubber stamp or corporate seal. This is despite the fact that many developed countries have moved away from the traditional requirement for corporate seals. The organisation has submitted an explanatory letter setting out its position, but this has not been accepted by the ROC.\n\nThis raises a broader question as to whether our registration procedures are keeping pace with evolving international corporate practices, or whether outdated administrative requirements are unnecessarily discouraging international organisations from establishing a presence in Sri Lanka. This is an example of bureaucratic obstacles to investment and ease of doing business.\n\nFor years, successive Governments have encouraged business owners to register as limited liability companies rather than operate as sole proprietorships or partnerships. Yet reports suggest some companies are now considering voluntary winding-up because of the added requirements, difficulties and costs, and some owners may revert to sole proprietorships or partnerships."
  },
