    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a7dfc786bd48bd4ef84c0c58cd1b1a1e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "For years, successive Governments have encouraged business owners to register as limited liability companies rather than operate as sole proprietorships or partnerships. Yet reports suggest some companies are now considering voluntary winding-up because of the added requirements, difficulties and costs, and some owners may revert to sole proprietorships or partnerships.\n\nBanks in Sri Lanka already collect BO information when an account is opened, along with details of directors, authorised signatories and people who control the account. They are expected to carry out customer checks and monitor transactions for suspicious activity.\n\nHas there been any publicly reported case where BO information alone helped identify money laundering, terrorist financing or drug-related activity? Such examples would show the practical value of collecting this information at scale. Of course, identifying a beneficial owner is not enough to establish wrongdoing. It must be examined alongside bank transactions, financial records, and other information.\n\nThe Registrar\u2019s office already appears to be struggling to process the submissions it receives. Does it have enough officers with the necessary knowledge and technical skills to examine this information, identify suspicious ownership structures and pass findings to the investigating authorities? Collecting thousands of records is one thing; analysing them to uncover illegal activity is quite another.\n\nThe authorities should look beyond collection. The Registrar should study how neighbouring countries maintain, verify, and use their BO registers, and seek technical assistance from bodies such as the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank to improve the system, train officers, and link the Registrar, banks, and the Financial Intelligence Unit."
  },
