    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4c87845a6688fcba2f248a999b2969a4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1587,
    "chunk_text": "The authorities should look beyond collection. The Registrar should study how neighbouring countries maintain, verify, and use their BO registers, and seek technical assistance from bodies such as the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank to improve the system, train officers, and link the Registrar, banks, and the Financial Intelligence Unit.\n\nThe Registrar of Companies and the Government should consider the following:\n\nIncrease the number of officers authorised to examine and approve BO submissions, so the workload is not concentrated among a few individuals.\n\nSet a clear time limit within which the Registrar must approve an application or return it with reasons.\n\nIntroduce an online tracking system showing the stage of each application and the reason for any delay.\n\nRecognise the date of successful online submission of BO5, BO7 and others such as BO2, BO4 as the date of compliance, subject to later verification and correction.\n\nAnnounce an immediate extension or waiver of penalties for companies delayed by the BO approval process.\n\nAllow companies to proceed with other statutory filings while BO applications await approval, wherever legally possible.\n\nIntroduce simplified procedures or exemptions, where justified, for Government-owned, dormant, small and charitable companies.\n\nReview the BO website with independent IT professionals and practising company secretaries, including adding the ability to save and edit applications.\n\nProvide proper human support, so applicants can resolve problems without repeatedly visiting the Registrar\u2019s office."
  },
