    "md5": "67477a0a6265335ff8fc675eba3b4a39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1523,
    "chunk_text": "India is region\u2019s economic giant. Will Sri Lanka remain a neighbour or become a partner?\n\nIndian Prime Minister Narendra Modi with President Anura Kumara Dissanayake during the former's visit to Sri Lanka in April 2025\n\nMore than 2,300 years of civilisational exchange have bound India and Sri Lanka together. Today, India's economic ascent presents another historic opportunity. But the greatest threat to\n\nSri Lankan sovereignty may not be India's growing influence; it may be Lanka's failure to develop the economic strength necessary to negotiate that influence\n\nMore than 2,300 years ago, an encounter between Arahat Mahinda and King Devanampiya Tissa at Mihintale transformed Sri Lankan civilisation. According to Buddhist chronicles, Arahat Mahinda arrived during Emperor Ashoka\u2019s reign, introducing a philosophical tradition that profoundly influenced the island\u2019s religious, intellectual and political institutions. Buddhism shaped the monasteries of Anuradhapura, the sacred Sri Maha Bodhi and traditions central to Sri Lankan identity. Yet this civilisational relationship extended beyond Buddhism. Hindu devales became incorporated into Buddhist religious complexes, while deities including Vishnu, Kataragama and Pattini acquired distinctive positions within Sinhala Buddhist worship. At Kandy, the Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic and neighbouring devales embody centuries of religious interaction. These influences were not merely imported; they became inseparable from Sri Lanka\u2019s cultural identity."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
