    "chunk_size_bytes": 1516,
    "chunk_text": "More than 2,300 years ago, an encounter between Arahat Mahinda and King Devanampiya Tissa at Mihintale transformed Sri Lankan civilisation. According to Buddhist chronicles, Arahat Mahinda arrived during Emperor Ashoka\u2019s reign, introducing a philosophical tradition that profoundly influenced the island\u2019s religious, intellectual and political institutions. Buddhism shaped the monasteries of Anuradhapura, the sacred Sri Maha Bodhi and traditions central to Sri Lankan identity. Yet this civilisational relationship extended beyond Buddhism. Hindu devales became incorporated into Buddhist religious complexes, while deities including Vishnu, Kataragama and Pattini acquired distinctive positions within Sinhala Buddhist worship. At Kandy, the Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic and neighbouring devales embody centuries of religious interaction. These influences were not merely imported; they became inseparable from Sri Lanka\u2019s cultural identity.\n\nIndia\u2013Sri Lanka relations cannot be reduced to civilisational friendship. India\u2019s involvement in Sri Lanka\u2019s ethnic conflict, the Indo-Lanka Accord of 1987, the Indian Peace Keeping Force and unresolved Tamil political grievances have left enduring consequences. Fisheries disputes demonstrate that geographical proximity can generate conflicting interests. These experiences warrant scrutiny, but allowing historical grievances to determine the limits of economic cooperation risks transforming foreign policy into a permanent negotiation with the past."
