    "chunk_size_bytes": 1780,
    "chunk_text": "India\u2013Sri Lanka relations cannot be reduced to civilisational friendship. India\u2019s involvement in Sri Lanka\u2019s ethnic conflict, the Indo-Lanka Accord of 1987, the Indian Peace Keeping Force and unresolved Tamil political grievances have left enduring consequences. Fisheries disputes demonstrate that geographical proximity can generate conflicting interests. These experiences warrant scrutiny, but allowing historical grievances to determine the limits of economic cooperation risks transforming foreign policy into a permanent negotiation with the past.\n\nIndia\u2019s economic prominence is measurable. The International Monetary Fund\u2019s October 2026 projections place its nominal gross domestic product at approximately $4.06 trillion, with real growth projected at 7%. Measured through purchasing power parity, its economy approaches $19.2 trillion, accounting for approximately 8.6% of global output. India is therefore not merely South Asia\u2019s dominant economy but an increasingly consequential centre of international economic activity.\n\nIts transformation reflects decades of reform, entrepreneurship, technological development and integration into global markets. Liberalisation introduced in 1991 reduced restrictions and enabled domestic enterprises to expand internationally. Tata Consultancy Services, Infosys and Wipro became globally competitive technology businesses, while Tata Motors, Mahindra and Bharat Forge developed manufacturing capabilities extending beyond India\u2019s borders. Tata\u2019s acquisition of Jaguar Land Rover in 2008 demonstrated an important transition: Indian corporations were no longer merely participating in global production networks but acquiring technological capabilities, established brands and international distribution systems."
