    "chunk_text": "Its transformation reflects decades of reform, entrepreneurship, technological development and integration into global markets. Liberalisation introduced in 1991 reduced restrictions and enabled domestic enterprises to expand internationally. Tata Consultancy Services, Infosys and Wipro became globally competitive technology businesses, while Tata Motors, Mahindra and Bharat Forge developed manufacturing capabilities extending beyond India\u2019s borders. Tata\u2019s acquisition of Jaguar Land Rover in 2008 demonstrated an important transition: Indian corporations were no longer merely participating in global production networks but acquiring technological capabilities, established brands and international distribution systems.\n\nSri Lanka must stop merely trading with India and start producing with it\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s bilateral trade relationship reveals the limitations of an economic strategy centred predominantly on market access. Department of Commerce figures show exports to India reached approximately $1.04 billion in 2025, while imports amounted to $4.33 billion, producing a merchandise trade deficit of approximately $3.3 billion. A trade deficit does not inherently indicate economic weakness, particularly when imported machinery and intermediate goods support production. The more consequential question is whether trade creates skilled employment, technological capabilities and competitive industries."
