    "chunk_size_bytes": 1748,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s bilateral trade relationship reveals the limitations of an economic strategy centred predominantly on market access. Department of Commerce figures show exports to India reached approximately $1.04 billion in 2025, while imports amounted to $4.33 billion, producing a merchandise trade deficit of approximately $3.3 billion. A trade deficit does not inherently indicate economic weakness, particularly when imported machinery and intermediate goods support production. The more consequential question is whether trade creates skilled employment, technological capabilities and competitive industries.\n\nA contrasting example exists in the CEAT\u2013Kelani partnership. Established through an alliance in 1999, the venture combined Indian industrial expertise with Sri Lankan manufacturing assets, subsequently expanding production capacity and technological capabilities. CEAT\u2019s documented investments included radial-tyre manufacturing and truck-and-bus tyre production. Unlike businesses dependent principally on temporary tariff advantages, manufacturing partnerships require equipment, skilled labour, technical knowledge and continuing investment.\n\nThe opportunity extends further. India\u2019s automotive-component industry generated approximately $85.9 billion in turnover during 2025\u201326, including $24 billion in exports, according to the Automotive Component Manufacturers Association of India. Sri Lanka already manufactures rubber products, electrical assemblies and specialised automotive components. Rather than attempting to replicate India\u2019s industrial scale, its enterprises could become qualified suppliers within Indian manufacturing networks through certification, technological partnerships and efficient logistics."
