    "chunk_size_bytes": 1438,
    "chunk_text": "The opportunity extends further. India\u2019s automotive-component industry generated approximately $85.9 billion in turnover during 2025\u201326, including $24 billion in exports, according to the Automotive Component Manufacturers Association of India. Sri Lanka already manufactures rubber products, electrical assemblies and specialised automotive components. Rather than attempting to replicate India\u2019s industrial scale, its enterprises could become qualified suppliers within Indian manufacturing networks through certification, technological partnerships and efficient logistics.\n\nThis raises a politically uncomfortable question: have successive Sri Lankan governments invoked sovereignty to defend national independence while neglecting the industrial reforms necessary to exercise it? Concerns about Indian influence are legitimate, but the country\u2019s broader economic record reveals a contradiction between protecting domestic markets and developing competitive domestic industries.\n\nThe World Bank\u2019s 2024 analysis identified Sri Lanka\u2019s trade regime as exceptionally restrictive, with an average tariff, including para-tariffs, of 17.6% in 2022. It found that high and unpredictable import duties increased manufacturers\u2019 input costs and discouraged export-oriented production. A policy framework defended partly through domestic protection was therefore making it harder for domestic businesses to compete internationally."
