    "chunk_size_bytes": 1486,
    "chunk_text": "The World Bank\u2019s 2024 analysis identified Sri Lanka\u2019s trade regime as exceptionally restrictive, with an average tariff, including para-tariffs, of 17.6% in 2022. It found that high and unpredictable import duties increased manufacturers\u2019 input costs and discouraged export-oriented production. A policy framework defended partly through domestic protection was therefore making it harder for domestic businesses to compete internationally.\n\nThis is the paradox of defensive sovereignty: a State may seek independence by insulating its economy while simultaneously weakening the productive foundations on which meaningful independence depends. Sri Lanka\u2019s industrial shortcomings cannot be attributed exclusively to anti-Indian politics; civil conflict, fiscal instability and governance failures are central explanations. Nevertheless, sovereignty cannot become a substitute for economic strategy, nor can protection be presumed beneficial merely because it is presented as patriotic.\n\nGlobal production networks are being reorganised amid geopolitical competition, technological change and supply-chain diversification. Smaller economies struggle to compete independently against established industrial powers, but regional manufacturing networks offer opportunities for specialised production. Sri Lanka\u2019s maritime location, workforce and existing industrial capabilities could support integration if accompanied by technological upgrading and consistent investment policy."
