    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Global production networks are being reorganised amid geopolitical competition, technological change and supply-chain diversification. Smaller economies struggle to compete independently against established industrial powers, but regional manufacturing networks offer opportunities for specialised production. Sri Lanka\u2019s maritime location, workforce and existing industrial capabilities could support integration if accompanied by technological upgrading and consistent investment policy.\n\nThe civilisational relationship provides an unusually deep foundation for cooperation. Buddhism\u2019s transmission from India, the incorporation of Hindu-associated deities into Buddhist practice and centuries of architectural and commercial exchange demonstrate interconnected societies capable of transforming external influences into local traditions. Contemporary relationships retain substantial people-to-people connections, including tourism, education and professional exchange.\n\nNevertheless, historical familiarity cannot guarantee equitable economic outcomes. Shared culture does not automatically generate competitive industries or responsible political leadership. Cultural diplomacy gives bilateral relations depth, but institutional capacity determines their economic value.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic collapse in 2022 exposed the vulnerability of a state possessing formal political independence but insufficient economic resilience. India\u2019s emergency assistance of approximately $4 billion demonstrated the importance of cooperation, yet financial support cannot substitute for sustainable prosperity. The World Bank\u2019s October 2026 assessment reported that Sri Lanka had returned to pre-crisis economic output levels, while poverty remained elevated at an estimated 16.9% in 2025. Stabilisation remains indispensable, but recovery without productive transformation risks preserving underlying vulnerabilities."
