    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s economic collapse in 2022 exposed the vulnerability of a state possessing formal political independence but insufficient economic resilience. India\u2019s emergency assistance of approximately $4 billion demonstrated the importance of cooperation, yet financial support cannot substitute for sustainable prosperity. The World Bank\u2019s October 2026 assessment reported that Sri Lanka had returned to pre-crisis economic output levels, while poverty remained elevated at an estimated 16.9% in 2025. Stabilisation remains indispensable, but recovery without productive transformation risks preserving underlying vulnerabilities.\n\nSri Lanka must consequently maintain diversified international partnerships while developing industries that make cooperation mutually valuable. The objective is not freedom from economic relationships, but freedom from negotiating them through structural weakness. Integration with India should therefore be neither unconditional nor instinctively rejected; it must be judged by whether it expands Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term economic and strategic choices.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s civilisational history demonstrates that engagement with India need not entail the erosion of national identity. Religious, intellectual and architectural influences crossed the Palk Strait and became part of a distinctly Sri Lankan civilisation. Modern economic diplomacy demands comparable confidence, strengthened by transparent negotiations and institutions capable of protecting national interests.\n\nFor decades, political debate has questioned whether closer relations with India threaten sovereignty. The more consequential question is whether successive governments have constructed an economy sufficiently productive to exercise sovereignty meaningfully. India\u2019s ascent will reshape South Asia regardless of Sri Lanka\u2019s participation, but the island retains the ability to determine whether its enterprises become valuable participants in that transformation or remain peripheral to it."
