    "md5": "d23ae95212c6388d8ece4146f2a3fa2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "Unpaid care work: Sri Lanka\u2019s invisible economy demands policy action\n\nMothers of children with disabilities often endure immense emotional and psychological distress, including stress, depression and anxiety, while facing isolation and marginalisation within their own families and society. Their contribution to their families through household chores and the demanding responsibility of caring for their children with disabilities often goes unnoticed, much like the unpaid work undertaken by millions of other women.\n\nFrom preparing meals and cleaning homes to caring for children, older persons and family members with disabilities, millions of hours of work sustain Sri Lankan households every day.\n\nYet much of this labour remains invisible in economic calculations, unrecognised in public policy and disproportionately shouldered by women and girls.\n\n\u201cA mother who comes to our daycare centre for children said she sleeps for a while as her youngest child is safely looked after in the centre. This shows how women sacrifice their time for the betterment of the family by working from dawn to dusk. But her contribution to caregiving responsibilities was not paid or appreciated,\u201d Aneesa Firthous of the Islamic Women\u2019s Association for Research and Empowerment (IWARE), based in Batticaloa, said.\n\nSpeaking at a panel discussion on community-led care, she highlighted the need to change attitudes towards women's caregiving responsibilities to enable women and girls to improve their living standards and personal wellbeing.\n\n\u201cIt is very difficult to bring about attitude changes in the Muslim community, as it is still a patriarchal society. There are still girls who get married at a young age and, throughout their lives, shoulder all the household unpaid work, as society still believes that looking after children and elders and doing household work belong to women,\u201d she said, stressing the need to make women aware of the importance of sharing responsibilities at home."
  },
  {
