    "md5": "f44a6fc81ba975616420d79b3504cbea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIt is very difficult to bring about attitude changes in the Muslim community, as it is still a patriarchal society. There are still girls who get married at a young age and, throughout their lives, shoulder all the household unpaid work, as society still believes that looking after children and elders and doing household work belong to women,\u201d she said, stressing the need to make women aware of the importance of sharing responsibilities at home.\n\n\u201cThey think women are just at home and are not doing any work. We gave them a timetable to make them understand the amount of work those women at home do daily. We also have street dramas and short films to make males and young boys understand the importance of sharing responsibilities at home,\u201d they said.\n\nWomen and girls shoulder a disproportionate share of household and caregiving responsibilities, limiting their economic opportunities and wellbeing. As Sri Lanka moves towards a dedicated National Policy on Unpaid Care Work, translating commitments into practical, adequately funded solutions is becoming an urgent priority.\n\nThe unequal distribution of unpaid care work continues to place a disproportionate burden on women and girls in Sri Lanka, restricting their access to employment, education, economic independence and personal wellbeing, and prompting renewed calls for a dedicated national policy backed by effective implementation.\n\nAccording to the Sri Lanka Child Youth Survey 2017, conducted by the Department of Census and Statistics, 87.3% of women and girls aged 10 years and above participated in unpaid domestic and caregiving activities, compared with 59.7% of men and boys.\n\nThe figures highlight a persistent gender divide in household responsibilities, reflecting social norms that continue to regard caring for children, older persons, persons with disabilities and other family members as primarily women's work."
  },
