    "md5": "daff304636008536e48f1c6037a2d172",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1720,
    "chunk_text": "The figures highlight a persistent gender divide in household responsibilities, reflecting social norms that continue to regard caring for children, older persons, persons with disabilities and other family members as primarily women's work.\n\nWith women's labour force participation remaining low, women's organisations and development partners are calling for a comprehensive national policy that recognises care as an essential economic and social contribution while establishing mechanisms to reduce and redistribute these responsibilities.\n\nThe call was made at the third National Convening on Recognising Unpaid Care Work, organised by The Asia Foundation and Women and Media Collective (WMC), with the support of Global Affairs Canada, in Colombo recently.\n\nHeld annually since 2024 to mark the International Day of Care and Support, the convening this year was themed \u2018From Policy to Community-driven Solutions: Engaging Communities to Share Unpaid Care Responsibilities\u2019.\n\nThe Canadian-funded project, Unpaid Care Work in Sri Lanka, supports national and local efforts to recognise, redistribute and reduce the disproportionate load of unpaid care work carried by women and girls, while representing and responding to their rights and needs.\n\nBringing together government representatives, development partners, civil society organisations, women's groups and private sector stakeholders, the event highlighted progress towards a draft National Policy on Unpaid Care Work and community-led initiatives addressing care needs across the country.\n\nHowever, participants stressed that the challenge now lies in ensuring that policy commitments translate into tangible improvements in the lives of women and girls."
