    "chunk_text": "Bringing together government representatives, development partners, civil society organisations, women's groups and private sector stakeholders, the event highlighted progress towards a draft National Policy on Unpaid Care Work and community-led initiatives addressing care needs across the country.\n\nHowever, participants stressed that the challenge now lies in ensuring that policy commitments translate into tangible improvements in the lives of women and girls.\n\n\u201cAcross Sri Lanka, women continue to carry a disproportionate share of unpaid care responsibilities, caring for children, elderly persons and households. Yet, this work remains largely unrecognised and undervalued,\u201d she said, adding that the consequences were significant: unpaid care responsibilities limit women's access to employment, reduce economic opportunities and contribute to persistent gender inequalities.\n\nMartin highlighted that nearly 60% of unemployed women in Sri Lanka identify care responsibilities as the main reason they remain outside the labour force.\n\n\u201cResearch shows that recognising and valuing unpaid care work could make a substantial contribution to Sri Lanka\u2019s economic growth and social wellbeing. Reducing the unequal burden of care will enable more women to participate in the labour force and contribute to the inclusive economy,\u201d she said.\n\nMartin said the initiative was guided by the five Rs framework \u2013 Recognise, Reduce, Redistribute, Represent and Respond \u2013 and worked at both policy and community levels.\n\nCommending the progress made towards the National Policy and Action Plan for Unpaid Care Work, she said it was an important step towards ensuring that care work was recognised not only as a private responsibility but also as a public policy responsibility and priority."
