    "chunk_text": "Commending the progress made towards the National Policy and Action Plan for Unpaid Care Work, she said it was an important step towards ensuring that care work was recognised not only as a private responsibility but also as a public policy responsibility and priority.\n\nWomen and Child Affairs Minister Saroja Savithri Paulraj said women's labour force participation currently stands at only 32%, highlighting the need to address the unequal distribution of unpaid care responsibilities.\n\nCiting the 2017 survey findings, she emphasised that 87.3% of women and girls aged 10 and above participated in unpaid domestic and caregiving activities, compared with 59.7% of men and boys.\n\n\u201cThese figures highlight the unequal distribution of care responsibilities and the need for meaningful action,\u201d the Minister said.\n\nShe noted that Sri Lanka had already introduced several policies and initiatives relevant to care and social protection, including the National Policy on Gender Equality and Women Empowerment, the Women Empowerment Act 2024, the National Policy on Child Day Care Facilities in Sri Lanka 2024, the National Social Protection Policy 2024, the National Action Plan on Social Care and the National Policy for Elders.\n\nWhile these frameworks provide important foundations, Paulraj stressed that they must be complemented by a dedicated policy addressing the structural causes of the unequal distribution of unpaid care work.\n\n\u201cWhile these frameworks strengthen services and support for people who need care, we must also address the everyday realities of women who spend countless hours caring for children, older persons, persons with disabilities and other family members, often without any provision of support,\u201d she said.\n\nPaulraj noted that unpaid care work was connected to several areas of public policy, including labour rights, social protection, early childhood development, health, disability inclusion and support for older persons."
  },
