    "language": "en",
    "md5": "49681cccb8a47f2e35e422f3cea9b70b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "Paulraj noted that unpaid care work was connected to several areas of public policy, including labour rights, social protection, early childhood development, health, disability inclusion and support for older persons.\n\nThe Minister added that policy implementation would require coordinated action across government institutions, development partners, women's organisations, service providers, trade unions, researchers and the private sector.\n\nPaulraj acknowledged the contribution of grassroots women's organisations in raising awareness, conducting community discussions, undertaking research and advocating for change.\n\n\u201cThrough awareness-raising, community discussions, research and advocacy, they are bringing unpaid care work into public discussion and helping ensure that women's experiences and voices are reflected in the policy process,\u201d she said.\n\nHowever, the Minister cautioned that women's organisations could not be expected to fill gaps in public services indefinitely.\n\n\u201cOften with limited funding and by relying on women's unpaid labour, these organisations need appropriate recognition, resources and support to sustain their work,\u201d she said.\n\nPaulraj emphasised that the ultimate objective was to ensure that women had greater opportunities to participate in education, employment and public life while having time for rest and their own aspirations.\n\n\u201cPolicy is only as meaningful as the difference it makes in people's lives,\u201d she said, emphasising the need to have a society where care is valued, shared and supported, and where no woman or girl is held back from realising her full potential because of unequal care responsibilities.\n\nWMC Director - Programs for Gender and Political Economy Dr. Sepali Kottegoda said the organisation's work on unpaid care had evolved through more than a decade of research, advocacy and engagement with communities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-27-dailyftlk-28bc9d6d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-28bc9d6d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-27",
    "description": "Unpaid care work: Sri Lanka\u2019s invisible economy demands policy action",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Unpaid-care-work-Sri-Lanka-s-invisible-economy-demands-policy-action/14-800665",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1795746872.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-27-dailyftlk-28bc9d6d-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "80c8054cab269a8b42a43c5146deab53",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "WMC Director - Programs for Gender and Political Economy Dr. Sepali Kottegoda said the organisation's work on unpaid care had evolved through more than a decade of research, advocacy and engagement with communities.\n\nKottegoda stressed that unpaid care work should be addressed as a structural issue affecting gender equality, labour force participation, poverty and human development, rather than being treated solely as a welfare concern.\n\n\u201cChallenges still remain. The category of labour remains confined to activities that are deemed to directly produce income, which contributes to the country\u2019s GDP. But we need to strengthen awareness and policy acceptance that unpaid care work does contribute economic value within the household as well as to the larger economy,\u201d she emphasised.\n\nAccording to Dr. Kottegoda, unpaid care work reflects a broader reality: women's labour is both essential and invisible, while women are expected to perform those tasks without recognition or compensation.\n\n\u201cThis reinforces the structural devaluation of care work in economic and policy frameworks,\u201d she said.\n\nDr. Kottegoda, stressing the need to integrate unpaid care work into government budgeting, planning and monitoring, and to ensure that recognition is accompanied by concrete institutional action, said that the draft policy seeks to recognise care work as an essential contribution to families, communities and the national economy.\n\nThe draft National Policy on Unpaid Care Work, developed by WMC in collaboration with the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs, with support from The Asia Foundation and Global Affairs Canada, is currently under review and is expected to be submitted for Cabinet approval in 2026.\n\n\u201cWomen's labour is both essential and invisible, but the expectation is that women will perform these tasks, regardless of recognition or compensation, reinforces the structural devaluation of care work in economic and policy frameworks,\u201d she noted."
  },
