    "language": "en",
    "md5": "56ff51018c72351582178d9019e4830a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWomen's labour is both essential and invisible, but the expectation is that women will perform these tasks, regardless of recognition or compensation, reinforces the structural devaluation of care work in economic and policy frameworks,\u201d she noted.\n\nThe policy is aligned with Sri Lanka's constitutional principles and international commitments, including the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women, the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action, and Sustainable Development Goal 5 on gender equality.\n\nThe Asia Foundation's Country Representative for Sri Lanka and the Maldives, Johann Rebert, said the initiative had been driven by a commitment to place the experiences, needs and rights of women and girls at the centre of efforts to address unpaid care work.\n\n\u201cWhen we started this journey, we shared one important priority,\u201d he said, adding that the priority was to challenge the perception that caring for families and communities was primarily women's work.\n\nHe said that care was a shared responsibility, with men and other family members, communities, the state and the private sector all having a role to play.\n\nRebert said the initiative had sought to generate evidence on how women from different communities experienced care responsibilities and to support solutions that responded to their realities.\n\nWhile national policy development continues, women's organisations across Sri Lanka are already working to address unpaid care responsibilities through community-led initiatives.\n\nThe convening highlighted women-led, community-based daycare models for older persons, children and persons with disabilities in Jaffna, Batticaloa and Anuradhapura.\n\nCommunity-led initiatives in Kurunegala, Nuwara Eliya, Hambantota and Batticaloa are also raising awareness about unpaid care work and advocating for a more equitable distribution of responsibilities."
  },
  {
