    "language": "en",
    "md5": "beab501e0981714b7d41f66960ac8731",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Community-led initiatives in Kurunegala, Nuwara Eliya, Hambantota and Batticaloa are also raising awareness about unpaid care work and advocating for a more equitable distribution of responsibilities.\n\nAt the panel discussions, women community leaders highlighted that access to affordable and accessible care services remains a major challenge. They explained that, for women who cannot afford private daycare or eldercare services, caring for family members can become a full-time responsibility, limiting their ability to seek employment or pursue other opportunities.\n\nHowever, the growing demand for eldercare is adding to the pressure, as Sri Lanka is among the fastest-ageing countries in South Asia, and the increasing number of older persons is expected to intensify the demand for care services.\n\nWomen who frequently provide care for both children and older family members face growing demands on their time and energy.\n\nThe panellists highlighted that this situation had affected women's rights to education, paid employment, professional development and rest, as well as their physical and mental wellbeing. It affects women across all social and economic backgrounds.\n\nIt was also said that care was connected to several areas of public policy, including labour rights, social protection, early childhood development, health, disability inclusion and support for older persons.\n\nAt the discussions, the community-led women's organisations in Jaffna, Batticaloa and Anuradhapura emphasised the importance of developing women-led, community-based daycare models for older persons, children and persons with disabilities. They also highlighted community-led solutions in Kurunegala, Nuwara Eliya, Hambantota and Batticaloa that raise awareness and promote the need to share unpaid care work responsibilities among men and boys."
