    "chunk_text": "Volfpack and SLTC develop Sri Lanka\u2019s first supercapacitors as industries confront power quality pressures\n\nSri Lanka has produced its first supercapacitors, the fast response energy storage that factories, data centres and power grids increasingly rely on. Volfpack Energy, a Sri Lankan deep tech startup working with Sri Lanka Technology Campus (SLTC), has made the cells in its laboratory and is now building towards commercial production. Apparel manufacturers and IT infrastructure companies are already evaluating the technology.\n\nThe timing reflects a shift in what industry needs from the grid. Sri Lanka now has more than 3,000 MW of solar capacity, yet the grid was designed for one way power flow and has limited storage to absorb sudden swings in supply. October\u2019s nationwide blackout and an 18% tariff increase this year have made power quality a board level concern. For manufacturers the risk is immediate, because a voltage dip lasting less than a second can stop an entire production line.\n\nCVolfpack Energy CEO and Co-founder harlie Karunaratne said: \u201cIt\u2019s not just about generating more electricity. It\u2019s about managing electricity better.\u201d"
  },
