    "chunk_text": "The timing reflects a shift in what industry needs from the grid. Sri Lanka now has more than 3,000 MW of solar capacity, yet the grid was designed for one way power flow and has limited storage to absorb sudden swings in supply. October\u2019s nationwide blackout and an 18% tariff increase this year have made power quality a board level concern. For manufacturers the risk is immediate, because a voltage dip lasting less than a second can stop an entire production line.\n\nCVolfpack Energy CEO and Co-founder harlie Karunaratne said: \u201cIt\u2019s not just about generating more electricity. It\u2019s about managing electricity better.\u201d\n\nAlongside supercapacitors, Volfpack is developing 10C batteries for applications that need more energy, with a target duration of up to 15 minutes. Both technologies share a common manufacturing platform.\n\nOver the past four years, Volfpack\u2019s team of young scientists and engineers has manufactured more than 5,000 cells and developed its own electrode and electrolyte formulations. The company is based at TRACE Expert City, where it works with SLTC at the SLTC Innovation & Industry Collaboration Hub.\n\nThe development also moves Sri Lanka further up a value chain in which it already competes globally. The country is one of the world\u2019s ten largest exporters of activated carbon, most of it made from coconut shells, a preferred material for supercapacitor electrodes. Volfpack aims to turn this local resource into finished energy storage products made in Sri Lanka. No other company in the country has made supercapacitors from these resources at this power level.\n\nSLTC students work alongside Volfpack\u2019s engineers on device design, production, automation, testing and quality control."
