    "chunk_text": "The development also moves Sri Lanka further up a value chain in which it already competes globally. The country is one of the world\u2019s ten largest exporters of activated carbon, most of it made from coconut shells, a preferred material for supercapacitor electrodes. Volfpack aims to turn this local resource into finished energy storage products made in Sri Lanka. No other company in the country has made supercapacitors from these resources at this power level.\n\nSLTC students work alongside Volfpack\u2019s engineers on device design, production, automation, testing and quality control.\n\nSLTC Applied Electronics Head Dr. Chamith Chandrarathna said: \u201cEngineering education becomes more meaningful when students can apply what they learn to real problems. Working with Volfpack exposes them to the pace of product development, the importance of testing and iteration, and the challenges of building technology that works beyond the classroom.\u201d\n\nVolfpack\u2019s first manufacturing milestone is one million cells a year, with India and the wider South Asian region as its first export markets. \u201cThe goal is to prove that we can manufacture consistently, at a competitive cost, while building products that solve problems customers are actually willing to pay for,\u201d said Karunaratne.\n\nVolfpack is inviting manufacturers and data centre operators to pilot the technology."
