    "chunk_text": "Digital Trust Alliance launches open dashboard tracking digital spending in Budget 2027\n\nFree public platform links every figure to its page in the official Budget documents, and invites citizens, practitioners and policymakers to review, question and comment\n\nThe Digital Trust Alliance (DTA) has launched \u201cBudget 2027: What\u2019s for Digital\u201d, a free, publicly accessible dashboard at budget.digitaltrust.lk that sets out how Sri Lanka\u2019s draft Budget 2027 allocates funds to digital government, cybersecurity, data protection, digital skills and related areas, and how these allocations compare with Budget 2026.\n\nBudget analyses published each year by professional services firms and policy think tanks provide valuable insight into revenue, expenditure and the fiscal outlook. The DTA dashboard is intended to complement this work by focusing on the digital and digital trust dimensions of public spending. Digital allocations are spread across the Appropriation Bill, the volumes of Detailed Budget Estimates, the medium-term public investment plan and the Budget Speech; the dashboard brings these lines together in one place and identifies Rs. 52.5 billion in digital spending in the 2027 draft.\n\nMinistry of Digital Economy: Rs. 26,000 million in 2027, up 62.5% from Rs. 16,000 million in 2026.\n\nDigital government (DigiGov-SL): Rs. 9,486 million in 2027, up from a revised Rs. 702 million in 2026, within an estimated total programme cost of Rs. 43,859 million to 2030. Of the 2027 allocation, Rs. 7,026 million is foreign loan financing.\n\nDigital identity (SLUDI): Rs. 1,500 million in 2027, following a revision of the 2026 allocation from Rs. 2,110 million to Rs. 910 million.\n\nCybersecurity: projects under Sri Lanka CERT receive Rs. 590 million (2026: Rs. 600 million), while its institutional grant rises from Rs. 375 million to Rs. 470 million. New Ministry of Defence lines for cyber infrastructure, a digital forensic laboratory and a cyber range total Rs. 1,053 million."
  },
