    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Cybersecurity: projects under Sri Lanka CERT receive Rs. 590 million (2026: Rs. 600 million), while its institutional grant rises from Rs. 375 million to Rs. 470 million. New Ministry of Defence lines for cyber infrastructure, a digital forensic laboratory and a cyber range total Rs. 1,053 million.\n\nSkills and industry: ICT Hubs for secondary schools rise from Rs. 575 million to Rs 2,500 million, while the Digital Industry Development line, which supports upskilling and start-up accelerators, is allocated Rs. 380 million, down 49.3% from Rs. 750 million.\n\nOversight: the National Audit Office, the Bribery Commission and the National Procurement Commission together hold under Rs. 45 million for software in 2027.\n\nDelivery: by August 2026, 25.4% of the 2026 capital budget had been spent across government. Budget 2026 commitments on GovPay and VAT on foreign digital services have been delivered.\n\nLegislation: Budget Speech 2027 expects the Digital Economy Bill, which establishes the Digital Economy Authority, to be passed in the first quarter of 2027. The Cyber Security Bill is with the Attorney General.\n\nDigital Trust Alliance President Lakmal Embuldeniya said: \u201cEach year, professional firms and think tanks provide valuable commentary on the Budget, and we aim to add a digital trust lens to that conversation. Budget 2027 makes a meaningful investment in digital public infrastructure. The dashboard allows anyone to see where that money is allocated, how it compares with last year, and whether investment in digital platforms is matched by investment in cybersecurity, data protection and oversight. It is a living tool that we will continue to improve, and we invite citizens to help shape it through their comments. We also hope it gives investors, both local and foreign, a clear and transparent view of Sri Lanka\u2019s digital priorities.\u201d"
