    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b82b3976e5ec938b735227e0e1306243",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2020,
    "chunk_text": "From village craft to new markets\n\nWomen artisans participating in Palms Together, Sivan Arul Hope's women's empowerment programme, carry out handloom weaving, yarn preparation, palmyra dyeing and basket weaving. Connecting these traditional skills with wider markets is central to the program\u2019s work in\n\nSri Lanka's North and East Photo credit: Courtesy of Sivan Arul Hope / Palms Together\n\nJohnny Fernando and Anil Koswatte help open doors for SMEs in Sri Lanka\u2019s north and east\n\nBusiness connections, retail expertise and a shared interest in supporting SMEs\u2014particularly in the war-affected North and East\u2014offer new possibilities for women artisans\n\nA beautifully woven palmyra basket or handloom textile represents skill, patience and knowledge passed between generations. But craftsmanship alone does not guarantee a livelihood. Someone must connect the artisan with the customer, explain what the market wants and help turn an occasional sale into a continuing business relationship.\n\nFor Palms Together, the women\u2019s empowerment program of Sivan Arul Hope, the interest shown by International Chamber of Commerce Sri Lanka Chairman Johnny Fernando and Lakarcade CEO and Managing Director Anil Koswatte, offers an opportunity to strengthen precisely these connections.\n\nICC Sri Lanka and Lakarcade are keen to promote small and medium-sized enterprises, particularly those in the war-affected North and East. For Palms Together, this shared interest opens a conversation about how rural women\u2019s skills can find a stronger place in Sri Lanka\u2019s commercial economy.\n\nTheir potential contributions are distinct but complementary: business networks and institutional facilitation on one side; practical knowledge of handicraft retail, regional artisan development, product presentation and customer expectations on the other. Together, these could help bring rural producers closer to Colombo\u2019s commercial life\u2014not simply as recipients of assistance, but as suppliers with something valuable to offer."
  },
