    "md5": "660f0c15129335d6d3267b47ebee6e50",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "Since then, the program has secured two international grants: support from the Avery Dennison\n\nFoundation in September 2025, followed by the Government of Japan\u2019s Grant Assistance for Grassroots Human Security Projects program in October 2026.\n\nThis support provides an important foundation. Yet the next challenge is clear: investment in production must be matched by investment in relationships with buyers.\n\nA centre can provide a place to work and sell. Business connections can help ensure that what is produced has somewhere to go. That is why the interest of Fernando and Koswatte matters at this stage of the program\u2019s development.\n\nPalms Together\u2019s approach rests on women who already possess the skills, local knowledge and confidence to lead others.\n\nNearly three decades of civil war disrupted education and livelihoods across the North and East. Nevertheless, women in these communities have retained considerable expertise in palmyra craft and handloom weaving.\n\nThe program works through experienced women leaders, including skilled teachers and award winning artisans. They understand the language, culture and circumstances of their communities. They communicate with government officials and local organisations, learn about exhibitions and opportunities, and share that information with other women.\n\nSome women are willing to produce but remain reluctant or unable to travel, negotiate with buyers or sell independently. The leadership model allows them to participate through home-based work while gradually building confidence through encouragement and dialogue.\n\nIn Thalaimannar, Kavitha, one of the program\u2019s key leaders, has built a team of more than 40 workers supporting her business. Her experience illustrates why local leadership matters: an opportunity reaching one capable woman can extend to many others.\n\nThe role of Colombo\u2019s business community is not to replace this leadership. It is to connect it with markets, knowledge and relationships that can help it grow."
  },
