    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "In Thalaimannar, Kavitha, one of the program\u2019s key leaders, has built a team of more than 40 workers supporting her business. Her experience illustrates why local leadership matters: an opportunity reaching one capable woman can extend to many others.\n\nThe role of Colombo\u2019s business community is not to replace this leadership. It is to connect it with markets, knowledge and relationships that can help it grow.\n\nPalmyra weaving and handloom production require time and skill. If these traditions are to remain viable, the women practising them need customers who value the work and commercial relationships that make continued production worthwhile.\n\nThis is where Lakarcade\u2019s retail and regional craft development perspective could be especially useful. Feedback on design, finishing, colour, presentation and product selection can help artisans understand how their work is received beyond their immediate communities.\n\nBusiness connections explored with Fernando and ICCSL\u2019s SME initiatives could complement that guidance by opening conversations with prospective buyers.\n\nThe two contributions belong together. Market access is more useful when a producer understands the buyer\u2019s requirements; product development is more worthwhile when there is a realistic route to customers.\n\nFor Palms Together, this is the connection between empowerment and enterprise.\n\nThe program\u2019s progress has already shown how much practical support can achieve.\n\nThamby Navaratnam, an entrepreneur whose business background includes his role as a key promoter of Apollo Hospital in Colombo, now Lanka Hospitals, has supported Palms Together from the beginning. His professional profile records his involvement in establishing the hospital with India\u2019s Apollo Hospitals Group."
