    "chunk_text": "Thamby Navaratnam, an entrepreneur whose business background includes his role as a key promoter of Apollo Hospital in Colombo, now Lanka Hospitals, has supported Palms Together from the beginning. His professional profile records his involvement in establishing the hospital with India\u2019s Apollo Hospitals Group.\n\nMr Stephen Samuel was the catalyst for orders from Barefoot, helping connect the program\u2019s products with an established Colombo retailer. His contribution demonstrates the difference one person can make by bringing producers and buyers together.\n\nBarefoot and 1948 Shop have helped introduce the products to Colombo markets.\n\nAriff Sinna Lebbe, whose FCC Mall sells Sivan Arul products, offered to purchase products upfront. For small producers, receiving payment rather than waiting for an eventual sale can make a meaningful difference to their ability to continue working.\n\nJekhan Aruliah MBE has offered gallery space at the Arc in Jaffna, free of charge, for an exhibition of products made through the program.\n\nThese contributions differ in form, but share a principle: access matters. A purchase, an introduction or a place to exhibit can bring an artisan closer to a customer she could not otherwise reach.\n\nThe developing connections with Fernando and Koswatte offer an opportunity to build on this foundation with greater commercial reach and practical market knowledge.\n\nThe argument for collaboration is not simply that rural women need help. It is that they have products, skills and knowledge that businesses and customers can value.\n\nColombo\u2019s retailers gain access to distinctive Sri Lankan goods. Prospective corporate buyers can explore locally made products. Artisans gain feedback, market information and opportunities to build more dependable income.\n\nThis is a different relationship from that of benefactor and recipient. It is an exchange in which each side brings something useful."
