    "md5": "1758848cacd7e01421c35df8066b6e90",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2014,
    "chunk_text": "New Zealand\u2019s ruling coalition falls short of parliamentary majority by one seat\n\nBBC - New Zealand's ruling coalition has fallen short of a parliamentary majority by one seat, following a campaign shaped by a sluggish economy and disillusioned voters.\n\nIt now needs the support of the centrist Opportunity Party to remain in power. If they cannot reach a deal, the country may have to go back to the polls.\n\nThe incumbent National Party led by Prime Minister Christopher Luxon secured 40 out of 130 seats, while its coalition partners ACT and New Zealand First took 13 and 12 respectively.\n\nA government led by the centre-left Labour Party is not possible, official results on Friday show, as the bloc would not have enough seats even if Opportunity threw its weight behind it.\n\nLuxon on Friday acknowledged that negotiations \"may be challenging\" and will take some time.\n\n\"Kiwis won't thank us for rushing to an agreement that falls apart or for sending them back to the polls,\" he told reporters.\n\nLabour leader Chris Hipkins said his party \"ran an incredible campaign\" but acknowledged that it doesn't have negotiating power despite winning the popular vote. \"More New Zealanders voted for change at this election than for the status quo,\" he said.\n\nNew Zealand's mixed-member proportional voting system means most governments are coalitions. Each person casts two votes \u2013 one for their preferred political party and another for their choice of local representative.\n\nLabour secured 36 seats, The Greens took 17, while Te P\u0101ti M\u0101ori, which represents M\u0101ori interests, picked up five.\n\nOpportunity, which has been described as this election's kingmaker, secured seven seats. Opportunity's leader Qiulae Wong told reporters her party will negotiate with National, but that all options remain on the table.\n\n\"There are seven parties in parliment and everyone is responsible for making sure we do everything we can to deliver a stable government for New Zealand. It's not just up to us,\" she said on Friday."
