    "chunk_size_bytes": 1610,
    "chunk_text": "Opportunity, which has been described as this election's kingmaker, secured seven seats. Opportunity's leader Qiulae Wong told reporters her party will negotiate with National, but that all options remain on the table.\n\n\"There are seven parties in parliment and everyone is responsible for making sure we do everything we can to deliver a stable government for New Zealand. It's not just up to us,\" she said on Friday.\n\nBut today's official tally shows it had lost two seats on special votes - cast by citizens who were overseas or outside of their registered electorate on election day - which typically skew left.\n\nSome of National's key election pledges include lower taxes, cheaper energy, more renewable generation, and a greater focus on defence.\n\nLabour, meanwhile, had campaigned to cut healthcare, public transport and fuel costs, more government support for social housing, among other things.\n\nIn earlier interviews with the BBC, many voters had expressed frustration over spiralling costs of living and high unemployment across the country, with declining faith in their political leadership.\n\n\"There's this kind of apathy and a bit of dread,\" said 21-year-old Imogen Vlutger.\n\nA 40-year-old man said he didn't vote as he could not bring himself to tick any of the boxes on the ballot paper.\n\nMore than half of the country no longer see New Zealand as a land of opportunity, according to a survey by the Helen Clark Foundation, a public policy think tank.\n\nKiwis have been leaving the country in record numbers, with many of them, including former prime minister Jacinda Arden, moving to Australia."
