    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "Public service shrinks, but salary bill keeps rising\n\nColombo, Nov. 27 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0Sri Lanka's public service lost more than 23,000 employees in the year to June 2026, and vacancies rose to 236,967, yet the state's salary bill kept climbing, official figures show.\n\nAccording to the Department of Management Services' cadre reports for 2025 and 2026, the public service employed 893,797 people as at June 30, 2026, compared with 917,137 a year earlier. During the same period, the approved cadre increased from 1,117,713 to 1,130,764. As a result, vacancies rose by 36,391 in a single year, and the share of approved posts that were filled declined from 82 percent to 79 percent.\n\nAlthough the public service has shrunk, the cost of employing it has continued to rise. The Treasury's Budget Economic and Fiscal Position Report 2027 shows that spending on the salaries and wages of public servants, including Provincial Council employees, increased by 8.7 percent to Rs. 827 billion in the first eight months of 2026, from Rs. 760.7 billion a year earlier. The Treasury attributed the increase to the second phase of the public sector salary revision, which took effect in January. Pension payments rose by 17.5 percent to Rs. 332.4 billion over the same period, and the Treasury's 2027 pension estimates are based on 762,570 pensioners.\n\nAround two-thirds of the increase in vacancies stemmed from the net loss of employees. The remaining third came from new posts added to the approved cadre."
  },
