    "chunk_size_bytes": 1417,
    "chunk_text": "Although the public service has shrunk, the cost of employing it has continued to rise. The Treasury's Budget Economic and Fiscal Position Report 2027 shows that spending on the salaries and wages of public servants, including Provincial Council employees, increased by 8.7 percent to Rs. 827 billion in the first eight months of 2026, from Rs. 760.7 billion a year earlier. The Treasury attributed the increase to the second phase of the public sector salary revision, which took effect in January. Pension payments rose by 17.5 percent to Rs. 332.4 billion over the same period, and the Treasury's 2027 pension estimates are based on 762,570 pensioners.\n\nAround two-thirds of the increase in vacancies stemmed from the net loss of employees. The remaining third came from new posts added to the approved cadre.\n\nThe Treasury bases those estimates on a public sector workforce of approximately 1.3 million employees. That figure is well above the 1,028,861 recorded in the cadre report, which includes state-owned enterprises but excludes the uniformed cadre of the tri-forces.\n\nThe losses were concentrated in the lower grades. Employees in the secondary and primary grades, which include clerical staff, teachers and minor employees, fell by 26,723, whereas the senior grade expanded by 2,912. As a result, the number of frontline employees for every senior officer fell from almost 20 a year ago to fewer than 18."
