    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "The losses were concentrated in the lower grades. Employees in the secondary and primary grades, which include clerical staff, teachers and minor employees, fell by 26,723, whereas the senior grade expanded by 2,912. As a result, the number of frontline employees for every senior officer fell from almost 20 a year ago to fewer than 18.\n\nSeveral essential services also have fewer staff than a year ago. The Police had 73,875 uniformed officers against 102,112 approved posts, 856 fewer than in 2025, even though the Cabinet said in March that the bulk of the 15,839 public sector vacancies it had approved would be absorbed by the defence and public security sectors. National schools lost 1,208 employees and the Samurdhi Department 1,448. The Sri Lanka Ports Authority's staff fell by 441 in a year when Colombo port faced congestion.\n\nThe Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) had 223 employees against an approved cadre of 972, down from 238 a year earlier, and had filled only four of its 463 tertiary-level posts. This is despite the Treasury's report committing to strengthen the \"institutional capacity and independence\" of the commission under the National Anti-Corruption Action Plan 2025-2029. The Attorney General's Department had 218 senior officers against 548 approved posts.\n\nWhile several key institutions operate well below their approved strength, others employ more people than they are permitted. According to the 2026 cadre report, 15 state institutions, excluding the Rural Development Ministry, together employ 973 people above their approved cadre.\n\nThe over-staffing is not confined to state firms. The Ministry of Transport, Highways and Urban Development had 531 employees against 457 approved posts, and the Court of Appeal had 139 against 132.\n\nLanka Sathosa had 608 more employees than approved posts in 2025. Its approved cadre has since been increased by 1,238 to 4,246."
