    "md5": "02cb39c4c8353293a716ff3e81324d7c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1862,
    "chunk_text": "Australia\u2019s sandpapergate probe to also include 2017-18 Ashes\n\nBBC - The terms of reference for the independent forum have been published and the scope will include \"ball-management practices in the Australian men's team during the 2017\u201318 season\", which includes the Ashes series Australia won 4-0.\n\nAlso included are all four Tests of the subsequent South Africa tour and the investigation which followed when the ball-tampering scandal emerged.\n\nCricket Australia has pledged to publish the full independent report and recommendations within 30 days of receiving them.\n\nAll those involved in Australia's 2018 tour of South Africa will be invited to speak to the independent forum, though none will have to do so and no retrospective sanctions will be handed out.\n\nThe scandal was reignited by the release of former batter Usman Khawaja's autobiography. In it, Khawaja claimed the entire team knew about ball-tampering in the third Test of their series against South Africa in Cape Town.\n\nThe four frontline bowlers in that 2018 team - Josh Hazlewood, Mitchell Starc, Nathan Lyon and current Test captain Pat Cummins - have always denied knowledge of the plan to use sandpaper on the ball.\n\nHowever, former team-mates David Warner and Cameron Bancroft - two of three players who were banned for their part in the scandal, alongside Steve Smith - have backed Khawaja's version of events.\n\n\"All participants in Australia's 2018 tour of South Africa, including players and support staff, will be invited to take part in the Independent Forum, as will CA management and directors at that time,\" Cricket Australia said.\n\n\"While all involved will be encouraged to participate, there will be no compulsion to do so. All materials supplied will be treated as confidential unless otherwise stated by the participant and no sanctions will be retrospectively applied."
  },
