    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "Although he did not name any union, Dr. Fernando took aim at professionals who rank their own contribution above everyone else\u2019s. \u201cIt could be a doctor, an engineer or an accountant. Yes, everyone\u2019s contribution to society is very, very important, but you can\u2019t say that \u2018my contribution is the biggest one\u2019. Everyone\u2019s contribution is critical,\u201d he insisted.\n\nHowever, Dr. Fernando linked the state\u2019s ability to deliver directly to its tax take, arguing that a government stands or falls on its ability to collect the revenue it has planned for. With few natural resources to fall back on, the government relies mainly on tax income to fund roads, healthcare, education and law and order, he explained.\n\nHe contrasted this with past experience, when governments collected too little and wasted much of what they did collect, opening the door to corruption. \u201cWe have closed all doors for corruption from the political sphere. That is the reason why we have been able to collect the revenue,\u201d he asserted.\n\nThat revenue, he noted, helped the government cushion two external shocks, namely natural disasters and the fallout from the Middle East war, through welfare benefits and concessions. Dr. Fernando, however, conceded that the state had absorbed the impact only \u201cto some extent\u201d. He warned that no one knows how long the geopolitical tensions will last or how intense they will become, a risk he described as unique to a Sri Lanka that is recovering from collapse and looking for \u201cthe big jump\u201d.\n\nMeanwhile, turning to insurance, Dr. Fernando described the industry at its core as a means of sharing risk, where everyone contributes but only those who suffer losses are indemnified. He observed that the profit motive could sometimes override those fundamentals. \u201cIt doesn\u2019t mean that profit is something bad. Profit is important, but it should be tied and connected to your value addition. Otherwise, there is no end to profit maximisation,\u201d he stressed."
