    "md5": "bed4ddff0f9cc1e1e3f66851658c37ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1836,
    "chunk_text": "International Finance Corporation taps HSBC for rupee funding in Asia first\n\nHSBC Sri Lanka provides six-year, fixed-rate rupee facility equivalent to US$ 5mn\n\nFacility helps fund IFC\u2019s 10-year rupee loan to a Sri Lankan renewable energy company\n\nThe International Finance Corporation (IFC) has secured its first rupee borrowing line in Sri Lanka from HSBC, helping fund a 10-year loan equivalent to US$10 million for a local renewable energy company and allowing the borrower to repay in rupees.\n\nThe World Bank Group yesterday said its Treasury had arranged the six-year, fixed-rate facility, equivalent to US$5 million, between IFC and HSBC Sri Lanka. It described the transaction as the first of its kind in Asia.\n\nThe HSBC facility, together with additional rupee funding arranged by the Treasury, supports IFC\u2019s loan to what the World Bank Group described as one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest renewable energy companies. The group did not name the borrower or disclose the interest rates on either facility.\n\nThe renewable energy loan is IFC\u2019s longest-tenor Sri Lankan rupee loan to date and its first rupee-denominated infrastructure loan. It also marks IFC\u2019s first infrastructure investment in Sri Lanka since the country\u2019s economic crisis.\n\nFor businesses that earn in rupees, borrowing in the same currency removes the risk that a weaker rupee will drive up the local cost of servicing a foreign currency loan. The World Bank Group said it would build on the HSBC transaction to expand access to longer-term rupee financing for private investment in Sri Lanka. \u201cAccess to long-term local currency financing can help businesses invest, grow and create jobs without taking on foreign exchange risk they may not be able to manage,\u201d said World Bank Group Director of Treasury Client Solutions and Structuring Keshav Gaur."
  },
