    "md5": "a152b7d333280ea586aef9debad683dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "Green trade rules pose risks for SL\u2019s rubber, apparel sectors\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s rubber producers could be hurt by emerging trade-related environmental rules, while textile and apparel firms could face higher compliance costs under new sustainability requirements, according to ODI Global Principal Research Fellow Jodie Keane said.\n\nDelivering the keynote at the Serendipity Knowledge Program\u2019s trade reform forum, Keane described the pressure on Sri Lanka as a \u201cgreen squeeze\u201d. The new regulations posed risks to producers in specific supply chains, with commodities still accounting for a substantial share of the country\u2019s export base, she said.\n\nKeane pointed to digital land mapping already under way to help smallholders adapt. In textiles and clothing, she flagged emerging corporate sustainability due diligence rules and the potential for higher compliance costs. The implications differed by industry, she said, making it important to assess the threats and opportunities in individual supply chains.\n\nKeane also highlighted potential openings for Sri Lankan suppliers as European and British companies develop production links in the region.\u00a0 She said the country should monitor the EU\u2019s Made in Europe initiative and examine how shifts in European trade and investment ties with India could affect Sri Lankan firms. \u201cThere may be opportunities to plug into specific EU and UK value chains,\u201d Keane said.\n\nCloser regional trade and stronger maritime links could shorten transit times and help Sri Lankan suppliers serve growing demand in India.\n\nKeane also linked port investment aimed at cutting emissions and withstanding extreme weather to the need for better digital connectivity, which could help smaller firms take advantage of new shipping and trade links."
  },
