    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka risks losing export ground to rivals: ADB cautions\n\nGlobal value chain trade accounted for 31% of Sri Lanka\u2019s gross trade in 2024, against 63% in Vietnam\n\nSays exports, investment and productivity must drive next phase of recovery\n\nAsserts wider market access must be matched by reforms to cut trade costs and improve the investment climate\n\nSri Lanka risks losing ground in key export markets as competitors secure preferential access through trade deals, Asian Development Bank (ADB) warned yesterday, calling for deeper trade integration and domestic reforms to help local firms compete.\n\nADB Country Director for Sri Lanka Shannon Cowlin, while citing the India-European Union free trade negotiations as an example of how other countries are moving to secure market access, noted Sri Lankan exporters also face higher requirements on quality, traceability and sustainability.\n\nShe shared her views while addressing the Serendipity Knowledge Program forum on \u2018From Trade Reform to Export Growth: Enabling Sri Lankan Firms to Compete Globally.\u2019\n\n\u201cAnd as competitors gain preferential access to key markets, Sri Lankan exporters risk losing ground unless the country pursues deeper trade integration and improved competitiveness,\u201d Cowlin said.\n\nThe gap with Vietnam illustrates the challenge. Global value chain trade accounted for approximately 31 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s gross trade in 2024, compared with 63 percent in Vietnam. Cowlin said Sri Lanka\u2019s export performance had lagged that of regional peers for decades, with merchandise exports concentrated in a few traditional sectors and foreign direct investment remaining low.\n\nHigh import protection, policy uncertainty and regulatory and institutional constraints had discouraged export diversification, she said.\n\n\u201cBut macroeconomic stability, while important, is not sufficient to deliver sustained growth and prosperity,\u201d Cowlin said."
